Question:
What are the three main wind azimuth regions that the FAA identifies as creating conditions conducive to LTE?

Original Answer:
The FAA identifies three critical wind azimuth regions: (1) Winds from 285° to 315° (the left quartering tailwind region), which can cause the main rotor vortex to interfere with tail rotor inflow; (2) Winds from 120° to 240° (the tail rotor vortex ring state region), where weathercock stability opposes the tail rotor and a vortex ring state can develop on the tail rotor itself; and (3) Winds from 210° to 330° (the weathercock stability region), where the helicopter tends to weathercock into the wind, demanding more tail rotor thrust than may be available.

Revised Answer:
The FAA identifies three critical wind azimuth regions: Main rotor vortex interference, Tail rotor vortex ring state, and Weathercock stability - The three wind azimuth regions cited (285°–315°, 120°–240°, and 210°–330°)

