Question ID:   PH.V.A.Effect.004
ACS Code:      Effect
Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
You are departing vertically from a confined area with a 15-knot left crosswind. As you rise through effective translational lift height, you notice the helicopter drifting right. What aerodynamic effect is contributing to this, and what corrective inputs do you apply?

Answer (at time of rejection):
As the helicopter transitions through translational lift (approximately 16–24 feet AGL), the rotor system becomes more efficient and the helicopter may suddenly accelerate in the direction opposite the crosswind — in this case, drifting right because the wind is pushing from the left. The pilot must apply left cyclic into the wind to counteract the drift. Additionally, as translational lift kicks in, expect a slight nose pitch change and be prepared to add left pedal if the increased lift requires more power (and therefore more torque compensation). The transition through ETL demands anticipation of all three control inputs simultaneously.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
Answer states 'helicopter drifting right because the wind is pushing from the left' — this is contradictory; wind from the left pushes the helicopter to the right, so drift to the right is expected wind effect, not an ETL transition effect
The explanation conflates wind drift with ETL transition drift; ETL transition actually improves efficiency and reduces drift when proper cyclic is maintained
The characterization of 'drifting right' as caused by ETL efficiency increase is aerodynamically imprecise — the drift is primarily wind effect, not ETL effect

