Difficulty:    basic

Question:
How does a wind sock indicate both wind direction AND approximate wind speed to a helicopter pilot during an approach?

Answer (at time of rejection):
A wind sock extends downwind from its mounting pole — so the open end of the sock points in the direction the wind is blowing toward, and the closed end (attached to the ring) faces into the wind. The pilot approaches FROM the direction the closed end faces (into the wind). Wind speed is indicated by the angle of the sock relative to horizontal: a fully extended, near-horizontal sock indicates approximately 15 knots or more; a drooping or partially extended sock indicates lighter winds; a limp, hanging sock indicates near calm conditions. Some airports have calibrated wind socks designed so full extension represents a specific calibrated speed (often 15 knots). This helps the helicopter pilot anticipate crosswind component and effective translational lift behavior during the approach.

