The answer provides a reasonable technique ('double the wind correction angle') but this is a rule of thumb that may not be precise for all scenarios. The calculation logic ('sin of drift ≈ 4/30') oversimplifies the wind vector geometry and doesn't rigorously account for along-track versus cross-track displacement. The answer conflates two separate problems: (1) determining what went wrong with the wind correction, and (2) calculating the intercept heading. A more rigorous approach would use the E6-B or flight computer to re-solve the wind problem based on observed drift, then apply the corrected wind correction angle. The statement should clarify whether the 4-mile displacement is perpendicular to the intended track or along it.

