Answer (at time of rejection):
Low rotor RPM at the point of collective application during the cushion indicates you delayed the collective pull too long or you pulled collective too early in the maneuver allowing RPM to bleed off without enough inertia remaining. At this point, you must apply collective smoothly but deliberately to use the remaining stored rotor energy to arrest the descent and cushion the landing before RPM decays below minimum. Any further delay risks a hard landing or loss of control. After landing, analyze the timing: the collective should be raised as the skids approach the ground, not before, to maximize the rotor energy available at the critical moment.

