Answer (at time of rejection):
Translating tendency (tail rotor thrust) pushes a counterclockwise-rotor helicopter to the right in a hover. During a leftward sideward hover, the pilot must apply left cyclic not only to move left but also to counter the ongoing rightward translating tendency — meaning the left cyclic demand is higher, reducing the available cyclic margin on the left side. During rightward sideward hover, the translating tendency is assisting the rightward movement, meaning the pilot may actually need to moderate or limit right cyclic input, and the lateral drift can build faster than intended. This makes rightward sideward hover higher risk for dynamic rollover from this factor alone, because translating tendency accelerates rightward drift, increasing the risk that the right skid contacts the surface with excess lateral momentum. Additionally, tail rotor considerations mean any loss of tail rotor effectiveness during a sideward hover would compound the lateral control challenge.

