Answer (at time of rejection):
Translating tendency is always present but is normally counteracted by left cyclic input and does not cause rollover by itself. It becomes a dynamic rollover threat only when three conditions converge: (1) a pivot point exists — a skid or wheel is in firm ground contact while the other side is or becomes airborne, creating a fulcrum; (2) a rolling moment is present or increasing — either from cyclic displacement toward the grounded side, increased collective, or external force; and (3) translating tendency adds an additional rightward force component to an already-developing rightward roll. The critical factor is the rate of roll. At low roll rates, the pilot has time to respond with opposite cyclic and reduced collective. The danger occurs when roll rate accelerates faster than perception and control inputs can counteract it — which is why conditions that allow rapid pivot (hard skid catch, firm ground contact, high collective application) combined with rightward translating tendency are particularly hazardous. In left-rolling scenarios, translating tendency provides slight resistance, but that margin should never be operationally relied upon.

