What is retreating blade stall, and which part of the rotor disk does it occur on in a helicopter configured for a right-hand rotating main rotor system?

Answer (at time of rejection):
Retreating blade stall occurs when the retreating rotor blade exceeds its critical angle of attack and stalls. On a helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor (right-hand torque), the retreating blade is on the left side of the disk — the 9 o'clock position — because that blade is moving opposite to the direction of flight and has reduced relative airspeed. To compensate for the reduced airspeed, blade pitch (angle of attack) increases to maintain lift equality across the disk, and if pushed too far — typically at high forward airspeeds — the blade stalls.

