A student consistently holds altitude well during stationary hover practice but loses altitude control during vertical takeoffs — specifically in the transition from skid contact through 3 feet AGL. As their instructor, what is the most likely control error and how would you remediate it?

Answer (at time of rejection):
The most likely error is a hesitation or lag on the collective during the transition from light-on-the-skids to full hover. Many students apply collective to unload the skids, feel the helicopter lighten, then pause before the helicopter has fully broken ground — causing an abrupt or uncoordinated liftoff. This creates altitude oscillation in the critical ±1/2 tolerance zone below 10 feet. Remediation involves teaching the student to apply collective smoothly and continuously through the liftoff, recognizing the 'light-on-skids' cue as a prompt to continue — not to pause. Ground reference practice focusing solely on the rate of collective application, combined with simulator hover work if available, helps build the muscle memory for a fluid, controlled vertical lift.

