Answer (at time of rejection):
Loss of favorable headwind during final approach means you lose translational lift more rapidly and at a higher indicated airspeed than expected. Your rate of descent may increase unexpectedly, and your rotor RPM could decay slightly if collective is not increased promptly. Immediately increase collective to arrest any unexpected sink rate and maintain rotor RPM within limits. Use aft cyclic as needed to control airspeed. Be aware that in a narrow canyon, your lateral abort options are gone — this increases the importance of recognizing the wind shift early and managing collective aggressively. If the wind becomes a tailwind component, consider aborting immediately since the power required will spike and your ability to decelerate to a controlled hover over the slope is compromised. The key is anticipating that canyon winds are notoriously unreliable and maintaining a conservative power margin throughout.

