Question ID:   PH.VII.A.S6.002
ACS Code:      PH.VII.A.S6
Difficulty:    basic

Question:
What is the difference between a wind correction angle applied during preflight planning and the actual wind correction angle you determine en route, and what instrument do you use to measure the actual one?

Original Answer:
The preflight wind correction angle (WCA) is calculated on the ground using forecast winds — typically from a winds-aloft forecast — and applied to your true course to determine a planned true heading. En route, you determine the actual WCA by tracking a ground reference or comparing your GPS ground track to your desired course, then adjusting your magnetic heading until you are on course. You use the heading indicator to read your actual magnetic heading and compare it to your planned heading; the difference tells you how much more or less wind correction is needed than planned. The actual WCA should be recorded on your navigation log to allow correction of subsequent legs.

Revised Answer:
The preflight wind correction angle (WCA) is calculated on the ground using forecast winds — typically from a winds-aloft forecast — and applied to your true course to determine a planned true heading. En route, you determine the actual WCA by tracking a ground reference or comparing your GPS ground track to your desired course, then adjusting your magnetic heading until you are on course. Read your actual magnetic heading and compare it to your planned heading; the difference tells you how much more or less wind correction is needed than planned. The actual WCA should be recorded on your navigation log to allow correction of subsequent legs.

Verifier Notes:
Answer states you 'use the heading indicator to measure the actual WCA' and compare planned to actual heading, but this is imprecise. The heading indicator alone does not measure WCA — you measure WCA by observing ground track (via pilotage landmarks, GPS ground track, or drift observation) and comparing it to your magnetic heading. The heading indicator displays your heading, not your actual track. The difference between magnetic heading flown and ground track observed is the actual WCA. The answer conflates heading measurement with WCA determination.

