Do not dismiss the indication simply because no warning lights are active — warning systems can lag or fail independently of sensors. Cross-check the N1 indication against other related parameters: torque, N2, ITT, and fuel flow. If the other instruments are normal and consistent with each other, the anomaly may indicate a sensor malfunction; if multiple parameters are diverging, treat it as a potential engine anomaly. Reference the AFM abnormal procedures checklist. Apply risk management by considering a diversion to a suitable landing area — do not continue to a distant destination if the anomaly cannot be explained. The guiding principle from ADM: if an outcome will only 'probably' work out, choose an option you know will work. Inform ATC and consider declaring a precautionary situation.

