Original Answer:
Operational pressure from the crew overdue situation is a classic external pressure that creates urgency to launch into marginal conditions. Tools and strategies to counteract it: (1) IMSAFE checklist — assess your own physical and mental readiness; stress from repeated calls can itself degrade performance. (2) PAVE risk model — Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures — explicitly naming and rating each category forces conscious evaluation rather than emotional reaction. (3) Personal minimums — pre-established go/no-go criteria that are set before the pressure exists, so they cannot be rationalized away in the moment. (4) Authority to say no — under 14 CFR 91.3, the PIC has final authority and is responsible for the safe operation of the flight; no dispatcher or operations manager can override this. (5) Remember that the Trescott Tip from the HFH applies: if a course of action will only 'probably' work, choose an option known to work. Marginal weather that is 'probably' okay is not an acceptable standard when crew overdue pressure is present.

