An unlatched door or window in a helicopter can swing open during liftoff or translation into forward flight, creating an immediate distraction at the worst possible time — during a low-altitude, low-airspeed phase where pilot attention is entirely required for aircraft control. In a helicopter, unlike most fixed-wing aircraft, the low altitude and limited autorotation margin during the initial departure means the pilot cannot simply maintain altitude while dealing with the distraction. An open door can also strike the fuselage or tail rotor, potentially causing structural or rotor system damage. Additionally, loose objects can be pulled out by airflow or interfere with controls. The check confirms a secure cockpit before entering the most vulnerable portion of the flight profile.

