Question:
What does a double solid yellow line paired with a double dashed yellow line across a taxiway represent, and which side of the marking are you required to hold on?

Original Answer:
This is a runway holding position marking (runway hold short marking). The solid double yellow lines are on the side facing the runway — you must hold on the dashed side (away from the runway) unless ATC has cleared you onto or across the runway. The solid lines indicate the side from which you need ATC clearance to cross; the dashed lines indicate the side you may cross from without clearance (when exiting the runway). Never cross the solid double yellow lines without an explicit ATC clearance or, at non-towered airports, ensuring the runway is clear.

Revised Answer:
This is a runway holding position marking (runway hold short marking). The solid double yellow lines are on the side facing the runway — you must hold on the dashed side (away from the runway) unless ATC has cleared you onto or across the runway. Standard runway hold short marking has solid double yellow lines on the runway side (facing the runway) and dashed on the taxiway side. A pilot on the taxiway side must not cross the solid lines without clearance.. Never cross the solid double yellow lines without an explicit ATC clearance or, at non-towered airports, ensuring the runway is clear.

Verifier Notes:
Answer contains confusing and potentially contradictory language about which side is solid vs. dashed and which side requires clearance. The explanation states 'The solid lines indicate the side from which you need ATC clearance to cross; the dashed lines indicate the side you may cross from without clearance (when exiting the runway)' — this mixing of runway approach and runway exit contexts is unclear. Standard runway hold short marking has solid double yellow lines on the runway side (facing the runway) and dashed on the taxiway side. A pilot on the taxiway side must not cross the solid lines without clearance. The parenthetical about 'exiting the runway' muddies the main concept.

