For a private helipad within Class D airspace, you must establish two-way radio contact with the Class D tower prior to any departure — the two-way contact requirement applies to the airspace you are entering, not just the runway you depart from. I would contact the Class D tower directly on their published tower frequency from the Chart Supplement or sectional chart, advise them of my location (the hospital helipad by name or coordinates), aircraft type, and intended departure direction or destination, and obtain acknowledgment of my call sign. Since the helipad is within Class D, I am already in controlled airspace, so prior to any movement off the surface that would enter the Class D environment I need that contact established. There is no specific CTAF or PCL applicable here — the tower frequency governs. I would also monitor tower throughout the departure and comply with any traffic advisories or instructions.

