Question ID:   PH.IV.C.006
ACS Code:      Towered
Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
At a nontowered airport, you are flying a helicopter to a marked helipad on the airport that is not aligned with any runway. Wind is calm and there are three fixed-wing aircraft in the standard left-hand pattern for Runway 30. How do you safely integrate into operations and land at the helipad?

Original Answer:
At a nontowered airport with no wind, you have flexibility in approach direction, but you must integrate safely with existing traffic. The steps are: (1) Monitor the CTAF frequency well before arrival and announce your position and intentions. (2) Identify where the fixed-wing pattern is relative to the helipad location. (3) Plan an approach path that avoids the fixed-wing traffic pattern legs — particularly final approach and base leg for Runway 30 — and minimizes conflict. (4) Fly a lower altitude than the fixed-wing pattern (typically 500 feet AGL) if crossing through their pattern area. (5) Make position reports on CTAF at standard reporting points and state your intention to land at the helipad. (6) Yield right-of-way to aircraft on final approach. (7) See and avoid all traffic throughout the approach. The helicopter is not required to fly the fixed-wing rectangular pattern but must remain alert, communicate, and not create a collision hazard.

Revised Answer:
Gusty conditions require you to add an airspeed correction to your approach speed — typically one-half the gust value above the steady-state wind component, up to a manufacturer-recommended maximum. With gusts 10 knots above steady-state (15 gusting to 25), adding 5 knots to approach airspeed is standard practice. On base leg, anticipate that the tailwind component (if runway alignment results in a crosswind) may cause the aircraft to overshoot final — shorten base or adjust bank accordingly. On final, the gust factor can cause sudden airspeed fluctuations, requiring more aggressive power management. Maintain a higher approach airspeed and be prepared for sudden sink from a gust decrease. Approach airspeed should not fall below ETL unless transitioning to hover with available power margin.

