Question ID:   PH.IV.C.Right-of-way.003
ACS Code:      Right-of-way
Difficulty:    basic

Question:
In the right-of-way hierarchy under 14 CFR 91.113, where does a helicopter rank compared to an airplane, a glider, and a balloon?

Original Answer:
Under 14 CFR 91.113(e), aircraft with lesser maneuverability have right-of-way. The hierarchy from highest to lowest right-of-way priority is: (1) balloon, (2) glider, (3) airship, (4) aircraft towing or refueling other aircraft, (5) all other engine-driven aircraft including airplanes and helicopters. A helicopter ranks the same as a powered airplane in this hierarchy — neither has priority over the other based on aircraft category alone. The converging, overtaking, and head-on rules then apply between similarly categorized aircraft.

Revised Answer:
Per 14 CFR 91.113(b)-(e), the correct priority order is: (1) balloon, (2) glider, (3) airship, (4) aircraft towing or refueling other aircraft, (5) powered parachute, weight-shift-control aircraft, and then all other engine-driven aircraft. A helicopter ranks the same as a powered airplane in this hierarchy — neither has priority over the other based on aircraft category alone. The converging, overtaking, and head-on rules then apply between similarly categorized aircraft.

Verifier Notes:
The hierarchy presented omits 'aircraft towing or refueling other aircraft' from its numbered list, yet includes it in the parenthetical — this is inconsistent and potentially confusing. Per 14 CFR 91.113(b)-(e), the correct priority order is: (1) balloon, (2) glider, (3) airship, (4) aircraft towing or refueling other aircraft, (5) powered parachute, weight-shift-control aircraft, and then all other engine-driven aircraft. The answer conflates 91.113(b) (distress) and 91.113(e) (category hierarchy) without clearly distinguishing them. Additionally, the answer does not cite 91.113(e) correctly — powered parachutes and weight-shift-control aircraft have lower priority than standard engine-driven aircraft under some interpretations, and the simplified grouping of 'all other engine-driven aircraft' may be misleading depending on aircraft type.

