Question:
A heliport is co-located on an airport ramp. You are on short final to the helipad when a fuel truck begins crossing your intended flight path. Describe how task saturation in the traffic pattern increases collision risk and what risk management strategies reduce it.

Answer (at time of rejection):
During short final, the pilot is managing airspeed, descent rate, collective, pedals, and power — a high cognitive workload phase. Task saturation can cause a narrowing of attention (tunneling), reducing awareness of ground-level hazards like vehicles. Risk management strategies include: conducting a thorough reconnaissance of the helipad area before entering the pattern; establishing stable references early on final so fewer control corrections are needed; designating checkpoints in the pattern where ground hazard scans are explicitly performed; using CTAF or ramp frequency to coordinate with ground vehicles; being willing to go around without hesitation if the landing area is not clear. Per ADM principles in FAA-H-8083-21B, breaking one link in the error chain (e.g., announcing a go-around) can prevent an accident.

