Note: This question ID 'PH.IV.C.RM.005' is a duplicate — it also appears earlier in the batch assigned to the 'Collision' ACS code. This second instance uses the 'Distractions,' ACS code. The duplicate ID will cause data integrity issues in the question bank and must be resolved.
The reference to FAA-H-8083-15B Chapter 9 for spatial disorientation is questionable. FAA-H-8083-15B is the Instrument Flying Handbook; spatial disorientation content appears in Chapter 5 of that handbook, not Chapter 9. Additionally, for a VFR helicopter private pilot context, FAA-H-8083-21B is the more appropriate primary reference.
The answer conflates spatial/vestibular disorientation with positional/navigational disorientation in the pattern. These are distinct concepts; the scenario describes positional disorientation (not knowing where the runway is), not spatial/vestibular disorientation (not knowing aircraft attitude). The reference to FAA-H-8083-15B spatial disorientation chapter is therefore misapplied.

