How does a headwind versus a tailwind affect the ground run distance and climb performance during a maximum performance takeoff?

Original Answer:
A headwind reduces the groundspeed at which ETL is reached, meaning the helicopter achieves the aerodynamic benefits of translational lift sooner and with less forward movement over the ground. This shortens the ground run, reduces power required during the transition, and improves obstacle clearance angle. A tailwind has the opposite effect — the helicopter must cover more ground before reaching ETL, increasing the distance required to clear obstacles and placing greater demands on available power. FAA guidance recommends repositioning to the most downwind portion of the available area before beginning a maximum performance takeoff to maximize the effective takeoff run into the wind.

