Water vapor is less dense than dry air. When humidity is high, water molecules displace some of the heavier oxygen and nitrogen molecules in the air, reducing overall air density. Lower air density means the rotor blades generate less lift per unit of blade angle, and the engine produces less power because there is less dense air-fuel mixture available for combustion (in piston engines particularly, but turbines are also affected). The result during a steep approach is that the collective must be raised higher to generate the same lift force, and the available power margin is narrower. This is why high humidity is factored into density altitude calculations — though standard density altitude charts typically assume dry air, actual performance on a very humid day will be worse than the chart predicts. The pilot should treat high humidity as an additive performance penalty and treat the day conservatively.

