Question:
During a steep approach in degraded visibility — say, haze reducing visibility to 3 miles — how does reduced visibility specifically alter your collision hazard risk, and what procedural adjustments should you make?

Original Answer:
Reduced visibility directly increases collision risk during a steep approach in several ways: obstacles that were identified during a clear-day reconnaissance may be difficult or impossible to see in time to avoid them; depth perception is degraded, making it harder to judge clearance from trees and structures; wire spotting becomes nearly impossible against a hazy background; and it is harder to detect other aircraft in the vicinity. Procedural adjustments include: (1) reassessing whether the approach should be attempted at all given visibility and ceiling — VFR minimums per 14 CFR 91.155 must be met, (2) flying the reconnaissance at a slower speed and lower altitude if safe to do so to re-verify obstacle positions, (3) increasing lateral clearance margins from all known obstacles, (4) briefing passengers and any crew on the hazards, and (5) ensuring the go-around path is obstacle-free and clearly planned before descent. If visibility makes the landing zone or obstacles difficult to confirm, the approach should be declined.

