Question ID:   PH.VI.F.RM.008
ACS Code:      Go-around.
Difficulty:    advanced

Question:
You are flying a turbine helicopter at high density altitude into a confined area. You verify during your power check that you have OGE hover capability, but only by a 5% torque margin. You identify your go-around point and begin the approach. At what point during the approach does your go-around capability actually change, and what makes this a dynamic rather than a static risk?

Answer (at time of rejection):
Go-around capability is dynamic because multiple performance variables change throughout the approach. At the start of the approach (higher altitude, higher airspeed), ETL is contributing to lift and power requirements are relatively lower — the go-around is most achievable here. As you descend below ETL (approximately 16–24 knots), induced drag increases sharply and power demand rises. As you also descend into the confined area, obstacle clearance margins decrease and the climb angle needed to exit safely increases. Meanwhile, if the OGE power margin was only 5% at the start, any changes in conditions — a slight temperature rise, a momentary wind reduction, or rotor RPM droop — can eliminate that margin entirely. Therefore, the go-around commitment point is most critical at the altitude where ETL is being lost, because below that point the power demand peaks and the escape corridor requires maximum performance climb. A 5% torque margin is extremely thin and likely insufficient to provide a safe go-around buffer throughout the entire approach profile, making this scenario high-risk regardless of the positive OGE check at the start.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
(none)

