Question ID:   PH.VI.G.S_radio.006
ACS Code:      Make
Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
You are operating under Part 135 at a remote pinnacle site and lose radio contact with your company operations center during final approach. Walk me through the radio calls you would still make and how you manage the communications failure.

Answer (at time of rejection):
Even without contact with company ops, you should continue making position calls on the appropriate air-to-air or advisory frequency to alert other aircraft in the area. Use 123.025 MHz as the common air-to-air frequency. Make calls at key points: beginning the approach, on short final, landing, and before departure. For the company communications failure itself, most Part 135 operators have required check-in times — if you miss a check-in, the company may initiate search and rescue protocols per their operations specifications. You should attempt contact on all available frequencies, including guard (121.5 MHz) if normal comms are unavailable. Document the communications failure in your logbook and report it to your chief pilot after the flight. Maintaining positional radio calls to other traffic remains your primary in-flight responsibility even during the comm failure.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The answer references 14 CFR 135.87 but does not cite the specific regulation that governs communications failure procedures for Part 135 operators. Part 135.87 covers general equipment and instrument requirements. The specific communications failure procedures are typically found in 14 CFR 135.405 (VFR/IFR flight plans and alternates) and operational specifications (OpSpecs) which vary by certificate holder.
The statement about 'required check-in times' is accurate but generic — Part 135 OpSpecs specify flight following and check-in procedures that vary significantly by operator, and the answer should note this individual variation.

