A tailwind does not directly increase the aerodynamic descent rate — the helicopter's airspeed and rotor aerodynamics are relative to the air mass. However, the tailwind increases groundspeed significantly, which means the helicopter will cover the ground distance much faster and may overshoot the intended landing area, arriving at the flare point with excessive groundspeed. The practical danger is that the pilot may arrive at the flare point with higher-than-normal groundspeed, creating a more dynamic and difficult landing rollout or slide. Additionally, from 800 feet AGL, a tailwind reduces the time available to manage the approach, and if the pilot slows airspeed to correct perceived overshoot, the rotor-relative aerodynamic descent rate will increase. The combination creates a compressed, high-energy arrival scenario.

