Excessive aft cyclic increases the pitch attitude and can momentarily increase rotor RPM by increasing upward airflow through the disk, but if coordinated poorly with collective position it can lead to an overspeed condition. More critically, abrupt or excessive cyclic inputs during autorotation disturb the stabilized descent and can cause uncoordinated flight, increasing drag on the rotor system and risking an RPM decay if the inputs are mismanaged. Smooth, coordinated control inputs are essential to maintain rotor RPM within the green arc throughout the maneuver.

