Pulling aft cyclic while rolling into a banked turn compounds the load factor on the rotor system. The bank angle already increases the effective weight the rotor must support; adding aft cyclic further loads the disk. This combination can push blade AOA toward stall on the retreating blade side, risk rotor RPM overspeed if upflow increases dramatically, and reduce the rate of descent below what is needed to maintain energy for the landing flare. Additionally, the combination distorts the intended flight path — the helicopter may climb, disrupting the planned approach geometry. The correct technique is to roll into the turn smoothly using primarily cyclic for bank and coordinated pedal, while keeping collective at the established autorotation position and allowing rotor RPM to stabilize within limits. Aft cyclic during the turn should be minimal and used only to manage RPM within limits, not as the primary RPM control tool.

