Left lateral drift during forward cyclic input in a hover is most commonly caused by dissymmetry of lift or retreating blade effect as the helicopter transitions out of the hover, combined with the loss of torque compensation from the failed engine. As engine torque drops to zero, the tail rotor — still being driven by the main rotor transmission in autorotation — is producing the same left pedal input that was trimmed for powered flight. This can induce a left yaw and left lateral drift if pedal trim is not adjusted. Additionally, in a clockwise-rotating rotor system (viewed from above), translational flight produces a tendency to roll and drift left due to tail rotor thrust offset. The pilot must apply right cyclic to correct the drift and right pedal to trim the yaw, maintaining directional control and keeping the aircraft aligned with the intended landing path. Allowing uncorrected drift means the skids will touch down in a lateral skid, which — even with low forward speed — creates a rollover risk, particularly on uneven or sloped surfaces.

