The answer attributes the left drift primarily to 'dissymmetry of lift or retreating blade effect' and then suggests it is caused by tail rotor thrust offset from loss of torque compensation. While both factors can contribute to yaw and roll, the explanation jumps between concepts without clearly prioritizing the actual cause for the student's scenario.
The statement 'the tail rotor — still being driven by the main rotor transmission in autorotation — is producing the same left pedal input that was trimmed for powered flight' is somewhat misleading. In autorotation, tail rotor thrust is still present, but the loss of engine torque means the pilot's previous pedal trim input is no longer appropriate. The cause is trim mismatch, not that the tail rotor is suddenly producing an unexpected input.
The answer correctly identifies the correction (right cyclic, right pedal) but does not clearly explain why a clockwise-rotating rotor (viewed from above) would specifically produce a left yaw and left lateral drift during the transition to forward flight. This omission makes it harder for students to understand the physics.

