Question:
What is a 'stabilization gate' in an approach, and why does it matter for OEI helicopter operations?

Answer (at time of rejection):
A stabilization gate is a defined altitude or point on the approach — commonly 500 feet AGL in IMC or 300 feet AGL in VMC — by which all approach parameters (airspeed, descent rate, power setting, configuration, and flight path) must be stabilized. If not stabilized by the gate, a go-around or missed approach must be executed. For OEI helicopter operations this gate is critically important because below that altitude the pilot has very limited time and, with only one engine, very limited excess power to correct deviations or safely execute a go-around. Reaching the gate in a stable condition maximizes the chance of a safe landing or a timely go-around decision.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The generic stabilization gate altitudes (500 ft IMC / 300 ft VMC) may not reflect OEI-specific procedures. Many multiengine helicopter OEI approaches require commitment or stabilization gates significantly higher than standard instrument approach gates due to limited power reserve and go-around capability on one engine.

