While the main rotor is mechanically driven and does not depend on the electrical system, many systems essential to safe flight do depend on electricity. These include: engine instruments (torque, TOT/EGT, N1/N2 RPM gauges on turbine aircraft — loss of these means the pilot cannot detect engine over-temp or over-torque); low rotor RPM warning systems and warning lights; fuel quantity indicators; flight instruments that may be electrically driven (turn coordinator, attitude indicator on some installations); navigation and communication radios; lighting systems required by regulation for night flight (14 CFR 91.209); and in some aircraft, stability augmentation or autopilot systems. On turbine helicopters, fuel control systems or FADEC may also have electrical dependencies. The bottom line is that an electrical failure does not mean the helicopter is uncontrollable, but it significantly degrades situational awareness, crew safety systems, and regulatory compliance — making it an emergency requiring prompt action and a decision to land.

