AI_EDIT	PH.I.F.R4.006	Uncertainty in load weight is a significant risk factor. The phrase 'give or take' could mean the load is actually heavier than 400 lbs, potentially pushing you over maximum gross weight. FAA guidance is clear that when an action will only 'probably' work out, you should choose an option that is known to work. In external load operations, load weight must be verified — not estimated — before flight. The correct action is to weigh the load accurately before departure. Additionally, even if the nominal weight is within limits, external load flight requires consideration of dynamic loading factors and the helicopter's external load flight manual supplement or STC documentation.	->	Uncertainty in load weight is a significant risk factor. The phrase 'give or take' could mean the load is actually heavier than 400 lbs, potentially pushing you over maximum gross weight. FAA guidance is clear that when an action will only 'probably' work out, you should choose an option that is known to work. In external load operations, load weight must be verified — not estimated — before flight. The correct action is to weigh the load accurately before departure. Additionally, even if the nominal weight is within limits, external load flight requires consideration of dynamic loading factors and the helicopter's RFM External Load Supplement or equivalent type certificate limitations.
AI_EDIT	PH.I.F.R4.007	The argument is flawed on multiple levels. First, the certified maximum gross weight in the RFM Limitations section is a legally binding limitation under 14 CFR 91.9 — operating above it is a regulatory violation regardless of the magnitude of exceedance. Second, while structural safety factors do exist in the certification process, those safety factors are not published or disclosed to operators and are not intended as a buffer for intentional exceedance — they account for variability in materials, manufacturing, and unexpected dynamic loads. Third, the performance data in the RFM (hover charts, climb gradients, autorotation distances) is only valid at or below the certified weight. Operating even slightly above that weight invalidates the performance data the pilot relied on for planning. Fourth, in an emergency such as a power failure or retreating blade stall onset, that 2% extra weight could be the difference between a survivable outcome and a fatal one.	->	The argument is flawed on multiple levels. First, the certified maximum gross weight in the RFM Limitations section is a legally binding limitation under 14 CFR 91.9 — operating above it is a regulatory violation regardless of the magnitude of exceedance. Second, while structural safety factors do exist in the certification process, those safety factors are not published or disclosed to operators and are not intended as a buffer for intentional exceedance — they account for variability in materials, manufacturing, and unexpected dynamic loads. Third, the performance data in the RFM (hover charts, climb gradients, autorotation distances) is only valid at or below the certified weight. Operating even slightly above that weight invalidates the performance data the pilot relied on for planning. Fourth, in an emergency such as a power failure or retreating blade stall onset, that 2% extra weight could be the difference between a survivable outcome and a fatal one. Follow-up: If a pilot discovers after landing that they were 3% over gross weight the entire flight, the pilot should notify the maintenance organization to determine whether an inspection is required per the manufacturer's maintenance manual or any applicable airworthiness directive. The aircraft must be returned to an airworthy condition before next flight. The FAA could pursue enforcement under 14 CFR 91.9 for operating in violation of aircraft limitations, or under 14 CFR 91.13 for careless or reckless operation. If it was a commercial operation, additional enforcement under Part 135 or Part 133 could apply, and the operator's certificate could be at risk.
AI_EDIT	PH.I.F.R5.002	The two primary types are forward CG exceedance and aft CG exceedance. A forward CG condition requires aft cyclic to maintain level flight; if CG is too far forward, the pilot may run out of aft cyclic travel and be unable to flare or arrest a nose-low pitch attitude. An aft CG condition requires forward cyclic to maintain level flight; if CG is too far aft, the pilot may run out of forward cyclic and be unable to correct a nose-high, tail-low attitude, which can lead to loss of translational lift and a pitch-over. Many helicopters also have lateral CG limits that, if exceeded, can cause the cyclic to reach its lateral stop.	->	The two primary types are forward CG exceedance and aft CG exceedance. A forward CG condition requires aft cyclic to maintain level flight; if CG is too far forward, the pilot may run out of aft cyclic travel and be unable to flare or arrest a nose-low pitch attitude. An aft CG condition requires forward cyclic to maintain level flight; if CG is too far aft, the pilot may run out of forward cyclic authority and be unable to correct a nose-high attitude. This can lead to retreating blade stall, settling with power, or on teetering rotor systems, mast bumping — all potentially resulting in loss of control. Many helicopters also have lateral CG limits that, if exceeded, can cause the cyclic to reach its lateral stop.
AI_EDIT	PH.I.F.R5.005	This scenario describes an off-CG condition, likely a forward or lateral CG exceedance caused by the external load's hook point location relative to the helicopter's CG. When CG is displaced, the pilot must hold a sustained cyclic offset to maintain attitude, which reduces the remaining cyclic authority available to correct for disturbances or gusts. The 'sluggish' response suggests the cyclic was approaching or near its travel limit. Before the flight, the pilot should have computed the combined CG with the external load factored in — sling load weight and hook arm must be included in the weight and balance calculation. If the computed CG falls outside limits with the load attached, the operation must not be conducted.	->	This scenario describes an off-CG condition, likely a forward or lateral CG exceedance caused by the external load's hook point location relative to the helicopter's CG. When CG is displaced, the pilot must hold a sustained cyclic offset to maintain attitude, which reduces the remaining cyclic authority available to correct for disturbances or gusts. The 'sluggish' response suggests the cyclic was approaching or near its travel limit. Before the flight, the pilot should have computed the combined CG with the external load factored in — sling load weight and hook arm must be included in the weight and balance calculation. If the computed CG falls outside limits with the load attached, the operation must not be conducted.
AI_EDIT	PH.I.F.R5.008	A heavy bag shifting aft moves the CG rearward, potentially outside the aft CG limit. The risk is reduced forward cyclic authority — the pilot may notice the aircraft tends to pitch nose-up and must hold increasing forward cyclic pressure to maintain attitude. If flight characteristics change noticeably — sustained nose-up tendency, unusual cyclic position required for level flight, or reduced pitch authority — the pilot should recognize this as a possible CG exceedance. The appropriate response is to reduce airspeed to reduce the margin consequences, communicate with ATC or dispatch, and land at the nearest suitable area as soon as practical. In flight, the pilot should not attempt to retrieve or reposition the bag if doing so is unsafe. After landing, baggage must be properly secured and weight and balance recomputed before continuing. This scenario also illustrates a preflight risk management failure: cargo must always be properly secured to prevent in-flight load shift.	->	A heavy bag shifting aft moves the CG rearward, potentially outside the aft CG limit. The risk is reduced forward cyclic authority — the pilot may notice the aircraft tends to pitch nose-up and must hold increasing forward cyclic pressure to maintain attitude. If flight characteristics change noticeably — sustained nose-up tendency, unusual cyclic position required for level flight, or reduced pitch authority — the pilot should recognize this as a possible CG exceedance. The appropriate response is to reduce airspeed to lower control forces required and increase time available for assessment and decision-making, communicate with ATC or dispatch, and land at the nearest suitable area as soon as practical. In flight, the pilot should not attempt to retrieve or reposition the bag if doing so is unsafe. After landing, baggage must be properly secured and weight and balance recomputed before continuing. This scenario also illustrates a preflight risk management failure: cargo must always be properly secured to prevent in-flight load shift.

