AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Purpose.006	The engine running smoothly at idle does not guarantee each ignition circuit is functioning correctly under load. The magneto or ignition check is performed at a higher power setting to verify that each ignition system is independently producing sufficient spark to fire the cylinders under real operating conditions. During this check, the pilot briefly selects each ignition position and looks for an RPM drop. A normal, small drop confirms that magneto is working but the other is carrying the load — as expected. No drop at all could indicate a shorted P-lead, meaning a magneto that cannot be shut down — a serious safety hazard. An excessive RPM drop or engine roughness indicates fouled plugs, a weak magneto, or ignition system failure. In turbine helicopters, the equivalent check verifies igniter function and engine acceleration characteristics. Without this check, an ignition system malfunction would only be discovered in flight, potentially leading to an engine failure or rough operation at a critical moment.	->	The engine running smoothly at idle does not guarantee each ignition circuit is functioning correctly under load. The magneto or ignition check is performed at a higher power setting to verify that each ignition system is independently producing sufficient spark to fire the cylinders under real operating conditions. During this check, the pilot briefly selects each ignition position and looks for an RPM drop. A normal, small drop confirms that magneto is working but the other is carrying the load — as expected. No RPM drop at all could indicate a shorted P-lead — the magneto continues to fire even with the ignition switch off, meaning the engine cannot be shut down by the ignition switch, creating a serious safety hazard on the ground. An excessive RPM drop or engine roughness indicates fouled plugs, a weak magneto, or ignition system failure. Without this check, an ignition system malfunction would only be discovered in flight, potentially leading to an engine failure or rough operation at a critical moment.
ESCALATE	PH.III.A.Purpose.008	The answer cites the '80% pilot error' statistic and 'avoid the word probably' guidance to specific chapters of FAA-H-8083-21B, but the verifier correctly notes these references require verification; the latter phrase is more commonly associated with other FAA handbooks (FAA-H-8083-25 or FAA-H-8083-2). Safety-critical ADM guidance must cite correct authoritative sources.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Purpose.001	Manufacturers develop configuration sequences based on engineering testing, flight test data, and system interdependencies specific to that helicopter model. Following the exact sequence ensures systems are engaged in the correct order — for example, activating certain avionics before generators are online, or ensuring hydraulics are pressurized before flight controls are cycled. A non-standard order can mask malfunctions or create unsafe states that a random check would not catch. The RFM-prescribed sequence is FAA-approved as part of the type certification process.	->	Manufacturers develop configuration sequences based on engineering testing, flight test data, and system interdependencies specific to that helicopter model. Following the exact sequence ensures systems are engaged in the correct order — for example, activating certain avionics before generators are online, or ensuring hydraulics are pressurized before flight controls are cycled. A non-standard order can mask malfunctions or create unsafe states that a random check would not catch. The Limitations section of the RFM is explicitly FAA-approved as part of type certification; the Normal Procedures checklist is developed by the manufacturer consistent with the approved flight envelope and is accepted during certification. Compliance with the RFM is required by 14 CFR 91.9.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Purpose.003	Examples include: (1) Transponder set to ALT mode — ensures ATC radar has accurate altitude encoding for traffic separation; (2) Doors latched or removed per the flight manual — prevents in-flight door separation that could strike the tail rotor or destabilize the aircraft; (3) Friction locks and throttle position set to the manufacturer-specified setting — ensures the pilot has proper throttle/collective control authority and prevents inadvertent movement during flight. The specific items vary by helicopter model and are defined in the RFM Normal Procedures section.	->	Examples include: (1) ADS-B Out verified active and transponder set to ALT mode — required by 14 CFR 91.225 in applicable airspace since January 1, 2020, ensuring ATC and other aircraft receive accurate position and altitude data for traffic separation; (2) Doors latched or removed per the flight manual — prevents in-flight door separation that could strike the tail rotor or destabilize the aircraft; (3) Friction locks and throttle position set to the manufacturer-specified setting — ensures the pilot has proper throttle/collective control authority and prevents inadvertent movement during flight. The specific items vary by helicopter model and are defined in the RFM Normal Procedures section.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Purpose.007	The applicant has confused a previous verification with a current confirmation. The purpose of the before takeoff configuration check is not simply to set items once — it is to verify their state immediately before committing to flight. Avionics settings can be inadvertently changed during taxi, bumped by a passenger, or reset after a momentary power interruption. The manufacturer specifies this check at this point in the sequence because it is the last opportunity to confirm configuration before departing the safety of the ground. Skipping a step because it was 'already done' reflects a normalization of deviance — a well-documented ADM failure where pilots bypass established safety steps because nothing has gone wrong before. This is the same cognitive trap implicated in a large percentage of aviation accidents. A disciplined pilot completes each checklist item every time regardless of perceived redundancy.	->	The applicant has confused a previous verification with a current confirmation. The purpose of the before takeoff configuration check is not simply to set items once — it is to verify their state immediately before committing to flight. Avionics settings can be inadvertently changed during taxi, bumped by a passenger, or reset after a momentary power interruption. The manufacturer specifies this check at this point in the sequence because it is the last opportunity to confirm configuration before departing the safety of the ground. Skipping a step because it was 'already done' reflects a normalization of deviance — a well-documented ADM failure where pilots bypass established safety steps because nothing has gone wrong before. This is the same cognitive trap implicated in a large percentage of aviation accidents. A disciplined pilot completes each checklist item every time regardless of perceived redundancy. Under 14 CFR 91.9, the pilot in command must comply with the limitations and procedures in the RFM; the ACS requires demonstrated proficiency in both checklist discipline and risk management (ACS Task PH.III.D).
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Purpose.008	The before takeoff hydraulic check is performed at the designated run-up/takeoff position because hydraulic system faults — particularly issues with hydraulic servos, actuators, and lines — can develop or change state with temperature, movement, or time. A system that felt normal during the run-up may exhibit stiffness or failure after taxiing due to a servo that binds at a different collective position, a slow hydraulic leak that has reduced system pressure, or an air pocket that migrated during movement. The manufacturer places this check immediately before takeoff to confirm the flight control system is functioning correctly at the power and configuration setting closest to takeoff — which is the most demanding phase. A failure detected at this point allows the pilot to abort on the ground. If the check is skipped and a hydraulic hard-over or stiffness occurs at low altitude during takeoff, the consequences can be fatal. The check also establishes a baseline: if controls feel normal before departure, any subsequent abnormality in flight is clearly identifiable as a new developing fault.	->	The before takeoff hydraulic check is performed at the designated run-up/takeoff position because hydraulic system faults — particularly issues with hydraulic servos, actuators, and lines — can develop or change state with temperature, movement, or time. A system that felt normal during the run-up may exhibit stiffness or failure after taxiing due to a servo that binds at a different collective position, a slow hydraulic leak that has reduced system pressure, or an air pocket that migrated during movement. The manufacturer places this check immediately before takeoff to confirm the flight control system is functioning correctly at the power and configuration setting closest to takeoff — which is the most demanding phase. A failure detected at this point allows the pilot to abort on the ground. If the check is skipped and a hydraulic hard-over or stiffness occurs at low altitude during takeoff, the consequences can be fatal. The check also establishes a baseline: if controls feel normal before departure, any subsequent abnormality in flight is clearly identifiable as a new developing fault. If a hydraulic anomaly is detected, the pilot should immediately return to the parking area and shut down for maintenance inspection — do not attempt flight. The specific abnormal or emergency procedure for a hydraulic malfunction will be found in the Emergency Procedures section of the FAA-approved Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) for that specific aircraft, and the RFM will specify which procedure steps are memory items that must be memorized; pilots should follow their aircraft's RFM guidance on this.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.D.RM.003	When a pilot relies on memory rather than a written checklist, cognitive workload increases because the pilot must simultaneously recall items, perform them, and track where they are in the sequence. This leaves less mental bandwidth for outside scanning and situational awareness. A written checklist externalizes the sequence, freeing working memory and reducing the likelihood of missed steps. The RFM for each helicopter includes a specific before takeoff checklist, and 14 CFR Part 91 requires compliance with operating limitations — which implicitly includes following approved procedures in the RFM.	->	When a pilot relies on memory rather than a written checklist, cognitive workload increases because the pilot must simultaneously recall items, perform them, and track where they are in the sequence. This leaves less mental bandwidth for outside scanning and situational awareness. A written checklist externalizes the sequence, freeing working memory and reducing the likelihood of missed steps. The RFM for each helicopter includes a specific before takeoff checklist. Operating in accordance with the aircraft's approved RFM procedures is required under 14 CFR 91.7 (requirement to maintain airworthiness) and 14 CFR 91.9 (adherence to operating limitations in the RFM).
AI_EDIT	PH.III.D.S-Review.003	The primary factors are: (1) density altitude — affected by field elevation, temperature, and humidity; (2) gross weight — total weight of aircraft, fuel, crew, and payload; (3) wind — headwind reduces effective translational lift distance; (4) obstacles — determine required climb angle and power margin; and (5) rotor RPM — must be in the normal operating range before departure. All of these interact to determine whether the planned takeoff profile is achievable.	->	The primary factors are: (1) density altitude — affected by field elevation, temperature, and humidity; (2) gross weight — total weight of aircraft, fuel, crew, and payload; (3) wind — a headwind component reduces the ground distance required to achieve effective translational lift (ETL), improving takeoff performance; calm or tailwind conditions require more ground distance and degrade performance; (4) obstacles — determine required climb angle and power margin; and (5) rotor RPM — must be in the normal operating range before departure. All of these interact to determine whether the planned takeoff profile is achievable.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Verify.001	You are verifying that all powerplant temperatures (such as turbine outlet temperature/TOT, exhaust gas temperature/EGT, engine oil temperature, and transmission oil temperature) and pressures (such as engine oil pressure, transmission oil pressure, and fuel pressure) are within the green arc or normal operating range. The acceptable ranges are found in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) limitations section and are also depicted on the cockpit gauges themselves as color-coded arcs. Per 14 CFR Part 91, pilots must comply with operating limitations specified in the approved RFM.	->	You are verifying that all powerplant temperatures and pressures are within the green arc or normal operating range specified for your aircraft. The primary temperature limit varies by helicopter type — common parameters include Turbine Gas Temperature (TGT), Interstage Turbine Temperature (ITT), Turbine Outlet Temperature (TOT), or Exhaust Gas Temperature (EGT), depending on manufacturer designation. Pressure parameters include engine oil pressure, transmission oil pressure, and fuel pressure. The acceptable ranges are found in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) limitations section and depicted on the cockpit gauges as color-coded arcs (green for normal, yellow for caution, red for not-to-exceed). Per 14 CFR Part 91.9, pilots must comply with operating limitations specified in the approved RFM. The color arc markings themselves are FAA-approved per the type certificate and 14 CFR Part 27 airworthiness standards.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Verify.003	Oil pressure can be within normal range immediately after start due to cold, viscous oil producing a momentary high reading, then settle into a stable normal range as the engine warms. Conversely, a slow oil leak or failing pump may cause pressure to drop gradually after start but before reaching the normal operating range. Checking oil pressure during the before-takeoff check — after the engine has had time to stabilize at run-up RPM — gives the most accurate picture of actual lubrication system health just prior to flight. The RFM specifies minimum oil pressure required for takeoff.	->	Oil pressure readings immediately after engine start may be misleading for two reasons: (1) Cold, viscous oil can produce a temporarily elevated pressure reading that normalizes as the oil warms — some RFMs specify a minimum oil temperature before takeoff is permitted; (2) A developing leak, failing pump, or other lubrication system degradation may cause pressure to drop gradually only after the engine has been running for several minutes at stable run-up RPM. Checking oil pressure during the before-takeoff check — after the engine has had time to warm and stabilize — gives an accurate picture of actual lubrication system health just prior to flight. The RFM specifies minimum oil pressure required for takeoff and, in some cases, minimum warm-up time or oil temperature thresholds before flight is permitted.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Verify.004	A reading above the green arc but below the red line places the indication in the yellow arc (caution range). The yellow arc means the value is outside normal continuous operation but is not an immediate exceedance. You should: (1) Reference the RFM limitations section to determine if there is a time limit for operation in that range and under what conditions it is permissible; (2) Allow more warm-up time to see if the temperature stabilizes or drops back into the green; (3) If TOT does not return to the green arc within the time limit specified in the RFM (or immediately if no time limit is given), do not depart — troubleshoot the cause. Elevated TOT before takeoff can indicate a fuel control issue, hot section problem, or inadequate cooling. Proceeding would be an unsafe operation per 14 CFR 91.9.	->	A reading above the green arc but below the red line is in the yellow arc (caution range). Yellow arc operation is to be avoided except when necessary and for the shortest duration possible. You should: (1) Consult the RFM limitations and abnormal procedures sections to determine if a time limit is specified for yellow arc operation and under what conditions it is permissible; (2) Allow additional warm-up time to observe whether the temperature stabilizes or returns to the green arc; (3) If TOT does not improve or remains elevated, do not depart — troubleshoot the cause with maintenance. Elevated TOT before takeoff can indicate a fuel control issue, hot section problem, or inadequate cooling. Proceeding would violate 14 CFR 91.7 (airworthiness requirement) and 91.9 (operating limitations compliance). This is an aeronautical decision-making situation where safety takes precedence over schedule.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.Verify.008	A consistent inter-engine temperature differential at matched power settings is not normal and suggests a developing problem with the #2 engine — possibly restricted oil cooling, a partially blocked oil cooler, a thermostat issue, or early bearing wear generating additional heat. Even though the reading is currently within the green arc, the trend and differential represent an anomaly requiring investigation before flight. Assessment steps: (1) Reference the RFM to determine if an inter-engine differential is addressed and what tolerance is acceptable; (2) If not addressed, treat it as an abnormal indication requiring maintenance review; (3) Do not depart without resolution. Regarding OEI implications: if you depart and #2 subsequently fails or requires shutdown due to this developing condition, your OEI performance is contingent on #1 functioning perfectly. Departing with a known anomaly on one engine means your safety margin in an OEI scenario is reduced — you may face an engine failure at a critical phase with a compromised fallback. The conservative and correct decision is no-go.	->	A consistent inter-engine temperature differential at matched power settings is not normal and suggests a developing problem with the #2 engine — possibly restricted oil cooling, a partially blocked oil cooler, a thermostat issue, or early bearing wear generating additional heat. Even though the reading is currently within the green arc, the trend and differential represent an anomaly requiring investigation before flight. Assessment steps: (1) Reference the RFM to determine if an inter-engine differential is addressed and what tolerance is acceptable; (2) If not addressed, treat it as an abnormal indication requiring maintenance review; (3) Do not depart without resolution. Regarding OEI implications: if you depart and #2 subsequently fails or requires shutdown due to this developing condition, your OEI performance is contingent on #1 functioning perfectly. Departing with a known anomaly on one engine means your safety margin in an OEI scenario is reduced — you may face an engine failure at a critical phase with a compromised fallback. The conservative and correct decision is no-go. Regulatory basis: 14 CFR 91.3 (PIC authority and responsibility) and 14 CFR 91.7 (airworthiness) require the PIC to ensure the aircraft is in a condition for safe flight; 14 CFR 91.213 applies only to inoperative instruments or equipment, not anomalous readings within green limits.
AI_EDIT	PH.III.A.S-Nr.006	High density altitude reduces air density, which affects both engine performance and rotor efficiency. In a piston helicopter, the engine may produce less power at high DA, which can cause Nr to stabilize at a lower value if the governor is working near its limit. In a turbine helicopter, torque limits may be reached sooner. The pilot should: (1) verify the Nr reading is still within the published green arc; (2) check the RFM for any density altitude limitations or power charts to determine if available power is sufficient for the intended departure; (3) consider whether the helipad is elevated or confined in a way that demands a steep departure requiring full power; and (4) perform a performance calculation for hover OGE to verify the helicopter can safely depart. If Nr is at the low edge of green only at flat pitch with no load, it may struggle further once collective is increased for takeoff.	->	High density altitude reduces air density, which affects both engine performance and rotor efficiency. In a piston helicopter with an engine-driven governor, the governor will attempt to maintain Nr by increasing throttle; however, if the engine has reached its power limit due to high density altitude, it cannot supply sufficient torque to maintain Nr despite governor demand, and Nr will droop. In a turbine helicopter, torque limits may be reached sooner at high DA. The pilot should: (1) verify the Nr reading is still within the published green arc; (2) check the RFM for any density altitude limitations or power charts to determine if available power is sufficient for the intended departure; (3) consider whether the helipad is elevated or confined in a way that demands a steep departure requiring full power; and (4) perform a performance calculation for hover OGE to verify the helicopter can safely depart. If Nr is at the low edge of green only at flat pitch with no load, it may struggle further once collective is increased for takeoff. Regulatory basis: 14 CFR 91.7 (airworthiness) requires the PIC to ensure the aircraft is capable of safe flight; refer to applicable RFM performance charts and FAA-H-8083-21B Chapters 5 and 9.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.A.RM.001	The primary risk is loss of situational awareness. While distracted by the passenger, you could miss a taxiway hold-short sign, cross an active runway without clearance, or fail to see conflicting ground traffic. On the ground, helicopters operate in close proximity to aircraft, vehicles, and personnel — even a brief lapse in attention can result in a collision or incursion. Task prioritization means aviate/navigate first: delay non-essential communication until you've stopped or reached a safe point.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.A.RM.006	This scenario stacks multiple SA-degrading factors: an unfamiliar environment, ambiguous visual cues (faded/similar markings), and high cockpit workload from EFB management and radio monitoring. Loss of situational awareness occurs when the pilot's mental model of where they are and what's around them becomes inaccurate — in this case, the pilot could land on the wrong pad (possibly unauthorized or occupied) or fail to detect another aircraft on CTAF. Risk reduction steps include: (1) thoroughly review the heliport chart, NOTAMs, and satellite imagery before arriving; (2) dedicate EFB management to before departure or after landing — not during hover operations; (3) fly a slow reconnaissance orbit of the facility before committing to a landing; (4) make CTAF position calls early to alert other traffic.	->	This scenario stacks multiple SA-degrading factors: an unfamiliar environment, ambiguous visual cues (faded/similar markings), and high cockpit workload from EFB management and radio monitoring. Loss of situational awareness occurs when the pilot's mental model of where they are and what's around them becomes inaccurate — in this case, the pilot could land on the wrong pad (possibly unauthorized or occupied) or fail to detect another aircraft on CTAF. Risk reduction steps include: (1) thoroughly review the heliport chart, NOTAMs, and satellite imagery before arriving; (2) dedicate EFB management to before departure or after landing — not during hover operations; (3) fly a slow reconnaissance orbit of the facility before committing to a landing; (4) make CTAF position calls early to alert other traffic. **Regulatory reference:** FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 9 and Chapter 12; AIM 4-1-9 (Traffic Advisory Practices at Airports Without Operating Control Towers); 14 CFR 91.126/91.127 (Uncontrolled Airport Operations).
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.A.RM.008	This is an evaluator-induced distraction, a standardized testing technique used to assess the applicant's ability to maintain task prioritization under stress. It reveals whether the applicant has automated their surface operations sufficiently that their attention can be divided, or whether their recognition of signs and markings is still at a 'controlled' cognitive level requiring full attention. An applicant who continues to the hold-short line correctly — or who verbally signals 'one moment' before answering — demonstrates genuine situational awareness. An applicant who crosses the hold-short line while responding to my question reveals that their sign/marking recognition is not yet automated and that they lack the self-awareness to recognize when distraction is overloading them. This is directly relevant to real-world risk because actual distractions (passengers, cockpit alerts, radio calls) do not pause for surface operations.	->	This is an evaluator-induced distraction, a standardized testing technique used to assess the applicant's ability to maintain task prioritization under stress. It reveals whether the applicant has automated their surface operations sufficiently that their attention can be divided, or whether their recognition of signs and markings is still at a 'controlled' cognitive level requiring full attention. An applicant who continues to the hold-short line correctly — or who verbally signals 'one moment' before answering — demonstrates genuine situational awareness. An applicant who crosses the hold-short line while responding to the question reveals that their sign/marking recognition is not yet automated and that they lack the self-awareness to recognize when distraction is overloading them. This is directly relevant to real-world risk because actual distractions (passengers, cockpit alerts, radio calls) do not pause for surface operations. **Regulatory reference:** Current FAA-S-ACS-14 (Private Pilot Helicopter ACS) or latest revision, PH.IV.A Risk Management; 14 CFR 61.43; ACS General Standards. **Follow-up answer:** A runway incursion or unauthorized runway crossing is evaluated as a safety-of-flight failure under ACS General Standards, and disapproval is supported. However, the ACS directs the evaluator to assess whether the applicant 'manages distractions, task prioritization, loss of situational awareness, or disorientation.' Context — including whether the deviation was immediate self-corrected, whether the applicant demonstrated awareness of the error and its implications, and the magnitude of the safety risk created — informs the evaluator's judgment. Crossing a hold-short line without clearance is categorically a significant safety event; a disapproval would be appropriate. The evaluator must document the specific behavior and the risk it created, referencing the appropriate ACS risk management element.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.A.RM.001	A runway incursion is any unauthorized intrusion onto a runway, regardless of whether it creates an immediate collision hazard. It involves an aircraft, vehicle, person, or object on a runway surface without clearance or contrary to ATC instructions. It is considered extremely dangerous because runways are used for high-speed takeoffs and landings, and any unexpected presence on the runway surface can result in a catastrophic collision with little or no time for avoidance.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.A.Comply.002	The 'H' designates the touchdown and liftoff area (TLOF) of a helipad or heliport. It is typically painted white on a yellow background for a surface-level helipad, or white on a contrasting surface for elevated helipads. The 'H' is oriented so that the top of the letter points toward the preferred approach direction or magnetic north, depending on facility design. Its presence confirms that the marked area is intended for helicopter landing and takeoff operations.	->	The 'H' designates the touchdown and liftoff area (TLOF) of a helipad or heliport. It is typically painted white on a yellow background for a surface-level helipad, or white on a contrasting surface for elevated helipads. Per FAA AC 150/5390-2C, the orientation of the 'H' follows facility design specifications, typically referencing magnetic north. Its presence confirms that the marked area is intended for helicopter landing and takeoff operations.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.006	Several in-flight options are available: (1) Tune to 121.5 MHz (guard/emergency frequency) and request the tower frequency from ATC — controllers monitor this frequency; (2) Contact the nearest ARTCC or approach control facility on a known frequency and ask them to relay or provide the tower frequency; (3) Contact FSS at 1-800-WX-BRIEF (if you have the phone patch capability or a portable device) for the frequency; (4) If equipped with an EFB or GPS with aviation database, look up the airport communication data; (5) Squawk 1200 and remain clear of Class D until communications are established. Class D airspace requires two-way radio communication prior to entry per 14 CFR 91.129.	->	Recommended in-flight options: (1) Contact the nearest ARTCC or approach control facility on a known frequency and request the tower frequency; (2) Contact FSS on 122.2 MHz or at 1-800-WX-BRIEF if equipped; (3) If equipped with an EFB or GPS with current aviation database, reference the airport communication data. Most important: 14 CFR 91.129(c) prohibits entry into Class D airspace without establishing two-way radio communication first. Remain clear of Class D airspace and establish contact via approach control or FSS before proceeding into the airport's Class D airspace. Do not rely on 121.5 MHz for routine frequency requests.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.K_COMM.002	A 'readback' means the pilot repeats the clearance or instruction back to the controller verbatim so the controller can confirm it was received correctly. Regardless of whether a controller asks for a readback, all altitude assignments, runway assignments, heading assignments, and transponder codes require a readback. Hold short instructions also always require a readback — this is specifically emphasized in the AIM as a runway incursion prevention measure.	->	A 'readback' means the pilot repeats the clearance or instruction back to the controller verbatim so the controller can confirm it was received correctly. Regardless of whether a controller asks for a readback, all altitude assignments, runway assignments, heading assignments, transponder codes, and hold-short instructions always require a readback. Hold-short instructions are specifically emphasized in AIM 4-3-20 and AC 91-73B as a runway incursion prevention measure.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.ATC.003	The six light gun signals and their meanings for an airborne aircraft are: (1) Steady green — cleared to land; (2) Flashing green — return for landing (not yet cleared); (3) Steady red — give way to other aircraft and continue circling; (4) Flashing red — airport unsafe, do not land; (5) Flashing white — return to starting point on airport (primarily a ground signal, but listed); (6) Alternating red and green — general warning, exercise extreme caution. These are defined in 14 CFR 91.125 and the AIM.	->	The five light gun signals and their meanings for an airborne aircraft are: (1) Steady green — cleared to land; (2) Flashing green — return for landing (not yet cleared); (3) Steady red — give way to other aircraft and continue circling; (4) Flashing red — airport unsafe, do not land; (5) Alternating red and green — general warning, exercise extreme caution. Note: flashing white is a ground signal only ('return to starting point on airport') and has no defined meaning for airborne aircraft per 14 CFR 91.125 and AIM 4-3-13.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.ATC.004	A flashing green while airborne means 'return for landing' — you are not yet cleared. You should acknowledge you understand by rocking the helicopter or flashing your landing light, continue flying the pattern, and wait for a steady green before committing to land. The steady green is your actual landing clearance. You should also watch for a steady red, which would mean give way, or a flashing red, which means the field is unsafe. After landing, you are required to contact ATC as soon as practicable.	->	A flashing green while airborne means 'return for landing' — you are not yet cleared. You should acknowledge you understand by flashing your landing light, continue flying the pattern, and wait for a steady green before committing to land. Ensure your transponder is set to 7600 to squawk the radio-failure code. The steady green is your actual landing clearance. You should also watch for a steady red, which would mean give way, or a flashing red, which means the field is unsafe. After landing, contact ATC as soon as practicable.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.ATC.005	Flashing white directed at an aircraft on the ground means 'return to starting point on airport.' For a helicopter already at a helipad waiting to depart, this is a signal to taxi or hover back to the starting point — essentially, hold your position or return to the ramp. You should acknowledge visually by flashing lights or rocking the helicopter, and comply immediately. Recognize that you are still in Class D airspace and must not depart without a clearance — a flashing white is not a clearance to leave.	->	Flashing white directed at an aircraft on the ground means 'return to starting point on airport.' For a helicopter already at a helipad waiting to depart, this signal directs you to return or move to your designated starting point (typically the ramp or original position) — you must not depart. Acknowledge visually by flashing your landing light, and comply immediately. Recognize that you are still in Class D airspace and must not depart without a clearance; radio failure procedures (squawk 7600) remain in effect.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.ATC.007	Yes — after landing with a radio failure, 14 CFR 91.185 requires you to contact ATC as soon as practicable. In Class D airspace specifically, you were required to establish two-way radio communication before entering. Since that was not possible due to failure, you should land, then contact the tower by phone or in person immediately to report the failure. You are not technically in violation if you made every reasonable effort to communicate and complied with light signals — the regulations have provisions for radio failure operations. However, failing to contact ATC after landing could constitute a violation. You should document the event and may expect a call from the FSDO.	->	Yes — after landing with a radio failure, 14 CFR 91.185 requires you to contact ATC as soon as practicable. In Class D airspace specifically, you were required to establish two-way radio communication before entering under 14 CFR 91.129. Since that was not possible due to failure, you should land, then contact the tower by phone or in person immediately to report the failure. You are not technically in violation if you made every reasonable effort to communicate and complied with light signals — the regulations have provisions for radio failure operations. However, failing to contact ATC after landing could constitute a violation. You should document the event and may expect a call from the FSDO.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.ATC.008	When the tower is closed, the airport operates as uncontrolled and no light gun authority exists — light signals are a tower function. However, if you then fly to or through Class B or Class C airspace, or enter a controlled airport, those controllers can issue light signals. Additionally, military airfields and some federal facilities can issue light signals regardless of normal tower hours if staffed. The practical point is: if the tower is closed, you operate on CTAF using standard uncontrolled airport procedures — light signals do not apply. A pilot should always verify tower operating hours via the Chart Supplement before assuming a tower is active.	->	If the tower has closed, the airport is now uncontrolled regardless of what just happened. There is no light gun authority and no ATC clearance required for departure. You may depart using standard uncontrolled airport procedures — announce on CTAF, check for traffic, and depart when safe. The radio failure is essentially moot because the tower closure means you are now operating under either 14 CFR 91.126 (if the airport is Class G airspace) or 91.127 (if Class E airspace) — check the sectional chart to determine the airspace designation — not 91.125. You are not in violation for departing without a clearance from a closed tower. The key is documenting the timeline — when the tower closed versus when the radio failed — to protect yourself if the events are questioned later.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.Lost.006	Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3), if operating IFR with a lost communication, you begin descent and approach at the destination at the EFC time if one was given — and you have not received a later clearance. If no EFC was given, you begin the approach at your estimated time of arrival based on your filed flight plan and the last known information. The EFC time is specifically intended to protect you in exactly this scenario — it gives you a definitive time to leave a holding fix and begin the approach without radio contact.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3), if operating IFR with a lost communication, the EFC time governs when you should leave the clearance limit fix and commence descent toward the destination. If no EFC was given, you proceed to the destination and begin the approach at your estimated time of arrival (ETA) calculated from your filed flight plan and the last known winds and winds aloft. The EFC time is specifically intended to protect you in exactly this scenario — it gives you a definitive time to leave a holding fix and proceed to the approach without radio contact.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.Lost.008	When cleared to a fix with no published holding pattern and you reach the clearance limit without a further clearance, you should hold on the course on which you approached the fix — meaning fly a standard pattern (right turns unless otherwise specified) on the inbound course that brought you to the fix. Use one-minute legs at or below 14,000 feet MSL. This is consistent with AIM and 14 CFR 91.185 guidance which states that when reaching a clearance limit before receiving a further clearance, the pilot should hold and expect a further clearance in 30 minutes — if one is not received and radio remains out, begin the approach at your EFC time or ETA. The key point for the examiner is that the pilot must construct a holding pattern themselves if none is charted, using the arrival course as the inbound leg.	->	When cleared to a fix with no published holding pattern and you reach the clearance limit without a further clearance, you should hold on the course on which you approached the fix — meaning fly a standard pattern (right turns unless otherwise specified) on the inbound course that brought you to the fix. Use one-minute legs at or below 14,000 feet MSL. Per 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3) and AIM 6-4-1, you must hold and begin the approach at your EFC time if one was given, or at the ETA based on your filed flight plan if no EFC was assigned. The key point is that you must construct a holding pattern yourself if none is charted, using the arrival course as the inbound leg.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.Equipment.002	Civilian ATC communication uses the VHF band from 118.000 to 136.975 MHz. If a pilot tunes a frequency outside this range — for example, inadvertently selecting a military UHF frequency or an aviation navigation frequency — ATC cannot receive the transmission because ATC ground equipment only monitors and responds within the 118–136.975 MHz window. A radio that is not capable of 25 kHz channel spacing may also be unable to access all assigned frequencies, effectively blocking communication on certain channels.	->	Civilian ATC communication in the US uses the VHF band from 118.000 to 136.975 MHz with 25 kHz channel spacing. If a pilot tunes a frequency outside this range — for example, inadvertently selecting a military UHF frequency or an aviation navigation frequency — ATC cannot receive the transmission because ATC ground equipment only monitors and responds within the 118–136.975 MHz window. Additionally, a radio equipped with only 50 kHz channel spacing cannot access all assigned frequencies in the modern 25 kHz-spaced ATC band, effectively blocking communication on certain channels.
