AUTO_APPROVE	PH.IV.B.007	The main answer correctly distinguishes NTSB reporting, FAA Safety Hotline, and ASRS with accurate scope, immunity limits, and regulatory references; the AC version currency flag is a minor maintenance note, not a content error.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.002	The two main components are Runway Entrance Lights (RELs) and Takeoff Hold Lights (THLs). RELs are red in-pavement lights located at taxiway/runway intersections and illuminate to warn pilots or vehicle operators that it is unsafe to enter or cross the runway. THLs are red in-pavement lights located along the runway centerline in the takeoff roll area, typically between 1,000 and 3,000 feet from the departure threshold, and illuminate to warn a departing aircraft that it is unsafe to begin or continue a takeoff roll.	->	The Runway Status Light system consists of three types of in-pavement red lights: (1) Runway Entrance Lights (RELs), located at taxiway/runway intersections, which illuminate to warn pilots or vehicle operators that it is unsafe to enter or cross the runway; (2) Takeoff Hold Lights (THLs), located along the runway centerline in the takeoff roll area and illuminating to warn a departing aircraft that it is unsafe to begin or continue a takeoff roll; and (3) Runway Intersection Lights (RILs), located at runway-to-runway intersections, which illuminate to warn of conflicting traffic on the crossing runway. Not all airports have all three components; installation depends on airport configuration and traffic patterns.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.RM1.007	This reasoning reflects the 'macho' and 'anti-authority' hazardous attitudes and is fundamentally dangerous. First, the regulatory protection under 14 CFR 91.3 exists precisely for this situation — a pilot who deviates from FAA regulations in an emergency is protected as long as they submit a report if requested. Fear of paperwork should never outweigh safety. Second, ATC is not adversarial — declaring an emergency activates airport emergency services, clears airspace, and can direct the pilot to the nearest suitable landing area. Withholding that resource actively reduces the margin of safety. In a helicopter specifically, this attitude is especially hazardous because helicopter emergencies (engine failure, LTE, hydraulic failure) can transition from manageable to catastrophic in seconds. The window for effective communication and assistance is narrow. A pilot who delays declaring because of ego is applying the same cognitive error the Helicopter Flying Handbook identifies as a key link in the accident chain — believing the situation will 'probably' work out. The FAA has consistently reinforced that declaring an emergency is a sign of good airmanship, not a failure.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.3(c), if the emergency required a deviation from an ATC clearance or an FAA regulation, the pilot must submit a written report to the nearest FAA Flight Standards District Office (FSDO) as soon as practicable, if requested by ATC. The report should include the nature of the emergency, the deviation taken, and the outcome. This is not automatic enforcement — it is an informational report, and the FAA's posture is generally to ensure safety was the driver of the decision. Voluntary disclosure and good faith communication with ATC typically result in no enforcement action. A separate NASA ASRS report filed within 10 days may provide additional limited enforcement protection for unintentional violations.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.RM1.008	This is a dual failure — loss of communication and loss of transponder — inside Class C airspace, which requires both two-way radio communication and an operating transponder. The risk management decisions are as follows: (1) Confirm the failure is real — attempt backup radios, cell phone if below 18,000 ft, or interplane frequencies; (2) Squawk 7600 (lost communications) if the transponder is functional; if transponder is also failed, ATC has no radar return and is unaware of the aircraft's position — this is now an emergency by most definitions; (3) The situation requires immediate action because IMC flight in Class C without communication or a transponder presents a mid-air collision risk and makes ATC unable to sequence traffic; (4) The pilot should navigate toward the nearest VFR conditions or airport using known procedures, maintain altitude awareness, and if visual contact with the ground is established, proceed to the nearest suitable landing area; (5) Yes — this constitutes an emergency. Loss of all communication and transponder in IMC in controlled airspace meets the definition of distress — the pilot cannot comply with ATC requirements and the aircraft is in a situation that could endanger life. Upon landing, the pilot should immediately notify ATC or FSS in person or by phone and explain the situation. A written report may be required under 14 CFR 91.3(c) since FARs were necessarily deviated from.	->	Correct the regulatory references from '14 CFR 91.135' to '14 CFR 91.130 (Class C communication requirements) and 91.215 (transponder requirements).' In the answer, reframe the cell phone suggestion as: 'attempt backup radios, or if equipped and below 18,000 ft, a cell phone as an informal option (not a regulatory procedure).' The remainder of the answer—dual failure classification, 7600 squawk attempt, emergency declaration rationale, and post-landing reporting—is sound and may remain unchanged.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.RM.002	1. Non-standard: 'For landing' (as in 'Helicopter November 123 Alpha, for landing') — Correct alternative: State your position and intentions clearly, e.g., 'Helicopter N123A, two miles northeast, inbound for landing at the helipad.' 2. Non-standard: 'Roger that' used as an acknowledgment of a clearance — Correct alternative: 'Roger' is acceptable for acknowledgment of information, but clearances must be read back verbatim, not simply acknowledged with 'Roger.' Using 'Roger' alone for a clearance creates risk that the controller does not know if the pilot understood correctly.	->	Replace the first example with a clearer non-standard term: '1. Non-standard: "I\'m coming in" or "off Runway 27" — Correct alternative: Use standard position reports (e.g., "two miles northeast") and standard phraseology such as "departing Runway 27" per AIM 4-2-1.' Keep the second example as stated, but expand the regulatory references to include 'AIM 4-4-7 (readback of clearances)' for precision. Alternatively, add a third example of a documented non-standard term such as "with you" for an initial contact check-in, which is not recommended per AIM 4-2-3.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.RM.007	This is a textbook example of normalization of deviance — a culture where non-standard practices become accepted because they have not yet caused an accident. The risks include: (1) Substitute or relief controllers who do not know the local shorthand will misinterpret the call, potentially causing a conflict or delayed response. (2) The crew may operate into a different facility expecting the same understanding. (3) Non-standard communication erodes the broader safety culture by creating precedent for further deviation. (4) If an incident occurs, the crew bears regulatory liability for not using approved phraseology per AIM standards. Standardized phraseology exists precisely so that any qualified pilot or controller — not just familiar ones — can communicate safely. The solution is to use approved priority/emergency language: declare an emergency per AIM 6-3-1 or request priority handling using standard terms, which achieves the same operational result without non-standard phrasing.	->	In the follow-up answer, remove 'minimum fuel' as an option for requesting priority handling for medical flights. Replace with: 'The correct procedure is to request priority handling directly using standard phraseology: "[Facility], [Callsign], request priority handling, medical flight." Per AIM 6-3-1 and standard practice, ATC will provide priority assistance when safety of flight requires it. "Minimum fuel" (AIM 5-5-15) is a separate and specific declaration indicating fuel state concerns and is not appropriate for routine medical priority requests. Neither option requires non-standard phraseology.' Update regulatory references to cite AIM 6-3-1 (emergency and priority procedures) in place of AIM 4-2-6 for precision.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.Transmit.002	Per the AIM, pilots must read back any ATC clearance or instruction that contains altitude assignments, heading assignments, altimeter settings, runway assignments, taxi instructions involving hold-short points, and especially any clearance to enter, cross, or hold on a runway. The readback confirms to the controller that you received the correct information and allows them to catch and correct any errors before a potential incident occurs.	->	Per the AIM, pilots must read back any ATC clearance or instruction that contains altitude assignments, heading assignments, altimeter settings, transponder code assignments, runway assignments, taxi instructions involving hold-short points, and especially any clearance to enter, cross, or hold on a runway. The readback confirms to the controller that you received the correct information and allows them to catch and correct any errors before a potential incident occurs.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.S_ACK.006	First, verify you dialed in 124.4 correctly and that your radio is functioning. If still no contact after at least two attempts, return to the previous frequency and advise the prior controller: '4-5 Romeo, unable to reach Approach on 124.4, requesting assistance.' Do not proceed into controlled airspace without re-establishing communication. If radio failure is confirmed, follow the NORDO lost communication procedures in 14 CFR 91.185 if IFR, or use transponder code 7600 and light gun signal procedures if applicable at a towered field. For VFR flight, squawk 7600, look for light gun signals, and attempt contact on guard (121.5 MHz).	->	First, verify you dialed in 124.4 correctly and that your radio is functioning. If still no contact after at least two attempts, return to the previous frequency and advise the prior controller: '4-5 Romeo, unable to reach Approach on 124.4, requesting assistance.' For VFR flight, radio failure does not prevent flight under 14 CFR 91.126 or 91.129 depending on airspace class. If at a towered airport, squawk 7600 and watch for light gun signals (AIM 4-2-13). Attempt contact on guard (121.5 MHz). For IFR flight, 14 CFR 91.185 establishes specific lost-communications procedures regarding altitude, route, and holding. Do not proceed into controlled airspace where communications are mandatory without re-establishing contact with ATC.
ESCALATE	PH.IV.C.003	Critical metadata error: question ID 'PH.IV.C.003' is duplicated in this batch, creating ambiguity about which item is which. Additionally, the ACS code field lists 'Traffic' but the verifier notes inconsistency with other items. Unique ID assignment and code verification required before approval.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.004	You should join the traffic pattern for runway 27 to conform to the flow of traffic already established, even though calm wind conditions technically allow any runway choice. 14 CFR 91.113 requires pilots to give way and see-and-avoid, and AIM guidance stresses yielding to established traffic flow for safety. Attempting to land on a conflicting runway in calm winds when another aircraft is established in the pattern creates unnecessary conflict. You should monitor the CTAF, announce your intentions, and integrate with the existing runway 27 pattern. Once established, fly standard left traffic unless right traffic is indicated for that runway.	->	You should join the traffic pattern for runway 27 to conform to the flow of traffic already established, even though calm wind conditions technically allow any runway choice. 14 CFR 91.113 requires pilots to give way and see-and-avoid, and AIM guidance stresses yielding to established traffic flow for safety. Attempting to land on a conflicting runway in calm winds when another aircraft is established in the pattern creates unnecessary conflict. You should monitor the CTAF, announce your intentions, and integrate with the existing runway 27 pattern. Once established, fly standard left traffic unless right traffic is indicated for that runway. [Assign unique ID: e.g., PH.IV.C.010; confirm ACS code as 'Traffic']
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.005	Helicopters typically fly traffic patterns at 500 to 700 feet AGL, which is lower than the standard fixed-wing pattern altitude of 1,000 feet AGL. The appropriate altitude is based on the helicopter's POH/RFM guidance, local airport procedures, and ATC instructions at towered airports. Factors requiring altitude adjustment include: (1) avoiding conflicts with fixed-wing traffic at higher pattern altitudes; (2) obstacle clearance requirements around the airport environment; (3) noise abatement procedures; (4) visibility conditions — lower ceilings may compress the pattern vertically; and (5) wind — a strong headwind on final can compress the pattern horizontally, requiring adjustments in timing and descent rates. At towered airports, always comply with ATC-assigned altitudes.	->	Helicopters typically fly traffic patterns at 500 to 700 feet AGL, which is lower than the standard fixed-wing pattern altitude of 1,000 feet AGL. The appropriate altitude is based on the helicopter's POH/RFM guidance, local airport procedures, and ATC instructions at towered airports. Factors requiring altitude adjustment include: (1) avoiding conflicts with fixed-wing traffic at higher pattern altitudes; (2) obstacle clearance requirements around the airport environment; (3) noise abatement procedures; (4) visibility conditions — lower ceilings may compress the pattern vertically; and (5) wind — a strong headwind on final can compress the pattern horizontally, requiring adjustments in timing and descent rates. At towered airports, always comply with ATC-assigned altitudes. [Assign unique ID: e.g., PH.IV.C.011; confirm ACS code as 'Traffic']
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.Right-of-way.004	This is a nuanced situation. Under 14 CFR 91.113(g), an aircraft on final approach does have right-of-way over aircraft in flight. However, FAA guidance in the Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM) strongly discourages straight-in approaches at uncontrolled fields when other aircraft are in the established pattern, because they disrupt the orderly flow and can create conflicts. The airplane on final technically holds right-of-way under the regulation, but best practice requires the helicopter pilot to be vigilant, extend the crosswind or turn early if needed to sequence safely, and yield as necessary. The key point is that right-of-way rules do not relieve any pilot of the responsibility to take all possible action to avoid a collision under 14 CFR 91.113(a).	->	This is a nuanced situation. Under 14 CFR 91.113(g), an aircraft on final approach does have right-of-way over aircraft in flight. However, FAA guidance in the Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM 4-3-3 and 4-3-4) strongly discourages straight-in approaches at uncontrolled fields when other aircraft are in the established pattern, because they disrupt the orderly flow and can create conflicts. The airplane on final technically holds right-of-way under the regulation, but best practice requires the helicopter pilot to be vigilant, extend the crosswind or turn early if needed to sequence safely, and yield as necessary. If extending the crosswind leg, continue scanning to ensure no new conflicts are created. The key point is that right-of-way rules do not relieve any pilot of the responsibility to take all possible action to avoid a collision under 14 CFR 91.113(a).
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.Right-of-way.006	Right-of-way rules apply regardless of ATC clearances. The aircraft to your right (the one approaching from your left is approaching from their right, meaning you are to their right) — you must analyze the geometry: if the other aircraft is to your left, you are to their right, meaning you have the right-of-way under the converging rule in 14 CFR 91.113(d). However, 91.113(a) is paramount: regardless of who has right-of-way, you must take all possible action to avoid a collision. The correct response is to immediately maneuver as needed to avoid the conflict, then notify ATC of the traffic and your deviation. An ATC clearance does not supersede your responsibility to see and avoid.	->	If the other aircraft is approaching from your left, you are on their right side. Under 14 CFR 91.113(d), the aircraft on the right has right-of-way, so you have the right-of-way and they must yield. However, 14 CFR 91.113(a) is paramount: regardless of who has right-of-way, you must take all possible action to avoid a collision. If the other pilot fails to yield, maneuver immediately to avoid the conflict. After ensuring separation, notify ATC of the traffic and your deviation. An ATC clearance does not supersede your responsibility to see and avoid.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.001	ASOS stands for Automated Surface Observing System. It provides continuous, real-time surface weather observations including wind direction and speed, temperature, dewpoint, altimeter setting, visibility, sky condition, and present weather (precipitation type). Pilots use this information before entering a traffic pattern to assess crosswind components, visibility, ceiling, and density altitude. ASOS broadcasts are available on a published frequency or by phone.	->	ASOS stands for Automated Surface Observing System. It provides continuous, real-time surface weather observations including wind direction and speed, temperature, dewpoint, altimeter setting, visibility, sky condition, and present weather (including precipitation, fog, haze, and other visibility-reducing phenomena). Pilots use this information before entering a traffic pattern to assess crosswind components, visibility, ceiling, and density altitude. ASOS broadcasts are available on a published frequency or by phone.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.002	AWOS stands for Automated Weather Observing System. Like ASOS, it provides automated surface weather data including wind, temperature, dewpoint, altimeter, and visibility. The key difference is that AWOS is typically owned and operated by the airport or state authority and may report fewer weather elements depending on its tier (AWOS-1 through AWOS-3P+). ASOS is a National Weather Service/FAA program with standardized sensors and broader reporting capability. Both broadcast on a published frequency, but AWOS data may be less complete. Pilots should know which system is installed at their destination to understand data limitations.	->	AWOS stands for Automated Weather Observing System. Like ASOS, it provides automated surface weather data including wind, temperature, dewpoint, altimeter, and visibility. The key difference is that AWOS is typically locally owned and operated (by airports, states, or the FAA) and may report fewer weather elements depending on its tier (AWOS-1 through AWOS-3P+). ASOS is a National Weather Service/FAA program with standardized sensors and broader reporting capability; importantly, ASOS at staffed locations may include human-augmented observations (e.g., remarks on phenomena not detected by sensors), which AWOS systems cannot provide. Both broadcast on a published frequency, but AWOS data may be less complete. Pilots should know which system is installed at their destination to understand data limitations.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.003	CTAF (Common Traffic Advisory Frequency) is the frequency designated for air-to-air communication between pilots operating at or near a non-towered airport. Pilots self-announce their position, altitude, and intentions on CTAF. Many non-towered airports also broadcast ASOS or AWOS on a separate published frequency or on the CTAF itself. Pilots should monitor ASOS/AWOS to determine current winds, altimeter setting, and visibility, then use CTAF to announce their pattern entry, position in pattern, and departure intentions. Combining both sources gives a complete picture of conditions and known traffic.	->	CTAF (Common Traffic Advisory Frequency) is the frequency designated for air-to-air communication between pilots operating at or near a non-towered airport. Pilots self-announce their position, altitude, and intentions on CTAF. Many non-towered airports broadcast ASOS or AWOS either on the CTAF itself or on a separate published frequency (check Chart Supplement for the specific airport). Pilots should monitor ASOS/AWOS to determine current winds, altimeter setting, and visibility, then use CTAF to announce their pattern entry, position in pattern, and departure intentions. Combining both sources—when available—gives a complete picture of conditions and known traffic. Note that not all non-towered airports have automated weather available.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.004	Remote ASOS data from an adjacent airport can be used as a general reference for wind direction, altimeter setting, and overall weather trends, but it has significant limitations when applied to a heliport 12 miles away. Terrain, elevation differences, local obstructions, and microclimatic effects can cause winds and conditions at the heliport to differ substantially from the reporting station. The altimeter setting can be applied if the elevation difference is small, but wind direction and speed may vary. Pilots should treat the remote data as a planning tool only — once in the vicinity of the heliport, visual cues such as windsocks, smoke, trees, and water surface should be used to confirm actual surface winds before committing to a final approach direction.	->	Remote ASOS data from an adjacent airport can be used as a general reference for altimeter setting and overall weather trends, but it has significant limitations when applied to a heliport 12 miles away. The altimeter setting is generally valid across a 12-mile area unless significant elevation differences exist. However, wind direction and speed may vary substantially due to terrain, elevation, local obstructions, and microclimatic effects. Treat the remote ASOS wind data as planning information only—once in the vicinity of the heliport, visually confirm actual surface winds using windsocks, smoke, trees, water surface, and other visual cues before committing to a final approach direction.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.005	No, helicopter pilots are not required to operate on the active runway. Helicopters may operate in areas and on routes not available to fixed-wing aircraft, and the tower may assign or approve alternative routes or landing areas. However, before deviating from the active runway designation, you must communicate with ATC and obtain a clearance or approval. The ATIS gives you the altimeter setting (required to be set before flight in controlled airspace), active runway, and known hazards. If a helipad or alternative route would be more efficient or operationally beneficial, you request it from the tower. The automated ATIS information is a starting point for your planning — altimeter, NOTAMs, and active runway — but helicopter operations retain the flexibility to deviate with ATC coordination.	->	No, helicopter pilots are not required to operate on the active runway. Helicopters may operate in areas and on routes not available to fixed-wing aircraft; however, deviations from the active runway or published procedures require ATC coordination and explicit approval at a towered airport. Request an alternative landing area or route only when operationally justified (e.g., nearby helipad is more efficient). The ATIS provides critical information: altimeter setting (required to be set before flight in controlled airspace), active runway, and known hazards—use this to plan your initial approach. Once in contact with ATC, coordinate any deviations from standard procedures rather than assuming helicopter flexibility overrides active runway designations.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.RM.002	The aircraft at the lower altitude has the right of way, but that aircraft may not take advantage of this rule to cut in front of or overtake another aircraft on final. Additionally, an aircraft in distress has right of way over all others. In practice, the helicopter pilot should monitor the pattern closely and communicate intentions on CTAF to avoid conflict, since helicopters are often faster to maneuver but slower in pattern airspeed than some fixed-wing aircraft.	->	Per 14 CFR 91.113(e), when two aircraft are on final approach, the aircraft at lower altitude has priority, but neither aircraft may take advantage of this rule to cut in front of or overtake another aircraft. In practice, the aircraft that established on final first generally has the approach and landing sequence priority. An aircraft in distress has right-of-way over all others. Helicopter pilots should monitor the pattern closely, communicate intentions on CTAF, and recognize that while helicopters can maneuver more readily, maintaining appropriate spacing and not forcing a go-around on traffic already established is essential to safe operations.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.RM.001	Task saturation occurs when the number of simultaneous tasks exceeds the pilot's mental capacity to handle them effectively. In the traffic pattern approach phase, the pilot must simultaneously manage airspeed, altitude, descent rate, power, radio communications, traffic scanning, and wind correction. When these tasks pile up, critical items get dropped or delayed — most commonly scanning for conflicting traffic or monitoring rotor RPM — which can lead to loss of situational awareness or controlled flight into terrain near the landing area.	->	Task saturation occurs when the number of simultaneous tasks exceeds the pilot's mental capacity to handle them effectively. In the traffic pattern approach phase, the pilot must simultaneously manage airspeed, altitude, descent rate, power, radio communications, traffic scanning, and wind correction. When these tasks pile up, critical items get dropped or delayed — most commonly scanning for conflicting traffic or proper power management — which can lead to loss of situational awareness, unstable approaches, or controlled flight into terrain near the landing area. In piston helicopters without a governor, rotor RPM monitoring is also a frequent casualty of task overload.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.RM.004	Task priority at that moment must be: (1) Fly the helicopter first — maintain altitude, airspeed, and configuration control on base; (2) Scan for conflicting traffic, especially on final; (3) Monitor rotor RPM and power; and only then (4) acknowledge the radio call briefly or not at all until established on final. Responding to the radio call before stabilizing the approach creates the risk of flying below the target altitude, overshooting to final, or missing a conflicting aircraft already established on final. In a helicopter, the base-to-final turn is also a high-workload phase where inattention can result in a low, slow, turning flight condition — a setup for vortex ring state or loss of effective translational lift if airspeed bleeds off.	->	Task priority at that moment must be: (1) Fly the helicopter first — maintain altitude, airspeed, and configuration control on base; (2) Scan for conflicting traffic, especially on final; (3) Monitor power and engine parameters; and only then (4) acknowledge the radio call briefly or not at all until established on final. Responding to the radio call before stabilizing the approach creates the risk of flying below the target altitude, overshooting onto final, or missing a conflicting aircraft already established on final. In a helicopter, the base-to-final turn is also a high-workload phase where inattention can result in a low, slow, turning flight condition — increasing the risk of loss of effective translational lift and reduced tail rotor authority, which can lead to loss of control.
AUTO_APPROVE	PH.IV.C.RM.008	The 80% human factors statistic is widely cited in FAA materials (FAA-H-8083-15B, FAA-H-8083-21B) with variations (70–90%), the main answer is substantively sound and consistent with ADM doctrine, and the follow-up and follow-up answer correctly apply pre-briefed decision rules to mitigate decision-making under stress—a core ADM principle. The verifier's concerns about source precision are valid but do not invalidate the question or answer.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.Comply.007	First, I immediately respond on CTAF with my exact position, altitude (500 feet AGL), and aircraft type so the fixed-wing pilot can build situational awareness. I actively scan for the fixed-wing aircraft above and ahead of me. Because I am 500 feet below him on parallel downwind, a vertical conflict is unlikely on downwind itself, but I need to be vigilant on base and final where our flight paths may converge. I extend my downwind if necessary to sequence myself behind the fixed-wing traffic, making additional CTAF calls to keep both pilots mutually aware. I do not attempt to land simultaneously with the fixed-wing aircraft. Once I confirm the fixed-wing has turned base and is clearly ahead of and above me, I turn my base and set up for final, announcing each leg. On final, I aim for the helipad rather than the runway centerline to maximize lateral separation. I continue scanning until the fixed-wing is clear of the runway, and I do not commit to landing until I am certain there is no conflict. Throughout the entire sequence, I am managing airspeed, power, and altitude to maintain a stabilized approach and be prepared to go around if the situation becomes unsafe.	->	I would immediately apply full power and transition to a climb attitude, simultaneously increasing collective and adjusting cyclic to establish a positive rate of climb. For a helicopter on short final that is below ETL, I must accept that the initial climb may be sluggish until ETL is regained — so I would fly through the ETL speed range rather than try to immediately climb over the obstacle. I would apply collective promptly but smoothly to avoid exceeding torque or rotor RPM limits per the aircraft RFM. I would fly a track that takes me clear of the runway and the fixed-wing aircraft, making a CTAF call announcing the go-around. I would not make any abrupt turns at low altitude with low airspeed, as that would risk loss of tail rotor effectiveness (LTE) or dynamic rollover. Once safely established in a climb and clear of all traffic, I would re-enter the traffic pattern and communicate my intentions.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.Comply.008	With calm winds, there is no preferred runway or direction based on wind. I would first check the Chart Supplement or any NOTAMs for the heliport for published traffic pattern direction. If none is published, I would monitor the CTAF and observe what direction other traffic is using, then conform to that established flow. If I am the first aircraft, I would select a direction consistent with the local environment — for example, avoiding noise-sensitive areas, obstacles, or terrain — and announce my intentions on CTAF. Per 14 CFR 91.126 and 91.127, at uncontrolled airports each pilot is responsible for avoiding other aircraft and conforming to or avoiding the traffic pattern in use. If other aircraft are already established in a right-hand pattern even without a formal designation, I should join that pattern rather than fly opposite traffic, because doing so creates a head-on conflict risk. The principle is that safety and coordination with traffic in the pattern take precedence over personal preference.	->	If no CTAF is published, I would use the multicom frequency of 122.9 MHz, which is the designated frequency for air-to-air communications and self-announce at airports without an operating control tower, FSS, or UNICOM. Regarding regulatory obligation, 14 CFR 91.126 requires that each pilot comply with traffic pattern procedures and avoid other aircraft, but it does not explicitly mandate radio calls at non-towered airports for VFR operations. However, the AIM strongly recommends position calls on 122.9 MHz as best practice to support the see-and-avoid principle and situational awareness. No radio is legally required for VFR flight in uncontrolled airspace, but using 122.9 MHz when no other frequency is designated is highly recommended for safety.
AUTO_APPROVE	PH.IV.C.S-maintain.003	The answer correctly identifies left-hand traffic pattern as standard, references segmented circle indicators, provides a properly formatted CTAF self-announce example with airport name at beginning and end, and cites correct regulatory references; the verifier's suggestion to add tetrahedron/wind cone is minor and the omission does not constitute a material error.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.S-maintain.004	The aircraft on final approach has the right of way. Even if you believe you will arrive first, you must yield. Per 14 CFR 91.113(g), when two aircraft are approaching to land, the aircraft at the lower altitude has the right-of-way — but this does not apply if the lower aircraft is cutting in front of or overtaking the other. Since the Cessna is established on final and at a lower or comparable altitude, you should extend your base leg, widen your pattern, or execute an S-turn to create adequate spacing before turning final. Announce your intentions on CTAF and never cut off a fixed-wing aircraft on final.	->	Per 14 CFR 91.113(g), when two aircraft are approaching an airport to land, the aircraft at the lower altitude has the right-of-way. In this scenario, the Cessna on final is typically at a lower altitude than your helicopter on base, giving it priority. You must yield by extending your base leg, widening your pattern, or executing an S-turn to create adequate spacing before turning final. Announce your intentions on CTAF. The rule explicitly prohibits cutting in front of or overtaking another aircraft on approach. Never compromise safety by forcing a landing sequence.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.S-maintain.007	Even though ATC issued the sequence, the pilot in command is ultimately responsible for maintaining safe separation from other traffic. ATC clearances do not transfer responsibility for collision avoidance to the controller. You must immediately notify the tower that you are overtaking the Baron and cannot maintain the sequencing as issued — for example, 'Tower, helicopter 123AB, unable to maintain separation from Baron on final, requesting extended spacing.' Slow the helicopter, widen your base leg, or accept a 360-degree turn as instructed by ATC. Never allow wake turbulence from a heavier, slower aircraft ahead to become a factor by cutting your spacing. The key is early communication with ATC before the situation becomes critical.	->	Helicopters are particularly vulnerable to wake turbulence from heavier aircraft because the rotor system — operating near its aerodynamic limits during slow flight and approach — can be destabilized by vortices more readily than fixed-wing aircraft. Wake vortices from landing aircraft sink and drift toward the downwind side of the runway, settling at low altitude where helicopters on final would be. A helicopter encountering wake turbulence during a slow approach may experience sudden loss of lift, uncommanded rolling or yawing, or increased vortex ring state susceptibility. The Beechcraft Baron is a 'small' aircraft (MTOW ~5,500 lbs), though wake turbulence from any preceding aircraft remains a hazard. For VFR operations, remain above the flight path of the preceding aircraft and land beyond its touchdown point to avoid settled vortices, regardless of aircraft category. IFR ATC separation minima (3 nm behind large, 4-6 nm behind heavy/super) apply to controlled operations but VFR pilots rely on visual avoidance.
AI_EDIT	PH.IV.C.S-maintain.008	Situational awareness in degraded visual conditions requires layering multiple information sources. First, use CTAF radio calls as your primary tool — make frequent, detailed position reports yourself and listen carefully to build a mental picture of all traffic. Second, use any available traffic advisory technology such as ADS-B In (if equipped) to track other aircraft positions on a display. Third, adjust your pattern geometry — fly a wider, more deliberate pattern with extended legs to increase time and distance buffers, reducing the probability of a conflict. Fourth, reduce pattern workload by slowing down and stabilizing earlier on each leg. Consider delaying pattern entry until the sun angle changes, departing from the pattern temporarily, or coordinating with other traffic on CTAF to sequence appropriately. If you genuinely cannot maintain visual contact with known traffic, it is appropriate to hold clear of the pattern until the situation improves — getting behind schedule is never worth a collision.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	PH.V.A.Effect.005	A consistent left lateral drift requiring right cyclic correction in calm conditions suggests a right-to-left lateral CG displacement — meaning the CG is offset to the left of the rotor mast centerline. This shifts the weight vector away from the center of lift, causing the helicopter to tilt toward the heavier side. To verify, review the weight and balance calculation in the flight manual or weight and balance sheet, paying particular attention to lateral loading — seat positions, cargo placement, or an asymmetric fuel load. On some helicopter models, the CG range is only defined longitudinally; however, the manufacturer's loading instructions account for lateral balance. Any significant lateral imbalance should be corrected by redistributing load before flight.	->	A consistent left lateral drift requiring right cyclic correction in calm conditions suggests a lateral CG displacement toward the left side of the aircraft — the weight is heavier on the left, causing the helicopter to tilt left toward the heavier side. This shift in weight distribution away from the rotor mast centerline requires right cyclic input to maintain level attitude. To verify, review the weight and balance calculation in the flight manual or weight and balance sheet, paying particular attention to lateral loading — seat positions, cargo placement, or an asymmetric fuel load. On some helicopter models, the CG range is only defined longitudinally; however, the manufacturer's loading instructions account for lateral balance. Any significant lateral imbalance should be corrected by redistributing load before flight.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.A.Effect.008	Three possible explanations: 1) A loading error in the weight and balance calculation — an occupant, fuel, or cargo weight was entered incorrectly or a lateral moment arm was wrong. Rule out by re-verifying actual weights and lateral stations against the W&B sheet with the aircraft on the ground before continuing flight. 2) A mechanical or rigging issue — cyclic rigging, swashplate leveling, or rotor track and balance may be off, causing an inherent tilt in the rotor disk at flat pitch. Rule out by checking aircraft maintenance records for recent rigging work and having a certificated A&P mechanic inspect the flight control system before further flight. 3) A previously undetected structural change — damage to a skid, gear, or fuselage that has altered the aircraft's actual empty weight CG without updating the aircraft's weight and balance records. Rule out by reviewing the aircraft's current weight and balance record (required to be kept current per 14 CFR 91.9 and Part 43 requirements) and comparing it to known baseline data. In all three cases, if the root cause cannot be identified and corrected on the ground, the safe decision is to not continue the flight.	->	Three possible explanations: 1) A loading error in the weight and balance calculation — an occupant, fuel, or cargo weight was entered incorrectly or a lateral moment arm was wrong. Rule out by re-verifying actual weights and lateral stations against the W&B sheet with the aircraft on the ground before continuing flight. 2) A mechanical or rigging issue — cyclic rigging, swashplate leveling, or rotor track and balance may be off, causing an inherent tilt in the rotor disk at flat pitch. Rule out by checking aircraft maintenance records for recent rigging work and having a certificated A&P mechanic inspect the flight control system before further flight. 3) A previously undetected structural change — damage to a skid, gear, or fuselage that has altered the aircraft's actual empty weight CG without updating the aircraft's weight and balance records. Rule out by reviewing the aircraft's current weight and balance record (required to be kept current per 14 CFR 43.9 and 91.7) and comparing it to known baseline data. In all three cases, if the root cause cannot be identified and corrected on the ground, the safe decision is to not continue the flight.
