AI_EDIT	PH.V.A.Ground.006	Ground effect quality depends on how effectively the surface reflects and restricts rotor downwash outflow. A hard, flat, non-porous surface such as a concrete pad or calm water provides the most efficient ground effect because it reflects the downwash uniformly. Dense grass will partially absorb and scatter downwash, slightly reducing ground effect compared to a hard surface. A porous surface like loose gravel or deep snow absorbs downwash rather than reflecting it, which can noticeably degrade ground effect and cause the helicopter to require more power than expected at the same hover height over a hard surface. Pilots should not assume maximum IGE benefit when operating over absorptive or uneven terrain.	->	Ground effect quality depends on how effectively the surface reflects and restricts rotor downwash outflow. A hard, flat, non-porous surface such as a concrete pad provides the most efficient ground effect because it reflects the downwash uniformly. Calm, smooth water can act as a reflective surface and provide good ground effect, but rotor downwash may disturb the water surface during extended hover, potentially reducing ground effect quality compared to a solid surface. Dense grass will partially scatter downwash, slightly reducing ground effect compared to a hard surface. A porous surface like loose gravel or deep snow allows downwash air to dissipate through the surface rather than being reflected back, reducing the restriction on outward airflow and noticeably degrading ground effect compared to a hard surface. Pilots should not assume maximum IGE benefit when operating over absorptive or uneven terrain.
