eading); (3) treat hold-short bars as mandatory stop points to verify position before proceeding; (4) use the IMSAFE and PAVE checklists before the flight to assess workload and fatigue, which amplify confirmation bias; (5) apply the SRM principle of identifying all available resources including ATC for position verification before acting on an assumption. Human factors contribute to a significant majority of accidents; confirmation bias during surface operations is a documented contributor. The immediate follow-up action sequence in case of a potential incursion is: (1) Stop immediately in place; (2) Scan the full runway environment for conflicting traffic in both directions; (3) If traffic is imminent, exit the runway via the most direct path and stop clear; (4) Contact ATC immediately and report your position in plain language; (5) Follow ATC instructions; (6) After the flight, consider filing a NASA ASRS report — a voluntary, confidential resource that provides legal protection and contributes to aviation safety data.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.RM.001	The H/V diagram, sometimes called the 'dead man's curve,' is a chart published in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual that defines combinations of altitude (height AGL) and airspeed where a safe autorotation landing cannot be guaranteed following a sudden engine failure. Operating within the shaded regions of the diagram means that if the engine fails, there may be insufficient altitude to build rotor RPM or insufficient airspeed to perform a successful autorotation and landing. Pilots should avoid operating within these regions whenever practical.	->	The H/V diagram, sometimes called the 'dead man's curve,' is a chart published in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual that defines combinations of altitude (height AGL) and airspeed where a safe autorotation landing cannot be guaranteed following a sudden engine failure. Operating within the shaded regions of the diagram means that if the engine fails, there may be insufficient altitude to build rotor RPM or insufficient airspeed to perform a successful autorotation and landing. Pilots should avoid operating within these regions whenever practical.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.RM.002	The two danger regions are: (1) The high-altitude, low-airspeed region — if the engine fails here, the helicopter is too high and too slow to build sufficient airspeed and glide distance for a safe landing. Rotor RPM may decay before ground contact can be controlled. (2) The low-altitude, low-airspeed region (often called the 'low hover' region) — the helicopter is close to the ground but without enough altitude for the pilot to arrest the descent during autorotation before impact. Both regions leave the pilot with insufficient energy (either rotor RPM or altitude/airspeed) to execute a survivable autorotation.	->	The two danger regions are: (1) The high-altitude, low-airspeed region — if the engine fails here, the helicopter is too high and too slow to build sufficient airspeed and glide distance for a safe landing. Rotor RPM may decay before ground contact can be controlled. (2) The low-altitude, low-airspeed region (often called the 'low hover' region) — the helicopter is close to the ground but without enough altitude for the pilot to arrest the descent during autorotation before impact. Both regions leave the pilot with insufficient energy (either rotor RPM or altitude/airspeed) to execute a survivable autorotation.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.RM.005	At high density altitude, rotor efficiency decreases because the air is less dense, meaning the rotor must work harder and spin faster to generate the same lift. In an autorotation at high density altitude: (1) Rotor RPM decays faster after engine failure because thinner air provides less aerodynamic resistance to slow that decay. (2) The helicopter requires a longer distance and more altitude to convert potential energy into the rotor RPM needed for a cushioned landing. (3) The H/V diagram's shaded 'safe' region effectively expands — combinations of altitude and airspeed that were outside the danger zone at sea level may now fall inside it at 10,500 feet density altitude. The pilot should increase caution significantly, plan air taxi routes that maintain more altitude and airspeed to stay well clear of the H/V curve's danger zones, and recognize that the published diagram underestimates risk in these conditions.	->	At high density altitude, rotor efficiency decreases because the air is less dense, meaning the rotor must work harder to generate the same lift. In an autorotation at high density altitude: (1) The rate of descent is significantly higher — autorotative performance is degraded because thinner air provides less aerodynamic resistance and buoyancy, requiring more altitude to arrest descent before landing. (2) The helicopter requires a longer distance and greater altitude to convert potential energy and build sufficient rotor RPM for a cushioned landing. (3) The H/V diagram's shaded 'safe' region effectively expands — combinations of altitude and airspeed that were outside the danger zone at sea level may now fall inside it at 10,500 feet density altitude. The pilot should increase caution significantly, plan air taxi routes that maintain more altitude and airspeed to stay well clear of the H/V curve's danger zones, and recognize that the published diagram underestimates risk in these conditions.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.RM.008	This is partially correct but dangerously oversimplified. Twin-engine helicopters do have H/V diagrams published in their RFMs, and those diagrams apply to the complete loss of both engines simultaneously — which remains a credible scenario (fuel exhaustion, catastrophic gearbox failure, fuel contamination). Furthermore, many light twin helicopters are not Category A certified or may not meet Category A performance requirements for all phases of flight, meaning a single-engine failure at certain combinations of altitude and airspeed may still not permit a safe continued flight or autorotation. For true Category A operations (14 CFR Part 29 transport category, CAT A performance charts), the helicopter is flown on a specific profile (takeoff flight path) that guarantees an engine failure at any point allows either a safe continued flight or a safe autorotation landing — this is a separate concept from the standard H/V diagram and requires strict adherence to Category A takeoff and landing profiles. The chief pilot's blanket statement ignores: (1) The dual-engine failure scenario. (2) Non-Category-A twins that have meaningful H/V limitations. (3) The fact that even on a Category A helicopter, H/V considerations apply outside of CAT A profiles. The pilot should consult the specific RFM for the aircraft being flown.	->	This is partially correct but dangerously oversimplified. Twin-engine helicopters do have H/V diagrams published in their RFMs, and those diagrams apply to the complete loss of both engines simultaneously — which remains a credible scenario (fuel exhaustion, catastrophic gearbox failure, fuel contamination). Furthermore, many light twin helicopters are not Category A certified or may not meet Category A performance requirements for all phases of flight, meaning a single-engine failure at certain combinations of altitude and airspeed may still not permit a safe continued flight or autorotation. For true Category A operations (14 CFR Part 29 transport category, CAT A performance charts), the helicopter is flown on a specific profile (takeoff or landing flight path) that guarantees an engine failure at any point allows either a safe continued flight or a safe autorotation landing — this is a separate concept from the standard H/V diagram and requires strict adherence to approved Category A takeoff and landing profiles. The chief pilot's blanket statement ignores: (1) The dual-engine failure scenario. (2) Non-Category-A twins that have meaningful H/V limitations. (3) The fact that even on a Category A helicopter, H/V considerations apply outside of approved CAT A profiles. The pilot should consult the specific RFM for the aircraft being flown.

Follow-up: What is the specific regulatory definition of Category A helicopter performance, and what flight profiles does it require?

Answer: Category A, as defined in 14 CFR 1.1, means multiengine rotorcraft designed with engine and system isolation features that ensure a single engine failure cannot affect any other engine or essential system, and continued safe flight and landing is assured in the event of one engine failure. Category A operations require adherence to approved takeoff and landing flight paths (profiles) that ensure that following an engine failure at any point along the path, the helicopter can either continue the takeoff and reach a safe altitude or, if below the Takeoff Decision Point (TDP), safely abort back to the takeoff surface. These profiles — typically a clear area takeoff profile or a helipad (confined area) takeoff profile — are published in the RFM performance section and must be flown precisely. The Category A concept originated from 14 CFR 29.67 and 29.79 certification standards and is required for helicopter IFR Part 135 operations under 14 CFR 135.179 and related provisions. Operating in compliance with approved CAT A profiles eliminates reliance on the standard H/V diagram during those specific takeoff and landing phases, but does not remove all H/V risk from the entire flight.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.RM.001	Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for, interpret, or favor information that confirms what you already expect or believe, while ignoring contradictory information. During an air taxi, a pilot who expects a clearance to a specific hover taxiway or pad may 'hear' what they expect from ATC rather than the actual instruction given. This can lead to taxiing to the wrong location, crossing an active runway, or conflicting with other traffic — all without realizing the error.	->	Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for, interpret, or favor information that confirms what you already expect or believe, while ignoring contradictory information. During an air taxi, a pilot who expects a clearance to a specific hover taxiway or pad may 'hear' what they expect from ATC rather than the actual instruction given. This can lead to taxiing to the wrong location, crossing an active runway, or conflicting with other traffic — all without realizing the error.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.RM.002	Expectation bias occurs when a pilot anticipates a specific clearance or instruction based on prior experience or routine, and mentally 'fills in' that expected information even when the actual clearance differs. For example, a pilot who routinely air taxis from the ramp to Pad 3 may receive a new clearance to Pad 1 but mentally process it as Pad 3 because that is what they expect. The pilot then departs toward Pad 3, placing the helicopter in an unauthorized area or in conflict with ground traffic.	->	Expectation bias occurs when a pilot anticipates a specific clearance or instruction based on prior experience or routine, and mentally 'fills in' that expected information even when the actual clearance differs. For example, a pilot who routinely air taxis from the ramp to Pad 3 may receive a new clearance to Pad 1 but mentally process it as Pad 3 because that is what they expect. The pilot then departs toward Pad 3, placing the helicopter in an unauthorized area or in conflict with ground traffic.
