AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.S002	The primary consideration is to keep the airspeed slow enough that the pilot can stop and set the helicopter down within the available clear area ahead, yet fast enough to maintain positive control and directional stability. Typically, air taxi is conducted at speeds no faster than a slow walking pace to a few knots — essentially just enough movement to translate across the ground while staying clear of the height-velocity diagram's avoid regions. The pilot must also ensure the airspeed does not approach ETL, which would require significant power changes and attitude adjustments.	->	The primary consideration is to maintain airspeed slow enough that the helicopter can be stopped and set down within the available clear area ahead, while maintaining positive control and directional stability. Air taxi is typically conducted at very low airspeeds (a slow walking pace to a few knots). This slow airspeed ensures the pilot remains in a favorable region of the height-velocity diagram and can respond to emergencies with immediate landing capability.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.S1.002	LTE is a critical, low-speed aerodynamic phenomenon where the tail rotor loses sufficient thrust to maintain directional control, allowing the helicopter to rotate uncommanded in the direction of main rotor torque. During air taxi operations, LTE is most likely to occur at low airspeeds — typically below 30 knots — where translational lift has not been established and the tail rotor is working near its aerodynamic limits. Certain wind azimuths (specifically winds from approximately 210° to 330° relative to the nose for counterclockwise rotor systems) can destabilize the tail rotor and trigger LTE.	->	LTE is a critical, low-speed aerodynamic phenomenon where the tail rotor loses sufficient thrust to maintain directional control, allowing the helicopter to rotate uncommanded opposite to translational movement. During air taxi operations, LTE is most likely to occur at low airspeeds — typically below 30 knots — where translational lift has not been established and the tail rotor is working near its aerodynamic limits. AC 90-95 identifies three distinct LTE mechanisms across different wind azimuth regions: tail rotor vortex ring state (approximately 210°–330°), loss of translational lift effectiveness (directly behind, ~180°), and main rotor disk interference. The specific azimuth danger zones and their severity depend on the helicopter's rotor system direction and atmospheric conditions.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.S1.004	A 270° wind relative to the aircraft's 360° heading means the wind is coming from the left — roughly a direct 9 o'clock crosswind. For a counterclockwise rotor system, winds from the left rear quadrant (approximately 210°–330° relative to the nose) are in the LTE susceptibility range because they can reduce the angle of attack differential across the tail rotor blades, degrading thrust output. At 15 knots, this is well within the low-speed LTE danger zone. To maintain desired track northbound, you apply right cyclic to lean into the drift (wind is pushing you right), which is correct. However, to avoid placing the wind in the most dangerous azimuth, you might angle the air taxi slightly to keep the wind more toward the nose or right side of the aircraft. Maintain a groundspeed that allows effective directional control, keep power demand moderate, and be prepared to add right pedal immediately if an uncommanded left yaw begins. If conditions permit, an airspeed above translational lift (approximately 16–24 knots depending on the aircraft) eliminates LTE susceptibility by providing aerodynamic tail rotor efficiency.	->	A 270° wind relative to the aircraft's 360° heading means the wind is coming from the left — roughly a direct 9 o'clock crosswind. For a counterclockwise rotor system, winds from the left rear quadrant (approximately 210°–330° relative to the nose) are in the LTE susceptibility range because they can reduce the angle of attack differential across the tail rotor blades, degrading thrust output. At 15 knots, this is well within the low-speed LTE danger zone. To maintain desired track northbound and counter the rightward drift, you apply LEFT cyclic to lean into the wind (toward the west), which places the aircraft into a more favorable wind azimuth and improves tail rotor effectiveness. Maintain a groundspeed that allows effective directional control, keep power demand moderate, and be prepared to add right pedal immediately if an uncommanded left yaw begins. If conditions permit, an airspeed above translational lift (approximately 16–24 knots depending on the aircraft) eliminates LTE susceptibility by providing aerodynamic tail rotor efficiency.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.C.S-Nr.001	The tachometer — typically a dual-needle or split-display instrument — shows both engine RPM (N2 or power turbine speed on turbines, engine RPM on piston) and main rotor RPM (Nr) simultaneously. In normal powered flight both needles should be married (overlapping) within the green arc. A split between the two needles signals a drivetrain or governor issue. Nr alone tells you whether the rotor is turning fast enough to produce the lift and control authority needed for flight. If Nr drops out of the green arc during air taxi, you may be approaching a low-rotor condition that can lead to blade stall.	->	The tachometer—typically a dual-needle or split-display instrument—shows both engine RPM (power turbine speed on turbines, commonly designated N2 or Np depending on aircraft type; refer to POH/RFM) and main rotor RPM (Nr) simultaneously. In normal powered flight both needles should be married (overlapping) within the green arc. A split between the two needles signals a drivetrain or governor issue. Nr alone tells you whether the rotor is turning fast enough to produce the lift and control authority needed for flight. If Nr drops out of the green arc during air taxi, you lose lift-producing capacity and control authority; on teetering rotor systems this increases mast bumping risk. Blade stall is associated with high-speed forward flight exceeding aerodynamic limits, not low-speed taxi conditions.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.002	A NOTAM (Notice to Air Missions) is a notice containing information about the establishment, condition, or change in any aeronautical facility, service, procedure, or hazard. Examples that directly affect helicopter taxi operations include: (1) a taxiway closure or construction zone that blocks a normal taxi route, and (2) a displaced threshold or runway closure that changes taxi flow patterns. Other relevant examples include temporary flight restrictions (TFRs) affecting ground movement, lighting outages on taxiways, or FOD on a specific taxiway.	->	A NOTAM (Notice to Air Missions) is a notice containing information about the establishment, condition, or change in any aeronautical facility, service, procedure, or hazard. Examples that directly affect helicopter taxi operations include: (1) a taxiway closure or construction zone that blocks a normal taxi route, and (2) a runway lighting system outage or displaced threshold that changes the active runway assignment and taxi flow patterns. Other relevant examples include a navigational aid outage affecting departure procedures, or FOD (foreign object debris) on a specific taxiway. TFRs restrict airspace and affect departure/arrival operations but do not directly restrict ground taxi movement.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.005	I would not proceed with a clearance I do not fully understand. The safest action is to read back only the portions I understood, then immediately request clarification from ground control — for example, 'Ground, N123AB, I need clarification on that routing, say again taxiway names.' The airport diagram (either printed from the Chart Supplement or on an EFB) is the most immediately useful resource because it shows all taxiway designations visually. ATC can also provide progressive taxi instructions if requested. FAR 91.123 requires compliance with ATC clearances, but pilot-in-command responsibility means I must not proceed if there is uncertainty about the routing.	->	I would not proceed with a clearance I do not fully understand. The safest action is to read back only the portions I understood, then immediately request clarification from ground control—for example, 'Ground, N123AB, I need clarification on that routing, say again taxiway names.' Requesting clarification before accepting or executing a clearance is not a deviation from 14 CFR 91.123; the regulation requires compliance only once a clearance is accepted and understood. The airport diagram (either printed from the Chart Supplement or on an EFB) is the most immediately useful resource because it shows all taxiway designations visually. ATC can also provide progressive taxi instructions if requested. This approach satisfies both regulatory compliance and pilot-in-command responsibility.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.002	That is a runway designation sign — red background with white letters identifies a runway hold short point or runway intersection. It indicates you are approaching the intersection of Runway 27L/9R. You must stop and hold short unless ATC has specifically cleared you to cross or enter that runway. Proceeding without a clearance onto an active runway constitutes a runway incursion.	->	That is a runway DESIGNATION sign with red background and white letters — it identifies which runway lies ahead. The actual hold short point is marked separately by a runway HOLD SHORT position sign (red background with white text reading 'HOLD SHORT OF RWY [number]') and painted holding position markings on the pavement (two solid yellow lines and two dashed yellow lines). You must not proceed past the hold short line onto the runway without specific ATC clearance. Proceeding without clearance constitutes a runway incursion and violates 14 CFR 91.129.
