AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.004	At sunset and beyond, position lights (red, green, and white) are required by 14 CFR 91.209(a). Anti-collision lights must be on whenever the engine is running per 91.209(b), unless creating a hazard. When crossing an active runway, the landing light should also be illuminated to maximize visibility to any aircraft on approach or other ground traffic. Strobes (if separate from the anti-collision beacon) should be on as well. This combination ensures maximum conspicuity during a high-risk ground operation.	->	At sunset and beyond, 14 CFR 91.209(a) requires position lights (red, green, and white) on until sunrise. Anti-collision lights (beacon or strobe, per your aircraft's equipment) must be on whenever the engine is running per 91.209(b), unless the PIC determines they create a hazard—a rare exception in runway crossing scenarios. The landing light should be illuminated to maximize visibility to approaching aircraft and ground traffic. Any additional supplemental strobes, if equipped, are a best-practice recommendation for conspicuity but are not independently mandated by regulation. This combination ensures maximum legal compliance and visibility during this high-risk ground operation.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.006	Under 14 CFR 91.205(b), anti-collision lights are required equipment for VFR day flight if the aircraft is certificated to require them. If the helicopter's type certificate or equipment list requires an anti-collision light and it is inoperative, the aircraft is not airworthy for that flight unless the defect is addressed per an approved MEL or the item is placarded inoperative per 14 CFR 91.213. You cannot simply depart with a known inoperative required light. You must either repair/replace the bulb, or determine via 91.213 whether the aircraft can be operated without it under an approved MEL. Absence of a legal MEL generally means the aircraft is grounded until repaired.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.213, if the anti-collision light is listed as required equipment on the aircraft's type certificate data sheet or approved equipment list, the aircraft is not airworthy for flight with the light inoperative unless the defect is addressed per an approved MEL or the item is placarded inoperative and deferred under 91.213. Anti-collision lights are required for night VFR operations under 14 CFR 91.205(c); for day VFR, the requirement depends on the aircraft's type certificate and equipment list. You cannot depart with a known inoperative required light. You must either repair/replace the bulb, or determine via 91.213 whether the aircraft can be operated without it under an approved MEL. Absence of a legal MEL means the aircraft is grounded until repaired.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.RM.008	Under Low Visibility Operations (LVO), airports with Category II/III ILS approaches implement Enhanced Taxi Procedures per FAA Order JO 7110.65 and AC 120-57. Specific protocols include: ATC issues explicit crossing or hold short instructions for every runway, even inactive ones—no 'proceed via' shortcuts; pilots must read back every runway hold short instruction verbatim; ATC may require position reports at specific points; movement area may be restricted to aircraft with airport surface detection equipment (ASDE) coverage; flight crew must have a current airport diagram and confirm each hold short visually before crossing. As a helicopter pilot, you must also be aware that in LVO your tendency to transition to hover to improve visibility is dangerous—transitioning to hover-taxi near runway edges in low RVR places you outside the protected area identified by lighting and signage. Remain in ground-taxi mode, taxi at reduced speed, keep exterior lights on, and do not proceed past any hold short point without explicit clearance confirmed by positive visual identification of the marking.	->	Under Low Visibility Operations (LVO) at Category II/III airports, 14 CFR 91.129 and FAA Order JO 7110.65 Chapter 3 require enhanced procedures. ATC will issue explicit crossing or hold short instructions for every runway, even inactive ones—no 'proceed via' shortcuts; you must read back every runway hold short instruction verbatim. ATC may require position reports at specific points and will restrict the movement area to aircraft with operational lighting and radio contact. Some airports have airport surface detection equipment (ASDE) coverage, but this is airport-dependent, not universally required. As a helicopter pilot, you must have a current airport diagram and visually confirm each hold short point before crossing. In LVO, do not transition to hover-taxi near runway edges—this places you outside the protected area identified by lighting and signage. Remain in ground-taxi mode, taxi at reduced speed, keep exterior lights on, and do not proceed past any hold short point without explicit clearance and positive visual identification of the marking.
