AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.SKI.007	The argument is fundamentally flawed because aircraft systems can develop anomalies between flights and even between engine starts without any crew awareness. Technically: hydraulic brake pressure can bleed down during a shutdown, a ground crew member could have moved or bumped a control, a nose wheel steering pin might not have been reinstalled after towing, or a shimmy dampener could have developed a leak while parked. Operationally, the before-taxi checklist exists because each ground operation is an independent event. A specific scenario: after a one-hour shutdown, the hydraulic brake system on a wheeled helicopter loses pressure due to a seal that was marginal but holding under heat. The pilot skips the brake check, begins taxiing, and discovers brake failure while approaching an active taxiway intersection with crossing traffic. The checklist would have caught the low pressure before any movement began.	->	14 CFR 91.7 requires the pilot in command to determine that the aircraft is in an airworthy condition before flight, and 14 CFR 91.9 requires operation within the limitations of the flight manual—which includes the required checklists. If an incident investigation revealed the pilot knowingly or negligently skipped checklist items, the FAA could pursue certificate action under 14 CFR 91.13 (careless or reckless operation) or 49 U.S.C. 44709 (reexamination/certificate action). Additionally, if the helicopter's POH/AFM designates certain checklist items as Required Actions within the Airworthiness Limitations section, bypassing them could constitute a violation of 14 CFR 43.16 and affect the aircraft's airworthiness status under its type certification, compounding PIC liability under 91.7.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.003	Current airport diagrams can be obtained from several sources: the FAA's free Aeronautical Chart Users' Guide and downloadable airport diagrams at faa.gov/air_traffic/flight_info/aeronav; the Chart Supplement (formerly A/FD) for the appropriate region; aviation apps and EFBs that pull FAA-sourced data; and some GPS/moving map avionics. The chart must be current — diagrams are updated on the 56-day AIRAC cycle. Outdated diagrams may not reflect recent taxiway or ramp construction.	->	Current airport diagrams can be obtained from several sources: the FAA's free Aeronautical Chart Users' Guide and downloadable airport diagrams at faa.gov/air_traffic/flight_info/aeronav; the Chart Supplement (formerly A/FD) for the appropriate region; aviation apps and EFBs that pull FAA-sourced data; and some GPS/moving map avionics. The chart must be current — FAA terminal procedure publications and airport diagrams are updated on the 28-day AIRAC cycle. Outdated diagrams may not reflect recent taxiway or ramp construction.
