AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.008	The clearance explicitly authorized only one runway crossing — Runway 15. Under ATC phraseology standards, each runway crossing requires a separate, explicit clearance. If the routing via Echo and Delta takes me past additional runways, I do not cross them based on the taxi clearance alone — I hold short of each subsequent runway and request crossing clearance before proceeding. I use the airport diagram to pre-identify every runway I will approach on the route and mark them mentally as requiring individual clearances. At night especially, taxiway and runway lighting can be confusing, so the diagram is essential to anticipate each runway hold-short point before I reach it, rather than reacting to signs in the dark.	->	Runway guard lights — also called 'wig-wag' lights — are alternating flashing yellow lights installed at taxiway/runway intersections to warn pilots they are approaching a runway. They can be in-pavement (embedded in the taxiway surface) or elevated on both sides of the taxiway. Additionally, runway hold-short positions are marked with elevated red stop bar lights at many airports. At airports equipped with a Surface Movement Guidance and Control System (SMGCS), red stop bar lights remain illuminated until ATC issues a crossing clearance and activates the green lead-on lights. This ATC-controlled feature is available at select major airports; pilots should not assume all airports with stop bars have remotely controlled systems. Regardless of stop bar type, a pilot must never cross an illuminated red stop bar without explicit ATC clearance. If I see alternating flashing yellows or red stop bars in my path, I stop immediately regardless of my positional awareness on the diagram.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.Position.005	As the wind shifts to a right quartering tailwind, you transition the cyclic from neutral/forward to a position that is both forward (into the tailwind component) and into the wind (right), so forward and slightly right. The primary risk you are now managing is the potential for the tailwind to get under the rotor disk and pitch the nose down while simultaneously creating a lateral tipping tendency toward the left (away from the right quartering wind). If cyclic is not repositioned promptly, the helicopter can become difficult to control directionally and there is increased risk of dynamic rollover if one wheel catches or if collective is inadvertently increased. Pedal inputs may also be needed to counteract any yawing tendency from the wind on the tail.	->	As the wind shifts to a right quartering tailwind, transition the cyclic forward (to counteract the tailwind component pitching the nose down) and right (to counteract the right crosswind). The primary risk you are now managing is dynamic rollover: the right crosswind unloads the left (upwind) wheel while the tailwind pitches the nose, making the right (downwind) wheel the pivot point. If cyclic correction is insufficient or delayed, the helicopter can tip over the downwind wheel. Pedal inputs may also be needed to counteract yaw. Early, smooth cyclic correction is essential before the upwind wheel lightens significantly.
