AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.008	With governor failure and Nr climbing above the normal (green arc) range, the immediate actions are: (1) Smoothly increase collective to add blade pitch and load the rotor, which will resist Nr climb; (2) Simultaneously reduce throttle/fuel control to reduce engine output — on a turbine, this may mean manually overriding the fuel control or moving the throttle/condition lever toward idle per RFM emergency procedures; (3) Monitor Nr, torque, and TOT/TGT simultaneously to ensure you are not overloading the drivetrain while reducing RPM; (4) Maintain situational awareness by keeping a slow, controlled taxi pace or stopping in place if clear — do not allow the emergency management to distract you into taxiing into obstacles or personnel; (5) Once Nr is stabilized manually, taxi clear of congested areas and shut down per RFM. Overspeed of Nr risks exceeding rotor and gearbox limits, which can cause structural damage or rotor separation.	->	With governor failure and Nr climbing above the normal (green arc) range, the immediate actions are: (1) Smoothly increase collective to add blade pitch and load the rotor, which will resist Nr climb; (2) Simultaneously reduce throttle/fuel control to reduce engine output — specific procedures vary by aircraft type; consult the RFM/AFM emergency procedures for your helicopter's fuel control system (some turbines require throttle movement, others fuel control lever override); (3) Monitor Nr, torque, and TOT/TGT simultaneously to ensure you are not overloading the drivetrain while reducing RPM; (4) Maintain situational awareness by keeping a slow, controlled taxi pace or stopping in place if clear — do not allow the emergency management to distract you into taxiing into obstacles or personnel; (5) Once Nr is stabilized manually, taxi clear of congested areas and shut down per RFM. Overspeed of Nr risks exceeding rotor and gearbox limits, which can cause structural damage or rotor separation. Post-flight: Under 14 CFR 9