AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.002	Cyclic is the primary control for directional tracking — lateral cyclic steers the helicopter left or right to stay on the centerline, and fore/aft cyclic controls speed. Anti-torque pedals control heading (yaw) to keep the nose aligned with the direction of travel. Collective manages rotor unloading so the wheels remain in contact with the ground while still allowing directional response. The coordination of all three is required to maintain precise track.	->	Cyclic is the primary control for directional tracking — lateral cyclic steers the helicopter left or right to stay on the centerline, and fore/aft cyclic controls taxi speed. Anti-torque pedals control heading (yaw) to keep the nose aligned with the direction of travel. Collective is managed to keep the helicopter sufficiently light on its wheels for responsive directional control while maintaining enough ground contact for rolling traction and preventing inadvertent liftoff. The coordination of all three controls is required to maintain precise track.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.006	During the turn, you must follow the inside or center of the taxiway turn radius as defined by the centerline or as directed. Begin the turn by applying lateral cyclic in the direction of the turn while using pedals to coordinate heading change. Manage taxi speed by reducing it before the turn so the helicopter doesn't swing wide — the centrifugal tendency of the wheeled undercarriage can cause tracking to the outside of the turn. Keep collective at a level that maintains rotor partially loaded for steering response without becoming airborne. Watch the nose position relative to the centerline continuously and be prepared to modulate cyclic pressure throughout to stay within the 4-foot tolerance as the turn arc changes radius. Complete the turn aligned with the new centerline before allowing speed to increase.	->	During the turn, you must follow the inside or center of the taxiway turn radius as defined by the centerline or as directed. Begin the turn by applying lateral cyclic in the direction of the turn while using pedals to coordinate the heading change. Manage taxi speed by reducing it before the turn so the helicopter doesn't swing wide — the inertia of the wheeled undercarriage can cause tracking to the outside of the turn. Anticipate the turn entry point slightly before the centerline arc begins to account for the helicopter's rolling inertia. Keep collective at a level that maintains rotor partially loaded for steering response without becoming airborne. Watch the nose position relative to the centerline continuously and be prepared to modulate cyclic pressure throughout to stay within the 4-foot tolerance as the turn arc changes radius. Complete the turn aligned with the new centerline before allowing speed to increase.
