AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.Receive.006	At a non-towered airport, there is no ATC clearance required to taxi. However, you must monitor and communicate on the published CTAF (Common Traffic Advisory Frequency). You should self-announce your intentions before taxiing, including your position, direction of movement, and intended runway. For example: '[Airport name] traffic, [callsign], taxiing to Runway XX, [airport name].' You must also visually clear all runway crossings before proceeding. The Aeronautical Information Manual recommends making traffic advisories at key points — prior to taxi, before entering the runway, etc. Compliance here is with best practices and situational awareness rather than a formal clearance.	->	At a non-towered airport, there is no ATC clearance required to taxi. However, you must monitor and communicate on the published CTAF (Common Traffic Advisory Frequency). You should self-announce your intentions before taxiing, including your aircraft type, position, direction of movement, and intended runway. For example: '[Airport name] traffic, [aircraft type] [callsign], taxiing to Runway XX, [airport name].' You must also visually clear all runway crossings before proceeding. The Aeronautical Information Manual and AC 90-66B recommend making traffic advisories at key points — prior to taxi, before entering the runway, and after landing. Compliance here is with best practices and situational awareness rather than a formal clearance.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.Receive.007	Your obligation to comply with the hold short instruction does not change because of degraded markings — the clearance you received and read back is a binding instruction under 14 CFR 91.123. You must stop before what you assess to be the runway hold short position and verify your location. Cross-reference airport signage (the mandatory instruction sign with white numerals on red background), painted surface markings, and airport diagram. If you are genuinely unsure of your position, stop and advise ground control of your position and the condition of the markings. Do not cross a runway without positive confirmation. You should also report the degraded markings to ATC so a NOTAM or maintenance action can be initiated.	->	Your obligation to comply with the hold short instruction does not change because of degraded markings — the clearance you received and read back is a binding instruction under 14 CFR 91.123. You must stop before what you assess to be the runway hold short position and verify your location. Cross-reference airport signage (the mandatory instruction sign with white numerals on red background), painted surface markings, and airport diagram. If you are genuinely unsure of your position, stop and advise ground control of your position and the condition of the markings. Do not cross a runway without positive confirmation. You should also report the degraded markings to ATC so a NOTAM or maintenance action can be initiated. Follow-up: If you inadvertently cross the hold short line before stopping — but no conflict results — you should immediately advise ATC that you have inadvertently crossed the hold short line and report your current position. ATC needs to know to adjust sequencing and ensure no conflicting traffic. Depending on the severity, ATC may file a pilot deviation report. A NASA ASRS filing is also recommended under the Aviation Safety Reporting Program (AC 00-46F) to protect against certificate action under 14 CFR 91.13 (Careless or Reckless Operation). Proactive disclosure and a NASA ASRS report are the appropriate professional responses.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.Receive.008	First, do not proceed down the closed taxiway based on ATC's instruction alone. Advise ground control immediately: 'Ground, [callsign], be advised NOTAM indicates [taxiway] is closed — please confirm routing.' ATC may have more current information than the NOTAM (e.g., the closure was lifted), or the controller may have made an error. ATC can issue amended instructions based on current field conditions. If ATC insists on routing you through a visually apparent closure or hazard, you have the authority and responsibility under 14 CFR 91.3 to deviate from ATC instructions to the extent necessary to avoid an unsafe situation. You would then immediately notify ATC of the deviation. Document the incident afterward.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.3(b), the PIC may deviate from any rule or ATC clearance to the extent necessary to meet an emergency. 14 CFR 91.123(b) requires the pilot to obtain an amended clearance when unable to comply with an ATC clearance; however, the authority to deviate resides in 91.3(b). After deviating, the PIC must notify ATC as soon as possible. If requested by ATC or the FSDO, the PIC must submit a written report of the deviation to the Administrator within 10 days.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.Comply.003	The taxiway centerline is a continuous single yellow line that runs along the middle of the taxiway. It provides directional guidance and helps pilots maintain proper alignment on the taxiway surface. When surface taxiing a wheel-equipped helicopter, you should track along or near the centerline to stay clear of taxiway edges, other aircraft, and obstacles, and to present the correct wingtip/rotor clearances from taxiway lighting and signs.	->	The taxiway centerline is a continuous single yellow line that runs along the middle of the taxiway. It provides directional guidance and helps pilots maintain proper alignment on the taxiway surface. When surface taxiing a wheel-equipped helicopter, you should track along or near the centerline to stay clear of taxiway edges, other aircraft, and obstacles, and to maintain proper main rotor disc clearance from taxiway lighting, signs, and other obstacles.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.Comply.008	Tower may use a light gun even with a working radio if there is a radio communication failure on the pilot's end (e.g., receiver failure where you can transmit but not receive), or to supplement a verbal instruction you may not have received. A steady red light gun signal to an aircraft on the ground means 'STOP.' You must stop the helicopter immediately and hold your position. Do not move until you receive a clearance to proceed — either via radio if communications are restored, or via the appropriate light gun signal (flashing green to a ground vehicle/aircraft means 'cleared to taxi,' though for aircraft the signal is technically flashing green to taxi). Squawk 7600 (radio failure) if you believe you have a comm failure, and look to the tower for light gun guidance to sequence your taxi and departure.	->	Tower may use a light gun even with a working radio if there is a radio communication failure on the pilot's end (e.g., receiver failure where you can transmit but not receive), or to supplement a verbal instruction you may not have received. A steady red light gun signal to an aircraft on the ground means 'STOP.' You must stop the helicopter immediately and hold your position. Do not move until you receive a clearance to proceed — either via radio if communications are restored, or via the appropriate light gun signal (flashing green means 'cleared to taxi'). Squawk 7600 (radio failure) if you believe you have a comm failure, and look to the tower for light gun guidance to sequence your taxi and departure. Acknowledge by flashing your landing/taxi lights or by other conspicuous movement.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.E.005	First, apply uphill cyclic to tilt the rotor disk level with the horizon (not parallel to the slope). Then smoothly increase collective to raise the downhill skid until both skids are in the same plane — effectively the helicopter is on the uphill skid only at this point with the rotor disk level. Continue increasing collective until the uphill skid lifts off, transitioning to a level hover directly above the slope. Throughout this process, maintain heading with pedals and keep cyclic inputs smooth to avoid dynamic rollover. Departure should be made into the wind when possible.	->	First, apply uphill cyclic to tilt the rotor disk level with the horizon (not parallel to the slope). Before beginning, verify that the slope angle does not exceed the maximum slope angle specified in the RFM/POH. Then smoothly increase collective to raise the downhill skid off the ground while the uphill skid remains the pivot point. Continue increasing collective until the uphill skid lifts off, transitioning to a level hover directly above the slope. Throughout this process, maintain heading with pedals and keep cyclic inputs smooth to avoid dynamic rollover. Departure should be made into the wind when possible.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.E.DR.002	The critical rollover angle is the angle of bank at which the main rotor thrust vector is tilted so far from vertical that full opposite cyclic can no longer stop or reverse the rolling moment. Once this angle is exceeded, recovery is impossible and the helicopter will continue to roll over regardless of control inputs. The exact critical angle varies by helicopter model but is generally reached quickly once dynamic rollover begins, which is why early prevention is far more effective than attempted recovery.	->	The critical rollover angle is the fuselage bank angle—approximately 8–12 degrees depending on helicopter type (consult the RFM/POH)—at which the main rotor thrust vector is tilted so far from vertical that full opposite cyclic can no longer stop or reverse the rolling moment. Once this angle is exceeded, recovery becomes impossible and the helicopter will continue to roll over regardless of control inputs. This angle can be reached within seconds once dynamic rollover initiates, which is why early prevention through proper technique and go-around decisions is far more effective than attempted recovery.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.E.RM.001	Dynamic rollover occurs when a helicopter begins to rotate about one skid or wheel acting as a pivot point, and the roll rate exceeds the pilot's ability to correct with opposite cyclic. On a slope, the uphill skid can catch on uneven terrain or a rock, creating a fixed pivot point. As collective is increased to lift off, lateral lift is generated; if the helicopter begins rolling toward the uphill side and the pilot is slow to respond, the rolling moment can overpower cyclic authority, resulting in a rollover. The critical rollover angle for most helicopters is between 5 and 8 degrees of roll.	->	Dynamic rollover occurs when a helicopter begins to rotate about one skid, wheel, or other contact point acting as a fixed pivot, and the roll rate exceeds the pilot's ability to correct with opposite cyclic. The critical rollover angle—typically 5–8 degrees of fuselage bank depending on helicopter type—is the angle at which cyclic authority becomes insufficient to arrest the rotation and recovery becomes impossible. On a slope, the uphill skid can catch on uneven terrain or a rock, creating a fixed pivot point. As collective is increased to lift off, lateral moments develop; if the helicopter begins rolling toward the uphill side and the pilot is slow to respond, the fuselage bank angle can exceed the critical angle before corrective action takes effect. While slopes are a common trigger due to uneven terrain and constrained cyclic authority, dynamic rollover can occur during any semi-ground-contact condition.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.E.RM.003	Most helicopters are designed to land on slopes of up to approximately 8 to 10 degrees, though the specific limitation varies by aircraft and must be verified in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM). The primary limiting factor is main rotor blade clearance to the tail boom or airframe — as cyclic is displaced to hold the rotor disk level or tilted into the slope, a point is reached where the low-side blade tip can strike the airframe during the transition. Exceeding the approved slope angle risks main rotor blade contact with the tail boom, which is catastrophic.	->	The specific slope limitation for any helicopter must be verified in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM), as it varies significantly by aircraft type, rotor system, and configuration. Slope limitations typically range from approximately 6 to 15 degrees. The primary limiting factors are (1) lateral cyclic authority — the amount of cyclic deflection available before reaching a control stop — which must be sufficient to maintain the rotor disk level during touchdown, and (2) main rotor blade clearance to the tail boom or airframe as cyclic is displaced. Exceeding the approved slope angle risks either rotor blade strike or loss of control authority, both of which are catastrophic.
