AI_EDIT	PH.XV.B.RM.001	It means the straight-in autorotation is a training maneuver performed to a termination point — typically a power recovery — not a mandatory landing. If at any point during the maneuver the approach looks unsafe (bad energy state, unsuitable landing area, loss of rotor RPM control, obstruction conflict, etc.), either the applicant or evaluator must initiate a power recovery and go around. Completing the maneuver is never more important than preventing an accident. The evaluator has authority to take the controls at any time, and the applicant should not hesitate to call for a go-around if the situation deteriorates.	->	It means the straight-in autorotation is a training maneuver performed to a termination point — typically a power recovery — not a mandatory landing. If at any point during the maneuver the approach looks unsafe (bad energy state, unsuitable landing area, loss of rotor RPM control, obstruction conflict, etc.), either the applicant or evaluator must initiate a power recovery and go around. Completing the maneuver is never more important than preventing an accident. The evaluator has authority to take the controls at any time, and the applicant should not hesitate to call for a go-around if the situation deteriorates.
AI_EDIT	PH.XV.B.RM.008	This performance reveals a fundamental gap in the pilot's understanding of what the 180-degree autorotation is actually designed to accomplish. The ACS standard explicitly states that the 180-degree reference is about ground track direction change, not reciprocal heading. Landing 200 feet laterally off the intended zone in a practice environment suggests that in an actual emergency — where the landing zone may be a small clearing surrounded by obstacles — this error could be catastrophic. The maneuver should be evaluated on whether the pilot achieved the intended ground track reversal and arrived at or near the designated landing area, not on whether the heading indicator showed an exact reciprocal. The pilot's argument also reveals a checklist-mindset failure: they completed a step rather than achieved an outcome. On the checkride, I would explore whether the pilot understood the wind conditions, whether they planned a wind correction during the turn, and whether they can articulate the difference between the two concepts clearly before determining if this is a knowledge deficiency or an execution deficiency — both of which could affect pass/fail.	->	This performance reveals a fundamental gap in the pilot's understanding of what the 180-degree autorotation is actually designed to accomplish. The ACS standard explicitly requires that the pilot achieve the intended ground track reversal and land at or near the designated landing area — not simply reverse heading. Landing 200 feet laterally off the intended zone suggests that in an actual emergency where the landing zone may be a small clearing surrounded by obstacles, this error could be catastrophic. The pilot's argument reveals a checklist-mindset failure: they completed a step (heading reversal) rather than achieved the desired outcome (ground track recovery and accurate landing). On the checkride, this performance would be unsatisfactory. I would explore whether the pilot understood the wind conditions, whether they planned a wind correction during the turn, and whether they can articulate the difference between heading and ground track before determining if this is a knowledge deficiency or an execution deficiency.
AI_EDIT	PH.XV.B.RM.008	At 320 feet AGL the rollout standard has been technically met, so the mandatory intervention trigger (not rolled out by 300 feet) has not been activated. However, the evaluator has an independent and overriding safety-of-flight obligation. Low rotor RPM at 320 feet AGL with no altitude buffer is a distinct safety emergency separate from the rollout standard. If RPM is at or approaching the bottom of the green arc and descending, the evaluator must immediately call for a power recovery — not because of the ACS rollout standard, but because continuation risks rotor stall, which is unrecoverable. The ACS rollout standard is one specific intervention trigger; it does not limit the evaluator's authority or obligation to intervene for any other developing emergency. The evaluator's primary duty is always safe flight, and that duty supersedes all checkride scoring considerations.	->	At 320 feet AGL the rollout standard has been technically met, so the mandatory intervention trigger (not rolled out by 300 feet) has not been activated. However, the evaluator has an independent and overriding safety-of-flight obligation. Low rotor RPM at 320 feet AGL with no altitude buffer is a distinct safety emergency separate from the rollout standard. If RPM is at or approaching the bottom of the green arc and descending, the evaluator must immediately call for a power recovery — not because of the ACS rollout standard, but because continuation risks rotor stall, which is unrecoverable. The ACS rollout standard is one specific intervention trigger; it does not limit the evaluator's authority or obligation to intervene for any other developing emergency. The evaluator's primary duty is always safe flight, and that duty supersedes all checkride scoring considerations. When the evaluator calls a power recovery for safety reasons, the task is evaluated on the pilot's performance against the ACS standard. In this case, allowing rotor RPM to decay to the bottom of the green arc at 320 feet AGL represents a failure to maintain rotor RPM within normal operating limits — a distinct ACS deficiency — regardless of whether the subsequent landing after the power recovery call is successful. The task would be unsatisfactory on the basis of RPM management, not on the basis of the rollout standard.
AI_EDIT	PH.XVI.A.RM1.008	High density altitude degrades autorotation performance in several compounding ways. First, the engine is producing less power even before failure, increasing the likelihood of partial or full power loss under stress. Second, rotor efficiency decreases at high density altitude, meaning the rotor requires a higher true airspeed and a faster descent rate to maintain RPM in autorotation — the autorotative glide ratio effectively degrades. Third, the landing flare requires more precise timing because the reduced air density makes the helicopter sink faster during the terminal phase, and the collective cushion is less effective. As a result, a pilot at a high-density-altitude location should: (1) increase cruise altitude AGL to provide more time and autorotative distance; (2) fly at a higher indicated airspeed to account for the higher true airspeed required for efficient autorotation; (3) be more conservative about marginal landing areas because the terminal phase will be faster and harder; and (4) consult the RFM performance charts for the specific density altitude to verify power margins and recognize that the H/V diagram may shift under these conditions.	->	High density altitude degrades autorotation performance in several compounding ways. First, the engine is producing less power even before failure, increasing the likelihood of partial or full power loss under stress. Second, rotor efficiency decreases at high density altitude, meaning the rotor requires a higher true airspeed and a faster descent rate to maintain RPM in autorotation — the autorotative glide ratio effectively degrades. Third, the landing flare requires more precise timing because the reduced air density makes the helicopter sink faster during the terminal phase, and the collective cushion is less effective. As a result, a pilot at a high-density-altitude location should: (1) increase cruise altitude AGL to provide more time and autorotative distance; (2) fly at a higher indicated airspeed to account for the higher true airspeed required for efficient autorotation; (3) be more conservative about marginal landing areas because the terminal phase will be faster and harder; (4) consult the RFM performance charts for the specific density altitude to verify power margins. The published H/V diagram does not change with density altitude, but your operational margin from the avoid regions effectively decreases because autorotation performance degrades. Treat the published H/V diagram as optimistic at high density altitudes and maintain a larger safety buffer from the avoid region boundaries than you would at sea level standard conditions.
