AI_EDIT	PH.XVI.B.RM.008	Using actual flight idle on one engine creates real asymmetric torque and yaw that the applicant must physically manage with pedal and collective — this is a fundamentally different exercise than a 'chair-flying' style simulation where only verbal flows are performed. Specific risks to brief for the actual flight idle method include: (1) yaw onset direction and magnitude specific to this helicopter's engine/transmission layout — the applicant must know which way the nose will go; (2) the actual power split between engines and whether the operating engine can carry the load at the current weight, density altitude, and airspeed without exceeding torque or turbine temperature limits; (3) flight idle is not zero thrust — the windmilling engine still produces some torque and the transition to and from flight idle produces transient power changes that affect aircraft stability; (4) at low airspeed on final, the loss of ETL combined with asymmetric torque can create a high workload, high-sink-rate situation very quickly; (5) re-acceleration time when power is restored from flight idle — the applicant must know that power restoration is not instantaneous and must not expect immediate torque recovery if the evaluator calls for a go-around. The applicant's required response to an actual flight idle simulation is more physically demanding and time-critical than a flow-only simulation, which must be explicitly stated in the briefing.	->	Using actual flight idle on one engine creates real asymmetric torque and yaw that the applicant must physically manage with pedal and collective — this is a fundamentally different exercise than a 'chair-flying' style simulation where only verbal flows are performed. Specific risks to brief for the actual flight idle method include: (1) yaw onset direction and magnitude specific to this helicopter's engine/transmission layout — the applicant must know which way the nose will go; (2) the actual power split between engines and whether the operating engine can carry the load at the current weight, density altitude, and airspeed without exceeding torque or turbine temperature limits; (3) flight idle maintains engine rotation but reduces rotor thrust to near-zero; however, the transition to and from flight idle produces transient power changes and lingering asymmetric effects from drivetrain lag that affect aircraft stability; (4) at low airspeed on final, the loss of ETL combined with asymmetric torque can create a high workload, high-sink-rate situation very quickly; (5) re-acceleration time when power is restored from flight idle — the applicant must know that power restoration is not instantaneous and must not expect immediate torque recovery if the evaluator calls for a go-around. The applicant's required response to an actual flight idle simulation is more physically demanding and time-critical than a flow-only simulation, which must be explicitly stated in the briefing.
AI_EDIT	PH.XVI.C.RM.003	The primary factor is providing enough altitude to complete a full recovery before reaching the ground. Recovery from VRS requires transitioning out of the vortex wake by accelerating forward, sideward, or rearward, or by entering autorotation — all of which require altitude and time. The entry altitude must account for the altitude lost during onset recognition, the altitude lost during the recovery maneuver itself, and an additional safety buffer. A commonly accepted minimum is 1,500 feet AGL or higher, though specific aircraft performance data and ACS task standards should guide the actual briefed altitude.	->	The primary factor is providing enough altitude to complete a full recovery before reaching the ground. Recovery from VRS requires transitioning out of the vortex wake by accelerating forward, sideward, or rearward, or by entering autorotation — all of which require altitude and time. The entry altitude must account for the altitude lost during onset recognition, the altitude lost during the recovery maneuver itself, and an additional safety buffer. The FAA-H-8083-21B emphasizes providing 'sufficient altitude' but does not mandate a specific minimum. A commonly taught minimum in the training community is 1,500 feet AGL or higher, though the specific altitude depends on aircraft performance characteristics, terrain, and instructor discretion. The altitude must be sufficient for worst-case recovery scenarios, not optimistic performance assumptions.
AI_EDIT	PH.XVI.C.RM.008	When the POH is silent on VRS-specific altitudes, the pilot must synthesize information from multiple sources: the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook guidance on VRS aerodynamics, the aircraft's known performance data (hover OGE ceiling, autorotation descent rate, time to recover from power-off conditions), and general training community standards. A conservative and defensible approach is to estimate the worst-case altitude consumption: assume 200-300 feet for recognition and initial control input, plus 300-500 feet for recovery transition through ETL, plus a hard safety buffer of 500+ feet — resulting in a minimum around 1,000-1,500 feet AGL at minimum. The absence of POH guidance on VRS is itself a significant risk flag — it means there is no manufacturer-validated data for this helicopter's specific behavior in VRS, which could include unique tendencies like asymmetric roll onset or rapid rate escalation. This gap means the examiner/instructor should brief more conservatively, not less. Relying solely on general handbook data without type-specific data means operating with uncertainty, which demands greater safety margins per sound ADM principles.	->	When the POH is silent on VRS-specific altitudes, the pilot must synthesize information from multiple sources: the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook guidance on VRS aerodynamics, the aircraft's known performance data (hover OGE ceiling, autorotation descent rate, time to recover from power-off conditions), and general training community standards. A conservative approach is to estimate altitude consumption in the worst case: recognition and initial control input (typically 200–300 feet, but variable based on pilot experience and VRS onset rate), recovery transition through ETL (300–500 feet), plus a substantial safety buffer (500+ feet) — resulting in a minimum around 1,000–1,500 feet AGL. This budget is conservative because VRS onset rates and recognition time vary significantly depending on aircraft type, weight, density altitude, and pilot technique. The absence of POH guidance on VRS is itself a significant risk flag — it means there is no manufacturer-validated data for this helicopter's specific behavior in VRS, which could include unique tendencies like asymmetric roll onset or rapid rate escalation. This gap means the examiner/instructor should brief more conservatively, not less, and the altitude minimum should be reviewed for each specific flight scenario rather than treated as a fixed rule.
AI_EDIT	PH.XVI.C.RM003	A generally accepted minimum is sufficient altitude to complete a full autorotation to a safe landing — typically at least 1,000 feet AGL or more depending on terrain and aircraft performance, though the specific number varies by aircraft and conditions. Altitude matters because VRS can produce very high rates of descent (up to 2,000–4,000 feet per minute in severe cases) with an almost complete loss of collective effectiveness. Recovery takes time and altitude; insufficient altitude beneath the helicopter eliminates the margin needed to recover or autorotate to a safe landing.	->	A generally accepted minimum is sufficient altitude to complete a full autorotation to a safe landing—typically at least 1,500 feet AGL or higher depending on terrain and aircraft performance. Altitude matters because VRS can produce very high rates of descent (up to 2,000–4,000 feet per minute in severe cases) with an almost complete loss of collective effectiveness. Recovery requires both time and altitude; the actual minimum is driven by the combined distance needed for pilot recognition of the condition plus the altitude consumed during the recovery maneuver (typically collective pitch increase and forward cyclic input). Insufficient altitude beneath the helicopter eliminates the margin needed to execute recovery or autorotate to a safe landing. Specific minimums should be determined by the aircraft manufacturer's data and tested in training with qualified instructors.
AI_EDIT	PH.XVI.D.RM.005	The primary human factors risk is automation complacency and habit transfer. The pilot has built procedural habits relying on the governor to regulate RPM, so they may fail to actively monitor and manually manage throttle and collective in the new aircraft. Research shows accident rates are higher in early hours on a new aircraft type regardless of total experience, with risk decreasing significantly after 100 hours in type. To address this, the pilot should receive formal transition training with emphasis on manual RPM management, conduct thorough self-briefings before each flight in the new type, and establish personal minimums that limit high-demand flight phases until proficiency is built. Awareness of the 'error chain' concept — recognizing that complacency is one link that could lead to an accident — is also essential.	->	The primary human factors risk is automation complacency and habit transfer. The pilot has built procedural habits relying on the governor to regulate RPM, so they may fail to actively monitor and manually manage throttle and collective in the new aircraft. Aviation accident data and FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13 indicate that accident risk is elevated during the initial hours in a new aircraft type regardless of total experience, with proficiency improving significantly after 50–100 hours in type (industry guideline, not regulatory requirement). To address this, the pilot should receive formal transition training with emphasis on manual RPM management, conduct thorough self-briefings before each flight in the new type, and establish personal minimums that limit high-demand flight phases until proficiency is built. Awareness of the 'error chain' concept—recognizing that complacency is one link that could lead to an accident—is also essential.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.A.K1.005	The pilot must: (1) hold a valid medical certificate appropriate for the operation (at minimum a third-class for private operations, but a first- or second-class medical is required for commercial operations requiring it); (2) be authorized under the operator's Part 135 certificate or applicable operations specification if conducted for hire; (3) meet any applicable 14 CFR Part 135 pilot qualification requirements including recent flight time, PIC experience thresholds, and any required company check rides or training; (4) ensure the certificate, ratings, and medical are current and on their person; and (5) have completed any required ground or flight training for the specific helicopter make and model if required by Part 135 ops specs.	->	The pilot must: (1) hold a valid First Class or Second Class medical certificate (BasicMed is NOT acceptable for Part 135 commercial operations); (2) be authorized under the operator's Part 135 certificate and applicable operations specification; (3) meet all 14 CFR Part 135 pilot qualification requirements including recent flight time, PIC experience thresholds, and any required company check rides or training; (4) ensure the certificate, ratings, and medical are current and on their person; and (5) have completed any required ground or flight training for the specific helicopter make and model if required by Part 135 ops specs. [Note: If the flight were conducted under Part 91 by a private pilot without compensation, fewer requirements would apply, but accepting compensation for carrying passengers typically requires Part 135 certification or falls under 14 CFR 61.113 limitations.]
AI_EDIT	CH.I.A.K3.003	Conditions causing temporary disqualification include: (1) use of any medication that affects faculties contrary to safety — such as over-the-counter antihistamines or sedatives; (2) any illness that would interfere with safe performance, such as a head cold affecting equilibrium or sinus pressure; (3) blood donation within the past 72 hours; (4) scuba diving within the prescribed time limits before flight (generally 12–24 hours depending on dive profile); (5) alcohol consumption within 8 hours of flight or while having a blood alcohol content of 0.04% or greater.	->	Conditions causing temporary disqualification include: (1) use of any medication that affects faculties contrary to safety — such as over-the-counter antihistamines or sedatives; (2) any illness or injury that would interfere with safe performance, such as a head cold affecting equilibrium or sinus pressure; (3) impairment from alcohol consumption within 8 hours of flight or while having a blood alcohol content of 0.04% or greater; (4) scuba diving within the prescribed time limits before flight (generally 12–24 hours depending on dive profile); (5) fatigue or lack of sleep sufficient to impair judgment and safe control. Per 14 CFR 61.53, a pilot cannot act as PIC if unable to meet the medical standards for their certificate due to illness, medication effects, or any condition impairing faculties or safe performance of duties.
