AI_EDIT	CH.I.A.K4.005	No, you cannot conduct the flight without your physical pilot certificate. 14 CFR 61.3(a) requires a pilot to have their pilot certificate (or acceptable alternative) in their personal possession or readily accessible in the aircraft while acting as PIC. A photo on a phone is not an FAA-accepted substitute for the physical certificate. However, you may present an acceptable alternative: a temporary pilot certificate issued by the FAA, or you may contact the FAA to request verification. Some operators may accept a certified copy. The safest course of action is to retrieve the physical certificate or obtain a temporary certificate before flight. Flying without it could result in a violation.	->	No, you cannot legally conduct this flight. 14 CFR 61.3(a) requires the pilot certificate to be in the pilot's personal possession or readily accessible in the aircraft. A digital photo does not satisfy this requirement. Acceptable alternatives include a temporary certificate issued under 14 CFR 61.17 or airman verification from the FAA. Part 135 operators may have stricter OpSpec requirements. The compliant option is to retrieve the physical certificate or obtain a temporary certificate before flight.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.A.K5.005	This scenario requires careful analysis. Even if the pilot receives no direct payment, operating an aircraft in furtherance of a business or where compensation is involved for the flight can be considered compensation or hire. If the media company is paying for the helicopter specifically to conduct the flight, the operation is likely being conducted 'for compensation or hire' regardless of whether the pilot is personally paid. This would disqualify the use of BasicMed. The pilot should consult FAA guidance or legal counsel, and would likely need a valid Part 67 medical certificate and potentially a commercial certificate for this type of operation.	->	No, this operation is NOT permissible under BasicMed. When an aircraft is operated in furtherance of a business for which compensation is received—even if paid to the owner rather than the pilot—the operation is considered 'for compensation or hire' under 14 CFR 61.113(i). BasicMed authorizes only operations that are not 'for compensation or hire.' The pilot must hold a valid Part 67 medical certificate and operate under commercial privileges for this photo mission.
