AI_EDIT	CH.I.A.R2.006	The 'feels different' sensation is a critical red flag — it indicates the pilot's internalized mental model of how the helicopter responds does not match reality, which is a known precursor to loss of control. Specific hazard differences include: (1) Articulated rotors have lead/lag movement, which introduces Coriolis effect — blade velocity changes as the center of mass moves relative to the rotor axis — affecting rotor behavior during aggressive inputs in ways a semirigid-rotor pilot would not expect. (2) Semirigid systems have mast bumping risk under low-G unloaded conditions; articulated rotors have different structural concerns. (3) Vortex ring state entry and recovery cues may feel different. (4) Control harmonics and required input forces differ. The pilot should stop, seek type-specific training, and not rationalize the difference away — using the 'error chain' concept, this discomfort is a link in a chain that could lead to an accident if ignored.	->	The 'feels different' sensation is a critical red flag — it indicates the pilot's internalized mental model of how the helicopter responds does not match reality, which is a known precursor to loss of control. Specific hazard differences include: (1) Articulated rotors have lead/lag hinge movement that allows blade flapping and lead/lag in separate planes. This introduces different inertial coupling effects (Coriolis and gyroscopic coupling) compared to semirigid systems, affecting rotor response during aggressive inputs and during recovery from unusual attitudes. (2) Semirigid systems have mast bumping risk under low-G unloaded conditions; articulated rotors have different structural concerns. (3) Vortex ring state entry and recovery cues may feel different. (4) Control harmonics and required input forces differ. The pilot should stop, seek type-specific training, and not rationalize the difference away — using the 'error chain' concept, this discomfort is a link in a chain that could lead to an accident if ignored.
