AI_EDIT	CH.I.C.K1.007	Flight Service offers three types of preflight briefings: (1) Standard Briefing — the full comprehensive briefing requested when you have not received any previous briefing; (2) Abbreviated Briefing — requested when you need specific information only, or to update a previous briefing; (3) Outlook Briefing — for flights 6 or more hours in the future, provides general forecast information only. For your scenario — already airborne with a previous briefing on file — you would request an Abbreviated Briefing and specify that you need an update to your previous briefing, providing your current position, altitude, route, and destination. This allows the FSS specialist to give you only what has changed or what is new since your original briefing, which is the most efficient use of time in an EMS operational context. You could reach FSS by radio through an FSS remote communications outlet (RCO) or VOR voice capability, or by phone if you have cell service. In practice, many EMS crews also use cockpit weather datalinks (XM Weather, ADS-B weather via ForeFlight) for real-time updates, but these supplement — not replace — an official briefing.	->	Flight Service offers three types of preflight briefings: (1) Standard Briefing — the full comprehensive briefing requested when you have not received any previous briefing; (2) Abbreviated Briefing — requested when you need specific information only, or to update a previous briefing; (3) Outlook Briefing — for flights 6 or more hours in the future, provides general forecast information only. For your scenario — already airborne with a previous briefing on file — you would request an Abbreviated Briefing and specify that you need an update to your previous briefing, providing your current position, altitude, route, and destination. This allows the FSS specialist to give you only what has changed or what is new since your original briefing, which is the most efficient use of time in an EMS operational context. You