AI_EDIT	CH.I.D.R3.005	Several requirements and risk factors apply: (1) Mode C veil — within 30 NM of a Class B primary airport, you must have an operable Mode C transponder with altitude encoding. Verify your transponder is Mode C compliant and functioning correctly. (2) Class B airspace boundaries — confirm whether your planned route actually enters Class B airspace (not just passes near it). Class B entry requires an ATC clearance; you cannot enter without one. (3) Traffic density — Class B airspace surrounds the busiest airports; expect high-density IFR and VFR traffic. Plan for potential routing deviations by ATC. (4) Altitude reporting — remember that Mode C reports pressure altitude to ATC, not indicated altitude; your Kollsman window setting does not affect what ATC reads. (5) Communication — ensure you have the correct ATC frequencies and anticipate potential holds or reroutes.	->	Several requirements and risk factors apply: (1) Mode C veil — within 30 NM of a Class B primary airport, you must have an operable Mode C transponder with altitude encoding. Verify your transponder is Mode C compliant and functioning correctly. (2) Class B airspace boundaries — confirm whether your planned route actually enters Class B airspace (not just passes near it). Class B entry requires an ATC clearance; you cannot enter without one. (3) Traffic density — Class B airspace surrounds the busiest airports; expect high-density IFR and VFR traffic. Plan for potential routing deviations by ATC. (4) Altitude reporting — Mode C reports pressure altitude directly to ATC regardless of your Kollsman altimeter setting. Your local altimeter setting affects only what you read in the cockpit, not what ATC receives from your transponder. (5) Communication — ensure you have the correct ATC frequencies and anticipate potential holds or reroutes.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.D.R5.003	Filing an IFR flight plan does not mean ATC is tracking you until you receive and accept an ATC clearance and depart. Additionally, flight plans time out 2 hours after the proposed departure time if not activated. ATC is not watching for an overdue aircraft until you are reported missing — there is no automatic alert if you simply fail to depart or deviate without contact. Pilots must ensure the flight plan is activated and communicate any changes to departure time or routing to avoid a gap in awareness.	->	Filing an IFR flight plan creates a record for search and rescue purposes, but ATC does not actively monitor the flight for emergency unless you are actively in contact with ATC and fail to respond. Flight plans time out 2 hours after the proposed departure time if not activated. Search and rescue procedures are initiated when the aircraft is reported missing by the flight plan originator or others — not automatically by ATC. This means there is no automatic alert if you simply fail to depart, deviate without contact, or experience an emergency until someone actively reports you missing. Pilots must ensure the flight plan is activated, communicate any changes to departure time or routing, and understand that relying on a passive flight plan creates a gap in real-time awareness.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.D.R5.008	The specific limitation most pilots overlook: during a center-to-center handoff, there is a brief period where neither center has formally accepted radar identification of the aircraft, and communication on the new frequency has not yet been established. In practice, this means there is a window — potentially 30 seconds to several minutes depending on pilot response — where no ATC facility is actively providing separation services to that aircraft. At low altitudes, this coincides with potentially reduced radar coverage and higher terrain proximity. Risk evaluation during planning should include: (1) Checking the route for known communication dead zones using pilot reports, NOTAMs, or preflight briefing; (2) Ensuring navigation is fully self-contained for that segment and does not rely on ATC vectors; (3) Carrying the receiving center's frequency in advance so handoff can be accomplished quickly; (4) Verifying the MEA/MOCA for the transition segment guarantees obstacle clearance independently of ATC guidance; (5) Having a contingency altitude or escape route planned if radio contact cannot be established promptly. The deeper ADM principle from the Helicopter Flying Handbook applies: don't rely on something that will only 'probably' work — the handoff communication must be treated as an action item you actively manage, not one you passively wait for ATC to complete.	->	The specific limitation most pilots overlook: during a center-to-center handoff, the previous center releases radar identification once the new center accepts the handoff, but there is a window where you must switch to the new frequency. If you do not promptly check in on the new frequency, there is a period of reduced radar awareness and unclear separation responsibility. In practice, this window is typically brief but can extend if you miss the frequency change or experience radio difficulties. At low altitudes near terrain, this requires careful frequency management and confirmation of handoff completion. Risk evaluation during planning should include: (1) Checking the route for known communication dead zones using pilot reports, NOTAMs, or preflight briefing; (2) Ensuring navigation is fully self-contained for that segment and does not rely on ATC vectors; (3) Carrying the receiving center's frequency in advance so handoff can be accomplished quickly; (4) Verifying the MEA/MOCA for the transition segment guarantees obstacle clearance independently of ATC guidance; (5) Having a contingency altitude or escape route planned if radio contact cannot be established promptly. The deeper ADM principle applies: do not rely on something that will only 'probably' work — actively manage the handoff rather than passively waiting for ATC to complete it.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.D.R6.007	At high density altitude, a turbine helicopter may actually burn slightly less fuel in some regimes due to reduced power requirements for the same torque, but a piston helicopter will burn more fuel per hour of operation because the engine must run at a higher throttle position to maintain power against the thinner air — or may not achieve the same cruise speed, extending flight time. Additionally, the pilot should account for: (1) increased climb fuel burn, as more time may be spent climbing to altitude at reduced power margins; (2) reduced cruise groundspeed if the helicopter cannot achieve the same TAS, extending flight time; (3) possible deviations around weather adding distance; and (4) taxi and runup fuel not always included in POH charts. With exactly legal minimum fuel and these unaccounted factors, actual fuel exhaustion before reaching the alternate — or even the destination — becomes a real risk. Conservative fuel planning requires a personal minimum above the regulatory minimum.	->	At high density altitude, a piston helicopter engine must produce more power to maintain the same true airspeed against the thinner air, directly increasing fuel burn per hour. This means either the flight burns more total fuel at the same cruise speed, or if the pilot accepts reduced airspeed to conserve fuel, the flight takes longer and burns more total fuel anyway. The pilot should account for: (1) increased climb fuel burn, as the helicopter must spend more time climbing to altitude with reduced power margins; (2) reduced cruise groundspeed if unable to achieve the planned TAS, extending flight time and total fuel burn; (3) possible deviations around weather adding distance; and (4) taxi and runup fuel, which is not always included in POH charts. With exactly legal minimum fuel and these unaccounted factors, actual fuel exhaustion before reaching the alternate — or even the destination — becomes a real risk. Conservative fuel planning requires establishing a personal minimum fuel reserve above the regulatory minimum, especially in high density altitude conditions.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.D.S2.002	A NOTAM (Notice to Air Missions) is a notice containing information essential to personnel concerned with flight operations that was not known far enough in advance to be publicized by other means. The two primary types are: (1) FDC NOTAMs — issued by the National Flight Data Center for regulatory changes such as TFRs, instrument procedure amendments, and chart corrections; and (2) Domestic NOTAMs (NOTAM D) — issued for airport/heliport conditions, runway or taxiway closures, NAVAID outages, and airspace-related information. Pilots must check both before any IFR cross-country flight.	->	A NOTAM (Notice to Air Missions) is a notice containing information essential to personnel concerned with flight operations that was not known far enough in advance to be publicized by other means. The two primary types are: (1) FDC NOTAMs — issued by the National Flight Data Center for regulatory changes such as TFRs, instrument procedure amendments, and chart corrections; and (2) Domestic NOTAMs (NOTAM D) — issued for airport/heliport conditions, runway or taxiway closures, NAVAID outages, and airspace-related information. Pilots must check both before any cross-country flight per 14 CFR 91.103, whether VFR or IFR.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.D.S3.008	Filing a VFR flight plan creates a framework for search and rescue (SAR) — if the pilot fails to close the plan, FSS initiates notification procedures 30 minutes after the destination ETA, ultimately leading to SAR activation. If the pilot flew a completely different route than filed, SAR resources would be searching the wrong geographic area, potentially fatally delaying rescue. While there is no specific FAR that prohibits changing a VFR route in flight without notification (VFR pilots have freedom of flight), failing to update FSS undermines the only safety net the VFR flight plan provides. Best practice and ADM dictate that any significant route deviation should be relayed to FSS en route via radio or phone so the flight plan reflects reality. This is a workload management and risk management failure — the pilot's situational awareness did not extend to the SAR system that depends on accurate information.	->	No. BasicMed under 14 CFR Part 68 and 14 CFR 61.113(i) addresses medical certification only — it imposes no additional or different flight plan filing requirements. VFR flight plan procedures under 14 CFR 91.153 apply equally regardless of whether the pilot holds a standard FAA medical certificate or is operating under BasicMed. The actual BasicMed limitations are: max 12,500 lbs certificated takeoff weight and operations within the United States. BasicMed does not restrict altitude, airspeed, number of occupants, or distance, and turbine-powered aircraft are permitted. None of these constraints alter flight plan filing or amendment obligations.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.E.K1.003	Both Class C and Class D require two-way radio communication prior to entry — you do not need a clearance, but you must establish contact. The key difference is that Class C has radar service and requires a transponder with Mode C, while Class D typically does not have radar and does not require a transponder (though Mode C is required within 30 NM of a Class B primary airport). For Class D, if you call the tower and they respond with your call sign — even if they say 'standby' — two-way communication is established and you may enter. If you cannot reach ATC at a Class D airport, you may not enter without receiving a clearance or explicit instructions.	->	Both Class C and Class D require two-way radio communication prior to entry — you do not need a clearance, but you must establish contact and receive acknowledgment of your intention to enter. The key difference is that Class C has radar service and requires a transponder with Mode C, while Class D typically does not have radar and does not require a transponder (though Mode C is required within 30 NM of a Class B primary airport). For Class D, two-way communication is established when ATC responds to your call with your call sign and further instruction or clearance to enter. Simply hearing 'standby' does not constitute clearance to enter—you must receive explicit approval or instruction before crossing the Class D boundary. If you cannot establish two-way communication at all with the Class D tower, you may not enter the airspace.
