AI_EDIT	CH.I.E.K2.008	The bold 'T' (Takeoff Minimums) indicates that non-standard takeoff minimums or departure procedures (DPs) apply at that airport. Standard takeoff minimums for Part 91 operators are 1 statute mile visibility for single and twin-engine aircraft — if the 'T' appears, the published departure procedure or takeoff minimums must be checked in the TPP (Terminal Procedures Publication). For helicopter operations, specific helicopter departure procedures or climb gradients may apply. The bold 'A' (Alternate Minimums) indicates that either the airport is not authorized for use as an alternate under IFR, or that non-standard alternate minimums apply. Standard alternate minimums require a precision approach available with 600-2 (600 ft ceiling, 2 SM visibility) or a non-precision approach with 800-2 at the alternate. If 'A' appears, pilots must consult the alternate minimums section of the TPP before listing that airport as an alternate on their IFR flight plan. Ignoring these symbols could result in filing an invalid alternate or departing without meeting required visibility standards.	->	Part 91 operators are required to list an alternate when the destination forecast does not meet the '1-2-3 rule' (ceiling below 2,000 ft or visibility below 3 SM within 1 hour before or after ETA). While 14 CFR Part 91 does not explicitly mandate compliance with TPP alternate minimums in the same manner as Part 121/135, the alternate must still be 'adequate' under 14 CFR 91.169(c) to execute an instrument approach if needed. Selecting an alternate known to be below published minimums likely violates the adequacy standard, creates unacceptable risk, and represents failure to comply with 14 CFR 91.103 (preflight check of weather and alternatives). Professional practice and sound ADM require a realistic alternate that meets published minimums.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.E.K3.006	Major sporting events with 30,000 or more expected attendees trigger a TFR under 14 CFR 91.145. This TFR typically extends to 3 NM radius and up to 3,000 feet AGL, active one hour before through one hour after the event. Flight is prohibited within the TFR unless you're arriving or departing a local airport within the area, are operating under IFR and in contact with ATC, or have received specific ATC authorization. As a helicopter pilot, I should check NOTAMs well before departure—these TFRs are published as NOTAMs and are time-specific. I would plan a route that avoids the TFR or obtain an IFR clearance routing me through or around it with ATC coordination.	->	Major sporting events with 30,000 or more expected attendees trigger a TFR under 14 CFR 91.145. Stadium TFRs typically extend 3 NM radius from the stadium with vertical extent to 3,000 feet AGL, but exact dimensions are published in the specific NOTAM and may vary. The TFR is active one hour before through one hour after the event. Flight is prohibited within the TFR unless you are arriving or departing a local airport within the area, operating under IFR and in contact with ATC, or have received specific ATC authorization. As a helicopter pilot, I should check the TFR NOTAM well before departure and plan a route that either avoids the TFR or obtains IFR clearance with ATC routing and coordination.
ESCALATE	CH.I.E.K4.002	The answer claims a helicopter-specific SVFR exemption under 14 CFR 91.157(b)(1) that removes the 3 SM visibility requirement for day SVFR, but 14 CFR 91.157(c) establishes uniform SVFR minimums (1 SM visibility, clear of clouds) for all aircraft day and night; no helicopter exemption exists in the regulatory text and this conflates distinct operational privileges.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.E.K4.006	Certain airports are designated as 'No SVFR' airports, meaning ATC is prohibited from issuing SVFR clearances at those locations due to high traffic density or other operational reasons. These airports are listed in the Chart Supplement (formerly Airport/Facility Directory) with the notation 'NO SVFR.' They are also indicated on sectional charts with the symbol in the airport data block. The prohibition applies to fixed-wing aircraft but ATC cannot override this restriction. The pilot should have checked the Chart Supplement or sectional for the destination airport before flight to know SVFR was unavailable.	->	Certain airports are designated as 'No SVFR' airports, meaning ATC is prohibited from issuing SVFR clearances at those locations due to high traffic density or other operational reasons. This prohibition applies to ALL aircraft (fixed-wing and helicopters). These airports are listed in the Chart Supplement (formerly Airport/Facility Directory) with the notation 'NO SVFR.' Pilots must check the Chart Supplement before flight to determine whether SVFR is available at the destination airport.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.E.R1.006	The risk is both a serious regulatory violation (TFRs often involve security-sensitive areas with potential intercept by military aircraft) and a collision risk if the TFR involves active aerial operations such as firefighting or VIP movement. Actions: (1) Contact ATC immediately — if you're in radar contact, the controller may already have flagged it. Request a deviation or reroute. (2) Contact FSS (1-800-WX-BRIEF) via radio for current TFR information. (3) If equipped with a moving map or ADS-B In, check for TFR overlays in real time. (4) Do not proceed into the TFR hoping for the best — the Trescott Tip applies: if entry into the TFR will 'probably' be okay, that is not an acceptable standard; divert to a known-safe routing. (5) Document all ATC communications in case of later inquiry.	->	The risk is both a serious regulatory violation (TFRs often involve security-sensitive areas with potential intercept by military aircraft) and a collision risk if the TFR involves active aerial operations such as firefighting or VIP movement. Actions: (1) Contact ATC immediately — if you're in radar contact, the controller may already have flagged it; request a deviation or reroute. (2) Contact FSS (1-800-WX-BRIEF) via radio for current TFR information. (3) If equipped with a moving map or ADS-B In, check for TFR overlays in real time. (4) Do not proceed into the TFR without explicit clearance — apply conservative risk management: if uncertain about TFR status or boundaries, request alternative routing and do not enter. (5) Document all ATC communications in case of later inquiry.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.E.S2.008	At 18,000 feet MSL you enter Class A airspace. Class A requires: (1) an IFR clearance for all operations regardless of meteorological conditions — VFR flight is not permitted; (2) two-way radio communication with ATC; (3) a transponder with Mode C; and (4) for most operations, equipment capable of RVSM compliance above FL290 (if applicable). Your IFR flight plan does not change — if you were already operating IFR, your clearance continues. However, if you were operating VFR in Class E below 18,000, you would need an IFR clearance before entering Class A. Altimeter setting at or above 18,000 feet MSL is always set to 29.92 in Hg (standard), and altitude is expressed in Flight Levels rather than MSL feet. ATC communication shifts from approach/center as appropriate to ARTCC high-altitude sectors.	->	At 18,000 feet MSL and above, you enter Class A airspace. Class A requires: (1) an IFR clearance for all operations regardless of meteorological conditions — VFR flight is not permitted; (2) two-way radio communication with ATC; (3) a transponder with Mode C; and (4) RVSM certification for operations above FL290 in RVSM airspace (which is mandatory in most of the continental U.S. and many other regions). Non-RVSM aircraft are prohibited from RVSM airspace without a specific exemption. Your IFR flight plan does not change — if you were already operating IFR, your clearance continues. However, if you were operating VFR in Class E below 18,000 feet MSL, you would need an IFR clearance before entering Class A. At and above 18,000 feet MSL, altimeter setting is always set to 29.92 in Hg (standard), and altitude is expressed in Flight Levels rather than MSL feet. ATC communication shifts from approach/center as appropriate to ARTCC high-altitude sectors.
