AI_EDIT	CH.I.F.R7.008	Blade flapping is the rotor system's mechanism to compensate for the velocity asymmetry between advancing and retreating blades — the advancing blade flaps up (reducing its AOA and effective lift), and the retreating blade flaps down (increasing its AOA to maintain lift). At moderate airspeeds, this is highly effective at equalizing lift across the disk. However, at high airspeeds, blade flapping becomes part of the problem: as the retreating blade flaps down to generate more lift, it must increase its AOA substantially. At very high forward speeds, the retreating blade can only flap down so far — it eventually reaches its maximum mechanical flapping limit AND its aerodynamic critical AOA simultaneously. Additionally, the retreating blade tip, which is moving slowest relative to the air, stalls first (per the HFH description that vortex ring/blade stall starts near the hub in VRS but in retreating blade stall, the blade stall characteristically begins near the tip of the retreating blade where the velocity is already reduced). So the very mechanism designed to protect the rotor system becomes overwhelmed at extreme airspeeds. This is precisely why VNE exists — it defines the upper boundary where the flapping system can no longer compensate adequately.	->	Blade flapping is the rotor system's mechanism to compensate for the velocity asymmetry between advancing and retreating blades — the advancing blade flaps up (reducing its AOA and effective lift), and the retreating blade flaps down (increasing its AOA to maintain lift). At moderate airspeeds, this is highly effective at equalizing lift across the disk. However, at high airspeeds, blade flapping becomes part of the problem: as the retreating blade flaps down to generate more lift, it must increase its AOA substantially. At very high forward speeds, the retreating blade can only flap down so far — it eventually reaches its maximum mechanical flapping limit AND its aerodynamic critical AOA simultaneously. The retreating blade tip, which already has the lowest velocity relative to the air in the advancing direction, must increase its AOA substantially to maintain lift; this high AOA on the low-velocity retreating blade tip exceeds the critical AOA first, initiating stall. So the very mechanism designed to protect the rotor system becomes overwhelmed at extreme airspeeds. This is precisely why VNE exists — it defines the upper boundary where the flapping system can no longer compensate adequately. (Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 2, Rotor System Aerodynamics and Mechanics.)
