AI_EDIT	CH.I.F.S1.006	Because the fuel tanks are located forward of the datum, the fuel has a negative or smaller positive arm. As that fuel burns off, you are removing weight from a forward location, which causes the CG to shift aft. This could move the CG toward or potentially beyond the aft limit by the time you reach the destination. Before departing, I must recompute the weight and balance at the fuel load expected for the second leg. I subtract the weight of fuel burned (180 lbs × fuel arm = moment reduction) and recalculate CG for the new gross weight. I then check the new CG against the RFM envelope to confirm it remains within limits for the entire second leg, accounting for additional fuel burn on that leg as well. If the CG goes out of limits, I must redistribute load or reduce fuel before departing.	->	Fuel tank location varies by aircraft type. If fuel tanks are located forward of the CG datum, burning fuel removes weight from that forward location, causing the CG to shift aft. If fuel tanks are located aft of the CG datum, burning fuel removes weight from that aft location, causing the CG to shift forward. In either case, the CG can drift toward or potentially beyond the aft (or forward) limit by the destination. Before departing the first stop, I must recompute the weight and balance for the fuel load expected at the second leg: I subtract the weight of fuel burned (180 lbs × fuel arm from the RFM = moment reduction) and recalculate CG for the new gross weight. I then check the new CG against the RFM envelope to confirm it remains within limits for the entire second leg, accounting for additional fuel burn on that leg as well. If the CG goes out of limits, I must redistribute load or reduce fuel before departing. (Note: Consult the specific RFM/POH for tank locations and arm values for your aircraft.)
AI_EDIT	CH.I.F.S2.006	The published range figures assume zero wind, so a headwind of 15 knots directly reduces your effective groundspeed and therefore your usable range — you cover less distance per gallon consumed. Endurance (time aloft) does not change with headwind since it depends on fuel flow and fuel on board, not groundspeed. To correct the range figure: determine your true airspeed from the table, subtract 15 knots to get groundspeed, then recalculate range using (groundspeed / TAS) × published range. You should also add a fuel reserve per 14 CFR 91.151 (VFR) or 91.167 (IFR) and plan your fuel stops accordingly. Never use no-wind range charts as your actual usable range in headwind conditions without applying the groundspeed correction.	->	The published range figures assume zero wind, so a headwind directly reduces your effective groundspeed and therefore your usable range — you cover less distance per gallon consumed. Endurance (time aloft) does not change with headwind since it depends on fuel flow and fuel on board, not groundspeed. To correct the range figure: determine your true airspeed from the table, subtract 15 knots to get groundspeed, then recalculate range using (actual groundspeed / published TAS) × published range. Alternatively, use actual groundspeed and fuel consumption rate to calculate distance = (groundspeed / fuel consumption per hour) × fuel available. You should also add a fuel reserve per 14 CFR 91.151 (VFR) or 91.167 (IFR) and plan your fuel stops accordingly. Never use no-wind range charts as your actual usable range in headwind conditions without applying the groundspeed correction.
