AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.K2.001	A white-light flashlight is recommended for night preflight inspections. Blue-green or red lens flashlights do not provide adequate color differentiation, making it very difficult to detect oil or hydraulic fluid leaks and accurately assess fluid levels. White light shows true colors and allows you to spot abnormalities you would otherwise miss.	->	A white-light flashlight is essential for night preflight inspections because white light shows true colors, allowing you to detect oil or hydraulic fluid leaks and accurately assess fluid levels—tasks where color differentiation is critical. For in-flight night operations, however, pilots should use red or blue-green lens flashlights to minimize disruption to dark adaptation and preserve night vision, as these colors are less disruptive to the eyes in low-light conditions.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.K2.002	Essential personal equipment for night helicopter flight includes: (1) a spare flashlight or backup light source in case the primary fails, (2) current aeronautical charts or approach plates for the intended route, including any relevant terminal procedures, (3) a personal communication device (cell phone or handheld radio) as a backup for in-flight emergencies. Additional items commonly recommended are a kneeboard for note-taking in reduced lighting, extra batteries for any battery-powered equipment, and headlamp for hands-free use during any precautionary or forced landing.	->	Essential personal items for night helicopter flight include: (1) a primary flashlight with white lens for preflight and red or blue-green lens for in-flight use, plus extra batteries; (2) a backup light source such as a headlamp or secondary flashlight for hands-free lighting if needed during an emergency landing; (3) a personal communication device (cell phone or handheld radio) as a backup for in-flight emergencies. Additional recommended personal carry items include a kneeboard for note-taking and a small chart case, while sectional charts and approach plates should be organized and accessible in the cockpit.
