AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.K4.003	Obstruction lighting uses red or white lights depending on height and type. Structures up to 500 feet AGL are typically marked with steady red lights or red flashing aviation obstruction lights. Structures above 500 feet AGL (high-intensity) use flashing white strobe lights during the day and may switch to flashing red or steady red at night to reduce glare. Medium-intensity dual-lighting systems may use both white strobes (day) and red lights (night). In general, steady red indicates the structure is marked but not exceptionally tall, while flashing lights (red or white) signal a significant or high-intensity hazard. Pilots should check sectional charts and NOTAMs for specific obstruction data along their route.	->	Structures up to 500 feet AGL typically use steady RED lights. Structures above 500 feet AGL use HIGH-INTENSITY WHITE STROBES during daylight hours, with RED LIGHTS (steady or flashing) during night hours; some use dual systems for day/night transition. The light color and flash pattern depend primarily on structure height and time of day, not on severity. Flashing white strobes are high-intensity day lights on very tall structures (typically 500+ feet). All obstruction lights should be treated as significant hazards and avoided. Check sectional charts and NOTAMs for specific obstruction details.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.K4.006	Without active taxiway lighting, you must slow to a near-stop after runway exit and use your landing/searchlight and taxi lights to visually clear your path before every movement. You should contact ground control (if available) or use the airport diagram to confirm your position before taxiing. At night, blue taxiway edge lights and green centerline lights are the primary means of taxiway identification — if none are illuminated, do not assume the surfaces are clear or that you can safely navigate at normal taxi speed. You may request that the FSS or UNICOM operator activate airport lighting if PCL is available. Regulatory context: 14 CFR 91.129 and 91.130 govern operations at controlled airports; FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 12 stresses that pilots must be especially cautious on unfamiliar airports at night due to the absence of lighting cues they rely on during day operations.	->	Without active taxiway lighting, slow to a near-stop after runway exit and use your landing/searchlight and taxi lights to visually clear your path before every movement. Contact ground control (if available) or use the airport diagram to confirm your position. Blue taxiway edge lights and green centerline lights are controlled by the airfield's centralized system (tower-controlled or PCL)—you cannot activate them individually. Request that ATC or UNICOM activate the system via PCL or manual control. If taxiway lighting remains off, perform very slow taxi using your own lights and the airport diagram, cross-checking position frequently. Regulatory context: 14 CFR 91.129 and 91.130 govern operations at controlled airports; FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 12 stresses caution on unfamiliar airports at night due to limited visual cues.
