AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.R3.003	Factors that degrade night vision include: (1) Vitamin A or C deficiency, (2) carbon monoxide exposure — even from cigarette smoking, (3) alcohol consumption, (4) hypoxia at altitude, and (5) smoking. Environmental factors include bright lights before or during flight that destroy dark adaptation. Any of these conditions reduces the pilot's ability to detect outside terrain and obstacles, increasing risk of spatial disorientation and loss of situational awareness.	->	Physiological factors that degrade night vision include: (1) Vitamin A deficiency (the primary nutritional factor affecting night vision); (2) carbon monoxide exposure — even from cigarette smoking; (3) alcohol consumption; and (4) hypoxia at altitude. Environmental factors include bright lights before or during flight that destroy dark adaptation, and fatigue. Any of these conditions reduces the pilot's ability to detect outside terrain and obstacles, increasing risk of spatial disorientation and loss of situational awareness. [FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 12]
AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.R6.002	Carbon monoxide (CO) reduces the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity, which directly degrades night vision because rod cells in the eye are highly sensitive to hypoxia. Even small amounts of CO can significantly impair dark adaptation and reduce the pilot's ability to detect terrain, obstacles, and other aircraft at night. Weather-related situations that increase CO risk include cold weather operations when pilots may be tempted to run cabin heat at high settings using engine bleed air systems (if applicable), or operating with doors closed in cold conditions with possible exhaust leaks. Poor ventilation combined with cold weather creates a higher risk. Pilots should be alert to symptoms like headache and drowsiness, which can be masked by weather-related fatigue.	->	Carbon monoxide (CO) reduces the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity, which directly degrades night vision because rod cells in the eye are highly sensitive to hypoxia. Even small amounts of CO can significantly impair dark adaptation and reduce the pilot's ability to detect terrain, obstacles, and other aircraft at night. Weather-related situations that increase CO risk include cold weather operations in helicopters equipped with cabin heaters that use engine exhaust or bleed air systems, or any operations with marginal ventilation combined with possible exhaust system leaks. Poor ventilation combined with cold weather creates a higher risk across all helicopter types. Pilots should be alert to symptoms like headache, dizziness, and drowsiness, which can be masked by weather-related fatigue. [FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 12]
AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.R6.005	This scenario illustrates several ADM failures. First, the pilot is exhibiting plan continuation bias — focusing on current conditions rather than trend analysis. A temperature-dewpoint spread of 2°C or less with calm winds is a textbook fog formation signature, especially in the hours after sunset when surface cooling accelerates. Second, the pilot is using the word 'probably' in their reasoning — it's technically VFR 'right now' probably won't change — which the FAA explicitly cautions against (Trescott Tip: if an action will only 'probably' work out, choose an option known to work). Third, the pilot is not accounting for the specific night-weather risk that fog forming en route will be nearly invisible from the cockpit, unlike daytime fog which has visible contrast. Fourth, no alternate plan or go/no-go criteria appear to have been established. The correct action is to delay the flight or establish clear divert criteria and ensure instrument currency before proceeding, because inadvertent IMC at low altitude in a helicopter at night is nearly unsurvivable.	->	This scenario illustrates several ADM failures. First, the pilot is exhibiting plan continuation bias — focusing on current conditions rather than trend analysis. A temperature-dewpoint spread of 2°C or less with calm winds is a textbook fog formation signature, especially in the hours after sunset when surface cooling accelerates. Second, the pilot is using optimism bias — it's technically VFR 'right now,' but this does not account for deterioration. Per FAA-H-8083-2A, hazardous attitudes in decision-making often involve assuming 'probably' outcomes will suffice when a known, safer alternative exists. Third, the pilot is not accounting for the specific night-weather risk that fog forming en route will be nearly invisible from the cockpit, unlike daytime fog which has visible contrast against terrain or sky. Fourth, no alternate plan or go/no-go criteria appear to have been established. The correct action is to delay the flight or establish clear divert criteria and ensure instrument currency before proceeding, because inadvertent IMC at low altitude in a helicopter at night presents extreme risk requiring immediate corrective action and poses severe safety hazards.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.R7.002	Under 14 CFR 91.205(c), for night VFR flight a helicopter must be equipped with: approved position lights (anti-collision lighting system if required by airworthiness cert), an adequate source of electrical energy for all installed electrical and radio equipment, and one spare set of fuses or three spare fuses of each kind required. Additionally, the position lights must be operational at night per 14 CFR 91.209.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.205(c), for night VFR flight a helicopter must be equipped with: (1) approved position lights; (2) an approved anticollision light system if required by the aircraft's type certificate or operations specifications; (3) an adequate source of electrical energy for all installed electrical and radio equipment; and (4) one spare set of fuses or three spare fuses of each kind required. Position lights must be operational, and if the aircraft is equipped with an anticollision light system, it must also be operational during night operations per 14 CFR 91.209.
AI_EDIT	CH.I.I.S1.006	Night scanning uses a stop-turn-stop technique rather than a smooth continuous sweep. You divide the visual field into sectors of approximately 30 degrees wide. At each stop, you pause for no more than 2 to 3 seconds to allow your eyes to detect movement or objects using peripheral rod vision. You then move to the next sector with a brief turn, overlapping each sector by approximately 10 degrees to ensure no gaps in coverage. The key differences from daytime scanning are the use of off-center viewing (looking about 10 degrees to the side of where you want to see), shorter pause times since the eyes detect movement more effectively at night, and deliberate avoidance of staring directly at any one point.	->	Night scanning uses a stop-turn-stop technique rather than a smooth continuous sweep. Divide the visual field into sectors of approximately 30 degrees wide. At each stop, pause for 2 to 3 seconds to allow your eyes to detect movement and objects using peripheral rod vision, which is more sensitive to low light and motion than central cone vision. Move to the next sector with a brief turn, overlapping each sector by approximately 10 degrees to ensure no gaps in coverage. The key differences from daytime scanning are: (1) use off-center viewing — look about 10 degrees to the side of where you want to focus, since rods are concentrated in the retina's periphery; (2) deliberately pause longer at each stop to allow rod receptors time to detect subtle movement and contrast; and (3) avoid staring directly at any one point for extended periods, as this shifts focus to the less light-sensitive cone cells at the center of the retina.
