AI_EDIT	CH.II.A.K4.001	The four main environmental factors are: (1) Weather — current and forecast conditions along the route including ceilings, visibility, winds, icing, and turbulence; (2) Terrain — elevation, terrain features, and proximity to high ground or obstacles below the MEA/MOCA; (3) Route selection — chosen airways, waypoints, and alternates that provide obstacle clearance and navigational signal coverage; and (4) Obstructions — towers, powerlines, antennas, and other man-made or natural obstacles, particularly relevant during low-altitude helicopter operations. These factors must be assessed together to form a complete picture of the operating environment.	->	14 CFR 91.103 requires the PIC to become familiar with all available information relevant to the flight. Key environmental factors to assess include: (1) Weather — current and forecast conditions along the route including ceilings, visibility, winds, icing, and turbulence; (2) Terrain — elevation, terrain features, and proximity to high ground ensuring compliance with MEA/MOCA; (3) Route selection — chosen airways, waypoints, and alternates that provide obstacle clearance and navigational coverage; and (4) Obstructions — towers, powerlines, antennas, and other obstacles, critical during low-altitude helicopter operations. These form the foundation of preflight planning and risk assessment.
