AI_EDIT	CH.II.A.S2.006	A field approval (documented on FAA Form 337) means a major repair or major alteration was performed that was not covered by the manufacturer's standard data. This is legal and the aircraft can still conform to its type design if done correctly. To verify airworthiness, you should: (1) Confirm the Form 337 is signed off by both the performing mechanic and an FAA Aviation Safety Inspector (or DER) as required; (2) Verify the repair described is consistent with what you observe on the aircraft; (3) Check that the Form 337 copy is retained in the aircraft permanent records; (4) Confirm no related ADs have been issued that would conflict with the repair; (5) Ensure the logbook return-to-service entry references the Form 337 and states the aircraft is airworthy. As PIC, you are ultimately responsible under 14 CFR 91.7.	->	A field approval (documented on FAA Form 337) means a major repair or major alteration was performed that was not covered by the manufacturer's standard data. This is legal and the aircraft can still conform to its type design if done correctly. To verify airworthiness, you should: (1) Confirm the Form 337 is properly completed with clear descriptions, dates, mechanic/technician credentials, and evidence of appropriate approval authority (FAA ASI or DER approval as required by the repair type); (2) Verify the repair described is consistent with what you observe on the aircraft; (3) Check that a copy of the completed Form 337 is retained in the aircraft permanent records per 14 CFR 43.9; (4) Confirm no related ADs have been issued that would conflict with the repair; (5) Ensure the logbook return-to-service entry references the Form 337 and states the aircraft is airworthy per 14 CFR 91.7. Note: Approval authority depends on repair type and certificate holder — for Part 91 operators, some major repairs may be filed after the fact, while certain alterations require pre-approval. As PIC, you are ultimately responsible under 14 CFR 91