AI_EDIT	CH.II.A.S3.005	This pilot should NOT fly. While they may feel 'fine,' diphenhydramine (Benadryl) is a first-generation antihistamine with a half-life of 4–8 hours and known residual cognitive and psychomotor impairing effects that can persist 12–24 hours after ingestion — often beyond the point where the pilot feels subjectively drowsy. The FAA specifically lists diphenhydramine as a medication that does not meet medical standards for flight. Under 14 CFR 61.53, if the underlying condition (allergies) would not disqualify the pilot, the medication itself can. The AME community and FAA guidance require that medications be 'off label' for a period of at least five half-lives before flight, which would not be met at 10 hours. Honest self-assessment means not relying on how you feel subjectively — residual impairment is the hidden hazard. The pilot should ground themselves and consult an Aviation Medical Examiner (AME) or use the FAA's 'Do I Need an AME?' resources if uncertain.	->	This pilot should NOT fly. While they may feel 'fine,' diphenhydramine (Benadryl) is a first-generation antihistamine with a half-life of 4–8 hours and known residual cognitive and psychomotor impairing effects that can persist 12–24 hours after ingestion — often beyond the point where the pilot feels subjectively drowsy. The FAA lists diphenhydramine in guidance as a commonly disqualifying medication due to its impairing effects on cognition and coordination. Under 14 CFR 61.53 and 91.17, any medication that impairs faculties or judgment is disqualifying for flight, even if the underlying condition would not be. Honest self-assessment means not relying on subjective drowsiness — residual impairment is a hidden hazard. The pilot should ground themselves and consult an Aviation Medical Examiner (AME) before flight, or use the FAA's 'Do I Need an AME?' resources if uncertain. Reference: 14 CFR 61.53; 14 CFR 91.17; FAA Advisory Circular AC 67-2D 'Medical Facts for Pilots.'
