AI_EDIT	CH.IV.B.K3.008	Yellow chevron markings indicate a non-movement area or unusable pavement — the surface is not approved for aircraft operations (e.g., blast pads, overrun areas, or closed taxiway sections). A helicopter pilot should not hover over or use chevron-marked areas for taxi routing. The ATC hold short instruction requires stopping before the runway 18L hold short marking. The wake turbulence caution from a Boeing 737 (a large transport category aircraft) is significant for a helicopter because rotor wash and fixed-wing wake vortices can cause rapid, uncontrollable roll inputs and loss of rotor RPM if the helicopter is positioned in the vortex path. The pilot should establish a stable hover well behind the hold short line — away from the chevron area — and be aware that wake vortices from the landing 737 will settle to the ground and may drift laterally toward the hover taxi path, particularly in light crosswind conditions. Hovering or hover taxiing in close proximity to the runway environment during a large aircraft landing increases risk of vortex encounter.	->	Yellow chevron markings indicate a non-movement area or unusable pavement — the surface is not approved for routine aircraft operations (e.g., blast pads, overrun areas, or closed taxiway sections). A helicopter pilot should normally avoid hovering over or using chevron-marked areas for taxi routing unless specifically cleared by ATC or required by operational necessity. The ATC hold short instruction requires stopping before the runway 18L hold short marking. The wake turbulence caution from a Boeing 737 (a large transport category aircraft) is significant for a helicopter because rotor wash and fixed-wing wake vortices can cause rapid, uncontrollable roll inputs and loss of rotor RPM if the helicopter is positioned in the vortex path. The pilot should establish a stable hover well behind the hold short line — away from the chevron area — and be aware that wake vortices from the landing 737 will settle to the ground and may drift laterally toward the hover taxi path, particularly in light crosswind conditions. Hovering or hover taxiing in close proximity to the runway environment during a large aircraft landing increases risk of vortex encounter. No fixed FAA time standard exists for helicopter crossings after large aircraft operations; the pilot should await ATC clearance and apply conservative judgment informed by wind conditions and aircraft type.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.B.K4.008	In calm wind conditions, a right yaw tendency requiring left pedal input is consistent with what is expected — for a counterclockwise main rotor system (most American helicopters), torque causes a right yaw tendency, and left (anti-torque) pedal is the normal correction. However, if the yaw tendency is unexpectedly strong or increasing, this could indicate: (1) the helicopter is entering or approaching effective translational lift (ETL) during hover taxi at approximately 16-24 knots ground speed, altering rotor efficiency and torque characteristics; (2) a localized thermal or surface wind shift not captured by the windsock — hot ramp surfaces can create rising air currents that affect rotor inflow asymmetrically; (3) a mechanical issue with the tail rotor system (less likely but must be considered). This illustrates that visual indicators give a baseline environmental picture, but the pilot must also continuously monitor the helicopter's actual flight characteristics as a real-time wind indicator. The aircraft's own behavior — power required, pedal position, drift tendency — is one of the most reliable and immediate indicators of changing wind conditions during hover taxi.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.B.K5.004	Under 14 CFR 91.209(b), the anti-collision light must be on when the engine is running. However, 14 CFR 91.213 addresses inoperative equipment. You must check the helicopter's MEL (if one exists) or the POH/AFM equipment list to determine if flight is permissible with this item inoperative. If no MEL exists and the beacon is required equipment, the helicopter is not airworthy and you should not continue flight operations. Additionally, since you are already airborne hover taxiing, the safest course is to land at the nearest safe area, secure the aircraft, and have the beacon repaired before resuming flight. You should also notify ATC or ground control of your situation.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.209(b), the anti-collision light must be on when the engine is running, making it a required piece of equipment. Under 14 CFR 91.213, before flight, you must verify that all required equipment is operative using the helicopter's MEL (if one exists) or the POH/AFM equipment list. If no MEL exists and the beacon is required equipment, the helicopter is not airworthy and flight operations cannot commence. If the beacon failed during flight operations, you are technically in violation of 91.209(b) and 91.213(d). The safest and most legally compliant action is to land immediately at the nearest suitable location without further flight. Notify ATC or ground control of your situation. Do not continue hover taxi to a distant parking area; instead, land at the nearest safe spot to minimize continued non-compliance. After landing, have the beacon repaired before resuming any flight operations.
