AI_EDIT	CH.IV.B.S11.007	In a helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor (most American helicopters), left pedal is used to initiate and sustain a right turn (reducing tail rotor thrust). This requires the student to actively reduce anti-torque input. The two most likely causes of overshooting right turns are: (1) The student is not applying left pedal early enough to arrest the turn — they may be relying on the natural torque tendency to decelerate the right turn, not realizing the helicopter's momentum continues the rotation; (2) The student's left foot may be less responsive or coordinated than the right, a common asymmetry. Additionally, in a right turn, as the nose passes through the tailwind condition the helicopter may temporarily accelerate due to reduced wind resistance, catching the student off guard. In training, I would have the student practice slow, controlled right turns at varying speeds, specifically focusing on anticipating the stop 20–30° early and actively pressing left pedal to arrest momentum. Using a specific ground reference point helps the student time the deceleration input.	->	In a helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor — standard for most North American helicopters (Robinson R22/R44, Bell 206, Schweizer, etc.) — left pedal input reduces tail rotor thrust and permits the nose to yaw right. The two most likely causes of overshooting right turns are: (1) The student is not applying left pedal early enough to arrest the turn; they may be passively waiting for natural torque tendencies to decelerate the rotation, not recognizing that the helicopter's angular momentum continues the yaw past the target heading; (2) The student's left foot may be less responsive or coordinated than the right — a common neuromuscular asymmetry that affects pedal timing and magnitude. Additionally, as the nose passes through tailwind during a right turn, temporary wind resistance reduction may accelerate the rotation, catching the student unprepared. In training, focus the student on slow, controlled right turns with specific ground references, emphasizing early anticipation of the stop point (20–30° before target) and active left pedal input to arrest momentum. For pilots transitioning to clockwise-rotor systems (certain Airbus/Eurocopter models), the asymmetry would shift to left turns.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.B.S12.006	The increase in hover height is likely due to a localized area of increased ground effect or a thermal column rising from the hot asphalt, which momentarily increases lift. The correct response is a smooth, small reduction in collective to recapture the desired hover altitude (typically 2–5 feet AGL or as required for the maneuver). Simultaneously, small pedal adjustments may be needed to compensate for the torque change resulting from the reduced collective. The cyclic should be kept steady unless drift begins. Abrupt collective reduction should be avoided to prevent a hard landing.	->	The increase in hover height is primarily due to a thermal updraft—warm air rising from the hot asphalt surface—which temporarily increases the effective lift available to the aircraft. The correct response is a smooth, small reduction in collective to recapture the desired hover altitude (typically 2–5 feet AGL or as required for the maneuver). Small pedal adjustments may be needed to compensate for the torque change resulting from the reduced collective. The cyclic should be kept steady unless drift begins. Abrupt collective reduction should be avoided to prevent a hard landing.
