AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.K5.006	No, the ground crew member should be cautious — seeing a green light on their right means the helicopter's right side (green) is facing them, indicating the helicopter is flying toward them or across their path from left to right. Green is on the right side of the aircraft; red is on the left. If you see only a red light, the aircraft is heading across your path from right to left. If you see both red and green, it is heading directly toward you. This knowledge is critical for both pilots and ground crew situational awareness.	->	No, the ground crew member should be cautious. If they see a green light, the helicopter's right wing is visible to them, indicating the aircraft is approaching from a specific direction. Remember: green light is always on the right wing; red light is always on the left wing. If ground crew see only a green light, they know the right side is toward them; if only red, the left side is toward them; if both red and green are visible, the helicopter is heading directly toward them. This knowledge is critical for both pilots and ground crew situational awareness.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.K6.006	When overflying a runway during air taxi at a towered airport, ATC must explicitly authorize the overflight — it is not implied by a standard taxi clearance. ATC may clear a helicopter to overfly a runway at a specific altitude. The pilot should fly high enough to clear any aircraft that may be on the runway or in the initial climb, but should avoid entering the traffic pattern altitude without further clearance. Pilots must also be aware of wake turbulence from departing or arriving jet aircraft on or near the runway being overflown, and should avoid flying directly in their wake. AIM recommends helicopters avoid the runway surface by at least 100 feet AGL when overflying.	->	When overflying a runway during air taxi at a towered airport, ATC must explicitly authorize the overflight—it is not implied by a standard taxi clearance. ATC may clear a helicopter to overfly a runway at a specific altitude. The pilot should maintain clearance of any aircraft on the runway or in initial climb, and must be aware of wake turbulence from departing or arriving aircraft (see AIM 7-3-2 through 7-3-6). Best practice and the Helicopter Flying Handbook recommend maintaining at least 100 feet AGL when overflying to ensure safe clearance, though compliance with all ATC clearances and 14 CFR 91.129 is the regulatory requirement.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.R2.005	FAA guidance in the Helicopter Flying Handbook states that tripped circuit breakers should not be reset by the pilot before flight — they should be left for maintenance personnel to evaluate. Attempting to reset a popped breaker may re-energize a shorted or overloaded circuit and cause a more serious electrical failure or fire. Proceeding without a required position light at night violates 14 CFR 91.209, which requires aircraft to display position lights from sunset to sunrise. The operational risk is that other aircraft and ground personnel cannot reliably see or distinguish your helicopter's direction of travel during the air taxi.	->	The pilot should consult the aircraft's POH for circuit breaker reset procedures—some aircraft allow crew reset of non-essential circuit breakers, while others do not. If the cause of the trip is unknown (potential short or overload), resetting without investigation risks re-energizing a failed circuit and causing electrical fire. The aircraft is unairworthy per 14 CFR 91.7 if a required position light is inoperative. Proceeding without that light at night violates 14 CFR 91.209 and makes night flight illegal. The safest action is not to attempt reset if unsure; stop the air taxi and request maintenance evaluation before flight.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.R5.005	The H/V diagram in most RFMs is published for the most critical conditions — typically maximum gross weight and sea level standard day — and may include corrections for density altitude in some models. At reduced gross weight, rotor inertia is proportionally lower relative to the aircraft mass, but power margins and performance are generally improved. At high density altitude, the main concern is increased rate of descent during autorotation and reduced rotor RPM recovery performance due to thinner air. High density altitude effectively expands the avoid region because the helicopter descends faster and rotor RPM decays more quickly. Unless the RFM provides specific density altitude corrections to the H/V diagram, the conservative approach is to treat the avoid region as potentially larger at high density altitude, and plan air taxi profiles accordingly.	->	The H/V diagram is typically published for the most critical conditions—maximum gross weight and sea level standard day. At reduced gross weight, absolute rotor inertia remains unchanged, but the mass-to-inertia ratio improves, which generally enhances autorotation capability and may actually shrink the avoid region. However, at high-density altitude, thinner air reduces rotor blade effectiveness, increasing rate of descent and rotor RPM decay. The avoid region may effectively expand due to density altitude effects. Unless the RFM provides specific corrections for density altitude, apply the published diagram conservatively and treat the avoid region as potentially larger at high-density altitude. Plan air taxi profiles accordingly.
