AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.S2.001	When an airport diagram or taxi chart is published for that airport, the pilot should use it during taxi operations to maintain situational awareness. FAA guidance emphasizes using all available resources, including published diagrams, to prevent runway incursions and maintain awareness of position relative to runways, taxiways, and other aircraft.	->	When operating at an airport with ATC services, pilots must comply with ATC clearances and instructions (14 CFR 91.123), which may require use of airport diagrams to correctly identify taxiways and runways. At uncontrolled airports, use of published airport diagrams is a best practice recommended by FAA guidance (AC 91-73B) to maintain situational awareness and prevent runway incursions, but is not a regulatory requirement. When an ATC clearance references specific taxiways or runways, the pilot is required to use available airport diagrams to execute the clearance correctly.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.S3.001	The recommended air taxi altitude is between 3 and 5 feet AGL — high enough to clear ground obstacles, taxiway markings, and other aircraft, but low enough to land immediately if power is lost. This range keeps the helicopter within effective translational lift height considerations while minimizing exposure to vortex ring state risk and allowing quick ground contact in an emergency.	->	The recommended air taxi altitude is typically 0–5 feet AGL, with the exact altitude selected based on aircraft performance, density altitude, surface obstacles, and wind conditions. This range is safe because it minimizes exposure to the height-velocity (H-V) diagram's avoid zone by allowing immediate ground contact if power is lost, while maintaining adequate clearance from ground hazards and personnel.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.S3.003	The H-V diagram defines combinations of height and airspeed from which a safe autorotation cannot be completed following engine failure — these are the 'avoid' zones. A typical hover taxi at 3–5 feet AGL and very low forward speed falls in the low-altitude avoid zone of the H-V diagram, which is why power reliability is critical during air taxi. Pilots must be aware that if power fails in this region, there is insufficient altitude to enter a successful autorotation, making immediate collective reduction and ground contact the only option.	->	The H-V diagram defines combinations of height and airspeed from which a safe autorotation cannot be completed following engine failure—these are the 'avoid' zones. A typical hover taxi at very low altitude (0–5 feet AGL at minimal airspeed) falls *outside* the classical avoid zone because immediate ground contact is achievable. However, pilots must maintain adequate power margin and be prepared to lower collective to ensure safe contact with the ground, recognizing that little room for error exists in this low-altitude flight regime.
