AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.S4.003	A headwind reduces groundspeed relative to airspeed, meaning the helicopter can fly a given track at a lower groundspeed without requiring a slow cyclic input. To maintain the desired groundspeed, you add a small amount of forward cyclic to compensate for the headwind component slowing your ground movement. Power (collective) may need a slight increase to maintain altitude as the headwind increases lift slightly. The goal is a consistent, moderate groundspeed that keeps the helicopter in a manageable energy state and avoids inadvertent hover out of ground effect.	->	A headwind naturally reduces groundspeed while maintaining airspeed, which is favorable for safe air taxi operations. During a controlled air taxi, you should allow the headwind to keep groundspeed low without adding forward cyclic; adding cyclic would increase airspeed and groundspeed needlessly. Collective should remain stable to maintain constant altitude; if the headwind increases rotor lift, reduce collective slightly to prevent an unwanted climb. The goal is to maintain a steady, low groundspeed and modest power setting by flying into the headwind.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.S4.004	A left quartering tailwind on a counterclockwise-rotor helicopter places the wind in the 210–330 degree range relative to the nose. This is a high-risk LTE azimuth because the wind can disrupt the tail rotor's efficiency by generating a turbulent or reversed flow environment around the tail rotor disk. Specifically, a left crosswind from behind the helicopter can cause a vortex ring state condition at the tail rotor or reduce the effective thrust angle. The tail rotor must work hardest at low airspeeds and high-power settings, and a tailwind removes the benefit of airflow that stabilizes tail rotor performance. You should avoid slow speeds in this wind condition, increase air taxi speed to improve translational directional stability, or change your taxi routing to avoid the adverse wind azimuth.	->	A left quartering tailwind on a counterclockwise-rotor helicopter places wind in the 200–270 degree range relative to the nose (left rear quadrant). This increases LTE risk because the tailwind prevents the tail rotor from generating sufficient sideforce in the direction required to counteract main rotor torque at low airspeeds, creating a directional-control deficit. The result is uncommanded right yaw that cannot be arrested by pedal input alone. LTE is most likely below approximately 30 knots airspeed. Mitigation includes increasing airspeed to restore translational flow, changing taxi routing to avoid the adverse wind direction, or reducing power.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.S5.006	On a helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor (as viewed from above, which is standard in most U.S. helicopters), left pedal increases tail rotor pitch to produce more anti-torque thrust. This reduces the load on the tail rotor and momentarily decreases the power demand on the main rotor transmission, which can cause a transient Nr increase. Additionally, abrupt pedal inputs can momentarily unload or load the rotor system asymmetrically, causing RPM fluctuations. The corrective technique is to make smooth, gradual pedal inputs and use only the pedal deflection required for heading control. On governor-equipped helicopters, the governor will usually compensate, but abrupt inputs can still cause transient exceedances.	->	Abrupt pedal inputs create transient rotor disk loading changes and asymmetric blade loading as the aircraft attitude shifts rapidly. Additionally, on governor-equipped helicopters, the governor system may lag slightly during rapid control inputs, causing momentary Nr spikes before compensation occurs. An abrupt pedal change can also momentarily alter the effective angle of attack across the rotor disk, creating brief power margin variations. The correct technique is to make smooth, gradual pedal inputs that allow the governor (if equipped) to respond proportionally, avoiding transient exceedances. This is especially critical during air taxi when the rotor system is already operating at reduced power margins.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.C.S6.007	First, recognize that a green/white rotating beacon during daylight hours indicates the airport is operating under IFR conditions, or at minimum, that the control tower (if any) is in operation — though at an uncontrolled airport, a beacon operating in the day may simply indicate weather below VFR or that the beacon switch was left on. Regardless, treat the airspace with extra caution. Since there is no ATC and no tower, you must self-sequence. Before crossing, make additional CTAF position calls announcing your position and intentions. Visually scan all final approach corridors and departure paths for the runway in question. Look for runway numbers and markings to positively identify the runway you are crossing. If vegetation is blocking signs, air taxi to a position that restores your visual reference. Do not cross until you are confident the runway is clear. Consider elevated hover height to improve visibility. Document the hazardous vegetation obstruction for a NASA ASRS report and notify airport management after landing.	->	Recognize that a rotating green/white beacon at an uncontrolled airport during daytime indicates the airport is open for operations; there is no control tower present. Since there is no ATC, you must self-sequence. Before crossing, make additional CTAF position calls announcing your position and intentions. Visually scan all final approach corridors and departure paths for the runway in question. Look for runway numbers and markings to positively identify the runway you are crossing. If vegetation is blocking signs, air taxi to a position that restores your visual reference. Do not cross until you are confident the runway is clear. Consider elevated hover height to improve visibility. Document the hazardous vegetation obstruction and notify airport management after landing. If you inadvertently cross without ensuring the runway is clear, immediately notify any aircraft in the area and file a NASA ASRS report, which provides limited liability protection if filed within 10 days.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.K1.007	ATC issues taxi instructions based on what they know at the time, but pilots bear independent responsibility under 14 CFR 91.103 to become familiar with all available information for the flight, including NOTAMs, before departure. ATC may not always flag every detail relevant to your specific aircraft type or route. A concrete example: suppose a NOTAM indicates that a taxiway has a weight-bearing restriction that prohibits aircraft over 12,500 lbs due to recent pavement damage. ATC may not be aware of your specific aircraft's weight and could issue a taxi instruction that routes you over that restricted taxiway. If you haven't reviewed the NOTAM, you follow ATC's instruction and potentially damage the pavement, incur liability, or receive an enforcement action. As the pilot in command, you are ultimately responsible for the safe conduct of the flight — including ground operations. Reviewing NOTAMs is not optional; it is a legal and safety requirement regardless of ATC guidance.	->	ATC issues taxi instructions based on what they know at the time, but pilots bear independent responsibility under 14 CFR 91.103 to become familiar with all available information for the flight, including NOTAMs, before departure. ATC may not always flag every detail relevant to your specific aircraft type or route. A concrete example: suppose a NOTAM indicates that a taxiway has a weight-bearing restriction that prohibits aircraft over 8,000 lbs due to recent pavement damage. ATC may not be aware of your specific aircraft's weight and could issue a taxi instruction that routes you over that restricted taxiway. If you haven't reviewed the NOTAM, you follow ATC's instruction and potentially damage the pavement, incur liability, or receive an enforcement action. As the pilot in command, you are ultimately responsible for the safe conduct of the flight — including ground operations. Reviewing NOTAMs is not optional; it is a legal and safety requirement under 14 CFR 91.103 regardless of ATC guidance.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.K3.004	Blue elevated or in-pavement lights mark the edges of taxiways and help define the taxi path at night or in low visibility. Green centerline lights, when present on a taxiway, guide you along the taxiway centerline. However, green lights ahead on the surface may also indicate the runway threshold or runway lead-on lights. If you see green lights transitioning to white-and-green alternating, that is a caution zone near a runway. Green threshold lights face the approach direction and mark the runway threshold. You must be alert that green centerline lighting stops at hold short positions, confirming where to stop before the runway. Always verify your position against the airport diagram before proceeding.	->	Blue elevated or in-pavement lights mark the edges of taxiways and help define the taxi path at night or in low visibility. Green centerline lights, when present on a taxiway, guide you along the taxiway centerline in low visibility. Green centerline taxiway lights typically end at or before a hold-short position or runway entrance, indicating you are approaching the runway environment. When green lights end or transition to white-and-green alternating patterns (runway threshold markings), verify your position against the airport diagram and confirm clearance from ATC before crossing the runway hold-short line. Green runway threshold lights face the approach direction and mark the runway threshold. Always maintain awareness of your position relative to taxiway hold-short lines and runway boundaries.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.K3.006	This is a runway safety area or taxiway shoulder marking — more specifically, it could be a Runway Safety Area (RSA) boundary or an enhanced taxiway centerline/runway approach area marking. Yellow chevrons or diagonal yellow hatching with a solid border indicate an unusable or non-load-bearing surface — you must not taxi onto this area. If the hatched area has a solid yellow border it is a non-movement area boundary or a blast pad/stopway. Pilots are prohibited from conducting normal taxi operations on these surfaces as they are not structurally intended for aircraft movement and may be soft, eroded, or unmarked hazard areas.	->	Diagonal yellow stripes with a solid yellow border mark a Runway Safety Area (RSA) boundary or non-movement area boundary. These areas are closed to aircraft and are not structurally intended to support aircraft movement — they may be soft, eroded, or contain unmarked hazards. You are prohibited from taxiing onto these surfaces. Do not enter or cross these marked areas during ground operations unless explicitly cleared by ATC and only for emergency purposes.
