AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.K4.004	I would begin my evaluation during the approach reconnaissance by observing multiple sources simultaneously: (1) Flag on the building — flags extend and flutter toward the downwind side, so the flag points away from the direction the wind is coming from; I would note which direction the flag is blowing toward and reverse that to identify the upwind direction. (2) Tree and vegetation movement — leaves and branches move away from the wind; treetops leaning consistently in one direction confirm the wind direction. (3) Pond or water surface — ripples move downwind and whitecaps or small waves form; the upwind side of the pond will have a smoother, darker appearance compared to the rippled downwind side. (4) Any dust, smoke, or debris — movement direction confirms wind. After cross-checking all indicators for consistency, I would establish my landing direction into the wind. If indicators conflict, I would orbit and gather more information or contact an FSS for weather data before committing.	->	I would begin my evaluation during the approach reconnaissance by observing multiple sources simultaneously: (1) Flag on the building — flags billow and extend in the downwind direction (the direction the wind is blowing toward); I would observe which way the flag is blowing and recognize the wind is coming from the opposite direction (upwind). (2) Tree and vegetation movement — leaves and branches move away from the wind; treetops leaning consistently in one direction confirm the wind direction. (3) Pond or water surface — ripples move downwind and whitecaps or small waves form; the upwind side of the pond will have a smoother, darker appearance compared to the rippled downwind side. (4) Any dust, smoke, or debris — movement direction confirms wind. After cross-checking all indicators for consistency, I would establish my landing direction into the wind. If indicators conflict, I would orbit and gather more information or contact an FSS for weather data before committing.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.K5.001	During night ground operations on an airport movement area, the helicopter must display position lights (navigation lights — red left, green right, white aft) as required by 14 CFR 91.209. Anti-collision lights must also be on unless the pilot-in-command determines they would create a hazard. A taxi/landing light should be used for visibility and to be seen by others, though it is not specifically mandated by regulation for taxiing. Position lights are the core regulatory requirement.	->	During night ground operations on an airport movement area, the helicopter must display position lights (navigation lights — red left, green right, white aft) as required by 14 CFR 91.209. Anti-collision lights are also required to be on during aircraft movement per 14 CFR 91.209(b), unless the aircraft is not equipped with them or operating under a specific exemption. A taxi/landing light should be used for visibility and to be seen by others, though it is not specifically mandated by regulation for taxiing. Position lights and anti-collision lights are the core regulatory requirements.
AUTO_APPROVE	CH.IV.D.R1.005	The core SRM answer (prioritize control, stop, request clarification) is sound; the 14 CFR 91.215 reference, though peripheral, is factually correct and does not undermine the response.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.R4.007	Dynamic rollover occurs when the helicopter begins rotating around a wheel pivot point and the rolling moment exceeds the restoring moment the pilot can generate with cyclic. During a taxi turn, centrifugal force pushes the helicopter outward (away from the turn center), while rotor thrust acts vertically. If speed is too high, the combination of lateral centrifugal force and any cyclic displacement that unloads the inside wheel allows the outside wheel to become a pivot point. The 'critical rollover angle' is the bank angle beyond which rotor thrust — even with full opposite cyclic — cannot generate a sufficient restoring moment to arrest the roll. For most helicopters this angle is between 5 and 8 degrees of lateral tilt (varies by aircraft). Beyond this angle, the rotor is actually adding to the rollover because the thrust vector has tilted past the pivot point. Ground speed matters because higher speed increases centrifugal force in the turn, reduces the time available to recognize and react, and increases the energy in the system that must be countered. Once the critical angle is exceeded, reducing collective is the only remaining option to reduce rotor thrust and stop the rollover — but at very low AGL this may result in ground contact. Prevention through appropriate pre-turn speed reduction is the only reliable mitigation.	->	Dynamic rollover occurs when the helicopter begins rotating around a wheel pivot point and the rolling moment exceeds the restoring moment the pilot can generate with cyclic. During a taxi turn, centrifugal force pushes the helicopter outward (away from the turn center), while rotor thrust acts vertically. If speed is too high, the combination of lateral centrifugal force and any cyclic displacement that unloads the inside wheel allows the outside wheel to become a pivot point. The 'critical rollover angle' is the bank angle beyond which rotor thrust — even with full opposite cyclic — cannot generate a sufficient restoring moment to arrest the roll. This critical angle varies significantly by aircraft type, weight, CG position, and surface conditions; consult the POH/RFM for aircraft-specific limits. Beyond this angle, the rotor is actually adding to the rollover because the thrust vector has tilted past the pivot point. Ground speed matters because higher speed increases centrifugal force in the turn, reduces the time available to recognize and react, and increases the energy in the system that must be countered. Once the critical angle is exceeded, reducing collective is the only remaining option to reduce rotor thrust and stop the rollover — but at very low AGL this may result in ground contact. Prevention through appropriate pre-turn speed reduction is the only reliable mitigation.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.R5.002	Applying excessive aft cyclic tilts the rotor disk rearward, increasing the download on the tail and potentially causing the tail skid or tail boom to contact the ground. At the same time, abrupt braking can cause the helicopter to pitch nose-down, which may result in the rotor blades striking the airframe or ground. The combination of aggressive aft cyclic and hard braking creates asymmetric forces that can cause dynamic instability or structural contact — both of which are serious safety hazards.	->	Applying excessive aft cyclic tilts the rotor disk aft, which redirects rotor thrust upward and back, reducing forward thrust and potentially causing the tail skid or tail boom to contact the ground. Abrupt braking can cause the helicopter to settle quickly or pitch unexpectedly, increasing the risk of rotor blade contact with the ground or airframe. Together, these inputs create a high-risk combination that compromises ground clearance and stable control, leading to structural contact or dynamic instability. Avoid simultaneous aggressive aft cyclic and hard braking.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.R5.004	This is a compounding risk scenario. The tailwind reduces the effectiveness of forward rotor thrust as a speed check, and the downhill slope adds gravitational acceleration. To manage this safely: (1) Use gentle forward cyclic input to maintain directional control, avoiding excessive forward tilt that would accelerate the aircraft further downhill. (2) Use brakes judiciously and intermittently rather than riding them continuously, which could cause brake fade or lockup. (3) If speed builds, consider using slight aft cyclic to increase rotor disk drag before applying brakes — but not so much aft cyclic that the tail approaches the ground. (4) Consider an alternate taxi route or waiting for wind conditions to improve. The tailwind also means the cyclic must be held more forward than normal to prevent wind from getting under the aft portion of the rotor disk.	->	This is a compounding risk scenario. The tailwind reduces the effectiveness of forward rotor thrust as a speed check, and the downhill slope adds gravitational acceleration. To manage this safely: (1) Use aft cyclic input to counteract the tailwind's tendency to push the helicopter backward and to generate rotor disk drag for speed control, avoiding excessive forward tilt that would accelerate the aircraft further downhill. (2) Use brakes judiciously and intermittently rather than riding them continuously, which could cause brake fade or lockup. (3) If speed builds, use aft cyclic to increase rotor disk drag before or in conjunction with measured brake application — but not so much aft cyclic that the tail approaches the ground. (4) Consider an alternate taxi route or waiting for wind conditions to improve. In a tailwind, the cyclic must be held aft of neutral to maintain directional control, counteract the wind's drift effect, and generate rotor disk drag as a speed control device. Forward cyclic would increase drift and speed, exacerbating the downhill and tailwind effects.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.S4.001	With a direct headwind, hold the cyclic slightly forward (into the wind) to prevent the nose from lifting and to keep the skids or wheels firmly on the ground. The collective should be kept low — just enough to reduce weight on the gear to aid ground steering — but not so high that lift is generated. The goal is to keep the aircraft firmly planted and controllable on the surface.	->	With a direct headwind, hold the cyclic slightly aft (into the wind) to prevent the nose from lifting and to keep the wheels firmly on the ground. The collective should be kept at the minimum setting required to maintain ground control and directional stability without generating significant lift. The goal is to keep the aircraft firmly planted on the surface.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.S4.002	The antitorque pedals are the primary control for maintaining directional control during ground taxi. On most wheel-equipped helicopters, the pedals actuate a tail rotor that generates side force to yaw the aircraft. Some models also have a steerable nose or tail wheel connected to the pedals. The cyclic assists by keeping the rotor disk tilted into crosswinds to prevent weather-vaning or loss of lateral control.	->	The steerable nosewheel is the primary control for maintaining directional control during ground taxi on wheel-equipped helicopters. The nosewheel is typically mechanically linked to or controlled by the antitorque pedals. The tail rotor provides yaw authority and trim during taxi, and the cyclic assists by maintaining lateral stability against crosswinds and helping keep the rotor disk properly aligned.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.S5.003	When taxiing into a headwind, the cyclic should be positioned slightly forward to prevent the nose from rising due to the increased rotor lift created by the headwind. This keeps the disk tilted slightly forward and helps maintain positive contact between the main gear and the ground, preventing the helicopter from becoming light on the nose wheel and losing directional control.	->	When taxiing into a headwind, position the cyclic slightly forward to counteract the nose-up pitching tendency caused by increased rotor lift in the headwind. This maintains positive contact with the nose wheel, prevents the helicopter from becoming light on the gear, and ensures directional stability and ground control.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.S5.005	With a quartering tailwind from the left, the wind is acting on the aft-left portion of the rotor disk, tending to tilt the disk and reduce weight on the left main gear. The correct response is to apply cyclic into the wind — in this case, to the left and slightly forward — to counteract the tilting effect and keep the rotor disk level. Simultaneously reduce the collective slightly if it was creeping up, and use the brakes gently to slow down, which reduces the aerodynamic effect of the wind. Slowing the taxi speed also reduces the relative wind component, giving more positive ground contact.	->	With a quartering tailwind from the left, the wind is acting on the aft-left portion of the rotor disk, tending to tilt the disk and reduce weight on the left main gear while also creating a pitch-down moment. The correct response is to apply cyclic into the wind — in this case, to the left and slightly aft — to counteract both the rolling (tilting) effect and the pitch-down tendency. Simultaneously reduce the collective slightly if needed, and use the brakes gently to slow down, which reduces the aerodynamic effect of the wind. Slowing the taxi speed reduces the relative wind component, giving more positive ground contact and control authority.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.S5.008	This is a safety-critical situation requiring immediate action. First, do not continue taxiing toward the congested ramp. Use whatever braking effectiveness remains combined with a slight increase in collective — just enough to reduce the rotor's downward thrust and unload weight from the wheels, which paradoxically reduces braking effectiveness, so this must be used carefully. A better technique is to use the cyclic to tilt the rotor disk slightly aft, increasing rotor drag and creating a slight nose-up pitch tendency that works against forward movement. If the aircraft is still moving and control is deteriorating, the safest option is to transition to a low hover immediately (if conditions allow) to completely stop translational movement, then set the aircraft back down and shut down in place. Call maintenance immediately — spongy brakes indicate air in the brake lines, fluid loss, or brake fade, all of which are airworthiness concerns that must be resolved before further flight under 14 CFR 91.7.	->	This is a safety-critical situation requiring immediate action. Do not continue taxiing toward the congested ramp. Use whatever braking effectiveness remains. If additional control authority is needed, apply cyclic slightly aft (pitch up) to create rotor drag opposing forward motion and a nose-up pitch attitude opposing the downslope. Do not increase collective — this increases pitch load and load on the airframe and will not help slow the aircraft. The safest response is to transition to a low hover immediately (if altitude and clearance permit) to completely stop translational movement, then set the aircraft back down in a safe location and shut down in place. Call maintenance immediately — spongy brakes indicate air in the brake lines, fluid loss, or brake fade, all of which are airworthiness concerns that must be resolved before further flight under 14 CFR 91.7.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.S10.004	The red background signs are runway designation signs, indicating runways ahead. The sign with the left arrow means runways 27L and 27R are to the left. The sign directly ahead with '9R-9L' indicates you are approaching the entrance to runways 9R and 9L — you are at a runway intersection and must hold short without ATC clearance. You stop before the runway holding position marking, contact ground control if needed to clarify your route, and do not proceed onto the runway until explicitly cleared.	->	Red background runway designation signs provide directional information. The sign '27L-27R' with a left-pointing arrow means runways 27L and 27R are located to the left. The sign '9R-9L' directly ahead indicates those runways are ahead in your direction of taxi. These signs tell you which runways are in which direction but do not by themselves indicate a hold-short requirement. Your actual action depends on the runway holding position marking — two solid yellow lines (runway side) and two dashed yellow lines (taxiway side) — which define the boundary where you must hold short. If you see the holding position marking ahead and lack ATC clearance to cross, stop before the marking and contact ground control for further instructions. Do not enter the runway without explicit clearance.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.D.S10.005	An alternating red and green light gun signal means 'exercise extreme caution.' This signal warns you of a specific hazard in the area — possibly crossing traffic, construction, or another aircraft. You should slow to a minimum safe taxi speed, look carefully in all directions for the source of the hazard, and acknowledge the signal by rocking the helicopter or using your landing light before proceeding cautiously. You should also consider requesting clarification via radio or hand signals if the hazard is not immediately apparent.	->	An alternating red and green light gun signal means 'GENERAL WARNING' — indicating a hazard exists that requires your attention. You should reduce taxi speed to a minimum safe speed, scan carefully in all directions to identify the source of the hazard (crossing traffic, construction, personnel, or other aircraft), and be prepared to stop or take evasive action. Acknowledge the signal by rocking the aircraft or cycling your landing light. If the hazard is not immediately apparent, request clarification from the tower via radio or hand signals before proceeding. Do not continue forward until the warning condition is resolved.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.K1.006	A crosswind from the uphill side (blowing downhill across the aircraft) adds a rolling moment in the same direction as the slope's downhill tendency. This compounds the risk of dynamic rollover because the wind is effectively pushing the helicopter in the direction it is already prone to roll. The pilot must apply even more cyclic into the uphill/upwind side to counteract both forces simultaneously, reducing available cyclic margin. This scenario narrows the acceptable slope gradient significantly. A crosswind from the downhill side would assist in counteracting the downhill roll tendency but may introduce other control challenges. The pilot must assess whether adequate cyclic authority exists before committing to the landing.	->	A crosswind with a component blowing from the uphill side toward the downhill side (a downslope wind component) creates a rolling moment that compounds the helicopter's natural tendency to roll downhill due to the slope itself. The pilot must apply even more cyclic control into the uphill/upwind side to counteract both the slope and wind effects simultaneously, which significantly reduces available cyclic margin and narrows the acceptable slope gradient. This scenario creates a more critical dynamic rollover risk. Conversely, a crosswind from the downhill side (upslope component) would help counteract the downhill roll tendency, though it may introduce other control challenges. The pilot must carefully assess whether adequate cyclic authority remains before committing to the landing.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.K2.005	An east-facing slope in early morning receives direct solar heating, which can cause significant snow melt, frost, or moisture on the surface — potentially making it slippery or soft. Conversely, a west-facing slope may still be frozen and more firm at that time of day. Sun angle also affects your ability to judge the slope angle accurately during approach; a low sun angle on approach can cause glare and make it difficult to assess terrain features and surface texture. Additionally, sun position affects shadow patterns that help or hinder accurate slope estimation. These factors — surface condition resulting from solar exposure and visibility/glare during approach — must factor into slope site selection.	->	An east-facing slope receives direct solar heating beginning in early morning, which initiates the daily warming cycle; however, the most significant surface changes (melting, softening, moisture development) typically occur during mid-to-late afternoon when solar intensity is maximum. A west-facing slope at early morning remains in shadow, retaining overnight frost and being firmer and more stable. The pilot should recognize that a slope site appearing acceptable at dawn may deteriorate throughout the day as cumulative solar heating warms the surface. Additionally, low sun angle in early morning can produce glare and long shadows that obscure terrain features and slope angle, making accurate assessment difficult during approach. Site selection should account for the forecasted time of day and anticipated surface condition changes due to solar heating effects.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.K3.002	The upslope skid is always placed on the slope first. Wind matters because if a crosswind is present, it can create a lateral drift that either assists or works against maintaining contact with the slope. A crosswind blowing from the upslope side (into the slope) helps push the helicopter toward the hill, while a crosswind from the downslope side can drift the helicopter away from the slope and increase the risk of the downslope skid contacting the ground prematurely, leading to dynamic rollover.	->	The upslope skid is always placed on the slope first. Wind matters because crosswind creates lateral drift that affects helicopter attitude relative to the slope. A crosswind blowing from the upslope side (into the slope) assists in maintaining contact with the slope and helps keep the rotor disk properly aligned. A crosswind from the downslope side can drift the helicopter laterally away from the slope, potentially causing the aircraft to tilt or settle unevenly, which increases the risk of exceeding cyclic limits or creating a dynamic rollover condition. Therefore, crosswind direction and magnitude must be evaluated in conjunction with slope orientation to ensure safe landing geometry and rotor disk control.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.K4.002	The critical rollover angle is the angle of bank beyond which dynamic rollover becomes unrecoverable — the rotor thrust and rolling moment can no longer be overcome by full opposite cyclic input. This angle varies by helicopter model but is typically around 10–15 degrees for most helicopters. During slope operations, the helicopter is already laterally tilted relative to the rotor disk, meaning the margin between normal operating attitude and the critical rollover angle is significantly reduced compared to flat-surface operations. This makes slope landings and takeoffs inherently higher risk for dynamic rollover.	->	The critical rollover angle is the angle of bank beyond which dynamic rollover becomes unrecoverable. This angle varies significantly by helicopter model, weight distribution, and rotor characteristics, but is generally in the range of 8–25 degrees for most light helicopters. During slope operations, the helicopter is already laterally tilted relative to the rotor disk, meaning the margin between normal operating attitude and the critical rollover angle is significantly reduced compared to flat-surface operations. This makes slope landings and takeoffs inherently higher risk for dynamic rollover.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.K4.003	During a slope landing, the helicopter should be positioned so the upslope skid or wheel touches down first, with the rotor disk tilted into the slope using lateral cyclic. To prevent dynamic rollover, the pilot should lower the collective slowly and smoothly after the upslope skid contacts the ground, allowing the downslope skid to settle gently. The key technique is to not allow the helicopter to develop any lateral rolling momentum — if the helicopter begins to roll toward the downslope side at a rate that feels abnormal, the pilot must be prepared to execute a go-around immediately rather than attempting to hold it down with collective.	->	During a slope landing, the helicopter should be positioned so the upslope skid or wheel touches down first, with the rotor disk tilted into the slope using lateral cyclic toward the uphill side. After the upslope skid contacts, maintain that upslope cyclic position while slowly lowering collective to allow the downslope skid to settle gently. The key technique is to monitor for any tendency to roll toward the downslope — if the roll rate becomes abnormal or cyclic reaches its limit, execute a go-around immediately rather than attempting to hold it down.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.K5.003	On a slope, the low side of the rotor disk is closer to the ground than it would be on level terrain. As the helicopter settles onto a slope, especially when the rotor is turning, the downhill rotor blade can strike the rising terrain surface. A slope oriented so that the tail is uphill (nose-low, tail-high) or that places the low rotor arc near the upslope terrain is typically most critical. The RFM defines maximum slope angle partly based on the rotor clearance geometry of that specific helicopter model.	->	On a slope, the downslope side of the rotor disk is closer to the ground than it would be on level terrain. As the helicopter settles onto a slope, the downslope rotor blade arc can strike the rising terrain surface, particularly during the approach, settling, and touchdown phase. This downslope rotor-to-terrain clearance is the primary geometric concern that limits maximum slope angle. Additionally, mast-to-rotor-hub geometry and the cone angle of the rotor disk become critical considerations on steeper slopes. The RFM defines maximum slope angle partly based on rotor clearance geometry specific to that helicopter model.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.R1.001	Dynamic rollover is a condition where a helicopter begins rotating about a skid or wheel that is fixed on the ground, and the rolling moment exceeds the pilot's ability to correct with opposite cyclic. On a slope, one skid or wheel is already in contact with the ground while the other is still airborne or just touching down, creating a ready-made pivot point. If the pilot applies collective too aggressively, allows lateral cyclic to drift toward the downslope side, or has a skid caught on an obstacle, the helicopter can roll past its critical angle — the point where gravity and momentum overcome cyclic control authority — and roll over. The confined, asymmetric footing on a slope dramatically shortens the time and cyclic travel available to recover.	->	Dynamic rollover is a condition where a helicopter begins rotating about a skid or wheel that is fixed on the ground, and the rolling moment exceeds the pilot's ability to correct with opposite cyclic. On a slope, one skid or wheel is already in contact with the ground while the other is still airborne or just touching down, creating a ready-made pivot point. If the pilot applies collective too aggressively, allows lateral cyclic to drift toward the downslope side, or has a skid caught on an obstacle, the helicopter can roll past its critical angle — the point where gravity and momentum overcome cyclic control authority — and roll over. The confined, asymmetric footing on a slope dramatically shortens the time and cyclic travel available to recover.

Regulatory Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapters 10–11 (Slope Operations and Dynamic Rollover)
