AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.R5.002	On a slope, the helicopter's rotor disk is often tilted closer to the uphill surface during landing and takeoff. Loose material can be violently disturbed by rotor downwash and become a projectile hazard to the tail rotor, which may be at a lower clearance height on the uphill side. Additionally, loose material can reduce visual cues needed to judge surface firmness and actual slope gradient, increasing the risk of misjudging the landing surface.	->	On a slope, the helicopter's rotor disk is tilted closer to the uphill surface during landing and takeoff. Loose material can be violently disturbed by rotor downwash and become a projectile hazard to the tail rotor. With disk tilt during slope approaches, downwash is directed upslope and can carry loose material toward tail rotor blades or other airframe components. Additionally, loose material can reduce visual cues needed to judge surface firmness and actual slope gradient, increasing the risk of misjudging the landing surface.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.R5.005	This is a dynamic rollover risk developing in real time. As the uphill skid sinks, the rotor mast tilts further uphill beyond the original slope angle, increasing the effective lean toward rollover. Immediately: (1) do not attempt to compensate with cyclic inputs that could worsen the situation or reach the critical rollover angle; (2) smoothly increase collective to lift off before the lean angle becomes unrecoverable — the critical rollover angle for most helicopters is approximately 35 degrees from vertical with normal control inputs; (3) once airborne, reassess the landing zone and select a firmer surface. Do not wait to 'see how bad it gets' — once the rollover angle is exceeded, recovery is not possible.	->	This is a dynamic rollover risk developing in real time. As the uphill skid sinks, the rotor mast tilts further uphill beyond the original slope angle, increasing the effective lean toward rollover. Immediately: (1) do not attempt to compensate with cyclic inputs that could worsen the situation or reach the critical rollover angle; (2) smoothly increase collective to lift off before the lean angle becomes unrecoverable — the critical rollover angle for most single-rotor helicopters is typically in the range of 25–35 degrees from vertical, depending on rotor design, weight distribution, and control authority; consult the POH for your specific aircraft; (3) once airborne, reassess the landing zone and select a firmer surface. Do not wait to 'see how bad it gets' — once the rollover angle is exceeded, recovery is not possible.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.R6.008	A crosswind blowing across a slope introduces several compounding collision hazards. For the main rotor: a crosswind from the downslope side (below) can cause the advancing blade on the downslope side to generate more lift and flap upward while the retreating upslope blade flaps down—increasing rotor tip clearance on the downslope but reducing it on the upslope side closest to the terrain. A crosswind from the upslope side (as in this scenario, blowing left-to-right across the cockpit if landing with the high side to the right) will produce the opposite effect, driving the upslope rotor tip toward the terrain. The pilot may need to apply additional cyclic away from the upslope obstacle to compensate. For the tail rotor: the crosswind will weathervane the nose and require continuous pedal input to maintain heading, increasing tail rotor thrust demand. If the crosswind is from the left on a counterclockwise-rotor helicopter, the tail rotor is working harder to counteract both torque and wind weathervaning—placing it closer to its thrust limit. Additionally, a crosswind can push the tail toward upslope terrain during the yaw required to align with the slope. The pilot must ensure the tail rotor arc is continuously clear of terrain during any heading change in the crosswind.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.S1.001	The FAA recommends slopes of 5 degrees or less for most helicopter operations. Beyond approximately 5 degrees, dynamic rollover becomes a serious risk because the rotor thrust vector can no longer adequately counteract the tendency of the helicopter to roll downhill once a skid or wheel is in contact with the slope. The exact limit varies by aircraft type and must be verified in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM).	->	The maximum slope angle is aircraft-specific and must be found in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) Limitations section. Most modern helicopters permit slopes between 8 and 15 degrees depending on design, though some may be more or less restrictive. The slope limit exists because beyond the manufacturer's specified angle, dynamic rollover risk increases significantly—the rotor thrust vector cannot adequately counteract the gravitational and wind-induced tendency of the helicopter to roll downhill, and on some rotor systems, mast-bumping risk increases if the mast tilts beyond design tolerance. Always consult the RFM for your specific aircraft type rather than applying a general guideline.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.S3.004	With a left crosswind, the primary risk is that left pedal inputs to compensate for the crosswind may cause the tail to weathervane and create an LTE-susceptible condition. I would plan an approach from the south (into the predominant wind component from the west/southwest if possible by angling the approach) to maintain maximum into-wind effectiveness. I would establish a stabilized hover before moving to the slope, then conduct a slow sideward approach so the upslope side remains on the appropriate side. I would verify I have cyclic travel remaining to roll into the slope, keep the tail pointed away from terrain, and be prepared to abort immediately if cyclic reaches a limit or if the crosswind pushes the tail toward obstacles. I would also confirm there are no obstacles on the downwind abort path.	->	With a left crosswind from the west toward a north-facing slope, I have a conflict: approaching from the south (the optimal downslope approach direction) means accepting a left crosswind rather than flying into the wind. I would prioritize slope geometry and approach direction over crosswind correction, so I would approach from the south/downslope despite the left crosswind. I would maintain maximum available cyclic travel for roll-into-slope correction, monitor tail rotor effectiveness carefully during left pedal inputs for yaw trim, confirm clear abort paths downwind (to the east), and establish a stabilized out-of-ground-effect hover before committing to the final approach. If the crosswind exceeds aircraft limitations or I cannot maintain sufficient cyclic reserve for slope correction, I would decline the landing or seek an alternative slope with better wind alignment.
