AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.S8.005	Collective should be reduced to zero only after both skids are firmly on the ground and the helicopter's full weight is supported by the slope. Reducing collective too early — while the downslope skid is still being lowered — removes rotor lift prematurely, causing an uncontrolled drop of the downslope skid and increasing rollover risk. Reducing it too late wastes power and can cause a rotor flap/mast bump situation if the cyclic is at an extreme position. Smooth, coordinated reduction of collective as the downslope skid contacts is the proper technique.	->	Collective should be reduced to zero only after both skids are firmly on the ground and the helicopter's full weight is supported by the slope. Reducing collective too early — while the downslope skid is still being lowered — removes rotor lift prematurely, causing an uncontrolled drop of the downslope skid and increasing dynamic rollover risk. Reducing it too late wastes power and may interfere with maintaining proper cyclic control coordination. Smooth, coordinated reduction of collective as the downslope skid contacts is the proper technique.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.S9.001	Neutralizing the controls after a slope landing means returning the cyclic to a neutral (centered) position, reducing the collective to full down, and centering the pedals once the helicopter is firmly on the ground and the rotor RPM has decreased to a safe level. During a slope landing, the cyclic is held into the slope to prevent dynamic rollover as the skids touch down sequentially. Once the helicopter is fully settled and stable on the slope, the pilot should no longer need to hold cyclic pressure into the hill — the skids and terrain geometry are supporting the aircraft. Leaving cyclic deflected after landing can stress the rotor system and may cause mast bumping or rotor contact during shutdown.	->	Neutralizing the controls after a slope landing means returning the cyclic to a neutral (centered) position, placing the collective at full down (minimum pitch), and centering the pedals once the helicopter is firmly on the ground and rotor RPM has decreased to a safe level. During a slope landing, the cyclic is held into the slope to prevent dynamic rollover as the skids touch down sequentially. Once the helicopter is fully settled and stable on the slope, the pilot should no longer need to hold cyclic pressure into the hill — the skids and terrain geometry are supporting the aircraft. Leaving cyclic deflected after landing can stress the rotor system. Control neutralization ensures safe shutdown and reduces unnecessary wear on the airframe.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.S9.005	What is likely happening is ground resonance — a potentially destructive coupling between the rotor blade lead/lag oscillation frequency and the natural frequency of the airframe on its landing gear (skids). This can occur during rotor deceleration through certain RPM ranges, particularly on an uneven or sloped surface where the skids may not have equal and firm contact. Yes, you should have anticipated this. Before fully neutralizing controls and initiating shutdown, the pilot should ensure both skids are on stable, solid ground. If the slope or terrain is uneven and one skid has less-than-solid contact, the rocking motion during rotor deceleration can trigger ground resonance. If ground resonance begins, the pilot must either immediately increase RPM and lift off, or shut down as rapidly as possible — there is no middle ground. Neutralizing controls does not increase ground resonance risk, but inadequate surface assessment before shutdown does.	->	What is likely happening is ground resonance — a potentially destructive coupling between rotor blade lead/lag oscillation frequency and the natural frequency of the airframe on its landing gear (skids). This can occur during rotor deceleration through specific RPM ranges, particularly when both skids do not have equal and firm contact with the landing surface, as on an uneven or sloped terrain. Yes, you should have anticipated this. Before fully neutralizing controls and initiating shutdown, ensure both skids are on stable, solid ground with equal contact pressure. If ground resonance begins, the pilot must either immediately increase RPM and lift off, or shut down as rapidly as possible — there is no middle ground. The hazard is primarily a function of surface condition and skid contact stability, not of control neutralization itself.
AI_EDIT	CH.IV.E.S10.002	The uphill skid or wheel lifts off first. Because the helicopter was landed with the uphill side low (cyclic tilted into the slope), the pilot gradually applies upward collective while simultaneously reducing the uphill cyclic input. As the aircraft becomes level, the uphill skid breaks contact with the terrain first. The downhill skid remains in contact until the helicopter is in a wings-level, level attitude before full lift-off.	->	The downhill skid lifts off first, or ideally both skids lift off simultaneously if controls are coordinated smoothly. The helicopter was landed with the uphill side low (cyclic tilted into the slope). The pilot gradually applies upward collective while simultaneously applying cyclic away from the slope (downslope direction) to level the aircraft. As the rotor disk becomes more level, the downhill side (which was lower) lifts first as it reaches the same altitude as the uphill skid. Proper technique aims for near-simultaneous lift-off by coordinating collective and cyclic smoothly to avoid any skid dragging during the transition.
