AI_EDIT	CH.V.A.K1.005	Hot and humid air is less dense than standard day air, raising the effective density altitude. This reduces both engine power output and rotor thrust for a given collective setting. Additionally, humid air has a slightly lower density than dry air at the same temperature and pressure, compounding the effect. Reduced rotor thrust means the helicopter must use more collective to maintain altitude, leaving less excess power available for climbing. To partially compensate: (1) Fly at the airspeed that yields best rate of climb per the POH/RFM for the actual density altitude conditions; (2) Reduce non-essential load or fuel if possible before flight; (3) Ensure engine and rotor RPM are at the top of the green arc; (4) Accept a reduced rate of climb and plan accordingly for obstacle clearance.	->	Hot and humid air is less dense than standard day air, raising the effective density altitude. This reduces both engine power output and rotor thrust for a given collective setting. At constant temperature and pressure, humid air is actually slightly less dense than dry air because water vapor (molecular weight 18) replaces heavier nitrogen and oxygen molecules (combined average weight ~29); however, the high temperature is the dominant factor in reducing air density and climb performance. Reduced rotor thrust means the helicopter must use more collective to maintain altitude, leaving less excess power available for climbing. To partially compensate: (1) Fly at the airspeed that yields best rate of climb per the POH/RFM for the actual density altitude conditions; (2) Reduce non-essential load or fuel if possible before flight; (3) Ensure engine and rotor RPM are at the top of the green arc; (4) Accept a reduced rate of climb and plan accordingly for obstacle clearance.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.A.R2.006	While the 3-minute rule is commonly associated with fixed-wing wake turbulence avoidance, helicopter rotor downwash also generates vortices, particularly from heavy helicopters. The vortices from a large helicopter tend to be less structured than fixed-wing tip vortices but can still cause significant turbulence. My assessment would include: the departed helicopter's weight and configuration (heavier = stronger vortices), wind conditions (calm winds allow vortices to linger; light winds can drift them into my departure path), and whether the prior helicopter departed along the same ground track. I would delay my departure by at least 3 minutes, or until I can confirm the departure path is clear. I would also plan to take off into the wind and climb above any potential vortex descent path. In calm wind conditions I would consider a longer delay, as vortices will not drift away and may settle directly onto the departure area.	->	While the 3-minute rule is commonly associated with fixed-wing wake turbulence avoidance, helicopter rotor downwash also generates vortices, particularly from heavy helicopters. The vortices from a large helicopter tend to be less structured than fixed-wing tip vortices but can still cause significant turbulence. My assessment would include: the departed helicopter's weight and configuration (heavier = stronger vortices), wind conditions (calm winds allow vortices to linger; light winds can drift them into my departure path), and whether the prior helicopter departed along the same ground track. I would delay my departure by at least 3 minutes, or until I can confirm the departure path is clear. I would also plan to take off into the wind and climb above any potential vortex descent path. In calm wind conditions I would consider a longer delay, as vortices will not drift away and may settle directly onto the departure area.
