AI_EDIT	CH.V.A.S2.005	After receiving taxi instructions from ground, you acknowledge and read back any hold short instructions. When you reach the runway and are ready for departure, you contact tower on the tower frequency (switching without needing explicit instruction is standard, but many controllers will tell you when to switch). You call tower with your position, call sign, and request: 'Riverside Tower, Helicopter N123AB, holding short runway 27, ready for takeoff, northbound departure.' After receiving your takeoff clearance, you read it back including any instructions (e.g., turn to a heading, frequency change). Once airborne and clear of Class C airspace (typically established by ATC advisory or your own position awareness), no specific 'leaving Class C' call is required unless instructed, but if you were on tower frequency and are departing the area, a frequency change is appropriate. You should have already been given a departure frequency or squawk code by Class C approach during your initial contact before entering or departing.	->	After receiving taxi instructions from ground, acknowledge and read back any hold-short instructions. Monitor tower frequency. When you reach the runway and are ready for departure, contact tower with your position, call sign, and request: 'Riverside Tower, Helicopter N123AB, holding short Runway 27, ready for takeoff, northbound departure.' After receiving your takeoff clearance, read it back including any instructions (e.g., turn to a heading, frequency change). Once airborne and clear of Class C airspace (typically when you've transitioned to cruise altitude outside the lateral/vertical Class C boundaries), no specific 'leaving Class C' call is required unless instructed. If tower provides a frequency change or departure frequency, comply as directed. Best practice: monitor tower frequency and switch when ready for takeoff rather than assuming automatic transition without explicit instruction from ground.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.A.S5.008	A 10-knot tailwind at near max gross weight significantly degrades takeoff performance for a helicopter. Hover performance may still be achievable but the transition to forward flight will be longer, obstacle clearance will be reduced, and if power is marginal, a running or rolling takeoff may be necessary. Decision-making process: First, check the RFM/POH for tailwind takeoff limitations—many helicopters prohibit or restrict tailwind takeoffs. Second, assess whether OGE hover power is available; if not, a vertical takeoff is not possible. Third, consider whether the available runway length compensates for the increased takeoff distance with a tailwind. Options include: requesting the opposite runway (runway 33) for a headwind departure; requesting a different takeoff direction if the airport layout allows; or accepting the takeoff if performance calculations confirm it is within limits and the POH permits it. You should communicate with the tower before accepting the clearance, as attempting a marginal takeoff to meet ATC expectations is a classic pressured decision-making trap.	->	A 10-knot tailwind at near max gross weight significantly degrades takeoff performance for a helicopter. Hover performance may still be achievable but the transition to forward flight will be longer, obstacle clearance will be reduced, and if power is marginal, a running or rolling takeoff may be necessary. Decision-making process: First, check the RFM/POH for tailwind takeoff limitations or tailwind performance data—if the POH contains tailwind procedures, follow those limits; if the POH only provides headwind/no-wind performance data, you cannot safely assume tailwind performance without exceeding approved flight manual limits (14 CFR 91.9). Second, assess whether OGE hover power is available; if not, a vertical takeoff is not possible. Third, consider whether the available runway length and performance data support the departure. Options include: requesting Runway 33 (opposite direction) for a headwind departure; requesting a different takeoff direction if the airport layout allows; or accepting the takeoff only if POH performance data explicitly permits the tailwind condition at your weight and density altitude. You should communicate with the tower before accepting the clearance, as attempting a marginal takeoff to meet ATC expectations is a classic pressured decision-making trap. Under 14 CFR 91.3, you as PIC retain final authority and responsibility.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.A.S6.006	Repositioning to take advantage of a headwind is consistent with FAA guidance — the Helicopter Flying Handbook specifically states that before a maximum performance takeoff, you should reposition the helicopter to the most downwind area of the available surface to maximize your takeoff run or climb angle. To establish the required stabilized hover prior to takeoff, hover-taxi carefully to the desired point at the downwind edge of the pad, come to a complete stationary hover, confirm aircraft trim, heading alignment into the wind, and power check. Only once all parameters are stabilized should you begin the departure. Factors guiding repositioning include: available hover power vs. OGE requirements, obstacle heights and positions, wind direction and strength, and confined area boundaries.	->	Repositioning to take advantage of a headwind is consistent with FAA guidance — the Helicopter Flying Handbook specifically states that before a maximum performance takeoff, you should reposition the helicopter to establish an optimal takeoff orientation that maximizes your headwind advantage, effective translational lift (ETL), and climb angle. To establish the required stabilized hover prior to takeoff, hover-taxi carefully to the desired repositioning point, come to a complete stationary hover, confirm aircraft trim, heading alignment into the wind, and power check. Only once all parameters are stabilized should you begin the departure. Factors guiding repositioning include: available hover power vs. OGE requirements, obstacle heights and positions, wind direction and strength, and confined area boundaries. Maximize the use of available headwind to improve climb performance and obstacle clearance.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.A.S10.005	In most American helicopters with counterclockwise-rotating main rotors, increasing collective increases torque, which requires right pedal. However, as airspeed increases through ETL, tail rotor efficiency improves and less right pedal may be needed. The leftward yaw tendency you're observing is likely torque-induced or a P-factor/dissymmetry effect. More importantly, with a right crosswind, the helicopter will weathervane into the wind (right), and the tendency to yaw left works against that. I need to coordinate pedals to maintain the desired heading or desired crab angle while simultaneously using right cyclic to prevent leftward ground track drift. These are two separate corrections — yaw is controlled with pedals, lateral drift with cyclic — and they must be managed independently but simultaneously.	->	In most American helicopters with counterclockwise-rotating main rotors, increasing collective increases torque, which requires right pedal to maintain heading. As airspeed increases through ETL, tail rotor efficiency improves and the aircraft naturally weathervanes into the wind (rightward in this case). The leftward yaw you initially observe is likely a transient effect of torque compensation or momentary loss of directional control authority at very low airspeed. With a right crosswind, use right pedal to maintain your desired heading or crab angle while simultaneously using right cyclic to prevent leftward ground track drift. Remember: pedal controls heading/yaw; cyclic controls ground track/lateral position. These inputs must be coordinated independently.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.A.S11.006	The noise abatement curfew is an operationally imposed restriction, not necessarily an FAA regulatory prohibition, but compliance is expected as a matter of good airmanship, community relations, and potentially contractual obligation to the operator. As PIC, you should inform your company dispatcher or operations that the 0745 departure conflicts with the published curfew. Unless there is an emergency, you should delay the departure until 0800. Departing early could result in violations of local ordinances, loss of operating agreements, and reputational damage to the operator. You have no regulatory authority to override a safety-of-flight issue, but here the curfew does not create a safety issue — delaying 15 minutes is the correct course of action.	->	A published noise abatement curfew is an operationally imposed or locally enforced restriction. Compliance is expected as a matter of good airmanship, community relations, and often contractual obligation to the operator. As PIC, you must inform your company dispatcher or operations that the 0745 departure conflicts with the published curfew. Unless there is a genuine emergency, you should delay the departure until 0800. Departing early could result in violations of local ordinances, loss of operating agreements, and reputational damage to the operator. You have no regulatory authority to override the curfew, and delaying 15 minutes poses no safety-of-flight risk — compliance is the correct course of action.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.K1.005	During a crosswind approach, translational lift is partially degraded compared to a direct headwind approach because the crosswind component does not contribute as effectively to airflow through the rotor disk. As a result, ETL is partially lost at a higher groundspeed than it would be in a direct headwind, and the transition through ETL may feel more abrupt or occur earlier in the approach profile. The pilot should anticipate that additional collective will be required sooner to compensate for the loss of translational lift. Additionally, the crosswind will try to weathervane the nose or push the helicopter off the intended ground track, requiring corrective cyclic and antitorque pedal inputs throughout the approach to maintain alignment and track.	->	ETL occurs at a specific airspeed regardless of wind direction. During a crosswind approach, ETL is achieved at the same airspeed as a headwind approach, but the groundspeed at which you reach ETL will be different because the crosswind vector reduces the effective headwind component. This means you may reach ETL at a higher groundspeed in a pure crosswind than in a direct headwind. The crosswind also creates a weather-vaning effect that will push the helicopter's nose or drift the ground track, requiring corrective cyclic and antitorque pedal inputs throughout the approach to maintain alignment. Anticipate that you will need additional collective sooner to compensate for the degraded energy profile, and be prepared to apply continuous cross-control coordination to track the desired ground path.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.K2.001	A headwind reduces your groundspeed for a given airspeed, which means you reach effective translational lift (ETL) at a lower groundspeed and lose ETL at a higher altitude during the approach. The wind essentially stretches out the approach, giving you more time at ETL speeds. Power requirements are generally reduced because the relative wind assists in maintaining rotor efficiency. The approach angle can be maintained more precisely because groundspeed is slower, giving the pilot more time to make corrections.	->	A headwind reduces your groundspeed for a given airspeed, which means you reach effective translational lift (ETL) at a lower groundspeed. The wind provides aerodynamic lift assistance, making it easier to manage the descent profile and giving you more time to establish a stable approach. Power requirements to hover do not change with wind direction, but the headwind allows you to transition through the deceleration phase more gradually, improving control authority and safety margins. The approach angle can be maintained more precisely because groundspeed is slower, giving the pilot more time to make corrections.
