AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.R1.002	Effective translational lift is the improved rotor efficiency that occurs when the rotor system moves out of its own disturbed downwash and begins encountering cleaner, undisturbed air. This typically occurs between 16 and 24 knots of forward airspeed, with maximum translational lift around 16–24 knots depending on the specific helicopter. During an approach, ETL is lost as the helicopter decelerates below approximately 16 knots. At that point, the pilot must increase collective to compensate for the loss of lift, requiring more power. On a steep approach, ETL is lost at a higher altitude than during a normal approach, requiring an earlier and larger collective increase.	->	Effective translational lift is the improved rotor efficiency that occurs when the rotor system moves forward and encounters cleaner, undisturbed air instead of its own downwash. ETL begins to develop around 16–24 knots of forward airspeed and peaks within that range, depending on the specific helicopter. During an approach to a hover, ETL is progressively lost as the helicopter decelerates through approximately 16–24 knots. Below this airspeed range, the pilot must increase collective to maintain altitude, requiring additional power. The exact airspeed at which maximum ETL occurs and is fully lost varies by helicopter type.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.R2.001	A tailwind increases groundspeed for a given airspeed, meaning the helicopter arrives over the landing point faster and with more energy to dissipate. It also reduces the effectiveness of translational lift because effective translational lift (ETL) depends on airflow through the rotor disk — a tailwind can cause the helicopter to lose ETL at a higher groundspeed and require more power to maintain the approach profile. A headwind, by contrast, provides beneficial airflow into the rotor disk, reduces groundspeed, and allows ETL to be maintained at lower groundspeeds. Tailwind approaches increase the risk of a hard landing, overrunning the landing area, and settling with power if the pilot is slow to add collective near touchdown.	->	A tailwind increases groundspeed for a given airspeed, meaning the helicopter covers more ground distance during deceleration and arrives over the landing point faster, requiring more distance and time to dissipate energy. This increases the risk of overshooting the landing zone and reduces the opportunity to complete a stable deceleration to hover. Additionally, because the pilot must reduce true airspeed to a lower value to maintain control, a tailwind may delay recovery of the benefits of effective translational lift. A headwind, by contrast, reduces groundspeed for the same airspeed, provides more time and distance for deceleration, and allows the helicopter to remain in the ETL regime longer, making the approach safer and more controllable.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.R2.002	Vortex ring state is an aerodynamic condition in which the helicopter descends into its own rotor downwash, causing the rotor to operate in a recirculating vortex. The blade stall begins near the hub and progresses outward. The three conditions that must exist simultaneously are: (1) a powered condition — the engine is producing some torque, not autorotation; (2) a low airspeed — typically below approximately 10–15 knots effective translational lift speed; and (3) a high rate of descent — typically 300 feet per minute or greater. All three must be present at the same time for VRS to develop. It is most likely during steep, slow, powered approaches.	->	Vortex ring state is an aerodynamic condition in which the helicopter descends into its own rotor downwash, causing the rotor to operate in a recirculating vortex. Blade stall begins near the hub and progresses outward. The three conditions that must exist simultaneously are: (1) a powered condition — the engine is producing torque, not autorotation; (2) a low airspeed — typically below approximately 10–15 knots (where effective translational lift is lost or degraded); and (3) a high rate of descent — typically 300 feet per minute or greater. All three must be present at the same time for VRS to develop. It is most likely during steep, slow, powered approaches.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.R3.005	A sudden crosswind increase during the go-around transition creates several risks: (1) The helicopter may drift laterally toward obstacles or terrain if cyclic is not immediately applied into the wind; (2) In ground effect at low speed, a strong crosswind can challenge the tail rotor's ability to maintain heading, especially if the crosswind is from the left in an American helicopter (worsening LTE susceptibility on the left quartering tailwind sector); (3) The pilot must apply and maintain into-wind cyclic to counteract drift while simultaneously adding collective, managing torque with pedals, and establishing a climb attitude — a high workload combination. The pilot should establish a climb attitude, clear obstacles, and transition away from the surface as efficiently as possible before attempting to correct course. The go-around path should be planned upwind when possible to improve climb performance and reduce drift risk.	->	A sudden crosswind increase during the go-around transition creates several risks: (1) The helicopter may drift laterally toward obstacles or terrain if cyclic is not immediately applied into the wind; (2) In ground effect at low speed, a strong crosswind can challenge the tail rotor's ability to maintain heading and may reduce tail rotor effectiveness in certain wind direction combinations; (3) The pilot must apply and maintain into-wind cyclic to counteract drift while simultaneously adding collective, managing torque with pedals, and establishing a climb attitude — a high workload combination. The pilot should establish a climb attitude, clear obstacles, and transition away from the surface as efficiently as possible before attempting to correct course. The go-around path should be planned upwind when possible to improve climb performance and reduce drift risk.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.R3.008	The specific risk is inadvertent IMC or complete loss of visual reference leading to spatial disorientation — a condition where vestibular senses override instrument interpretation, causing the pilot to make control inputs that drive the helicopter into an unusual attitude. At 30 feet AGL with no visual horizon and no established instrument scan, the pilot has no reliable sensory means to determine the helicopter's attitude. This is compounded by the leans, somatogravic illusions from acceleration during the go-around, and the fact that the vestibular system cannot distinguish between pitch, roll, and linear acceleration. The correct procedure is to immediately transition to instruments: establish a wings-level, coordinated climb using the attitude indicator as the primary reference, set climb power, trim appropriately, and do not attempt to return to visual conditions by descending — climbing to a safe altitude first eliminates terrain risk. If instrument rated and current, execute an instrument departure; if not, declare an emergency with ATC and request vectors and assistance.	->	The specific risk is inadvertent IMC or complete loss of visual reference leading to spatial disorientation — a condition where vestibular senses override instrument interpretation, causing the pilot to make control inputs that drive the helicopter into an unusual attitude. At 30 feet AGL with no visual horizon and no established instrument scan, the pilot has no reliable sensory means to determine the helicopter's attitude. This is compounded by the leans, somatogravic illusions from acceleration during the go-around, and the fact that the vestibular system cannot distinguish between pitch, roll, and linear acceleration. The correct procedure is to immediately transition to instruments: establish a wings-level, coordinated climb using the attitude indicator as the primary reference, set climb power, trim appropriately, and do not attempt to return to visual conditions by descending — climbing to a safe altitude first eliminates terrain risk. If instrument rated and current, execute an instrument departure; if not, declare an emergency with ATC and request vectors and assistance. Historical accident data consistently shows that a VFR pilot who becomes fully IMC in this scenario faces extremely high risk of loss of control within minutes, emphasizing the critical importance of pre-approach planning to avoid this scenario entirely.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.R5.001	The most common sources of distraction during a helicopter approach include ATC communications, cockpit tasks such as checklist review or radio frequency changes, passenger activity, and fixation on the landing spot itself. Fixation on the touchdown point is particularly dangerous because it causes the pilot to neglect monitoring airspeed, rate of descent, and rotor RPM — all critical parameters during the approach. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook emphasizes maintaining a systematic scan of outside references and cockpit instruments throughout the approach to prevent tunnel vision.	->	Common sources of distraction during a helicopter approach include ATC communications, cockpit tasks such as checklist review or radio frequency changes, passenger activity, and fixation on the landing spot itself. Fixation on the touchdown point is particularly dangerous because it causes the pilot to neglect monitoring airspeed, rate of descent, and rotor RPM — all critical parameters during the approach. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook emphasizes maintaining a systematic scan of outside references and cockpit instruments throughout the approach to prevent tunnel vision and loss of situational awareness.
