AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.R6.008	LTE is an aerodynamic phenomenon — the tail rotor is mechanically intact and producing thrust, but the thrust is aerodynamically insufficient to overcome torque due to wind azimuth, low airspeed, high power demand, or a combination. A mechanical tail rotor failure involves an actual loss of drive (broken shaft, separated blade, drive system failure), which typically produces a sudden, violent, and complete loss of anti-torque with no pedal response whatsoever, often accompanied by unusual vibration or a yaw onset that is immediate and complete. In LTE, pedal input may partially slow the yaw rate (especially early), and accelerating forward may restore effectiveness. In a mechanical failure, no amount of pedal or airspeed change will restore directional control. The distinction matters critically because: for LTE, the first response is forward cyclic and pedal input to try to recover; for mechanical failure, that time is wasted — autorotation must be entered immediately. A pilot who mistakes LTE for mechanical failure and immediately enters autorotation may unnecessarily lose a recoverable situation; a pilot who mistakes mechanical failure for LTE and spends precious altitude trying aerodynamic recovery inputs may lose the chance for a controlled autorotation.	->	LTE is an aerodynamic phenomenon — the tail rotor is mechanically intact and producing thrust, but the thrust is aerodynamically insufficient to overcome torque due to wind azimuth, low airspeed, high power demand, or a combination. A mechanical tail rotor failure involves an actual loss of drive (broken shaft, separated blade, drive system failure), which typically produces a sudden, violent, and complete loss of anti-torque with no pedal response whatsoever, often accompanied by unusual vibration or a yaw onset that is immediate and complete. In LTE, pedal input may partially slow the yaw rate (especially early), and accelerating forward may restore effectiveness. In a mechanical failure, no amount of pedal or airspeed change will restore directional control. The distinction matters critically because: for LTE, the first response is forward cyclic and pedal input to try to recover; for mechanical failure, that time is wasted — autorotation must be entered immediately. A pilot who mistakes LTE for mechanical failure and immediately enters autorotation may unnecessarily lose a recoverable situation; a pilot who mistakes mechanical failure for LTE and spends precious altitude trying aerodynamic recovery inputs may lose the chance for a controlled autorotation. [Follow-up correction:] With a complete tail rotor mechanical failure, the helicopter will spin in the torque direction (right yaw for counterclockwise rotor systems). Landing into the wind can cause the aircraft to weathervane unpredictably as it enters ground effect, potentially worsening control authority. However, the primary objective is maintaining rotor RPM and achieving the lowest possible vertical speed at impact in a relatively flat attitude. The best heading for touchdown depends on terrain, obstacles, and aircraft attitude at the moment—not a universal rule. Reference FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11 guidance on autorotative touchdown to minimize energy at contact.
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.S2.003	When a control tower is not in operation, the CTAF (Common Traffic Advisory Frequency) is used for self-announce position reports. The CTAF is published on sectional charts (shown with a magenta 'C' symbol next to the frequency), in the Chart Supplement (Airport/Facility Directory), and on approach plates. At many non-towered airports the CTAF is the UNICOM frequency, commonly 122.8 MHz, but it varies by airport.	->	The CTAF (Common Traffic Advisory Frequency) is published on sectional charts and in the Chart Supplement (Airport/Facility Directory) under the airport entry. CTAF frequencies vary by airport and must be looked up for each location—do not assume a default frequency. At many non-towered airports the CTAF is the UNICOM frequency (such as 122.8 MHz), but the specific frequency is airport-dependent. Pilots use CTAF to make self-announce position reports at airports without operating control towers.
