AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.S6.003	As the helicopter decelerates through ETL (approximately 16–24 knots of translational airflow), the efficiency of the rotor system decreases and the helicopter becomes more susceptible to lateral drift from the crosswind. The cyclic crosswind correction must be actively managed and often increased to prevent the helicopter from drifting off the ground track, because the stabilizing effect of forward airspeed is reducing. More pedal input may also be needed to maintain directional control as translational lift is lost.	->	As the helicopter continues to decelerate below ETL airspeed near the ground (approximately 16–24 knots of translational airflow), control authority and lateral stability decrease significantly. The helicopter becomes more susceptible to lateral drift from the crosswind component because forward airspeed—which provides aerodynamic stability and control effectiveness—is reducing. The cyclic crosswind correction must be actively managed and often increased to prevent the helicopter from drifting off the ground track. More pedal input may also be needed to maintain directional control as translational lift effects diminish and hover controls dominate.
