AI_EDIT	CH.V.D.K4.006	High humidity reduces air density because water vapor (which is lighter than dry air) displaces oxygen and nitrogen molecules. This effectively increases density altitude beyond what pressure altitude and temperature alone would indicate. Standard density altitude calculations using an E6B or flight computer typically use only pressure altitude and temperature — they do not account for humidity. This means on a hot, humid day, actual performance will be worse than the density altitude figure suggests. During a steep approach, the pilot may find the helicopter performing more poorly than expected — requiring more collective, producing less power margin, and experiencing a lower hover ceiling than the charts predict. FAA guidance acknowledges humidity's effect on performance and advises pilots to treat high humidity as an additional performance-degrading factor, particularly critical when power margins are already tight on a steep approach.	->	High humidity does reduce air density (water vapor is lighter than dry air), but the effect is typically smaller and secondary to temperature effects. Standard density altitude calculations using pressure altitude and temperature do not mathematically include a humidity adjustment per international standard atmosphere convention. However, humidity does degrade actual performance beyond what standard DA alone predicts. On a hot, humid day, pilots should treat the calculated DA as a lower bound and add an additional conservative safety margin to estimated performance capability, particularly critical during steep approaches where power margins are already tight. FAA guidance (FAA-H-8083-21B) acknowledges humidity's performance effects and advises pilots to apply conservative margins rather than attempt humidity-corrected density altitude calculations.
