AI_EDIT	CH.V.G.K1.008	This scenario combines multiple go-around triggers: (1) Loss of reliable visual reference is itself a go-around/missed approach criterion. If the pilot cannot positively determine altitude, sink rate, or distance from the landing area, continuing the approach is unsafe. The approach becomes a guess, and guesses at low altitude are lethal. (2) Night visual illusions: The Helicopter Flying Handbook (Chapter 12) describes several illusions specific to night flight. A featureless or dark approach environment (black hole approach) removes the normal visual cues used to judge height above ground, angle of approach, and rate of closure. Pilots tend to fly lower and steeper than intended in black hole conditions because the visual references that normally indicate the correct profile are absent. This can result in controlled flight into terrain (CFIT). (3) Unlit helipads remove the visual anchor points — perimeter lights, touchdown zone markers, and obstruction lights — that normally allow the pilot to judge approach angle. Without these, the pilot may misidentify the pad, misjudge the approach angle, or fail to see obstacles in the approach path. (4) A go-around should be initiated any time the pilot has doubt about the safety of continuing. The HFH states that night VFR seeing conditions are classified as high or low lighting based on moon illumination, cloud cover, and surface reflectivity — low lighting conditions dramatically reduce situational awareness on approach.	->	This scenario combines multiple go-around triggers: (1) Loss of reliable visual reference is itself a go-around/missed approach criterion. If the pilot cannot positively determine altitude, sink rate, or distance from the landing area, continuing the approach is unsafe. (2) Night visual illusions: The Helicopter Flying Handbook (Chapter 12) describes several illusions specific to night flight. A featureless or dark approach environment (black hole approach) removes the normal visua