AI_EDIT	CH.V.G.K3.008	A stabilized approach gate is a pre-defined point on the approach — typically at a specific height AGL — at which the pilot evaluates whether all approach parameters are within acceptable limits. If they are not, the go-around is executed automatically without further deliberation. For helicopter operations, a reasonable gate might be set at 100–200 feet AGL on a normal approach. At that gate, the pilot confirms: correct approach angle (on profile), appropriate airspeed for the approach type, stable descent rate, correct alignment with the landing area, obstacle clearance confirmed, and adequate power margin. If any single parameter is outside limits at the gate, the go-around is initiated — no re-evaluation, no 'maybe I can fix it.' This system leverages ADM principles by removing in-the-moment deliberation under pressure, replacing it with pre-planned, objective criteria. It directly combats get-there-itis, press-on-itis, and the tendency to rationalize continuing a deteriorating approach. The FAA's ADM material notes that accidents typically involve a chain of poor decisions rather than one catastrophic error — the stabilized approach gate is designed to break that chain early.	->	A stabilized approach gate is a pre-defined point on the approach — typically at a specific height AGL — at which the pilot evaluates whether all approach parameters are within acceptable limits. If they are not, the go-around is executed automatically without further deliberation. For helicopter operations, a reasonable gate might be set at 100–200 feet AGL on a normal approach. At that gate, the pilot confirms: correct approach angle (on profile), appropriate airspeed for the approach type, stable descent rate, correct alignment with the landing area, obstacle clearance confirmed, and adequate power margin. If any single parameter is outside limits at the gate, the go-around is initiated — no re-evaluation, no 'maybe I can fix it.' This system leverages ADM princ