AI_EDIT	CH.VII.A.RM.008	Under 14 CFR 91.113, a helicopter does not have an inherent right-of-way over a fixed-wing aircraft in all situations. Specifically, 91.113(d) states that an aircraft in distress has right of way over all others; 91.113(e) gives right of way to the aircraft on final approach or landing over those in flight or on the surface. However, in this scenario the fixed-wing is departing (rolling for takeoff), not on approach. The helicopter is in a go-around climb and is already airborne — both aircraft have conflicting flight paths. The helicopter pilot's immediate obligations are: (1) Under 91.113(b), see and avoid — regardless of right-of-way, both pilots share this duty. (2) Make an immediate CTAF broadcast identifying position and intentions to alert the departing aircraft. (3) Maneuver the helicopter laterally or vertically to increase separation — helicopters have superior maneuverability and can sidestep, climb rapidly, or decelerate in ways fixed-wing cannot. (4) Do not rely solely on the assumption the fixed-wing pilot will see and avoid the helicopter. At a non-towered airport with no ground control, runway incursion risk is highest when CTAF communication fails — the pilot must treat the situation as an active conflict and maneuver accordingly.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.113, a helicopter does not have an inherent right-of-way over a fixed-wing aircraft in all situations. Specifically: 91.113(d) states that an aircraft in distress has right of way over all others; 91.113(f) gives right of way to a landing aircraft over those in flight or on the ground — but a departing aircraft is neither a landing aircraft nor on final approach and therefore does not receive this priority. The helicopter is in a go-around climb and is already airborne — both aircraft have conflicting flight paths. The helicopter pilot's immediate obligations are: (1) Under 91.113(b), see and avoid — regardless of right-of-way, both pilots share this duty. (2) Make an immediate CT