AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.RM.Low.006	Flying too fast during autorotation increases parasite drag significantly — parasite drag increases with the square of velocity. This higher drag on the airframe and rotor blade surfaces will tend to drag rotor RPM upward, potentially into the yellow or red arc overspeed range, especially if the pilot raises the nose to slow down and simultaneously allows RPM to spike. However, the more immediate risk of excess speed in a turn is that it requires a steeper bank to achieve the same rate of turn, increasing load factor and rotor drag. Flying too slowly creates a different but equally dangerous problem: increased induced drag as the blades work harder at high AOA to generate lift at low airspeed — this accelerates RPM decay toward the lower limit. The published autorotation airspeed represents the optimal balance where the rotor system sustains RPM most efficiently and glide distance is maximized.	->	Flying too fast during autorotation increases parasite drag significantly — parasite drag increases with the square of velocity. This higher drag on the airframe requires the pilot to carry more collective to maintain proper descent rate, and creates difficulty in controlling the descent profile precisely, making accurate flare timing more challenging. Flying too slowly creates a different but equally dangerous problem: at low airspeed, the rotor blades must operate at high angles of attack to generate lift, increasing induced drag and accelerating rotor RPM decay toward the lower limit. The published autorotation airspeed represents the optimal balance where rotor RPM is sustained most efficiently, descent rate is manageable, and glide distance is maximized.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.RM.002	During autorotation, upward airflow through the rotor disk driven by the descent keeps the rotor spinning. An excessively high rate of descent can initially drive rotor RPM above limits (overspeed), requiring collective to be raised to bleed off RPM. However, if the excessive rate is not arrested before the flare and landing phase, there may be insufficient rotor energy (inertia) remaining after the flare to cushion the landing. Additionally, if the descent is too fast and the flare is late or inadequate, the helicopter will impact the ground with high vertical velocity. The FAA notes that rotor stall in helicopters is unrecoverable, making early recognition and control of descent rate critical.	->	During autorotation, upward airflow through the rotor disk driven by the descent keeps the rotor spinning. An excessively high rate of descent increases the velocity of this airflow, which can drive rotor RPM above limits (overspeed). To control overspeed caused by excessive descent, the pilot raises the collective pitch, which increases blade drag and slows rotor RPM. However, if the descent rate is not arrested before the flare and landing phase, there may be insufficient rotor energy remaining after the flare to cushion the landing. Additionally, if descent is too fast and the flare is late or inadequate, the helicopter will impact with high vertical velocity. Conversely, if descent rate is too shallow or too slow, rotor RPM may decay. The pilot must balance descent rate to maintain rotor RPM in the normal operating range while preserving sufficient energy for landing.
AI_EDIT	Rolling.004	At 200 feet AGL, options are severely limited. The skid means excess right yaw is occurring, suggesting insufficient left pedal was applied during rollout in a counterclockwise rotor system. The risks include: (1) heading misalignment with the landing area, (2) increased rotor drag from uncoordinated flight contributing to RPM decay, and (3) insufficient altitude to make large corrections. Management involves immediately applying smooth left pedal to stop the yaw, accepting the remaining heading offset rather than attempting an aggressive correction, and focusing on rotor RPM and landing area alignment. Large corrective inputs at 200 feet AGL can cause further RPM loss or loss of control.	->	At 200 feet AGL, options are severely limited. The skid means excess right yaw is occurring. In a counterclockwise rotor system (typical for most U.S. helicopters), this suggests insufficient left pedal was applied during rollout. The risks include: (1) heading misalignment with the landing area, (2) increased rotor drag from uncoordinated flight contributing to RPM decay, and (3) insufficient altitude to make large corrections. Management involves immediately applying smooth left pedal to stop the yaw, accepting the remaining heading offset rather than attempting an aggressive correction, and focusing on rotor RPM and landing area alignment. Large corrective inputs at 200 feet AGL can cause further RPM loss or loss of control.
AI_EDIT	Rolling.006	Being high at rollout creates a risk of landing long or fast, potentially overrunning the intended landing area, or arriving at the flare point with insufficient time and altitude to bleed airspeed effectively. Options include: (1) increasing the rate of descent by using a steeper approach angle — but this requires awareness that loss of ETL occurs at a higher altitude than a normal approach, requiring earlier collective increase to arrest the descent. (2) If the landing area is large, accepting the long landing point. (3) Performing a go-around if power is available — but in a power-off autorotation this option doesn't exist. The primary lesson is to plan turns during autorotation to roll out on the correct glide slope, not just on heading.	->	Being high at rollout creates a risk of landing long or fast, potentially overrunning the intended landing area, or arriving at the flare point with insufficient time and altitude to bleed airspeed effectively. Options include: (1) increasing the rate of descent by using a steeper approach angle — but this requires awareness that in a steeper glide, the helicopter will reach ETL (the point where autorotation transitions to powered flight or flare) at a higher altitude, requiring earlier collective increase to arrest the descent and leaving less altitude margin for error. (2) If the landing area is large, accepting the long landing point. (3) Performing a go-around if power is available — but in a power-off autorotation this option doesn't exist. The primary lesson is to plan turns during autorotation to roll out on the correct glide slope, not just on heading.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.SK_RADIO.008	Under 14 CFR 91.3(b), a pilot who deviates from an ATC clearance during an emergency must, upon request from ATC, send a written report to the Administrator. If ATC does not request a report, you are still encouraged to notify ATC as soon as practical after landing and explain the deviation. Regarding ASRS: filing a NASA Aviation Safety Report (ASRS) within 10 days of the incident provides immunity from civil penalty for inadvertent violations — however, it does NOT provide immunity from certificate suspension or for criminal acts, deliberate violations, or accidents. In this scenario, the emergency nature of the deviation would likely result in no action, but an ASRS report provides an additional layer of protection and contributes to the safety database. After landing, the first radio action is to notify ATC of your status and location, then file ASRS as a precaution.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.3(b), if the Administrator or ATC requests a written report of the emergency deviation, you must submit it. Separately, under 14 CFR 91.25 and FAA AC 00-46F, you may file a NASA Aviation Safety Report (ASRS) within 10 days of the incident; this provides immunity from civil penalty for inadvertent violations. ASRS immunity does not apply to deliberate violations, accidents, or certificate enforcement actions under 14 CFR 91.13 (careless/reckless operation). After landing, notify ATC immediately of your status and location. Filing an ASRS report proactively within 10 days provides a layer of protection and contributes to the safety database, but does not shield you from enforcement if the deviation resulted from your own carelessness or deliberate conduct.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.SK.006	As the helicopter decelerates through approximately 16–24 knots on the autorotation approach, ETL is lost and rotor efficiency decreases. In a powered approach you would add collective to compensate, but in autorotation you must conserve collective pitch for the flare and cushion. The practical effect is that rotor RPM may trend slightly higher or lower depending on descent rate and disk angle. You plan for ETL loss by ensuring you enter the flare at the correct airspeed and altitude — typically 40–60 knots and 75–100 feet AGL — so that the flare arrests descent rate before ETL is fully lost, and the final collective application uses stored rotor inertia to cushion the touchdown. In a steep approach, ETL is lost at a higher altitude, so you must recognize this earlier and adjust your collective timing accordingly.	->	As the helicopter decelerates through approximately 16–24 knots during the autorotation approach, effective translational lift is progressively lost and rotor efficiency decreases. In a powered approach you would add collective to maintain rotor RPM; in autorotation, you must conserve collective pitch for the flare and landing cushion. The practical effect is that rotor RPM may trend higher or lower depending on descent rate and rotor disk angle. You plan for ETL loss by entering the flare at the airspeed and altitude specified in your aircraft's AFM—typically in the range of 40–60 knots and 50–100 feet AGL for most single-rotor helicopters—so that the flare arrests descent rate before ETL is fully lost and the final collective application uses rotor inertia to cushion touchdown. Recognize that in a steep approach, ETL is lost at a higher altitude; adjust your flare timing accordingly and verify all parameters in your specific aircraft's AFM.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.SK_CLEAR.003	Clearing the area is performed before initiating the maneuver. The pilot should climb to or confirm the appropriate entry altitude, stabilize airspeed, and then conduct the area-clearing scan. This ensures that all preparatory tasks do not distract from the clearing scan, and that the area remains clear at the moment of maneuver entry.	->	Clearing turns should be performed after achieving the appropriate entry altitude and airspeed and stabilizing the helicopter. Once at entry altitude and stabilized on airspeed, conduct a thorough 360-degree visual clearing scan to ensure the area is free of other aircraft and obstacles. Re-confirm the area is clear immediately before initiating the autorotation maneuver itself. This sequence ensures that the area is confirmed clear at the moment of actual maneuver entry, and that your clearing scan occurs from a stabilized platform without the distraction of establishing altitude or airspeed.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.SK_ENTALT.001	The minimum entry altitude for a practice autorotation is typically 500 feet AGL above the intended touchdown point, though many training programs and operators specify 700-1000 feet AGL to allow adequate time for rotor RPM stabilization, turn execution, flare, and cushion. Altitude matters because once you commit to autorotation entry, you have a fixed energy budget — insufficient altitude means insufficient time to complete the maneuver safely before ground contact.	->	The minimum entry altitude for a straight-in autorotation is approximately 500 feet AGL in favorable conditions, but turning autorotations are significantly more demanding and typically require 700–1000 feet AGL or higher, depending on aircraft rotor inertia, sink rate, and density altitude. Altitude matters because once you commit to autorotation entry, you have a fixed energy budget—insufficient altitude means insufficient time to complete the maneuver safely before ground contact. Always consult the specific aircraft POH/RFM and your training program minimums for exact entry altitudes.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.Initiate.005	The turn to final should be initiated early enough to roll out aligned with the intended landing area with sufficient altitude remaining for a proper flare — typically no lower than 100–150 feet AGL depending on the aircraft. During the turn, the centrifugal loading on the blades increases, which can cause rotor RPM to rise. The pilot must monitor and manage RPM throughout the turn using collective, being careful not to over-raise collective and bleed RPM excessively. Steep bank angles increase load factor and RPM rise more aggressively, so bank angle must be managed to avoid exceeding rotor RPM limits or arriving on final with RPM outside the normal range.	->	The turn to final should be initiated early enough to roll out aligned with the intended landing area with sufficient altitude remaining for a proper flare—typically no lower than 100–150 feet AGL depending on the aircraft. During the turn, the pilot must increase collective to maintain adequate pitch control and arrest descent rate. If collective is raised too much, the rotor becomes over-pitched and rotor RPM may rise above the desired operating range. The pilot must carefully manage collective throughout the turn to maintain RPM within the green arc while maintaining adequate control authority for pitch and roll. Steep bank angles increase the control inputs required, so bank angle must be managed to avoid excessive RPM rise or arriving on final with RPM outside the normal range.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.002	Properly trimmed in autorotation means the helicopter is in coordinated flight with no slip or skid — the fuselage is aligned with the relative wind and the pilot is not holding unbalanced control pressure. In a helicopter, trim is primarily achieved with the antitorque pedals. During autorotation, torque from the engine is absent, so pedal inputs are adjusted to keep the nose aligned and eliminate any yaw tendency, resulting in a balanced, efficient glide.	->	Properly trimmed in autorotation means the helicopter is in coordinated flight with no slip or skid—the fuselage is aligned with the relative wind and the pilot is not holding unbalanced control pressure. Trim is achieved through integrated use of antitorque pedals (to eliminate yaw and maintain heading alignment) and cyclic inputs (to maintain balanced pitch and roll attitude). During autorotation, the loss of engine torque removes the normal nose-left yaw effect, requiring reduced left pedal and/or slight right pedal input. The cyclic should be neutral with the aircraft balanced; any persistent pressure indicates poor trim. A properly trimmed autorotation is balanced, efficient, and allows the pilot to focus on altitude and landing area management.
