AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.005	The high airspeed indicates excessive nose-low pitch attitude, likely caused by insufficient aft cyclic input after entry or forward cyclic drift. The high rotor RPM is a consequence — faster airspeed increases airflow up through the rotor disk, which accelerates rotor RPM. The correction is to apply gentle aft cyclic to raise the nose toward the proper glide attitude, which simultaneously reduces airspeed back toward the target and reduces the energy input to the rotor system, allowing RPM to stabilize within the green arc. If RPM is approaching the upper limit, a small increase in collective may be momentarily used to load the rotor and reduce RPM, but this must be done carefully to avoid a collective-induced altitude change that disrupts the glide profile.	->	The high airspeed indicates excessive nose-low pitch attitude, likely caused by insufficient aft cyclic input after entry or forward cyclic drift. The high rotor RPM is a consequence — faster airspeed increases airflow up through the rotor disk, which accelerates rotor RPM. The correction is to apply gentle aft cyclic to raise the nose toward the proper glide attitude, which simultaneously reduces airspeed back toward the target and reduces the energy input to the rotor system, allowing RPM to stabilize within the green arc. Maintain collective in the full-down position throughout entry and do not adjust collective to manage RPM, as this disrupts the power-off glide profile and can introduce unintended altitude changes that compromise the maneuver.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.S_Nr.004	When you roll into a banked turn during autorotation, you load the rotor disk — the rotor must produce more lift to maintain the descent rate against the increased effective weight. This increased loading raises blade pitch (if collective is unchanged) and adds drag to the rotor system, which tends to slow Nr. Additionally, if the turn is coordinated with cyclic, the change in disk attitude alters the relative airflow through the rotor, which can also affect RPM. To compensate, the pilot may need to lower collective slightly to reduce blade drag and allow Nr to recover or stabilize within the green arc. The steeper the bank and longer the turn, the more vigilant the pilot must be in monitoring and adjusting collective to prevent Nr decay.	->	When you roll into a banked turn during autorotation, the rotor disk tilts to generate the horizontal force component for the turn. This disk tilt increases rotor blade loading, which increases induced drag in the rotor system. Increased rotor drag tends to slow Nr. To compensate and maintain Nr within the green arc, the pilot must proactively lower collective slightly during the turn entry to reduce blade pitch and drag, allowing the upward airflow to sustain rotor RPM. Continuously monitor the tachometer and make small collective adjustments as needed throughout the turn. The steeper the bank angle and longer the duration of the turn, the more attentive the pilot must be in managing collective to prevent Nr decay. Recovery to a shallower bank or wings-level descent will allow Nr to stabilize.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.S_Nr.006	Corrective action: Immediately lower collective to the full-down or near-full-down position to reduce blade drag and allow upward airflow to restore Nr. Do not raise collective until Nr is firmly back in the green arc. Use aft cyclic if needed to optimize the descent attitude for rotor recovery. Monitor tachometer continuously until Nr stabilizes within limits. Prevention: The decay likely occurred because the pilot either didn't lower collective sufficiently when the turn loaded the rotor, delayed monitoring the tachometer, or used excessive bank angle for the available rotor inertia. During turns in autorotation, the pilot should anticipate increased rotor loading and proactively manage collective — slightly lower before and during the turn — while continuously cross-checking Nr against the green arc. Low-inertia rotor systems have very little margin for delay.	->	Corrective action: Immediately lower collective to the full-down or near-full-down position to reduce blade drag and allow upward airflow to restore Nr. Do not raise collective until Nr is firmly back in the green arc. Use aft cyclic if needed to optimize the descent attitude for rotor recovery. Monitor the tachometer continuously until Nr stabilizes within limits. Prevention: The decay likely occurred because the pilot did not lower collective sufficiently in anticipation of the turn's rotor loading effect, delayed in monitoring the tachometer, or used excessive bank angle for the available rotor inertia. During turns in autorotation, proactively lower collective slightly before the turn entry and adjust further during the turn to manage the increased blade loading. Continuously cross-check Nr against the green arc throughout the maneuver. Low-inertia rotor systems have very little margin for delay in control inputs.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.S.001	To extend the glide and correct an undershoot, the pilot should reduce the rate of descent by slightly reducing the airspeed with aft cyclic — but only within the autorotative glide speed range. Flying at the minimum rate of descent airspeed (rather than best glide) reduces forward progress less than it reduces sink rate, helping stretch the glide. The pilot should NOT raise the collective to arrest the descent, as this rapidly depletes rotor RPM and energy stored in the rotor system. Turns should be minimized since they increase the rate of descent and reduce glide distance.	->	To extend the glide and correct an undershoot, reduce descent rate by flying at a slower airspeed—closer to the helicopter's minimum rate of descent speed—using aft cyclic. This extends glide distance modestly because descent rate decreases slightly more than forward speed decreases. Do not raise collective, as this rapidly depletes rotor RPM and stored rotor inertia, which is dangerous. Minimize turns, which increase descent rate and reduce effective glide distance. Note that the glide extension achieved by slowing is modest and cannot substitute for better planning from cruise altitude.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.S.005	With excess altitude and an overshoot situation, the pilot has several options: execute a planned S-turn or additional 360-degree turn to dissipate altitude; select a new, farther landing area if terrain permits; or a combination of both. When executing the additional turn, the pilot must monitor rotor RPM carefully since the increased blade loading in a turn will affect RPM, and entry into the turn must be smooth to avoid an RPM spike above the red line. The situation becomes unsafe when there is insufficient altitude remaining to complete the corrective turn and set up a proper flare — generally, a pilot should not initiate a 360-degree turn below approximately 500 feet AGL (exact minimums depend on the specific helicopter and POH guidance) because there may not be enough altitude remaining to complete the turn, roll out, and perform a controlled flare and cushion before touchdown.	->	With excess altitude and an overshoot situation, execute a planned S-turn or additional 360-degree turn to dissipate altitude; select a new, farther landing area if terrain permits; or combine both. When executing the additional turn, monitor rotor RPM carefully because increased blade loading causes RPM decay, requiring smooth cyclic inputs and careful collective management to maintain RPM in the green arc. The situation becomes unsafe when insufficient altitude remains to complete the corrective turn and set up a proper flare. A 360-degree turn should generally not be initiated below 600–700 feet AGL in typical training helicopters to allow adequate descent during the turn and proper flare altitude; consult the POH/RFM for specific aircraft minimums.
ESCALATE	CH.VIII.C.SK.Roll.001	The 300-foot AGL rollout minimum is widely taught but the cited ACS CH.VIII.C standard should be verified to confirm it specifies this exact figure; the distinction between industry training standards and explicit regulatory requirements is unclear.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.SK.Roll.002	The 300-foot AGL minimum is required because rolling out at low altitude while in autorotation leaves very little time and rotor RPM margin to correct for misalignment, obstacles, or a poor approach angle. Below 300 feet AGL, an uncoordinated turn or late rollout can result in an uncontrolled descent into the landing area with no time to recover. The altitude provides a buffer for final approach assessment and control stabilization.	->	Autorotation training standards recommend completing the turn and rolling out at or above 300 feet AGL to maintain a margin for final approach assessment and recovery. The ACS specifies that the helicopter must be 'rolled out and aligned with the landing area' and maintain a 'stabilized approach,' but does not prescribe a fixed altitude floor. The 300-foot benchmark reflects sound aeronautical judgment: rolling out at low altitude during autorotation leaves very little time and rotor RPM margin to correct for misalignment, obstacles, or a poor approach angle. Below this altitude, an uncoordinated turn or late rollout can result in an uncontrolled descent into the landing area with no time to recover. The altitude provides a practical buffer for final approach assessment and control stabilization.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.SK.Roll.004	At 350 feet AGL with 20 degrees of turn remaining, you have very little altitude cushion to complete the turn and still meet the 300-foot rollout standard. Your options are: (1) carefully complete the turn promptly with minimal bank to roll out at or above 300 feet, (2) select an alternate landing area that is already aligned with your current heading, or (3) execute a go-around if power is available. The risk of continuing an aggressive turn below 350 feet is that the bank angle increases induced drag on the turning blade, can disturb rotor RPM, and the helicopter may roll out below 300 feet with insufficient altitude to stabilize the approach before the flare point.	->	At 350 feet AGL with 20 degrees of turn remaining, you have minimal altitude cushion to complete the turn while maintaining a stabilized approach. Your options are: (1) carefully complete the turn promptly with minimal bank to roll out at or above 300 feet, or (2) select an alternate landing area that is already aligned with your current heading. The risk of continuing an aggressive turn below 350 feet is that the bank angle increases induced drag on the turning blade, can disturb rotor RPM, and the helicopter may roll out below stabilized approach altitude with insufficient altitude to correct for misalignment or establish a proper flare before touchdown. In a practice autorotation where engine power is unavailable or disabled, a go-around is not an option; you must complete the landing.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.S_TAILCLEAR.008	With low RPM, low altitude, and minimal airspeed, the pilot has very limited energy. The priority order is: first, maintain RPM within limits — do not allow RPM to drop below the bottom of the green arc, as rotor stall is not recoverable. If RPM is at the bottom of the green, a shallow forward cyclic input to increase inflow may help recover RPM, but must be used judiciously given the low altitude. Second, level the pitch attitude enough to ensure the tail boom is not the lowest point of the aircraft — a drastically nose-high attitude with minimal altitude leaves the tail extremely vulnerable. Third, apply collective smoothly but promptly to arrest the rate of descent using whatever rotor energy remains. A level or slightly nose-up attitude during collective application keeps the tail higher than the skids. The landing will likely be a firm touchdown, but tail boom clearance is preserved by avoiding a nose-high attitude at the moment of ground contact. Running the skids on is acceptable and preferable to a tail strike. The pilot must accept a firmer-than-normal landing rather than chase a cushioned one with inadequate RPM or altitude.	->	With low RPM, low altitude, and minimal airspeed, the pilot has very limited energy. The priority order is: first, maintain RPM within limits — do not allow RPM to drop below the bottom of the green arc, as a full rotor stall is unrecoverable at low altitude and must be avoided at all costs. If RPM is at the bottom of the green, a shallow forward cyclic input to increase inflow may help stabilize or recover RPM, but must be used judiciously given the low altitude. Second, level the pitch attitude enough to ensure the tail boom is not the lowest point of the aircraft — a drastically nose-high attitude with minimal altitude leaves the tail extremely vulnerable. Third, apply collective smoothly but promptly to arrest the rate of descent using whatever rotor energy remains. A level or slightly nose-up attitude during collective application keeps the tail higher than the skids. The landing will likely be a firm touchdown, but tail boom clearance is preserved by avoiding a nose-high attitude at the moment of ground contact. Running the skids on is acceptable and preferable to a tail strike. The pilot must accept a firmer-than-normal landing rather than chase a cushioned one with inadequate RPM or altitude.
AI_EDIT	CH.VIII.C.008	Both reactions are caused by the sudden change in torque during power application. (1) Yaw right (for a counterclockwise main rotor system): As power is applied, main rotor torque increases dramatically and rapidly. The tail rotor, which was in a reduced-power autorotation configuration, must now suddenly counteract this increased torque. If left pedal is not applied proactively, the helicopter will yaw right (opposite to main rotor rotation for counterclockwise systems). Correction: Apply left pedal simultaneously with power application to maintain heading. (2) Nose drop: As Nr and airflow through the rotor increase with power, translational lift-like effects and increased rotor thrust can shift the pitch attitude. Also, increasing collective pitch without holding aft cyclic pressure can allow the nose to drop. Additionally, for low-inertia rotor systems, the rapid Nr change can produce gyroscopic precession effects. Correction: Maintain back-cyclic pressure during power application to hold attitude. The coordinated recovery requires simultaneous collective up, left pedal, and aft cyclic pressure — all adjusted in concert as power is restored.	->	Both reactions are caused by the sudden change in torque and rotor thrust during power application. (1) Yaw right (for a counterclockwise main rotor system): As power is applied, main rotor torque increases dramatically. The tail rotor, which was in a reduced-power autorotation configuration, must now counteract this increased torque. If left pedal is not applied proactively, the helicopter will yaw right (opposite to main rotor rotation for counterclockwise systems). Correction: Apply left pedal simultaneously with power application to maintain heading. (2) Nose drop: This occurs when collective is raised without maintaining sufficient aft cyclic pressure. The increased rotor thrust combined with increased blade pitch will allow the nose to lower if not actively held back. Correction: Maintain aft cyclic pressure during power application to hold pitch attitude. The coordinated recovery requires simultaneous collective up, left pedal, and aft cyclic pressure — all adjusted in concert as power is restored.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.A.001	Pilotage is navigation by visual reference to landmarks on the ground — you identify your position by comparing what you see outside to a chart. Dead reckoning is navigation by calculation: you use a known starting point, a heading, airspeed, and elapsed time to estimate your current position. Under IFR, pilotage is largely unavailable since outside visual references are lost; dead reckoning becomes the primary backup method when electronic navigation is degraded or unavailable. The two techniques complement each other — dead reckoning gets you to an area, and pilotage (when VMC is restored) confirms your position.	->	Pilotage is navigation by visual reference to landmarks on the ground — you identify your position by comparing what you see outside to a chart. Dead reckoning is navigation by calculation: you use a known starting point, a heading, airspeed, and elapsed time to estimate your current position. Both are VFR navigation techniques. When flying IFR, you rely on electronic navigation aids (VOR, NDB, GPS) and instrument references. Pilotage and dead reckoning serve as backup methods if electronic navigation is lost while operating VFR or if forced to descend to VFR conditions due to weather or emergency. The two complement each other in VFR operations — dead reckoning gets you to an area, and pilotage confirms your position.
AUTO_APPROVE	CH.IX.A.004	The dead reckoning calculation is mathematically correct and clearly demonstrates drift caused by uncompensated wind; the question and answer properly illustrate why wind correction angle is essential during dead reckoning navigation in helicopters.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.A.004	Flying a northerly heading with a failed heading indicator requires managing two primary errors: (1) Northerly turning error — when rolling out of any turn to 360°, the compass will lag, meaning it will show a heading less than your actual heading. To compensate, you must roll out early — a common rule is to lead the rollout by the number of degrees equal to your latitude plus half your bank angle. At mid-latitudes (around 30°N), this could mean rolling out when the compass shows approximately 330° to end up on 360°. (2) Oscillation error — moderate turbulence combined with helicopter vibration will cause the compass to oscillate significantly, making it hard to pinpoint the exact rollout heading. Technique: use small, shallow banks to minimize turning error; time the rollout using an average of the oscillating compass readings; once established on heading, verify with GPS ground track if available and cross-check with VOR radials. Also account for any known compass deviation from the correction card.	->	Flying a northerly heading with a failed heading indicator requires managing two primary errors: (1) Northerly turning error — when rolling out of any turn to 360°, the compass will lag during a turn from south toward north, meaning it will show a heading less than your actual heading. To compensate, you must roll out early by approximately half your bank angle in degrees. For a 20° bank angle, roll out when the compass shows approximately 350° to end up on actual 360°. (2) Oscillation error — moderate turbulence combined with helicopter vibration will cause the compass to oscillate significantly, making it hard to pinpoint the exact rollout heading. Technique: use small, shallow banks (15° or less) to minimize turning error; average the compass needle position over several seconds rather than attempting an instantaneous reading at any single moment; once established on heading, verify with GPS ground track if available and cross-check with VOR radials. Also account for any known compass deviation from the correction card.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.A.007	At 35°N latitude, northerly turning error is significant — the compass lags when turning through north and leads when turning through south. To intercept an inbound course of 180°, the pilot first needs to determine the current position relative to the desired course, then execute a turn to 180°. Steps: (1) Establish straight-and-level coordinated flight and take a steady compass reading — average the oscillation to get current heading. (2) Determine the shortest turn direction to 180°. If currently heading north (360°/0°), a turn to 180° requires either a 180° turn through east or west. Choose the direction that avoids prolonged flight through north where error is greatest. (3) Use a standard rate turn (3°/second) or half-standard rate for better compass control. (4) When turning through southerly headings approaching 180°, the compass will LEAD — it will advance faster than actual heading. To compensate, roll out LATE: lead the rollout by approximately latitude ÷ 2 = about 17-18° when coming from a southerly direction. So begin rollout when compass shows approximately 162°-163° to roll out on actual 180°. (5) Once established on 180°, confirm with any available nav aid cross-check. (6) Fly wings-level for at least 10-15 seconds and re-read compass before making any corrections to avoid chasing oscillation.	->	At 35°N latitude, northerly turning error is significant — the compass lags when turning through north and leads when turning through south. To intercept an inbound course of 180°, the pilot first needs to determine the current position relative to the desired course, then execute a turn to 180°. Steps: (1) Establish straight-and-level coordinated flight and take a steady compass reading — average the oscillation to get current heading. (2) Determine the shortest turn direction to 180°. If currently heading north (360°/0°), a turn to 180° requires either a 180° turn through east or west. Choose the direction that avoids prolonged flight through north where error is greatest. (3) Use a standard rate turn (3°/second) or half-standard rate for better compass control. (4) When turning TO a southerly heading approaching 180°, the compass will LEAD — it will advance faster than actual heading. To compensate, roll out EARLY: begin rollout when compass shows approximately 162-163° (about 17-18° before your desired heading) so that by the time the compass catches up with your actual heading, you will be established on 180°. (5) Once established on 180°, confirm with any available nav aid cross-check. (6) Fly wings-level for at least 10-15 seconds and re-read compass before making any corrections to avoid chasing oscillation.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.A.Topography.005	Possible explanations include: (1) Positional error — you are not where you think you are, and the ridge you are viewing is a different terrain feature further along or off-track; (2) Chart orientation error — the sectional chart may not be aligned with your direction of flight, causing misidentification; (3) Perspective distortion — viewing a ridge at an oblique angle from a different altitude or distance than planned can alter its apparent orientation; (4) Variation in terrain from chart data — rare but possible with older charts in dynamic terrain areas. Correct procedure: cross-reference at least two additional positively identifiable checkpoints (rivers, highways, lakes, towns) to re-establish your position, use GPS as a backup to confirm your actual position, confirm heading against the heading indicator and magnetic compass, and do not continue along the planned route until positive ground position is established. In IFR or low-level conditions, squawk 7700 and contact ATC if unable to establish position.	->	Possible explanations include: (1) Positional error — you are not where you think you are, and the ridge you are viewing is a different terrain feature further along or off-track; (2) Chart orientation error — the sectional chart may not be aligned with your direction of flight, causing misidentification; (3) Perspective distortion — viewing a ridge at an oblique angle from a different altitude or distance than planned can alter its apparent orientation; (4) Variation in terrain from chart data — rare but possible with older charts in dynamic terrain areas. Correct procedure: cross-reference at least two additional positively identifiable checkpoints (rivers, highways, lakes, towns) to re-establish your position; use GPS as a backup to confirm your actual position; confirm heading against the heading indicator and magnetic compass. If you are unable to establish position and are flying IFR, contact ATC immediately and request vectors or radar assistance. If flying VFR and unable to establish position, contact ATC and request vectors before fuel or weather become critical. Squawk 7700 and declare an emergency only if safety becomes threatened due to fuel state, weather deterioration, or complete navigation failure.
