Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
You are operating from a small, non-towered airport that does not publish an official airport diagram. How do you maintain situational awareness during an air taxi in the absence of a published chart?

Answer (at time of rejection):
When no official airport diagram is published, I would use alternative resources such as the airport description in the Chart Supplement, satellite imagery available through EFB applications, and a thorough on-the-ground or preflight visual survey of the airport layout. I would taxi at a slow, controlled speed and use visual scanning to identify runway markings, hold-short signs, and taxiway edge markings. I would self-announce on the CTAF throughout the movement, monitor for other traffic, and plan my route conservatively, avoiding areas of ambiguity. The absence of a published diagram increases reliance on visual discipline, traffic advisories, and conservative movement.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
See duplicate ID note under the first PH.V.C.SK.006 entry. This record is the 'Use' ACS lost-on-airport question. Content is accurate: stopping, contacting ATC, and requesting progressive taxi is the correct procedure per AIM 4-3-18.
Reference to 14 CFR 91.13 (careless or reckless operation) is appropriate as a motivator for conservative behavior but is not the primary regulatory hook for this scenario — AIM 4-3-18 and the runway incursion avoidance framework are more directly applicable. The citation is not wrong but may benefit from adding AIM 4-3-19 (Taxiing) for completeness.

