Question ID:   PH.V.E.RM.005
ACS Code:      Conditions
Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
How does the direction of main rotor rotation affect which side of a slope landing is more susceptible to dynamic rollover, and why does this matter during your slope landing assessment?

Answer (at time of rejection):
On a helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor (standard US configuration), the retreating blade is on the right side and the advancing blade is on the left. Due to dissymmetry of lift compensation via flapping, the rotor disk tends to roll left (toward the retreating blade side) when power is applied in low-speed flight — this is called the transverse flow and blade flapping effect. This means that when hovering on a left-side-low slope (right skid upslope), the natural tendency of the rotor system already creates a rolling moment toward the left/downslope direction, making dynamic rollover more likely than on a right-side-low slope. During your slope landing assessment, you should recognize that a left-side-low slope increases risk because both the natural rotor tendency and any collective application will drive roll toward the downslope skid. A right-side-low slope is generally more forgiving for counterclockwise rotor helicopters. This should factor into your decision of which direction to approach and land.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The answer conflates dissymmetry of lift with lateral rotor roll tendencies in slope operations. While dissymmetry of lift (blade flapping) is a real phenomenon, the specific claim that counterclockwise rotor 'naturally creates a rolling moment toward the left/downslope direction' when on a left-side-low slope needs clarification. The natural rotor cyclic compensation and slope geometry interact, but the answer attributes the roll tendency solely to rotor direction without fully explaining the slope contact geometry.
The answer does not mention that on a slope, the rotor disk naturally tilts into the slope (downslope) due to the skid geometry and terrain — this is the primary driver of rollover risk on one side vs. the other, not rotor rotation direction alone.

