AI_EDIT	CH.IX.B.S007	When ATC says 'resume own navigation,' they are terminating the vector service and returning navigation responsibility entirely to you. You must navigate via your last ATC clearance — typically the published route or the direct clearance they issued. Acknowledge with: 'Resuming own navigation, N123AB.' You remain radar identified — ATC can still see you on their scope and can issue traffic advisories — but they are no longer providing active navigation guidance. Your responsibilities shift: you must now ensure you are on the correct route, intercepting the correct course, and complying with any altitude restrictions or crossing restrictions on the chart. Simply being radar identified means ATC has confirmed your position on their display; it does not mean they are providing navigation services or terrain separation beyond standard IFR separation. Radar identification is a status; radar vectors and navigation services are active services that must be explicitly provided.	->	If proper trim still results in a consistent high-altitude drift, the first suspect is altimeter setting error — verify the current altimeter setting from ATIS or ATC and reset the Kollsman window, as even a small error will shift indicated altitude. Second, in helicopters especially, consider whether the weight distribution or CG has shifted due to fuel burn or passenger/cargo movement, which can noticeably affect pitch attitude and trim requirements. Third, an alternate static source or cabin pressurization effects might introduce a bias (the altimeter will over-read if static pressure is slightly lower than ambient). Only after ruling out these items would I suspect a blocked static port or other system malfunction.
