AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.003	On a sectional chart, airports with fuel available are depicted with a solid blue or magenta airport symbol (depending on whether they have a control tower or not). The airport's data block will include the notation 'L' for lighting and fuel type abbreviations such as '100' for 100LL avgas or 'Jet A' for jet fuel. Absence of fuel service is typically indicated in the Chart Supplement (formerly Airport/Facility Directory). For helicopter operations, confirming Jet A availability is critical for turbine-powered aircraft. This information is found in the FAA Chart User's Guide and Chart Supplement.	->	Airport symbols on a sectional chart (blue for non-towered, magenta for towered) indicate control tower presence and runway type, NOT fuel availability. Fuel services and fuel types (100LL, Jet A, etc.) must be confirmed by consulting the Chart Supplement (airport data block), which lists all available fuel services for each airport. While the airport data block may contain abbreviated notations, the symbol color itself provides no information about fuel availability. For helicopter operations, fuel type confirmation is essential for turbine-powered aircraft requiring Jet A.
