AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.006	VFR flight plans time out 2 hours after the proposed departure time if not activated — the FSS will not initiate search and rescue based on an unactivated plan. This means if you divert and have an accident, no one will know your original intended route or activate SAR on your behalf based on that plan. The risk is a delayed search and rescue response. To manage this: (1) Immediately contact FSS at 1-800-WX-BRIEF or any ATC facility to update or cancel the original flight plan; (2) File a new flight plan or position report with your diversion destination; (3) Notify ATC of your diversion so there is an official record; (4) Brief someone on the ground (passenger contact, FBO) of your new destination. Proactive communication with FSS and ATC is the primary mitigation — do not assume an old, unactivated plan provides any safety net.	->	A VFR flight plan that has not been activated by the pilot will be closed by FSS 2 hours after the proposed departure time. If the plan was never activated, SAR will not be initiated based on that plan if you divert and have an accident. The key risk is that no one is looking for you based on an unactivated plan, and your original intended route will not be on file. To manage this: (1) Immediately contact FSS at 1-800-WX-BRIEF or any ATC facility to update or cancel the original flight plan; (2) File a new flight plan or position report with your diversion destination; (3) Notify ATC of your diversion so there is an official record; (4) Brief someone on the ground (passenger contact, FBO) of your new destination. Proactive communication with FSS and ATC is the primary mitigation — do not assume an old, unactivated plan provides any safety net.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.003	A suitable helipad or heliport is one that your helicopter can safely reach with fuel remaining, that is accessible given current weather and terrain, and that can physically accommodate your helicopter's size and weight. Suitability includes: adequate surface dimensions and load-bearing capacity, obstruction-free approach and departure paths, no hazardous surface conditions (ice, soft ground, standing water), and ideally the services you need such as fuel or emergency services if warranted. An off-airport landing zone may be the nearest option but might not be 'suitable' if it is obstructed, too small, or unsafe to land on.	->	A suitable landing site for emergency diversion is one that your helicopter can safely reach with remaining fuel and that can physically accommodate your aircraft given current weather and terrain conditions. This includes heliports, helipads, and emergency landing zones. Suitability criteria include: adequate surface dimensions and load-bearing capacity for your helicopter weight, unobstructed approach and departure paths, no hazardous surface conditions (ice, soft ground, standing water), and ideally available services such as fuel or emergency response. Even an off-airport landing zone (field, parking area, open ground) may be suitable if it meets these basic safety criteria. The nearest spot may not be suitable if it is obstructed, too small, or unsafe to land on.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.S1.008	Promptly turning toward the alternate does not override terrain avoidance — both obligations exist simultaneously, and this is where judgment and situational awareness are critical. If turning east immediately puts you into terrain or cuts your clearance dangerously close, you must modify the initial turn to maintain safe terrain clearance while still trending in the right general direction. Options include: (1) climbing before or during the turn to increase terrain separation; (2) taking a slightly indirect routing that keeps you clear of the highest terrain while still heading generally toward the alternate; (3) contacting ATC immediately for radar vectors if available, since ATC has terrain separation obligations in controlled airspace. The key principle is that 'prompt' does not mean 'reckless.' ADM and SRM both require the pilot to evaluate all hazards simultaneously. A turn that promptly puts the helicopter into a mountain is not a diversion — it's an accident. The examiner wants to see that you can initiate the diversion quickly AND factor in terrain, altitude, and obstacles as part of that immediate decision.	->	Promptly turning toward the alternate does not override terrain avoidance — both obligations exist simultaneously, and this is where judgment and situational awareness are critical. If turning east immediately puts you into terrain or cuts your clearance dangerously close, you must modify the initial turn to maintain safe terrain clearance while still trending in the right general direction. Options include: (1) climbing before or during the turn to increase terrain separation; (2) taking a slightly indirect routing that keeps you clear of the highest terrain while still heading generally toward the alternate; (3) contacting ATC immediately for radar vectors if available, since ATC has terrain separation obligations in controlled airspace. The key principle is that 'prompt' does not mean 'reckless.' ADM and SRM both require you