AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.002	A PIREP (Pilot Weather Report) is a firsthand observation of actual meteorological conditions reported by a pilot in flight. PIREPs report conditions such as cloud bases and tops, turbulence intensity and altitude, icing, and visibility. They are especially valuable for helicopter pilots because they reflect real-time conditions at specific locations and altitudes along a route, which may differ significantly from forecast data. Since helicopters often fly at lower altitudes than fixed-wing aircraft, pilots should note the altitude of any PIREP and assess its relevance to their flight level.	->	A PIREP (Pilot Weather Report) is a firsthand observation of actual meteorological conditions reported by a pilot in flight. PIREPs report conditions such as cloud bases and tops, turbulence intensity and altitude, icing, and visibility. They are especially valuable for helicopter pilots because they reflect real-time conditions at specific locations and altitudes along a route, which may differ significantly from forecast data. Since helicopters often fly at lower altitudes than fixed-wing aircraft, pilots should note the altitude of any PIREP and assess its relevance to their flight level. (Difficulty: intermediate, reflecting practical judgment required during cross-country flight planning.)
