AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.008	A frozen altimeter indicates a blocked static system — the altimeter freezes at the altitude where the blockage occurred, the VSI will read zero continuously, and the ASI will become inaccurate. First, I would immediately activate the alternate static source if equipped — understanding that this will cause a slightly higher altitude indication (lower static pressure inside cabin), slightly faster ASI reading, and a momentary upward VSI bump. If no alternate static source is available, I may carefully break the VSI glass as a last resort to vent the static system to cabin pressure, accepting the same instrument errors. With instrument reliability now degraded, my weather interpretation becomes even more conservative: I need to maintain clear visual contact with the ground, increase margins from terrain and obstacles, and resist the temptation to use the frozen altimeter for terrain separation. For the diversion itself, I would fly a GPS-derived altitude reference if available, maintain a clearly visible ground track, slow to a safe airspeed, communicate with ATC to request radar altitude monitoring, and prioritize getting on the ground at the nearest suitable site before conditions worsen. The error chain here is compounding — instrument failure plus deteriorating weather demands immediate workload management and a priority decision: manage the aircraft and get down safely.	->	Primary answer (frozen altimeter / alternate static / diversion) is correct as stated. For the follow-up: The alternate static source reads cabin static pressure, which is slightly lower than outside ambient static pressure because the airflow around the fuselage creates a slight suction effect. Lower static pressure registers as a higher altitude on the altimeter — typically a small error of a few tens of feet. Regarding Mode C: The transponder encodes pressure altitude directly from the aircraft's static system input. When you select the alternate static source, the transponder encodes the altitude based on that alternate static pressure, which may differ slightly from the primary static reading. This means Mode C may report a slightly different altitude value to ATC when alternate static is selected. The Kollsman window setting does NOT affect Mode C output — only the actual static pressure sensed by the encoder matters. Therefore, pilots should be aware that alternate static source selection may result in a small change to the altitude reported to ATC via Mode C, and you should note this discrepancy if it occurs.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.003	Mode C reports pressure altitude to ATC — it is NOT corrected for local altimeter setting. ATC adjusts the displayed altitude on their scope using the local altimeter, but the raw Mode C value is always pressure altitude. On a digital traffic display (such as TIS or ADS-B traffic), traffic altitude is shown as a relative difference from your own altitude. Understanding this means you must be aware that traffic altitudes shown are based on pressure altitude references and that the Kollsman window setting on your altimeter has no effect on what other aircraft transmit or what ATC receives.	->	Mode C reports pressure altitude to ATC — it is NOT corrected for local altimeter setting. On a digital traffic display (such as TIS or ADS-B traffic), traffic altitude is shown as the absolute Mode C pressure altitude reported by that aircraft's transponder. Your own altitude is also displayed as pressure altitude on the same reference, so both you and traffic operate on a common pressure altitude basis. The Kollsman window setting on your altimeter does not affect what other aircraft transmit or what appears on ATC radar or your traffic display — only the actual static pressure sensed by the transponder matters.
