AI_EDIT	Assistance.008	An ASR (Airport Surveillance Radar) approach is a radar-based instrument approach where ATC provides heading and altitude information to the pilot using surveillance radar — no airborne navigation equipment is required other than a working radio and transponder. The controller tells the pilot what heading to fly and when to descend, providing no-gyro vectors if the pilot is disoriented. For a helicopter, the ASR approach has several considerations: (1) Minimums are typically published for fixed-wing aircraft; helicopter minimums may be lower if specifically published; (2) The controller cannot see the helicopter's altitude directly on surveillance radar — the pilot must report altitude or have Mode C for the controller to verify; (3) The approach ends at the missed approach point, after which the pilot must see the runway environment to land; (4) If the pilot cannot see the runway at the MAP, they must execute a missed approach as instructed. The controller will provide recommended altitudes and will advise the pilot when to begin descent based on distance from the runway. No-gyro procedures are available if the pilot has lost spatial reference — the controller will say 'turn left' and 'stop turn' rather than giving heading assignments.	->	An ASR (Airport Surveillance Radar) approach is a radar-based instrument approach where ATC provides heading and altitude information to the pilot using surveillance radar — no airborne navigation equipment is required other than a working radio and transponder. The controller tells the pilot what heading to fly and when to descend, providing no-gyro vectors if the pilot is disoriented. For a helicopter, the ASR approach has several considerations: (1) ASR approach minimums are published for the airport and are based on the radar facility's capabilities and terrain; helicopter-specific minimums are rare and would only apply where formally published; (2) The controller cannot see the helicopter's altitude directly on surveillance radar — the pilot must report altitude or have Mode C for the controller to verify; (3) The approach ends at the missed approach point, after which the pilot must see the runway environment to land; (4) If the pilot cannot see the runway at the MAP, they must execute a missed approach as instructed. The controller will provide recommended altitudes and will advise the pilot when to begin descent based on distance from the runway. No-gyro procedures are available if the pilot has lost spatial reference — the controller will say 'turn left' and 'stop turn' rather than giving heading assignments.
