AI_EDIT	CH.IX.D.007	This is a dangerous misconception. Under 14 CFR 91.3(b), a pilot who deviates from a rule during an emergency must send a written report to the FAA if requested — but the FAA does not automatically investigate or certificate-action every emergency declaration. More importantly, under the Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS/NASA), if a pilot files a NASA report within 10 days of a regulatory deviation, it provides immunity from FAA civil penalty for unintentional violations (with limited exceptions). The pilot's reluctance to declare an emergency due to fear of paperwork is a classic example of prioritizing ego or administrative concern over safety — exactly the kind of human factors failure ADM training is designed to prevent. Declaring a MAYDAY gets the pilot immediate priority handling, ATC radar assistance, emergency services alerting, and potentially saves lives. The cost is a possible written report; the benefit is survival.	->	This is a dangerous misconception. Under 14 CFR 91.3(b), a pilot who deviates from a rule during an emergency may be required to submit a written report to the FAA. However, immunity from civil penalty is provided under FAA Order 2150.3B and 49 U.S.C. § 44703(d) if a pilot files a NASA ASRS report within 10 days of an unintentional regulatory deviation (with limited exceptions for deliberate or criminal acts). The pilot's reluctance to declare an emergency due to fear of administrative consequences is a classic human factors failure—prioritizing ego over safety. Declaring a MAYDAY provides immediate priority handling, ATC radar assistance, emergency services alerting, and potentially saves lives. The cost is documentation; the benefit is survival. Regarding repeated ASRS immunity: while ASRS protection applies to each separate incident, current FAA guidance indicates that immunity generally does not apply to violations of the same regulation within a 5-year period if a prior ASRS report was already filed for that same rule. Each incident and regulatory section must be evaluated independently.
