AI_EDIT	CH.X.A.008	The 'dead man's curve' — formally called the Height-Velocity (H-V) diagram in the POH/RFM — defines combinations of altitude and airspeed from which a safe autorotation cannot be completed following a sudden engine failure. The low-altitude portion of the H-V diagram (the lower left area, sometimes called the 'low-hover avoid region') identifies hover heights where there is insufficient altitude for rotor RPM to stabilize and for the pilot to complete a controlled landing. At very low hovers (typically under 2-3 feet AGL, model dependent), there is actually enough proximity to the ground that ground effect cushions the landing and the region is safe again. But at intermediate hover heights in the 'avoid' zone, there is too much altitude to simply 'drop in' safely and not enough altitude to complete a proper hover autorotation. The H-V diagram is derived from actual flight test data by the manufacturer and represents a statistical boundary — not an absolute guarantee. Pilots should understand that operations within the avoid region are not prohibited by regulation but represent elevated risk, and that the envelope shrinks further with increased gross weight, density altitude, or degraded pilot response time.	->	The 'Height-Velocity (H-V) Diagram' — the official term for this safety boundary in the POH/RFM — defines combinations of altitude and airspeed from which a safe autorotation cannot be completed following a sudden engine failure. The low-altitude portion of the H-V diagram (the lower left area, sometimes called the 'low-hover avoid region') identifies hover heights where there is insufficient altitude for rotor RPM to stabilize and for the pilot to complete a controlled landing. At very low hovers (typically under 2-3 feet AGL, model dependent), there is actually enough proximity to the ground that ground effect cushions the landing and the region is safe again. But at intermediate hover heights in the 'avoid' zone, there is too much altitude to simply 'drop in' safely and not enough altitude to complete a proper hover autorotation. The H-V diagram is derived from actual flight test data by the manufacturer and is aircraft-type and configuration-specific; the diagram must be obtained from the actual RFM being used. It represents a statistical boundary — not an absolute guarantee. Pilots should understand that operations within the avoid region are not prohibited by regulation but represent elevated risk, and that the envelope shrinks further with increased gross weight, density altitude, or degraded pilot response time.
