AI_EDIT	CH.X.D.001	Helicopter vibrations are categorized as low frequency (1/rev or 2/rev), medium frequency (tail rotor frequency, typically 4–6/rev depending on number of tail rotor blades), and high frequency (engine and drive components). Low frequency vibrations occur at main rotor rotational speed and are most often associated with main rotor system issues. Medium frequency vibrations occur at tail rotor speed and relate to tail rotor problems. High frequency vibrations are faster than main rotor speed and typically point to engine, transmission, or drive shaft issues.	->	Helicopter vibrations are categorized as low frequency (primarily 1/rev, or once-per-main-rotor-revolution; occasionally 2/rev depending on blade count), medium frequency (tail rotor frequency, typically 4–6/rev depending on number of tail rotor blades), and high frequency (engine and drive components). Low-frequency vibrations occur at main rotor rotational speed and are most often associated with main rotor system issues such as blade imbalance, blade cracks or damage, track-and-balance problems, or loose pitch links. Medium-frequency vibrations occur at tail rotor speed and relate to tail rotor problems. High-frequency vibrations are faster than main rotor speed and typically point to engine, transmission, or drive shaft issues.
