AI_EDIT	CH.X.E.001	Vortex ring state is an aerodynamic condition where the helicopter descends into its own downwash, causing the rotor to recirculate disturbed, turbulent air rather than drawing in clean air from above. It develops when three conditions exist simultaneously: (1) a powered condition — the rotor is producing thrust, (2) a low or near-zero airspeed (typically below effective translational lift, around 15–20 knots), and (3) a descent rate high enough (typically 300 feet per minute or more) that the helicopter is settling into its own vortex wake. The blade stall begins near the hub and progresses outward, causing a loss of lift and an uncontrollable increase in descent rate.	->	Vortex ring state is an aerodynamic condition where the helicopter descends into its own downwash, causing the rotor to recirculate disturbed, turbulent air rather than drawing in clean air from above. It develops when three conditions exist simultaneously: (1) a powered condition — the rotor is producing thrust, (2) a low or near-zero airspeed (typically below effective translational lift, around 15–20 knots), and (3) a descent rate high enough (typically 300 feet per minute or more) that the helicopter is settling into its own vortex wake. The blade stall begins near the hub and progresses outward, causing a loss of lift and an uncontrollable increase in descent rate. A strong, steady headwind reduces VRS risk by allowing the pilot to maintain translational airspeed or a shallower descent angle while approaching a landing zone, keeping the helicopter above the critical airspeed/descent-rate envelope. However, gusty or variable wind conditions increase VRS risk because sudden wind loss or direction changes can cause rapid airspeed decay into the VRS envelope; a consistent headwind is far more protective than a variable one.
