AI_EDIT	CH.X.K.001	The three common classes are: (1) CO2 (carbon dioxide) — effective on Class B (flammable liquid) and Class C (electrical) fires; suppresses oxygen but leaves no residue. (2) Dry chemical (e.g., Halon alternatives or PKP) — effective on Class B and C fires; leaves residue that can damage avionics. (3) Halon (or Halon replacement agents) — effective on Class B and C fires; leaves no residue and is preferred for aircraft use due to minimal equipment damage. Class A fires (ordinary combustibles like paper/fabric) are less common in helicopters but some agents cover them as well.	->	The three common classes are: (1) CO2 (carbon dioxide) — effective on Class B (flammable liquid) and Class C (electrical) fires; suppresses oxygen but leaves no residue. (2) Dry chemical (e.g., monoammonium phosphate-based formulations or PKP) — effective on Class A (ordinary combustibles such as upholstery and insulation), Class B, and Class C fires; leaves residue that can damage avionics. (3) Halon 1211 or Halon replacement agents (e.g., Halotron I) — effective on Class B and C fires; leave no residue and are preferred for aircraft use due to minimal equipment damage. Class A fires can occur in helicopter cabins and involve materials like upholstery, insulation, and wiring jackets, making dry chemical agents valuable for cabin fire suppression.
