Question ID:   PH.VI.B.S11.003
ACS Code:      PH.VI.B.S11
Difficulty:    basic

Question:
Why must the pilot apply right pedal (in a helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor, as viewed from above) when raising collective during power recovery from autorotation?

Original Answer:
As collective is raised and engine power is restored, torque on the fuselage increases significantly. In a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor system, this torque reaction tends to yaw the nose to the right. Right pedal (increasing tail rotor thrust) is applied simultaneously with the collective increase to counteract this torque and maintain coordinated, on-heading flight. Failure to apply right pedal will result in an uncontrolled left yaw.

Revised Answer:
As collective is raised and engine power is restored, torque on the fuselage increases significantly. In a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor system, this torque reaction tends to yaw the nose to the right. Left pedal (increasing tail rotor thrust) is applied simultaneously with the collective increase to counteract this torque and maintain coordinated, on-heading flight. Failure to apply left pedal will result in an uncontrolled right yaw.

Verifier Notes:
Answer states 'right pedal results in left yaw' but this is backwards. Increased torque from raising collective causes RIGHT yaw (nose right), not left yaw. Right pedal counteracts this by pushing nose left to maintain heading.

