Question ID:   PH.VI.B.S11.005
ACS Code:      PH.VI.B.S11
Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
During a straight-in autorotation power recovery, a student raises collective smoothly but does not add any right pedal. Describe exactly what will happen to the helicopter and why.

Answer (at time of rejection):
As collective is raised and engine power is restored, increasing torque will yaw the nose to the left (for standard counterclockwise-rotating U.S. helicopters). Without right pedal input, the helicopter will begin a left yaw that, if uncorrected, can become significant. The aircraft will depart the intended heading and could present a hazard, especially near the ground during a low-altitude power recovery. The student must be taught to anticipate this torque increase and apply right pedal progressively as collective is raised, just as in any normal power application. This is a fundamental coordination failure.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
Answer states helicopter will yaw LEFT without right pedal, but this contradicts basic helicopter torque dynamics. Increased torque from power application causes RIGHT yaw (nose right) in standard counterclockwise-rotating helicopters. Without right pedal, nose yaws RIGHT, not left.

