AI_EDIT	CH.X.L.004	A frozen altimeter combined with a VSI showing zero (or erratic) and ASI inaccuracy are classic signs of a blocked static system. However, if the VSI is showing a descent trend, it's possible only partial blockage exists, or an alternate static source has not been selected. A fully blocked static port causes the altimeter to freeze at the altitude of blockage, the VSI to read zero continuously, and the ASI to become inaccurate. The correct response is to select the alternate static source if installed — this draws static from inside the cabin, which has slightly lower pressure than ambient, causing the altimeter to read slightly high and the ASI to read slightly fast. The pilot must apply known correction values and notify ATC. If no alternate static source exists, breaking the VSI glass (in unpressurized aircraft) vents the static system to cabin air.	->	A frozen altimeter combined with VSI and ASI anomalies suggests a static system problem. If the VSI shows a steady descent trend (rather than zero), this indicates partial blockage or incomplete static blockage—a fully blocked static port would freeze the VSI at zero with no needle movement. The correct response is to select the alternate static source if installed; this draws static pressure from inside the cabin (slightly lower than ambient), causing the altimeter to read slightly high and the ASI to read slightly fast. Apply known correction values and notify ATC. Expect the altitude indication to rise as the alternate source is engaged. If no alternate static source is available and you are in unpressurized flight, do not attempt to break the VSI glass (not standard procedure in modern aircraft). Instead, rely on the attitude indicator as your primary pitch reference, cross-check with airspeed trends, and request priority handling from ATC to descend to VFR conditions or execute an instrument approach. Reference: FAA-H-8083-15B, Chapter 5.
