AI_EDIT	CH.X.L.S_DESC.006	Begin the level-off lead at approximately 10% of the descent rate above the target altitude — for example, if descending at 500 FPM, begin leveling off at roughly 50 feet above the target, meaning initiation at about 250 feet above target is slightly early but ensures a smooth capture. Gradually increase collective to arrest the descent while simultaneously applying slight forward cyclic to maintain 70 knots as power increases (torque effect may require pedal adjustment). Transition instrument scan: shift altimeter to primary for pitch as the descent rate arrests. Cross-check the VSI to confirm it is trending toward zero. Once level and stabilized, verify altitude is within ±200 feet of the assigned altitude, airspeed is within ±10 knots, and heading is within ±20 degrees. Trim as needed.	->	Apply the 10% rule for level-off lead: 10% of the descent rate (500 FPM × 0.10 = 50 feet). Begin increasing collective at approximately 150 feet above the target altitude (200 feet above target minus the 50-foot lead). Gradually increase collective to arrest the descent while simultaneously applying slight forward cyclic to maintain 70 knots as power increases; torque effect may require pedal adjustment. Transition instrument scan: shift altimeter to primary for pitch as the descent rate arrests. Cross-check the VSI to confirm it is trending toward zero. Once level and stabilized, verify altitude is within ±200 feet of the assigned altitude, airspeed is within ±10 knots, and heading is within ±20 degrees. Trim as needed.
