AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.RM.003	Spatial disorientation is the inability to correctly determine the aircraft's attitude, altitude, or motion relative to the earth's surface, typically because visual cues are ambiguous or absent and the pilot relies on false sensory signals. During pinnacle operations, the highest risk phase for disorientation is the departure. When the helicopter lifts off from a ridgeline or peak and transitions away from the landing surface, the pilot may lose visual reference to the horizon — particularly if the pinnacle drops sharply away on all sides, if there is flat light or featureless terrain, or if the operation is conducted in marginal visibility. This sudden loss of external reference can cause the pilot to misinterpret aircraft attitude and make incorrect control inputs.	->	Spatial disorientation is the inability to correctly determine the aircraft's attitude, altitude, or motion relative to the earth's surface, typically because visual cues are ambiguous or absent and the pilot relies on false sensory signals. During pinnacle operations, the highest risk phase for disorientation is the APPROACH phase. When descending toward a pinnacle, the pilot must simultaneously evaluate terrain geometry, identify obstacles, manage power, and maintain visual references while external cues may be ambiguous due to flat light, featureless terrain, steep slopes, or the complex three-dimensional geometry of the landing site. The absence of clear visual references to the horizon and the cognitive load of managing multiple tasks increases the risk of misinterpreting aircraft attitude. The departure phase also presents disorientation risk when the helicopter lifts off and loses visual reference to the landing surface, but this typically occurs after approach challenges have been overcome and the pilot has confirmed the aircraft's spatial position.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.RM.001	The tail rotor is the most dangerous hazard. It is often difficult to see due to its high speed, small blade profile, and the noise environment near a running helicopter. Passengers exiting toward the rear of the aircraft may walk directly into the tail rotor arc without realizing it. The main rotor is a hazard as well, particularly on sloped terrain where the low side of the rotor disk can be at head or shoulder height.	->	Both the tail rotor and main rotor present critical hazards during pinnacle passenger exit. The tail rotor is particularly dangerous because it is difficult to see due to its high speed, small blade profile, and the noise environment near a running helicopter; passengers exiting toward the rear of the aircraft may walk directly into the tail rotor arc without realizing it. The main rotor hazard is equally severe on sloped pinnacle terrain: the low side of the rotor disk can be at head or shoulder height, creating a strike hazard for passengers exiting downslope. Pilots must brief passengers on both hazards and direct them to exit toward the high side of the aircraft on sloped terrain, maintaining awareness of rotor clearance and keeping a safe distance from both rotor systems.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.RM.008	In a low-G condition caused by a sudden wind shift to a tailwind on a pinnacle approach, the immediate hazard for a two-bladed semirigid rotor system is mast bumping, but the broader forced landing risk is equally severe: the low-G unloaded rotor produces drastically reduced lift at a moment when the helicopter is at low altitude over terrain with no altitude margin for recovery. If the pilot reacts instinctively by pulling aft cyclic to arrest the nose drop (a common and dangerous error), they further unload the disk and worsen the condition. The correct immediate action is to apply a gentle lateral cyclic input to reload the rotor and restore positive G loading before any aft cyclic is used. Once disk loading is restored, normal recovery inputs can follow. The forced landing risk escalates because the unloaded rotor in a low-G state cannot arrest a descent, and the rotor RPM may spike upward momentarily then decay as load returns — if the pilot is slow or uses wrong control inputs, the helicopter may impact terrain on or near the pinnacle with no meaningful autorotation entry possible due to the altitude and rotor state.	->	In a low-G condition caused by a sudden wind shift to a tailwind on a pinnacle approach, the immediate hazard for a two-bladed semirigid rotor system is mast bumping, but the broader forced landing risk is equally severe: the low-G unloaded rotor produces drastically reduced lift at a moment when the helicopter is at low altitude over terrain with no altitude margin for recovery. The correct immediate action is to reduce collective pitch and neutralize back-stick pressure (apply forward cyclic if needed) to restore positive G loading and disk lift. If the pilot reacts instinctively by pulling aft cyclic to arrest the nose drop (a common and dangerous error), they further unload the disk and worsen the condition. Once positive G loading and rotor lift are restored through collective and cyclic input, normal recovery inputs can follow. The forced landing risk escalates because the unloaded rotor in a low-G state cannot arrest a descent, and the rotor RPM may spike upward momentarily then decay as load returns — if the pilot is slow or uses wrong control inputs, the helicopter may impact terrain on or near the pinnacle with no meaningful autorotation entry possible due to the altitude and rotor state.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.SK.006	With a radio failure en route to a remote pinnacle, your SRM priorities shift immediately. First, troubleshoot — check volume, squelch, frequency, and circuit breakers. If truly failed, you lose your primary means of traffic deconfliction at the site. Before proceeding, assess whether the site is expected to have other traffic. If it is a high-traffic site, strongly consider diverting or delaying until communications are restored, because the risk of mid-air conflict increases significantly without radio calls. If you proceed, increase your vigilance — perform extended reconnaissance to visually clear the area, approach from a direction that maximizes visibility to other inbound traffic, and keep approaches slow enough to abort. Document the radio failure for maintenance. No regulatory requirement exists to squawk 7600 in VFR conditions, but doing so on a transponder-equipped helicopter alerts ATC to your status.	->	With a radio failure en route to a remote pinnacle, your SRM priorities shift immediately. First, troubleshoot — check volume, squelch, frequency, and circuit breakers. If truly failed, you lose your primary means of traffic deconfliction at the site. Before proceeding, assess whether the site is expected to have other traffic. If it is a high-traffic site, strongly consider diverting or delaying until communications are restored, because the risk of mid-air conflict increases significantly without radio calls. If you proceed, increase your vigilance — perform extended reconnaissance to visually clear the area, approach from a direction that maximizes visibility to other inbound traffic, and keep approaches slow enough to abort. Document the radio failure for maintenance. In VFR conditions, there is no regulatory requirement to squawk 7600 (14 CFR 91.185 applies only to IFR flight), but if equipped with a transponder, squawking 7600 alerts ATC to your radio loss as a courtesy.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.Accomplish.002	During a low reconnaissance, the pilot should fly close enough to clearly inspect the surface — typically within 100–300 feet AGL depending on conditions — at a slow but safe airspeed that allows thorough visual inspection without sacrificing the ability to escape if needed. Maintaining adequate airspeed ensures effective translational lift is available and prevents drifting into vortex ring state conditions. The altitude must be high enough to clear all obstacles while still allowing the pilot to evaluate surface texture, slope, ground cover, and any hazards not visible from the high recon. Flying too slow or too low during the low recon increases risk with no additional safety margin.	->	During a low reconnaissance, descend as low as safely possible to inspect the surface texture, slope, ground cover, and hazards — altitude is determined by obstacles and evaluation requirements rather than a fixed range. Maintain adequate airspeed to sustain effective translational lift and prevent settling into vortex ring state; this requires balancing a slow speed for detailed inspection against the need to avoid descent rates and low airspeeds that increase sink rate and loss of rotor efficiency. The altitude must remain high enough to clear all obstacles while still allowing thorough visual evaluation of landing suitability.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.S002	An upslope wind flows up the side of a hill or mountain toward the summit. This type of wind is favorable for a pinnacle approach because it provides a consistent headwind component as you climb toward the landing point, supports rotor efficiency, and reduces the rate at which you lose ETL. You should select an approach path that takes advantage of upslope airflow, generally approaching from the downwind side of the terrain feature so the wind is moving up toward you.	->	An upslope wind flows up the side of a hill or mountain toward the summit. This type of wind is favorable for a pinnacle approach because it provides a consistent headwind component as you climb toward the landing point, supports rotor efficiency, and reduces the rate at which you lose ETL. You should select an approach path that takes advantage of upslope airflow by approaching from the upwind side of the terrain feature (the side where the wind originates) so that the upslope wind is moving up toward you and provides a headwind.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.S.Track.005	This situation signals that you may not have sufficient power reserve to safely complete the approach, arrest any unexpected sink, or conduct an OGE hover at the termination point. Using approximately 80% of available power principles from the Helicopter Flying Handbook, being within 5% of maximum power this early in the approach leaves almost no margin for deceleration, ETL loss, or wind changes. The correct action is to immediately initiate a go-around while still at an altitude and airspeed that permit a safe escape route. On the go-around, avoid flying directly over the pinnacle; fly to the side to maintain access to a lower escape route. Reassess density altitude, hover power requirements, and consider whether an OGE hover at the pad is achievable before attempting another approach.	->	This situation signals that you may not have sufficient power reserve to safely complete the approach, arrest any unexpected sink, or conduct an OGE hover at the termination point. Using your aircraft's performance charts (POH) and the current density altitude, being within 5% of maximum power this early in the approach leaves almost no margin for deceleration, ETL loss, or wind changes. The correct action is to immediately initiate a go-around while still at an altitude and airspeed that permit a safe escape route. On the go-around, avoid flying directly over the pinnacle; fly to the side to maintain access to a lower escape route. Reassess density altitude, verify OGE hover power requirements from your POH, and confirm whether a safe OGE hover at the pad is achievable before attempting another approach.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.S005	A downwind approach into a pinnacle is a high-risk situation and should be avoided if at all possible. However, if terrain or obstacles leave no alternative, the pilot must account for: (1) reduced effective translational lift (ETL) because it is achieved at a higher groundspeed and may not occur until closer to terrain; (2) higher power demand since you'll be fighting both inertia and a tailwind during deceleration; (3) longer deceleration distance needed, which may not be available on a pinnacle. Before committing, verify via the performance chart that OGE hover power is available at that density altitude with that weight — if you can't hover OGE, you cannot safely abort a downwind approach over a confined pinnacle. If ETL is lost early and you can't sustain a hover, you may have no escape route.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.S006	Wires are one of the most serious hazards in low-level operations because they are nearly invisible in certain lighting conditions. Upon spotting wires during the low recon, the pilot should: (1) immediately increase altitude and expand the recon orbit to avoid flying below or through the wire span; (2) attempt to trace both ends of the wires to utility poles or structures to estimate height and direction; (3) evaluate whether any portion of the approach or departure path can be flown safely above the wire height with sufficient margin — typically at least 50 feet of clearance; (4) consider an alternative approach or departure direction; (5) if height cannot be positively determined or clearance confirmed, do not land — wires are a no-go item when doubt exists. This is an example of breaking the error chain before a poor decision accumulates into an accident.	->	Wires are one of the most serious hazards in low-level operations because they are nearly invisible in certain lighting conditions. Upon spotting wires during the low recon, the pilot should: (1) immediately increase altitude and expand the recon orbit to avoid flying below or through the wire span; (2) attempt to trace both ends of the wires to utility poles or structures to estimate height and direction; (3) evaluate whether any portion of the approach or departure path can be flown safely above the wire height with sufficient margin — typically at least 50 feet of clearance; (4) consider an alternative approach or departure direction; (5) if height cannot be positively determined or clearance confirmed, do not land — wires are a no-go item when doubt exists. This is an example of breaking the error chain before a poor decision accumulates into an accident.
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.S005	On a sloped surface, the primary hazard during termination is dynamic rollover. As collective is reduced to settle onto the slope, the downhill skid or wheel touches first and becomes a pivot point. If cyclic is not immediately moved upslope to maintain rotor disk level and unloading is not controlled smoothly, the helicopter can roll around that pivot point faster than the pilot can recover. The technique requires touching the upslope skid/wheel first whenever possible, or at minimum making contact simultaneously, then smoothly reducing collective while applying upslope cyclic to compensate for the terrain angle. Monitor that no lateral rolling tendency develops during touchdown. If the slope exceeds the helicopter's maximum allowable slope limitation (per the RFM), a surface termination must not be attempted — terminate to a hover and reposition or abort the operation.	->	On a sloped surface, the primary hazard during termination is dynamic rollover. As collective is reduced to settle onto the slope, the downhill skid or wheel touches first and becomes a pivot point. If cyclic is not immediately moved upslope to maintain rotor disk level and unloading is not controlled smoothly, the helicopter can roll around that pivot point faster than the pilot can recover. The technique requires touching the upslope skid/wheel first whenever possible, or at minimum making contact simultaneously, then smoothly reducing collective while applying upslope cyclic to compensate for the terrain angle. Monitor that no lateral rolling tendency develops during touchdown. If the slope exceeds the helicopter's maximum allowable slope limitation (per the RFM), a surface termination must not be attempted — terminate to a hover and reposition or abort the operation. (Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B, Chapter 10: Advanced Flight Maneuvers; Chapter 11: Helicopter Emergencies and Hazards)
AI_EDIT	CH.XII.B.S006	The sluggishness and reduced control responsiveness is likely indicating you are operating at or near maximum power available for OGE hover — you may have very little or no power margin remaining. This can also be an early indication of vortex ring state if a high rate of descent existed during the approach and airspeed was low, but in a hover termination context the more immediate concern is power margin. If the helicopter cannot maintain a stable OGE hover with acceptable control response and power margin, continuing to hold the hover risks an uncontrolled settling. The appropriate action is to transition immediately to a surface termination — land while you still have control authority — rather than holding a marginal hover. Consult the hover power charts; if density altitude has increased or performance is degraded beyond RFM limits, an abort may be required.	->	The sluggishness and reduced control responsiveness indicates you are operating at or near maximum power available for OGE (Out of Ground Effect) hover — you have little to no power margin remaining. In a stabilized hover, vortex ring state is not the primary concern, as VRS develops during descent at low airspeed and high rate of descent. The immediate hazard is insufficient power margin to maintain control authority and stabilize the aircraft. The appropriate action is to transition immediately to a surface termination — land while you still have control authority — rather than holding a marginal hover. Consult the hover power charts; if density altitude has increased or performance is degraded beyond RFM limits, an abort may be required. (Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B, Chapter 10: Advanced Flight Maneuvers; Chapter 11: Helicopter Emergencies and Hazards)
