AI_EDIT	CH.XIII.A.001	Documenting discrepancies creates a written record of any mechanical irregularities, malfunctions, or abnormalities observed during or after flight. The pilot in command is responsible for ensuring that any discrepancy is recorded in the aircraft maintenance logbook or a separate discrepancy log before the next flight. This ensures maintenance personnel are aware of the issue and can make an airworthiness determination before the aircraft is returned to service.	->	Documenting discrepancies creates a written record of any mechanical irregularities, malfunctions, or abnormalities observed during or after flight. The pilot in command is responsible for ensuring that any discrepancy is recorded in the aircraft maintenance logbook or a separate discrepancy log before the next flight. This ensures maintenance personnel are aware of the issue and can make an airworthiness determination before the aircraft is returned to service. Regulatory reference: 14 CFR 91.7 (airworthiness), 14 CFR 91.405 (maintenance responsibility), 14 CFR 43.9 (mechanic logbook entry requirements).
ESCALATE	CH.XIII.A.002	Question ID is duplicated (same as first item); claim that FAA-H-8083-21B explicitly lists cyclic trim as non-deferrable is not verifiable and likely overstates handbook authority; MEL deferability is aircraft-specific per approved MEL, not prescribed by the handbook. Requires correction of ID and substantive revision of answer to avoid regulatory misstatement.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIII.A.003	The FAA considers an approved MEL to be a supplemental type certificate (STC) for that specific aircraft. This means the MEL is a legal, FAA-approved document that effectively amends the aircraft's type design to allow flight with certain inoperative equipment under specified conditions and limitations. Any discrepancy that falls outside the scope of what the MEL permits must be documented and repaired before flight.	->	An approved MEL is an FAA-approved document that provides relief from type certificate and airworthiness directive requirements, allowing flight with specific inoperative equipment under defined conditions and limitations. It operates under the authority granted by the type certificate and FAA approval, but it is not itself an STC. Any discrepancy that falls outside the scope of what the MEL permits must be documented and repaired before flight per 14 CFR 91.213.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIII.A.004	First, document the discrepancy in detail in the aircraft maintenance logbook or discrepancy log — note the specific symptom, when it occurred, and flight conditions. Per FAA-H-8083-21B, a rotor tachometer is not a deferrable item under a typical MEL because it is essential for monitoring rotor RPM and safe autorotation. This means the aircraft is grounded until a certificated A&P mechanic inspects and signs off the repair. You cannot legally defer this item and fly home. You should contact the owner/operator, arrange for a mechanic to come to the aircraft or have it transported, and do not conduct another flight until the discrepancy is corrected and signed off in the logbook.	->	First, document the discrepancy in detail in the aircraft maintenance logbook — note the specific symptom, when it occurred, and flight conditions. Whether the rotor tachometer can be deferred depends on whether the aircraft has an FAA-approved MEL and what that MEL permits. If the MEL does not allow deferral, or if no MEL exists and the rotor tach is required by the type certificate, then the aircraft is grounded until a certificated A&P mechanic inspects and signs off the repair. You should contact the owner/operator and arrange for maintenance; do not conduct another flight until the discrepancy is corrected and signed off in the logbook per 14 CFR 91.213(d) and 91.417.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIII.A.008	When in doubt, document it. The conservative and legally correct action is to enter the suspected hard landing in the aircraft maintenance logbook, describe what occurred (approximate sink rate, surface conditions, any crew or passenger observations), and note that the RFM requires a hard landing inspection if G-limits were exceeded. The determination of whether the hard landing inspection is required is not the pilot's call to make unilaterally — a certificated A&P mechanic familiar with the helicopter's RFM and maintenance manual must review the entry and make that determination. Flying the aircraft before that determination is made risks operating an unairworthy aircraft under 14 CFR 91.7. ADM principles from FAA-H-8083-21B support the conservative choice: if you're using the word 'probably' (probably didn't exceed limits), choose the known-safe option instead.	->	When in doubt, document it. Enter the suspected hard landing in the aircraft maintenance logbook, describe what occurred (approximate sink rate, surface conditions, any crew or passenger observations), and note that the RFM requires a hard landing inspection if G-limits were exceeded. The determination of whether the hard landing inspection is required is not the pilot's call to make unilaterally — a certificated A&P mechanic familiar with the helicopter's RFM and maintenance manual must review the entry and make that determination. Do not fly the aircraft before that determination is made and documented, as flying an unairworthy aircraft violates 14 CFR 91.7. Under Part 135, this is mandatory per 14 CFR 135.65 and 135.75, and the PIC has final authority and responsibility for airworthiness. ADM principles support the conservative choice: when you're uncertain, defer to a qualified mechanic.
AI_EDIT	Parking.002	Passengers and ground crew should approach only from the front or sides of the helicopter — never from the rear. Approaching from the rear exposes a person to the tail rotor, which is nearly invisible when spinning and operates at very high RPM. The FAA specifically states that people should approach helicopters from the side, always crouch when near the aircraft, and never attempt to retrieve blown-off hats or other loose items that might drift toward the tail rotor.	->	Ground personnel should approach from the FRONT of the helicopter only, where the pilot can see them and where they are clear of both the main rotor arc and the tail rotor hazard. Approaching from the rear or sides exposes a person to the tail rotor, which is nearly invisible when spinning or during rundown and operates at very high RPM. The pilot must be able to maintain visual contact with all approaching personnel. Crouching is important only when transiting under the main rotor disc while rotors are decaying or stopped. Never approach from the rear, and never attempt to retrieve blown-off hats or other loose items that might drift toward the tail rotor.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIII.A.RM.006	During hot refueling, the pilot must remain at the flight controls and be ready to immediately shut down and egress — this is an FAA-mandated requirement. Because the pilot cannot leave the cockpit to escort passengers, it is essential that either a trained ground crew member or FBO line personnel escort any approaching passengers. No passenger should approach an aircraft during hot refueling without a qualified escort who knows the rotor hazard zones. The passenger must be kept at a safe distance until refueling is complete and the area is declared clear. This differs from normal boarding because the pilot cannot physically monitor or intercept a passenger who approaches incorrectly — the pilot is committed to flight controls. Pre-brief all passengers before any hot-refueling stop so they understand to wait for an escort and never approach the aircraft independently.	->	During hot refueling, the pilot should remain alert and positioned to respond immediately to any emergency; many operators and the FAA recommend the pilot remain at or near the controls, though specific procedures vary by operator and aircraft type. Because the pilot may have limited ability to monitor approaching personnel during refueling operations, it is essential that a trained ground crew member or FBO line personnel escort any approaching passengers. No passenger should approach an aircraft during hot refueling without a qualified escort who knows the rotor hazard zones. The passenger must be kept at a safe distance until refueling is complete and the area is declared clear. This differs from normal boarding because the pilot's attention is divided and cannot reliably intercept a passenger who approaches incorrectly. Pre-brief all passengers before any hot-refueling stop so they understand to wait for an escort and never approach the aircraft independently.
