AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.A.001	When no slope limitations are published for the helicopter being used, parallel slope operations of approximately 5 to 10 degrees may be demonstrated. This range represents the generally accepted safe envelope for slope operations in the absence of specific manufacturer guidance.	->	When no slope limitations are published, the pilot must exercise aeronautical judgment based on the specific aircraft's design constraints, including main rotor blade-to-terrain clearance, tail rotor clearance, skid geometry, available lateral cyclic travel, and dynamic rollover risk. While many light helicopters may safely operate in a 5–10 degree range, this is not a universal standard but rather reflects typical design margins for those aircraft types. The pilot must verify that the specific aircraft can safely accomplish the landing sequence at the observed slope angle before committing to the demonstration.
