AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.A.004	You should not attempt the landing. Without a manufacturer-published limit, the ACS default of 5–10 degrees applies. At 12 degrees, you are beyond this limit, and the risk of dynamic rollover increases significantly. As you apply lateral cyclic into the slope and the upslope skid contacts the ground, the pivot point shifts to that skid. If the center of gravity moves beyond the tipping point before the downslope skid settles, the helicopter can roll over rapidly. The pilot may not have enough cyclic authority to recover. The correct action is to reconnaissance another landing area or wait for a more suitable site.	->	You should not attempt the landing. When no manufacturer-published slope limitation exists, you must apply extreme conservatism and decline the operation. A 12-degree slope exceeds the safe operating margin for dynamic rollover protection on most helicopters. As you apply lateral cyclic to land upslope, the upslope skid contacts the ground and becomes a pivot point. If the center of gravity moves beyond the tipping point before the downslope skid settles, the helicopter can roll over rapidly, and the pilot may not have sufficient cyclic authority to recover. The correct action is to reconnaissance an alternative landing area or decline the mission. Always consult the POH/RFM for manufacturer-specific slope limits; when those limits are unavailable, treat the slope as unsuitable unless you can obtain explicit authorization.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.A.006	An upslope wind blowing against the rotor disc can affect translational lift and cyclic effectiveness. Because the wind strikes the downslope side of the disc and tends to push the helicopter upslope, you may need to apply additional downslope cyclic to maintain position during the approach. Power requirements may also change. The benefit is that an upslope wind increases rotor efficiency, similar to a headwind during normal operations. You must also ensure rotor blade clearance from the terrain on the upslope side, particularly the advancing blade, which will sweep closest to the hillside. Approach should be planned with this blade clearance in mind.	->	An upslope wind creates an effective headwind component to the rotor disc, increasing rotor efficiency and lift generation, similar to a headwind in normal operations. During the approach, this wind pressure on the lower rotor disc surface may require additional downslope cyclic input to maintain position and prevent drift upslope. Power requirements may be reduced due to increased rotor efficiency. The primary rotor clearance concern is ensuring the upslope rotor blade arc clears the rising terrain as the helicopter tilts into the slope — this is driven by slope angle and cyclic deflection (disc attitude), not wind direction. An upslope wind does not change blade clearance geometry, but the increased available lift can aid control and reduce power demand during the approach.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.A.S_SLOPE_RECON.002	A standard slope reconnaissance is conducted at a low, slow orbit — typically at a hover or slow forward flight altitude that allows a clear view of the surface and surrounding obstacles without being so low that you create a hazard yourself. The standard practice is at least one 360-degree orbit around the intended landing area to observe the slope from all angles, checking for surface hazards, gradient, rotor clearance on the uphill side, and approach/departure path obstacles. Altitude is selected to maintain a safe margin above terrain while allowing visual assessment — commonly 300–500 feet AGL depending on terrain and obstacles.	->	A standard slope reconnaissance consists of at least one complete 360-degree orbit conducted at low and slow airspeed. Altitude is selected low enough to clearly observe surface hazards, slope gradient, and rotor clearance on the uphill side, but high enough to maintain safe margins above terrain and obstacles. The exact altitude depends on terrain elevation, obstacle proximity, and visibility conditions — the focus is on clear visual assessment of all landing zone hazards, not a prescribed altitude AGL. During the orbit, systematically check the slope gradient, surface composition, rotor blade clearance on the upslope side, and any approach and departure path obstacles.
