AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.B.S004	At 300 feet AGL you still have options: (1) S-turns or a modified approach path to burn off excess energy and altitude; (2) increasing airspeed briefly to steepen the glide path; or (3) adjusting the aim point slightly. However, a go-around in autorotation is only practical if an engine restart is possible (power-on go-around) — in a simulated autorotation on a checkride, the examiner may allow a power recovery. The ACS standard is clear: if you cannot meet the task standards (touch down within 200 feet of the target) through normal technique adjustments, a go-around represents a failure of this task element. The goal is to recognize energy state problems early enough — ideally above 500 feet AGL — to make corrections without requiring a go-around. Waiting until 300 feet limits your options significantly.	->	At 300 feet AGL you still have options: (1) S-turns or a modified approach path to burn off excess energy and altitude; (2) increasing airspeed briefly to steepen the glide path; or (3) adjusting the aim point slightly. However, if the approach deteriorates and cannot be salvaged through these techniques, the correct action is to request a power recovery from the examiner rather than attempting a 'go-around.' A true go-around in a simulated autorotation (engine-inoperative scenario) is not a standard recovery technique and represents task failure. A power recovery is a distinct maneuver performed when power is restored by the examiner. The ACS standard is clear: if you cannot meet the task standards (touch down within 200 feet of the target) through normal technique adjustments, a power recovery represents a failure of this task element. The goal is to recognize energy state problems early enough — ideally above 500 feet AGL — to make corrections without requiring a power recovery. Waiting until 300 feet significantly limits your options.
AI_EDIT	At.004	During a banked autorotation turn, the increased load factor can actually cause RPM to increase slightly due to additional blade loading changing the pitch angle of the blades relative to the relative wind — combined with the possibility of having entered the turn with excess airspeed. Apply a small amount of collective to bring RPM back into the normal operating range, being careful not to over-apply and cause RPM decay. Roll out smoothly and reassess before beginning the second 90-degree turn.	->	During a banked autorotation turn, the increased load factor increases induced drag on the rotor, which typically causes RPM to decay if collective is not managed properly. If rotor RPM is trending toward the upper green arc limit during a turn, this typically indicates you entered the turn with excess airspeed or have not lowered collective sufficiently. Apply a small amount of collective to reduce blade pitch and bring RPM back into the normal operating range, being careful not to over-apply and cause RPM decay. Roll out smoothly and reassess before beginning the second 90-degree turn.
AI_EDIT	At.007	During any banked turn, the rotor disk must produce a horizontal component of lift to change direction in addition to the vertical component supporting the aircraft's weight. This increases total load factor beyond 1G. The higher load factor increases induced drag on the rotor blades, which can cause RPM decay if collective is set for straight-ahead autorotation. Additionally, the rate of descent may increase during turns because effective lift is reduced unless the pilot compensates. The pilot must anticipate this RPM tendency and manage collective carefully — typically allowing a small collective reduction is not needed, but the pilot must be ready to make small adjustments to stay in the green arc.	->	During any banked turn, the rotor disk must produce a horizontal component of lift to change direction in addition to the vertical component supporting the aircraft's weight. This increases total load factor beyond 1G. The higher load factor increases induced drag on the rotor blades, which causes RPM decay if collective is maintained at the setting used for straight-ahead autorotation. Additionally, the rate of descent may increase during turns because some lift is diverted to the horizontal component unless the pilot compensates. The pilot must anticipate this RPM tendency and manage collective carefully — typically by lowering collective slightly during the turn to reduce blade pitch, which allows airflow to accelerate the rotor back to the proper RPM range. Small collective adjustments throughout the turn are necessary to maintain RPM in the green arc.
