AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.C.RM.006	This situation presents compounding risks. Continuing the turn while managing low rotor RPM demands divided attention at a critical phase. Your priority actions are: (1) Arrest rotor RPM decay — this may require reducing bank angle, adjusting airspeed, or adjusting entry attitude. (2) Assess whether you can complete the remaining 90 degrees of turn AND roll out by 300 feet AGL with acceptable RPM. (3) If the answer is 'probably not,' initiate a power recovery immediately — do not wait until 300 feet to make that decision. Low rotor RPM in a banked autorotation below 600 feet is a convergence of hazards. In a real emergency, low RPM means reduced energy for flare and collective cushion, increasing the chance of a hard landing or rotor stall. The correct risk management decision is to recover power early rather than press on.	->	question text changed
