AI_EDIT	Roll.007	The priority sequence is: (1) Rotor RPM first — if RPM is low but within limits and declining, apply very slight forward cyclic to increase airspeed, which increases upward airflow through the disk and drives RPM back up. Do NOT lower collective at this altitude as it removes the cushion needed for flare. (2) Establish proper glide airspeed — the forward cyclic input to recover RPM will simultaneously begin to recover airspeed. (3) Plan the flare — at approximately 75–100 feet AGL (varies by aircraft), initiate the flare with aft cyclic to slow descent rate and airspeed while rotor RPM increases due to flare effect. (4) Level the aircraft and cushion — use collective at 8–15 feet to cushion touchdown. The key principle is that you cannot trade altitude for time at 300 feet AGL — every decision must be deliberate and conservative. A low RPM at rollout with low airspeed is a compounding emergency requiring immediate, smooth correction.	->	The priority sequence is: (1) **Rotor RPM first** — if RPM is low but within limits and declining, apply very slight forward cyclic to increase airspeed, which increases upward airflow through the disk and drives RPM back up. Do NOT lower collective at this altitude when RPM is low, as it removes the altitude cushion needed for flare. (2) Establish proper glide airspeed — the forward cyclic input to recover RPM will simultaneously begin to recover airspeed. (3) Plan the flare — at approximately 75–100 feet AGL (varies by aircraft per RFM), initiate the flare with aft cyclic to slow descent rate and airspeed while rotor RPM increases due to flare effect. (4) Level the aircraft and cushion — use collective at 8–15 feet to cushion touchdown. **Note:** If RPM were instead above the green arc (overspeed) at rollout, a small, smooth upward collective input would load the blades and reduce RPM; however, this must be applied cautiously to preserve collective authority for touchdown cushion. The key principle is that you cannot trade altitude for time at 300 feet AGL — every decision must be deliberate and conservative, and all control inputs must be smooth and proportional to the error.
