AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.E.002	A preflight briefing is required because simulated powerplant failures in multiengine helicopters involve real risk — unexpected asymmetric thrust, torque changes, and yaw inputs can lead to loss of control if the applicant is unprepared. The briefing ensures the applicant understands the stimulus they will receive, the expected response, and agreed-upon safety abort criteria, reducing the risk of an actual accident during the simulated emergency.	->	The 1,000-foot AGL minimum is a safety buffer specific to OEI training because the helicopter is operating with reduced power availability and potentially degraded performance margins. Introducing the simulated failure at or above 1,000 feet AGL allows the crew adequate time to assess the situation, configure the aircraft, establish a stable approach profile, and retain sufficient altitude to recover if the approach becomes unstable or the simulated failure must be restored. This provides a safety margin unavailable at lower altitudes.
