AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.F.RM.002	The three conditions are: (1) a power setting of 20–100% of available power (some power applied but not full), (2) a vertical descent rate between approximately 300 and 2,000 fpm, and (3) low forward airspeed — typically less than ETL speed (around 16–24 knots depending on the helicopter). Knowing these conditions helps establish entry altitude because the pilot must deliberately set up all three, which takes some maneuvering time and altitude. You need enough altitude above obstacles and the ground to configure, enter, allow VRS to develop, AND recover — so the entry altitude briefed with the evaluator accounts for all of those phases.	->	The three conditions are: (1) a power setting of approximately 40–100% of available power (higher power settings increase VRS likelihood), (2) a vertical descent rate between approximately 300 and 2,000 fpm, and (3) low forward airspeed — typically below ETL (Effective Translational Lift) speed, which ranges from approximately 16–24 knots depending on the helicopter type. Knowing these conditions helps establish entry altitude because the pilot must deliberately set up all three, which takes maneuvering time and altitude. You need enough altitude above obstacles and the ground to configure, enter, allow VRS to develop, AND recover. Exact threshold values vary significantly by helicopter model and configuration, so instructors should brief type-specific parameters.
