AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.F.RM.007	This scenario illustrates why the briefed entry altitude must include a pre-determined abort/escape altitude. If standard recovery inputs (forward cyclic to accelerate through ETL) are applied and the sink rate is not decreasing by a certain altitude floor — ideally briefed beforehand as something like 2,500 ft AGL — then the pilot must transition to a full autorotation to stop rotor RPM decay and manage energy, rather than continuing to apply power in a worsening VRS condition. Continuing to apply collective in a deep VRS state can worsen the condition. The decision point is not a fixed number but should be agreed upon before the maneuver: 'If I have not broken out by X altitude AGL, I will lower collective, enter autorotation, and execute a power recovery from there.' This reinforces why entry altitude and abort criteria must be explicitly briefed with the evaluator — not assumed.	->	question text changed
