Question ID:   CH.I.B.K3.002
ACS Code:      CH.I.B.K3
Difficulty:    basic

Question:
The FAA considers an approved MEL to be what kind of FAA approval document, and what does that mean practically for your helicopter?

Answer (at time of rejection):
The FAA considers an approved MEL to be a Supplemental Type Certificate (STC) for that specific aircraft. Practically, this means the MEL is aircraft-specific — it is tied to the individual helicopter's registration and serial number, not to the make and model generally. You cannot use an MEL from a different helicopter of the same model; it must be the MEL specifically approved for your tail number.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The characterization of an approved MEL as an 'Supplemental Type Certificate (STC)' is misleading. While an MEL is an FAA approval document, it is not technically an STC. An MEL is issued as an operational approval document under 14 CFR 21.25 and becomes part of the aircraft's Type Certificate Data Sheet, but it is not the same thing as an STC.
The statement that 'you cannot use an MEL from a different helicopter of the same model' is overly restrictive. An FAA-approved MEL for a specific aircraft type (e.g., all Robinson R44s) CAN be used by any aircraft of that model, provided the operator has an approved MEL document. The MEL is type-specific but not tail-number-specific in the way stated. However, some operators may have aircraft-specific MELs that differ by serial number or configuration.

