Answer (at time of rejection):
You should immediately request clarification from ATC — do not proceed based on an assumption. The word 'probably' is a critical red flag in ADM; the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook (Trescott Tip) specifically warns that if an action will only 'probably' work out, you should choose the option you know will work. Proceeding on an assumption that you 'probably' heard the right thing, or that ATC 'probably' meant the expected instruction, is exactly how confirmation bias leads to accidents. The correct action is to ask ATC to 'say again' or confirm the instruction — ATC would rather repeat a clearance than deal with a runway incursion. Always operate on known facts, not probable guesses.

