Difficulty:    advanced

Question:
A pilot initiates a rolling takeoff and notices the helicopter is tracking slightly left of the intended departure path as it begins rolling. They apply right cyclic to correct the drift. Why is this the wrong control input, and what is the correct technique?

Answer (at time of rejection):
Right cyclic will tilt the rotor disk to the right, causing the helicopter to roll or drift right, but it does not correct directional tracking on the ground the way a rudder corrects a fixed-wing aircraft. On wheeled helicopters during a ground roll, directional control is primarily maintained through the antitorque pedals — right pedal increases tail rotor thrust and yaws the nose right. Applying right cyclic instead introduces a lateral rotor tilt that can cause the helicopter to begin moving sideways, load one set of wheels unevenly, and potentially introduce a dynamic rollover condition, especially if the surface is soft or uneven. The correct technique is right pedal to yaw the nose back onto the takeoff heading while keeping cyclic centered on the forward takeoff attitude.

