The power band is critical because VRS can only develop when the rotor is producing thrust — specifically in the range of approximately 20–100% of available power. If power is too low (near autorotation — below roughly 20%), the rotor downwash weakens and the recirculating vortex does not form; the helicopter essentially begins to autorotate instead. If power is at maximum with low airspeed, the rotor wash is directed predominantly downward and VRS is less likely because the induced velocity is high enough to prevent significant recirculation. The most dangerous zone is intermediate power with a moderate to high descent rate at low airspeed, because the induced velocity through the disk is low enough to allow the helicopter to descend into and recirculate within its own vortex. Setting approximately 60–80% torque while establishing a 300+ fpm descent at near-zero airspeed replicates the ideal VRS entry conditions for training.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The statement 'if power is at maximum with low airspeed, the rotor wash is directed predominantly downward and VRS is less likely because the induced velocity is high enough to prevent significant recirculation' is oversimplified and potentially misleading. VRS can occur at high power settings; the critical factor is descent rate into the recirculation, not power percentage alone. The answer conflates rotor orientation with induced velocity in a way that could confuse understanding of VRS physics.
The 20-100% power band is accurate, but the explanation of why power above 80% reduces VRS likelihood is not well-founded. High power with low airspeed and high descent rate can absolutely produce severe VRS. The real issue is that at very high power near-hover, the rotor may be operating at steep collective angles that prevent the recirculating pattern.

