Answer (at time of rejection):
A crosswind from the upslope direction pushes the fuselage toward the downslope side while simultaneously trying to tilt the rotor disk. This means you must apply more upslope cyclic than you would in calm conditions to counteract the wind's lateral push, but you must also correct for the cyclic required to manage the crosswind component along the rotor disk. The rollover hazard unique to this crosswind scenario is that the wind can cause the downslope skid to weathervane toward the slope surface even as you're holding the upslope skid on the ground — creating a lateral rolling moment in the same direction as an unintended dynamic rollover. If the pilot misidentifies this yaw/roll tendency and applies downslope cyclic thinking they're correcting for drift, they accelerate the rollover. Correct technique is to hold firm upslope cyclic, use pedals only to manage yaw/trim, and be prepared to go around if the wind makes stable single-skid contact impossible.

