You're conducting a rolling takeoff and encounter a microburst windshear at approximately 50 feet AGL — airspeed suddenly drops 20 knots and the helicopter begins to sink. Walk me through your immediate control response and explain why each input is made in the specific order it is.

Answer (at time of rejection):
At 50 feet AGL in a microburst encounter after a rolling takeoff, the sequence is: (1) Immediately apply full available power — collective up to maximum torque/power limit. This is the first and most critical input because the sudden headwind loss removes rotor inflow efficiency and reduces lift; the only compensation available is maximum power. (2) Simultaneously lower the nose slightly with cyclic to maintain or regain airspeed — the helicopter needs to accelerate through the loss of headwind and cannot afford to trade the limited airspeed for pitch attitude. (3) Maintain rotor RPM in the green — monitor the tachometer and do not allow collective inputs to bog the rotor; if RPM decays, slight reduction of collective may be necessary even though altitude is being lost, because a rotor stall at 50 feet is unrecoverable. (4) Do not attempt to arrest the sink with aft cyclic alone — this increases induced drag on the rotor and will worsen the descent. The order matters because power is the only tool that directly counters the lift loss; attitude adjustments and rotor management are secondary but must happen simultaneously to avoid compounding the problem. If the helicopter contacts the ground during this sequence, the landing gear and ground roll characteristics of wheel-type gear provide a better chance of a survivable forced landing than skid-type gear would.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The answer states 'lower the nose slightly with cyclic to maintain or regain airspeed' as step 2, but in a microburst encounter at 50 feet AGL where the helicopter is already sinking and has just lost 20 knots, lowering the nose (increasing pitch-down) would increase descent rate further. The correct technique in severe windshear at low altitude typically emphasizes maintaining or increasing collective for power while using cyclic to maintain level or slight nose-up attitude to maximize climb capability, not to increase descent rate.
The guidance 'slight reduction of collective may be necessary even though altitude is being lost, because a rotor stall at 50 feet is unrecoverable' is somewhat contradictory — while rotor stall prevention is critical, at 50 feet in a microburst, immediate power application and rotor RPM maintenance are paramount; the nuance about trading altitude for RPM needs clarification that this is only a last-resort measure and differs from standard windshear recovery at higher altitudes.
ASRS Advisory Circular reference should be verified — AC 00-46 is 'Aviation Weather Services' and may not be the primary reference for microburst encounter procedures; AC 00-54B 'Pilot Windshear Guide' would be more appropriate.

