At very cold temperatures, air density increases significantly above standard, which increases the mass flow through the rotor disk. This has several effects: induced drag on the rotor blades increases, which helps maintain rotor RPM in the normal range without overspeeding; the collective is more effective during the flare and cushion because each degree of pitch change moves more air mass; and overall autorotation performance is improved compared to a hot/high day. The denser air provides more aerodynamic braking naturally, which can prevent rotor RPM from running too high during the glide. Engine performance also tends to be better in cold dense air. Cold temperatures can genuinely work in the pilot's favor for autorotation efficiency, but the pilot must still account for potential ice, reduced visibility, and the risk of carburetor icing in applicable powerplants.

