The follow-up answer contradicts the main answer's conclusion. It states 'the rotor will tend to run at higher RPM for a given collective setting' in cold dense air, then advises to 'carry slightly more collective during the glide to keep RPM within the normal operating range and avoid an overspeed condition.' This is contradictory — if cold air naturally causes higher RPM, carrying MORE collective would further increase RPM and worsen overspeed risk. The correct response should be to use LESS collective (lower pitch) or accept the higher RPM within limits.
The regulatory references are appropriate, but the aerodynamic explanation needs clarification for advanced students.

