Low-inertia rotor systems store less kinetic energy in the spinning rotor blades due to lower blade mass. When windshear reduces effective airflow through the disk, rotor RPM decays much faster than in high-inertia systems, leaving the pilot a shorter time window to respond before RPM falls below the minimum for effective autorotation. At 300 feet AGL, the altitude and time available are already severely limited. Regarding rotor type: articulated rotors allow blade flapping and lead/lag movement, which can momentarily help accommodate asymmetric loading during windshear; semirigid rotors permit feathering and flapping but not lead/lag, and are common in light training helicopters that often have low-inertia systems; rigid rotors permit only feathering and transfer more stress to the hub. In all cases, the rotor type does not fundamentally change the pilot's recovery technique — immediate reduction of collective to reduce drag and allow upward airflow to restore RPM remains the primary response — but the urgency is greater in low-inertia designs because the RPM decay rate is faster and the margin for error is smaller.

