The compounding risk factors are: (1) task saturation — four simultaneous instructions exceed normal single-pilot IFR processing capacity; (2) communication interruption — missing the altimeter setting means the altimeter may be incorrectly set, creating an altitude error of potentially hundreds of feet near terrain or obstacle clearance altitudes (each 1 Hg = 1,000 feet of indication error); (3) frequency change without completing readback — transitioning to tower without confirming the full clearance means you may be on the wrong frequency or missing critical approach information; (4) the descent clearance combined with a potentially wrong altimeter creates a serious CFIT risk. Correct action: Do not change frequency immediately. Key the mic and request a full repeat: 'Unable to copy complete instructions, please re-state.' If already on a new frequency, advise tower of the issue and request they coordinate the altimeter setting via TRACON. Do not descend until the altimeter setting is confirmed. This situation illustrates how distractions compound — each item alone is manageable, but layered together they create an error chain that can end in CFIT.

