Difficulty:    advanced

Question:
Ground resonance has been described as a form of destructive vibration. Explain the mechanical origin of ground resonance, which specific rotor system components are involved, and why it is not possible on a two-bladed teetering rotor system.

Answer (at time of rejection):
Ground resonance is a self-reinforcing, potentially destructive oscillation that occurs when the helicopter is on the ground and the natural frequency of the airframe on its landing gear matches the frequency of main rotor blade lead-lag (in-plane) movement. In an articulated rotor system, each blade can move forward and aft in the plane of rotation (lead-lag motion) about a vertical hinge. If the blades become unequally spaced in the lead-lag plane — due to a hard landing, rough ground contact, or worn dampeners — the rotor hub center of gravity moves away from the axis of rotation. This imbalance creates a lateral rocking of the airframe. If the rocking frequency resonates with the rotor's lead-lag frequency, the oscillations reinforce each other and can rapidly grow in amplitude until the helicopter rolls over or structurally fails. The specific components involved are the lead-lag (drag) hinges, blade lag dampeners (which are designed to absorb this energy and prevent resonance buildup), and the landing gear oleo struts (which, if improperly inflated, change the airframe's natural frequency). A two-bladed teetering (semi-rigid) rotor cannot experience classic ground resonance because teetering rotors have no lead-lag hinges — the blades are fixed to the hub in the lead-lag plane. Without the ability for individual blades to independently move in-plane, the blade-spacing imbalance that initiates ground resonance cannot develop.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The statement 'a two-bladed teetering rotor cannot experience classic ground resonance because teetering rotors have no lead-lag hinges' is oversimplified and potentially misleading. While it is true that teetering rotors lack the lead-lag hinges found on articulated systems, ground resonance-like phenomena can still occur on teetering rotor systems under specific conditions involving the pitch-flap-yaw coupling inherent in teetering designs. The answer conflates 'ground resonance' with only articulated rotor systems, which may not be entirely accurate for all helicopter types.
The answer correctly describes ground resonance in articulated rotor systems, but the explanation for why teetering rotors are immune needs clarification: the primary reason is the rigid-in-plane constraint and the aerodynamic self-stabilizing characteristics of teetering rotors, not merely the absence of lead-lag hinges. Some advanced texts note that teetering rotor systems exhibit different but still potentially problematic ground resonance behaviors.

