Answer (at time of rejection):
Coning angle is the upward deflection of rotor blades caused by the balance between centrifugal force (which flattens the disk) and aerodynamic lift (which pushes blades upward). As rotor RPM decreases, centrifugal force — which is proportional to RPM squared — decreases dramatically. With less centrifugal force to hold the blades flat, the blades cone upward more steeply. This increased coning shifts the effective thrust vector inward and reduces the rotor disk area, making the rotor less efficient at producing lift. To compensate for this lift loss, the pilot may increase collective pitch further, which adds more blade drag and accelerates RPM decay. This creates a reinforcing cycle: lower RPM → more coning → less lift efficiency → more collective → more drag → even lower RPM. This is why the response to low RPM must be immediate and must prioritize rotor RPM recovery — not lift maintenance — by reducing collective before the cycle becomes unrecoverable.

