Answer (at time of rejection):
First priority is recognizing the failure — an attitude indicator that has tumbled or failed may show a false horizon, which is more dangerous than no attitude indicator at all. Immediately transition to partial-panel: use the turn-and-slip indicator for bank information (needle deflection indicates direction and rate of turn; ball indicates coordination), airspeed indicator for pitch trend (increasing ASI indicates nose-low pitch; decreasing indicates nose-high), altimeter for pitch confirmation (rising or falling), and VSI for pitch rate. For bank control: center the turn needle to stop the turn, then hold it centered to maintain wings-level. For pitch control: target a specific airspeed on the ASI and stabilize VSI at zero for level flight. Avoid abrupt control inputs — the FAA emphasizes small, precise corrections to prevent oscillation and worsening spatial disorientation. Declare an emergency, request radar vectors to a clear area or airport, and brief ATC on the partial-panel situation to get priority handling.

