Question ID:   Loss.002
ACS Code:      Loss
Difficulty:    basic

Question:
Name the three main wind azimuth conditions that contribute to loss of tail rotor effectiveness, and describe where those wind directions come from relative to the nose of the helicopter.

Answer (at time of rejection):
The three LTE-contributing wind azimuths are: (1) Tail rotor vortex ring state — wind from approximately 210° to 330° (left rear quartering to right rear), where the tail rotor can enter its own vortex ring state and lose thrust; (2) Main rotor disk vortex interference — wind from approximately 285° to 315° (left rear), where main rotor tip vortices are directed into the tail rotor; (3) Weathercock stability — wind from approximately 120° to 240° (right rear to right side), which weathercocks the nose away from the wind, adding an unwanted right yaw in addition to torque. All bearings are relative to the helicopter nose.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
Wind azimuth description is imprecise and potentially confusing. The stated wind azimuths (210°-330°, 285°-315°, 120°-240°) do not align clearly with FAA-H-8083-21B and AC 90-95 standard presentations. AC 90-95 typically describes LTE susceptibility in terms of wind direction relative to the helicopter's heading (e.g., quartering tail winds from the left-rear), not absolute compass bearings. The answer conflates absolute wind direction with relative wind direction to the aircraft, which could confuse students.
The three named conditions (tail rotor vortex ring state, main rotor disk vortex interference, weathercock stability) are valid LTE contributors, but the wind azimuth ranges provided are not standard FAA terminology and may not match all helicopter types or operational scenarios. FAA materials typically describe LTE risk in relative wind terms (e.g., winds from 150°-270° relative to the nose in some references) rather than absolute compass headings.

