Answer (at time of rejection):
With a radio failure, you must use all available means to comply with 14 CFR 91.185 procedures, which apply to IFR flight, but the underlying principle of see-and-avoid under VFR still governs. For the uncontrolled airstrip with a CTAF, there is no legal requirement to have a functioning radio to land at an uncontrolled field under VFR — radio communication is not required at non-towered airports. However, your obligations are: (1) maintain vigilant visual scanning for traffic at all times, (2) follow standard traffic pattern entry and departure procedures using light signals if needed at a towered field, (3) rock your wings or use other visual signals if possible to alert other traffic, (4) if the destination has a tower, squawk 7600 on your transponder if equipped, and (5) land as soon as practical and have the radio repaired before further flight if the failure affects required equipment. The priority is always safe aircraft control — continuing the confined area departure safely before addressing the communication issue.

