Yes, rotor system type affects urgency primarily through inertia characteristics, not through blade movement degrees of freedom. Articulated rotor systems — which allow blade pitch change, flapping, and lead/lag — typically appear on larger, heavier helicopters with greater rotor inertia, meaning RPM decay after engine failure is somewhat slower, giving marginally more time to respond. Semirigid rotors (feathering and flapping, no lead/lag) tend to appear on lighter helicopters with lower rotor inertia, so RPM decay is faster. The ACS does not differentiate by rotor type in the 1,000-foot minimum because that minimum is set as an absolute safety floor that must be adequate for the worst-case scenario — the lowest-inertia certified helicopter. The actual technique (immediate collective reduction, aft cyclic at cruise, pedal input) is the same regardless of rotor type; only the time available before RPM decay becomes critical differs.

