AI_EDIT	PH.IV.B.005	A NOTAM or temporary frequency change issued through the ATC system takes precedence over printed chart data, since charts are only updated periodically and may not reflect recent changes. To verify, cross-reference the frequency against the Chart Supplement (which may reflect more recent updates than the sectional), check NOTAMs for the departure airport, and confirm with ATC directly on the ground before becoming airborne. When in doubt, contacting the facility on any known working frequency — including FSS — to confirm the current operational frequency is the safest approach.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.103, pilots must review current aeronautical information before flight. A NOTAM or temporary frequency change issued through the ATC system takes precedence over printed chart data, since charts are only updated periodically and may not reflect recent changes. To verify, cross-reference the frequency against the Chart Supplement (which may reflect more recent updates than the sectional), check NOTAMs for the departure airport, and confirm with ATC directly on the ground before becoming airborne. When in doubt, contact the facility on any known working frequency — including FSS — to confirm the current operational frequency is the safest approach.
AI_EDIT	PH.VIII.A.S4.006	Yes, wind affects the landing area selection decision. When hovering into a headwind, the wind is pushing from ahead of the helicopter toward the tail. If rotor RPM decays and the helicopter begins to settle without corrective cyclic input, the aircraft may drift in the downwind direction — that is, rearward relative to the pilot's facing direction, because the wind is blowing from ahead. The pilot must therefore ensure the area behind and below the helicopter is clear of obstacles and suitable for touchdown, not only the area directly beneath. Additionally, FAA-H-8083-21B notes that some translational lift benefit may be present even in a low hover when a headwind exists, which can slightly influence the settling characteristics, though this is secondary to the pilot's immediate need to cushion the descent using remaining rotor inertia and to select a clear touchdown area. The proximity to the grass airstrip threshold may offer a suitable surface both ahead and behind, but the pilot must account for the wind-driven drift direction when mentally confirming that landing area is usable.	->	Yes, wind significantly affects the landing area selection decision. When hovering into a headwind, wind pushes from ahead toward the tail. If rotor RPM decays and power is lost, the aircraft will drift downwind — that is, backward relative to the pilot's facing direction. The pilot must ensure that the area behind and below the helicopter is clear of obstacles and suitable for touchdown. Additionally, even at low hover altitude, the headwind provides translational lift benefit; as rotor RPM decays and this benefit is lost, the settling rate increases noticeably. The pilot must plan to cushion the descent using remaining rotor inertia and select a suitable touchdown area accounting for downwind drift. In this scenario, the grass airstrip threshold ahead may offer suitable surface, but the pilot must confirm that the area to the rear is also clear.
