AI_EDIT	PH.V.D.Position.001	With a direct headwind, position the cyclic slightly forward of neutral. According to FAA-H-8083-21B, when taxiing into a headwind the cyclic should be moved forward to tilt the rotor disk into the wind, which helps maintain control of the rotor system. Because the wind is coming from directly ahead, no lateral cyclic correction is needed — left and right lift forces on the advancing and retreating blades are essentially symmetrical. The forward cyclic position counteracts the tendency of the relative wind to lift the leading edge of the rotor disk. A headwind also reduces the differential between advancing and retreating blade airspeeds compared to a quartering tailwind, which makes rotor control more manageable; however, in gusty conditions the pilot must remain alert to sudden changes in airflow that can momentarily create asymmetric lift, requiring prompt cyclic correction to keep the rotor disk from flapping unexpectedly. The primary rule from the handbook is to always tilt the cyclic into the wind — forward for a headwind — to maintain positive control of the rotor disk during ground taxi.	->	With a direct headwind, position the cyclic aft (back) of neutral. According to FAA-H-8083-21B, when taxiing into a headwind the cyclic should be moved aft to tilt the rotor disk away from the wind and prevent the nose from pitching up. A direct headwind generates relative airflow that would otherwise lift the nose; by tilting the disk aft, the pilot maintains level attitude and positive control of the rotor system. Because the wind is coming from directly ahead, no lateral cyclic correction is needed—left and right lift forces on the advancing and retreating blades are essentially symmetrical. A headwind also reduces the differential between advancing and retreating blade airspeeds, which makes rotor control more manageable. However, in gusty conditions the pilot must remain alert to sudden changes in airflow that can momentarily create asymmetric lift, requiring prompt cyclic correction to keep the rotor disk from flapping unexpectedly. The primary rule from the handbook is to always tilt the cyclic into the wind direction—aft for a headwind—to maintain positive control of the rotor disk during ground taxi.
AI_EDIT	PH.VI.B.SKL-RC.005	Under 14 CFR 91.130(c), two-way radio communication is required throughout Class C airspace — not just for entry. Losing communication after entry constitutes a deviation from that requirement, not a permitted operating condition. You should immediately squawk 7600 to alert ATC of the communication failure. Attempt to re-establish contact on any available frequency, including the guard frequency 121.5 MHz, and try the previously assigned frequency as well as any published ATIS or approach frequencies. Because Class C airspace is associated with a primary airport that has an operating control tower, you should look for light gun signals from the tower if you are in visual range; however, light gun procedures are formally described in FAA guidance for Class D operations, and their applicability depends on whether the tower has visual contact with your aircraft. If you are already established on final and have a previously issued clearance, continuing the approach while actively attempting to restore communication and monitoring for tower light signals is a reasonable course of action consistent with the principle of completing a safe landing. After landing, you must notify ATC of the communication failure as soon as practicable. Document the event thoroughly, as the deviation from 91.130(c) may require a report or explanation to the responsible ARTCC or approach facility.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.130(c), two-way radio communication is required throughout Class C airspace. Loss of communication is a deviation from that requirement. Immediately squawk 7600 to alert ATC. Attempt to restore communication on the assigned frequency, clearance delivery, ground, approach, ATIS, and guard frequency (121.5 MHz). Light gun signals are formally prescribed for Class D and Class E operations (14 CFR 91.125); if the Class C tower has visual contact, monitor for light gun signals as a backup. If you are already established on final approach with a previous clearance and cannot restore communication, continuing to a safe landing while actively attempting communication and monitoring for tower signals is a reasonable emergency mitigation procedure. Land as soon as practicable and notify ATC immediately after landing. Document the event thoroughly; this is a deviation from 91.130(c) that may require explanation to the controlling facility.
AUTO_APPROVE	PH.VI.B.Establish.002	The answer correctly identifies cyclic as the primary control for rate of closure and explicitly clarifies that cyclic and collective work together in a coordinated approach; the structure and substance are sound.
AI_EDIT	PH.VI.D.RM.Effects.002	On a steep approach, ETL is lost at a higher altitude than on a normal approach because the steeper angle means the helicopter decelerates through the ETL airspeed range earlier in the descent. According to FAA-H-8083-21B, ETL is associated with airspeeds in the range where rotor efficiency begins to decrease as the helicopter slows, but the specific airspeed at which ETL is lost varies by helicopter type and loading condition. When windshear is present, particularly a sudden tailwind shift, it can abruptly reduce airspeed and cause the helicopter to lose ETL even earlier and more suddenly than anticipated. This requires an earlier and larger collective increase, which in turn demands more power at a time when the pilot may not be fully prepared for it.	->	Effective translational lift (ETL) is an airspeed phenomenon occurring in the range of approximately 10–15 knots (depending on helicopter type and loading). As the helicopter decelerates during approach, it passes through the ETL airspeed range and transitions to hover flight, where rotor efficiency decreases. On a steep approach, the helicopter reaches and passes through the ETL airspeed threshold at a higher altitude above the landing point than on a normal approach, because the steeper descent profile causes faster deceleration. When windshear introduces a sudden tailwind shift, airspeed decreases abruptly, causing the helicopter to transition through ETL more suddenly than anticipated. This requires an earlier and larger collective input to maintain rotor RPM and lift, demanding more power at a critical point in the approach when the pilot may not be fully prepared.
AI_EDIT	PH.VI.G.S_radio.006	Even without contact with company ops, you should continue making position calls on the appropriate air-to-air or advisory frequency to alert other aircraft in the area. Make calls at key points: beginning the approach, on short final, landing, and before departure. For the company communications failure itself, Part 135 operators are required to maintain flight following and communications procedures as outlined in their individual Operations Specifications (OpSpecs) issued by the FAA — these vary by certificate holder, so the specific check-in intervals, required actions, and escalation procedures (including when to initiate search and rescue protocols) are defined in your company's OpSpecs and General Operations Manual, not by a single universal regulation. You should attempt contact on all available frequencies, including guard (121.5 MHz) if normal comms are unavailable. After the flight, document the communications failure and report it to your chief pilot so the company can address the gap in flight following coverage and comply with any reporting requirements in their OpSpecs. Maintaining positional radio calls to other traffic remains your primary in-flight responsibility even during the comm failure.	->	Even without contact with company ops, continue making position calls on the appropriate air-to-air or advisory frequency to alert other aircraft in the area at key points: beginning approach, on short final, landing, and before departure. Part 135 operators are required to maintain flight-following and communications procedures as outlined in their FAA-approved Flight Operations Manual and Operations Specifications (OpSpecs). Specific check-in intervals, required actions, and escalation procedures—including when to initiate SAR protocols—are defined in your company's approved manual and OpSpecs. Attempt contact on all available frequencies, including 121.5 MHz (guard) if normal comms are unavailable. After landing, document the failure and immediately notify your chief pilot, who must report the communications loss to the assigned Principal Operations Inspector (POI) if it impacts safe operations, per 14 CFR 135.63 (hazmat/incident reporting) and company reporting protocols. Maintaining positional radio calls to traffic remains your primary in-flight responsibility during the comm failure.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.H.S4.006	A sudden headwind gust will increase indicated airspeed while groundspeed remains relatively stable or may briefly decrease due to the helicopter's inertia. Because the helicopter now has more lift-producing airspeed than needed for the approach, it will tend to float above the desired glide path — the approach angle flattens and the rate of closure to the touchdown point slows. To correct, reduce collective slightly to re-establish the proper descent rate and maintain the approach angle. Monitor that the aiming point returns to its stable reference position on the windscreen. If the approach becomes destabilized — for example, if the helicopter continues to balloon or airspeed remains excessive — be prepared to apply a go-around. This scenario reinforces the importance of cross-checking both instruments and outside visual references throughout the shallow approach profile.	->	A sudden headwind gust increases indicated airspeed, which increases lift and causes the helicopter to balloon above the intended descent path, flattening the approach angle and slowing the rate of closure to the touchdown point. Groundspeed may decrease due to the opposing wind, but the primary effect is the increased lift. Correct by reducing collective slightly to re-establish the proper descent rate and maintain approach angle. Monitor that the aiming point returns to its stable reference position on the windscreen. If the approach becomes destabilized—for example, if the helicopter continues to balloon or airspeed remains excessive—be prepared to execute a go-around. This reinforces the importance of cross-checking both instruments and outside visual references throughout the shallow approach.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.I.S4.005	When verifying the takeoff path for a 'fly runway heading' instruction, you confirm the initial climb heading aligns with the extended runway centerline direction. The distinction between runway heading and magnetic course is important: runway numbers are derived from the magnetic course of the runway rounded to the nearest 10 degrees (per AIM 4-3-18), so Runway 27, for example, has a magnetic course somewhere in the range that rounds to 270. 'Fly runway heading' as an ATC instruction means fly the magnetic direction aligned with the runway centerline — which may differ somewhat from the rounded runway number but is not necessarily a precisely published single value. Pilots should use the heading indicator (verified against the magnetic compass during runup) to fly the heading that corresponds to the actual runway centerline direction as depicted on airport charts or approach plates. During the pre-takeoff path check, you also verify obstacle clearance along that extended centerline, confirm no conflicting traffic is on the departure path, and ensure the intended flight path is clear of hazards.	->	When verifying the takeoff path for a 'fly runway heading' instruction, confirm the initial climb heading aligns with the extended runway centerline direction. Runway numbers are assigned based on the magnetic course rounded to the nearest 10 degrees per AIM 4-3-18. For example, Runway 27 has a magnetic course between 265° and 275°. The instruction 'fly runway heading' directs you to fly the actual published magnetic course of the runway centerline, which is fixed and shown on airport charts and approach plates. Verify this heading on your compass and heading indicator during runup. During the pre-takeoff path check, you also verify that the extended runway centerline is clear of obstacles, confirm no conflicting traffic is on the departure path, and ensure your initial climb heading aligns with the centerline before advancing to takeoff power.
AI_EDIT	PH.VII.A.K4a.008	To determine magnetic course: (1) Mark your estimated current position on the sectional using your last known checkpoint, elapsed time, groundspeed, and heading. (2) Draw a straight line from your current estimated position to the divert airport. (3) Use the plotter to measure the true course of that line against a meridian (line of longitude) near the midpoint of the segment. (4) Apply magnetic variation from the nearest isogonic line to convert true course to magnetic course (TC ± variation). (5) Apply wind correction angle based on current winds aloft to determine the magnetic heading to fly. For dead reckoning arrival estimate: (1) Measure the distance to the divert airport (22 NM given). (2) Calculate groundspeed using current airspeed and the wind component along the new route. (3) Time = Distance ÷ Groundspeed (e.g., 22 NM ÷ 90 kt GS = 0.244 hours = ~15 minutes). (4) Add this to current time to get estimated time of arrival. Also verify fuel remaining is sufficient for the divert plus a reserve. If the direct route crosses special use airspace such as restricted areas, note that under 14 CFR 91.3 a pilot in command may deviate from regulations to the extent necessary to meet an emergency, but this authority does not automatically grant entry into active restricted airspace. ATC coordination with the controlling agency is still required and must be sought; entry may not be authorized if a military operation is in progress. If authorization cannot be obtained, the pilot should plan a route that avoids the restricted area.	->	To determine magnetic course: (1) Mark your estimated current position on the sectional using your last known checkpoint, elapsed time, groundspeed, and heading. (2) Draw a straight line from your current estimated position to the divert airport. (3) Use the plotter to measure the true course of that line against a meridian (line of longitude) near the midpoint of the segment. (4) Apply magnetic variation from the nearest isogonic line to convert true course to magnetic course (TC ± variation). (5) Apply wind correction angle based on current winds aloft to determine the magnetic heading to fly. For dead reckoning arrival estimate: (1) Measure the distance to the divert airport (22 NM given). (2) Calculate groundspeed using current airspeed and the wind component along the new route. (3) Time = Distance ÷ Groundspeed (e.g., 22 NM ÷ 90 kt GS = 0.244 hours = ~15 minutes). (4) Add this to current time to get estimated time of arrival. Also verify fuel remaining is sufficient for the divert plus a reserve. If the direct route crosses special use airspace such as restricted areas, note that while 14 CFR 91.3 grants PIC authority to deviate from regulations in emergencies, entry into active restricted airspace still requires immediate coordination with ATC or the controlling agency. Emergency authority does not grant automatic entry rights if military operations are active. If authorization cannot be obtained, plan an alternate route that avoids the restricted area. Only as a last-resort safety measure should entry be considered, and the situation must be documented for post-flight incident reporting.
AI_EDIT	PH.VII.B.K1.004	Not necessarily. At the MOCA, obstacle clearance is guaranteed, but VOR navigation signal reception is only guaranteed along the entire airway segment when flying at the MEA. The MOCA provides obstacle clearance but only assures acceptable navigation signal coverage within approximately 22 NM of the NAVAID as a general standard — actual reception can vary depending on terrain, facility type, and altitude, so this should not be treated as an absolute guarantee in all conditions. At 18 NM you may well have usable signal, but you cannot assume it. Flying at the MEA guarantees both obstacle clearance and navigation signal reception for the full segment. As a practical matter, especially in mountainous terrain where a helicopter might be operating near MEA/MOCA altitudes, you should verify CDI needle movement and station identification continuously. If signal appears marginal or unreliable, climb to the MEA or transition to GPS if available and authorized.	->	Not necessarily guaranteed, but likely reliable. At the MOCA, obstacle clearance is guaranteed, and VOR navigation signal reception is guaranteed within the reception limits published for that NAVAID—typically 25 NM at standard altitude for Class B VOR facilities. At 18 NM from the station, you should have reliable signal coverage under normal conditions. However, reception can be affected by terrain shielding, antenna orientation, and low-altitude propagation effects. Flying at the MEA guarantees both obstacle clearance and full navigation signal coverage along the entire airway segment. As a practical matter, especially in mountainous terrain where a helicopter might be operating near MEA/MOCA altitudes, you should verify CDI needle movement and station identification continuously. If signal appears marginal or unreliable, climb to the MEA or transition to GPS if available and authorized.
AI_EDIT	PH.VII.B.S3.004	First, set 360° in the OBS. A radial is a magnetic bearing FROM the station, so the 360 radial extends northward from the station. Since the station is 20 miles to your west and you are flying eastbound at 090°, you are positioned south of the 360 radial — that radial runs due north from the station, and you are south of the station's latitude, meaning the radial is ahead and to your northwest. To intercept it, you need a heading that angles you toward it. A 45° intercept angle is standard; turning left from 090° by 45° gives you a 045° heading, but since you need to fly northwest toward the radial, you would turn left to approximately 315° — this is 45° off the radial course of 360°, giving a proper intercept angle. As the CDI begins to move toward center, turn right to 360° to track the radial outbound northbound, applying wind correction as needed. Monitor CDI movement rate carefully — at low altitude or close range to the station, needle deflection can change rapidly.	->	First, set 360° in the OBS. A radial is a magnetic bearing FROM the station, so the 360 radial extends northward from the station. Since the station is 20 miles to your west and you are flying eastbound at 090°, you are positioned south of the 360 radial. To intercept it, apply a standard 45° intercept angle: turn left from your current heading of 090° to an intercept heading of 045°. This positions you to approach the radial from the southeast at a proper intercept angle. Monitor the CDI; as the needle begins to center and move toward the right edge (indicating you are approaching the radial), turn right to a heading of 360° to track outbound on the radial, applying wind correction as needed. At low altitude or close range to the station, CDI needle deflection can change rapidly, so monitor carefully and maintain smooth control inputs during the intercept and track.
AI_EDIT	PH.IX.D.S3.008	Aviate first: maintain controlled flight and a known heading. Squawk 7700 and confirm Mode C is active so any radar facility that does have coverage can read your altitude and identity without voice contact.

Regarding altitude: continue climbing as practical for your helicopter's performance and airspace. Greater altitude improves radio reception range and may improve the likelihood of radar contact, but do not assume any specific altitude guarantees radar coverage — ARTCC and TRACON radar coverage varies significantly by facility, location, and terrain, and is not guaranteed at any given altitude over flat terrain.

Regarding the GPS discrepancy: a moving-map inconsistency can have two fundamentally different causes, and the distinction matters. First, it may be a database or display issue — the underlying GPS coordinate could be accurate while the map overlay is wrong due to an outdated database, incorrect map scale, or display anomaly. Second, the GPS position itself could be in error due to signal degradation, insufficient satellite geometry, or receiver fault. Cross-checking the GPS ground track against your heading and wind is useful but only confirms internal consistency — it cannot independently validate whether the GPS coordinate reflects your true position. If your receiver displays satellite count or RAIM status, check those; degraded RAIM or a low satellite count reduces position confidence. Because you cannot confirm which cause is responsible, treat the GPS position as advisory only, not as confirmed navigation. Do not navigate solely by GPS until the discrepancy is explained.

For heading: with GPS unreliable, maintain your last known purposeful heading. If you have any external reference — a road, river, railroad, or prominent landmark — use it to orient yourself consistent with your sectional chart. Continue attempting 121.5 at intervals. If you reach a point where fuel or safety requires a decision under 14 CFR 91.3(b), you may declare an emergency and take whatever action is necessary, including diverting to any suitable landing area in sight.	->	Aviate first: maintain controlled flight and a known heading. Squawk 7700 and confirm Mode C is active to enable radar contact without voice. Continue climbing as practical to improve radio reception range, but recognize radar coverage is not guaranteed at any altitude in flat terrain. Regarding the GPS discrepancy: distinguish between a display/database error and a position error by checking satellite count and RAIM status if available. Treat GPS as advisory only until validated. Maintain your last known heading and use external references (roads, landmarks) to orient yourself with your sectional chart. Continue attempting 121.5 at intervals. If fuel or safety requires a decision, declare an emergency and divert to suitable landing area under 14 CFR 91.3(b).
