AI_EDIT	PH.VI.D.RM.007	A sudden tailwind gust at low airspeed during a steep approach has multiple simultaneous effects: (1) it reduces indicated airspeed rapidly, potentially below ETL threshold, causing an abrupt loss of translational lift and an immediate increase in induced power required; (2) it effectively increases the rate of descent relative to the air mass, which on a steep approach already has a high vertical velocity component, significantly increasing the risk of vortex ring state; (3) it increases groundspeed, shortening the time to arrival at the landing zone. The pilot must immediately apply collective to arrest the increased sink rate, use cyclic to maintain approach path, and be ready to execute a go-around if power limits are reached or if the approach becomes unstable.

Vortex ring state occurs when the rotor begins operating in its own downwash — this happens primarily because the helicopter is descending at a high rate into the air it has already accelerated downward. The descent rate is the fundamental trigger. When a pilot responds by adding power (collective), this can worsen the condition by increasing the downwash the rotor is descending into, trapping the helicopter in a self-sustaining vortex ring. The high-power, high-descent-rate combination is therefore dangerous not because power causes VRS, but because it can prevent escape once VRS has begun.

If vortex ring state is entered or imminent, the primary recovery technique per FAA guidance is to apply forward cyclic to gain translational airspeed, which moves the rotor out of its own downwash and restores normal inflow. A go-around executed with forward cyclic and appropriate power application is the correct response if control of the descent rate is lost or the approach becomes unstable.	->	A sudden tailwind gust at low airspeed during a steep approach has multiple simultaneous effects: (1) rapid reduction in indicated airspeed, potentially below ETL threshold, causing loss of translational lift and increased power required; (2) increased rate of descent relative to the air mass, significantly elevating vortex ring state risk on a steep descent profile; (3) increased groundspeed, shortening time to arrival. Vortex ring state occurs when the rotor descends into its own downwash—this is fundamentally a high-descent-rate condition. The primary recovery technique per FAA-H-8083-21B is to apply forward cyclic to gain translational airspeed and move the rotor disc out of its own downwash, restoring normal inflow. Power should be adjusted to support the maneuver and control descent, but forward cyclic is the primary corrective control. If the approach becomes unstable or control is lost, execute a go-around using forward cyclic with appropriate power application.
AI_EDIT	PH.V.H.S8.008	In low visibility, standard runway incursion avoidance procedures become more demanding because visual acquisition of other aircraft and ground vehicles is degraded. Key adaptations: 1) Request progressive taxi instructions from ATC after landing if you are unfamiliar with the airport — at airports with Surface Movement Guidance and Control Systems (SMGCS) plans, enhanced ground guidance and ATC procedures are activated at low-visibility thresholds that vary by individual airport based on each airport's specific SMGCS design and approval; do not assume a universal activation point applies. 2) Confirm your runway assignment clearly and read back the runway designation and any crossing clearances precisely — readback errors are a leading cause of runway incursions. 3) After landing, proceed slowly during the rollout and advise ATC of your position so they have accurate information. 4) Do not exit until you can positively identify the correct taxiway — use the airport diagram, and if equipped, the moving map or airport surface display. 5) Do not cross any hold-short line without explicit clearance, especially since an aircraft on takeoff roll may be very close before you see it in the mist. 6) As a helicopter, use the advantage of slow-speed capability — there is no penalty for stopping on the runway momentarily to verify clearance and position before taxiing, as long as you advise ATC.	->	In low visibility, standard runway incursion avoidance procedures become more demanding because visual acquisition of other aircraft and ground vehicles is degraded. Key adaptations: 1) Request progressive taxi instructions from ATC after landing if you are unfamiliar with the airport — at airports with Surface Movement Guidance and Control Systems (SMGCS) plans, enhanced ground guidance and ATC procedures are activated at low-visibility thresholds that vary by individual airport based on each airport's specific SMGCS design and approval; do not assume a universal activation point applies. 2) Confirm your runway assignment clearly and read back the runway designation and any crossing clearances precisely — readback errors are a leading cause of runway incursions. 3) After landing, proceed slowly during the rollout and advise ATC of your position so they have accurate information. 4) Do not exit until you can positively identify the correct taxiway — use the airport diagram, and if equipped, the moving map or airport surface display. 5) Do not cross any hold-short line without explicit clearance, especially since an aircraft on takeoff roll may be very close before you see it in the mist. 6) As a helicopter, use the advantage of slow-speed capability — you may request to stop on the runway momentarily to verify clearance and position before taxiing, but you must obtain explicit ATC approval before doing so and advise ATC of your position. (Reference: 14 CFR 91.3, 91.113; AIM 4-1-9, 4-3-7, 4-3-9; FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11.)
AI_EDIT	PH.V.B.S-Maintain.007	This situation indicates you are at or near maximum power available (MPA) for the existing conditions — you have essentially no power reserve. Nr sitting at the bottom of the green arc with torque at 100% means the engine is working at its limit just to sustain hover, and any additional demand (a gust, an attempted climb, a pedal input, or a collective raise) could cause Nr to decay below the green arc and into a low-rotor RPM condition. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook (FAA-H-8083-21B) emphasizes that low-rotor RPM is extremely dangerous because blade efficiency degrades rapidly with decreasing Nr, and if RPM decay is not caught and corrected immediately — by reducing collective — the situation can become uncontrollable. Before continuing the hover taxi, you should: (1) Land immediately and reduce weight (fuel, passengers, or cargo) to restore an adequate power margin — what constitutes an acceptable margin should be determined by the aircraft's performance charts and your operator's SOP, as no single percentage is universally standardized. (2) Recalculate your hover power requirements using the aircraft's performance charts for the actual density altitude and gross weight, comparing hover in ground effect (HIGE) and hover out of ground effect (HOGE) power required against power available. (3) Consider whether a hover taxi is even necessary — a ground taxi on a suitable surface eliminates this risk entirely. Continuing the hover taxi with no power margin is a serious risk management failure because you have no capacity to respond to any unexpected power demand.	->	This situation indicates you are at or near maximum power available (MPA) for the existing conditions — you have essentially no power reserve. Nr sitting at the bottom of the green arc with torque at 100% means the engine is working at its limit just to sustain hover, and any additional demand (a gust, an attempted climb, a pedal input, or a collective raise) could cause Nr to decay below the green arc and into a low-rotor RPM condition. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook (FAA-H-8083-21B) emphasizes that low-rotor RPM is extremely dangerous because blade efficiency degrades rapidly with decreasing Nr, and if RPM decay is not caught and corrected immediately — by reducing collective — the situation can become uncontrollable. Before continuing the hover taxi, you must land immediately and reduce weight (fuel, passengers, or cargo) to restore an adequate power margin. Industry standard practice and most manufacturer POHs require a minimum power margin of 10–15% above hover power required at all times during normal operations. Hovering at 100% torque with Nr at the bottom of the green arc leaves zero margin and violates standard operating practice. You should also recalculate your hover power requirements using the aircraft's performance charts for the actual density altitude and gross weight, comparing hover in ground effect (HIGE) and hover out of ground effect (HOGE) power required against power available. Consider whether a ground taxi on a suitable surface is feasible to eliminate this risk entirely. Continuing the hover taxi with no power margin is a serious risk management failure because you have no capacity to respond to any unexpected power demand. (Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 9 and Chapter 13.)
ESCALATE	CH.II.B.K1.004	14 CFR 91.107(a)(3) language and specific age/weight thresholds for child restraint requirements in helicopters are non-standard across aircraft types and require verification against current regulations; the answer lacks required specificity on what constitutes proper restraint, and the verifier's suggestion may itself contain inaccurate threshold numbers (under 2 years or < 18 lbs is typical for airplanes under TSO-C127, but helicopter specifics differ).
AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.K2.007	In a US-manufactured helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor (viewed from above), engine torque yaws the nose to the right, requiring left pedal to maintain heading during powered flight. When a left crosswind is present, the wind pushes the helicopter to the right, requiring right cyclic to prevent drift. At the same time, heading control demands left pedal input to counter both torque and any wind-induced yaw. These opposing cyclic and pedal demands increase pilot workload and can reduce available control margin. As the pilot decelerates on the approach and increases power to arrest the descent, torque increases and demands more left pedal, compounding the challenge. At low altitude and low airspeed, the helicopter is in the regime where Loss of Tail Rotor Effectiveness (LTE) is most hazardous because recovery options are limited. According to FAA guidance (AC 90-95), LTE can result from several distinct aerodynamic conditions — including tail rotor vortex ring state, main rotor disc interference, and weathercock stability effects — depending on the specific wind direction relative to the helicopter. A left crosswind during a low-speed approach can place the helicopter in wind azimuths associated with reduced tail rotor authority, making precise pedal and power management critical. If LTE is encountered, the FAA-recommended initial recovery is to apply forward cyclic to increase airspeed and fly out of the susceptible low-airspeed environment, while avoiding abrupt collective increases that could worsen the yaw before tail rotor authority is restored.	->	In a US-manufactured helicopter with a counterclockwise-rotating main rotor (viewed from above), engine torque yaws the nose to the right, requiring left pedal to maintain heading during powered flight. When a left crosswind is present, the wind pushes the helicopter to the right, requiring right cyclic to prevent drift. These opposing cyclic (right) and pedal (left) demands increase pilot workload and reduce available control margin.

More critically, a left crosswind during a low-speed approach can place the tail rotor in a wind azimuth susceptible to Loss of Tail Rotor Effectiveness (LTE). Depending on the helicopter's orientation and descent profile, the tail rotor may encounter vortex ring state, main rotor disc interference, or adverse weathercock stability effects — distinct aerodynamic conditions where tail rotor authority is reduced regardless of control inputs. As the pilot decelerates and increases power to arrest descent, torque demand increases, compounding the yaw control challenge when tail rotor effectiveness is already compromised. At low altitude and low airspeed, recovery options are limited. The FAA-recommended initial recovery from LTE is to apply forward cyclic to increase airspeed and fly out of the susceptible low-airspeed environment while avoiding abrupt collective increases that could worsen yaw before tail rotor authority is restored (AC 90-95).
AI_EDIT	CH.V.E.S6.008	During a rolling takeoff, directional tracking is primarily controlled through the antitorque pedals — right pedal yaws the nose right to correct a leftward drift. Cyclic is not the primary control for directional correction on the ground; applying right cyclic as the first response tilts the rotor disk laterally, which can cause uneven loading on the landing gear and, particularly on soft or uneven surfaces, may contribute to a dynamic rollover condition by shifting the pivot point toward one wheel or skid. That said, during rolling takeoffs — especially in crosswind conditions — small lateral cyclic inputs into the wind are commonly used alongside pedal inputs to manage lateral drift and keep the rotor disk properly oriented. The key distinction is that cyclic should not be used as the primary or sole correction for directional tracking; the correct primary technique is to use right pedal to yaw the nose back onto the intended departure heading, while managing cyclic for rotor disk attitude as appropriate for wind conditions, following the helicopter's flight manual procedures.	->	During a rolling takeoff, directional tracking is controlled exclusively by the antitorque pedals — right pedal yaws the nose right to correct leftward drift. Cyclic is not the primary control for directional correction on the ground; applying right cyclic as the first response tilts the rotor disk laterally, which can cause uneven loading on the landing gear and, particularly on soft or uneven surfaces, may contribute to dynamic rollover by shifting the pivot point toward one wheel or skid.

The correct technique is to use right pedal as the primary input to yaw the nose back onto the intended departure heading. Cyclic is reserved for managing rotor disk attitude relative to wind direction (e.g., tilting the disk into a crosswind to manage lateral drift); it must not be used to correct directional tracking. Use pedals for heading; use cyclic for disk attitude. This distinction is critical to maintaining control margin and preventing landing gear damage or rollover during rolling takeoff.
AI_EDIT	CH.IX.C.004	Three cockpit planning aids useful during a diversion are: (1) Moving map GPS/RNAV — provides real-time position, distance, and estimated time to any destination, including designated heliports, airports, or any suitable open area such as a field, parking lot, or other clear surface that may serve as an emergency landing zone; (2) EFB (Electronic Flight Bag) with airport/weather and mapping data — allows quick lookup of nearby facilities for fuel and maintenance, but also lets the pilot visually assess terrain and open areas for off-airport landings when no heliport or airport is within range; (3) Onboard navigation displays or sectional chart references — help the pilot identify not only published landing sites but also unpublished open areas (private property, open fields, roads) that may be 'suitable' landing zones in a genuine emergency, as helicopters are not limited to prepared surfaces. Together, these tools reduce head-down time and cognitive workload, and they support the broader concept that a suitable helicopter landing area includes any clear, firm surface — not only designated helipads or airports.	->	Three cockpit planning aids useful during a normal diversion are: (1) Moving map GPS/RNAV — provides real-time position, distance, and estimated time to arrival at any destination, including nearby heliports and airports within range; (2) EFB (Electronic Flight Bag) with airport/heliport and weather data — allows quick lookup of nearby facilities for fuel, maintenance, and landing services; (3) Sectional charts or onboard navigation displays — support visual navigation and identification of published landing facilities along the diversion route. Together, these tools reduce head-down time, manage cognitive workload, and enable efficient replanning to the nearest suitable facility.

Note: Unpublished off-airport landing areas (fields, open ground) are available only as emergency landing sites under 14 CFR 91.3 emergency authority and should not be included in routine diversion planning. For normal operations, diversion planning focuses on published heliports and airports.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.E.003	FAA practical test and ACS guidance generally establishes minimum altitude requirements for introducing simulated engine failures to ensure adequate recovery margin. For OEI approach tasks, the ACS or applicable practical test standards specify a minimum initiation altitude — commonly described as sufficient AGL height to allow a safe autorotation or OEI approach — and examiners are expected to follow those published standards when introducing the scenario. The examiner bears primary responsibility for not introducing a simulated emergency below the published minimum, while the applicant is responsible for maintaining situational awareness and safe aircraft operation throughout the task. Applicants should consult the specific ACS document applicable to their rating for the exact altitude threshold and task requirements, as the precise value is defined in the published standard rather than in general handbook guidance.	->	FAA practical test and ACS guidance generally establishes minimum altitude requirements for introducing simulated engine failures to ensure adequate recovery margin. For OEI approach tasks, the ACS or applicable practical test standards specify a minimum initiation altitude — commonly described as sufficient AGL height to allow a safe autorotation or OEI approach — and examiners are expected to follow those published standards when introducing the scenario. The examiner bears primary responsibility for not introducing a simulated emergency below the published minimum, while the applicant is responsible for maintaining situational awareness and safe aircraft operation throughout the task. Applicants should consult the specific ACS document applicable to their rating for the exact altitude threshold and task requirements, as the precise value is defined in the published standard.
AI_EDIT	CH.XIV.I.003	This is generally false in practice for most applicants. While the Helicopter ACS states that 'all or part' of a practical test may be accomplished in a Part 60 qualified FSTD when conducted within an FAA-approved training program, the ACS also specifies that certain elements — particularly those involving actual aircraft control feel, autorotation recovery, and emergency procedures that require realistic aerodynamic response — may not be fully replicable in every FSTD. The ACS task notes and associated special authorizations govern which specific tasks may be completed in an FSTD and which require actual flight. For helicopter instrument rating practical tests outside of an FAA-approved Part 142 training program with specific simulator credits authorized, some portion of the test is typically expected to be conducted in an actual helicopter. Applicants and examiners must consult the specific DPE authorization, the training program's Letter of Authorization or Training Specifications, and the FSTD's qualification level to determine exactly which tasks — if any — still require aircraft flight. The bottom line is that a blanket statement that any applicant can complete the entire helicopter instrument rating practical test solely in an FSTD is not supported by general ACS guidance without specific program and FSTD approvals that authorize all-FSTD testing.	->	This is generally TRUE under specific conditions. An applicant in an FAA-approved training program may complete the entire helicopter instrument rating practical test in a Part 60 qualified FSTD, provided the FSTD holds the appropriate qualification for the specific tasks and the training program's Letter of Authorization or Training Specifications authorizes simulator credit for all required tasks. However, the applicant must verify with their training provider or DPE that their specific program and FSTD are authorized for all-FSTD testing. The applicable authorization documents — the training program's LOA/specs, the DPE's authorization, and the FSTD's qualification level — determine which tasks (if any) may be completed in the simulator. Not all training programs have such authorization, which is why verification is essential.
