AI_EDIT	CH.V.B.S10.007	Immediately upon recognizing the incursion, I stop the helicopter as quickly as safely possible and contact tower to report my position: 'Tower, [callsign], I have inadvertently crossed onto Runway 27 without clearance — please advise.' I follow all ATC instructions immediately. Once clear of the runway and on the ground safely, I should consider whether the engine issue requires maintenance before further flight. Regarding reporting obligations: 14 CFR 91.25 prohibits interference with air traffic control, and the FAA may investigate a runway incursion as a potential violation. Whether this specific event rises to the level of a reportable 'incident' under NTSB 830 depends on the circumstances. Regardless of any mandatory reporting obligation, filing a NASA Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS) report is strongly recommended. The ASRS program provides a degree of protection from FAA certificate action for inadvertent violations; however, the exact conditions and limitations of that protection are defined by NASA ASRS program guidance and FAA Order 8000.88, and pilots should consult current program details. The ASRS filing demonstrates good faith and contributes to the safety database. The key takeaway is that prompt, honest communication with ATC followed by an ASRS report is the appropriate response to an inadvertent runway incursion.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	Power.003	In a constant-airspeed descent, the airspeed indicator is primary for pitch — the pilot adjusts pitch attitude to hold the desired airspeed throughout the descent. The VSI serves as a supporting instrument, confirming the resulting rate of descent. Power is reduced from the straight-and-level setting to initiate the descent; once the target airspeed and descent rate are established, power is adjusted as needed to maintain the desired airspeed. The power instrument (manifold pressure or torque gauge, depending on aircraft type) is used to set and monitor power, but the ASI remains the controlling reference for both pitch and power corrections during a constant-airspeed descent.	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	CH.X.A.RM.007	Rotor system design influences how quickly rotor RPM decays following an engine failure and how much kinetic energy is stored for cushioning the landing. However, rotor inertia is not determined solely by rotor type classification — it is a function of blade mass, blade length, and the distribution of that mass relative to the rotor axis. An articulated system does not inherently have higher inertia than a semirigid or rigid system; what matters is the specific blade design and total blade mass relative to the aircraft's weight. Some semirigid (teetering) systems, such as those on certain light helicopters, are specifically designed with lower blade mass, which results in lower rotor inertia and faster RPM decay after power loss — a characteristic the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook explicitly highlights for those aircraft types. Pilots must know the specific inertia characteristics of the aircraft they fly, not assume inertia based on rotor head type alone. Pre-mission risk planning should account for: the specific aircraft's hover power required versus power available at the planned density altitude, the height-velocity diagram for that model at the planned gross weight, and the pilot's reaction proficiency in that specific aircraft type. Lower time in type increases risk during time-critical emergencies such as a hover engine failure, because correct, timely collective reduction is essential to preserve rotor RPM regardless of the rotor system design.	->	A left roll is generally harder to arrest with cyclic for a counterclockwise rotor system. The primary reason is that the retreating blade is on the left side of a counterclockwise rotor system, and the retreating blade inherently produces less lift due to reduced airspeed relative to the advancing blade. During a developing left roll, this asymmetry makes the left side more susceptible to further lift loss and rollover development. Additionally, in hover, the pilot must already hold left cyclic to compensate for translating tendency (tail rotor thrust pushes the helicopter right), reducing the available cyclic travel for left roll correction. These factors combine to make the left side the more critical direction for dynamic rollover risk in a counterclockwise rotor system, and FAA-H-8083-21B emphasizes that pilots must apply immediate, aggressive cyclic opposite to the roll direction and reduce collective if rollover appears imminent.
AI_EDIT	CH.X.A.RM.007	A left roll is generally harder to arrest with cyclic for a counterclockwise rotor system. The primary reason is translating tendency: tail rotor thrust (in a counterclockwise rotor system) pushes the helicopter to the right, so the pilot must already hold left cyclic to maintain a level hover over a level surface. This pre-positioned cyclic input reduces the remaining cyclic travel available to arrest a leftward roll, meaning the pilot has less corrective authority in that direction when it is needed most. Additionally, the retreating blade is on the left side of a counterclockwise rotor system, and that side inherently produces less lift, which can make a leftward roll develop more readily. These factors combine to make the left side the more critical direction for dynamic rollover in a counterclockwise rotor system — the effective rollover angle threshold is functionally smaller to the left — and FAA-H-8083-21B emphasizes that pilots must apply immediate, aggressive cyclic opposite to the roll direction and reduce collective if rollover appears imminent.	->	A left roll is generally harder to arrest with cyclic for a counterclockwise rotor system. The primary reason is that the retreating blade is on the left side of a counterclockwise rotor system, and the retreating blade inherently produces less lift due to reduced airspeed relative to the advancing blade. During a developing left roll, this asymmetry makes the left side more susceptible to further lift loss and rollover development. Additionally, in hover, the pilot must already hold left cyclic to compensate for translating tendency (tail rotor thrust pushes the helicopter right), reducing the available cyclic travel for left roll correction. These factors combine to make the left side the more critical direction for dynamic rollover risk in a counterclockwise rotor system, and FAA-H-8083-21B emphasizes that pilots must apply immediate, aggressive cyclic opposite to the roll direction and reduce collective if rollover appears imminent.
AI_EDIT	CH.X.A.Establish.005	Weathervaning is the tendency of the helicopter's nose to align with the relative wind because the fuselage acts like a weather vane — the larger surface area of the tail section generates more aerodynamic drag, causing the nose to swing into the wind. This is a wind-driven directional behavior and is distinct from torque-induced yaw. On the ground or at very low altitude, as the helicopter lifts off and the skids lose contact with the surface, the fuselage becomes free to rotate, and a crosswind can cause the nose to swing unexpectedly toward the wind. If the wind is directly ahead, weathervaning can actually assist alignment; however, if the aircraft is not precisely aligned or the wind is gusty, the nose may overrotate past the desired heading, making it difficult to establish a stable into-wind hover. Technique: before lifting, visually identify a reference point on the horizon aligned with the wind direction to use as a heading cue. As collective is increased and the skids begin to lighten, anticipate the weathervaning tendency and use antitorque pedals to control the rate and direction of yaw — independently of torque compensation — to moderate the nose swing and stop the aircraft on the desired heading. Torque compensation pedal input (for a counterclockwise rotor system, right pedal) remains necessary to counteract rotor torque, but pedal inputs to manage weathervaning are made in response to the wind-driven yaw, not torque reaction. Combining these two separate pedal demands requires smooth coordination.	->	Weathervaning is the tendency of the helicopter's nose to align with the relative wind because the fuselage acts like a weather vane — the larger surface area of the tail section generates more aerodynamic drag, causing the nose to swing into the wind. This is a wind-driven directional behavior and is distinct from torque-induced yaw. On the ground or at very low altitude, as the helicopter lifts off and the skids lose contact with the surface, the fuselage becomes free to rotate, and a crosswind can cause the nose to swing unexpectedly toward the wind. If the wind is directly ahead, weathervaning can actually assist alignment; however, if the aircraft is not precisely aligned or the wind is gusty, the nose may overrotate past the desired heading, making it difficult to establish a stable into-wind hover. Technique: before lifting, visually identify a reference point on the horizon aligned with the wind direction to use as a heading cue. As collective is increased and the skids begin to lighten, anticipate the weathervaning tendency and use antitorque pedals to control the rate and direction of yaw — independently of torque compensation — to moderate the nose swing and stop the aircraft on the desired heading. Torque compensation pedal input (for a counterclockwise rotor system, right pedal) remains necessary to counteract rotor torque, but pedal inputs to manage weathervaning are made in response to the wind-driven yaw, not torque reaction. Combining these two separate pedal demands requires smooth coordination. (See FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 2.)
AI_EDIT	CH.X.B.Effect.008	A significant headwind at altitude that diminishes to calm at the surface creates a challenging and deceptive environment. At altitude with a 25-knot headwind, if you are indicating 65 knots airspeed your groundspeed is only 40 knots — you are covering less ground per foot of altitude lost, so your actual glide distance to the ground is shorter than it appears. As you descend through the wind gradient, the headwind component reduces. To maintain the same indicated airspeed the pilot must push the cyclic forward to increase groundspeed, which also changes the rotor's inflow conditions. If the pilot does not actively maintain indicated airspeed with forward cyclic, the nose-low attitude relative to the air mass changes and the rotor's angle of attack relationships shift — this can cause rotor RPM to decay because the balance between the driving and driven regions of the rotor disk is disrupted. The practical risk is that a pilot who fails to recognize and compensate for the diminishing headwind may allow indicated airspeed to bleed off near the flare point, at which time RPM decay is particularly dangerous and there is little altitude remaining to recover. Additionally, the apparent glide path angle will steepen visually as groundspeed drops, potentially causing the pilot to overshoot a previously well-planned landing zone. Pilots must anticipate the airspeed loss through the gradient, actively manage cyclic to maintain proper indicated airspeed, be ready to manage RPM proactively, and plan a landing zone with a buffer for the steepening apparent descent angle.	->	A significant headwind at altitude that diminishes to calm at the surface creates a challenging and deceptive environment. At altitude with a 25-knot headwind, if you are indicating 65 knots airspeed your groundspeed is only 40 knots — you are covering less ground per foot of altitude lost, so your actual glide distance to the ground is shorter than it appears. As you descend through the wind gradient, the headwind component reduces. To maintain the same indicated airspeed as the headwind decreases, the pilot must increase nose-up pitch attitude (pull back on cyclic), which changes the rotor's inflow angle. However, the primary risk of RPM decay stems from failure to maintain proper autorotation descent rate and airspeed as the wind gradient is encountered — if the pilot does not actively compensate for the diminishing headwind by adjusting pitch and descent rate, indicated airspeed will bleed off, breaking the power-off descent profile and degrading rotor RPM. The practical risk is that a pilot who fails to recognize and compensate for the diminishing headwind may allow indicated airspeed to decay near the flare point, at which time RPM recovery is difficult and there is little altitude remaining to recover. Additionally, the apparent glide path angle will steepen visually as groundspeed drops, potentially causing the pilot to overshoot a previously well-planned landing zone. Pilots must anticipate the airspeed change through the gradient, actively manage pitch attitude to maintain proper indicated airspeed and descent rate, monitor rotor RPM proactively, and plan a landing zone with a buffer for the steepening apparent descent angle. Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11 (Autorotation).
AI_EDIT	CH.X.B.SK007	This mindset is a cognitive shortcut that bypasses the systematic evaluation required for safe autorotation landings. 'Biggest field' does not account for: (1) Slope — even a large flat-looking field can have a grade that causes dynamic rollover on landing; FAA-H-8083-21B discusses slope hazards and notes that even moderate slopes present significant risk, particularly because landing upslope requires caution regarding tail rotor clearance from the rising terrain — the tail rotor's proximity to the ground is the primary geometric constraint on upslope landings, not just general rotor strike risk; (2) Surface condition — large fields may have irrigation ditches, furrows, boulders, or soft soil hidden from altitude; (3) Approach path obstructions — a large field surrounded by tall trees or wires on the upwind end is more dangerous than a smaller but obstruction-free surface; (4) Wind — landing downwind in a large field increases groundspeed significantly, elevating rollover risk. The correct approach is to evaluate all factors simultaneously: altitude, wind, terrain, and obstructions. The 'best' field is the one that, considering all factors, gives the highest probability of a controlled, survivable landing. Situational awareness must begin before the emergency — pilots should continuously scan terrain ahead and identify potential landing zones before they need them.	->	This mindset is a cognitive shortcut that bypasses the systematic evaluation required for safe autorotation landings. 'Biggest field' does not account for: (1) Slope — even a large flat-looking field can have a grade that causes dynamic rollover on landing; FAA-H-8083-21B discusses slope hazards and notes that dynamic rollover risk, particularly on downslope approaches, is the primary limiting factor for landing area selection, while upslope landings require careful attention to tail rotor clearance from rising terrain; (2) Surface condition — large fields may have irrigation ditches, furrows, boulders, or soft soil hidden from altitude; (3) Approach path obstructions — a large field surrounded by tall trees or wires on the upwind end is more dangerous than a smaller but obstruction-free surface; (4) Wind — landing downwind in a large field increases groundspeed significantly, elevating rollover risk. The correct approach is to evaluate all factors simultaneously: altitude, wind, terrain, and obstructions. The 'best' field is the one that, considering all factors, gives the highest probability of a controlled, survivable landing. Situational awareness must begin before the emergency — pilots should continuously scan terrain ahead and identify potential landing zones before they need them. Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13 (Emergency Procedures).
ESCALATE	Loss.002	The three LTE mechanisms are directionally correct in concept, but the specific wind azimuth ranges and precise FAA terminology in AC 90-95 should be verified before shipping; weathercock stability is a helicopter characteristic, not a wind phenomenon, and needs clarification.
AI_EDIT	CH.X.H.Preventive.007	The reasoning is dangerous because cyclic authority is highly limited when a pivot point exists. With one skid on the ground, the rotor disk can tilt, but the fuselage rotates around the fixed contact point — not around its center of gravity as in free flight. This means the effective lever arm for cyclic input is compromised and cyclic alone cannot generate enough counter-moment to overcome the rolling tendency once it is developing. Aggressive cyclic may also cause blade-to-fuselage clearance issues at low rotor heights during the roll. The primary recovery control is the collective, lowered immediately to reduce thrust and eliminate the rolling moment. However — cyclic becomes the primary control in one specific scenario: if the helicopter has already lifted off and dynamic rollover is developing in free flight with no pivot point contact, then the normal unusual attitude recovery applies and cyclic is appropriate. The distinction is entirely dependent on whether a physical pivot point exists. If a skid remains on the ground, collective reduction is the priority response. If fully airborne, cyclic is used to stop the roll. Dynamic rollover is a distinct hazard from ground resonance; while both involve skid contact, ground resonance is caused by blade lead-lag oscillations coupling with the helicopter's landing gear frequency and is a separate phenomenon that requires its own recognition and corrective actions.	->	The reasoning is dangerous because dynamic rollover occurs when a pivot point (a grounded skid or wheel) is established. With one skid on the ground, the rotor disk can tilt, but the fuselage rotates around the fixed contact point — not around its center of gravity as in free flight. This means the effective lever arm for cyclic input is severely compromised; cyclic alone cannot generate enough counter-moment to overcome the rolling tendency. Aggressive cyclic may also cause blade-to-fuselage clearance issues at low rotor heights. The primary recovery control is collective reduction to eliminate rotor thrust and the rolling moment. However, if the helicopter is fully airborne with no pivot point contact, an accelerating roll is an unusual attitude (not dynamic rollover), and standard unusual attitude recovery applies with cyclic as the appropriate control. The critical distinction: dynamic rollover is exclusively a ground-contact phenomenon. Once fully airborne, a rolling tendency is managed via cyclic. Per FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11, if a skid remains on the ground, collective reduction is the priority response. Ground resonance, caused by blade lead-lag oscillations coupling with landing gear frequency, is a separate phenomenon requiring its own corrective actions.
AI_EDIT	CH.X.H.RM.Sideward.008	Dynamic rollover susceptibility is influenced by the relationship between the center of gravity, the pivot point (a grounded skid or wheel), and the rotor thrust vector. High-skid and wheeled helicopters typically have a higher CG above the pivot point compared to low-skid designs. According to the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook (FAA-H-8083-21B), once a rolling moment begins and the rotor thrust vector tilts past the critical angle — where it acts through or beyond the pivot point rather than back toward center — rollover becomes self-sustaining and accelerates rapidly. The specific critical angle varies by aircraft design and is found in the applicable POH or flight manual; the FAA handbooks do not establish a universal critical angle for high-skid versus low-skid types or characterize one as more 'forgiving' than the other. What the handbook does emphasize is that a higher CG increases the moment arm available to drive an accelerating roll once the critical angle is exceeded, making high-skid aircraft particularly vulnerable to rapid rollover progression. For risk management in either configuration, the strategy is the same: minimize lateral velocity at ground contact, avoid applying upward collective after a skid contacts the surface during sideward hover, and treat any rolling tendency as requiring immediate collective reduction to remove rotor thrust — not cyclic correction, which is ineffective once a pivot has been established.	->	Dynamic rollover susceptibility is influenced by the relationship between center of gravity, the pivot point (a grounded skid or wheel), and the rotor thrust vector. Per FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11, once a rolling moment begins and the rotor thrust vector tilts past the critical angle — where it acts through or beyond the pivot point rather than back toward the CG — rollover becomes self-sustaining and accelerates rapidly. A higher CG above the pivot point (characteristic of high-skid aircraft) increases the moment arm available to drive an accelerating roll, making these helicopters particularly susceptible to rapid rollover progression. The specific critical angle varies by aircraft design and is found in the aircraft POH; consult the flight manual preflight. For risk management in either configuration, the strategy is identical: minimize lateral velocity at ground contact, avoid applying upward collective after a skid contacts the surface during sideward hover, and treat any rolling tendency as requiring immediate collective reduction to remove rotor thrust. Cyclic correction is ineffective once a pivot point is established. Dynamic rollover risk is primarily a function of control technique and situational awareness; proper technique mitigates type differences.
AI_EDIT	CH.X.K.008	Regarding legality, 14 CFR 91.207 governs ELT requirements for U.S.-registered civil aircraft. The regulation specifies ELT requirements but does not explicitly prohibit continued use of a 121.5 MHz ELT that was already installed — however, the pilot should verify the specific text of 91.207 and any applicable AD notes to confirm current compliance status for their aircraft, because the regulatory landscape around ELTs has evolved. A thorough preflight planning step would be to consult with an A&P or the FAA to confirm no retrofit mandate applies to their specific aircraft category and operation. Regardless of the legal question, the survival implications are severe: as of February 1, 2009, COSPAS-SARSAT satellites no longer monitor 121.5 MHz. A 121.5 MHz ELT activation will only be detected if a manned aircraft or ATC ground station with a receiver happens to be within line-of-sight range and is actively monitoring 121.5 MHz. While ATC facilities and some military installations still monitor 121.5, coverage in remote or low-altitude areas is sporadic and unreliable. In remote helicopter operations — mountain flying, offshore, wilderness EMS — a 121.5-only ELT provides dramatically reduced probability of detection compared to a 406 MHz unit, which transmits a digitally encoded signal with GPS position data that is monitored by satellites worldwide. The FAA strongly recommends upgrading to 406 MHz. From a risk management and ADM standpoint, operating in remote environments with a 121.5-only ELT as the sole distress signaling device represents a significant increase in survival risk, and a prudent pilot should treat this as a go/no-go factor for remote operations.	->	Regarding legality: 14 CFR 91.207(c)(2) permits ELTs installed before February 1, 2009 to remain in service. A 121.5 MHz ELT installed prior to that date is legal, and there is no mandate to retrofit to 406 MHz for existing installations. However, the survival implications are severe and warrant serious consideration: COSPAS-SARSAT satellites ceased monitoring 121.5 MHz on February 1, 2009. A 121.5 MHz ELT activation will only be detected if a manned aircraft or ATC ground station within line-of-sight range is actively monitoring the frequency. While ATC and some military stations still monitor 121.5, coverage in remote or low-altitude areas — mountains, offshore, wilderness EMS — is sporadic and unreliable. A 406 MHz ELT with GPS transmits a digitally encoded signal with position data monitored by satellites worldwide, providing vastly superior probability of detection. The FAA strongly recommends upgrading to 406 MHz. From a risk management and aeronautical decision-making standpoint, operating in remote environments with a 121.5-only ELT as the sole distress signaling device represents significantly increased survival risk and should be treated as a go/no-go factor for remote operations planning.
AI_EDIT	CH.X.L.003	The six basic flight instruments are grouped into two categories: (1) Pitch and bank instruments — the attitude indicator, altimeter, airspeed indicator, and turn coordinator — which provide information about the helicopter's pitch attitude, bank attitude, and the results of those attitudes; and (2) Power instruments — the manifold pressure gauge and tachometer (RPM) — which indicate engine and rotor output. The attitude indicator is the primary reference for both pitch and bank, while the other instruments serve as supporting or performance references. Effective instrument flight requires cross-referencing all six instruments together rather than fixating on any single one.	->	The six basic flight instruments are grouped into three functional categories: (1) Pitch control instruments — attitude indicator (primary), altimeter (supporting), and airspeed indicator (supporting); (2) Bank control instruments — attitude indicator (primary), turn coordinator (supporting), and airspeed indicator (supporting); (3) Power instruments — manifold pressure gauge and tachometer (RPM), which indicate engine and rotor output. The attitude indicator serves as the primary reference for both pitch and bank attitude, while the altimeter and airspeed indicator provide supporting information on the results of pitch changes. The turn coordinator supports bank control. Effective instrument flight requires cross-referencing all six instruments together rather than fixating on any single one.
AI_EDIT	CH.XI.A.008	This is a genuine emergency under 14 CFR 91.3, which gives the pilot-in-command authority to deviate from any rule to the extent necessary to meet that emergency. The practical options are limited but include: (1) Attempt to locate VMC at lower altitude by using ATC to identify if any lower minimum obstruction clearance altitude (MOCA) exists — MOCAs provide IFR obstruction clearance but only guarantee navigation signal within 22 NM of the VOR; (2) Reverse course toward the last known VMC area if terrain and aircraft performance allow a controlled descent; (3) If terrain permits, descend to a safe altitude below the cloud bases while maintaining obstacle clearance, using ATC radar services for terrain advisories. The pilot should immediately declare an emergency (squawk 7700 and verbally), state the situation clearly — VFR pilot in IMC, unable to maintain MEA — and request any available assistance including radar vectors to VMC or to terrain-safe lower altitudes. Under 91.3, the pilot is not in violation for deviating from the MEA requirement during the emergency, but must submit a written report to the FAA if requested. Part 135 operations also require notifying the certificate holder's operations control. Any subsequent enforcement review of how the pilot came to be in this situation would be conducted by the FSDO and could involve examination of whether applicable Part 135 and Part 61 requirements — such as currency, dispatch authorization, and pilot qualifications — were met prior to the flight.	->	This concept highlights that unfamiliarity with a specific aircraft's automation architecture — not just general flying ability — creates significant risk. A pilot with thousands of hours in an older, steam-gauge helicopter transitioning to a glass cockpit helicopter with AFCS may be highly experienced overall but dangerously unfamiliar with how to quickly disengage automation modes, recognize automation-induced unusual attitudes, or fly partial panel on an unfamiliar PFD. During unusual attitude recovery, time is critical: a pilot who hesitates because they cannot find the autopilot disconnect, are unsure which AFCS mode is active, or do not know the scan priority of the new PFD is at severe risk. This reinforces the FAA's ADM guidance that pilots must invest in aircraft-specific training and build hours in type before flying in demanding IMC environments. The error chain in an automation-related unusual attitude accident almost always begins with insufficient type-specific proficiency. From a CFI perspective, the instructor has a duty under general standards of care and 14 CFR 61.3 (pilot-in-command proficiency requirements) to ensure a transitioning pilot has adequate aircraft-specific knowledge and skills before endorsing independent operation; a CFI who signs off such a transition without ensuring competency may face enforcement action through FSDO channels.
