AI_EDIT	Control.008	First priority is recognizing the failure — an attitude indicator that has tumbled or failed may show a false horizon, which is more dangerous than no attitude indicator at all. Immediately transition to partial-panel: use the turn-and-slip indicator for bank information (needle deflection indicates direction and rate of turn; ball indicates coordination), airspeed indicator for pitch trend (increasing ASI indicates nose-low pitch; decreasing indicates nose-high), altimeter for pitch confirmation (rising or falling), and VSI for pitch rate. For bank control: center the turn needle to stop the turn, then hold it centered to maintain wings-level. For pitch control: target a stabilized airspeed on the ASI and a zero VSI indication for level flight. Avoid abrupt control inputs — the FAA emphasizes small, precise corrections to prevent oscillation and worsening spatial disorientation. The magnetic compass remains available for heading reference, but it is subject to significant errors during and immediately after turns due to magnetic dip — the compass card will lead, lag, and oscillate depending on the direction of turn, the bank angle, and the pilot's latitude; reliable compass readings should only be taken during straight, unaccelerated, coordinated flight after allowing the compass to stabilize. Declare an emergency, request radar vectors to a clear area or airport, and brief ATC on the partial-panel situation to get priority handling.	->	question text changed
