The left lateral drift is most likely caused by a trim mismatch after engine failure combined with tail rotor thrust characteristics in a clockwise-rotating rotor system. When the engine fails, engine torque drops to zero. The pedal position that was trimmed for powered flight — which was applying left pedal to counteract engine torque — is now excessive. The tail rotor is still producing thrust, but without engine torque to counteract, that pedal input now drives the nose left and pushes the tail right, creating a left yaw tendency. Because the tail rotor on a counterclockwise (viewed from above) main rotor system is mounted on the right side of the tail boom and produces thrust to the left (to counteract torque), the net effect during the transition to forward flight with untrimmed pedals is a left yaw and associated left lateral drift. The pilot must apply right pedal to reduce tail rotor thrust to the new trim requirement, and right cyclic to stop the lateral drift and keep the nose aligned with the intended landing path. Uncorrected lateral drift at touchdown means the skids contact the ground in a sideward sliding condition, which creates a significant rollover risk — especially on sloped or uneven terrain — as described in FAA-H-8083-21B discussions of dynamic rollover and settling with power.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The answer contains a technical contradiction regarding rotor rotation direction and tail rotor thrust direction. The answer states the main rotor is counterclockwise when viewed from above, and describes the tail rotor mounted on the right side producing leftward thrust. However, the explanation of how this creates left yaw during engine failure needs clarification — the mechanics described are somewhat confused regarding the relationship between pedal trim in powered flight and the resulting yaw tendency after engine failure.
The explanation of left lateral drift during forward movement is more complex than presented. The answer conflates tail rotor thrust direction with yaw tendency, but does not clearly explain why left LATERAL drift (sideways movement) would occur from the described pedal mismatch. Lateral drift during forward flight relates to bank angle and cyclic position, not directly to pedal trim or tail rotor thrust.
While the conclusion about dynamic rollover risk and the need for right cyclic correction is sound, the causal explanation leading to that conclusion contains aerodynamic inaccuracies that could confuse a student.

