You are flying at 8,000 feet MSL on a hot summer afternoon with a density altitude of 10,500 feet. The engine fails. How does this combination of temperature and density altitude change your autorotation entry and execution compared to a sea-level, standard-day failure?

Answer (at time of rejection):
At high density altitude, the thinner air means the rotor blades generate less lift and less drag per revolution. Because there is less aerodynamic drag acting on the rotor, RPM may actually decay more slowly initially — but the blades are also producing less lift, so maintaining adequate RPM through proper collective management is still critical. Lower collective immediately upon engine failure to preserve rotor RPM, just as you would at sea level, but recognize that the rotor is operating less efficiently throughout. Your descent rate will be higher than at sea level because the rotor system produces less total lift in the thinner air. Best autorotative airspeed may need to be slightly higher to sustain adequate rotor RPM and airflow through the disk. At the bottom of the autorotation, the flare will produce less aerodynamic deceleration and the collective pitch increase will generate less lift per degree of blade pitch change, making the cushioning effect less effective than at sea level. Plan for a longer ground roll or a firmer touchdown, choose the largest and most unobstructed landing area available, and mentally account for the reduced performance margin throughout the entire sequence.

