AI_EDIT	FI.I.B.K15.002	The FAA identifies several factors that influence retention: (1) Praise and positive feedback — students retain information better when they associate it with a positive, rewarding experience; (2) Association — new material is retained more effectively when it is connected to something the student already knows; (3) Favorable attitudes — a student who is motivated and has a positive attitude toward the subject retains information longer. Other factors include the meaningfulness of the material, the frequency of practice and review, and whether the student understands the relevance of the subject to their flying.	->	The FAA-H-8083-9B identifies several factors that influence retention, including: (1) Association — new material is retained more effectively when connected to something the student already knows; (2) Meaningfulness — material that is relevant and clearly explained is retained better; (3) Positive feedback and favorable attitudes — students retain information longer when they associate learning with success and have a positive attitude toward the subject. Additional factors include the frequency of practice and review, understanding the relevance of material to flying, and the feeling of accomplishment. These factors work together to enhance long-term retention.
AI_EDIT	FI.I.B.R1.003	The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that accident rates decrease significantly after 100 hours in a specific aircraft type, indicating that type-specific unfamiliarity is a major risk factor. This obligates an instructor to provide thorough, model-specific instruction — not just rely on a student's total hours. The instructor must cover that aircraft's specific systems, limitations, emergency procedures, performance data, and handling characteristics. Assuming a high-time pilot needs minimal instruction on a new type is itself an example of the risk of incomplete instruction.	->	Accident analysis demonstrates that type-specific experience is one of the strongest predictors of safety; students with limited hours in a new aircraft type remain in a higher-risk category regardless of total flight hours. This obligates an instructor to provide thorough, model-specific instruction rather than assuming a high-time pilot requires minimal transition training. The instructor must systematically cover that aircraft's systems, limitations, emergency procedures, performance data, and handling characteristics. Under 14 CFR 61.87 and 61.193, the CFI is personally responsible for verifying that the student is proficient across all required areas before endorsing any solo flight. Assigning a high-time pilot to a new type without comprehensive instruction is a significant instructional risk.
