AI_EDIT	FI.I.E.K3.007	Operating as a flight instructor with a lapsed certificate violates 14 CFR 61.3, which prohibits acting in the capacity of a required pilot flightcrew member or as an instructor without the appropriate, current certificate. Any endorsements provided during this period are legally invalid. The CFI may face certificate action including suspension or revocation under 14 CFR 61.15 and potentially civil penalties. Students who received training and endorsements during that window may need re-endorsement from a current instructor. Professionally, the FAA's Aviation Instructor's Handbook holds that a flight instructor must maintain not only currency in skills but also administrative currency in their credentials — this is part of exercising personal responsibility and integrity, both hallmarks of professionalism. Reference: 14 CFR 61.3; 14 CFR 61.197; 14 CFR 61.15.	->	The solo endorsement would be invalid because it was issued by a person not authorized to act as a flight instructor at the time — essentially, no qualified instructor provided the required endorsement. The student's solo flight technically lacked the legally required authorization under 14 CFR 61.87, which could expose both the student and the CFI to regulatory action. The CFI's primary violation is 14 CFR 61.3 (operating without appropriate current certificate), which carries potential certificate revocation. If the CFI knowingly misrepresented their certification status, 14 CFR 61.59 (falsification) could apply, but a good-faith inadvertent lapse is a violation of 61.3 without the additional falsification charge. The student, if acting without knowledge of the lapse, may face lesser consequences at FAA discretion. This underscores why professional self-monitoring of certificate validity is not optional — it directly affects the safety and legal status of students.
AI_EDIT	FI.I.E.K3.008	Under 14 CFR 61.195(a), a flight instructor may not conduct more than 8 hours of flight training in any 24-consecutive-hour period. There is no monthly hour cap specified in Part 61 for general flight instruction; however, for flight instructor fatigue management, this 8-hour daily limit is the primary regulatory guardrail. The safety purpose is to prevent instructor fatigue, which degrades judgment, reaction time, and the quality of instruction — all of which directly affect student and flight safety. From a professionalism standpoint, a CFI who deliberately schedules back-to-back students to maximize income at the expense of their own fitness is compromising both safety and the quality of education they provide. The Aviation Instructor's Handbook identifies good judgment and safety consciousness as cornerstones of professionalism, which includes knowing personal limits and managing workload accordingly. Reference: 14 CFR 61.195(a); FAA-H-8083-9B Chapter 8.	->	Under 14 CFR 61.195(a), a flight instructor may not conduct more than 8 hours of flight training in any 24-consecutive-hour period. There is no separate monthly hour cap specified in Part 61 for general flight instruction; the 8-hour daily limit is the primary regulatory guardrail for Part 61 CFIs. Note that 14 CFR Part 117 (duty time and rest) applies only to Part 121 and Part 135 pilots and crew members, not directly to independent Part 61 CFIs unless they operate in those commercial contexts. The safety purpose of the 8-hour limit is to prevent instructor fatigue, which degrades judgment, reaction time, and the quality of instruction — all of which directly affect student and flight safety. From a professionalism standpoint, a CFI who deliberately schedules back-to-back students to maximize income at the expense of their own fitness is compromising both safety and the quality of education they provide. The Aviation Instructor's Handbook identifies good judgment and safety consciousness as cornerstones of professionalism, which includes knowing personal limits and managing workload accordingly. CFIs conducting training under Part 141 must also comply with any duty or rest limitations imposed by the approved training course outline. Reference: 14 CFR 61.195(a); FAA-H-8083-9B Chapter 8.
