AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K1.004	First, calculate density altitude. Using the standard formula or a flight computer: pressure altitude at 7,500 feet MSL with standard altimeter setting is approximately 7,500 feet (assuming standard pressure). Then correct for temperature. Standard temperature at 7,500 feet is approximately 7°C (15°C minus 2°C per 1,000 feet). The actual OAT is 30°C, which is 23°C above standard. Each degree above standard adds approximately 120 feet of density altitude, so 23 × 120 = 2,760 feet. Density altitude ≈ 7,500 + 2,760 = approximately 10,260 feet. Compare this to the HOGE limit of 9,200 feet density altitude at your planned gross weight. Your density altitude of 10,260 feet exceeds the HOGE limit by over 1,000 feet — the planned departure is not within HOGE limits and you would need to reduce weight, wait for cooler conditions, or depart using a running/rolling takeoff if terrain permits and the RFM authorizes it.	->	First, calculate density altitude using an E6B or electronic flight computer for precision. Standard temperature at 7,500 feet is approximately 7°C (15°C minus 2°C per 1,000 feet). The actual OAT is 30°C, which is 23°C above standard. Using a density altitude calculator or E6B, with pressure altitude of 7,500 feet and 23°C above standard, density altitude will be significantly higher — typically in the range of 10,000–13,000 feet depending on the calculation method and exact altitude band. Using the approximation rule of 120 feet per degree Celsius above standard yields roughly 10,260 feet. Compare this to the HOGE limit of 9,200 feet density altitude at your planned gross weight. Your calculated density altitude exceeds the HOGE limit by a significant margin — the planned departure is not within HOGE limits and you would need to reduce weight, wait for cooler conditions, or (if terrain and RFM permit) depart using a running/rolling takeoff.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K1.005	Fuel flow per hour may decrease at higher altitude due to reduced density and mixture leaning (for piston helicopters) or fuel control scheduling (for turbines), but range is determined by specific range — nautical miles per pound of fuel — which is the product of true airspeed and fuel efficiency. At higher density altitudes, the helicopter must maintain a higher power setting or true airspeed to achieve the same indicated airspeed, which offsets the reduced fuel flow. Additionally, for turbine helicopters, specific fuel consumption can increase at certain power settings and temperatures. The payload-range chart already accounts for these trade-offs at specified conditions. If the chart shows diminishing range returns at higher altitudes for your gross weight and conditions, it means the increased true airspeed is not enough to overcome the less efficient operating point. Always use the specific altitude and weight inputs on the chart rather than assuming higher altitude always equals better range.	->	Fuel flow per hour may decrease at higher altitude due to reduced air density and (for piston helicopters) mixture leaning, or automatic fuel control scheduling (for turbines). However, range is determined by specific range — nautical miles per pound of fuel — which depends on true airspeed and fuel efficiency. At higher altitudes, to maintain the same indicated airspeed the helicopter must fly at a higher true airspeed and power setting. For turbines, specific fuel consumption (fuel burned per unit of power output) varies with power setting; many turbine helicopters are most efficient at moderate cruise power, and higher altitudes may force operation into a less efficient power regime. The payload-range chart already accounts for these trade-offs and altitude effects at specified conditions. If the chart shows diminishing or flat range at higher altitudes for your gross weight, it indicates the increased true airspeed does not sufficiently offset the less-efficient operating point. Always consult the specific altitude and weight inputs on the chart rather than assuming higher altitude always improves range.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K3.006	That argument is incorrect. Some helicopters have a published minimum weight limit, and operating below it creates real hazards. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook identifies three specific problems with operating below minimum weight: (1) reduced control range — with less weight on the aircraft, certain control inputs may lack their normal range of authority; (2) adverse effects on autorotational characteristics — the lighter the helicopter, the harder it may be to manage rotor RPM during an autorotation; and (3) the pilot may be unable to achieve the desired rotor RPM during autorotation because the reduced blade loading changes the rotor's aerodynamic response. Additionally, lateral CG limits still apply regardless of how light the aircraft is — an unbalanced light load can be just as dangerous as an overloaded one.	->	That argument is incorrect. Some helicopters have a published minimum weight limit, and operating below it creates real hazards. The primary risks stem from reduced blade loading and changed aerodynamic characteristics: (1) reduced control effectiveness — with less weight on the aircraft, the aerodynamic effect of control inputs changes because the loading on rotor blades is reduced, making control inputs less responsive and potentially requiring larger inputs to achieve desired aircraft response; (2) adverse effects on autorotational characteristics — lighter blade loading changes the rotor's RPM droop characteristics, making it more difficult to achieve and maintain appropriate rotor RPM during autorotation and complicating the flare maneuver; (3) the lateral and longitudinal CG envelope must still be respected regardless of total weight — an unbalanced light load can place the helicopter outside its CG limits just as an overloaded one can, potentially causing uncontrollable flight characteristics. Operating below minimum weight or outside CG limits, even if below maximum gross weight, can render the helicopter unsafe.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K3.008	This scenario reveals that a static takeoff weight and balance calculation only validates one snapshot in time. For in-flight operations, several dynamic factors can shift CG beyond what was calculated: (1) passenger movement — passengers shifting seats or leaning can move significant weight fore or aft, especially in small helicopters where individual passenger weight is a large fraction of useful load; (2) fuel burn, as discussed previously; (3) cargo shifting in flight. For passenger operations, pilots should: use actual passenger weights (or standard weights per AC 120-27 if applicable), calculate CG with passengers in their assigned seats and ensure they remain seated appropriately during critical phases; check that even with reasonable in-flight weight shifts, CG remains within limits — this may mean running multiple CG scenarios (e.g., worst-case forward and aft passenger seating). In helicopters with lateral CG limits, asymmetric passenger loading must also be evaluated. The RFM may provide specific guidance on passenger placement.	->	This scenario reveals that a static takeoff weight and balance calculation only validates one snapshot in time. For in-flight operations, several dynamic factors can shift CG beyond what was calculated: (1) passenger movement — passengers shifting seats or leaning can move significant weight fore or aft, especially in small helicopters where individual passenger weight is a large fraction of useful load; (2) fuel burn, as previously discussed; (3) cargo shifting in flight. For passenger operations, pilots should: calculate CG with passengers in their assigned seats and ensure they remain seated appropriately during critical phases; check that even with reasonable in-flight weight shifts, CG remains within limits — this may require running multiple CG scenarios (e.g., worst-case forward and aft passenger seating); evaluate lateral CG shifts from asymmetric passenger loading. For Part 91 operations, use actual passenger weights or methods approved in the POH/RFM. For Part 135 operators, use either actual weights or standard weights per an FAA-approved method in the operations specifications; AC 120-27 is one acceptable reference for standard passenger/crew weights, but alternatives may be approved depending on the operator's specific operations specifications.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K4.001	You need the weight of the item, its arm (the distance in inches from the datum to the item's location), and the resulting moment (weight multiplied by arm). Once you have all moments, you sum them and divide by the total weight to get the CG location in inches from the datum.	->	You need two pieces of information for each item: (1) the weight of the item, and (2) its arm (the distance in inches from the datum to the item's location). The moment is then calculated by multiplying weight by arm. Once you have all moments calculated, you sum them and divide by the total weight to get the CG location in inches from the datum. In practice, these values are found in the RFM loading diagrams or weight and balance tables.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K4.002	The datum is an imaginary vertical plane from which all horizontal distances (arms) are measured for weight and balance purposes. It is established by the manufacturer and defined in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) or Pilot's Operating Handbook (POH). Items forward of the datum have a positive arm; items aft of the datum have a negative arm (or the manufacturer may place the datum at the nose so all arms are positive).	->	The datum is an imaginary vertical plane from which all horizontal distances (arms) are measured for weight and balance purposes. It is established by the manufacturer and defined in the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) or Pilot's Operating Handbook (POH). Items aft of the datum have a positive arm; items forward of the datum have a negative arm. However, manufacturers may place the datum ahead of the aircraft so that all arms are positive. Always refer to your specific RFM for the sign convention used.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K5.002	The three types are profile drag, induced drag, and parasite drag. Profile drag results from skin friction and form drag on the blade itself; it remains relatively constant regardless of airspeed. Induced drag is a byproduct of lift production and decreases as airspeed increases because the rotor requires less angle of attack at higher speeds. Parasite drag is caused by non-lift-producing parts of the helicopter and increases with the square of velocity — so it rises rapidly at higher airspeeds.	->	The three types are profile drag, induced drag, and parasite drag. Profile drag results from skin friction and form drag on the blade itself and increases with the square of airspeed. Induced drag is a byproduct of lift production and decreases as airspeed increases because the rotor requires less angle of attack at higher speeds to maintain lift. Parasite drag is caused by non-lift-producing parts of the helicopter (fuselage, landing gear) and increases with the square of velocity, rising rapidly at higher airspeeds.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.E.K5.006	When aft cyclic is applied, the rotor disk tilts rearward, which increases the upward airflow component through the rotor disk. This upward relative wind through the disk acts more directly on the blade angle of attack in a way that tends to accelerate rather than retard rotor rotation — essentially the blades are in a better position to extract energy from the relative wind and maintain or increase rotor RPM. Additionally, aft cyclic slows the helicopter's forward speed, which reduces parasite drag and allows the rotor system to enter a more efficient autorotative state. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook specifically notes that aft cyclic movement increases airflow up through the rotor disk, contributing to rotor RPM increase during autorotation entry.	->	When aft cyclic is applied during autorotation entry, the helicopter's forward airspeed is reduced. This decreases rotor disc loading and allows the rotor to transition into a more efficient autorotative state. The reduction in rotor loading and forward speed helps establish the optimal balance of aerodynamic torque and mechanical resistance needed to maintain or increase rotor RPM as the helicopter descends. Aft cyclic also transitions the rotor disc to a more favorable angle relative to the oncoming airflow, which is essential for efficient autorotation.
