AI_EDIT	HI.III.G.S4.006	Approximately 30 NM from the destination, the GPS automatically transitions from en route sensitivity (±2 NM) to terminal sensitivity (±1 NM) — you should notice the CDI becomes more sensitive at this point. As you reach the IAF waypoint, the GPS will provide an alert (message/chime), the distance counter reaches zero, and the unit sequences to the next leg of the approach. The CDI re-centers on the new inbound course. If the approach requires a procedure turn or hold, the GPS may suspend automatic sequencing ('SUSP' annunciation) and require manual sequencing after completing the required maneuver.	->	Typically 25–35 NM from the destination (varies by GPS unit and approach design), the GPS automatically transitions from en route sensitivity (±2 NM) to terminal sensitivity (±1 NM) — you should notice the CDI becomes more sensitive at this point. As you reach the IAF waypoint, the GPS will provide an alert (message/chime), the distance counter reaches zero, and the unit sequences to the next leg of the approach. The CDI re-centers on the new inbound course. Depending on the published approach procedure, the GPS may suspend automatic sequencing ('SUSP' annunciation) if the procedure requires a procedure turn, holding pattern, or course reversal. If suspension occurs on a straight-in approach, you will need to manually sequence the GPS to continue to the MAP. Always review the published approach procedure during your briefing to anticipate the expected GPS behavior at each waypoint.
ESCALATE	HI.III.G.S4.008	SUSP behavior at the FAF is GPS unit and approach-design dependent, and specific button nomenclature (OBS/SUSP) varies significantly across approved IFR GPS models; the answer should not assume universal behavior without noting equipment limitations and requiring pilot knowledge of their specific avionics.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K1.008	A VOR receiver failure en route on a VOR airway affects your ability to meet the IFR equipment requirements under 14 CFR 91.205 and the airway navigation requirements. However, if your IFR GPS is operational and approved, it may substitute for VOR navigation on Victor airways under FAA AC 90-108, which allows GPS to be used in lieu of a ground-based NAVAID for enroute and terminal operations provided the GPS is properly approved and the route is in the database. You must notify ATC of the equipment failure as soon as practicable per 14 CFR 91.187, which requires reporting any malfunction of navigational equipment that affects safety or compliance with your ATC clearance. ATC will note the equipment status and adjust your flight plan symbol in their system. You should request a route that your remaining navigation capability can support — in this case, GPS-based airways or direct GPS routing. If your GPS also failed, leaving no IFR navigation capability, you would declare an emergency, request radar vectors, and execute a safe landing at the nearest suitable airport. Options include continuing on GPS, requesting a revised clearance, diverting, or declaring an emergency depending on the severity and remaining alternatives.	->	A VOR receiver failure en route on a VOR airway affects your ability to meet IFR equipment requirements under 14 CFR 91.205. However, if your IFR GPS is operational, approved (TSO-C129 or better for your planned operations), and current in its database, it may substitute for VOR navigation under FAA AC 90-108, provided the approach and route do not explicitly require ground-based NAVAIDs. You must notify ATC of the equipment failure as soon as practicable per 14 CFR 91.187, which requires reporting any malfunction affecting safety or compliance with your clearance. ATC will update your equipment status and may provide a revised clearance supporting GPS/RNAV navigation, direct routing, or radar vectors as appropriate. Options include: (1) continuing on your current route if ATC authorizes GPS substitution; (2) requesting a revised clearance for RNAV or direct GPS routing; (3) requesting radar vectors; or (4) diverting to a suitable airport. If both VOR and GPS failed, declare an emergency, request radar vectors, and execute a safe landing at the nearest suitable airport. Always confirm with ATC that your remaining navigation capability meets their requirements before continuing IFR.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K2.004	Wind significantly affects altitude selection over terrain. A headwind of 25 knots reduces your groundspeed and increases flight time over hostile terrain, extending exposure to engine failure scenarios with limited autorotation options. More critically, wind flowing over a ridge can produce mechanical turbulence and downdrafts on the lee side — a 25-knot headwind indicates you are approaching the windward side, but be alert to strong rotor turbulence and downdrafts if you cross the ridge. FAA guidance recommends crossing mountain ridges at least 1,000 feet (preferably 2,000 feet) above the ridge elevation to allow escape from downdrafts and to have sufficient altitude for autorotation to a suitable area on either side. The helicopter's specific autorotation glide distance, the terrain on both sides of the ridge, and the location of suitable forced-landing areas should all factor into the chosen crossing altitude.	->	Wind significantly affects altitude selection over terrain. A headwind of 25 knots reduces your groundspeed and increases flight time over hostile terrain, extending exposure to engine failure scenarios with limited autorotation options. A 25-knot headwind indicates you are approaching the windward side of the ridge, which may provide beneficial updrafts. However, if forced to descend on the lee (downwind) side during an emergency, you must be alert to rotor turbulence and strong downdrafts that could prevent safe descent to suitable landing areas. FAA guidance recommends crossing mountain ridges at least 1,000 feet (preferably 2,000 feet) above the ridge elevation to allow escape from downdrafts and to have sufficient altitude for autorotation to a suitable area on either side. The helicopter's specific autorotation glide distance, the terrain on both sides of the ridge, and the location of suitable forced-landing areas should all factor into the chosen crossing altitude.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K2.008	The MEA provides a useful reference for obstacle clearance but is designed for IFR operations on airways — a VFR helicopter is not required to use it. However, the fact that the nearest MEA is 10,500 feet MSL suggests significant terrain in the area. For VFR obstacle clearance, 14 CFR 91.119 requires remaining clear of obstacles, and over mountainous terrain a minimum of 2,000 feet above the highest obstacle within 4 NM of the course is the IFR standard (91.177), which can serve as a conservative VFR planning benchmark. If terrain peaks at 9,200 feet MSL and the helicopter's service ceiling is 10,000 feet MSL, flying at 9,500 feet MSL provides only 300 feet of terrain clearance — dangerously inadequate. Compounding factors that could make this altitude dangerous include: (1) Density altitude — on a hot day at high elevation, the helicopter may be unable to maintain 9,500 feet MSL or may have dramatically reduced performance margins; (2) Updrafts and downdrafts — wind flowing over a 9,200-foot ridge can generate strong mechanical turbulence and downdrafts that could push the helicopter into terrain even with 300 feet of apparent clearance; (3) The service ceiling is typically defined at a very low rate of climb (100 fpm) — at near-ceiling altitudes, the helicopter has essentially no performance reserve for maneuvering or obstacle avoidance; (4) An engine failure at this altitude over this terrain would almost certainly result in an unsurvivable forced landing. The correct decision is to reroute around the terrain or postpone the flight if safe altitude cannot be maintained.	->	The MEA provides a useful reference for obstacle clearance but is designed for IFR operations on airways — a VFR helicopter is not required to use it. However, the fact that the nearest MEA is 10,500 feet MSL suggests significant terrain in the area. For VFR obstacle clearance, 14 CFR 91.119 requires remaining clear of obstacles. The 2,000-foot-above-obstacle standard within 4 NM specified in 14 CFR 91.177 applies to IFR flight, but serves as a useful conservative reference for VFR planning in mountainous terrain. If terrain peaks at 9,200 feet MSL and the helicopter's service ceiling is 10,000 feet MSL, flying at 9,500 feet MSL provides only 300 feet of terrain clearance — dangerously inadequate. Compounding factors that could make this altitude dangerous include: (1) Density altitude — on a hot day at high elevation, the helicopter may be unable to maintain 9,500 feet MSL or may have dramatically reduced performance margins; performance is published in terms of density altitude, not true altitude, making accurate calculation critical; (2) Updrafts and downdrafts — wind flowing over a 9,200-foot ridge can generate strong mechanical turbulence and downdrafts that could push the helicopter into terrain even with 300 feet of apparent clearance; (3) The service ceiling is typically defined at a very low rate of climb (100 fpm) — at near-ceiling altitudes, the helicopter has essentially no performance reserve for maneuvering or obstacle avoidance; (4) An engine failure at this altitude over this terrain would almost certainly result in an unsurvivable forced landing. The correct decision is to reroute around the terrain or postpone the flight if safe altitude cannot be maintained.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K3.006	Start with the True Course (TC): 275°. Apply magnetic variation — 12°E means East is least, so subtract: 275° - 12° = 263° Magnetic Course (MC). Then apply wind correction angle (WCA) if known to get Magnetic Heading (MH) — assume no wind for this example, so MH = 263°. Finally, apply compass deviation from the deviation card — deviation is +3°, meaning the compass reads 3° high, so subtract 3° to get Compass Heading (CH): 263° - 3° = 260°. The memory aid for the full sequence is TVMDC: True, Variation, Magnetic, Deviation, Compass ('True Virgins Make Dull Companions'). In actual flight planning you'd also apply wind correction angle between MC and MH.	->	Start with the True Course (TC): 275°. Apply magnetic variation — 12°E means East is least, so subtract: 275° - 12° = 263° Magnetic Course (MC). Then apply wind correction angle (WCA) if known to get Magnetic Heading (MH) — assume no wind for this example, so MH = 263°. Finally, apply compass deviation from the deviation card. A +3° deviation card entry means the compass needle points 3° higher (eastward) than the magnetic heading. To convert magnetic heading to compass heading, subtract the deviation: 263° - 3° = 260° Compass Heading (CH). The sequence is TVMDC: True, Variation, Magnetic, Deviation, Compass. In actual flight planning you'd also apply wind correction angle between MC and MH.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K4.005	First, I would recalculate fuel burn and range using the new altitude and the RFM fuel flow tables for that altitude and OAT. If the lower altitude increases fuel consumption enough to jeopardize legal IFR fuel reserves (45 minutes for VFR, 45 minutes past the first intended destination for IFR under 14 CFR 91.167), I must act. Options include: requesting a higher altitude from ATC to restore planned fuel flow; reducing power to a best-range setting to maximize miles per gallon even if it slightly reduces airspeed; or identifying and requesting a closer alternate. If no adjustment can restore adequate reserves, I must declare the situation to ATC and divert to a suitable airport. The key is using the RFM power/fuel flow tables — not estimates — to make this determination.	->	First, I would recalculate fuel burn and range using the new altitude and the RFM fuel flow tables for that altitude and OAT. If the lower altitude increases fuel consumption enough to jeopardize legal IFR fuel reserves, I must act. Under 14 CFR 91.167, I must have fuel to reach the first airport of intended landing, then any required alternate, then 45 minutes more. If this requirement cannot be met with the new altitude, my options are: request a higher altitude from ATC to restore planned fuel flow; reduce power to a best-range setting to maximize miles per gallon even if it slightly reduces airspeed; or identify and request a closer alternate. If no adjustment can restore adequate reserves, I must declare the situation to ATC and divert to a suitable airport. The key is using the RFM power/fuel flow tables — not estimates — to make this determination.
ESCALATE	HI.III.H.K4.008	The question premise and answer attempt to establish a direct causal link between power setting selection and VOR signal reliability that is not well-supported in FAA sources; the connection is indirect (power affects sustainable altitude, altitude affects MEA compliance) and the answer risks confusing students about whether power setting itself directly affects navigation system function.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K5.004	With a course of 360° (due north) and wind from 270° (due west) at 25 knots, the wind is a direct crosswind — it has no headwind or tailwind component. The groundspeed equals TAS plus the tailwind component. Since the wind is 90° off the nose (pure crosswind), the headwind/tailwind component is 0 knots. Therefore groundspeed ≈ 110 knots (ignoring the slight groundspeed reduction from crab angle, which for a 25/110 kt scenario is minimal — cos(arcsin(25/110)) ≈ 0.974, so GS ≈ 107 knots). For practical planning: GS ≈ 107 knots. Time en route = 180 NM ÷ 107 knots ≈ 1.68 hours, or approximately 1 hour 41 minutes. On a practical E6B or flight computer, you input TAS, wind direction/speed, and true course to derive both the wind correction angle and groundspeed directly.	->	With a course of 360° (due north) and wind from 270° (due west) at 25 knots, the wind is a direct crosswind — perpendicular to your ground track with no headwind or tailwind component. Therefore, groundspeed ≈ TAS ≈ 110 knots. You would need a crab angle of approximately 13° to the west to maintain a ground track of 360°, but this crab does not reduce your groundspeed. Time en route = 180 NM ÷ 110 knots ≈ 1 hour 38 minutes. On an E6B or flight computer, you input TAS (110 kt), wind direction (270°), wind speed (25 kt), and true course (360°) to derive both the wind correction angle and groundspeed directly.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K5.007	Step 1 — Fuel burned during 20-minute hold: 31 GPH × (20/60 hr) = 10.3 gallons. Step 2 — Fuel remaining after hold: 85 − 10.3 = 74.7 gallons. However, you still need to consider fuel burned from the hold fix to the destination. Assuming the hold is at the destination (direct arrival after hold), the remaining fuel upon landing is approximately 74.7 gallons. Step 3 — Required VFR day reserve per 91.151: 20 minutes at normal cruise speed = 28 GPH × (20/60) = 9.3 gallons. Step 4 — Is 74.7 gallons above the 9.3-gallon reserve? Yes, significantly. You have adequate fuel. However, the critical point is that a pilot must account for holding burn rate versus cruise burn rate when estimating remaining fuel, and must continuously update fuel state. If the hold extended significantly beyond 20 minutes, that calculation changes rapidly, and the decision to divert or declare minimum fuel should be made proactively — not reactively.	->	Step 1 — Fuel burned during 20-minute hold: 31 GPH × (20/60 hr) = 10.3 gallons. Step 2 — Fuel remaining after hold (assuming direct landing at hold fix): 85 − 10.3 = 74.7 gallons. Step 3 — Required VFR day reserve per 14 CFR 91.151: 20 minutes at normal cruise speed = 28 GPH × (20/60) = 9.3 gallons. Step 4 — Adequate fuel check: 74.7 gallons remaining is significantly above the 9.3-gallon reserve; yes, you have adequate fuel. Critical consideration: If the hold is at a fix before your destination, you must subtract additional fuel burn from the hold to the destination. Continuously monitor fuel state; if holding time extends significantly beyond 20 minutes or destination is not immediately accessible, declare 'minimum fuel' to ATC when fuel state means arrival with planned reserves is no longer assured.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K7.002	You should check: (1) FDC NOTAMs, which contain regulatory changes such as temporary flight restrictions (TFRs) and amendments to instrument approach procedures; (2) D-NOTAMs (Distant NOTAMs), which cover airport and navaid outages, runway closures, and other facility status; and (3) GPS NOTAMs (now part of the FNS NOTAM system), which identify GPS signal outages or RAIM anomalies along the route. All are available through 1800wxbrief.com or Leidos Flight Service.	->	You should check: (1) FDC NOTAMs, which contain regulatory changes including Temporary Flight Restrictions (TFRs), Special Use Airspace (SUA) changes, and amendments to instrument approach procedures; (2) Facility NOTAMs, which cover airport and navaid outages, runway closures, equipment status, and other facility-related changes; and (3) GPS/RAIM NOTAMs, which identify GPS signal outages, RAIM unavailability, or unreliable GPS areas along your route. These are accessible through the FAA's integrated NOTAM Search (FNS) at faa.notam.info or through Leidos Flight Service at 1800wxbrief.com.
