ESCALATE	HI.III.H.K9.004	The original answer contains contradictory and inaccurate descriptions of northerly turning error: it states the compass will show 'a heading less than 360' during a left turn FROM north, but northerly turning error causes the compass to LAG (read HIGHER) when turning FROM north, not lower. The latitude-based roll-out rule is also oversimplified and the verifier's correction logic appears inverted. This requires clarification against FAA-H-8083-15B before shipping.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.K9.008	For IFR operations, 14 CFR 91.205 requires a magnetic compass as part of the minimum required instruments, and it must be operational. The FAA standard (per AC 43.13-1B and instrument airworthiness standards) requires that residual deviation on any heading not exceed 10 degrees after compensation. If a compass cannot be compensated within 10 degrees on all headings, it must either be replaced, or if it remains installed, each heading on which deviation exceeds acceptable limits must be clearly annotated on the compass correction card — and the pilot must factor that known error into operations. As pilot-in-command, you are responsible for ensuring the compass correction card is current, legible, and mounted in plain view near the compass. If deviation errors are large enough to render the compass unreliable for IFR use, the aircraft is not airworthy for IFR operations under 14 CFR 91.205. The PIC should also be aware that recent avionics installations can alter the magnetic environment in the cockpit, making a new compass swing mandatory after such work — this is an airworthiness requirement, not optional maintenance.	->	For IFR operations, 14 CFR 91.205 requires a functioning magnetic compass. While the regulation does not explicitly specify a maximum allowable deviation, AC 43.13-1B recommends that residual deviation after compensation not exceed 10 degrees on any heading—this is the standard industry practice. If a compass cannot be compensated to within acceptable limits, it must be repaired or replaced, as an unreliable compass renders the aircraft unairworthy for IFR operations. Annotating deviations exceeding limits on the compass correction card is prudent practice. As pilot-in-command, you are responsible for ensuring the compass correction card is current, legible, and mounted in plain view. A new compass swing is mandatory after avionics installation, as new equipment can alter the magnetic environment in the cockpit.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.R1.001	The pilot's own fitness and currency. Research shows approximately 80% of aviation accidents result from pilot error (human factors). Before any IFR flight, the pilot must assess personal minimums, recent IFR experience, currency (6 approaches, holds, and intercepting/tracking within 6 months per 14 CFR 61.57(c)), fatigue, health, and mental readiness. No amount of good weather or well-planned routing compensates for a pilot who is not genuinely fit and current for the flight.	->	Pilot fitness and currency. Pilot error is a significant factor in most aviation accidents. Before any IFR flight, the pilot must assess personal minimums, recent IFR experience, currency (6 approaches, holds, and intercepting/tracking within 6 months per 14 CFR 61.57(c)), fatigue, health, and mental readiness. No amount of good weather or well-planned routing compensates for a pilot who is not genuinely fit and current for the flight.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.R1.004	The word 'probably' is a significant risk flag. FAA ADM guidance specifically warns pilots to avoid actions that will 'probably' work out — instead, choose an option known to work. If the forecast only 'probably' keeps the ceiling above minimums, the pilot must treat that as a risk requiring mitigation. This means filing an alternate when required (14 CFR 91.169 — alternate required unless destination weather is forecast to be at least 2,000-foot ceiling and 3 SM visibility from 1 hour before to 1 hour after ETA), selecting an alternate that has a high probability of usable conditions, and considering delaying departure or selecting a different destination. Flying toward a 'probable' legal destination without a solid backup plan is poor risk management.	->	The word 'probably' is a significant risk flag. FAA ADM guidance (FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13; AIM 1-1-1 to 1-1-15) specifically warns pilots to avoid actions that will 'probably' work out — instead, choose an option known to work. If the forecast only 'probably' keeps the ceiling above minimums, the pilot must treat that as a risk requiring mitigation. This means filing an alternate when required (14 CFR 91.169 — alternate required unless destination weather is forecast to be at least 2,000-foot ceiling and 3 SM visibility from 1 hour before to 1 hour after ETA), selecting an alternate that has a high probability of usable conditions, and considering delaying departure or selecting a different destination. Flying toward a 'probable' legal destination without a solid backup plan is poor risk management.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.R2.004	An expired GPS navigation database presents a significant risk and legal issue. Under 14 CFR 91.227 and FAA guidance, IFR flight using GPS as a primary means of navigation requires a current navigation database. Flying IFR with an expired database to fly GPS instrument approaches or to use GPS as the sole navigation source is not authorized. Even if the weather is VMC, the aircraft's IFR certification is compromised for GPS-based navigation. Risk mitigation options include: updating the database before flight, using an alternate IFR navigation system (VOR, ILS) if legal and equipped, or filing VFR if weather permits and regulations allow. The database must be current per the avionics manufacturer's guidance and 14 CFR 91.227 for WAAS GPS units used in IFR.	->	An expired GPS navigation database presents significant risk, particularly for precision approaches. Per 14 CFR 91.227 and AC 90-100A, GPS database currency requirements vary by operation: terminal procedures (approaches, departures, arrivals) require a current database per the avionics manufacturer's specifications, while en route operations may have more flexible guidance depending on the specific RFM supplement. The safest risk management decision is to verify database currency status with your avionics manufacturer and RFM before flight. If the database is expired, mitigation options include: updating the database before departure, using alternate IFR navigation systems (VOR, ILS) if equipped and legal, or filing VFR if weather permits. Declaring any database currency concern to ATC is advisable. The aircraft's IFR certification remains valid, but GPS-based navigation procedures may be restricted pending database update.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.R2.008	A transponder failure in Class B airspace is a serious situation. Under 14 CFR 91.215, transponder (Mode C) is required in Class B airspace and within 30 NM of the primary Class B airport. If the transponder fails in flight, 14 CFR 91.215(d) allows the flight to continue to its destination if the pilot obtains an ATC authorization to continue. You must immediately notify ATC of the failure. ATC may allow you to continue under IFR radar identification using primary radar, or may reroute you out of the Class B. For IFR flight, ATC radar separation services are degraded without a transponder, increasing collision risk and ATC workload. Risk factors driving the divert-vs-continue decision include: remaining distance to destination, weather conditions and ability to maintain VMC if needed, traffic density along the route, ATC's ability to provide services with primary-only radar, and whether the destination airport is within Class B or other transponder-required airspace. The safest risk management choice is typically to land at the nearest suitable airport, repair the transponder, and continue.	->	A transponder failure in Class B airspace is a serious situation. Under 14 CFR 91.215, Mode C transponder is required in Class B airspace and within 30 NM of the primary Class B airport. If the transponder fails in flight, 14 CFR 91.215(d) allows the flight to continue to its destination if the pilot obtains an ATC authorization to continue. You must immediately notify ATC of the failure. ATC may allow you to continue under IFR radar identification using primary radar, or may reroute you out of the Class B. For IFR flight, ATC radar separation services are degraded without a transponder, increasing collision risk and ATC workload. Risk factors driving the divert-vs-continue decision include: remaining distance to destination, weather conditions and ability to maintain VMC if needed, traffic density along the route, ATC's ability to provide services with primary-only radar, and whether the destination airport is within Class B or other transponder-required airspace. The safest risk management choice is typically to land at the nearest suitable airport, repair the transponder, and continue. Note: ADS-B Out failure is governed separately under 14 CFR 91.225 with similar relief provisions.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.R4.002	Approximately 80% of aviation accidents are attributed to pilot error driven by human factors. External pressures are a significant contributor because they lead pilots to make poor decisions — continuing into IMC, accepting an overloaded helicopter, or skipping a thorough preflight — when they feel pressured to complete the flight. The accident is rarely caused by a single bad decision; it is typically an error chain, each link of which can often be traced back to a pilot who felt pressure to press on.	->	Research indicates that approximately 70–85% of aviation accidents are attributed to pilot error, with the specific percentage varying by accident category and aircraft type. External pressures are a significant contributor because they lead pilots to make poor decisions — continuing into IMC, accepting an overloaded helicopter, or skipping a thorough preflight — when they feel pressured to complete the flight. The accident is rarely caused by a single bad decision; it is typically an error chain, each link of which can often be traced back to a pilot who felt pressure to press on.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.S4.001	Your planned reserve is 1.3 hours (78 minutes). Under 14 CFR 91.167, for IFR flight, a helicopter must have enough fuel to complete the flight to the first airport of intended landing, fly from there to the alternate (if required), and then fly for 30 minutes at normal cruising speed. Your 78-minute reserve exceeds the 30-minute IFR reserve requirement, so it is legal. However, always verify your specific aircraft's fuel burn rate against actual fuel on board to confirm.	->	Your planned reserve is 1.3 hours (78 minutes). Under 14 CFR 91.167, for IFR flight, a helicopter must have enough fuel to complete the flight to the first airport of intended landing, fly from there to the alternate (if required), and then fly for 30 minutes at normal cruising speed. Your 78-minute reserve exceeds the 30-minute IFR reserve requirement, so it is legal. However, always verify your specific aircraft's fuel burn rate against actual fuel on board to confirm. (Note: For VFR day flight, 14 CFR 91.151 requires only 20 minutes reserve, so VFR reserves would be even more generous.)
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.S4.004	First, determine fuel used so far. You are halfway through a 1.8-hour planned leg, so 0.9 hours have elapsed, leaving 3.1 hours of fuel. Now calculate the remaining distance. The original remaining distance to destination was half of (1.8 hr × 110 kts) = 99 NM. Add 45 NM for the reroute = 144 NM remaining. At 90 knots groundspeed, that is 144 ÷ 90 = 1.6 hours to destination. Subtract from remaining fuel: 3.1 − 1.6 = 1.5 hours reserve upon arrival. Under 14 CFR 91.167, your IFR reserve must include fuel to fly to an alternate (if required) plus 30 minutes. If no alternate is required, the 1.5-hour reserve is legal and comfortable. However, if an alternate is required, you must verify that the alternate leg plus 30 minutes does not exceed 1.5 hours.	->	This is an IFR flight scenario (indicated by ATC reroute). First, determine fuel used so far. You are halfway through a 1.8-hour planned leg, so 0.9 hours have elapsed, leaving 3.1 hours of fuel. Now calculate the remaining distance. The original remaining distance to destination was half of (1.8 hr × 110 kts) = 99 NM. Add 45 NM for the reroute = 144 NM remaining. At 90 knots groundspeed, that is 144 ÷ 90 = 1.6 hours to destination. Subtract from remaining fuel: 3.1 − 1.6 = 1.5 hours reserve upon arrival. Under 14 CFR 91.167, for IFR flight, you must have fuel to reach an alternate airport (if required) plus 30 minutes. An alternate is required unless the destination weather forecast indicates VFR conditions for a specified period. If an alternate is required, you must verify that the fuel needed to fly to the alternate at your revised groundspeed plus 30 minutes does not exceed 1.5 hours. If the alternate requirement is waived based on destination forecast, your 1.5-hour reserve exceeds the 30-minute minimum and is legal.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.H.S4.006	Original reserve = 3.0 − 2.3 = 0.7 hours (42 minutes). The detour added 0.5 hours of flight time already incurred (or to be incurred), and reduced groundspeed means the remaining distance takes longer. If you are roughly at the midpoint of your original route (about 1.15 hours in), your remaining distance at the original plan was approximately 1.15 hours × 100 kts = 115 NM. At 85 knots, that same 115 NM now takes 115 ÷ 85 = 1.35 hours. Adding the 0.5-hour detour: total remaining = 1.35 + 0.5 = 1.85 hours. Fuel remaining = 3.0 − 1.15 = 1.85 hours. Reserve after arrival = 1.85 − 1.85 = 0 hours. Under 14 CFR 91.151, VFR helicopters must have fuel to fly to the first point of intended landing plus 20 minutes at normal cruise speed. With zero reserve, this is illegal and dangerous. You must divert to the nearest suitable airport immediately to refuel.	->	Original reserve = 3.0 − 2.3 = 0.7 hours (42 minutes). At the 1.15-hour checkpoint, fuel remaining = 3.0 − 1.15 = 1.85 hours. Remaining planned distance = 1.15 hours × 100 kts = 115 NM. At reduced groundspeed of 85 knots, that 115 NM now requires 115 ÷ 85 = 1.35 hours. Adding the 0.5-hour detour: total remaining flight time needed = 1.35 + 0.5 = 1.85 hours. Fuel reserve after arrival = 1.85 − 1.85 = 0 hours. Under 14 CFR 91.151, VFR helicopters must have fuel to fly to the first point of intended landing plus 20 minutes at normal cruise speed. With zero reserve, this violates the regulation. You must declare minimum fuel to ATC and divert to the nearest suitable airport immediately to refuel.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.I.K1.006	Under 14 CFR 91.103, before beginning a flight, the PIC must familiarize themselves with all available information concerning that flight. For any flight, this includes runway lengths at airports of intended use and takeoff and landing distance data. For IFR flights or any flight not in the vicinity of the departure airport, the pilot must additionally review weather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements, alternatives available if the planned flight cannot be completed, and any known ATC delays. For a local hover practice flight, the minimum requirement is familiarity with relevant available information — at a minimum, NOTAMs, weather, and the aircraft's airworthiness status. For a cross-country IFR flight, the pilot must also review the full IFR weather picture, alternate airport requirements, fuel reserves per 14 CFR 91.167 (45 minutes at normal cruise speed after reaching destination for IFR), and applicable SIDs, STARs, and instrument procedures.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.103, before beginning any flight, the PIC must familiarize themselves with all available information concerning that flight. For any flight, this includes runway lengths at airports of intended use and takeoff and landing distance data. For IFR flights or any flight not in the vicinity of the departure airport, the pilot must additionally review weather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements, alternatives available if the planned flight cannot be completed, and any known ATC delays. For a local hover practice flight, the minimum requirement is familiarity with relevant available information — at a minimum, NOTAMs, weather, and the aircraft's airworthiness status. For a cross-country IFR flight, per 14 CFR 91.167, the pilot must ensure fuel is available to reach the first airport of intended landing, then proceed to the alternate airport, and maintain normal cruise speed for 30 minutes after arrival at the alternate airport. The pilot must also review the full IFR weather picture, alternate airport requirements, and applicable SIDs, STARs, and instrument procedures.
