AI_EDIT	HI.III.I.K4.006	No. You should use the helicopter-specific Instrument Rating ACS. The FAA publishes separate ACS documents for different aircraft categories. The helicopter instrument ACS contains tasks, standards, and tolerances that are specific to rotorcraft operations — such as hovering tasks, autorotation considerations, and helicopter-specific approach tolerances — that differ from fixed-wing standards. Using the wrong ACS could leave you unprepared for helicopter-specific tasks the examiner is required to test, and could cause you to prepare for tasks that are irrelevant to the helicopter checkride. Always verify that the ACS you are studying matches both the certificate/rating you are seeking and the aircraft category.	->	No. You should use the helicopter-specific Instrument Rating ACS. The FAA publishes separate ACS documents for different aircraft categories. The helicopter instrument ACS contains tasks, standards, and tolerances that are specific to rotorcraft operations — such as hovering tasks, autorotation considerations, and helicopter-specific approach tolerances — that differ from fixed-wing standards. Using the wrong ACS could leave you unprepared for helicopter-specific tasks the examiner is required to test, and could cause you to prepare for tasks that are irrelevant to the helicopter checkride. Always verify that the ACS you are studying matches both the certificate/rating you are seeking and the aircraft category.
