AI_EDIT	HI.III.J.K1.005	When logging simulator time, the logbook entry must reflect: (1) the date, (2) the type of simulator or FTD used (including the make and model or the FAA approval level — e.g., Level D FFS), (3) the training location or operator, (4) the type of training conducted, (5) the instructor's signature, certificate number, and expiration date. Per 14 CFR 61.51(b)(1), simulator time is logged separately from actual flight time. For instrument rating purposes under 14 CFR 61.65(i), a student may use time in an approved FFS or FTD toward the instrument experience requirements — but the amount creditable depends on the level of the device. Up to 20 hours of instrument time may be credited if the device is used with an authorized instructor. The instructor should also log the session in their own records per 14 CFR 61.189(a).	->	When logging simulator time, the logbook entry must reflect: (1) the date, (2) the type of simulator or FTD used (including the make and model or the FAA approval level — e.g., Level D FFS), (3) the training location or operator, (4) the type of training conducted, (5) the instructor's signature, certificate number, and expiration date. Per 14 CFR 61.51(b)(1), simulator time is logged separately from actual flight time. For instrument rating purposes under 14 CFR 61.65, a student may use time in an approved FFS or FTD toward instrument experience requirements, with the amount creditable depending on the device level and rating type. Up to 20 hours of instrument time may be credited in an approved device when used with an authorized instructor. The instructor should also log the session in their own records per 14 CFR 61.189(a).
AI_EDIT	HI.III.J.K1.008	This is a nuanced logging situation. You, as the CFII providing instruction, may log flight instructor time for the entire training flight. For instrument time specifically: during the portion the student flew under the hood, you were serving as the required safety pilot per 14 CFR 91.109(c). A safety pilot serving as required crew may log that time as second-in-command (SIC) time per 14 CFR 61.51(f)(2). However, you may only log it as instrument time if you were also operating the aircraft solely by reference to instruments — which would not be the case if you were the visual safety pilot while the student was under the hood. The student may log the time under the hood as simulated instrument time. For the portion you flew under the hood while the student acted as safety pilot, you may log that as simulated instrument time for your own currency. The key distinction is that logging instrument flight time requires actually manipulating the controls while on instruments — not merely observing or acting as safety pilot.	->	This is a nuanced logging situation. You, as the CFII providing instruction, may log flight instructor time for the entire training flight. For instrument time specifically: during the portion the student flew under the hood, you were serving as the required safety pilot per 14 CFR 91.109(c). A safety pilot serving as required crew may log that time as second-in-command (SIC) time per 14 CFR 61.51(f)(2). However, you may not log it as instrument time because you were the visual safety pilot while the student manipulated the controls on instruments — only the student (who was manipulating the controls) may log that period as simulated instrument flight time. You cannot simultaneously log the same flight time as both safety pilot and instrument pilot. For the portion you flew under the hood while the student acted as safety pilot, you may log that as simulated instrument time for your own currency. The key principle is that only the pilot actually manipulating the controls on instruments during any given period may log instrument time for that period.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.J.K2.003	The solo endorsement must be recorded in the student pilot's logbook by the certifying flight instructor. Per 14 CFR 61.87(n), the endorsement for solo flight in a specific make and model helicopter does not expire, but the student pilot certificate itself is valid for 60 calendar months from the date of issuance for applicants who are at least 14 years old (the minimum age to solo a helicopter). However, 14 CFR 61.87(p) requires an additional endorsement from a flight instructor within the 90-day period preceding the solo flight for the student to exercise solo privileges.	->	The solo endorsement must be recorded in the student pilot's logbook by a certificated flight instructor. Per 14 CFR 61.87(n), the solo endorsement for a specific make and model helicopter does not expire once entered in the logbook. However, to actually exercise solo privileges, 14 CFR 61.87(p) requires a separate endorsement from a flight instructor within the 90-calendar-day period immediately preceding the solo flight. Additionally, the student pilot certificate itself is valid for 60 calendar months from issuance (per 14 CFR 61.19).
AI_EDIT	HI.III.J.K3.001	A logbook endorsement is a written, signed statement from a certificated flight instructor recorded in a pilot's logbook that certifies the pilot has received training in, and is proficient in, a specific maneuver or operation. For touchdown autorotations, an endorsement is required because the maneuver carries significant risk of aircraft damage or injury, and the FAA requires an instructor to certify the pilot has received proper instruction before performing it solo. The endorsement serves as a regulatory safeguard and accountability record.	->	A logbook endorsement is a written, signed statement from a certificated flight instructor recorded in a pilot's logbook that certifies the pilot has received training in, and is proficient in, a specific maneuver or operation. Touchdown autorotations are an emergency procedure; 14 CFR 61.87(d) requires an instructor to train the student in emergency procedures, and 61.87(e) requires the instructor to endorse that the student is proficient before solo flight. While the regulation addresses emergency procedures generically, many flight schools and instructors impose additional specific endorsements for touchdown autorotations as a prudent risk-management practice, given the maneuver's potential for aircraft damage or injury.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.J.K3.003	Before a student pilot may perform a solo touchdown autorotation, a certificated flight instructor must provide a logbook endorsement certifying that the student has received training in and is proficient in that specific maneuver. This is required under 14 CFR 61.87, which lists the maneuvers an instructor must train and endorse a student to perform prior to solo flight, including those involving simulated emergency procedures such as autorotations. The endorsing instructor must hold at least a flight instructor certificate with a helicopter rating.	->	Before a student pilot may perform a solo touchdown autorotation, a certificated flight instructor must train the student in emergency procedures (which include autorotations) per 14 CFR 61.87(d), and then endorse in the logbook that the student is proficient in those maneuvers per 14 CFR 61.87(e). The endorsing instructor must hold at least a flight instructor certificate with a helicopter rating per 14 CFR 61.195. Note: the regulation addresses emergency procedures as a category; individual schools or instructors may require a separate endorsement specifically for touchdown autorotations as best practice, though this is not explicitly mandated by 61.87.
AI_EDIT	HI.III.J.K3.005	The instructor must ensure the student has been trained in and demonstrated proficiency in all maneuvers required under 14 CFR 61.87 before endorsing for solo flight. For helicopter students, the endorsement must specifically address each maneuver the student is authorized to perform solo. If touchdown autorotations are to be included in solo operations, the instructor must have trained the student in that specific maneuver and found the student proficient before endorsing it. It would be inappropriate and potentially unsafe — and likely a regulatory violation — to endorse a student for solo autorotation practice if training was limited only to power recovery autorotations. The instructor's endorsement carries legal and safety accountability.	->	The instructor must ensure the student has been trained in and demonstrated proficiency in all maneuvers required under 14 CFR 61.87(d) and (e) before endorsing for solo flight. For helicopter students, the endorsement must specifically address each maneuver the student is authorized to perform solo, and any limitations. If touchdown autorotations are to be included in solo operations, the instructor must have trained the student in that specific maneuver and found the student proficient before endorsing it. It would be inappropriate and potentially unsafe—and likely a regulatory violation—to endorse a student for solo autorotation practice if training was limited only to power recovery autorotations. The instructor's solo endorsement under 61.87(e) carries legal and safety accountability, and the instructor may appropriately restrict the endorsement to exclude touchdown autorotations if the student has not demonstrated proficiency in them.
