AI_EDIT	HI.IV.A.K2.006	Under 14 CFR 61.113(d), a private pilot may conduct charitable flights on behalf of a charitable organization without violating the compensation prohibition, provided the charitable organization holds a letter of authorization from the FAA under Part 91.501 or a similar exemption. Alternatively, under the Angel Flight/charity flight provisions, the pilot may not receive compensation. The pilot must ensure: the flight is conducted under Part 91, the aircraft is properly certificated, the pilot meets all currency requirements (including instrument if flight into IMC is planned), and the charitable organization has proper FAA authorization if required. The pilot cannot be paid for the flight and the aircraft operating costs must be handled appropriately.	->	Under 14 CFR 61.113(d), a private pilot may conduct charitable flights without compensation when flying on behalf of charitable, educational, or scientific organizations, provided the organization meets specific criteria. Key requirements: (1) the pilot must not receive compensation; (2) the charitable organization may contribute to direct operating costs of the aircraft; (3) the flight must comply with all Part 91 requirements; (4) the aircraft must be properly certificated and airworthy; (5) the pilot must maintain currency and medical certification. No formal FAA 'letter of authorization' is required for basic charitable flights under 61.113(d), though certain exemptions or special authorizations may apply to specific charitable operations. The pilot must verify the charitable organization qualifies under the rule and ensure all flight operations remain within Part 91 standards.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.A.K3.005	To operate under BasicMed (14 CFR Part 68 / 61.113(i)), the pilot must: (1) have held a valid FAA medical certificate at any time after July 14, 2006; (2) complete a medical education course every 24 calendar months; (3) receive a physical examination from a state-licensed physician every 48 calendar months using the CMEC form. Operational limitations for the helicopter include: maximum certificated takeoff weight of 12,500 lbs; no more than 6 occupants (pilot plus 5 passengers); maximum altitude of 18,000 ft MSL; maximum airspeed of 250 KIAS; operations must remain within the United States. The pilot cannot fly for compensation or hire under BasicMed, cannot act as PIC of a helicopter requiring a type rating in operations requiring an ATP, and the helicopter must be authorized to carry no more than 6 occupants.	->	To operate under BasicMed (14 CFR Part 68 / 61.113(i)), the pilot must: (1) have held a valid FAA medical certificate at any time after July 14, 2006; (2) complete a medical education course every 24 calendar months; (3) receive a physical examination from a state-licensed physician every 48 calendar months using the CMEC form. Operational limitations for helicopter operations include: maximum certificated takeoff weight of 12,500 lbs; no more than 6 occupants total (pilot plus up to 5 additional occupants); maximum altitude of 18,000 ft MSL; maximum airspeed of 250 KIAS; operations must remain within the United States; no distance limitation. The pilot cannot fly for compensation or hire under BasicMed, cannot act as PIC of a helicopter requiring a type rating in operations requiring an ATP, and the helicopter must be authorized to carry no more than 6 occupants.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.A.K3.007	Several BasicMed restrictions are directly relevant here. First, the weight is within limits — 10,000 lbs is below the 12,500 lb maximum. Second, 4 passengers plus the pilot equals 5 occupants, which is within the 6-occupant maximum. Third, there is no prohibition on turbine-powered aircraft under BasicMed. However, the critical issue is the destination: BasicMed operations must remain within the United States. Alaska is a U.S. state, so the destination itself is not the problem — but flying from the contiguous U.S. to Alaska would require passing over or near Canadian airspace or flying over open ocean depending on the route. Operations must remain within the United States; transiting Canadian airspace or flying internationally would disqualify BasicMed use. If an all-U.S. routing exists (e.g., staying entirely over U.S. territory and waters), the flight may be permissible, but the pilot must verify the entire route remains within U.S. airspace.	->	This flight is permissible under BasicMed. All weight, occupant, and destination requirements are met. The weight of 10,000 lbs is below the 12,500 lb maximum; 5 occupants (4 passengers plus pilot) is within the 6-occupant limit; there is no prohibition on turbine-powered aircraft under BasicMed; and Alaska is part of the United States, so the flight remains within U.S. territory. BasicMed does not restrict distance or require routing to the contiguous 48 states—it requires operations to remain within the United States, which includes all 50 states.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.A.K4.006	A student pilot conducting a solo cross-country flight must have in their possession: (1) a valid student pilot certificate, (2) a valid medical certificate (at minimum third-class, since the student certificate and medical are combined on the same document for student pilots issued before April 2016; or separate documents if issued after that date under 14 CFR 61.83), (3) a valid photo ID per 14 CFR 61.3(a)(2), and (4) a solo flight endorsement from a certificated flight instructor in their logbook for the specific make and model of helicopter per 14 CFR 61.87, as well as a cross-country endorsement for the specific flight per 14 CFR 61.93. The logbook endorsements are not 'carried' per se but must be verifiable and current.	->	A student pilot conducting a solo cross-country flight must have in their possession: (1) a valid student pilot certificate, (2) a valid third-class medical certificate (or comply with BasicMed if eligible per 14 CFR Part 68), (3) a valid photo ID per 14 CFR 61.3(a)(2), and (4) current logbook entries documenting endorsements for solo flight in that helicopter make/model and for the specific cross-country flight per 14 CFR 61.87 and 14 CFR 61.93. The logbook must be accessible to verify current endorsements.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.A.K4.007	Under 14 CFR Part 68 and 14 CFR 61.113(i), a pilot operating under BasicMed must be able to demonstrate: (1) their valid pilot certificate (in possession per 14 CFR 61.3), (2) a valid U.S. driver's license as proof of medical fitness in lieu of an FAA medical certificate — this must be in their possession, (3) documentation that they have completed the aeromedical education course within the preceding 24 calendar months (the online course completion certificate — pilots are advised to carry or have accessible proof of completion), and (4) documentation that they have received a physical examination from a state-licensed physician within the preceding 48 calendar months and that the physician completed the required CMEC (Comprehensive Medical Examination Checklist), which is maintained by the pilot (not submitted to the FAA). The CMEC and course completion record should be kept in the pilot's logbook or personal records. The pilot must also have held a valid FAA medical certificate issued on or after July 14, 2006 at some point in their history.	->	Under 14 CFR Part 68, a pilot operating under BasicMed must be able to demonstrate: (1) a valid pilot certificate in possession, (2) a valid U.S. driver's license (required proof of medical fitness in lieu of an FAA medical certificate), (3) completion of an FAA-approved aeromedical education course within the preceding 24 calendar months, and (4) a medical examination by a state-licensed physician within the preceding 48 calendar months, with CMEC documentation maintained by the pilot. The pilot must have held a valid FAA medical certificate at some point on or after July 14, 2006 (one-time eligibility requirement). BasicMed permits operation of any aircraft (including turbine helicopters) regardless of weight or distance, provided they remain within U.S. airspace.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.A.R1.003	The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that accident rates correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours, and that accidents decrease significantly after approximately 100 hours in type. This is directly relevant to the proficiency versus currency distinction — a pilot who is legally current but has very few hours in a particular helicopter model may lack the type-specific proficiency needed to operate safely, especially in demanding conditions.	->	The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook emphasizes that accident rates correlate strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model rather than total flight hours alone. While exact proficiency thresholds vary by mission, aircraft type, training quality, and operator, research and accident data show that pilot proficiency and accident risk improve significantly during the first 50–200 hours in type. This directly illustrates the distinction between legal currency and actual proficiency — a pilot who meets minimum currency requirements but has very few hours in a particular helicopter model may lack the type-specific proficiency needed to operate safely, especially in demanding conditions or emergencies like autorotation.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.A.S1.006	You would explain that rotor RPM in autorotation is influenced by the weight of the helicopter and the energy stored in the rotor system. Operating below minimum weight means there is less mass and gravitational force driving rotor energy storage, which can prevent the pilot from achieving the desired rotor RPM during autorotation — reducing their ability to flare and cushion landing effectively. Operating above maximum weight degrades climb performance, increases the descent rate in autorotation, and stresses the airframe beyond its certified limits. The connection to preflight qualification is this: the PIC must verify weight and balance before every flight under 14 CFR 91.9, and an incorrect calculation or overlooked cargo item can shift the aircraft outside safe operating limits before the first hover. Instruct the student to always compute, not estimate, and to account for fuel burn and CG movement throughout the planned flight.	->	You would explain that minimum weight exists to ensure adequate control effectiveness and predictable autorotational characteristics. Operating below minimum weight can reduce control authority — particularly in pitch and roll — and may degrade the helicopter's ability to respond to control inputs needed for a stabilized autorotational descent and flare. Weight directly affects the descent rate and energy dissipation in autorotation; below minimum weight the helicopter may descend too fast for a safe landing, or control inputs may be ineffective due to reduced aerodynamic authority. Operating above maximum weight conversely stresses the airframe, increases descent rate beyond design limits, and demands more rotor energy than the system can reliably deliver in autorotation. The connection to preflight qualification is this: the PIC must verify weight and balance before every flight under 14 CFR 91.9, and an incorrect calculation or overlooked cargo item can shift the aircraft outside safe operating limits before the first hover. Instruct the student to always compute, not estimate, and to account for fuel burn and CG movement throughout the planned flight.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.B.K1.008	The owner is incorrect. While the Airworthiness Certificate does not expire on its own, a valid and current registration certificate is a separate and independently required document under 14 CFR 91.203(a)(2). Operating without a valid registration certificate is a violation of 14 CFR 91.203 regardless of whether the airworthiness certificate is valid. The aircraft registration must be renewed per 14 CFR Part 47 — registration certificates now have a three-year expiration period under FAA rules updated in 2010. An expired registration makes the aircraft legally non-compliant for flight operations even if the aircraft is mechanically sound and the airworthiness certificate is current. To resolve this, the owner must renew the registration through the FAA Civil Aviation Registry. The renewal can be initiated online at FAA.gov. Until renewed, the aircraft should not be flown. Note that if the aircraft were to be flown for an emergency or ferry situation, a Special Flight Permit would still require a valid registration as part of the process.	->	The owner is incorrect. While the Airworthiness Certificate does not expire on its own, a valid and current registration certificate is a separate and independently required document under 14 CFR 91.203(a)(2). Operating without a valid registration certificate violates 14 CFR 91.203 regardless of whether the airworthiness certificate is valid. Aircraft registration certificates issued by the FAA Civil Aviation Registry have a 3-year validity period and must be renewed per 14 CFR Part 47. An expired registration makes the aircraft legally non-compliant for flight operations even if the aircraft is mechanically sound and the airworthiness certificate is current. To resolve this, the owner must renew the registration through the FAA Civil Aviation Registry using FAA Form 8050-1 or the online renewal process at FAA.gov. Until renewed, the aircraft should not be flown. Even in emergencies, a Special Flight Permit requires a valid registration as a prerequisite.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.B.K2.008	This is a gray area that requires careful analysis. 14 CFR 43.3(g) states the pilot must 'own or operate' the aircraft. A partner in an LLC that owns the aircraft may qualify as an 'operator' for preventive maintenance purposes, but the FAA has not issued a universal bright-line rule for all LLC structures. If the LLC is structured such that each partner has legitimate operational authority over the aircraft and is listed as an operator, the FAA has generally accepted that the partner-pilots may perform preventive maintenance. However, if the LLC holds the aircraft as a business asset and the pilots are more akin to renters or lessees, the 'own or operate' requirement may not be met. The safest course is to obtain a legal interpretation from the FAA Office of Chief Counsel or ensure the operating agreement clearly establishes each partner as an operator with a bona fide ownership interest. The commercial certificate itself imposes no additional restriction for this purpose — 43.3(g) applies to any pilot certificated under Part 61, including commercial.	->	This is a gray area that requires careful analysis. 14 CFR 43.3(g) states the pilot must 'own or operate' the aircraft. A partner in an LLC that owns the aircraft may qualify as an 'operator' for preventive maintenance purposes, but the FAA has not issued a universal bright-line rule for all LLC structures. If the LLC is structured such that each partner has legitimate operational authority over the aircraft and is listed as an operator, the FAA has generally accepted that the partner-pilots may perform preventive maintenance. However, if the LLC holds the aircraft as a business asset and the pilots are more akin to renters or lessees, the 'own or operate' requirement may not be met. The safest course is to obtain a legal interpretation from the FAA Office of Chief Counsel or ensure the operating agreement clearly establishes each partner as an operator with a bona fide ownership interest. The commercial certificate itself imposes no additional restriction for this purpose — 43.3(g) applies to any pilot certificated under Part 61, including commercial. [Regulatory reference: 14 CFR 43.3(g); FAA Legal Interpretations regarding operator definitions under 43.3(g).]
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.B.K3.002	An FAA-approved MEL is treated as a Supplemental Type Certificate (STC) for that specific aircraft. It is approved by the FAA and is specific to the individual helicopter — identified by registration number and serial number. Once approved, the MEL authorizes flight with certain specified equipment inoperative, essentially modifying the aircraft's type certificate for that individual airframe. The operator must comply with the MEL's conditions and limitations exactly as written.	->	An FAA-approved MEL is an alternative operating standard approved by the FAA under 14 CFR 91.213(a) that is specific to the individual helicopter (identified by registration number and serial number). The MEL authorizes flight with certain specified equipment inoperative, provided all conditions and limitations in the MEL are met. While an MEL functions as an approval mechanism at the individual aircraft level, it is not a Supplemental Type Certificate; rather, it establishes regulatory authorization for operation without modifying the aircraft's type certificate itself. The operator must comply with the MEL's conditions and limitations exactly as written.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.B.K4.004	First, review the FAA-issued operating limitations document specific to that aircraft — these limitations are unique to the individual helicopter and may explicitly prohibit or restrict flight over populated areas beyond the default 14 CFR 91.319 prohibition. Under 91.319(a)(1), flight over densely populated areas is prohibited; however, 'densely populated' is not numerically defined in the regulation, so you must use reasonable judgment and consult the specific operating limitations. The FAA may have granted an exception or defined geographic areas in the limitations document. If the limitations are silent or ambiguous, contact the issuing FSDO for clarification before flight. You should not assume the flight is permissible simply because the town is small. Additionally, verify the aircraft's maintenance logbooks reflect compliance with the condition inspection requirements applicable to experimental aircraft (14 CFR 91.409(e)) and that all required instruments and equipment are functional.	->	First, review the FAA-issued operating limitations document specific to that aircraft — these limitations are unique to the individual helicopter and may explicitly prohibit or restrict flight over populated areas. Under 14 CFR 91.319(a)(1), flight over densely populated areas is prohibited; 'densely populated' is not numerically defined in the regulation, but FAA guidance indicates this typically refers to large metropolitan areas with significantly higher population density and concentration. A town of 8,000 people would generally not meet this threshold unless the operating limitations explicitly restrict flight over such areas. Consult the specific operating limitations document to confirm permissibility, and contact the issuing FSDO if the limitations are ambiguous. Additionally, verify the aircraft's maintenance logbooks reflect compliance with the condition inspection requirements applicable to experimental aircraft (14 CFR 91.409(e)) and that all required instruments and equipment are functional.
