AI_EDIT	HI.IV.C.S1.008	There is no specific regulatory requirement that mandates obtaining a second briefing within a set time interval — 14 CFR 91.103 requires only that the pilot in command become familiar with all available information appropriate to the flight before departure. However, there is a strong operational and ADM argument for updating the briefing. Weather can change significantly in one to two hours, especially in dynamic conditions or near frontal systems. If the original briefing showed marginal IFR or any adverse condition trends, an updated check of METARs, PIREPs, and ATIS (if available at departure) is prudent and consistent with sound ADM. Operationally, a briefing becomes insufficient when conditions have changed materially from what was briefed — the pilot cannot know this without updating. The FAA's guidance in the AIM recommends updating weather information close to departure time. For IFR operations, checking departure ATIS, reviewing updated METARs and any new PIREPs immediately before departure is best practice and directly supports the 'adequate weather briefing' standard. From an examiner perspective: a certificated helicopter IFR pilot who departs based solely on a two-hour-old briefing without any update check has not met the intent of 14 CFR 91.103 in a dynamic weather environment.	->	While 14 CFR 91.103 does not specify a mandatory time interval for re-briefing, the regulation requires the PIC to become familiar with all available information appropriate to the flight—reasonably interpreted to mean current information at the time of departure, not information from one to two hours prior. For IFR operations under 14 CFR 91.167, this obligation is strengthened: the pilot must verify immediately before departure that the destination and alternate airports still meet their weather minimums. Weather can change significantly in one to two hours, especially in dynamic or frontal conditions. An updated check of METARs, PIREPs, ATIS (if available), and any new AIRMETs immediately before departure is operationally required and legally consistent with 91.103. Departing on a briefing older than one to two hours without any update check does not meet the intent of 'adequate weather briefing' in a dynamic environment and represents poor ADM.
AI_EDIT	HI.IV.C.S2.002	High temperature at a high field elevation produces a density altitude significantly above field elevation. Using the rule of thumb, density altitude increases approximately 120 feet for every degree Celsius above standard temperature. Standard temperature at 4,500 feet MSL is approximately 7°C; the actual temperature is 38°C — a deviation of 31°C above standard. That adds roughly 3,720 feet to density altitude, placing density altitude near 8,200 feet MSL. At this density altitude, the helicopter will have reduced engine power output, degraded rotor lift efficiency, and will require greater takeoff power. Hover ceiling may be exceeded, and you must verify performance charts to confirm whether out-of-ground-effect hover is achievable with your gross weight and configuration.	->	High temperature at a high field elevation produces a density altitude significantly above field elevation. Using the rule of thumb, density altitude increases approximately 120 feet for every degree Celsius above standard temperature. Standard temperature at 4,500 feet MSL is approximately 5°C (using the 2°C per 1,000-foot lapse rate from sea level standard of 15°C); the actual temperature is 38°C — a deviation of 33°C above standard. That adds roughly 3,960 feet to density altitude, placing density altitude near 8,460 feet MSL. At this density altitude, the helicopter will have reduced engine power output, degraded rotor lift efficiency, and will require greater takeoff power. Hover ceiling may be exceeded, and you must verify performance charts to confirm whether out-of-ground-effect hover is achievable with your gross weight and configuration.
