AI_EDIT	HI.V.A.K3.008	This is an excellent edge-case question that tests instrument interpretation depth. The desired outcome for a timed turn includes rolling out within ±10 degrees of the target heading as shown on the heading indicator, which should have been set against the magnetic compass during straight-and-level flight prior to the turn. If the two instruments disagree at rollout, the completion standard is initially evaluated against the heading indicator because it is the primary bank instrument during turns and is not subject to the magnetic dip errors and acceleration/deceleration errors that affect the compass in turns. However, the student must understand that the magnetic compass is the only certified, legally required heading reference — the heading indicator has no north-seeking ability and precesses over time. A disagreement at rollout should prompt the student to: (1) note whether the heading indicator has precessed from its last setting; (2) re-evaluate the compass reading only after stabilizing in straight-and-level flight, because compass errors in turns — particularly the northerly turning error and the ANDS/UNOS rules for compass turns — make in-turn compass readings unreliable. This scenario directly reveals whether the student understands that each instrument has a specific role in the cross-check and that no single instrument is authoritative in all phases of flight.	->	ANDS stands for: Accelerate North, Decelerate South — referring to acceleration/deceleration errors. UNOS stands for: Undershoot North, Overshoot South — referring to the northerly turning error caused by magnetic dip. When turning to a southerly heading, the pilot should overshoot the desired heading before rolling out. At 35 degrees north latitude, the northerly turning error is approximately 35 degrees. However, the exact rollout point also depends on bank angle and rollout lead rate. As a general rule, subtract the latitude from the target heading when turning to south; at 35°N latitude turning right to 180°, the pilot would begin rollout when the compass shows approximately 145–155° (the range accounts for variations in rollout lead), expecting the compass to continue swinging toward 180° as wings level. This should be briefed as: 'Begin rollout when compass indicates approximately [calculated overshoot heading based on latitude and rollout lead], and expect the compass to swing toward the assigned heading as you roll wings level.' This precision in the briefed completion standard demonstrates to the student that desired outcomes are maneuver-specific, aircraft-specific, and location-specific.
AI_EDIT	HI.V.A.R1.005	Using 'probably' signals that the outcome is uncertain and that a safer or more reliable option may exist. The FAA's Trescott Tip from the ADM chapter specifically warns against this — if an action will only 'probably' work, the pilot or instructor should choose an option known to work. In the context of maneuver selection, if there is doubt about whether the student is ready to perform a specific instrument maneuver to standards, the instructor should select a more foundational maneuver with a predictable successful outcome, rather than proceeding with an uncertain one. Replace 'probably' with a concrete go/no-go decision criterion.	->	Using 'probably' signals that the outcome is uncertain and that a safer or more reliable option may exist. FAA risk management guidance emphasizes that if an action will only 'probably' work, the pilot or instructor should choose an option known to work with predictable success. In the context of maneuver selection, if there is doubt about whether the student is ready to perform a specific instrument maneuver to standards, the instructor should select a more foundational maneuver with a demonstrable successful outcome, rather than proceeding with an uncertain one. Replace 'probably' with a concrete go/no-go decision criterion based on objective performance standards or decision rules.
AI_EDIT	HI.VI.A.K4.005	T-routes (low-altitude RNAV routes) require RNAV navigation capability with equipment that meets specific certification standards. If your helicopter's navigation system is not certified to the required standard (TSO-C129/C145/C146 as applicable), you cannot legally file or fly a T-route under IFR. This means you must select an alternate routing using conventional airways (Victor airways or direct routing with appropriate NAVAID coverage). You should confirm your aircraft's avionics certification against the Airplane/Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM/AFM) supplement before planning any RNAV route. Attempting to fly a T-route without approved equipment is a regulatory violation under 14 CFR 91.205 and 91.409 as applied to navigation equipment.	->	T-routes (low-altitude RNAV routes) require navigation equipment certified to TSO-C129 or an equivalent RNAV standard approved in your aircraft's AFM RNAV supplement. If your helicopter's navigation system is not approved for RNAV per the AFM, you cannot legally file or fly a T-route under IFR. You must select an alternate routing using conventional airways (Victor airways) or direct routing with appropriate NAVAID coverage, provided your equipment meets the requirements for that method. Before planning any RNAV route, confirm your aircraft's avionics certification against the current Airplane/Rotorcraft Flight Manual (AFM) supplement. Attempting to fly a T-route without approved equipment is a regulatory violation under 14 CFR 91.205 and 91.409.
