AI_EDIT	HI.VI.A.S2.004	First, verify whether the helicopter has an FAA-approved Minimum Equipment List (MEL) by checking for it in the aircraft documents — an MEL is a specific FAA-approved document for that tail number, not a generic list. MELs for Part 91 operations require FAA approval through a Letter of Authorization (LOA). If no approved MEL exists, you must determine whether the fuel quantity indicator is required equipment under 14 CFR 91.205, the Kinds of Operations Equipment List (KOEL) in the RFM, or the aircraft's type certificate data sheet. For VFR day flight, 14 CFR 91.205(b) does not specifically list a fuel quantity indicator, but the RFM KOEL may require it. If the equipment is required by any of those sources and no approved MEL deferral exists, the aircraft is not airworthy. If it is not required by any of those sources, the instrument can be placarded inoperative under 14 CFR 91.213(d) and the aircraft may be legal. Either way, you must also operationally verify fuel quantity by visual inspection or dipping the tanks.	->	First, verify whether the helicopter has an FAA-approved Minimum Equipment List (MEL) by checking the aircraft documents. An MEL is aircraft-specific and must be FAA-approved, either as an integral part of the RFM or via a separate Letter of Authorization (LOA). If an approved MEL exists and the fuel quantity gauge is listed with an acceptable deferral, the deferral is valid. If no approved MEL exists, you must determine whether the fuel quantity indicator is required by: (1) 14 CFR 91.205 equipment list for your class of aircraft and operation, (2) the RFM KOEL (Kinds of Operations Equipment List), or (3) the aircraft's Type Certificate Data Sheet. For VFR day flight in most helicopters, 14 CFR 91.205 does not mandate a fuel quantity indicator, but the RFM KOEL may. If the equipment is required by any of these sources and no approved MEL deferral exists, the aircraft is not airworthy and cannot legally fly. If the equipment is 