AI_EDIT	HI.VI.B.K2.008	Not fully. FAA guidance on hot refueling in the Helicopter Flying Handbook is specific: the pilot must remain at the flight controls and be ready to immediately shut down and egress during hot refueling — that is a safety-critical constraint. However, the PIC's overall responsibility for airworthiness under 14 CFR 91.7 means that delegating checklist completion without any verification or oversight creates a gap in the safety net. In multi-crew operations, the concept of challenge-and-response requires both crewmembers to participate in verification of critical items, not just completion by one. A co-pilot calling items done while the PIC monitors controls is an accepted crew coordination practice — but the PIC should at minimum be receiving verbal confirmation of each item and be able to cross-check critical items visible from the left seat. Simply accepting 'it's done' without any crew resource management structure does not meet the FAA's intent for checklist use as a verification tool.	->	Not in compliance with proper checklist discipline. As PIC, you are responsible under 14 CFR 91.7 for the airworthiness of the aircraft. Accepting checklist completion from a crew member without any verification or monitoring—particularly for critical items—delegates a non-delegable responsibility. During hot refueling, the PIC remaining at controls is an operational safety constraint, but this does not excuse the PIC from verifying checklist items or implementing challenge-and-response crew coordination. The PIC cannot blindly accept 'it's done' without being able to account for each critical item; this violates both the letter of 91.7 (PIC responsibility for airworthiness) and FAA guidance on checklist discipline as a verification tool, not merely a completion task.
AI_EDIT	HI.VI.B.K3.004	Operating with an expired database is not automatically illegal under Part 91, but you may not use the GPS as the primary navigation source for an instrument approach if the database is expired unless you cross-check all procedure data against current official sources and determine it is still valid. Most GPS AFM supplements and TSO requirements require a current database for IFR approaches. Practically speaking, the safest and most defensible course of action is to update the database before flight. Alternatively, if the airport has a conventional approach (VOR, ILS) and your helicopter is equipped, you could use that instead. Flying the RNAV approach with an expired database that has not been verified against current charts would not meet the requirement for current and appropriate navigation data.	->	You should not conduct this approach with an expired database. Although 14 CFR Part 91 does not explicitly address database currency, most GPS AFM supplements and TSO-C129/-C196 certification standards mandate current databases as a requirement for IFR approach operations. Using an expired database would violate your AFM operating limitations and fail to meet the preflight planning and airworthiness standards of 14 CFR 91.103. Your options are: (1) update the database before flight, or (2) use an alternative conventional approach (VOR, ILS) if available. Flying the RNAV approach with an expired, unverified database is not compliant with your equipment certification or preflight duty.
AI_EDIT	HI.VI.B.K4.005	Potential hazards include: (1) a shift in center of gravity toward the aft limit, which in a helicopter can reduce cyclic authority and make the aircraft pitch nose-high; (2) the bag could slide into the tail boom area or block access to emergency exits; (3) loose items may interfere with control linkages in the aft section depending on helicopter type. Prevention should occur during the passenger briefing: the PIC is responsible for briefing passengers on proper stowage and securing of all personal items before flight. Loose items must be placed in approved storage areas or secured with tie-downs. The FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 8 emphasizes that passenger briefings and pre-departure checks of the cabin are PIC responsibilities.	->	Potential hazards include: (1) a shift in center of gravity toward the aft limit, which in a helicopter can reduce cyclic authority and make the aircraft pitch nose-high; (2) the bag could slide into the tail boom area or block access to emergency exits; (3) loose items may interfere with control linkages in the aft section depending on helicopter type. Prevention should occur during the passenger briefing: the PIC is responsible for briefing passengers on proper stowage and securing of all personal items before flight. Loose items must be placed in approved storage areas or secured with tie-downs. The FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 8 emphasizes that passenger briefings and pre-departure checks of the cabin are PIC responsibilities under 14 CFR 91.7 (general PIC responsibility and authority).
