AI_EDIT	HI.VI.B.S2.005	The emergency procedures portion of the briefing should cover: (1) Brace position — on PIC command, secure loose items, tighten shoulder harness, and assume a braced position with hands protecting the face; (2) Do not attempt to exit the helicopter while the rotors are still turning unless there is fire or immediate danger — wait for PIC instruction; (3) Door operation — demonstrate how to open the door from inside, including any door jettison or emergency release if equipped; (4) After landing in an emergency, exit away from the rotor arc and move to a safe distance (at least 100 feet) from the helicopter in case of fire or fuel leak; (5) If ditching over water, wait until rotor stops before attempting exit to avoid being struck by rotor blades; (6) Location of any emergency equipment (ELT, fire extinguisher, first aid kit) if applicable.	->	The emergency procedures portion of the briefing should cover: (1) Brace position — on PIC command, secure loose items, tighten shoulder harness, and assume a braced position with hands protecting the face; (2) Do not attempt to exit the helicopter while the rotors are still turning unless there is fire or immediate danger — wait for PIC instruction; (3) Door operation — demonstrate how to open the door from inside, including any door jettison or emergency release if equipped; (4) After landing in an emergency, exit away from the rotor arc and move to a safe distance (at least 100 feet) from the helicopter in case of fire or fuel leak; (5) If ditching over water, remain in the aircraft until rotor blades stop turning due to water drag — typically within 30–60 seconds — before attempting exit to avoid being struck by rotor blades; (6) Location of any emergency equipment (ELT, fire extinguisher, first aid kit) if applicable. This briefing is based on FAA-H-8083-21B guidance; 14 CFR 91.107 specifically mandates seatbelt briefing, while emergency egress procedures are best practices per the handbook.
AI_EDIT	HI.VI.B.S2.006	During hot refueling, the passenger briefing must be significantly expanded. Passengers must be instructed to: (1) Remain seated with safety belts fastened during the entire refueling operation, or if they must exit, to stay clear of the refueling area and tail rotor; (2) Not smoke or use any open flame within the refueling area; (3) Understand that the rotor system remains turning at flight RPM — standard rotor blade avoidance rules apply with heightened urgency; (4) Exit from the front or designated safe side only, crouching low; (5) Be aware that the pilot will remain at the flight controls throughout and is ready to immediately shut down if a hazard develops. The FAA specifies that during hot refueling the pilot must remain at the controls, which means the pilot's ability to personally escort passengers is limited — the briefing before shutdown must be thorough.	->	During hot refueling, the passenger briefing must be significantly expanded. Passengers must be instructed to: (1) Remain seated with safety belts fastened during the entire refueling operation, or if they must exit, to stay clear of the refueling area and tail rotor; (2) Not smoke or use any open flame within the refueling area; (3) Understand that the rotor system remains turning at flight RPM — standard rotor blade avoidance rules apply with heightened urgency; (4) Exit from the front or designated safe side only, crouching low; (5) Be aware that the pilot will remain at the flight controls throughout and is ready to immediately shut down if a hazard develops. Per FAA-H-8083-21B, the pilot must remain at the controls during hot refueling; ground personnel (FBO staff, lineman, or other crew) should conduct the passenger briefing and personally escort passengers clear of the refueling and rotor hazard areas.
