AI_EDIT	HI.VI.C.K1.002	The rotor blades should be positioned perpendicular to the fuselage before engine start and rotor engagement. This is to ensure that if tie-downs or blade restraints were attached, they are removed and that blades are free to rotate without striking ground personnel or obstacles. Starting with a blade restraint attached can cause catastrophic rotor damage. Perpendicular positioning also helps the pilot verify the blades are clear of the tail boom before engagement.	->	Before rotor engagement, the pilot must verify that rotor blade restraints and tie-downs have been removed and that the blades are positioned as specified in the aircraft's POH/RFM. Many helicopters position blades perpendicular to the fuselage (lateral alignment: one blade forward, one aft) as a standard practice, but the exact position requirement varies by aircraft type. The key safety objectives are ensuring no external restraints remain attached, confirming proper weight distribution to avoid asymmetric rotor mast loading, and verifying that blades will not strike ground personnel, equipment, or the tail boom during rotation.
