AI_EDIT	HI.VI.C.S1.001	The nose should generally be pointed into the wind or at least positioned so that the tail rotor is not directing exhaust or rotor wash toward other aircraft, personnel, or structures. Positioning into the wind also aids engine cooling and provides better engine start characteristics. Most importantly, the tail rotor arc must be kept clear of obstructions and personnel since the tail rotor is nearly invisible when turning.	->	The helicopter must be positioned so that both the main rotor and tail rotor arcs are clear of personnel, equipment, and structures — this is the primary safety requirement. Positioning into the wind is a secondary benefit that aids engine cooling and start characteristics. The tail rotor is nearly invisible when turning and must never be pointed toward areas where personnel or critical equipment are present.
AI_EDIT	HI.VI.C.S2.007	If collective pitch cannot be reduced quickly due to excessive friction, the engine may be overloaded during start, potentially causing an overtemperature (hot start) condition. Additionally, if the blades are carrying too much pitch at low RPM, induced drag on the rotor system can prevent it from reaching governed RPM, causing a rotor droop. In extreme cases, this can result in rotor stall — which the HFH states is not recoverable. This scenario illustrates that flight control friction is a tool, not a lock; it should be set to a light-resistance level that prevents inadvertent movement without impeding rapid corrective inputs. The pilot should always be able to override friction with normal hand/foot pressure for emergency corrections.	->	If collective pitch cannot be reduced quickly due to excessive friction, the engine may be overloaded during start, potentially causing an overtemperature (hot start) condition. Additionally, if the blades are carrying too much pitch at low RPM, excessive rotor blade loading can prevent RPM acceleration, causing a rotor droop and potential rotor stall — a condition that is difficult to recover from and should be prevented by never allowing collective to be locked at high pitch during start. Recovery, if possible, requires immediate reduction of collective and airspeed; however, the margin for recovery is extremely narrow and rotor stall should be treated as an emergency condition to be avoided. This scenario illustrates that flight control friction is a tool, not a lock; it should be set to a light-resistance level that prevents inadvertent movement without impeding rapid corrective inputs. The pilot should always be able to override friction with normal hand/foot pressure for emergency corrections.
