AI_EDIT	HI.VI.D.K1.006	Completing a before takeoff check from memory — sometimes called a 'flow check' — relies entirely on the pilot's recall under potentially high workload, distraction, or fatigue. Human memory is fallible; items can be skipped, especially familiar ones that are assumed to be correct. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook and FAA accident data support that approximately 80% of aviation accidents involve human factors, and complacency on familiar procedures is a well-documented contributor. Reading from the actual printed or approved electronic checklist provides: (1) A verification step that each item was physically checked and confirmed, not just assumed; (2) A systematic order that prevents jumping ahead or skipping items; and (3) A reference to the manufacturer's exact requirements for that specific configuration. A malfunction that causes a subtle abnormal indication — such as a hydraulic pressure that is technically in the green but at the lower edge — is more likely to be noticed when the pilot is specifically directed to look at that gauge by the checklist than during a memory flow.	->	Completing a before takeoff check from the actual printed or approved electronic checklist provides a systematic reference point and verification step that each item was physically checked and confirmed, not just assumed. Reading from a checklist ensures a methodical order that prevents skipping items, particularly those that seem familiar or routine. While some operators and aircraft POHs may authorize a 'flow check' as an initial step, the formal verification step of reading the checklist ensures compliance with manufacturer requirements. Human factors research and accident data consistently show that checklist discipline prevents omissions, particularly on subtle indications—such as a hydraulic pressure that is technically in the green but at the lower edge—which are more likely to be detected when the pilot is specifically directed to assess that gauge by the checklist than during a memory flow or assumed status.
AI_EDIT	HI.VI.D.K1.008	The student's reasoning reflects a fundamental misunderstanding of why checklists exist. The before takeoff check is not a redundant repeat of the run-up — it is a verification of the current state of the aircraft at the moment of commitment to flight. Systems can and do change between the run-up area and the takeoff position. A specific helicopter system that exemplifies this: the hydraulic system. On some helicopters, hydraulic pressure can appear normal at idle during run-up but begin degrading as rotor RPM increases and system demand rises closer to takeoff power. A pilot who skips the hydraulic pressure check on the before takeoff checklist may lift off and immediately encounter heavy or unpredictable control forces as hydraulic pressure drops — at the worst possible moment, at low altitude with no energy to manage the emergency. Additionally, the student's decision to skip checklist items because of pressure from deteriorating weather is a classic example of a hazardous attitude — 'impulsivity' — combined with 'invulnerability.' The ADM principle that approximately 80% of accidents involve human factors directly applies; skipping checklists under pressure is a well-documented link in the error chain. The correct response is to complete the checklist regardless of time pressure, or to not depart.	->	The student's reasoning reflects a fundamental misunderstanding of why checklists exist. The before takeoff check is not a redundant repeat of the run-up—it is a verification of the current state of the aircraft at the moment of commitment to flight. Systems can and do change between the run-up area and the takeoff position. A specific helicopter system that exemplifies this: the hydraulic system. Depending on the aircraft type and design, hydraulic pressure can appear normal at idle during run-up but may begin degrading as rotor RPM increases and system demand rises closer to takeoff power. A pilot who skips the hydraulic pressure check on the before takeoff checklist may lift off and immediately encounter heavy or unpredictable control forces as hydraulic pressure drops—at the worst possible moment, at low altitude with no energy to manage the emergency. Additionally, the student's decision to skip checklist items because of pressure from deteriorating weather reflects hazardous attitudes—'impulsivity' and 'invulnerability.' Human factors research consistently demonstrates that skipping checklists under time or weather pressure is a documented link in accident chains. The correct response is to complete the checklist regardless of time pressure, or to delay departure. The aircraft RFM or POH specifies all hydraulic pressure parameters and limitations; the before-takeoff check is the final opportunity to detect any system degradation before flight.
