AI_EDIT	HI.VI.D.S2.004	First, calculate density altitude: using the approximation, DA = PA + (120 × (OAT - ISA temp)). ISA temp at 1,200 ft = 15°C - (1.98°C × 1.2) ≈ 12.6°C. Deviation = 35 - 12.6 = 22.4°C above ISA. DA ≈ 1,200 + (120 × 22.4) ≈ 1,200 + 2,688 ≈ 3,888 ft DA. This is within the OGE hover ceiling of 4,500 ft, but it's close. Next, check actual gross weight against RFM hover charts at that DA. If weight is near maximum, OGE hover may be marginal or not achievable. In that case, an IGE takeoff profile accelerating immediately to best climb airspeed would be required. You must also verify the departure procedure's climb gradient can be met. If performance is marginal, consider reducing fuel or payload, or delaying departure to cooler conditions.	->	First, calculate density altitude using RFM-provided charts or tables, or apply the formula: DA = field elevation + (120 × (OAT − ISA temperature at field elevation)). ISA temperature at 1,200 ft MSL is approximately 12.6°C; deviation from ISA is 35 − 12.6 = 22.4°C. DA ≈ 1,200 + (120 × 22.4) ≈ 3,888 ft. Next, consult your helicopter's RFM performance charts at 3,888 ft DA and your actual gross weight to determine achievable hover capability. The 4,500 ft OGE hover ceiling applies at maximum gross weight; if your current loading is lighter, your actual ceiling may be higher. Compare your actual weight and achieved OGE hover altitude against the DA. If OGE hover is not achievable, plan an IGE takeoff with immediate acceleration to best climb airspeed. Verify that the climb gradient of your departure procedure can be met at this DA and weight. If performance is marginal, consider reducing fuel or payload, or delaying departure to cooler conditions. The final decision must be based on actual RFM charts, not approximations.
