ESCALATE	HI.VIII.A.R1.002	The specific wind azimuth degree ranges (210°–330°, 285°–315°, 120°–240°) cannot be verified against authoritative FAA AC 90-95 text without access to that document; over-specificity without source confirmation on a safety-critical LTE topic warrants expert review and source cross-check.
AI_EDIT	HI.VIII.A.R1.007	While LTE is most common at low airspeeds below ETL, the FAA's AC 90-95 notes that high-power settings at any airspeed can create conditions where tail rotor demand exceeds authority — particularly during steep climbing turns in the critical wind azimuths or during heavy-load operations at high density altitude. During a vertical takeoff transitioning to forward flight, LTE risk is highest in three phases: (1) The initial lift-off to OGE hover where full power is applied and airspeed is zero; (2) The deceleration zone between ETL entry and loss of translational lift if a go-around is attempted, where airspeed is decaying but power demand spikes; (3) During a climbing turn immediately after liftoff in a quartering tailwind. Risk management requires: planning the departure heading to remain in favorable wind azimuths during the power-intensive low-airspeed phase, avoiding steep climbing turns below ETL, and treating any uncommanded yaw during the climb as a potential LTE onset requiring immediate acceleration rather than a heading correction attempt.	->	LTE risk is highest when tail rotor demand exceeds authority at low airspeeds and high power settings — particularly during the initial lift-off to out-of-ground-effect hover, during climbing turns below ETL airspeed in adverse wind azimuths, and during deceleration approaches if a go-around is executed. LTE is not a cruise-altitude phenomenon but remains a critical risk during the power-intensive, low-airspeed phases of vertical takeoff and climb. Risk management requires: planning the departure heading to remain in favorable wind azimuths during liftoff and initial climb below ETL, avoiding steep climbing turns below ETL, and treating any uncommanded yaw during low-airspeed climb as potential LTE onset, requiring immediate acceleration to ETL rather than pedal correction alone. Per FAA AC 90-95 and FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11, the zones of highest LTE risk are during (1) initial lift-off to OGE hover, (2) climbing turns below ETL in crosswind or tailwind azimuths, and (3) deceleration zones approaching ETL during go-around attempts.
ESCALATE	HI.VIII.A.R1.008	The answer claims NOTAR systems have 'reduced LTE susceptibility' per FAA treatment, but the specific mechanisms (Coanda effect disruption in crosswind gusts) lack explicit FAA regulatory citation in AC 90-95 or FAA-H-8083-21B; the characterization of FAA position should be verified against primary regulatory documents or deferred to type-specific guidance without overstating FAA endorsement.
AI_EDIT	HI.VIII.A.S2.001	Read back all altitude assignments, clearance limits, and any conditional instructions verbatim. For example: 'Cleared vertical takeoff to 500 feet, [callsign].' The key items that must always be read back are altitudes, headings, runway/helipad assignments, and hold-short instructions. Omitting any of these constitutes an incomplete read-back.	->	Read back all altitude assignments, clearance limits, and any conditional instructions using standard phraseology that includes the facility name, your full callsign, altitude, and any restrictions. Example: 'Denver Tower, Skycrane 7 Alpha Bravo, cleared vertical takeoff, maintain 500 feet.' The key items that must always be read back are altitudes, headings, runway or helipad assignments, and hold-short instructions. Omitting any of these constitutes an incomplete read-back. The requirement to read back clearances is established in 14 CFR 91.123 (compliance with ATC clearances), with specific phraseology standards in AIM 4-2-3.
AI_EDIT	HI.VIII.A.S2.003	The evaluator is acting as a simulated ATC authority or exercising examiner authority; you must stop the vertical climb and establish a stable hover at 50 feet AGL. You must verbally acknowledge the instruction with your callsign to confirm you have heard and understood it. Failure to acknowledge or comply is a demonstration of non-compliance with ATC/evaluator instructions and is a task failure item.	->	The evaluator is acting as a simulated ATC authority or exercising examiner authority; you must stop the vertical climb and establish a stable hover at 50 feet AGL. You must verbally acknowledge the instruction with your callsign to confirm you have heard and understood it. Failure to acknowledge or comply is a demonstration of non-compliance with ATC/evaluator instructions and is a task failure item.

Regulatory Reference: Helicopter ACS (HI.VIII.A.S2 performance criteria); 14 CFR 91.123
AI_EDIT	HI.VIII.A.S3.003	Nr is the rotational speed of the main rotor system, while Nf (in turbine helicopters) or N2 refers to the engine or power turbine RPM. In normal coupled flight, they are directly proportional and matched through the transmission and freewheeling unit. Both matter during vertical takeoff and landing because Nr reflects actual rotor aerodynamic performance and blade lift capability, while engine RPM indicates whether the powerplant is producing adequate power. A split between the two — where engine RPM is high but Nr is low — could indicate a transmission problem or freewheeling unit issue. Conversely, if both drop together, it indicates an overall power deficiency or throttle problem.	->	Nr is the main rotor RPM, while engine/power source RPM varies by powerplant type. In turbine helicopters, the power turbine (often labeled Nf or N2) provides input to the transmission; in piston helicopters, engine RPM directly drives the transmission. Both matter during vertical takeoff and landing because Nr reflects actual rotor aerodynamic performance and blade lift capability, while engine RPM indicates whether the powerplant is producing adequate power. A split between the two — where engine RPM is high but Nr is low — could indicate a transmission problem or freewheeling unit issue. Conversely, if both drop together, it indicates an overall power deficiency or throttle problem. Always consult your aircraft's POH for specific nomenclature and gauge interpretation.
