AI_EDIT	HI.XII.B.R2.001	The three simultaneous inputs are: (1) lower collective to reduce drag and allow upward airflow to sustain rotor RPM, (2) apply aft cyclic to slow the aircraft and prevent rotor RPM overspeed, and (3) apply appropriate pedal to maintain trim. If collective is not lowered immediately, rotor RPM decays rapidly — especially in low-inertia rotor systems — potentially leading to a rotor stall, which is not recoverable. Delay in aft cyclic risks rotor overspeed. Missing pedal input can produce a yaw divergence.	->	The three simultaneous inputs are: (1) lower collective to reduce blade pitch angle, which decreases rotor drag and allows upward airflow to sustain and recover rotor RPM, (2) apply aft cyclic to slow the aircraft and prevent rotor RPM overspeed, and (3) apply appropriate pedal to maintain trim. If collective is not lowered immediately, rotor RPM decays rapidly — especially in low-inertia rotor systems — potentially leading to a rotor stall, which is not recoverable. Delay in aft cyclic risks rotor overspeed. Missing pedal input can produce a yaw divergence.
