AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.D.S4.005	The ball displaced to the right means the tail is skidding to the right — the helicopter is in a left skid if turning left, or the rate of turn is too high relative to bank angle. The correction in a helicopter is to apply right anti-torque pedal to push the tail back into line and re-center the ball. Unlike fixed-wing aircraft where you 'step on the ball,' in a helicopter the relationship between torque and pedal position must be considered. If the skid is caused by excessive power (collective up), a slight power reduction may also help. I continue cross-checking the turn-and-slip indicator alongside the heading indicator and attitude indicator to confirm the turn is now coordinated and on the correct bank angle for standard rate.	->	The ball displaced to the right indicates a skid — the rate of turn is too high for the bank angle, so inertia is carrying the helicopter to the outside of the turn. The correction in a helicopter is to apply right anti-torque pedal to yaw the tail back into alignment and re-center the ball. If the skid is caused by excessive power, a slight reduction in collective may also help. Continue cross-checking the turn-and-slip indicator alongside the heading indicator and attitude indicator to confirm the turn is coordinated and at the correct bank angle for standard rate.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.E.K1.001	The three broad categories are: (1) Flight causal factors — poor instrument cross-check, fixation on a single instrument, distraction, or improper control inputs; (2) Physiological factors — spatial disorientation, vestibular illusions, visual illusions, hypoxia, or fatigue that cause the pilot to misinterpret or distrust their instruments; and (3) Environmental factors — turbulence, inadvertent IMC, icing, or rapidly changing weather conditions that overwhelm the pilot or mask actual aircraft attitude. System and equipment failures, such as a failed attitude indicator or erroneous instrument readings, are also a recognized contributor.	->	The three broad categories are: (1) Flight causal factors — poor instrument cross-check, fixation on a single instrument, distraction, or improper control inputs; (2) Physiological factors — spatial disorientation, vestibular illusions, visual illusions, hypoxia, or fatigue that cause the pilot to misinterpret or distrust their instruments; and (3) Environmental factors — turbulence, inadvertent IMC, icing, or rapidly changing weather conditions that overwhelm the pilot or mask actual aircraft attitude. Equipment failures such as a failed attitude indicator may compound or accelerate entry into an unusual attitude but are typically classified as environmental or causal contributors.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.E.R3.005	High-workload moments are exactly when control input errors are most likely to occur. With attention divided between radios, approach plate, navigation, and aircraft control, the pilot is susceptible to fixation on a single instrument (such as the glidepath indicator) and may make an aggressive collective pull to regain altitude quickly. This abrupt collective increase will simultaneously increase torque (requiring left pedal compensation), change rotor pitch (affecting RPM transiently), and pitch the nose — creating multiple simultaneous attitude changes the pilot must manage while already task-saturated. The correction for 200 feet below glidepath should be measured: increase collective smoothly to establish a climb rate approximately double the altitude deviation (roughly 400 FPM initially), cross-check the VSI and altimeter together, and stabilize before reconfiguring. The broader risk management strategy is workload shedding — if workload is unmanageable, execute a missed approach rather than forcing a correction at a high-error-risk moment. This reflects the ADM principle of choosing options known to work over those that will 'probably' work.	->	High-workload moments are exactly when control input errors are most likely to occur. With attention divided between radios, approach plate, navigation, and aircraft control, the pilot is susceptible to fixation on a single instrument (such as the glidepath indicator) and may make an aggressive collective pull to regain altitude quickly. This abrupt collective increase will simultaneously increase torque (requiring left pedal compensation), change rotor pitch (affecting RPM transiently), and pitch the nose — creating multiple simultaneous attitude changes the pilot must manage while already task-saturated. The correction for 200 feet below glidepath should be measured: increase collective smoothly to establish a climb rate approximately double the altitude deviation (roughly 400 FPM initially, as a rule of thumb), cross-check the VSI and altimeter together, and stabilize before reconfiguring. The broader risk management strategy is workload shedding — if workload is unmanageable, execute a missed approach rather than forcing a correction at a high-error-risk moment. This reflects the ADM principle of choosing options known to work over those that will 'probably' work.
