AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.F.S1.008	A simultaneous VRS and single-engine failure creates two competing emergencies. The priority is: (1) Aircraft control above all else — you must first exit VRS because without rotor thrust authority, the engine failure checklist is irrelevant. Continue VRS recovery: lower collective, forward cyclic, regain translational lift. (2) Once VRS is broken and the aircraft is under control, transition to the single-engine failure emergency checklist — confirm failed engine identification, secure it per the POH engine failure procedure, and establish single-engine flight parameters (airspeed, power limits, weight limits). (3) Declare emergency on appropriate frequency, identify a suitable landing area, and follow the single-engine approach checklist. The key principle is that both checklists must be completed, but they must be sequenced correctly — VRS recovery first, engine failure second. Attempting the engine failure checklist before VRS recovery risks losing the aircraft entirely.	->	Yes. If the engine failure resulted in an engine shutdown in flight, it likely qualifies as a serious incident or accident per 49 CFR 830.2. An engine shutdown in flight is typically classified as a serious incident unless there is substantial damage or fatal/serious injury (which would make it an accident). Under 49 CFR Part 830 (NTSB regulations), the pilot-in-command must immediately notify the NTSB. A formal accident or serious incident report (NTSB Form 6120.1) must be filed within 10 days. For Part 91 general aviation operations in VFR, malfunction/defect reporting follows 14 CFR 91.25 or operator-specific rules; 14 CFR 91.187 applies only to IFR operations in controlled airspace. For Part 135 commercial operations, operator-specific mandatory reporting applies. The pilot should also consider an ASRS (Aviation Safety Reporting System) report to contribute to system safety learning.
