AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.G.K2.006	If density altitude exceeds the helicopter's hover ceiling for the given weight, the engine simply cannot produce enough power to sustain hover without rotor RPM decaying. The pilot would have to either lower collective — accepting a descent — or accept dangerously low rotor RPM, potentially leading to rotor blade stall, which is unrecoverable. A weather factor that could worsen the situation is high humidity. While humidity's effect on performance is sometimes underestimated, humid air is less dense than dry air at the same pressure and temperature, further increasing the effective density altitude beyond what standard calculations might show, compounding the power deficit and accelerating RPM decay.	->	If density altitude exceeds the helicopter's hover ceiling for the given weight, the engine simply cannot produce enough power to sustain hover without rotor RPM decaying. The pilot would have to either lower collective—accepting a descent—or accept dangerously low rotor RPM, potentially leading to rotor blade stall, which is unrecoverable. A weather factor that could worsen the situation is high humidity. Humid air is actually less dense than dry air at the same pressure and temperature because water vapor (molecular weight 18) displaces heavier nitrogen and oxygen molecules. This reduces air density further, increasing the effective density altitude beyond standard calculations and compounding the power deficit and accelerating RPM decay.
