AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.G.K6.002	Blade stall occurs when the angle of attack on a rotor blade exceeds the critical angle of attack, causing airflow to separate from the blade surface and the blade to lose lift. In a helicopter, blade stall typically begins near the blade root (hub) where blade velocity is lowest and rotor blade pitch is highest relative to local airflow. According to the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook, during vortex ring state blade stall begins near the hub and progresses outward as conditions worsen. Similarly, in a low rotor RPM condition leading to full rotor stall, the stall progression starts inboard and moves outboard — and unlike fixed-wing stall, rotor stall is not recoverable once fully developed.	->	Blade stall occurs when the angle of attack on a rotor blade exceeds the critical angle of attack, causing airflow to separate from the blade surface and the blade to lose lift. In vortex ring state, blade stall typically begins near the blade root (hub) where blade velocity is lowest and progresses outward as conditions worsen. However, stall initiation location can vary depending on blade design, twist, and pitch distribution. Once a full rotor stall is established, recovery becomes extremely difficult or impossible, and the helicopter will experience loss of control. This is why early recognition and prevention of low RPM conditions is critical—unlike fixed-wing stalls, rotor stall offers virtually no recovery options.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.G.K7.002	Because rotor stall in a helicopter is not recoverable. Once rotor RPM decays below the critical stall threshold, the blades lose the ability to produce lift and the helicopter will experience a catastrophic loss of control. Unlike fixed-wing aircraft where a stall can often be recovered with altitude and proper technique, a fully stalled rotor system cannot be recovered. Early recognition — monitoring the RPM gauge and listening for audio warnings — is imperative because recovery options disappear rapidly as RPM continues to decay.	->	Because rotor stall in a helicopter is extremely difficult or impossible to recover from. Once rotor RPM decays below the critical stall threshold, the blades lose the ability to produce sufficient lift and the helicopter will experience loss of control. Unlike fixed-wing aircraft where a stall can often be recovered with altitude and proper technique, a fully stalled rotor system offers virtually no recovery options. Early recognition—monitoring the RPM gauge and listening for audio warnings—is imperative because recovery options disappear rapidly as RPM continues to decay.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.G.K7.005	This represents a workload management and situational awareness failure — two core components of SRM. When pilots focus attention on a single task (such as precise hovering position or a specific maneuver), they often neglect instrument scanning, creating 'tunnel vision.' The error chain begins with poor scan technique, progresses to delayed recognition of RPM decay, and can culminate in rotor stall. SRM correction involves training the pilot to develop a systematic instrument scan that includes rotor RPM as a primary reference during all phases of flight — not just cruise. The instructor should also emphasize that approximately 80% of aviation accidents stem from pilot error and human factors, reinforcing that disciplined scanning habits prevent this fatal error chain from developing.	->	This represents a workload management and situational awareness failure — two core components of SRM. When pilots focus attention on a single task (such as precise hovering position or a specific maneuver), they often neglect instrument scanning, creating 'tunnel vision.' The error chain begins with poor scan technique, progresses to delayed recognition of RPM decay, and can culminate in rotor stall. SRM correction involves training the pilot to develop a systematic instrument scan that includes rotor RPM as a primary reference during all phases of flight — not just cruise. The instructor should emphasize that disciplined scanning habits are fundamental to preventing this fatal error chain from developing, as pilot error and human factors are significant contributors to aviation accidents.
