ESCALATE	HI.XIII.G.R2.008	The answer conflates rotor stall with vortex ring state characteristics and lacks precise FAA-H-8083-21B citations on blade stall progression; the distinction between recoverable low RPM and non-recoverable rotor stall aerodynamics requires authoritative handbook verification before shipping.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.G.R3.003	Unlike a fixed-wing aircraft stall, a helicopter rotor stall — where RPM decays to the point that the blades can no longer produce sufficient lift — is not recoverable. Once the rotor stalls, there is no remaining control authority; the helicopter will enter an uncontrolled descent. This makes early recognition critical, because once rotor stall occurs, the pilot cannot prevent collision with whatever is below or nearby. Every second of delay in recognizing and correcting low RPM reduces the pilot's options for maneuvering away from collision hazards. The FAA states that early recognition is imperative precisely because of this non-recoverability.	->	Rotor stall—the aerodynamic failure where blade lift collapses entirely—is non-recoverable. However, low rotor RPM is recoverable if recognized and corrected immediately through lowering collective and increasing throttle. The critical distinction is that early recognition and immediate corrective action prevent the transition from recoverable low RPM to unrecoverable rotor stall. This makes early recognition imperative, not because all low RPM is non-recoverable, but because the window for recovery closes very rapidly—especially in low-inertia rotor systems. Once stall occurs, there is no remaining control authority and the helicopter will enter an uncontrolled descent, making collision avoidance impossible. The FAA emphasizes early recognition precisely because it is the pilot's only tool to prevent reaching the non-recoverable state.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.G.S2.004	One 90-degree turn is not adequate. A proper clearing consists of at minimum two 90-degree turns in opposite directions, or one complete 180-degree turn, so that the pilot can visually scan the entire surrounding airspace including the area that was previously behind and below the helicopter. A single 90-degree turn leaves significant blind spots. The correct procedure is to complete two 90-degree clearing turns — left and right — or one 180-degree clearing turn, while actively scanning for traffic, obstacles, and terrain at all altitudes.	->	One 90-degree turn is not adequate. The proper clearing procedure consists of two 90-degree clearing turns in opposite directions (typically left then right, or right then left) to visually scan all quadrants including the areas above and below the helicopter. This ensures full 360-degree coverage plus vertical scanning. A single 180-degree turn does not provide equivalent coverage and leaves potential blind spots depending on bank angle and execution. Each 90-degree turn should include active scanning upward, downward, and around the aircraft before beginning the low rotor RPM recovery maneuver.
