AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.H.S1.005	First action: select the alternate static source immediately. This restores instruments to cabin air — note that altitude will read slightly high and airspeed slightly fast; reference your RFM for exact corrections. If alternate static source is not effective or unavailable: transition to the GPS altitude data (if equipped and approved), cross-check with vertical deviation on the ILS glideslope indicator which provides vertical guidance independent of pitot-static — fly the glidepath needle as primary vertical reference. Use power and attitude to maintain a stabilized approach. Maintain published approach speeds by referencing the ASI, corrected for any blockage error. Brief yourself on MDA/DA using baro altitude from GPS if available. Declare an emergency if needed to gain priority handling or vectors to an airport with better conditions. Do not attempt the approach below minimums.	->	First action: select the alternate static source immediately. This restores instruments to cabin air — note that altitude will read slightly high and airspeed slightly fast; reference your RFM for exact corrections. If alternate static source is not effective or unavailable: cross-check vertical deviation on the ILS glideslope indicator, which provides vertical guidance independent of pitot-static — fly the glidepath needle as primary vertical reference. Use power and attitude to maintain a stabilized approach. Maintain published approach speeds by referencing the ASI, corrected for any blockage error. GPS altitude or GPS-derived baro correction may be used as a cross-check only, but per 14 CFR 91.175, you must not descend below published DA/MDA unless visual reference to the runway environment is clearly established and the approach is stabilized. Do not attempt descent below minimums on instruments alone. Declare an emergency if needed to gain priority handling. Helicopter note: ILS capability is limited on most helicopter types; verify aircraft equipment before relying on glideslope guidance.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.K1.008	Weight: A heavier helicopter requires more collective pitch and therefore more rotor thrust to lift off. More rotor thrust means a greater lateral force component is available to drive the rollover once the critical angle is approached. Heavier aircraft also have greater rotational inertia, but the increased rotor thrust more than offsets this — heavier conditions are generally more hazardous for dynamic rollover. CG Location: A lateral CG displacement toward the pivot-side skid dramatically worsens dynamic rollover susceptibility. If the CG is already offset toward the grounded skid, the helicopter's weight itself contributes to the overturning moment, reducing the cyclic authority needed to hold the opposite skid down and shortening the time to critical rollover angle. Conversely, lateral CG on the opposite side from the pivot provides a slight restoring moment, giving marginally more margin. Longitudinal CG extremes affect how much collective is required (aft CG tends to require more antitorque pedal which affects lateral forces), but lateral CG is the dominant factor. Rotor RPM: Lower rotor RPM means less overall rotor thrust is available at a given collective position, which might seem to reduce rollover risk. However, if RPM decays during the lift attempt — for example, during a high-density-altitude departure — the pilot may over-apply collective to compensate, causing an abrupt thrust increase that rapidly loads one skid as a pivot point. Additionally, low inertia rotor systems decay RPM quickly, giving the pilot less time to react before rotor stall compounds the emergency. Higher RPM systems with more blade inertia provide slightly more time but do not eliminate the risk.	->	Weight: A heavier helicopter requires more collective pitch and therefore more rotor thrust to lift off. More rotor thrust means a greater lateral force component is available to drive the rollover once the critical angle is approached. Heavier aircraft also have greater rotational inertia, but the increased rotor thrust more than offsets this — heavier conditions are generally more hazardous for dynamic rollover. CG Location: A lateral CG displacement toward the pivot-side skid dramatically worsens dynamic rollover susceptibility. If the CG is already offset toward the grounded skid, the helicopter's weight itself contributes to the overturning moment, reducing the cyclic authority needed to hold the opposite skid down and shortening the time to critical rollover angle. Conversely, lateral CG on the opposite side from the pivot provides a slight restoring moment, giving marginally more margin. Longitudinal CG extremes affect how much collective is required (aft CG tends to require more antitorque pedal which affects lateral forces), but lateral CG is the dominant factor. Rotor RPM: Dynamic rollover risk is driven primarily by rotor thrust magnitude and its geometric relationship to the grounded skid (pivot point), not by absolute RPM. However, lower RPM reduces the total rotor thrust available at a given collective position, which decreases the lateral force component driving rollover — so RPM in itself does not increase rollover risk. The practical concern is thrust margin: if RPM decays during high-density-altitude operations, the pilot may apply excessive collective to maintain lift, which increases rotor thrust and worsens rollover risk. Additionally, low-inertia rotor systems are more vulnerable to stall if over-pitched, eliminating corrective cyclic authority. Maintain adequate RPM margin to ensure sufficient control authority for recovery, not because RPM alone drives dynamic rollover.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.K2.004	At that moment, multiple forces are combining: (1) Gravity is pulling the helicopter downslope (to the right in this case) because the right skid is lower and acts as a pivot; (2) The left crosswind is pushing the fuselage to the right, adding to the rolling tendency; (3) The rotor thrust vector, if not properly directed, may have a lateral component contributing to the roll. The most critical input is right (upslope) cyclic to tilt the rotor disk and create a lateral thrust component opposing the roll. Simultaneously, collective must be managed carefully — too much power increases rotor thrust and can accelerate the roll, while too little may cause the skid to dig in further and allow the roll to continue. The pilot must apply right cyclic to hold the tip-path plane level or slightly into the hill while carefully reducing collective to settle the helicopter onto the slope.	->	At that moment, multiple forces are combining: (1) Gravity is pulling the helicopter downslope (to the right in this case) because the right skid is lower and acts as a pivot; (2) The left crosswind is pushing the fuselage to the right, adding to the rolling tendency; (3) The rotor thrust vector, if not properly directed, may have a lateral component contributing to the roll. The most critical input is right (upslope) cyclic—applied with sufficient authority to tilt the rotor disk into the hill and generate a lateral thrust component that opposes the roll. During normal slope landing, collective is managed conservatively to avoid over-pitching the rotor and accelerating the roll. However, if rollover becomes imminent (helicopter approaches critical roll angle and cyclic authority is nearly exhausted), the correct emergency response is immediate full collective DOWN to eliminate rotor thrust and stop the overturning moment; this is the primary dynamic rollover recovery input. In a controlled slope landing, the pilot maintains right cyclic (toward full up-slope position) while carefully reducing collective to settle the helicopter. If roll rate accelerates or roll angle exceeds ~15°, transition immediately to full-down collective to arrest the rollover.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.K3.001	Keep the cyclic centered or tilted slightly into the slope/wind during any ground contact. Avoid excessive lateral cyclic inputs that could generate a rolling moment around the grounded skid or wheel. Additionally, apply collective smoothly and avoid abrupt power changes while any part of the skid or gear remains in contact with the ground.	->	The single most important preventive technique is to execute a positive, clean liftoff using smooth, coordinated cyclic and collective inputs that establish rotor thrust upslope and into the wind before committing weight to the skids. Avoid the gray zone where one skid remains grounded while rotor thrust is applied asymmetrically, which creates a pivot point and rolling moment. Once committed to ground contact, maintain cyclic tilted into the slope and apply collective smoothly to break ground contact cleanly. The key principle is never allowing a grounded skid to become a pivot point with unbalanced rotor thrust acting on it.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.K3.006	A crosswind from the uphill side is the most hazardous combination because the wind pushes the helicopter toward the downhill direction, reinforcing the natural tendency to roll downhill once a skid becomes a pivot point. With a crosswind from the uphill side, the pilot must apply additional into-slope (uphill) cyclic to maintain rotor disk tilt against both the slope and the wind. The pilot should also consider whether the crosswind component exceeds the aircraft's demonstrated crosswind capability for slope operations. If so, landing should be declined. During collective reduction to touch down, inputs must be especially smooth since the wind can introduce asymmetric lift across the rotor disk, making the rollover initiation more abrupt and unpredictable.	->	A crosswind from the uphill side can compound rollover risk by making it more difficult to establish solid uphill skid contact and by introducing asymmetric rotor disk loading. The pilot must apply and maintain into-slope (uphill) cyclic throughout the approach and touchdown to counter both the natural slope-induced rolling moment and any wind-induced destabilization. The approach must be stabilized with the rotor disk tilted into the slope and into the wind before committing weight to the skids. During collective reduction and touchdown, inputs must be especially smooth to avoid abrupt weight transfer that could initiate uncommanded roll. The pilot should refer to the aircraft's POH for demonstrated hover crosswind limits and use professional judgment to assess whether the combined slope and crosswind conditions exceed safe operating parameters. If in doubt, decline the landing.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.K4.001	The most common error is applying lateral cyclic in the direction of the slope (upslope cyclic) while the skid or wheel is still in contact with the ground. This allows the helicopter to begin rolling about the grounded skid as a pivot point. Once the rolling moment exceeds the cyclic authority available to counteract it, recovery becomes impossible. The correct technique is to lift vertically using collective, not lateral cyclic, to break ground contact.	->	The most common error is applying downslope lateral cyclic (tilting the rotor disk away from the slope) while one or both skids remain in contact with the ground. This tilts the rotor thrust vector downslope, creating a rolling moment about the upslope skid as a pivot point. Once rolling, recovery becomes impossible because the rotor authority cannot overcome the moment generated by the grounded skid. The correct technique is to apply into-slope cyclic to maintain rotor disk alignment with the slope, then raise collective smoothly to lift vertically and break ground contact before increasing forward airspeed.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.K4.003	Applying excessive upslope cyclic to level the rotor disk while one skid is still on the slope can cause the rotor's lateral thrust component to push the helicopter into a roll about the downslope skid as a pivot point. The more upslope cyclic the pilot applies, the greater the rolling moment toward the downslope side. This can rapidly exceed the helicopter's ability to recover, resulting in dynamic rollover. The correct technique is to use conservative cyclic inputs and set down one skid at a time while maintaining situational awareness of roll rate.	->	Allowing the cyclic to move away from (downslope of) the slope, or applying insufficient into-slope cyclic while one skid is in contact, causes the rotor disk to tilt away from the slope. This creates a lateral thrust component that pushes the helicopter into a roll about the upslope skid as a pivot point. The rotor's tilting moment grows as the cyclic drifts further downslope, and this rolling moment can rapidly exceed the helicopter's ability to recover, resulting in dynamic rollover. The correct technique is to maintain firm into-slope cyclic throughout landing, keep the descent rate controlled, set down one skid at a time, and maintain situational awareness of roll rate.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.R1.001	Dynamic rollover occurs when a helicopter begins to roll about a pivot point on the surface — such as a skid or wheel that is caught, stuck, or snagged — and the rolling moment exceeds the pilot's ability to correct with opposite cyclic. Once the critical rollover angle is reached (typically around 8–12 degrees depending on the aircraft), the helicopter will roll over even with full opposite cyclic applied. The surface pivot point is the key ingredient: it transfers lateral rolling forces to the fuselage instead of allowing the helicopter to slide laterally as it should.	->	Dynamic rollover occurs when a helicopter begins to roll about a pivot point on the surface — such as a skid or wheel that is caught, stuck, or snagged — and the rolling moment exceeds the pilot's ability to correct with opposite cyclic. Once the critical rollover angle is reached, the helicopter will roll over even with full opposite cyclic applied. The critical rollover angle varies by helicopter type and is typically in the range of 5–8 degrees; consult the specific Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) for the aircraft being operated. The surface pivot point is the key ingredient: it transfers lateral rolling forces to the fuselage instead of allowing the helicopter to slide laterally as it should.
