AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.R2.001	Dynamic rollover occurs when a helicopter begins to rotate about a pivot point — such as a skid or wheel in contact with the ground — rather than its normal center of gravity. When landing gear catches on an obstruction (rock, rut, tie-down ring, curb), that contact point becomes a fixed pivot. Lateral cyclic input or a gust can then cause the helicopter to roll at an increasing rate that may exceed the pilot's ability to correct, because the main rotor's thrust vector moves outside the pivot point and accelerates the roll. The critical rollover angle — typically around 35 degrees from vertical for most helicopters — can be reached in seconds.	->	Dynamic rollover occurs when a helicopter begins to rotate about a pivot point — such as a skid or wheel in contact with the ground — rather than its normal center of gravity. When landing gear catches on an obstruction (rock, rut, tie-down ring, curb), that contact point becomes a fixed pivot. Lateral cyclic input or a gust can then cause the helicopter to roll at an increasing rate that may exceed the pilot's ability to correct, because the main rotor's thrust vector moves outside the pivot point and accelerates the roll. The critical rollover angle varies by aircraft design, center of gravity position, and rotor system type — typically around 35 degrees from vertical for many helicopters, but pilots must consult their aircraft's RFM/POH for the specific threshold and prevention procedures for their helicopter.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIII.I.R2.006	Rotor system design influences how quickly a rolling moment builds and how much pilot correction authority exists. Semirigid (teetering) rotor systems, common on lighter helicopters like the Robinson R22/R44, have less resistance to rapid roll because the rotor disc tilts readily with cyclic input and there is no lead/lag damping. This means the rolling moment can accelerate very quickly once the pivot is established, leaving the pilot less reaction time. Articulated rotor systems allow individual blade flapping and lead/lag movement, which can slightly moderate the initial roll rate, but they do not eliminate dynamic rollover risk. In all systems, once the critical rollover angle is exceeded, recovery is not possible regardless of rotor type. Pilots must be aware that lighter helicopters with semirigid rotors are particularly unforgiving of delayed corrective action.	->	Dynamic rollover risk is fundamentally determined by pivot-point geometry, center of gravity position, and rotor thrust vector — not primarily by rotor system type (articulated vs. semirigid). However, rotor system type does affect how quickly a helicopter responds to control inputs. Semirigid (teetering) rotor systems, common on lighter helicopters like the Robinson R22/R44, respond quickly to cyclic input, which means that once a pivot point is established, the rolling acceleration may increase rapidly, leaving less reaction time for corrective action. Lighter helicopters with semirigid rotors also typically have lower rotor inertia, further reducing the time available for recovery. Articulated rotor systems allow individual blade flapping and lead/lag movement, which may provide slightly more damping to cyclic inputs, but they do not eliminate dynamic rollover risk. In all systems, once the critical rollover angle is exceeded, recovery is not possible. The key is that lighter, more responsive helicopters require faster corrective action — awareness of your specific aircra