AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.A.K1.006	Preventive technique focuses on minimizing the duration and nature of ground contact during transitions through vulnerable RPM ranges. During takeoff: bring rotor RPM to the full operating range before lifting off — do not attempt to lift off at low RPM. Apply collective smoothly and positively to get light on the skids and then fully airborne quickly; avoid hovering taxi or prolonged partial-weight ground contact. During landing: plan and execute a smooth, even touchdown — avoid landing hard on one skid, which can jar blades out of equal spacing. After touchdown, either hold collective up to maintain light contact briefly and then fully set down smoothly, or reduce collective promptly to fully unload the rotor while it's still at operating RPM. The most critical RPM range to avoid during ground contact is low RPM — when RPM is below the normal operating range, centrifugal force is insufficient to rigidly maintain blade spacing, making the rotor susceptible to lead-lag disturbances. For the Bell 206/407 family, this is generally below approximately 95% Nr, but the specific range is aircraft-dependent per the RFM.	->	Preventive technique focuses on minimizing the duration and nature of ground contact during transitions through vulnerable RPM ranges. During takeoff: bring rotor RPM to the full operating range before lifting off — do not attempt to lift off at low RPM. Apply collective smoothly and positively to get light on the skids and then fully airborne quickly; avoid hovering taxi or prolonged partial-weight ground contact. During landing: plan and execute a smooth, even touchdown — avoid landing hard on one skid, which can jar blades out of equal spacing. After touchdown, either hold collective up to maintain light contact briefly and then fully set down smoothly, or reduce collective promptly to fully unload the rotor while it's still at operating RPM. The most critical RPM range to avoid during ground contact is the low-RPM range — when