AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.A.K2.002	On helicopters with wheeled landing gear, you inspect the oleo struts for proper inflation/extension and fluid leaks, tires for correct pressure and condition, and shimmy dampers for security and fluid level. On helicopters with skid gear, you inspect the skid tube attachment points and cross-tube fittings for cracks, deformation, or loose hardware. On both types you look for any asymmetry in gear height or condition, since uneven damping on one side versus the other can allow resonance to begin. You are specifically looking for anything that would reduce or eliminate the gear's ability to absorb and dampen vertical and lateral oscillations.	->	On helicopters with articulated main rotors, lead-lag dampers must be inspected by grasping each blade near the root and checking for equal resistance to fore-aft movement. Dampers should be firm and not weeping fluid; soft or leaking dampers significantly increase ground resonance risk and are typically a go/no-go item for flight. On helicopters with wheeled landing gear, inspect the oleo struts for proper inflation/extension and fluid leaks, tires for correct pressure and condition, and shimmy dampers for security and fluid level. On helicopters with skid gear, inspect the skid tube attachment points and cross-tube fittings for cracks, deformation, or loose hardware. On both gear types, look for any asymmetry in gear height or condition, since uneven damping on one side versus the other can allow resonance to begin. You are specifically looking for anything that would reduce or eliminate the gear's ability to absorb and dampen vertical and lateral oscillations, as well as the rotor system's ability to manage blade lead-lag motion.
