AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.A.S1.002	Only fully articulated rotor systems are susceptible to ground resonance. These rotor heads allow individual blades to move fore and aft (lead-lag) independently on their drag hinges, which enables the out-of-phase blade spacing condition that causes the imbalance. Rigid rotor and semi-rigid (teetering) two-bladed rotor systems do not have individual lead-lag hinges that allow this independent blade movement, so the resonance coupling cannot develop in the same way.	->	Articulated rotor systems are most susceptible to ground resonance because individual blades move fore and aft (lead-lag) independently on their drag hinges, enabling the out-of-phase blade spacing condition that causes the imbalance. Semi-rigid (teetering) two-bladed rotor systems can also experience ground resonance, particularly in larger helicopters, but typically with less severity due to structural constraints that limit blade independence. Rigid rotor systems have the greatest inherent protection because their structural rigidity prevents the independent lead-lag movement required for resonance coupling to develop.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.B.K1.003	The correct immediate recovery from a low-G condition is to apply aft cyclic smoothly to reload the rotor disk — but ONLY after first applying collective to re-establish positive G loading on the rotor. The critical point is that you must add collective first (or simultaneously with aft cyclic depending on the specific aircraft's RFM) to restore rotor thrust before attempting to change aircraft attitude. Using lateral cyclic alone to correct an unexpected roll or yaw response is the wrong action because in a low-G state, the rotor disk is unloaded and applying cyclic can cause extreme and uncontrollable blade flapping on a semirigid system, leading to mast bumping. The RFM for the specific helicopter always takes precedence.	->	The correct immediate recovery from a low-G condition is to apply aft cyclic and add collective together (or per your specific aircraft RFM) to restore positive G-loading on the rotor disk. The critical point is that you must restore rotor thrust loading before attempting aggressive attitude changes. The exact timing and sequence may vary by aircraft — always follow your RFM. Using lateral cyclic alone to correct roll while in a low-G state is wrong because the unloaded rotor disk can flap excessively, leading to mast bumping on semi-rigid systems.
