AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.B.R1.004	Pushing forward cyclic aggressively to correct an unwanted pitch-up unloads the rotor, creating a low-G condition. In a teetering rotor system like the R22, the tail rotor thrust (which is always present and pushing the tail to the right) can then roll the fuselage to the left while the unloaded rotor disk tilts right — potentially causing mast contact. The proper technique is to apply gentle, coordinated right cyclic first to re-load the rotor and level the fuselage, then smoothly return to level flight with forward cyclic. The key principle: NEVER push forward cyclic aggressively to recover from an unwanted pitch-up in a teetering rotor helicopter.	->	Pushing forward cyclic aggressively to correct an unwanted pitch-up unloads the rotor, creating a low-G condition. In a teetering rotor system like the R22, when the rotor is unloaded, the tail rotor thrust (which is always present and pushing the tail to the right) acts on a fuselage with no restoring force from rotor lift, causing the fuselage to roll left. Simultaneously, the unloaded rotor disk becomes susceptible to asymmetric blade flapping, allowing it to tilt relative to the mast without the centrifugal force tension that normally limits flapping. This angular separation between the fuselage/mast and the tilted rotor disk increases the risk of blade-to-mast contact. The proper technique is to apply gentle lateral (right) cyclic first to roll the helicopter back to wings-level, ensuring the rotor disk is upright and reloaded with G-force, then smoothly apply aft cyclic to recover pitch attitude. The key principle: NEVER push forward cyclic aggressively to recover from an unwanted pitch-up in a teetering rotor helicopter.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.B.R1.008	Step 1 — Abrupt forward cyclic tilts the rotor disk forward, simultaneously reducing the angle of attack across the rotor blades and reducing rotor thrust (lift) rapidly. Step 2 — The helicopter's inertia keeps the fuselage momentarily in its original orientation while rotor thrust drops; the load factor approaches zero (low-G or weightless condition). Step 3 — With rotor thrust removed, the tail rotor — which is continuously producing thrust to counteract main rotor torque — now acts on a fuselage with no restoring force from rotor lift. This causes the fuselage to roll left (in a counterclockwise main rotor system like most American helicopters). Step 4 — As the fuselage rolls left, the rotor disk, now unloaded and free from the stabilizing effect of lift-induced coning, tends to tilt in the opposite direction (right) due to gyroscopic precession and blade flapping mechanics. Step 5 — The angular separation between the fuselage/mast and the tilted rotor disk increases until the rotor blade or hub contacts the mast. Step 6 — Contact with the mast can be catastrophic — the mast can be severed or structurally compromised, resulting in loss of the helicopter. The entire sequence can occur in under one second at cruise airspeed, which is why recognition and avoidance of the precipitating control input is the only reliable defense.	->	Step 1 — Abrupt forward cyclic tilts the rotor disk forward, simultaneously reducing the angle of attack across the rotor blades and reducing rotor thrust (lift) rapidly. Step 2 — The helicopter's inertia keeps the fuselage momentarily in its original orientation while rotor thrust drops; the load factor approaches zero (low-G or weightless condition). Step 3 — With rotor thrust removed, the tail rotor — which is continuously producing thrust to counteract main rotor torque — now acts on a fuselage with no restoring force from rotor lift. This causes the fuselage to roll left (in a counterclockwise main rotor system like most American helicopters). Step 4 — As the fuselage rolls left, the rotor disk — now unloaded and with reduced coning angles — becomes susceptible to asymmetric blade flapping. The blades can flap independently without the centrifugal force tension that normally limits flapping. This asymmetric flapping can cause the disk to tilt laterally relative to the mast. Step 5 — The angular separation between the fuselage/mast and the tilted rotor disk increases until the rotor blade or hub contacts the mast. Step 6 — Contact with the mast can be catastrophic — the mast can be severed or structurally compromised, resulting in loss of the helicopter. The entire sequence can occur in under one second at cruise airspeed, which is why recognition and avoidance of the precipitating control input is the only reliable defense.
ESCALATE	HI.XIV.B.R2.003	The answer prescribes aft cyclic as the first input for low-G recovery, but HI.XIV.B.R1.008 (and standard R22 POH guidance) specifies RIGHT cyclic first to counteract roll before aft cyclic; this internal contradiction requires clarification on which aircraft type and scenario applies, and whether FAA-H-8083-21B supports this sequencing uniformly.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.C.K1.003	Under 14 CFR 91.207, an ELT is required on all U.S.-registered civil aircraft when operated in the United States, except for certain exemptions including helicopters operating within 50 NM of the departure airport, aircraft engaged in training operations in the vicinity of the departure airport, and ferry flights. The ELT battery (or battery pack) must be replaced or recharged when the transmitter has been in use for more than 1 cumulative hour, or when 50% of the useful battery life has expired. The new expiration date must be marked on the outside of the transmitter.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.207, an ELT is required on all U.S.-registered civil aircraft when operated in the United States, except for certain exemptions. For helicopters, exemptions include training operations conducted entirely within 50 NM of the departure airport, helicopters on water operations within power-off gliding distance of shore, and certain ferry and experimental flights. The ELT battery (or battery pack) must be replaced or recharged when the transmitter has been in use for more than 1 cumulative hour, or when 50% of the useful battery life has expired. The new expiration date must be marked on the outside of the transmitter.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.C.K1.006	Immediately switch the ELT off to stop the false distress transmission. Then contact ATC or the nearest FSS on 121.5 MHz or by phone to report the inadvertent activation — give them your location, aircraft registration, and the approximate time the ELT was activated. This allows ATC to cancel any SAR response that may have been initiated. Regarding regulatory requirements: since the unit has transmitted for more than 1 cumulative hour, the battery must be replaced or recharged before the ELT is returned to service per 14 CFR 91.207(d), and the new battery expiration date must be marked on the transmitter. You should also document the inadvertent activation. Before the next flight, verify the ELT is returned to ARM position and is airworthy.	->	Immediately switch the ELT off to stop the false distress transmission. Then contact ATC or the nearest FSS on 121.5 MHz or by phone to report the inadvertent activation — give them your location, aircraft registration, and the approximate time the ELT was activated. This allows ATC to cancel any SAR response that may have been initiated. Regarding regulatory requirements: since the transmitter has only been in use for 20 minutes (less than 1 cumulative hour), battery replacement is NOT required per 14 CFR 91.207(d) at this time. However, the battery condition should be verified and documented to ensure the battery remains serviceable. Before the next flight, verify the ELT is returned to ARM position and is airworthy.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.C.K2.001	The two most common portable fire extinguisher types in helicopters are Halon (or Halon replacement/clean agent) and dry chemical (Purple K). Halon/clean agent extinguishers are effective on Class B (flammable liquid) and Class C (electrical) fires, making them well-suited for cockpit electrical and fuel fires without leaving corrosive residue. Dry chemical extinguishers are effective on Class A (ordinary combustibles), Class B, and Class C fires, but they leave a corrosive powder residue that can damage avionics and airframe components. Most rotorcraft operations favor clean-agent extinguishers to avoid post-discharge damage.	->	Modern rotorcraft increasingly use clean-agent (halogenated) extinguishers such as HFC-236fa as the preferred type, along with dry chemical (Purple K) as a secondary option. Clean-agent extinguishers are effective on Class B (flammable liquid) and Class C (electrical) fires without leaving corrosive residue, making them well-suited for cockpit electrical and fuel fires. Dry chemical extinguishers are effective on Class A (ordinary combustibles), Class B, and Class C fires, but they leave a corrosive powder residue that can damage avionics and airframe components. Most modern rotorcraft operations favor clean-agent extinguishers to avoid post-discharge damage and to comply with environmental regulations on Halon phase-out.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.C.S2.008	In a time-constrained briefing, the pilot must prioritize items in order of life-safety impact and likelihood of passenger use without pilot assistance. Non-negotiable items that cannot be skipped: (1) Exit locations and how to open them — passengers may need to egress without pilot assistance if the pilot is incapacitated; (2) ELT location and activation — this is the primary search and rescue locating tool; (3) Stay with the aircraft unless in immediate danger. Secondary priorities if time allows: (4) Signal mirror and whistle use — simple, high-impact, no skill required; (5) Space blanket — hypothermia is a leading cause of survival failure; (6) VHF radio operation — most valuable if the passenger can be shown the guard frequency (121.5 MHz) and push-to-talk in 15 seconds. Waterproof matches require the most instruction and carry risk of misuse; they should be mentioned last or deferred to a printed card. The key principle is: brief for the scenario where you are unable to assist. Every item briefed should answer the question — 'can this passenger use this alone if I am unconscious?'	->	In a time-constrained briefing, the pilot must prioritize items in order of life-safety impact and likelihood of passenger use without pilot assistance. Non-negotiable items that cannot be skipped: (1) Exit locations and how to open them — passengers may need to egress without pilot assistance if the pilot is incapacitated; (2) ELT location and activation — this is the primary search and rescue locating tool; (3) Stay with the aircraft unless in immediate danger. Secondary priorities if time allows: (4) Signal mirror and whistle use — simple, high-impact, no skill required; (5) Space blanket — hypothermia is a leading cause of survival failure; (6) VHF radio operation — most valuable if the passenger can be shown the guard frequency (121.5 MHz) and push-to-talk in 15 seconds. Waterproof matches require the most instruction and carry risk of misuse; they should be mentioned last or deferred to a printed card. The key principle is: brief for the scenario where you are unable to assist. Every item briefed should answer the question — 'can this passenger use this alone if I am unconscious?' Under 47 CFR 87.107 and FCC emergency exemptions, any person may transmit on 121.5 MHz during distress to summon assistance without requiring an FCC license.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.D.K1.008	Under 14 CFR 91.3(b), the pilot in command may deviate from any rule in Subpart B of Part 91 to the extent necessary to meet an emergency. However, the RFM is not a FAR — it is FAA-approved operating limitations and procedures established under the aircraft's type certificate or supplemental type certificate. For certificated aircraft, the RFM limitations (not just procedures) have regulatory force through 14 CFR 91.9. The emergency procedures section of the RFM is advisory in nature regarding the specific steps, but failing to follow RFM procedures in an emergency does not itself constitute a regulatory violation — it may, however, constitute a violation of 91.13 (careless or reckless operation) if the deviation was unreasonable. Practically: in an antitorque emergency, if the RFM says reduce power and execute a run-on landing but the pilot instead attempts a zero-airspeed hover landing because of terrain, the pilot has the authority to make that judgment call under 91.3(b) and must be prepared to justify the deviation. The standard applied is: what would a reasonably prudent pilot with the same training and experience have done given those specific circumstances?	->	Under 14 CFR 91.3(b), the pilot in command may deviate from any rule in Subpart B of Part 91 to the extent necessary to meet an emergency. However, the RFM is not itself a FAR — it is FAA-approved operating limitations and procedures established under the aircraft's type certificate or supplemental type certificate. For certificated aircraft, RFM procedures and limitations have regulatory force through 14 CFR 91.9 and are mandatory, not advisory. In an antitorque emergency, the pilot must follow RFM emergency procedures as the regulatory standard. Deviation from RFM procedures is permitted under 91.3(b) only when the specific emergency circumstances make strict RFM compliance impossible or would worsen the situation — for example, if the RFM specifies a run-on landing but terrain or circumstances make an autorotation landing necessary for survival. Any such deviation must be justified and defensible under the specific facts. The standard applied is: what would a reasonably prudent pilot with the same training and experience have done given those specific circumstances? Following RFM procedures exactly is the regulatory expectation and provides strong evidence of reasonable airmanship.
AI_EDIT	HI.XIV.D.S1.008	Autorotation is preferable when: (1) the tail rotor failure is accompanied by other system failures (engine failure simultaneously); (2) the terrain ahead is unsuitable for a run-on but an autorotation landing spot exists; (3) airspeed is already reduced below effective weathervaning speed and the helicopter is spinning uncontrollably. Entering autorotation reduces power to near zero, which eliminates torque production — this stops or greatly reduces the yaw. During autorotation, the main rotor is autorotating and engine torque is disconnected via the freewheeling unit, so tail rotor thrust demand drops dramatically. The tail rotor is still driven by the main rotor transmission during autorotation (not the engine), so it continues to produce thrust as long as rotor RPM is maintained. This means the pilot retains some heading control via pedals during the autorotation descent. The technique is: enter autorotation normally, use pedals for heading control (they will be effective), and execute a normal autorotation landing. The risk is that if rotor RPM decays, tail rotor thrust decays simultaneously, and heading control is lost at the worst moment — so maintaining rotor RPM is critical.	->	Autorotation is preferable when: (1) the tail rotor failure is accompanied by other system failures (simultaneous engine failure); (2) the terrain ahead is unsuitable for a run-on but an autorotation landing spot exists; (3) airspeed is already reduced below effective weathervaning speed and the helicopter is spinning uncontrollably. Entering autorotation reduces power to near zero, which eliminates engine torque production — this stops or greatly reduces the yaw. During autorotation, the main rotor continues to drive the tail rotor through the transmission as long as rotor RPM is maintained and the freewheeling unit functions properly, so the tail rotor produces thrust proportional to that RPM. This means the pilot retains some heading control via pedals during the autorotation descent. The technique is: enter autorotation normally, use pedals for heading control, and execute a normal autorotation landing. The critical risk is that if rotor RPM decays, tail rotor thrust decays simultaneously, and heading control is lost at the worst moment — so maintaining rotor RPM is critical.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.K1.005	On a steep approach into a confined area with tall obstacles, ETL (Effective Translational Lift) is lost at a higher altitude above the landing spot than on a normal shallow approach. This means the pilot must anticipate the loss of translational lift earlier and apply an increase in collective at a higher point during the descent to compensate. Failure to anticipate this early power demand can result in rapid decay of rotor RPM or an uncontrolled descent into obstacles. The pilot should verify available power margins during reconnaissance and plan the approach angle to cross obstacles with enough altitude to manage the deceleration and ETL transition while maintaining rotor RPM in the green arc.	->	On a steep approach into a confined area with tall obstacles, the deceleration phase and loss of translational lift occur at the same airspeed as a shallow approach (~16–24 knots), but this critical power deficit occurs at a higher altitude AGL above the landing zone. The pilot must anticipate the increased power requirement earlier in the descent and establish the collective increase sooner than in a normal approach to avoid rotor RPM decay while still at altitude above obstacles. The pilot should plan sufficient altitude buffer above the obstacles to manage the ETL transition, verify available power margins during reconnaissance, and maintain rotor RPM in the green arc throughout the deceleration and landing phase.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.K2.006	Both changes degrade performance. The 20°F temperature increase raises density altitude significantly — roughly 600–700 ft of additional density altitude per 10°F rise, so this could add 1,200–1,400 ft of effective density altitude. Combined with the additional 200 lbs of gross weight, the power required for OGE hover has increased substantially while available engine power has decreased due to hotter, less-dense air. What was adequate power margin at arrival may now be insufficient for a vertical or maximum performance departure. I would recalculate density altitude and re-check hover ceiling charts for the new weight and temperature. I would consider: offloading cargo or fuel, waiting for cooler conditions, or selecting the longest available takeoff path in the confined area to achieve ETL before obstacle clearance is required. This scenario illustrates why performance planning must be done for the departure conditions, not just arrival.	->	Both changes degrade performance. The 20°F temperature increase raises density altitude significantly — approximately 600–700 ft of additional density altitude per 10°F rise (varying with pressure altitude and humidity), so this could add roughly 1,200–1,400 ft of effective density altitude. Combined with the additional 200 lbs of gross weight, the power required for OGE hover has increased substantially while available engine power has decreased due to hotter, less-dense air. What was adequate power margin at arrival may now be insufficient for a vertical or maximum performance departure. I would recalculate density altitude and re-check hover ceiling charts for the new weight and temperature. I would consider: offloading cargo or fuel, waiting for cooler conditions, or selecting the longest available takeoff path in the confined area to achieve ETL before obstacle clearance is required. This scenario illustrates why performance planning must be done for the departure conditions, not just arrival. Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B, Chapter 10.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.K3.002	A confined area approach and landing is recommended when: (1) the only suitable landing zone is surrounded by obstacles such as trees, wires, or structures; (2) winds are calm or variable, requiring a landing in the most favorable wind direction available; (3) the intended landing site cannot be confirmed as obstacle-free from altitude and requires a closer inspection; (4) the landing surface is unknown and requires a slow, deliberate approach to assess condition; (5) performance is marginal and the approach angle must be adjusted to clear obstacles while maintaining OGE or IGE hover capability; and (6) rescue or off-airport operations demand precision placement in a restricted area.	->	A confined area approach and landing is recommended or required when: (1) the only suitable landing zone is surrounded by obstacles such as trees, wires, or structures; (2) winds are calm or variable, requiring a landing in the most favorable wind direction available rather than any direction; (3) the intended landing site cannot be confirmed as obstacle-free from altitude and requires closer inspection during approach; (4) the landing surface condition is unknown and a slow, deliberate approach is needed to assess landing suitability before touchdown; (5) rescue or off-airport operations demand precision placement within a restricted area. Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B, Chapter 10 (Confined Area Operations).
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.K4.002	A high reconnaissance is typically performed at 300–500 feet AGL at a normal cruise airspeed that allows adequate observation time. The altitude is selected to remain safely above obstacles while still providing a clear view of the landing zone, surface conditions, and surrounding terrain. It must also be high enough to allow a safe autorotation if power loss occurs during the orbit.	->	A high reconnaissance is typically performed at 500–1000 feet AGL (or higher depending on terrain and obstacle heights) at a normal cruise airspeed that allows adequate observation time. The altitude is selected to provide a clear view of the landing zone, surface conditions, and surrounding terrain while maintaining sufficient altitude to allow a safe autorotation to be completed to ground level if power is lost during the reconnaissance orbit. This altitude also ensures adequate time for the pilot to maintain situational awareness and plan the approach.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.K5.003	Three primary factors that reduce power available are: (1) High density altitude — as pressure altitude increases and/or temperature rises, air density decreases, reducing engine and rotor efficiency; (2) High gross weight — heavier aircraft require more power to hover and climb, shrinking the power margin; (3) High humidity — moist air is less dense than dry air, further reducing engine output and rotor effectiveness. Additional factors include degraded engine health, hot or contaminated fuel, and high winds (though wind can also be beneficial for ETL).	->	Three primary factors that reduce power available are: (1) High density altitude — as pressure altitude increases and/or temperature rises, air density decreases, reducing engine and rotor efficiency; (2) High gross weight — heavier aircraft require more power to hover and climb, shrinking the power margin; (3) High humidity — while moist air is actually less dense than dry air at the same temperature and pressure, high humidity typically accompanies warmer conditions that reduce the cooling effect on engines, effectively reducing available power output. Additional factors include degraded engine health, hot or contaminated fuel, and certain wind conditions.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.R4.005	During reconnaissance, the pilot must identify not only the intended landing spot and approach path, but also a specific go-around exit corridor — the lowest obstacle or widest gap in the surrounding terrain that can be used to escape if the approach is aborted. The pilot should identify: the direction and angle of the exit path, obstacles along that path, the altitude and airspeed at which a go-around becomes impractical (the abort point), wind direction relative to the exit (ideally into wind for maximum climb performance), and the power required to execute a successful go-around given current conditions. This planning must occur before beginning the approach because workload, narrow focus, and degrading energy state during the approach make in-flight replanning unreliable. The Trescott ADM principle applies: the pilot should commit to a plan known to work, not one that will 'probably' work.	->	During reconnaissance, the pilot must identify not only the intended landing spot and approach path, but also a specific go-around exit corridor — the lowest obstacle or widest gap in the surrounding terrain that can be used to escape if the approach is aborted. The pilot should identify: the direction and angle of the exit path, obstacles along that path, the altitude and airspeed at which a go-around becomes impractical (the abort point), wind direction relative to the exit (ideally into wind for maximum climb performance), and the power required to execute a successful go-around given current conditions. This planning must occur before beginning the approach because workload, narrow focus, and degrading energy state during the approach make in-flight replanning unreliable. Standard FAA decision-making frameworks emphasize that decisions made before committing to an action are more reliable than reactive replanning under stress — the pilot should commit to a plan known to work, not one that will probably work.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.R7.002	The critical rollover angle is the point during a dynamic rollover event at which the lateral component of main rotor thrust exceeds the helicopter's ability to be corrected with full opposite cyclic input. Beyond this angle — generally estimated at around 5 to 8 degrees depending on helicopter type — rollover is inevitable regardless of pilot input. It is significant because once this angle is exceeded, the only effective recovery is immediate, aggressive reduction of collective to eliminate rotor lift entirely, and even that may not prevent rollover at extreme angles.	->	The critical rollover angle is the point during a dynamic rollover event at which the lateral component of main rotor thrust exceeds the helicopter's ability to be corrected with full opposite cyclic input. This angle varies significantly by helicopter type, weight distribution, and rotor configuration and is typically in the range of 5 to 8 degrees — but is not a universal constant. Pilots must consult their aircraft POH for type-specific performance data. The critical angle is significant because once exceeded, rollover is inevitable regardless of pilot input. The only effective recovery is immediate, aggressive reduction of collective to eliminate rotor lift entirely, and even that may not prevent rollover at extreme angles.
ESCALATE	HI.XV.A.R10.002	Wind azimuth ranges for LTE conditions (tail rotor vortex ring state, main rotor disk vortex interference, and weathercock stability) vary by helicopter type, rotor direction, and source documentation; the specific degree ranges cited should be verified against the current FAA AC 90-95 and the type-specific aircraft being trained in order to avoid propagating potentially inaccurate numbers into the question bank.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.R11.008	Low sun angle creating direct glare during approach is a significant collision hazard multiplier in confined areas because it eliminates the pilot's primary hazard detection mechanism — visual acquisition. Mitigation strategies: (1) Conduct the recon from a sun angle that puts the glare to the side or eliminates it, even if this means flying the recon in a direction different from the intended approach; (2) Adjust the approach path angle or direction to take the sun out of the direct glare zone — a slightly offset approach heading may eliminate the problem while maintaining adequate obstacle clearance; (3) Use a ground observer positioned to the side or downrange to provide verbal obstacle clearance confirmation during the approach; (4) Increase approach timing so the sun angle changes (wait until the sun is lower or past the critical angle); (5) Use a steeper approach angle to limit the glare exposure time and distance. This becomes a no-go factor when: the pilot cannot visually acquire the LZ or any portion of the obstacle corridor during the approach despite all mitigation attempts, and no reliable alternate confirmation method (ground observer, known and verified clear path) is available. The word 'probably' clear is not acceptable — if you cannot confirm the path is clear, do not proceed. This directly applies the Trescott ADM principle cited in the HFH: if the outcome is only 'probably' satisfactory, choose an option known to work.	->	Low sun angle creating direct glare during approach is a significant collision hazard multiplier in confined areas because it eliminates the pilot's primary hazard detection mechanism — visual acquisition. Mitigation strategies: (1) Conduct the recon from a sun angle that puts the glare to the side or eliminates it, even if this means flying the recon in a direction different from the intended approach; (2) Adjust the approach path angle or direction to take the sun out of the direct glare zone — a slightly offset approach heading may eliminate the problem while maintaining adequate obstacle clearance; (3) Use a ground observer positioned to the side or downrange to provide verbal obstacle clearance confirmation during the approach; (4) Increase approach timing so the sun angle changes (wait until the sun is lower or past the critical angle); (5) Use a steeper approach angle to limit the glare exposure time and distance. This becomes a no-go factor when: the pilot cannot visually acquire the LZ or any portion of the obstacle corridor during the approach despite all mitigation attempts, and no reliable alternate confirmation method (ground observer, known and verified clear path) is available. The word 'probably' clear is not acceptable — if you cannot confirm the path is clear, do not proceed. This directly applies the Trescott ADM principle: if the outcome is only 'probably' satisfactory, choose an option known to work. Additionally, note that if waiting for the sun angle change will result in a night departure and you are not night current per 14 CFR 61.57(b), you would violate 14 CFR 61.3(e) by acting as PIC — such a time-pressure scenario is a no-go factor regardless of mitigation attempts.
ESCALATE	HI.XV.A.R12.002	The verifier raises a credible technical concern that the hub-to-tip stall progression description may contradict current FAA-H-8083-21B aerodynamic explanations of VRS, which describe more uniform disk disruption; this requires expert aerodynamics review against the actual handbook to confirm whether the original answer or the suggested correction is correct.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.R14.003	The FAA states that approximately 80% of aviation accidents are caused by pilot error (human factors), and that accidents rarely result from a single poor decision — they typically result from a chain of multiple errors accumulating over time. If a pilot becomes distracted during the reconnaissance of a confined area and misses a wire, slope condition, or escape route assessment, that single missed item becomes the first link in the error chain. As the approach progresses, subsequent poor judgments or distractions add links. The key principle is that breaking even one link — stopping to re-evaluate, going around, or asking a crewmember to confirm — can prevent the accident. Pilots should treat any accumulation of problems as a warning sign.	->	The FAA addresses the error chain concept in FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13, emphasizing that accidents typically result from a chain of multiple errors accumulating over time rather than a single poor decision. If a pilot becomes distracted during the reconnaissance of a confined area and misses a wire, slope condition, or escape route assessment, that single missed item becomes the first link in the error chain. As the approach progresses, subsequent poor judgments or distractions add links. The key principle is that breaking even one link — stopping to re-evaluate, going around, or asking a crewmember to confirm — can prevent the accident. Pilots should treat any accumulation of problems as a warning sign.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.S2.005	Under 14 CFR 91.185, the lost communications procedures apply in IMC, but in VMC you are expected to continue using best judgment. For a Part 135 operation, the Operations Specifications and company procedures govern lost comms, and you must follow them. In VMC, you may continue to a safe landing provided you can maintain see-and-avoid and the airspace does not require two-way communication (e.g., you are not in Class D/C/B). If ground intercom with the LZ coordinator is functional, coordinate the approach via that channel, confirm the LZ is clear, and proceed. You should squawk 7600 if a transponder is installed. After landing, notify company and ATC as required by Part 135 ops specs. The key principle is that you must positively confirm the LZ is clear before descending, using all available means including ground personnel.	->	For a Part 135 EMS operation in VMC, 14 CFR 91.185 (lost communications procedures) applies only to IFR flight and does not govern VMC operations. Instead, your Part 135 Operations Specifications and company procedures govern lost comms actions. In VMC outside controlled airspace, you may continue flight using see-and-avoid and maintain contact via any available means (e.g., ground intercom with LZ coordinator). If the intercom with the LZ coordinator is functional, coordinate the approach via that channel and confirm the LZ is clear before descending. Squawk 7600 is primarily used for IFR flights and has limited application in VMC uncontrolled airspace. After landing, notify company dispatch and ATC as required by your Part 135 ops specs. The key principle is maintaining positive confirmation that the LZ is clear before descent, using all available means including ground personnel.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.S5.002	The high reconnaissance is typically performed at approximately 300 to 500 feet AGL at a normal cruise or reduced airspeed — high enough to see the entire area without losing detail, and slow enough to evaluate all relevant factors. The altitude allows you to assess the overall landing zone, surrounding terrain, wind indicators (smoke, vegetation movement), and obstacle heights relative to your intended approach and departure paths while maintaining safe obstacle clearance throughout the orbit.	->	High reconnaissance should be performed at an altitude high enough to see the entire area and surrounding terrain without losing detail — often 300–500 feet AGL, but adjusted based on obstacle heights and terrain elevation to maintain safe clearance. Airspeed should be slow enough (normal cruise or reduced) to evaluate surface conditions, wind indicators (smoke, vegetation), and obstacle positions relative to intended approach and departure paths, while remaining fast enough to maintain adequate flying speed and control authority. The specific altitude and speed are selected to ensure complete visibility of the landing zone, adequate obstacle clearance, and ability to execute a safe go-around if needed.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.S6.003	The termination point should ideally be as far into the confined area as possible — specifically near the upwind edge of the landing zone. This maximizes the available distance for the approach over the lowest obstacles, provides the longest possible landing run if required, and ensures the helicopter is pointed into the wind for the departure. It also keeps the tail clear of obstacles during the approach flare.	->	The termination point should be selected to maximize safe approach distance over entry-point obstacles while maintaining adequate clearance margins, and it should be positioned to allow a stabilized, safe final approach. The exact position depends on obstacle locations and required approach angle — not merely as far into the area as possible, which could reduce approach safety margins. The termination point should also be positioned to leave the helicopter pointed favorably into the wind for departure, and to keep the tail clear of obstacles during approach and landing.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.S7.004	If the termination point appears to be rising in the windshield, you are undershooting — your approach angle is too shallow and you are descending below the desired glidepath. Left uncorrected, you risk striking obstacles at the entry point or landing short of the intended termination point. The correct correction is to reduce collective slightly to increase descent rate, or adjust cyclic to steepen the approach angle while monitoring airspeed and rotor RPM. You should also reassess whether sufficient obstacle clearance remains, and be prepared to execute a go-around if the approach becomes unacceptable.	->	If the termination point is rising in the windshield, you are overshooting — your approach angle is too steep and/or your closure rate is too fast. You risk landing long of the intended termination point or overflight. The correct correction is to reduce forward speed by adding back cyclic and add collective to cushion the descent rate, stabilizing the approach. If instead the termination point appears lower in the windshield than desired, you are undershooting; reduce collective slightly to increase descent rate or lower the nose to increase forward speed and steepen the approach angle. In either case, continuously assess obstacle clearance and be prepared to execute a go-around if the approach becomes unacceptable.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.S9.008	The most likely cause is the candidate reducing collective too aggressively during the initial departure acceleration — possibly lowering collective to gain airspeed by 'pushing through' ETL onset — which suddenly unloads rotor drag and allows Nr to overspeed into the upper yellow or even red arc. This is especially common in candidates who over-correct for the translational lift surge felt as the helicopter accelerates. On governed turbine helicopters, the governor normally prevents overspeed, but on piston helicopters or during a governor malfunction, the candidate's collective management directly controls Nr. Prolonged operation above the green arc causes centrifugal stress on rotor blade attachments, hub components, and the transmission — potentially exceeding component fatigue limits. Most RFMs require maintenance inspection if Nr exceeds specific limits, and some overspeed events can render the aircraft unairworthy until inspected. The examiner should debrief this specifically and ask the candidate to explain what they felt and why they made that collective input.	->	The most likely cause is too-aggressive reduction of collective pitch during the acceleration phase, which decreases rotor drag faster than the engine/governor system can respond. On non-governed (piston) helicopters, this creates uncontrolled Nr overspeed. On governed (turbine) helicopters, the governor should normally prevent this; if overspeed occurs, it indicates either a governor malfunction or the pilot has overshot the collective reduction during translational lift transition. The candidate may be failing to increase engine RPM demand proportionally during acceleration, or misjudging the collective input needed to transition through ETL. Prolonged operation above the green arc causes centrifugal stress on rotor blade attachments, hub components, and the transmission—potentially exceeding component fatigue limits. Most RFMs require maintenance inspection if Nr exceeds specific limits, and overspeed events can render the aircraft unairworthy until inspected. After landing, the pilot must consult the Rotorcraft Flight Manual and maintenance manual to determine inspection requirements before the next flight per 14 CFR 91.7 and 91.409.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.S12.006	ETL is typically achieved around 16–24 knots of airspeed (approximately 16 knots is commonly cited). During a steep departure, the helicopter climbs more vertically and accelerates to ETL later in the profile, meaning it operates longer in the less efficient, higher-power-required regime. On a steep approach back into the area, ETL is lost at a higher altitude above the landing zone than on a normal approach — the helicopter transitions out of ETL earlier in the descent. This means the pilot must increase collective earlier on a steep approach to compensate for the loss of ETL-generated lift, or risk an excessive sink rate. Anticipating this power demand is critical to safe confined area operations.	->	ETL is typically achieved around 16–24 knots of airspeed (approximately 16 knots is commonly cited). During a steep departure, the helicopter climbs more vertically and accelerates to ETL later in the profile, meaning it operates longer in the less efficient, higher-power-required regime. On a steep approach back into the confined area, because the descent path is more vertical, the helicopter will be at a higher altitude AGL when it decelerates back through ETL (approximately 16 knots). This means the pilot must increase collective earlier in the descent profile to prevent an excessive sink rate as lift from ETL decreases. The critical principle is that ETL is lost at the same airspeed regardless of approach angle, but on a steep approach the geometric relationship means greater power compensation is needed over a longer altitude range.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.A.S13.001	SRM stands for Single-Pilot Resource Management. Its core components include risk management, situational awareness (SA), controlled flight into terrain (CFIT) awareness, workload management, and automation management. In confined area operations, these components work together to help the solo pilot evaluate the landing zone, manage the approach profile, identify escape routes, and avoid task saturation during a high-workload phase of flight.	->	SRM stands for Single-Pilot Resource Management. Its core components include: (1) Risk Management (using the 5P model: Plan, Plane, Pilot, Passengers, Programming); (2) Situational Awareness (accurate perception and understanding of current conditions); (3) Workload Management (prioritizing tasks and avoiding task saturation); (4) Decision-Making (using the DECIDE model or similar structured approach); and (5) Automation Management. In confined area operations, the pilot integrates these components to evaluate the landing zone, manage the approach profile, identify escape routes, avoid CFIT, and maintain control during a high-workload phase of flight.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.K4.008	The most likely phenomenon is that the helicopter has entered a downdraft or mechanical turbulence caused by wind flowing over the ridgeline and separating into a rotor-like downwash on the leeward side — commonly called a 'rotor' or lee-side rotor effect. Additionally, if the departure is not directly into the wind, the helicopter may be in a region of downflow that is reducing effective rotor lift. There is also the possibility that as the helicopter moved forward over the edge, it departed ground effect (if any existed on the narrow pinnacle) simultaneously with a power-demanding maneuver, and density altitude has reduced the available power to arrest the sink. The correct technique: do not pull additional collective beyond power limits — exceeding torque or turbine limits will only damage the aircraft without providing additional lift, and could cause engine failure or rotor RPM decay. Instead, immediately nose forward to accelerate and attempt to gain translational lift — the terrain dropping away below provides the altitude cushion to trade for airspeed. The moment ETL is achieved, climb performance will improve significantly. The pilot must maintain rotor RPM within limits throughout. If rotor RPM begins decaying, down-collective may paradoxically be required momentarily to unload the disk and allow RPM recovery before re-applying power. This scenario underscores why pinnacle departures must be planned with adequate abort altitude and power margin, and why departure directly into the wind is critical.	->	The most likely phenomenon during a forward-moving departure over a downslope edge is mechanical turbulence or a downdraft caused by wind separation over the ridgeline (lee-side rotor effect), or loss of ground effect if any existed on the narrow pinnacle. The helicopter may also be at high density altitude, reducing available power margin. The correct technique is: do not pull additional collective beyond power limits, as this will not provide additional lift and risks engine damage or rotor RPM decay. Instead, immediately nose forward to accelerate and attempt to gain translational lift — the terrain dropping away provides the altitude cushion to trade for airspeed. Once ETL is achieved, climb performance will improve significantly. Maintain rotor RPM within limits throughout. If rotor RPM begins decaying, momentarily reduce collective to unload the disk and allow RPM recovery before reapplying power. This scenario underscores why pinnacle departures require adequate abort altitude, power margin, and departure directly into the wind. Note: Vortex ring state (VRS) is less likely during a forward-moving departure; VRS typically develops at hover or very low airspeed with high power setting and is characterized by an accelerating descent rate despite increasing collective, along with vibration and control mushiness.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.K5.003	A high reconnaissance is typically conducted at approximately 500 feet AGL above the highest obstacle or the intended landing area. This altitude provides a safe buffer to assess the site without entering potential hazard zones such as wire strikes or rotor-to-terrain conflicts, while still giving the pilot sufficient visibility to evaluate wind indicators, approach and departure corridors, surface conditions, and overall site suitability. It also keeps the aircraft in a favorable energy state if an immediate abort is required.	->	A high reconnaissance is typically conducted at several hundred feet AGL (commonly 500 feet or more above the highest obstacle), though the exact altitude should be adjusted based on terrain steepness, visibility, and aircraft performance. This altitude provides a safe buffer to assess the site without entering potential hazard zones such as wire strikes or rotor-to-terrain conflicts, while still giving the pilot sufficient visibility to evaluate wind indicators, approach and departure corridors, surface conditions, and overall site suitability. It also keeps the aircraft in a favorable energy state if an immediate abort is required.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.K6.002	A steep approach to a pinnacle causes the helicopter to lose ETL at a higher altitude than a normal approach would. This is dangerous because the pilot must increase collective earlier and more aggressively to compensate for the loss of ETL-derived lift. If the pilot is not anticipating this, they may be caught behind the power demand, resulting in a high rate of descent close to the surface with insufficient power or rotor RPM to arrest it. The error is typically failing to recognize the steeper approach profile requires earlier collective management.	->	A steep approach to a pinnacle reduces the pilot's energy state and safety margin. If the approach is too steep or conducted at insufficient airspeed, the pilot may not achieve or maintain effective translational lift before needing to arrest descent close to the surface. Additionally, a steep approach requires increased collective demand early; if the pilot delays collective application while focusing on descent rate or approach geometry, they can quickly run out of available power and rotor RPM margin. The error is typically inadequate airspeed management on approach, delayed recognition of increased power requirements, or committing to landing before adequate performance margin exists. This leaves insufficient altitude and power to level off safely if conditions prove unsuitable.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.R4.006	The statement reflects a false sense of security — the assumption that a go-around is always available at a pinnacle. The risk management gap is the failure to pre-plan the go-around before the approach begins. At a pinnacle, a go-around may not be possible at all points in the approach due to power limitations, terrain obstruction of the escape route, or an airspeed condition below ETL. The student should understand that go-around viability must be assessed during the high reconnaissance — identifying the escape route, verifying power margins, confirming wind direction, and establishing a clear abort decision point (e.g., 'If I'm not stabilized by X altitude/airspeed, I go around immediately'). The word 'probably' (as in 'I can probably go around') should be a red flag — if a go-around is only probable, a different approach strategy or abort before entry is warranted.	->	The statement reflects a false sense of security — the assumption that a go-around is always available at a pinnacle. The risk management gap is the failure to pre-plan the go-around before the approach begins. At a pinnacle, a go-around may not be possible at all points in the approach due to power limitations, terrain obstruction of the escape route, or an airspeed condition below ETL. The student should understand that go-around viability must be assessed during the high reconnaissance — identifying the escape route, verifying power margins, confirming wind direction, and establishing a clear abort decision point (e.g., 'If I'm not stabilized by X altitude/airspeed, I go around immediately'). The word 'probably' (as in 'I can probably go around') should be a red flag — if a go-around is only probable, a different approach strategy or abort before entry is warranted. Regulatory Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 10 (Reconnaissance Procedures); FAA-H-8083-25A Chapter 13 (ADM/Risk Management).
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.R9.008	The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that accident rates correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours, and that accident rates decrease significantly after 100 hours in type. At 30 hours in type, this pilot is in a statistically higher-risk period. Specific concerns include: unfamiliarity with the aircraft's RPM decay rate — turbine helicopters vary significantly in rotor inertia and how quickly RPM responds to collective inputs; not yet having an ingrained 'feel' for when the aircraft is approaching its power limits; and possible unfamiliarity with the specific audio/visual low RPM warning system in this aircraft and how it correlates to actual RPM state. Risk management strategies should include: flying with an experienced check pilot or instructor for initial pinnacle familiarization in this specific model; establishing a personal minimum power margin (e.g., refusing approaches if OGE hover capability is not comfortably confirmed); briefing the low RPM warning system and practicing the correct response (lower collective) until it is reflexive; starting with benign pinnacles at lower altitudes/density altitudes before progressing to challenging sites; and applying conservative personal minimums throughout the SRM process. The word 'probably' should never be applied to power margins at pinnacle sites — if there is uncertainty about whether power is adequate, the correct choice is to not attempt the operation.	->	The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that accident rates correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours, and that accident rates decrease significantly after 100 hours in type. At 30 hours in type, this pilot is in a statistically higher-risk period. Specific concerns include: unfamiliarity with the aircraft's RPM decay rate — turbine helicopters vary significantly in rotor inertia and how quickly RPM responds to collective inputs; not yet having an ingrained 'feel' for when the aircraft is approaching its power limits; and possible unfamiliarity with the specific audio/visual low RPM warning system in this aircraft and how it correlates to actual RPM state. Risk management strategies should include: flying with an experienced check pilot or instructor for initial pinnacle familiarization in this specific model; establishing a personal minimum power margin (e.g., refusing approaches if OGE hover capability is not comfortably confirmed); briefing the low RPM warning system and practicing the correct response (lower collective) until it is reflexive; starting with benign pinnacles at lower altitudes/density altitudes before progressing to challenging sites; and applying conservative personal minimums throughout the SRM process. The word 'probably' should never be applied to power margins at pinnacle sites — if there is uncertainty about whether power is adequate, the correct choice is to not attempt the operation. **Regarding the follow-up:** Torque remaining is not a fully reliable indicator that low rotor RPM is not a risk. Torque is an engine output parameter — it tells you how hard the engine is working, not what the rotor RPM is doing. Several scenarios can cause low rotor RPM even with apparent torque margin: a governor malfunction or droop can allow RPM to decay even with engine torque available; in piston helicopters, a throttle-collective correlation issue can cause RPM decay even before torque limits are reached; rapid collective application can cause a momentary RPM sag that the engine has not yet responded to. Additionally, a 15% torque margin that looks comfortable in cruise may disappear instantly when transitioning to OGE hover at a high-density-altitude pinnacle. The pilot must monitor rotor RPM directly and continuously — it is the definitive indicator of rotor system health, not torque alone. Torque and RPM must both be within limits simultaneously. When low rotor RPM is detected, the only correct initial action is to lower collective to reduce blade pitch and rotor drag; aft cyclic does not address the root cause (excessive blade pitch) and risks driving the tail into terrain at a pinnacle.
ESCALATE	HI.XV.B.R12.002	The specific wind azimuth ranges stated (285–315°, 210–330°, 120–240°) do not align with standard FAA-H-8083-21B guidance and are stated with unsupported precision; the verifier's concern about conflation of distinct LTE phenomena with overly specific degree ranges is valid and requires source verification before shipping.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.R12.006	Main rotor vortex ring state occurs when the helicopter descends into its own rotor downwash, typically during a steep approach at low airspeed with a high power setting. Tail rotor vortex ring state is a different phenomenon where the tail rotor ingests its own recirculating tip vortices, reducing net thrust output even though the tail rotor is spinning normally and pitch inputs are being made — the pilot may apply full left pedal with little or no effect. At a pinnacle, winds from approximately 210° to 330° relative to the aircraft nose (left rear quartering) can cause the tail rotor's own exhaust to recirculate back into its disk, creating this vortex ring condition. The turbulent, swirling airflow common around elevated terrain makes this azimuth range particularly hazardous, and the condition can be insidious because pedal inputs appear to have diminishing returns without an obvious cause.	->	Main rotor vortex ring state occurs when the helicopter descends into its own rotor downwash, typically during a steep approach at low airspeed with a high power setting. Tail rotor vortex ring state is a different phenomenon where the tail rotor ingests its own recirculating tip vortices, reducing net thrust output even though the tail rotor is spinning normally and pitch inputs are being made — the pilot may apply full left pedal with little or no effect. At a pinnacle, winds from the left-rear quadrant (generally 180° to 315° relative to the aircraft nose) can cause the tail rotor's own exhaust to recirculate back into its disk, creating this vortex ring condition. The specific azimuth range may vary by aircraft type and rotor system design. The turbulent, swirling airflow common around elevated terrain makes these conditions particularly hazardous, and the condition can be insidious because pedal inputs appear to have diminishing returns without an obvious cause.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.R13.008	This is a compounding emergency where two separate limitations have merged: (1) Aircraft performance limitation — you are at max torque with no additional power available; (2) Aerodynamic limitation — vortex ring state (VRS) has developed because you were descending near vertical at low airspeed with power applied, exactly the condition a high-altitude pinnacle steep approach creates. VRS recovery requires: aggressive forward cyclic to gain airspeed and escape the recirculating vortex, simultaneous increase in collective — but you are already at max power, so collective increase may overspeed or overtorque the engine/transmission. At a pinnacle specifically: there is no altitude below to safely recover since terrain is immediately underneath. Recovery options: (1) If any altitude exists off the side of the pinnacle, aggressively apply forward or lateral cyclic to fly off the edge into clear air and recover with airspeed — accepting a lower altitude; (2) If directly over the pinnacle pad with no room, collective increase to attempt to cushion impact while keeping rotor RPM in range is the last resort. Prevention is the only real answer — never allow VRS conditions to develop on a pinnacle approach. Recognize warning signs (high descent rate, ineffective controls, increasing collective with worsening descent) and initiate go-around immediately at first indication.	->	This is a compounding emergency where two separate limitations have merged: (1) Aircraft performance limitation — you are at max continuous power with no additional power margin available; (2) Aerodynamic limitation — vortex ring state (VRS) has developed because you were descending near vertical at low airspeed with power applied, exactly the condition a high-altitude pinnacle steep approach creates. VRS recovery requires: aggressive forward cyclic to gain airspeed and escape the recirculating vortex is the PRIMARY recovery action. Collective increase is secondary — while you cannot increase available engine power further, you CAN increase collective pitch, though the resulting RPM response and torque load may be constrained by already being at power limits. At a pinnacle specifically: there is no altitude below to safely recover since terrain is immediately underneath. Recovery options: (1) If any altitude exists off the side of the pinnacle, aggressively apply forward or lateral cyclic to fly off the edge into clear air and recover with airspeed — accepting a lower altitude; (2) If directly over the pinnacle pad with no room, the aircraft will impact terrain. Prevention is the only acceptable risk mitigation — never allow VRS conditions to develop on a pinnacle approach. Recognize warning signs (high descent rate, ineffective controls, increasing collective with worsening descent) and initiate go-around immediately at first indication.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.R14.006	FAA research cited in the Helicopter Flying Handbook indicates that accidents correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours. Pilots are at elevated risk in the first 100 hours in a new aircraft type because they are still building the intuitive understanding of that aircraft's power margins, control harmony, rotor system response, and warning signs of impending problems. In this scenario, the pilot has extensive overall experience — which may create overconfidence — but limited type experience, meaning their mental model of the aircraft's actual performance envelope under gusty conditions at a pinnacle is not calibrated. This gap directly increases the risk of loss of SA, because the pilot may not recognize early cues (rotor RPM decay, torque approaching limits, abnormal vibration) as quickly as a pilot with more hours in type. Mitigation includes increased personal minimums for pinnacle operations until 100+ hours in type are accumulated.	->	FAA research cited in the Helicopter Flying Handbook indicates that accidents correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours. Pilots are at significantly elevated risk in the initial phase of type-specific experience because they are still building intuitive understanding of that aircraft's power margins, control harmony, rotor system response, and warning signs of impending problems. In this scenario, the pilot has extensive overall experience — which may create overconfidence — but limited type experience, meaning their mental model of the aircraft's actual performance envelope under gusty conditions at a pinnacle is not yet fully calibrated. This gap directly increases the risk of loss of SA, because the pilot may not recognize early cues (rotor RPM decay, torque approaching limits, abnormal vibration) as quickly as a pilot with more established experience in type. Mitigation includes increased personal minimums for pinnacle operations and deliberate skill-building in the aircraft type before undertaking high-risk operations in marginal conditions.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.S2.003	1. High density altitude — as altitude increases, air density decreases, reducing the mass airflow through the engine and rotor system, which lowers both engine power output and rotor efficiency. 2. High temperature — hot air is less dense than cool air, further reducing engine power output and rotor performance. 3. High humidity — water vapor displaces oxygen in the air mixture, reducing combustion efficiency and engine power. Together these factors can dramatically reduce the margin between power available and power required, and must be accounted for using POH/RFM performance charts before committing to any arrival or departure at a high pinnacle.	->	1. High pressure altitude — as altitude increases, air density decreases, reducing the mass airflow through the engine and rotor system, which lowers both engine power output and rotor efficiency. 2. High temperature — hot air is less dense than cool air, further reducing engine power output and rotor performance. 3. High gross weight — the combination of high density altitude conditions and higher operating weight increases power required while reducing power available. Together these factors can dramatically reduce the margin between power available and power required, and must be accounted for using POH/RFM performance charts before committing to any arrival or departure at a high pinnacle. (Note: Humidity has a minor effect on air density and is not a primary factor in turbine performance calculations.)
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.S3.007	For radio failure in VFR conditions, 14 CFR 91.185 does not technically apply — that rule governs IFR radio failure. In VFR, there is no specific regulatory procedure, but the guidance is to squawk 7600 on the transponder immediately to alert ATC of the communication failure. For the Class D airspace: you must receive an ATC clearance prior to entry — without radio communication, you cannot legally enter Class D. You should divert your departure route to remain clear of the Class D surface area and land as soon as practicable to resolve the radio issue. Pressing on through Class D without communication would violate 14 CFR 91.129. Squawking 7600 is important because ATC may attempt light gun signals if they can see you.	->	For radio failure in VFR conditions, 14 CFR 91.185(c) requires you to continue the flight under VFR and attempt to land as soon as practicable. Immediately squawk 7600 on the transponder to alert ATC of the communication failure; ATC may attempt to use light gun signals. For Class D airspace entry: 14 CFR 91.129 requires two-way radio communication before entering Class D. Without radio communication, you cannot legally enter the Class D surface area. You must divert your departure route to remain clear of the Class D controlled airspace and land at the nearest suitable location to resolve the radio issue before continuing flight.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.S4.003	The high reconnaissance is typically conducted at approximately 500 feet AGL above the highest obstacle in the area. This altitude provides a safe margin above terrain and obstacles while still allowing the pilot to observe the landing zone, assess wind indicators, identify approach and departure paths, and determine whether the area is suitable — all while remaining clear of the height-velocity (H-V) diagram's avoid curve.	->	The high reconnaissance is typically conducted at approximately 500–1000 feet AGL above the landing zone. This altitude provides a safe margin above terrain and obstacles while still allowing the pilot to observe the landing zone, assess wind indicators, identify approach and departure paths, and determine whether the area is suitable for landing. The pilot should ensure the reconnaissance altitude and flight path remain outside the height-velocity diagram's avoid curve based on pre-flight performance calculations.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.S5.008	At high density altitude, the air is less dense, which means the tail rotor produces less thrust for a given pitch angle, and the main rotor requires higher RPM or collective to generate the same lift. The combination of high altitude, high temperature, and the collective demand of transitioning to OGE hover can push the aircraft beyond its power available. The full left pedal indicates the tail rotor is at maximum pitch trying to counteract main rotor torque but is insufficient — this is a precursor to loss of tail rotor effectiveness (LTE) due to high power demand rather than a classic LTE scenario. Rotor RPM decay means power required is exceeding power available. The correct immediate action is: simultaneously lower collective to reduce torque load on the transmission and allow rotor RPM to recover, increase airspeed (accelerate forward) to regain ETL which reduces induced power demand, and exit the hover attempt. The pilot should not continue the pinnacle operation if OGE hover cannot be maintained at a safe RPM with control authority remaining — this confirms the aircraft is operating outside its performance envelope for that site under those conditions.	->	At high density altitude, the air is less dense, which means the tail rotor produces less thrust for a given pitch angle, and the main rotor requires higher RPM or collective to generate the same lift. The full left pedal and rotor RPM decay indicate the aircraft is operating at or beyond maximum available power at this density altitude — this is a power-limited condition, not loss of tail rotor effectiveness. The tail rotor is demanding maximum pitch because the main rotor's high torque load requires it, but the air density cannot support the thrust needed. Rotor RPM decay means power required is exceeding power available. The correct immediate action is to simultaneously lower collective to reduce power demand and allow rotor RPM to recover, then increase airspeed to regain effective translational lift which reduces induced power demand and improves overall power margins, and exit the hover attempt. The pilot should not continue the pinnacle operation if OGE hover cannot be maintained at a safe RPM with control authority remaining — this confirms the aircraft is operating outside its performance envelope for that site under those conditions. A hover power check performed at a safe altitude before pinnacle approach, in actual conditions at the pinnacle elevation, is the most reliable preflight safeguard against this situation.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.S6.001	The approach should be made into the wind and, where possible, along the upsloping terrain of the pinnacle. Flying along the upslope means that if a go-around is necessary, the pilot can fly away from rising terrain. Approaching over a dropoff (downsloping side) is preferred over approaching over rising terrain, because a rejected approach over a dropoff allows the helicopter to fly away into open air rather than into an obstacle.	->	The approach should be made into the wind. Terrain consideration should prioritize the go-around escape path: the approach should be oriented so that if a go-around is required, the helicopter can climb or fly away from rising terrain. Generally, this means approaching along the upslope side of the pinnacle so that a rejected approach climbs away from the peak. Approaching along or over the downsloping (dropoff) side should generally be avoided because a go-around from that direction may not provide adequate clearance from terrain or may offer no escape route into clear air.
AI_EDIT	HI.XV.B.S11.002	During a pinnacle approach, the helicopter descends toward an elevated landing area. As it approaches the pinnacle and decelerates through ETL airspeed, it is already at or near the surface elevation of the pinnacle. There is less altitude available to cushion the transition and the pilot must increase collective earlier to compensate for the power required when ETL is lost. On a flat approach, ETL is lost while the helicopter still has more altitude above the touchdown point, providing more time and altitude to manage the power increase.	->	ETL loss occurs when the helicopter slows to a specific airspeed (typically 10–15 knots depending on aircraft type) regardless of altitude above ground. During a pinnacle approach, as the helicopter approaches an elevated landing area while decelerating, ETL is lost while the helicopter is still several feet above the surface. The critical difference from a flat approach is that there is less altitude margin between ETL loss and the landing surface to manage the sudden power increase required when ETL is lost. On a flat approach, ETL is lost at higher altitude above the ground, providing more time and altitude to increase collective smoothly. This reduced margin on a pinnacle approach is why power planning and margin are critical.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVI.A.K3.001	A common error is failing to position the rotor blades perpendicular to the fuselage before engine shutdown and securing. If blades are not properly oriented before tying them down, the pilot may attempt to reposition them after rotor RPM has dropped to a potentially dangerous, unpredictable speed, creating a blade strike hazard. The FAA recommends positioning blades for securing as part of the normal shutdown checklist.	->	A common error is failing to position the rotor blades perpendicular to the fuselage (or in the configuration specified by the aircraft RFM) as part of the normal shutdown checklist while the rotor is still turning at a safe, controlled speed. If blade positioning is deferred until rotor RPM has dropped significantly, the pilot may face difficulty repositioning blades due to unpredictable rotor behavior, increasing the risk of blade strike or damage. Proper blade positioning during the normal shutdown sequence—before securing and tying down—prevents this hazard. Specific blade positioning procedures and timing vary by helicopter type and manufacturer; always follow the aircraft's Rotorcraft Flight Manual.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVI.A.R2.006	During hot refueling, the FAA requires the pilot to remain at the flight controls and be prepared to immediately shut down the aircraft and direct an emergency egress if a fire or other hazard develops. Passengers may remain on board only if they are briefed on the emergency exit procedures and can evacuate quickly. The fueling must be conducted with no smoking enforced in the area, proper bonding and grounding of the aircraft, and the fueling crew must be trained for hot fueling operations. The pilot is the final authority for safety and must monitor the entire fueling process from the cockpit.	->	During hot refueling, the FAA requires the pilot to remain at the flight controls and be prepared to immediately shut down the aircraft and direct an emergency egress if a fire or other hazard develops. Passengers may remain on board only if they are briefed on the emergency exit procedures and can evacuate quickly. The fueling must be conducted with no smoking enforced in the area, proper bonding and grounding of the aircraft, and the fueling crew must be trained for hot fueling operations. The pilot is the final authority for safety and must monitor the entire fueling process from the cockpit. (Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 8 Ground Operations and Safety; AC 91-34 Hot Refueling of Turbine Powered Aircraft; 14 CFR 91.3 Pilot in Command)
AI_EDIT	HI.XVI.A.S2.005	On a sloped surface, the primary risk is dynamic rollover during shutdown if the skids sink unevenly into soft ground, or blade/mast contact if the rotor disc tilts toward terrain on a steep slope. Key considerations: (1) Park with the uphill skid slightly lower if possible, or align the fuselage so the rotor disc has maximum terrain clearance in all directions; (2) Avoid parking where one skid could sink into soft or unstable ground; (3) Ensure rotor tip clearance from rising terrain or vegetation on the uphill side; (4) Keep personnel uphill and to the side — never downslope behind the tail; (5) Brief any passengers before they exit about directional hazards. The FAA recommends a maximum slope of approximately 5 degrees for most helicopter parking operations, though this is aircraft-specific per the RFM.	->	On a sloped surface, consult the Aircraft RFM for maximum allowable slope—limits typically range from 2 to 5 degrees depending on aircraft type. Key considerations: (1) Avoid positioning where one skid could sink unevenly into soft or unstable ground, as uneven settlement can trigger dynamic rollover during shutdown; (2) Orient the aircraft to ensure the rotor disc has maximum clearance from rising terrain or vegetation, particularly on the uphill side; (3) Position personnel uphill and to the side of the aircraft—never downslope or behind the tail rotor; (4) Brief any passengers before egress about directional hazards and safe exit routes. Always refer to the specific aircraft's RFM for slope limitations and parking procedures, as these vary significantly by helicopter type and design.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVI.A.S3.006	During hot refueling, the aircraft remains running with rotor turning, so the full shutdown and securing checklist is NOT completed. Instead, the pilot must follow the aircraft's specific hot refueling checklist or procedures found in the POH/RFM, which typically requires: avionics and non-essential electrical equipment off, passengers deplaned and clear of the aircraft, and the pilot remaining at the flight controls for the entire fueling evolution — ready to immediately execute an emergency shutdown and egress if a fuel fire or other emergency develops. The FAA explicitly states the pilot must remain at the controls and be prepared to shut down immediately during hot refueling. After fueling is complete and prior to departure, the normal before-takeoff checklist resumes. Completing only the appropriate checklist for the specific phase of operation (hot refuel vs. full shutdown) is an important distinction.	->	During hot refueling, the aircraft remains running with rotor turning, so the full shutdown and securing checklist is NOT completed. Instead, the pilot must follow the aircraft's specific hot refueling checklist or procedures found in the POH/RFM. Typical requirements include: passengers deplaned and clear of the aircraft, non-essential electrical loads minimized per POH direction (exact configuration is aircraft-specific and must be verified in the POH/RFM), and the pilot remaining at the flight controls for the entire fueling evolution — ready to immediately execute an emergency shutdown and egress if a fuel fire or other emergency develops. The pilot must be prepared to shut down immediately during hot refueling. After fueling is complete and prior to departure, the normal before-takeoff checklist resumes. Following the appropriate checklist for the specific phase of operation (hot refuel vs. full shutdown) and adhering to aircraft-specific POH/RFM procedures is an important distinction.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVI.A.S4.008	Immediately ground the aircraft and document the bird strike in the maintenance record with maximum detail: location of impact (inlet lip, o'clock position if identifiable), evidence observed (feather/biological residue, dent dimensions and location), flight phase when suspected to have occurred, all engine parameters during and after suspected event (ITT, NG/NF, torque, oil pressure/temp), and that no chip light or abnormal indications were observed. The aircraft requires a bird strike inspection per the engine manufacturer's maintenance manual before return to service. For a turbine engine, this typically includes: borescope inspection of the compressor stages for FOD or blade damage, inspection of the inlet screen if equipped, examination of the inlet lip dent against allowable damage limits in the structural repair manual, and possibly an oil filter check for metallic contamination. Regarding NTSB reporting under 49 CFR Part 830: a bird strike alone is not a reportable accident or incident unless it results in substantial aircraft damage or serious injury. However, the FAA Wildlife Strike Database (voluntary reporting via FAA Form 5200-7) strongly encourages all bird strike reports. If the borescope reveals compressor blade damage, the aircraft may now have 'substantial damage' as defined under 49 CFR 830.2, which would require an immediate NTSB notification (within 24 hours by most practical interpretations, per 49 CFR 830.5) and preservation of the aircraft. A formal report is due within 10 days per 49 CFR 830.15.	->	Immediately ground the aircraft and document the bird strike in the maintenance record with maximum detail: location of impact (inlet lip, o'clock position if identifiable), evidence observed (feather/biological residue, dent dimensions and location), flight phase when suspected to have occurred, all engine parameters during and after suspected event (ITT, NG/NF, torque, oil pressure/temp), and that no chip light or abnormal indications were observed. The aircraft requires a bird strike inspection per the engine manufacturer's maintenance manual before return to service. For a turbine engine, this typically includes: borescope inspection of the compressor stages for FOD or blade damage, inspection of the inlet screen if equipped, examination of the inlet lip dent against allowable damage limits in the structural repair manual, and an oil filter check for metallic contamination. Regarding NTSB reporting under 49 CFR Part 830: a bird strike alone with only minor dent and no compressor damage is not automatically reportable. However, all bird strike incidents should be reported to the FAA Wildlife Strike Database (voluntary reporting via FAA Form 5200-7). If the borescope reveals compressor blade damage that meets the definition of substantial damage (damage that affects structural strength, performance, or flight characteristics and requires major repair or replacement per 49 CFR 830.2), the aircraft may now have a reportable accident or incident. Substantial damage requires immediate notification to the NTSB by the most expeditious means available (typically phone/email within hours). A written report is due within 10 days per 49 CFR 830.15. Minor cosmetic blade damage that does not meet substantial damage criteria may not require NTSB notification but should still be documented and reported to the FAA Wildlife Strike Database.
