AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.A.K4.004	This is a direct checkride failure indicator. Even in VMC during IFR operations, 14 CFR 91.113 requires see-and-avoid procedures whenever visual conditions permit. The applicant should be managing cockpit workload so that FMS programming occurs during periods of lower traffic density, or broken into short intervals with frequent outside scans in between. Best practice is the sterile cockpit concept adapted for IFR flight: complete heads-down tasks quickly, scan outside, then return. The evaluator is required under HI.XVII.A.K4 to assess visual scanning throughout the entire test, so sustained heads-down fixation is a continuous evaluation failure, not a single event.	->	This is a checkride deficiency that should be documented and discussed with the applicant. Even in VMC during IFR operations, 14 CFR 91.113 requires see-and-avoid procedures whenever visual conditions permit. The applicant should manage cockpit workload so that FMS programming occurs during periods of lower traffic density or is broken into short intervals with frequent outside scans in between. Best practice is to use the sterile cockpit concept adapted for IFR flight: complete heads-down tasks quickly, scan outside, then return. Under HI.XVII.A.K4, the evaluator assesses visual scanning throughout the test. Depending on context (proximity to traffic, altitude, duration, and whether this is an isolated incident or pattern), sustained inattention to outside scanning could range from a minor deficiency note to an unsatisfactory rating in the visual scanning task. However, a single period of heads-down activity is not automatically a discontinuation unless it creates a safety hazard or represents a pattern of non-compliance with scanning requirements.
