ESCALATE	HI.XVII.C.K4.008	The answer reasonably addresses night IFR adaptation strategies, but the claim that 'tactile confirmation' and brief lighting adjustments fully satisfy the visual check requirement in low-light conditions lacks explicit FAA guidance; the distinction between tactile as supplement versus substitute remains operationally ambiguous and warrants clarification from helicopter-specific regulatory sources.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.C.R1.001	The standard three-step procedure is: (1) The pilot wanting to transfer control says 'You have the flight controls.' (2) The receiving pilot acknowledges by saying 'I have the flight controls.' (3) The transferring pilot confirms with 'You have the flight controls.' Each step is necessary to ensure there is never ambiguity about who is flying. A verbal acknowledgment alone is insufficient — the physical confirmation and verbal loop-close ensure both pilots have the same understanding at the same moment.	->	The standard three-step procedure is: (1) The pilot wanting to transfer control says 'You have the flight controls.' (2) The receiving pilot acknowledges by saying 'I have the flight controls' AND visually confirms they have physically assumed the controls. (3) The transferring pilot confirms 'You have the flight controls' after visually verifying the other pilot has the controls. Each step is necessary to ensure there is never ambiguity about who is flying. Both pilots must perform visual confirmation of the physical handoff — verbal acknowledgment alone is insufficient because a crewmember may not have actually grasped the controls due to distraction or miscommunication. The visual confirmation closes the loop and ensures no gap in positive aircraft control exists.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.C.S1.004	In an emergency or safety-critical situation, taking the controls physically is the immediate priority — safety comes first. However, operational command responsibility transfers when you verbally assert 'I have the flight controls' and the student acknowledges 'You have the flight controls,' followed by your confirmation 'You have the flight controls' — wait, in this case YOU are taking back control, so you say 'I have the flight controls,' the student must confirm 'You have the flight controls,' and you complete with 'I have the flight controls.' Even in urgent situations, once immediate control is established, the verbal exchange must be completed to eliminate any ambiguity. Under IFR in IMC, unambiguous control assignment is critical — dual inputs or neither pilot flying is especially dangerous in instrument conditions.	->	In an emergency or safety-critical situation, taking the controls physically is the immediate priority — safety comes first. However, operational command responsibility is formalized when the three-step positive exchange is completed unambiguously. When the instructor takes control, the sequence is: (1) Instructor says 'I have the flight controls,' (2) Student acknowledges 'You have the flight controls,' (3) Instructor confirms 'You have the flight controls.' The verbal exchange must be completed to eliminate any ambiguity about who is in control. Under IFR in IMC, unambiguous control assignment is critical — dual inputs or neither pilot flying is especially dangerous in instrument conditions.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.C.S2.001	Visually confirming the exchange means the pilot transferring controls watches the other pilot's hands physically take hold of the cyclic, collective, and anti-torque pedals before releasing their own. Verbal acknowledgment alone is insufficient because a crewmember may not have actually grasped the controls — they may have misheard, been distracted, or responded out of habit. The visual confirmation closes the loop and ensures no gap in positive aircraft control exists. The FAA three-step positive exchange procedure is: 'You have the controls' → 'I have the controls' → 'You have the controls' (confirming handoff visually).	->	Visually confirming the exchange means the pilot transferring controls watches the other pilot's hands physically take hold of the cyclic, collective, and anti-torque pedals throughout the handoff, not just at the final verbal step. Verbal acknowledgment alone is insufficient because a crewmember may not have actually grasped the controls — they may have misheard, been distracted, or responded out of habit. The visual confirmation ensures no gap in positive aircraft control exists. The FAA three-step positive exchange procedure is: (1) 'You have the controls' (2) 'I have the controls' (3) 'You have the controls' — this verbal sequence must occur simultaneously with the transferring pilot visually confirming that the receiving pilot has physically assumed the controls.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.C.S2.002	Step 1: The pilot initiating the exchange says 'You have the controls.' Step 2: The receiving pilot physically grasps the cyclic, collective, and anti-torque pedals and responds 'I have the controls.' Step 3: The transferring pilot confirms visually that the other pilot has the controls, releases them, and responds 'You have the controls.' The transferring pilot must visually watch the receiving pilot's hands and feet make contact with all flight controls before fully releasing them. This three-step process prevents inadvertent dual-input or a momentary no-input situation.	->	The three-step verbal exchange is: (1) Pilot A (transferring): 'You have the flight controls.' (2) Pilot B (receiving): 'I have the flight controls.' (3) Pilot A (confirming): 'You have the flight controls.' Throughout this exchange, the transferring pilot must visually confirm that the receiving pilot has grasped the cyclic, collective, and anti-torque pedals with a positive grip before fully relinquishing control. The visual confirmation occurs concurrently with the verbal exchange, not as a separate step, and ensures that no moment exists where controls are unattended or subject to inadvertent dual input.
