AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.E.K4.004	The Notice of Disapproval would show at least two failed tasks: (1) the specific task that was failed due to the ADM lapse — in this case the instrument approach task — because the applicant's decision-making directly degraded task performance; and (2) the ADM Skill element from the Flight Deck Management Task. This is a direct application of HI.XVII.E.K4: any ADM failure in any task results in that task being failed AND the Flight Deck Management ADM Skill element being added to the disapproval. The evaluator does not have discretion to waive the Flight Deck Management notation.	->	The Notice of Disapproval would list the instrument approach task as failed. The basis for that failure would be cited as the ADM Skill element from the Flight Deck Management section of the ACS. This reflects HI.XVII.E.K4: any ADM failure during task performance causes that task to be failed, with the evaluator noting the ADM deficiency from the Flight Deck Management Skill element as the regulatory authority for the failure.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.E.K4.007	This scenario as described would generally not be a valid disapproval under the ACS framework as written. HI.XVII.E.K4 specifies that the Flight Deck Management ADM Skill element is added to the Notice of Disapproval when an applicant fails to use ADM in 'any Task' — meaning the ADM deficiency must be connected to a specific task failure. The ADM Skill notation is a secondary consequence of a task failure, not a standalone failure mode. If every task was passed to standards, the evaluator would need to identify which specific task the ADM failure was embedded in. An evaluator who disapproves solely on the ADM element without linking it to a task-level failure would have a weak basis for that disapproval and the applicant could appeal to the FSDO.	->	Yes, this is a valid disapproval. Flight Deck Management is a discrete ACS task with ADM as a Skill element. If the evaluator observes pervasive ADM deficiencies throughout the checkride—such as fixation, poor workload management, or failure to cross-check—the evaluator may fail the Flight Deck Management task itself based on the ADM Skill element, even if individual maneuver tasks remained within tolerances. The evaluator would cite the Flight Deck Management task failure (anchored to the ADM Skill element) as the basis for disapproval. This is a valid and defensible failure mode under the ACS framework.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.E.R1.006	The 'probably' principle — sometimes called the Trescott Tip — means that if your plan will only 'probably' work out, you should choose a course of action that is known to work. Applied to an autorotation to a confined area: if I am setting up and think 'this will probably work out,' that is a signal I am accepting unquantified risk. In a real or simulated autorotation, if I am not confident in my altitude, energy state, and LZ suitability, the sound ADM decision is to execute a go-around or power recovery rather than continue. During the checkride, demonstrating that I recognize 'probably' is not good enough — and choosing the known-safe option — is exactly what the examiner is looking for in terms of risk management judgment.	->	This ADM principle means that if your plan will only 'probably' work out, you should choose a course of action that is known to work reliably. Applied to an autorotation to a confined area: if you are setting up and think 'this will probably work out,' that signals unquantified risk acceptance. In a real or simulated autorotation, if you are not confident in your altitude, energy state, and landing zone suitability, the sound ADM decision is to execute a go-around or power recovery rather than continue. During the checkride, demonstrating that you recognize 'probably' is not good enough and choosing the known-safe option is exactly what the examiner is looking for in terms of risk management judgment.
ESCALATE	HI.XVII.E.R2.001	The five-competency framework listed does not align with standard FAA SRM definitions in FAA-H-8083-21B; the verifier correctly notes conflation of SRM competencies with ADM elements, and the exact framework requires verification against current FAA publications.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.F.K3.001	During simulated powerplant failure practice, the pilot deliberately reduces collective to simulate autorotation. If the actual engine fails or is accidentally shut down at that moment, the helicopter is already in a low-power or no-power state. Additionally, if rotor RPM decays too far during the simulation, it may not be recoverable even with engine power restored — leaving a forced landing as the only option. The simulation creates a condition where the margin between safety and an actual forced landing is narrow.	->	During simulated powerplant failure practice, the pilot deliberately reduces collective to simulate autorotation. The critical risk is rotor RPM decay: if RPM drops below the minimum autorotation RPM specified in the POH/RFM, it becomes unrecoverable even if engine power is restored, leaving a forced landing as the only option. This is why POH/RFM procedures specify entry speeds and RPM limits—they define the safe window. The simulation creates a condition where the margin between the safe RPM operating range and an actual unrecoverable condition is narrow, which is why altitude minimums and POH/RFM procedures are mandatory.
ESCALATE	HI.XVII.F.K4.001	The question does not specify which pilot certificate level (Private, Commercial, or Instrument), yet the answer cites only the Instrument Rating ACS; landing site standards may differ by certificate level, and this ambiguity requires clarification against the applicable ACS standard.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.F.S1.007	This argument reflects complacency, which the FAA identifies as one of the most dangerous hazardous attitudes. The ACS requires this briefing as a skills item — not administrative paperwork — because accident data shows approximately 80% of aviation accidents are caused by human factors and pilot error, not mechanical failures. The 'error chain' concept from FAA-H-8083-21B illustrates that most accidents result from multiple compounding failures; the pre-flight briefing is specifically designed to break one of those links before the maneuver begins. Experienced pilots are not immune — in fact, familiarity breeds complacency. Cases where instructors and students both assumed the other would recover power, resulting in actual autorotations or ground contact, are exactly the scenario this briefing prevents. The briefing also ensures that if aircraft controls are configured differently from the last aircraft flown, or if a new student has different expectations, nothing is assumed. Every simulation requires a fresh briefing because no two flights, students, or aircraft configurations are identical.	->	This argument reflects complacency, which the FAA identifies as one of the most dangerous hazardous attitudes. The ACS requires this briefing as a skills item—not administrative paperwork—because accident data shows that human factors contribute to approximately 70–80% of aviation accidents (per NTSB and FAA data), with the specific percentage varying by accident type. The 'error chain' concept from FAA-H-8083-21B illustrates that most accidents result from multiple compounding failures; the pre-flight briefing is specifically designed to break one of those links before the maneuver begins. Experienced pilots are not immune—in fact, familiarity breeds complacency. Cases where instructors and students both assumed the other would recover power, resulting in actual autorotations or ground contact, are exactly the scenario this briefing prevents. The briefing also ensures that if aircraft controls are configured differently from the last aircraft flown, or if a new student has different expectations, nothing is assumed. Every simulation requires a fresh briefing because no two flights, students, or aircraft configurations are identical. An instructor who skips the required pre-flight briefing and an accident results could face enforcement action under 14 CFR 91.13 (careless or reckless operation) and potential certificate action under 14 CFR 61.415 for failure to meet flight instructor responsibilities.
