AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.G.K2.005	No, this does not count against the applicant. The ACS standard at HI.XVII.G.K2 specifically addresses discontinuations caused by the applicant's lack of judgment or skill. An ATC-mandated go-around is an external factor unrelated to applicant performance. In this case, the examiner would typically allow a repeat of the task. The key distinction is causation: if the examiner's intervention was driven by something the applicant did wrong, the task fails; if it was driven by an external operational factor, it does not.	->	No, this does not count against the applicant. An ATC-mandated go-around due to traffic or runway conditions is an external factor entirely unrelated to applicant performance or judgment. The ACS standard addresses discontinuations caused by the applicant's lack of skill or judgment. In this case, the examiner would not mark the task as unsatisfactory. The key distinction is causation: if the examiner's intervention was driven by something the applicant did wrong (poor descent rate, loss of control, inability to establish a stabilized approach), the task fails; if it was driven by an external operational factor beyond the applicant's control, it does not.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.G.K3.001	No. The ACS specifically states that landing area safety concerns beyond the control of the applicant or evaluator that necessitate a go-around will not be considered unsatisfactory. A vehicle entering the landing area is a hazard entirely outside your control, and executing a go-around in that situation reflects sound aeronautical decision-making.	->	No. A vehicle or other hazard entering the landing area is entirely outside the applicant's control and represents a legitimate safety concern that necessitates a go-around. This does not count against you as unsatisfactory. The ACS specifies that unsafe landing area conditions beyond the applicant's control do not constitute a failed task. Executing a go-around in response to this hazard demonstrates sound aeronautical decision-making and risk management.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.G.S1.003	When collective pitch is increased and engine power is applied, the main rotor generates increased torque on the fuselage. Without a corresponding antitorque pedal input (right pedal in most US helicopters with counterclockwise rotor rotation — actually left pedal is added to counteract torque in counter-clockwise rotor systems), the aircraft will yaw in the opposite direction of rotor rotation. Failure to coordinate pedal input will result in an uncontrolled yaw, loss of directional control, and a potential unsafe condition. Pedal input must be smooth and matched to the rate of power application.	->	When collective pitch is increased and engine power is applied, the main rotor generates increased torque on the fuselage. In most US helicopters with counterclockwise main rotor rotation, the fuselage tends to rotate clockwise (right yaw). The left antitorque pedal must be increased simultaneously to counteract this torque effect. Without coordinated pedal input, the aircraft will yaw uncontrollably, resulting in loss of directional control and a potential unsafe condition. Pedal input must be smooth and matched to the rate of power application to maintain directional control.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.G.S1.006	At high density altitude, the helicopter's engine produces less power (reduced air density reduces combustion efficiency) and the rotor blades generate less lift per unit of collective pitch. This means: (1) Power recovery requires more lead time — the engine takes longer to spool up and produce useful power; (2) Available power may be significantly reduced, potentially limiting climb performance after recovery; (3) Rotor RPM may decay faster during the autorotation because the thinner air requires higher blade pitch to maintain lift; (4) The go-around may require more distance and altitude to establish a positive rate of climb. The pilot must initiate power recovery earlier, be more conservative with the decision altitude, and verify performance charts in the RFM for the actual density altitude conditions before the flight. VNE also decreases with altitude per the cockpit placard.	->	At high density altitude, the helicopter's engine produces less power (reduced air density reduces combustion efficiency) and the rotor blades generate less lift per unit of collective pitch. This means: (1) Power recovery requires more lead time — the engine takes longer to spool up and produce useful power; (2) Available power may be significantly reduced, potentially limiting climb performance after recovery; (3) The rotor requires higher collective inputs to maintain lift during the autorotation, which increases pitch demands; (4) Go-arounds may require more distance and altitude to establish a positive rate of climb. The pilot must initiate power recovery earlier, be more conservative with the decision altitude, and verify performance charts in the RFM for the actual density altitude conditions before the flight.
ESCALATE	HI.XVII.H.K1.002	The specific regulatory citation and hour requirement (200 hours) should be verified directly against the current text of 14 CFR 61.195(h)(2) to ensure accuracy, as CFI endorsement requirements are safety-critical and subject to regulatory updates.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.H.K2.001	The endorsement must attest that the applicant received touchdown autorotation training AND is competent in the instruction of the elements, performance, common errors, and correction of common errors for both straight-in autorotations and autorotations with turns. It is not enough to simply state the training was received — competency in teaching the maneuver must also be confirmed.	->	For a CFI applicant, the endorsement must attest that the applicant received touchdown autorotation training AND is competent in the instruction of the elements, performance, common errors, and correction of common errors for both straight-in autorotations and autorotations with turns. The endorsement confirms instructional competency in teaching the maneuver, not merely that training was received.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.H.K4.005	Beyond RFM approval for touchdown autorotations, the applicant should verify: (1) The aircraft's rotor RPM operating range and autorotation entry procedures are compatible with safe touchdown autorotation practice; (2) The landing gear is appropriate for the intended surface (skid-equipped helicopters handle touchdown autorotations differently than wheeled aircraft); (3) The helicopter is within its weight and balance envelope for the anticipated conditions; (4) Maintenance is current — including 100-hour or annual inspection as applicable under 14 CFR 91.409; and (5) The applicant holds the appropriate category/class rating to act as pilot in command of that aircraft. Turbine aircraft are not prohibited under BasicMed or for this endorsement, but the aircraft must still comply with all applicable airworthiness standards.	->	Beyond RFM approval for touchdown autorotations, the applicant should verify: (1) The aircraft's rotor RPM operating range and autorotation entry procedures are compatible with safe touchdown autorotation practice; (2) The landing gear is appropriate for the intended surface (skid-equipped helicopters handle touchdown autorotations differently than wheeled aircraft); (3) The helicopter is within its weight and balance envelope for the anticipated conditions; (4) Maintenance is current — including 100-hour or annual inspection as applicable under 14 CFR 91.409; and (5) The applicant holds the appropriate category/class rating to act as pilot in command of that aircraft. There is no prohibition on using turbine-powered aircraft for this endorsement. The aircraft must comply with all applicable airworthiness standards.
ESCALATE	HI.XVII.H.S1.001	The answer asserts that a logbook endorsement may substitute for in-flight demonstration during a CFI practical test, but this provision requires verification against the current FAA-S-ACS-14 helicopter CFI ACS; it is unclear whether this allowance exists, under what circumstances, and for which certificate levels.
ESCALATE	HI.XVII.H.S1.003	The regulatory authority for who may endorse touchdown autorotation competency is ambiguous; 14 CFR 61.189 governs general flight instructor records and endorsements, but whether a DPE (as an examiner distinct from a flight instructor) can legally provide this specific endorsement before the practical test, or whether only a current CFI-H instructor may do so, requires clarification from FAA ACS guidance or official interpretation.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVII.H.S2.007	The helicopter is immediately unairworthy — visible tail rotor blade damage is a known defect that makes the aircraft unsafe for flight per 14 CFR 91.7. The aircraft must not be flown until inspected and returned to airworthy condition by an A&P mechanic or IA as appropriate. Regarding reporting: if the damage meets the threshold under 49 CFR Part 830, it may constitute an accident or incident requiring NTSB notification — specifically, if there was substantial damage to the aircraft (likely in this case) or if it meets NTSB 830.2 definitions. The operator and owner must be notified immediately. The tail rotor strike also raises the question of a required inspection per the manufacturer's maintenance manual before return to service. As the person who furnished the aircraft, you bear responsibility under 14 CFR 91.7 for having presented a non-airworthy aircraft if pre-existing conditions contributed, and under general PIC responsibility for the outcome of the training flight.	->	The helicopter is immediately unairworthy — visible tail rotor blade damage is a known defect that makes the aircraft unsafe for flight per 14 CFR 91.7. The aircraft must not be flown until inspected and returned to airworthy condition by an A&P mechanic or IA as appropriate. Regarding reporting: tail rotor blade damage from a ground strike likely constitutes 'substantial damage' under 49 CFR 830.2(a)(4), triggering NTSB notification requirements — the operator/owner and NTSB must be notified immediately. The tail rotor strike also requires a comprehensive inspection per the manufacturer's maintenance manual (including tail rotor blades, hub, gearbox, driveshaft, tail boom, and control linkages) before return to service. The pilot in command is responsible under 14 CFR 91.7 for ensuring airworthiness before flight; if the PIC also furnished the aircraft, they bear full responsibility for the outcome of this training flight.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.A.K1.003	Under 14 CFR 91.213(d), all four of the following must be true: (1) The inoperative equipment is not part of the VFR day, VFR night, or IFR equipment list required by 14 CFR 91.205; (2) The equipment is not required by the helicopter's Type Certificate Data Sheet (TCDS), Airworthiness Directives, or other airworthiness requirements; (3) The inoperative equipment does not constitute a hazard to the aircraft; and (4) The inoperative equipment is either removed and the cockpit placarded 'Inoperative,' or deactivated and placarded 'Inoperative.' A certificated and appropriately rated pilot, or a certificated and appropriately rated mechanic, must make this determination.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.213(d), all three of the following must be true: (1) The inoperative equipment is not part of the VFR day, VFR night, or IFR equipment list required by 14 CFR 91.205; (2) The equipment is not required by the helicopter's Type Certificate Data Sheet (TCDS), Airworthiness Directives, or other airworthiness requirements; and (3) The inoperative equipment is either removed and the cockpit placarded 'Inoperative,' or deactivated and placarded 'Inoperative.' A certificated and appropriately rated pilot or a certificated and appropriately rated mechanic must make this determination.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.A.K2.002	First, check whether the aircraft has a Minimum Equipment List (MEL) approved for that aircraft — if so, follow the MEL procedures. Second, if no MEL exists, the pilot must determine that the equipment is not required by the applicable VFR/IFR regulations (14 CFR 91.205), not required by an Airworthiness Directive, not part of the aircraft's type certificate or required equipment list, and not required by a placard or the RFM. If all conditions are met, the item may be placarded INOPERATIVE and flight may continue. However, even if legal, the equipment must not be needed for any task on the practical test.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.213, there are two primary methods: (1) If the aircraft has an FAA-approved Minimum Equipment List (MEL), follow the MEL procedures to determine if the inoperative item is permitted for the intended flight. (2) If no MEL exists, the pilot must apply 14 CFR 91.213(d) criteria: the equipment must not be required by 14 CFR 91.205 (VFR/IFR equipment lists), must not be required by the aircraft's Type Certificate, Airworthiness Directive, or RFM, and the inoperative item must be placarded 'INOPERATIVE.' A certificated pilot or mechanic must make this determination. Separately, under ACS standards for a practical test, the applicant must also verify that the inoperative equipment does not prevent safe demonstration of any required test task.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.A.K4.003	Yes. Under 14 CFR 61.45(c), for an instrument rating practical test, the aircraft must have controls that are accessible and operable from both the pilot and examiner positions. Specifically, it must have functioning dual controls unless the examiner determines that a particular maneuver can be safely performed without dual controls and the applicant is competent to perform it. For instrument testing in helicopters, dual flight controls are generally required.	->	Under 14 CFR 61.45(c), if the aircraft is equipped with dual flight controls, they must be functioning and accessible from both the pilot and examiner positions. However, the regulation permits an examiner to authorize performance of a specific maneuver or task using single controls if the examiner determines it can be accomplished safely and the applicant is competent. Dual controls are standard practice for helicopter instrument practical tests but are not absolutely mandatory in all circumstances.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.A.K4.005	A foreign-registered aircraft may be used for a practical test, but only under specific conditions outlined in 14 CFR 61.45(a)(1). The foreign-registered aircraft is permissible if: (1) the test is for a certificate or rating based on that aircraft, or (2) the FAA has authorized use of that foreign-registered aircraft for the test. Additionally, the aircraft must still be in airworthy condition and have all required equipment. The applicant must ensure the aircraft complies with all applicable foreign airworthiness requirements and that it is authorized for the operations to be conducted during the test.	->	A foreign-registered aircraft may be used for a practical test only if 14 CFR 61.45(a)(1) conditions are met: the test must be for a certificate or rating based on that specific aircraft, meaning the applicant trained in that aircraft and the rating/certificate sought is for that aircraft type. The aircraft must be in airworthy condition per its country of registration, must have all required equipment, and the examiner must authorize its use. Foreign registration alone does not disqualify the aircraft, but it may not be used for scheduling convenience if the applicant's training was in a different aircraft.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.A.R2.004	First, check whether the ADF is required IFR equipment under 14 CFR 91.205(d) — it is not listed as a universal IFR requirement, but it IS required if the flight requires ADF navigation (i.e., if you plan to fly an NDB approach). Next, check the aircraft's MEL or apply 91.213(d) to determine if the ADF can be legally inoperative. Even if legal to fly, the NDB approach task cannot be demonstrated without a functioning ADF receiver, because the ADF needle is the primary instrument for tracking to the NDB. You must then discuss with the examiner whether an alternative approach procedure (e.g., a VOR or GPS approach) can substitute to satisfy that ACS task element, or whether the test must be rescheduled or conducted in a different aircraft.	->	First, determine whether the specific approach procedure selected requires ADF. Per 14 CFR 91.205, ADF is not a universal IFR requirement; it is only required if the approach chart or flight plan requires NDB/ADF navigation. Check the aircraft's MEL or apply 14 CFR 91.213(d) to determine if the inoperative ADF can be legally operated. If the ADF is inoperative and cannot be legally operated, you must select an alternative approach procedure (VOR, GPS, ILS) that the aircraft can support. If no suitable alternative exists at the planned destination, you must change the destination or reschedule. Discuss with the examiner whether an alternative approach procedure can substitute to satisfy the ACS task element, or whether the test must be rescheduled or conducted in a different aircraft.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.A.R2.008	The citation of 14 CFR 27.1337(b)(1) regarding fuel gauge accuracy requirements needs verification against current regulation and Type Certificate Data Sheet (TCDS) for the specific helicopter. The answer's risk management logic is sound, but the regulatory foundation should be confirmed by an FAA expert before shipping to ensure the cited regulation actually supports the claim that gauges are 'only required to be accurate at empty.'
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.A.S2.003	Per 14 CFR 27.1337(b)(1), fuel quantity gauges are only required to be accurate when reading empty — they are not required to be accurate at any other level. However, the pilot must still know the actual fuel quantity before flight, verified by visual inspection or other means. If the gauge is placarded and the actual fuel quantity can be confirmed through other means (dipstick, fuel receipt, visual), the helicopter may still be airworthy. The examiner and pilot must confirm this inoperative or inaccurate gauge does not prevent performance of any required practical test task.	->	Fuel quantity gauges on helicopters are required under 14 CFR Part 27 certification standards, but you must verify the specific accuracy requirement in the aircraft's Type Certificate Data Sheet (TCDS). Before flight, the pilot must determine the actual fuel quantity through visual inspection, dipstick, or fuel receipt — not by relying on an inaccurate gauge. The stuck gauge must be placarded INOPERATIVE per 14 CFR 91.213(d). If the actual fuel quantity can be reliably verified and the inoperative gauge is properly placarded, the helicopter may be airworthy for the checkride, provided the inoperative gauge does not prevent performance of any required practical test task. Confirm the RFM or TCDS permits operation with this gauge inoperative.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.B.K5.001	The view limiting device must do two things: first, it must completely prevent the applicant from having any visual reference outside the aircraft; second, it must not restrict the evaluator's ability to see and avoid other traffic. Both conditions must be met simultaneously throughout the instrument portions of the checkride.	->	The view limiting device must do two things: first, it must effectively prevent the applicant from using outside visual references to control the helicopter (minor peripheral gaps that do not permit horizon or terrain reference are acceptable); second, it must not restrict the evaluator's ability to see and avoid other traffic. Both conditions must be met simultaneously throughout the instrument portions of the checkride. Reference: 14 CFR 61.45(d)(2).
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.B.K7.001	An applicant who brings a multiengine helicopter must demonstrate all Tasks specific to multiengine helicopter operations in addition to every other Task required for the instrument rating. In other words, the multiengine-specific Tasks are added on top of — not substituted for — the standard required Tasks.	->	An applicant who brings a multiengine helicopter must demonstrate all required instrument rating Tasks per the ACS, with specific attention to multiengine-relevant procedures and systems (such as OEI performance, engine failure recognition in IMC, and torque management). The evaluation context is multiengine-specific—for example, approach and emergency procedures are evaluated with reference to single-engine service ceilings and OEI performance charts. Tasks specifically designed for multiengine operations (such as OEI hover or engine-failure scenario management) are included in the evaluation in addition to core instrument rating competencies. Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B (Helicopter Flying Handbook); Helicopter Instrument Rating ACS, HI.XVIII.B.K7.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.B.K7.006	The answer conflates instrument rating structure with class/type endorsement requirements and overstates whether a separate 'instrument rating in a multiengine helicopter' is needed; the distinction between rating privileges, class/type requirements, and operational proficiency is unclear and requires clarification of current FAA guidance.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.C.K2.004	The answer cites Part 141 Appendix C limits (20 hrs FTD, 30 hrs FFS) for rotorcraft, but Part 141 Appendix C governs only airplane ratings; rotorcraft instrument requirements are in Part 141 Appendix H, and the specific credit limits for helicopter FTD/FFS time must be verified against that appendix to confirm accuracy.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.C.K2.005	The answer does not address a critical issue: whether Part 135 permits an initial instrument proficiency check to be conducted entirely in an FSTD or whether the regulations or FAA policy require at least some portion in actual aircraft; this distinction is material to the regulatory completeness of the answer.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.C.K2.006	The answer describes a 'continuing qualification evaluation' process and discrepancy log procedures, but Part 60, Subpart B does not clearly define such a process by that name; the specific regulatory provisions in 14 CFR 60.7 and related sections governing whether out-of-tolerance devices must be immediately suspended or may continue with restrictions need verification, and AC 120-40 is primarily for airplanes, not helicopters.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.C.K2.008	The answer conflates FTD and FFS credit limits (stating 20 hours for FTD and 30 hours for FFS) but the verifier correctly flags that 14 CFR 61.65(i) may not distinguish between them or may specify different limits; the actual regulatory text must be verified before shipping this advanced question.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.C.K3.001	The answer treats FFS and FTD as having identical 20-hour credit limits under 14 CFR 61.65(i), but the verifier correctly identifies this conflation as a potential error; the actual regulatory text distinguishing (or not) FFS vs. FTD credit must be verified before approval.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.C.K3.008	Under 14 CFR 61.159, the ATP certificate requires 1,500 hours of total time as a pilot. However, 14 CFR 61.159(a)(5) allows credit for flight simulator time under specific conditions: a maximum of 100 hours of FSTD time may be credited toward the 1,500-hour total time requirement. The device must be an FFS that represents an aircraft, and the training must be conducted by an authorized instructor. Additionally, Part 61.159 separately governs specific sub-hour requirements (e.g., cross-country, night, instrument) that have their own FSTD credit provisions. Critically, the applicant cannot substitute FFS time for the actual helicopter flight time requirements that are category-specific — the ATP applicant must still meet the helicopter-specific aeronautical experience in the rotorcraft category.	->	Under 14 CFR 61.159, the ATP certificate requires 1,500 hours of total time as a pilot. However, 14 CFR 61.159(a)(5) allows credit for flight simulator time under specific conditions: a maximum of 100 hours of FSTD time may be credited toward the 1,500-hour total time requirement. The device must be an FFS that represents an aircraft, and the training must be conducted by an authorized instructor. Additionally, Part 61.159 separately governs specific sub-hour requirements (e.g., cross-country, night, instrument) that have their own FSTD credit provisions. Critically, the applicant cannot substitute FFS time for the actual helicopter flight time requirements that are category-specific — the ATP applicant must still meet the helicopter-specific aeronautical experience in the rotorcraft category. Reduced-hour pathways (e.g., 1,250 hours for Part 141 CTP graduates, 750 hours for military pilots) are governed by 14 CFR 61.159(c)-(d) and have their own FSTD credit allowances as outlined in those subsections.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.C.R1.008	An FAA-approved AATD may be used toward the instrument rating aeronautical experience requirements, but with significant limitations. Under 14 CFR 61.65(i), an AATD can be used for up to 10 hours of the required instrument time toward a helicopter instrument rating when used under an approved Part 141 curriculum that specifically authorizes the AATD for that purpose. However, AATDs are not qualified under 14 CFR Part 60 — they are approved under 14 CFR 61.4 and the applicable FAA approval notice. The key risk here is that if the student logged 40 hours in an AATD expecting full credit, only up to 10 of those hours may be applied toward the instrument rating requirements. The remaining hours would need to be made up in an actual helicopter or a qualified FSTD under Part 60. The examiner must carefully review what the approved training program specifies for the AATD's use and confirm the AATD holds a current FAA approval, since AATD approvals must also be current and the specific device must be listed in the school's approved curriculum.	->	An FAA-approved AATD may be used toward the instrument rating aeronautical experience requirements under 14 CFR 61.65(i), but with strict limitations. Under 14 CFR 61.65(i), an AATD can provide credit for up to 10 hours of the required instrument time toward a helicopter instrument rating when the device holds a current FAA approval under 14 CFR 61.4 and is authorized in the school's approved Part 141 curriculum. The 10-hour statutory limit applies regardless of what the training program curriculum specifies—the regulatory ceiling cannot be exceeded. If the student logged 40 hours in an AATD, only up to 10 of those hours may be applied toward the instrument rating requirements. The remaining hours must be completed in an actual helicopter or a Part 60–qualified FSTD. At the checkride, the examiner must verify the AATD's current FAA approval status, confirm it is listed in the school's curriculum, and ensure that no more than 10 hours of AATD time are credited toward the practical test eligibility. Any excess AATD hours must be made up in qualifying aircraft or Part 60 FSTD before the applicant is eligible to take the practical test.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.C.S1.001	An FSTD must be evaluated and qualified by the FAA under 14 CFR Part 60, which establishes qualification standards for Flight Simulation Training Devices. The device must hold a current Letter of Authorization (LOA) issued by the FAA's Flight Standards Service. The qualification level (e.g., FTD Level 5, 6, 7, or FFS Level A–D) determines which tasks and training credits are authorized. Using an unqualified or expired device does not generate loggable training time or credit toward certificate requirements.	->	An FSTD must be evaluated and qualified by the FAA under 14 CFR Part 60, which establishes qualification standards for Flight Simulation Training Devices. The device must hold a current Statement of Qualification (SOQ) issued by the FAA's Flight Standards Service. The qualification level (e.g., FTD Level 5, 6, 7, or FFS Level A–D) determines which tasks and training credits are authorized. Using an unqualified or expired device does not generate loggable training time or credit toward certificate requirements.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.C.S1.005	For the DPE to conduct a checkride task in an FSTD, the following must be in place: (1) The FSTD must be FAA-qualified at a level that authorizes the specific task — for an ILS approach, a helicopter FTD Level 7 or FFS is typically required. (2) The applicant must have completed training for that task within an FAA-approved training program. (3) The DPE must be authorized to conduct practical test tasks in that specific FSTD. (4) The practical test must otherwise conform to the applicable ACS, including task completion standards. (5) The applicant's training records must reflect that the task was trained in an approved program. Importantly, HI.XVIII.C.S1 requires that FSTD tasks be demonstrated only within an FAA-approved training program — meaning the applicant cannot simply 'hop in' an FSTD for the first time on a checkride.	->	For the DPE to conduct a checkride task in an FSTD, the following must be in place: (1) The FSTD must hold a current Statement of Qualification (SOQ) from the FAA under 14 CFR Part 60 at a level that authorizes the specific task—the required FTD level or FFS level for ILS approaches is determined by the device's SOQ and Part 60 Appendix C. (2) The applicant must have previously completed training for that task within an FAA-approved training program using that same device or an equivalent qualified device—the task cannot be evaluated in an FSTD for the first time on a checkride. (3) The DPE must be authorized to conduct practical test tasks in that specific FSTD. (4) The practical test must conform to the applicable ACS, including task completion standards. (5) The applicant's training records must document prior training of the task in an approved program. Under 14 CFR 61.43 and ACS guidance, tasks in an FSTD may be credited toward a practical test only if the applicant trained them previously in an approved program.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.D.K2.007	The argument is flawed. While 14 CFR 61.1 provides the baseline definition of an ATD, the credit limitations and applicability of ATD time toward specific ratings are governed by 14 CFR 61.65 and the FAA's Letter of Authorization (LOA) issued for each specific device. The LOA specifies what aircraft category or type of training the device is approved for. A BATD approved only for fixed-wing instrument training cannot be used to log helicopter instrument time. Additionally, the FAA may impose category-specific equipment requirements through the LOA process. The definition in 61.1 establishes what an ATD is — it does not authorize universal credit across all certificate categories.	->	The argument is flawed. While 14 CFR 61.1 provides the baseline definition of an ATD, the credit limitations and applicability of ATD time toward specific ratings are governed by 14 CFR 61.65 and the FAA's Letter of Authorization (LOA) issued for each specific device. The LOA specifies what aircraft category or type of training the device is approved for and is the controlling document determining whether a device may be credited toward helicopter training. A BATD approved only for fixed-wing instrument training cannot be used to log helicopter instrument time, as its LOA does not authorize rotorcraft credit. Under 14 CFR 61.65(i), even if a BATD's LOA did include rotorcraft, a maximum of 10 hours of BATD time may be credited toward instrument rating aeronautical experience requirements (20 hours for an AATD). The definition in 61.1 establishes what an ATD is — it does not authorize universal credit across all certificate categories.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.D.K3.001	The citation of 14 CFR 61.4(c) as the basis for a specific 'General Aviation and Commercial Division' approval authority is not substantiated by that regulation; 61.4(c) grants general delegation to the Administrator, not to named divisions. FAA organizational structure changes and the specific office handling ATD approvals may differ from what is stated.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.D.K3.002	14 CFR 61.4(c) authorizes the FAA to evaluate and approve training devices other than flight simulators. This provision gives the FAA's General Aviation and Commercial Division the legal basis to grant ATD approval, and the approval process itself is governed by FAA Order 8900.1.	->	14 CFR 61.4 grants the FAA Administrator the authority to establish and oversee training device approval requirements under Part 61. The definition and legal framework for ATDs are established in 14 CFR 61.1. The approval process, evaluation standards, and procedures are detailed in FAA Order 8900.1. This combination of regulatory authority and FAA guidance documents provides the legal basis for ATD approval and use in pilot training and certification.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.D.K4.001	The 60-month validity period for ATD LOAs is commonly cited in training materials but requires confirmation against current FAA Order 8900.1 and actual LOA issuance documentation; the referenced appendix (FAA-H-8083-15B Appendix 3) needs verification as current guidance.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.D.K4.002	An ATD LOA contains two primary types of information: (1) the specific pilot time credit allowances — meaning how many hours and for what training tasks or certificate/rating requirements the device may be used — and (2) the associated limitations on that credit, such as the types of operations, currency events, or certificate levels for which the device is authorized.	->	An ATD LOA contains two primary types of information: (1) the specific pilot-in-command (PIC) time credit allowances—meaning the maximum number of hours and the specific certificate or rating tasks for which the device may be credited toward pilot certification requirements—and (2) the limitations on that credit, such as the certificate level (e.g., private, commercial), aircraft category, and any operational restrictions. The LOA is issued in accordance with FAA Order 8900.1 and specifies exactly how the device may be used for training credit.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.D.K5.005	When transitioning from Part 141 to Part 61 training, the Part 61 ATD hour limits govern the remaining training. Any ATD hours already logged at the Part 141 school may still count toward the Part 61 experience requirements, but only up to the Part 61 cap for that certificate. The student and instructor should carefully audit logged ATD hours against the applicable Part 61 limit to ensure none of the creditable hours exceed the regulatory maximum. The school's Part 141 TCO limits do not expand what Part 61 allows.	->	When transitioning from Part 141 to Part 61 training, ATD hours already logged at the Part 141 school count as completed experience and reduce the remaining requirement. However, the Part 61 ATD hour cap (14 CFR 61.109(a)(3)—up to 10 of 20 required instrument hours for commercial pilot) is the absolute regulatory maximum that may be credited toward the certificate, regardless of the Part 141 TCO allowance. Previously logged ATD hours count against this cap. The student and instructor must verify that total ATD credit—both completed at Part 141 and any additional ATD hours planned under Part 61—does not exceed the Part 61 limit before the certificate can be issued.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.D.K5.006	No. Under 14 CFR 61.187(a) and the applicable flight instructor requirements, the instrument time requirements for a CFII certificate are subject to the same ATD limitations as those for an instrument rating under 61.65. Up to 20 hours may be logged in an ATD toward the 40-hour instrument experience requirement, but the specific 15-hour flight instructor instrument requirement and overall aeronautical experience must still be carefully cross-referenced with 61.183 and 61.187 to ensure ATD hours do not exceed the regulatory caps. The applicant should verify with a FSDO or DPE which specific hours must be in an aircraft.	->	Under 14 CFR 61.187(a), a CFII applicant must meet the instrument experience requirements of 14 CFR 61.65. Per 61.65(i), up to 20 hours of ATD time may be credited toward the total 40-hour instrument experience requirement for an instrument rating. Because the 15-hour CFII instrument requirement is part of the broader 40-hour experience requirement, the 20-hour ATD cap applies. Therefore, all 15 hours could theoretically be logged in an ATD, but doing so would consume 15 of the 20 allowable ATD hours, leaving only 5 ATD hours for any other instrument experience needs. The ATD must be authorized by a current Letter of Authorization (LOA), and the training must be conducted by a certificated flight instructor.
ESCALATE	HI.XVIII.D.R1.007	The answer asserts a 10-hour BATD limitation for helicopter instrument rating but 14 CFR 61.65(i) specifies BATD limits for airplane ratings; the exact applicability and limits for helicopter instrument rating require confirmation in the regulation and current FAA guidance before this can be certified as correct.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.D.S1.001	The pilot must provide a copy of the manufacturer's Letter of Authorization (LOA) for the Aviation Training Device. The LOA is issued by the FAA to the ATD manufacturer and confirms that the specific device has been approved for use in meeting training and experience requirements. Without it, the ATD time cannot be credited toward the rating.	->	The pilot must provide evidence of the Letter of Authorization (LOA) for the Aviation Training Device—typically a copy obtained from the flight school or ATD facility. The LOA is issued by the FAA to the ATD manufacturer and confirms that the specific device has been approved for use in meeting training and experience requirements. The flight school normally retains the original LOA on file. Without verification that an unexpired, applicable LOA exists and covers the specific type of training (e.g., helicopter vs. airplane), the ATD time cannot be credited toward the rating.
