AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.E.K3.001	Landings with the helicopter facing directly downhill or directly uphill are explicitly excluded from testing during certification. Only sideward slope landings — where the helicopter lands with its longitudinal axis across the slope — are tested.	->	Landings with the helicopter's longitudinal axis aligned uphill or downhill are explicitly excluded from testing during certification (per ACS HI.XVIII.E.K3). The tested maneuver requires the helicopter to be positioned with its longitudinal axis across the slope contour, on a slope typically between 5 and 10 degrees.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.E.K4.008	Flat, uniform lighting from a low sun angle shining directly onto a slope can create a phenomenon where surface texture, rocks, depressions, and elevation changes are washed out — all features appear as a smooth, featureless surface. This is sometimes called 'whiteout' conditions in snow (though it applies to any uniformly lit terrain). Shadows are what give the human eye the depth cues needed to distinguish terrain features. Without shadows, a rock, a stump, or a depression can be completely invisible from the air. The pilot may incorrectly assess the surface as clear when significant obstacles are present. The mitigation strategy is to delay the approach until sun angle changes to produce shadow relief, approach from a different direction that creates better contrast, or conduct a thorough low hover with rotor wash to disturb the surface and reveal hidden objects before committing full weight. If none of these options are feasible, an alternate landing site must be selected.	->	Flat, uniform lighting from a low sun angle shining directly onto a slope can create a phenomenon where surface texture, rocks, depressions, and elevation changes are washed out — all features appear as a smooth, featureless surface. This effect is similar to the depth-cue loss in whiteout conditions but occurs in any uniformly lit terrain and is more accurately called 'flat light' or 'loss of shadow contrast.' Shadows are what give the human eye the depth cues needed to distinguish terrain features. Without shadows, a rock, a stump, or a depression can be completely invisible from the air. The pilot may incorrectly assess the surface as clear when significant obstacles are present. The mitigation strategy is to delay the approach until sun angle changes to produce shadow relief, approach from a different direction that creates better contrast, or conduct a thorough low hover with rotor wash to disturb the surface and reveal hidden objects before committing full weight. If none of these options are feasible, an alternate landing site must be selected.
