AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.F.R1.001	The minimum entry altitude for a straight-in autorotation is 500 feet AGL. This altitude provides sufficient height to establish a stabilized autorotative descent, make necessary control adjustments, and complete the flare and cushion before touchdown. Below this altitude, there may not be enough time or height to recover if the entry is improperly executed or rotor RPM decays.	->	The minimum entry altitude for a straight-in autorotation under ideal conditions (calm or light wind, high-inertia rotor, trained pilot) is 500 feet AGL. This altitude provides sufficient height to establish a stabilized autorotative descent, make necessary control adjustments, and complete the flare and cushion before touchdown. However, higher entry altitudes are required in strong wind conditions, high density altitude, low-inertia rotor systems, turbulent air, and other adverse conditions. The 500-foot figure is a baseline floor, not a standard recommendation; always assess environmental factors and aircraft-specific characteristics before committing to entry altitude.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.F.R2.005	Checkride pressure creates a 'press-on' bias where a pilot may accept increasing risk to complete a maneuver rather than admit the approach has gone unsatisfactorily. This is a classic ADM hazardous attitude (invulnerability or macho). Approximately 80% of aviation accidents involve pilot error rooted in human factors. The mitigation strategy is to pre-brief personal abort criteria before the maneuver begins, treat the examiner as a crew member, and recognize that a demonstrated power recovery with sound reasoning shows better judgment than a forced, unsafe touchdown.	->	Checkride pressure creates a 'press-on' bias where a pilot may accept increasing risk to complete a maneuver rather than admit the approach has gone unsatisfactorily. This reflects the hazardous attitude of invulnerability or machismo. Per 14 CFR 91.3, the pilot-in-command retains final authority and responsibility for safe flight regardless of external pressure. The mitigation strategy is to pre-brief personal abort criteria before the maneuver begins, treat the examiner as a crew member, and recognize that a demonstrated power recovery with sound aeronautical decision-making and reasoning shows superior judgment to a forced, unsafe touchdown.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.F.S1.002	The three primary control inputs are: (1) lower the collective immediately to reduce pitch and allow upward airflow through the rotor disk to maintain RPM, (2) apply aft cyclic to slow the helicopter and increase airflow up through the disk which also helps sustain rotor RPM, and (3) apply pedal input (typically right pedal in most U.S.-built helicopters) to maintain trim and counteract the torque change. These inputs must be nearly simultaneous, with collective being the most time-critical.	->	The three primary control inputs are: (1) Lower the collective immediately to reduce pitch and allow upward airflow through the rotor disk to maintain rotor RPM, (2) Apply aft cyclic to increase pitch attitude and slow airspeed, which increases descent rate and sustains upward airflow through the disk, and (3) Apply pedal input to maintain directional trim and control—the specific direction (left or right) depends entirely on the aircraft's rotor rotation direction and design and must be determined from the aircraft POH/RFM. These inputs must be nearly simultaneous, with collective being the most time-critical.
