AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.F.S2.006	Rotor system type significantly affects go-around characteristics. Semirigid (teetering) rotor systems allow feathering and flapping but no lead/lag movement. They typically have lower rotor inertia, meaning rotor RPM decays more rapidly during autorotation and there is less stored energy available for the go-around flare and power recovery window. The pilot must act quickly and precisely. Articulated rotor systems have blades capable of pitch change, flapping, and lead/lag movement, and generally have higher inertia — providing a wider margin for go-around execution. Both system types require smooth collective inputs to avoid overshooting rotor RPM limits in the high direction or decaying into the stall range during power restoration. The specific RPM range limitations are found in the applicable Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) and must be managed precisely during any go-around.	->	Rotor system type significantly affects go-around characteristics. Semirigid (teetering) rotor systems allow feathering and flapping but no lead-lag movement. They typically have higher rotor inertia due to the rigid mast connection, meaning rotor RPM is more stable during autorotation but the system has less blade-flapping freedom during gusts or aggressive control input. Articulated rotor systems have separate flapping and lead-lag hinges, allowing individual blade motion and more complex inertia characteristics depending on design. Both system types require smooth collective inputs to avoid overshooting rotor RPM limits in the high direction or decaying into the stall range during power restoration. The specific RPM range limitations are found in the applicable Pilot's Operating Handbook (POH) or Aircraft Flight Manual (AFM) and must be managed precisely during any go-around.
AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.G.K1.005	This scenario illustrates why the minimum altitude standard exists and why the emphasis is on *above* 700 feet AGL. At 710 feet AGL, the available altitude margin is razor-thin. When rotor RPM decays during the turn, the pilot must apply corrective inputs — typically lowering collective slightly or reducing bank — which takes both time and altitude to resolve. The Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that aft cyclic during autorotation entry increases upward airflow through the disk, helping to rebuild RPM, but in a turn this input is compromised. Low-inertia rotors decay rapidly, and rotor stall in helicopters is described as unrecoverable — making early recognition imperative. The proper response is to shallow the bank or reduce turn rate, allow RPM to recover, and terminate the maneuver safely. This event reinforces that 700 feet AGL is a minimum, not a target, and that wind is not the only factor that may require a higher entry altitude.	->	This scenario illustrates why the minimum altitude standard exists and why the emphasis is on *above* 700 feet AGL. At 710 feet AGL, the available altitude margin is razor-thin. When rotor RPM decays during the turn, the pilot must apply corrective inputs—typically lowering collective or reducing bank—which takes both time and altitude to resolve. The Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that aft cyclic during autorotation entry increases descent rate and inflow velocity, helping to rebuild RPM, but in a turn this input is compromised. Low-inertia rotors decay rapidly, and a complete rotor stall is extremely difficult to recover from at low altitude. The proper response is to shallow the bank or reduce turn rate, allow RPM to recover via lowered collective, and terminate the maneuver safely. This event reinforces that 700 feet AGL is a minimum, not a target, and that recognition of incipient RPM decay in the caution range—and immediate corrective action—is essential to prevent progression to an unrecoverable