AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.G.K3.006	With a 20-knot tailwind pushing you southward, your ground track is southbound and faster over the ground than your airspeed alone would indicate. To reverse that southbound ground track and head back northward toward the departure runway, you would initially think of rolling out around 360 degrees. However, the tailwind from the south is now a headwind during the return, and the key point is that during the turn itself the wind continues to drift your ground track. You must think in terms of reversing your southbound over-ground path, which means rolling out on approximately 360 degrees — but recognizing that the actual rollout heading could vary slightly based on the arc of drift accumulated during the turn. The main concept is that you are not simply adding 180 degrees to your compass heading; you are reversing the direction you were traveling over the ground.	->	With a 20-knot tailwind pushing you southbound, your ground track is southbound. To reverse that southbound ground track and track back northward toward the departure runway, you will roll out on approximately 360 degrees (north). The key distinction is that you are navigating by ground track (your actual path over the ground), not by magnetic heading alone. During the turn, wind continues to drift your position, so you observe your ground track and adjust your rollout heading as necessary to ensure you are tracking back toward your departure point. This is why a simple 180-degree turn from your outbound magnetic heading may not be sufficient—you must reference your actual movement over the ground.
