AI_EDIT	HI.XVIII.I.R1.002	At 1,000 feet AGL, the crew has adequate time and altitude to identify the engine failure, confirm it, configure the helicopter for single-engine flight, stabilize on the approach, and still have options including a missed approach. Below this altitude, time compression and limited glide margin significantly reduce the ability to recover from errors or deteriorating conditions.	->	Adequate altitude during OEI approach initiation allows the crew sufficient time to identify and confirm the engine failure, apply proper single-engine control inputs, stabilize the aircraft on approach, and execute a missed approach or go-around if the approach is not stabilized. The specific minimum altitude is determined by the helicopter's Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) and type-rating requirements. Below adequate altitude, time compression and limited glide margin eliminate recovery options and increase accident risk.
