Difficulty:    advanced

Question:
Explain how the power setting you select for IFR cruise affects not just fuel burn but also your ability to navigate accurately using VOR on an en route IFR segment. Is there a connection between power setting selection and navigation system reliability?

Answer (at time of rejection):
Yes, there is an indirect but operationally important connection. The Minimum En Route Altitude (MEA) published on IFR charts guarantees both obstacle clearance and adequate VOR signal reception. If a pilot selects a power setting that requires a higher airspeed than the aircraft can sustain at MEA — for example, attempting to maintain an aggressive schedule by flying at maximum power — they may be tempted to accept a lower altitude from ATC or descend below MEA to avoid turbulence. Flying below the MEA eliminates the guarantee of VOR signal reception; the MOCA (Minimum Obstruction Clearance Altitude) only guarantees navigation signal within 22 NM of the VOR. Conversely, selecting a conservative cruise power that is sustainable at or above MEA ensures continuous navigation signal coverage throughout the route. Additionally, excessively high power settings can cause electrical system stress in some older helicopters, though modern turbine helicopters have robust electrical systems. The primary lesson is that power setting selection must be consistent with the altitudes at which you intend to fly, and those altitudes must be at or above the MEA for reliable IFR navigation.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
