The three wind azimuth sectors stated do not align precisely with standard FAA published LTE guidance. The ranges provided (285-315°, 210-330°, 120-240°) overlap significantly and are stated with excessive specificity that is not found in FAA-H-8083-21B. Standard FAA guidance identifies LTE risk in general terms: left-rear quadrant winds (approximately 180-315°), right-rear quadrant winds, and high-power/low-airspeed conditions, but not with these specific degree ranges.
The answer conflates three separate phenomena (weathervane effect, tail rotor vortex ring state, and main rotor vortex interference) and assigns specific degree ranges to each, but FAA handbooks do not typically break down LTE azimuth risk into three distinct 120-degree sectors in this manner. This level of specificity may not be supported by the referenced handbook.

