All three factors impair the function of rods and cones. Hypoxia reduces the oxygen supply to retinal cells; rods are particularly sensitive to oxygen deprivation because they operate at very low light levels and have high metabolic demands. Night vision begins to degrade at altitudes as low as 5,000 feet MSL even in otherwise healthy, unacclimatized pilots — the ability to detect dim, moving, or stationary contrast targets is reduced before any other hypoxia symptom becomes noticeable. Carbon monoxide (CO) binds to hemoglobin with 200 times the affinity of oxygen, reducing the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity and causing functional hypoxia even at sea level — a pilot flying with a small CO leak from a heater may have significantly impaired night vision without any awareness of CO exposure. Nicotine from smoking causes peripheral vasoconstriction and CO from combustion has the same hemoglobin-binding effect. In all three cases, the pilot's ability to detect a stationary collision-course target — already marginal due to the no-motion illusion — is further degraded. A pilot should not smoke within 30 minutes of flight, should use supplemental oxygen at night above 5,000 feet MSL, and should ensure proper cabin ventilation to guard against CO.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The claim that night vision begins to degrade at 5,000 feet MSL in otherwise healthy, unacclimatized pilots is stated too absolutely and lacks specific FAA handbook citation. FAA-H-8083-21B and medical references indicate night vision degradation is gradual and variable; some sources cite 5,000 feet as a point of concern, but the physiology is individual and altitude-dependent on acclimatization.
The regulatory reference FAA-AM-400-96/2 is difficult to verify as current; recommend cross-checking with current FAA guidance on hypoxia and physiology (e.g., FAA-P-8740-60).
The statement about supplemental oxygen at night above 5,000 feet MSL is not a regulatory requirement under 14 CFR 91.211 (which applies only above 12,500 feet MSL). This is a best-practice recommendation but should be clearly distinguished from regulatory requirement.

