Difficulty:    basic

Question:
Name the six main errors that affect a magnetic compass in flight.

Answer (at time of rejection):
The six main magnetic compass errors are: (1) Variation — the angular difference between true north and magnetic north; (2) Deviation — error caused by magnetic fields within the aircraft itself; (3) Magnetic dip — the tendency of the compass needle to dip toward the closer magnetic pole, which causes most of the turning and acceleration errors; (4) Northerly turning error — the compass leads or lags depending on turn direction when near the north magnetic pole; (5) Acceleration/deceleration error — on east/west headings, acceleration causes a false northerly indication and deceleration causes a false southerly indication (remembered by the acronym ANDS: Accelerate North, Decelerate South); (6) Oscillation error — erratic swinging of the compass caused by turbulence or uncoordinated flight.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The answer lists 'oscillation error' as a sixth distinct magnetic compass error; however, oscillation is typically a symptom or manifestation of other errors (particularly magnetic dip and acceleration/deceleration errors) under turbulent or uncoordinated flight conditions, rather than a standalone independent error in the same category as variation, deviation, dip, and turning errors. Most FAA handbooks treat the primary compass errors as five main categories: variation, deviation, magnetic dip (which causes northerly turning error and acceleration/deceleration error as secondary effects). Oscillation is better characterized as a transient condition rather than a fundamental error source.

