During a turning autorotation at approximately 300 feet AGL, a pilot applies an aggressive lateral cyclic input to steepen the bank and tighten the turn toward the landing zone. What aerodynamic error is this, what immediate danger does it create, and why is low altitude the critical factor?

Answer (at time of rejection):
Applying aggressive lateral cyclic to steepen the bank rapidly increases the load factor on the rotor system. In a banked turn, effective lift must support both the vertical weight component and the centripetal force requirement, so total rotor thrust demand increases significantly with bank angle. If the collective is not adjusted to compensate and the pilot simultaneously has rotor RPM near the lower end of the green arc from prior mismanagement, the combination of increased blade pitch demand and reduced RPM can push the rotor toward a low-RPM condition — and in extreme cases, contribute to conditions associated with blade stall beginning at the root. Additionally, the abrupt lateral input can cause a temporary unloading and reloading of the rotor disc, creating a transient loss of effective lift. At 300 feet AGL, there is insufficient altitude to recover from a rotor RPM undershoot or to re-establish an adequate autorotative glide before ground impact. Low altitude is the critical factor because recovery from any rotor RPM anomaly requires altitude and time — neither of which exists at 300 feet in a descending autorotation.

