Answer (at time of rejection):
Disk loading (gross weight divided by rotor disk area, expressed in lbs/ft²) directly affects the induced velocity of rotor downwash. A helicopter with high disk loading (smaller rotor disk relative to its weight, common in some military and tandem-rotor designs) produces a higher-velocity downwash column. This means the helicopter must achieve a higher descent rate before it begins to 'catch up' with its own downwash and enter VRS — at first glance suggesting more margin, but in reality the energy of the recirculating vortex when VRS does occur is more violent. Conversely, a helicopter with low disk loading (large rotor relative to weight, typical of light training helicopters) has a lower induced velocity, which means even modest descent rates can exceed the downwash velocity and initiate VRS at surprisingly low sink rates. Additionally, low inertia rotor systems found on lighter helicopters allow rotor RPM to decay more rapidly once VRS-induced blade stall begins, compounding the emergency. Rotor RPM decay rate is particularly critical — the FAA notes that low-inertia rotor systems lose RPM rapidly at autorotation entry, making the VRS-to-autorotation recovery transition more time-critical on light helicopters.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The statement that 'high disk loading produces higher-velocity downwash' is correct, but the subsequent claim that 'the helicopter must achieve a higher descent rate before it catches up with its own downwash' oversimplifies the relationship. High disk loading does correlate with higher induced velocity, but this does not necessarily provide more margin before VRS entry — the relationship is more nuanced and aircraft-specific. The statement about low-inertia rotor systems is accurate and well-supported.
The characterization that low disk loading helicopters are 'generally more vulnerable' needs clarification: while the lower induced velocity threshold is true, vulnerability also depends heavily on rotor inertia, pilot technique, and operating procedures. The answer conflates disk loading effects with inertia effects in a way that could mislead students about which factor is primary in VRS susceptibility.

