Question:
How does the Coriolis effect on rotor blades interact with a low rotor RPM condition, and could blade folding or flapping behavior in an articulated rotor system contribute to or mask an impending low RPM situation?

Answer (at time of rejection):
The Coriolis effect in rotor systems describes the tendency of a rotating mass to change its rotational velocity as its mass distribution relative to the axis changes. In an articulated rotor system, blades can lead and lag (move fore and aft in the plane of rotation) and flap up and down. When a blade flaps up, its center of mass moves closer to the axis of rotation, and by conservation of angular momentum, it tends to accelerate (lead). When it flaps down, it tends to decelerate (lag). Under low RPM conditions, the reduced centrifugal force that normally holds blades taut and planar decreases. This allows greater flapping amplitudes and potentially increased lead/lag motion. Increased flapping raises the effective drag on the rotor system and creates aerodynamic asymmetries that can further load and slow the rotor. Additionally, in some rotor configurations, the blade flapping that accompanies low RPM may feel similar to normal gusty or turbulent conditions to an inexperienced pilot, potentially masking the true cause. Pilots must rely on instrument indications (rotor tachometer) and audio warnings rather than feel alone to detect low RPM, because the tactile cues can be ambiguous or delayed.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
