The main rotor generates blade tip vortices — rotating columns of disturbed air — that trail downward and outward from each blade tip as it passes. When the relative wind places these vortices in the path of the tail rotor disk, the disrupted, turbulent inflow degrades tail rotor thrust production, causing fluctuating and reduced antitorque output. This effect is most pronounced in the left crosswind azimuth (approximately 285°–315° for counterclockwise main rotor systems) because the main rotor vortex path aligns directly with the tail rotor disk, causing intermittent thrust loss rather than a steady reduction — the erratic yaw response is harder to detect and counter than a smooth loss. At other wind azimuths, the vortices trail away from the tail rotor or the relative wind geometry prevents ingestion. A second and distinct mechanism is the main rotor disk vortex (the toroidal vortex formed by the entire rotor system) during low-speed flight with tailwind components between approximately 210° and 330°, which can interfere with tail rotor inflow from the opposite side. Pilots cannot see these vortices, making the condition extremely difficult to anticipate without understanding the wind azimuth danger zones and actively managing heading relative to wind during low-speed flight.

