Question:
You are flying your helicopter IFR on a magnetic heading of 360 degrees and begin a standard-rate left turn toward 270 degrees. Describe what the magnetic compass will do during the turn, and explain when you should roll out to roll out on the correct heading.

Answer (at time of rejection):
When turning from north, the compass initially lags — meaning it will appear to show a heading less than 360 or may even briefly move in the opposite direction of the turn before catching up. This northerly turning error (a form of magnetic dip error) is most pronounced when starting from a north heading. Because the compass lags in turns through north, you must roll out before reaching 270 degrees on the compass — the compass will be indicating a heading greater than 270 when you actually reach 270 degrees magnetic. The rule of thumb is to roll out when the compass reads your desired heading plus your latitude in degrees. For example, at 30° North latitude, roll out when the compass reads approximately 300° (270 + 30 = 300). In practice, under IFR, pilots rely on the heading indicator (directional gyro) rather than the compass for precision turns, and use the compass primarily for periodic verification and correction of the heading indicator.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The rule of thumb stated ('roll out when compass reads desired heading plus latitude') is oversimplified and potentially confusing. The actual northerly turning error rule is more nuanced: when turning FROM north, roll out early (at a heading less than desired); when turning TO north, roll out late (at a heading greater than desired). The latitude rule is a crude approximation that does not reliably account for which quadrant of the turn you are in.
The answer states compass will 'appear to show a heading less than 360 or may even briefly move in the opposite direction' during a left turn from 360 toward 270 — this is imprecise. During a left (northerly) turn FROM north, the compass lags and reads higher than the actual heading being flown, not lower.
Reference to 'northerly turning error (a form of magnetic dip error) is most pronounced when starting from a north heading' is correct, but the explanation of what happens during the turn should clarify that the compass leads or lags depending on direction of turn, not just that it 'lags.'

