With a counterclockwise rotor, the tail rotor is on the left side of the tail boom (for most American helicopters). A direct tailwind increases airflow into the tail rotor from behind, which can reduce tail rotor thrust efficiency at that moment. More importantly, the tailwind creates a weathervaning tendency — the nose wants to turn into the wind, meaning the nose yaws left (toward the tailwind). To counter this, the pilot needs increased right pedal to maintain heading. Additionally, the tailwind striking the fuselage broadside moment increases yaw tendency, so overall right pedal demand increases compared to calm conditions.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
