AI_EDIT	IH.I.C.S2.002	Under 14 CFR 91.151(b), IFR helicopter operations require enough fuel to fly to the first point of intended landing, then to the alternate airport if one is required, and then for 30 minutes at normal cruising speed. For VFR helicopter operations, 14 CFR 91.151(a) requires enough fuel to fly to the first point of intended landing and then for 20 minutes at normal cruising speed. The IFR reserve is therefore higher — 30 minutes — and also includes alternate airport fuel if applicable, making it significantly more conservative than the VFR standard.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.151(b), IFR helicopter operations require enough fuel to (1) fly to the first point of intended landing, (2) then to the alternate airport if required by weather, and (3) then for 30 minutes at normal cruising speed. VFR operations under 14 CFR 91.151(a) require fuel to reach the first landing point plus 20 minutes at normal cruising speed. The IFR reserve is therefore higher — 30 minutes instead of 20 minutes — and the requirement explicitly includes sufficient fuel to reach a required alternate, making the IFR standard significantly more conservative and operationally demanding than VFR.
AI_EDIT	IH.I.C.S2.005	Fuel to destination: 45 min × 18 GPH = 13.5 gallons. Fuel to alternate: 20 min × 18 GPH = 6.0 gallons. Required IFR reserve: 30 min × 18 GPH = 9.0 gallons. Total required: 13.5 + 6.0 + 9.0 = 28.5 gallons. You only have 22 gallons remaining. You are 6.5 gallons short of the legal IFR fuel requirement under 14 CFR 91.151(b). Additionally, since destination weather is at minimums (300 OVC, 1/2 SM equals the 200-1/2 minimums), an alternate is required under 14 CFR 91.169 unless the 1-2-3 rule is met. You should immediately declare the fuel situation to ATC, request vectors to a closer fuel stop or the alternate, and consider declaring minimum fuel or an emergency if the situation deteriorates further.	->	Fuel to destination: 45 min × 18 GPH = 13.5 gallons. Required IFR reserve: 30 min × 18 GPH = 9.0 gallons. Total required: 13.5 + 9.0 = 22.5 gallons. You have 22 gallons remaining. Since destination weather is 300 OVC, 1/2 SM—which is ABOVE the minimums of 200-1/2—the destination is legal for approach and an alternate is not required under 14 CFR 91.169. However, your fuel situation is critical: you are 0.5 gallons short of the legal IFR requirement, with essentially no positive margin. You should immediately declare minimum fuel to ATC and request priority handling. The alternate fuel calculation is not a legal requirement in this scenario since the destination weather is above minimums.
AI_EDIT	IH.I.C.S4.001	V23 is a VOR-based Victor airway in the low-altitude domestic route structure. Victor airways are designated with a 'V' prefix followed by a number. They exist from 1,200 feet AGL (or the base of Class A airspace floor in some areas) up to but not including 18,000 feet MSL. The odd/even numbering convention relates to general orientation — odd numbers tend to run north/south, even numbers east/west, though this is a general guideline. To fly V23 IFR, you must be IFR-equipped and filed on that airway.	->	V23 is a VOR-based Victor airway in the low-altitude domestic route structure. Victor airways are designated with a 'V' prefix followed by a number. They operate from the surface or 1,200 feet AGL (whichever is higher, as established on the chart) up to but not including 18,000 feet MSL (FL180). The odd/even numbering convention relates to general orientation — odd numbers tend to run north/south, even numbers east/west, though this is a general guideline. Above FL180, the high-altitude jet route structure (Q-routes and J-routes) operates within Class A airspace. To fly V23 IFR, you must be IFR-equipped and filed on that airway.
AI_EDIT	IH.I.C.S4.006	FL180 is the lower boundary of Class A airspace and the floor of high-altitude jet route structure — Q-routes and J-routes operate within Class A airspace, which begins at 18,000 feet MSL (FL180). Victor airways top out below FL180. The crossing restriction 'at or above FL170' means you must arrive at HERON at no lower than FL170 — this is a minimum crossing altitude (MCA). On your charts, look for the MCA symbol (a flag or specific notation near the intersection) which would depict this requirement. The significance is obstacle clearance and airspace structure compliance: arriving below FL170 may place you below the MEA for the next segment or violate terrain/obstacle clearance before you reach FL180 Class A airspace. You must plan your climb profile to comply.	->	FL180 is the lower boundary of Class A airspace. Q-routes operate from FL180–FL450, and J-routes operate from FL240–FL450. Victor airways top out below FL180. The crossing restriction 'at or above FL170' is a minimum crossing altitude (MCA) depicted on your chart near the intersection. This MCA ensures obstacle clearance and/or compliance with airway MEA requirements before you reach the Class A floor at FL180. Arriving below FL170 could place you below required terrain/obstacle clearance margins for that segment. You must plan your climb profile to comply with this restriction and reach FL170 by HERON intersection.
AI_EDIT	IH.I.C.S5.001	Frost disrupts the smooth airflow over the rotor blade surface, which alters the blade's aerodynamic profile and reduces lift. Unlike fixed-wing aircraft where frost on wings is dangerous, rotor blades operate at high rotational speeds and precise aerodynamic tolerances — even a thin layer of frost can cause significant degradation in rotor efficiency, increase drag, and alter the balance and vibration characteristics of the rotor system. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook states that any contamination on rotor blades must be removed before flight. Unlike some fixed-wing operations, there are no approved 'contaminated rotor blade' takeoff procedures for helicopters.	->	Frost disrupts the smooth airflow over the rotor blade surface, altering the blade's aerodynamic profile and reducing lift. Rotor blades operate at high rotational speeds and precise aerodynamic tolerances—even a thin layer of frost causes significant degradation in rotor efficiency, increases drag, and alters balance and vibration characteristics. Any contamination on rotor blades must be removed before flight per FAA-H-8083-21B and 14 CFR 91.7 (airworthiness requirement). Unlike some fixed-wing ice protection systems, no approved contaminated rotor blade procedure exists for helicopters.
AI_EDIT	IH.II.B.K1.002	The magnetic compass directly measures the aircraft's magnetic heading by sensing the Earth's magnetic field. The two main errors are: (1) Magnetic Dip (Northerly Turning Error and Acceleration/Deceleration Error) — caused by the compass card tilting toward the Earth's magnetic field lines; turns through north and south show lagging or leading indications depending on hemisphere and direction. (2) Variation — the angular difference between true north and magnetic north, which varies by geographic location and must be applied to convert between true and magnetic headings. Additional errors include deviation (caused by aircraft magnetic fields) and oscillation error from turbulence or rotor vibration.	->	The magnetic compass directly measures the aircraft's magnetic heading by sensing the Earth's magnetic field. The two main compass errors are: (1) Magnetic Dip—the physical tilting of the compass card toward Earth's magnetic field lines, which causes Northerly Turning Error (lagging or leading indication during turns through north/south depending on hemisphere) and Acceleration/Deceleration Error (oscillation from north-south acceleration). (2) Variation—the angular difference between true north and magnetic north, which varies by geographic location and must be applied to convert between true and magnetic headings. Deviation, caused by the aircraft's magnetic field, is documented on a deviation card and is separate from variation.
AI_EDIT	IH.II.B.K1.004	The most likely cause is a blocked static port with an open pitot drain hole, or more commonly, a blocked pitot tube with the drain hole also blocked — but if only the pitot tube is blocked and the drain is open, the ASI will slowly bleed down to zero because the trapped ram air escapes through the drain. If the pitot tube is completely blocked (both tube and drain), the ASI acts like an altimeter — it increases as you climb and decreases as you descend because the static pressure continues to act on the reference side. If the altimeter and VSI are working normally, the static system is intact. To confirm, cross-check with GPS groundspeed (adjusting for wind) and check whether the indication changes with altitude changes in the manner described. Activate pitot heat if icing is suspected as the cause of pitot blockage.	->	The most likely cause is a blocked or partially blocked pitot tube. If the drain hole remains open, trapped ram air will bleed down and the ASI will gradually fall toward zero. If both the pitot tube and drain are blocked, the ASI will freeze at the reading when blockage occurred. Since the altimeter and VSI are working normally, the static system is intact. Confirm by cross-checking GPS groundspeed (adjusting for wind) against the suspected airspeed, and verify pitot heat is functioning per the RFM if icing is suspected. Do not continue IFR flight with a blocked pitot system; descend VFR and land.
