AI_EDIT	IH.II.B.S1.006	A VSI stuck at zero while the altimeter is still changing is a classic indicator of a blocked static port. The VSI requires a change in static pressure to show a reading; if the static line is blocked, it shows zero regardless of actual climb or descent. However, if the altimeter is still changing, either the block is partial, or the altimeter has its own static source. I would: (1) activate the alternate static source if equipped — this typically uses cabin static pressure and will cause a slight over-read on the altimeter and slightly high ASI indication; (2) verify the ASI is functioning correctly — a fully blocked static would also cause the ASI to become inaccurate; (3) cross-check attitude and power to maintain altitude using the attitude indicator and tachometer/torque; (4) advise ATC of the malfunction and request any necessary assistance. I would not rely on the VSI for climb/descent rates until the static system is confirmed functional.	->	A VSI stuck at zero while the altimeter is still gradually increasing suggests a partially blocked static port or static system degradation. The VSI is more sensitive to small static pressure changes and will show zero before the altimeter completely freezes; a fully blocked static would eventually freeze both instruments. Some aircraft also have separate static sources for different instruments, which could explain the discrepancy. I would: (1) activate the alternate static source if equipped — this typically uses cabin static pressure and will cause a slight over-read on the altimeter and slightly high ASI indication; (2) verify the ASI is functioning correctly — a fully blocked static would also cause the ASI to become inaccurate; (3) cross-check attitude and power to maintain altitude using the attitude indicator and tachometer/torque; (4) advise ATC of the malfunction and request any necessary assistance. I would not rely on the VSI for climb/descent rates until the static system is confirmed functional.
AI_EDIT	IH.II.C.K1.004	Possible causes include a calibration error within the altimeter itself, a partial static port blockage, a leak in the static system, or an incorrect altimeter setting being used. Per 14 CFR 91.411, an altimeter used for IFR flight must be tested and inspected within the preceding 24 calendar months. However, the regulation does not specify a maximum ground error allowance — that is typically stated in the helicopter's RFM or the altimeter's accuracy standards. As a practical standard, most operators and examiners reference the instrument accuracy tolerance of plus or minus 75 feet when set to the current altimeter setting at a known elevation. An 80-foot error is beyond that tolerance and should be investigated before IFR flight.	->	Possible causes include a calibration error within the altimeter itself, a partial static port blockage, a leak in the static system, or an incorrect altimeter setting being used. Per 14 CFR 91.411, an altimeter used for IFR flight must be tested and inspected within the preceding 24 calendar months. However, 14 CFR 91.411 does not specify a maximum ground error tolerance; instead, TSO-C10b (altimeter technical standard order) and most aircraft RFMs establish an acceptable tolerance of approximately ±75 feet when set to the current altimeter setting at a known elevation. An 80-foot error exceeds this standard tolerance and should be investigated before IFR flight.
AI_EDIT	IH.II.C.K1.006	Excessive precession in a heading indicator is generally considered a defect when the instrument drifts more than approximately 3 degrees in 15 minutes under normal operating conditions, though manufacturer specifications in the RFM may define a tighter tolerance. For helicopter IFR operations, this is especially critical because helicopters frequently operate at lower altitudes, make more frequent heading changes, and may fly in confined or complex airspace where precise heading control is essential for navigation and obstacle clearance. An unreliable heading indicator increases the risk of spatial disorientation and navigational error during IMC, particularly during approaches and missed approach procedures. The pilot must synchronize the heading indicator with the magnetic compass frequently, and excessive precession makes reliable heading information impossible to maintain.	->	Excessive precession in a heading indicator is typically defined as drift exceeding approximately 3 degrees in 15 minutes of operation under normal conditions. This tolerance is established by TSO-C4 standards; many manufacturers and operators require tighter limits per their RFM. Precession exceeding the serviceable limit (i.e., more than 3 degrees per 15 minutes) constitutes a defect and disqualifies the instrument for IFR flight. For helicopter IFR operations, this is critical because helicopters frequently operate at lower altitudes, make more frequent heading changes, and may fly in confined or complex airspace where precise heading control is essential for navigation and obstacle clearance. An unreliable heading indicator increases the risk of spatial disorientation and navigational error during IMC, particularly during approaches and missed approach procedures.
ESCALATE	IH.II.C.K2.003	GRABCARD is a fixed-wing mnemonic with unclear helicopter applicability per FAA sources; the answer also conflates VFR baseline items with IFR-specific additions in a way that may confuse the logical hierarchy of 91.205(b) vs. (d), and does not clearly address TAWS/terrain awareness requirements for rotorcraft under 91.205(d).
AI_EDIT	IH.II.C.K2.005	First, check the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) — if the VSI is listed as required equipment, it cannot be deferred and the flight cannot be conducted IFR. If not listed in the RFM as required, proceed to 14 CFR 91.213(d). Under 91.213(d), an inoperative instrument may be deferred if: (1) it is not required by 14 CFR 91.205 or any other regulation applicable to the flight, (2) it is not required by the RFM or type certificate data sheet, (3) the inoperative instrument is deactivated and placarded INOPERATIVE, and (4) if deactivation requires maintenance (such as pulling a circuit breaker permanently or removing equipment), a certificated maintenance person must perform it. The VSI is listed under 14 CFR 91.205(d) as a required IFR instrument — it is the rate-of-turn indicator reference for some helicopters, but the VSI specifically is not listed as a required IFR instrument under 91.205(d). However, if it is required by the specific helicopter's RFM or TCDS, it cannot be deferred. Assuming it is not required by those documents, it may be deferred per 91.213(d) after being placarded INOPERATIVE.	->	First, check the Rotorcraft Flight Manual (RFM) — if the VSI is listed as required equipment, it cannot be deferred and the flight cannot be conducted IFR. If not listed in the RFM as required, proceed to 14 CFR 91.213(d). Important distinction: 14 CFR 91.205(d) requires a rate-of-turn indicator (turn coordinator or turn indicator), not a vertical speed indicator (VSI). These are separate instruments. The VSI is not explicitly required by 91.205(d) for IFR flight. Under 91.213(d), an inoperative instrument may be deferred if: (1) it is not required by 14 CFR 91.205 or any other regulation applicable to the flight, (2) it is not required by the RFM or type certificate data sheet, (3) the inoperative instrument is deactivated and placarded INOPERATIVE, and (4) if deactivation requires maintenance (such as pulling a circuit breaker or removing equipment), a certificated maintenance person must perform it. Since the VSI is not listed as a required IFR instrument under 91.205(d), it may be deferred per 91.213(d) after being placarded INOPERATIVE, provided the rate-of-turn indicator is functional and the RFM does not require the VSI.
AI_EDIT	IH.II.C.R1.001	Under 91.213(d), all four of these must be satisfied: (1) The helicopter does not have an FAA-approved MEL; (2) the inoperative equipment is not required by the VFR-day type certificate instruments list, the certification rule under which the aircraft was type certificated, or any AD; (3) the inoperative equipment is not required by 14 CFR 91.205 or any other rule for the specific flight being conducted; and (4) the inoperative equipment is either removed and the cockpit control placarded, or deactivated and placarded 'INOPERATIVE.' If deactivation or removal requires maintenance, a certificated maintenance person must perform the work.	->	The four conditions in 91.213(d) are: (1) The inoperative equipment is not required by the VFR-day type certificate instruments list, the aircraft equipment list, or any airworthiness directive; (2) the inoperative equipment is not required by 14 CFR 91.205 or any other regulation for the specific flight being conducted; (3) the inoperative equipment is either removed and the cockpit control placarded, or deactivated and placarded 'INOPERATIVE,' with any required maintenance performed by a certificated maintenance person; and (4) the PIC determines the inoperative equipment does not constitute a hazard to the aircraft or persons on board.
ESCALATE	IH.III.A.K1.001	The answer's 'CRAFT' mnemonic and the list of five elements do not match FAA standard references; AIM 5-2-1 does not define these five specific elements or use this memory aid, and frequency/transponder are typically separate from the formal clearance elements.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.K1.006	When cleared for an approach without a specific routing to the IAF, the pilot may proceed via the most direct route to the IAF if already established on a segment of the approach or on a published feeder route. If not on a published route, the pilot should request clarification from ATC to ensure obstacle clearance is maintained. ATC has the responsibility to provide appropriate separation and issue routing to the IAF. The pilot should not assume random routing is acceptable — they should either be on a published route, a radar vector, or should query ATC. During arrival, the pilot must also comply with any published altitude restrictions on the STAR or approach procedure unless otherwise amended by ATC.	->	When cleared for an approach without a specific route to the IAF, the pilot should assume that ATC will either provide radar vectors or that the pilot is already established on a published feeder route or STAR transition. If not on a published route, the pilot must request clarification from ATC to obtain routing and confirm that vectors or a transition route will be provided. The pilot should not independently select a direct route. ATC has the responsibility to issue appropriate routing to ensure obstacle clearance and separation. The pilot must comply with any published altitude restrictions on the STAR or approach unless amended by ATC.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.K2.007	The FAA has broad enforcement discretion, but 14 CFR 91.3(b) protects PICs who deviate from rules during emergencies. The key legal question is whether the PIC's belief that an emergency existed was reasonable at the time — the regulation does not require the emergency to be objectively verified afterward, only that one existed from the PIC's perspective in the cockpit. Courts and NTSB have generally upheld that a PIC who acts in good faith based on the information available is protected. However, the FAA could still investigate whether the emergency declaration was made in good faith. If the PIC genuinely believed spatial disorientation constituted an emergency requiring immediate deviation, that is a defensible position. The written report (if requested) should accurately document the conditions as the PIC perceived them.	->	Declaring an emergency does not automatically trigger certificate action or a mandatory medical review. However, if the emergency involved a medical issue affecting the PIC (such as hypoxia, cardiac event, or incapacitation), the FAA may investigate whether the PIC is medically qualified to continue holding a certificate. NASA ASRS (Aviation Safety Reporting System) provides limited immunity from FAA enforcement for certain violations if the report is filed within 10 days and meets criteria — this is separate from the Aviation Safety Hotline (which does not grant immunity). The FAA's enforcement philosophy generally discourages punishing honest emergency declarations made in good faith, as it could deter future emergency declarations. Filing an ASRS report is a mitigating factor but does not shield you from enforcement if the emergency declaration was not made in good faith or if medical disqualification is discovered.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.K2.008	Multiple regulations apply simultaneously: (1) 14 CFR 91.3(b) — PIC emergency authority authorizes deviation from any Part 91 rule including altitude, routing, and airspace requirements; (2) 14 CFR 91.185 — lost communications procedures in IFR apply (squawk 7600, fly last assigned altitude or MEA, proceed to destination via filed route), but these are superseded by 91.3(b) if following them would compromise safety; (3) 14 CFR 91.135 — Class A operations requirements are waived under the emergency authority; (4) Squawk 7700 is appropriate to declare the emergency to ATC via transponder since voice is unavailable. The PIC's obligation is to navigate safely, use the transponder emergency code, land at the nearest suitable airport, and submit a written report of deviations if requested by the FAA. The lost comms procedures of 91.185 would apply as a baseline, but the oil emergency under 91.3(b) takes priority and justifies any departure from those procedures.	->	Most transponders can only transmit one code at a time — you select either 7600 (communications failure) or 7700 (emergency), not both simultaneously. When both emergency and communications failure exist, squawk 7700 first to alert ATC to the active emergency requiring immediate ATC coordination and airspace clearing. ATC will infer communications failure from your non-compliance with clearances and can sequence accordingly. Do not attempt to alternate or retain an assigned code while declaring 7700 — transmit 7700 continuously. This is the unambiguous guidance in AIM 6-4-2 and is the highest priority alert.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.K3.007	Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(1), when being radar vectored and communications are lost, the pilot must proceed by direct route from the point of radio failure to the fix, route, or airway specified in the vector clearance. If no specific fix was included in the vector clearance, the pilot should interpret the clearance in context — typically returning direct to the last assigned fix or airway the vector was intended to lead to, as understood from the clearance. For altitude, fly the highest of last assigned altitude, expected altitude, or the MEA for that route segment. The pilot should squawk 7600, document the time and position of failure, and fly as predictably as possible to allow ATC to sequence traffic.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(1), when being radar vectored and communications are lost, you must proceed by direct route from the point of radio failure to the fix, route, or airway specified in the vector clearance. If the vector clearance specified only a heading (270°) without an explicit termination fix or route, you should proceed direct to the last assigned fix or published airway you were being vectored toward, based on your understanding of the clearance context. For altitude, fly the highest of: last assigned altitude, expected altitude, or the MEA for that route segment. Squawk 7600, document the time, position, and exact heading assigned, and fly as predictably as possible to allow ATC to infer your position.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.R3.005	The risk is CFIT (Controlled Flight Into Terrain). When ATC issues a direct routing off of published airways, the responsibility for terrain and obstacle clearance shifts significantly to the pilot. The MEA and MOCA published on Victor airways guarantee obstacle clearance and navigation signal coverage; a random direct routing provides neither guarantee. Per the IFH, the MOCA only guarantees obstacle clearance within 22 NM of the NAVAID. If your assigned altitude has no published obstacle clearance assurance for that routing, you must evaluate your GPS terrain awareness tools, check sectional/IFR low charts for terrain along the direct route, and if any doubt exists, request the published airway routing instead. You should also confirm your GPS database is current (updated every 28 days) before relying on it for off-airway navigation. State 'Unable direct [fix], request [airway routing] for terrain clearance' if necessary.	->	The risk is CFIT (Controlled Flight Into Terrain). When ATC issues a direct routing off of published airways, the responsibility for terrain and obstacle clearance shifts significantly to the pilot. MEA and MOCA published on Victor airways guarantee obstacle clearance and navigation signal coverage along that airway within its lateral limits. A direct routing has no published obstacle clearance guarantee and is not covered by any published MOCA. Per the IFH, you must evaluate your GPS terrain awareness tools, check sectional/IFR low charts for terrain along the direct route, and if any doubt exists, request the published airway routing instead. You should also confirm your GPS database is current (updated every 28 days) before relying on it for off-airway navigation. State 'Unable direct [fix], request [airway routing] for terrain clearance' if necessary.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.R4.007	Immediate action: Advise ATC immediately — declare the situation clearly: 'Center, Helicopter N123HA, we inadvertently descended to 5,000, we were assigned 6,000, returning to 6,000 now.' Comply with any ATC instructions to resolve traffic conflict, which may include maintaining the lower altitude temporarily if ATC needs to sequence other traffic. After the flight, if the deviation resulted in a potential near-midair collision or NMAC, a NASA ASRS report (Aviation Safety Reporting System) should be filed within 10 days to retain immunity from certificate action under 14 CFR 91.25. If ATC issues a pilot deviation report, the pilot has the right to request 90 days to respond. The error chain here likely included distraction, incomplete call sign monitoring, and failure to verify — each link that could have been broken.	->	Immediate action: Advise ATC immediately — declare the situation clearly: 'Center, Helicopter N123HA, we inadvertently descended to 5,000, we were assigned 6,000, returning to 6,000 now.' Comply with any ATC instructions to resolve traffic conflict, which may include maintaining the lower altitude temporarily if ATC needs to sequence other traffic. After the flight, if the deviation resulted in a potential near-midair collision or NMAC, you may file a NASA ASRS report (Aviation Safety Reporting System) within 10 days to request immunity from FAA certificate action for unintentional violations—provided the violation had not already been reported to you by the FAA. However, ASRS immunity does not protect against criminal penalties, accidents involving alcohol or drugs, or deliberate violations. If ATC issues a pilot deviation report, you have the right to request 90 days to respond. The error chain here likely included distraction, incomplete call sign monitoring, and failure to verify — each link that could have been broken.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.S1.002	CRAFT stands for: Clearance limit (usually destination airport or fix), Route (the route you are cleared to fly), Altitude (initial assigned altitude and any climb restrictions), Frequency (departure control frequency), and Transponder (assigned squawk code). Using CRAFT as a structured template on your clearance sheet ensures you capture all critical elements of the clearance in an organized format, reducing the chance of missing information during a busy or complex ATC transmission.	->	CRAFT is a widely-used pilot memory aid (not an official FAA acronym) that stands for: Clearance limit (usually destination airport or fix), Route (the route you are cleared to fly), Altitude (initial assigned altitude and any climb restrictions), Frequency (departure control frequency), and Transponder (assigned squawk code). Using CRAFT as a structured template on your clearance sheet ensures you capture all critical elements of the clearance in an organized format, reducing the chance of missing information during a busy or complex ATC transmission. While not mandated by the FAA, this mnemonic aligns with the clearance elements emphasized in AIM 4-4-7.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.S1.004	The correct readback would be: 'Cleared to Riverside Airport, heading 270, radar vectors to Victor 23, then direct. Climb and maintain 3,000, expect 6,000 ten minutes after departure. Departure 124.4, squawk 4721, Helicopter 34 Whiskey.' Key elements: the clearance limit, route, altitude assignments (both 3,000 and the expected altitude must be read back), frequency, and squawk code are all included. The aircraft call sign is included at the end (or beginning) of the readback. The phrase 'climb and maintain' is repeated verbatim because it contains an altitude assignment. Omitting the expected altitude or the frequency would be an incomplete readback.	->	The correct readback would be: 'Cleared to Riverside Airport, heading 270, radar vectors to Victor 23, then direct. Climb and maintain 3,000, expect 6,000 ten minutes after departure. Departure 124.4, squawk 4721, Helicopter 34 Whiskey.' Key elements that must be read back per AIM 4-4-7: clearance limit, route of flight, assigned altitude (3,000 — 'climb and maintain' is repeated verbatim because it contains an altitude assignment), frequency, and squawk code. The expected altitude is informational; acknowledging it demonstrates good practice and situational awareness but is not mandatorily required by the readback standard. The aircraft call sign is included at the beginning or end of the readback. Omitting the assigned altitude, frequency, or squawk code would be an incomplete readback; omitting the expected altitude would not constitute a regulatory violation, though it is professionally advisable to acknowledge it.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.S1.008	Per the AIM, a 'cruise clearance' authorizes the pilot to conduct flight at any altitude from the minimum IFR altitude up to and including the assigned cruise altitude. It also authorizes the pilot to execute the approach at the destination and, once you have begun a descent from cruise altitude in the context of an approach, you may not return to cruise altitude without additional ATC clearance. A common misinterpretation is that 'cruise 6,000' means you can climb back up to 6,000 after descending during the approach — this is incorrect. Once you commence descent for the approach, the cruise clearance is considered used. The dangerous application for helicopter pilots specifically: some pilots treat the cruise clearance as blanket authorization to descend to MIA/MVA level and conduct low-altitude visual reconnaissance of a destination area before flying the instrument approach. This is not authorized — you may not descend below published minimum IFR altitudes for that segment without being established on an instrument approach procedure or having received a specific clearance to do so.	->	Per the AIM, a 'cruise clearance' authorizes the pilot to conduct flight at any altitude from the minimum IFR altitude up to and including the assigned cruise altitude, and also authorizes execution of the approach at the destination. Importantly, once you report departing an altitude during descent for the purpose of executing the instrument approach procedure, the cruise clearance is considered used and you may not climb back above that departure altitude without additional ATC clearance. Tactical descents during the cruise phase for ride quality, terrain, or navigation do not end cruise authorization — you retain the right to climb back to the assigned cruise altitude. A common misinterpretation is that 'cruise 6,000' means you can descend to MIA/MVA level and conduct low-altitude visual reconnaissance before the approach — this is incorrect. A dangerous helicopter-specific misinterpretation: some pilots treat cruise clearance as blanket authorization to descend to minimum altitudes and conduct low-altitude visual reconnaissance of a destination area without being established on an instrument approach procedure. This is not authorized — you may not descend below published minimum IFR altitudes for that segment without being established on an instrument approach or having received a specific clearance to do so.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.S4.006	First, read back the clearance correctly to confirm the route and intercept fix. Then: (1) Identify the T-route designation and associated waypoints from your charts or moving map; (2) Load the route segment into the GPS as a direct-to or by modifying the flight plan to include the T-route waypoints; (3) Determine your current position relative to the T-route and calculate a reasonable intercept angle — avoid angles greater than 45 degrees if close to the route; (4) Use the GPS CDI to track the intercept — confirm the CDI is sourced to GPS and cross-check with your situational awareness display; (5) Arm LNAV if using autopilot for the intercept; (6) Monitor CDI needle movement and anticipate the turn-on point. Low-altitude RNAV T-routes are only available to GPS-equipped aircraft and require an IFR-approved GPS. Timely compliance means the route is loaded and intercept is underway without extended delay in vectoring off cleared airspace.	->	First, read back the clearance correctly to confirm the route and intercept fix. Then: (1) Identify the T-route designation and associated waypoints from your charts or moving map; (2) Load the route segment into the GPS as a direct-to or by modifying the flight plan to include the T-route waypoints; (3) Determine your current position relative to the T-route and calculate a reasonable intercept angle — avoid angles greater than 45 degrees if close to the route; (4) Use the GPS CDI to track the intercept — confirm the CDI is sourced to GPS and cross-check with your situational awareness display; (5) Arm LNAV if using autopilot for the intercept; (6) Monitor CDI needle movement and anticipate the turn-on point. Low-altitude RNAV T-routes are only available to GPS-equipped aircraft and require an IFR-approved GPS. Timely compliance means the route is loaded and intercept is underway without extended delay in vectoring off cleared airspace. Regulatory basis: AIM 5-3-4 (RNAV routes), 14 CFR 91.111 (compliance with clearances), FAA-H-8083-15B Chapter 1 (T-routes).
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.S5.002	A CDI has a total deflection of full-scale (typically representing 2.5 NM each side for a VOR, or ±0.3 NM full scale for LNAV). Three-quarter scale deflection means the needle has moved 75% of the way toward full deflection. The ACS requires you to track a course, radial, or bearing and keep the CDI within ¾-scale of center. Exceeding ¾-scale deflection indicates unacceptable deviation from the assigned course.	->	A CDI's full-scale deflection represents different distances depending on the navigation source. For a standard VOR, full-scale deflection = ±5 NM (2.5 NM per side from course centerline), so ¾-scale deflection ≈ 3.75 NM from centerline. For LNAV precision approaches, full-scale is typically ±0.3 NM, so ¾-scale ≈ 0.225 NM. The ACS requires you to track a course, radial, or bearing and keep the CDI within ¾-scale of center during IFR operations. Exceeding ¾-scale deflection indicates unacceptable deviation from the assigned course and requires corrective action.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.S6.002	Approximately 80% of aviation accidents are caused by pilot error (human factors). This statistic underscores why SRM is critical during ATC clearance compliance — the pilot must manage workload so that reading back, copying, and executing a clearance does not overwhelm cognitive capacity and lead to errors in aircraft control, navigation, or situational awareness.	->	Human factors, including pilot decision-making and error, are the primary contributors to aviation accidents (cited in various studies across 50–80% of accidents depending on definition and database). This statistic underscores why SRM is critical during ATC clearance compliance — the pilot must manage workload carefully so that reading back, copying, and executing a clearance does not overwhelm cognitive capacity and lead to errors in aircraft control, navigation, or situational awareness. Effective SRM includes using checklists, reducing distractions, and breaking complex clearances into manageable steps.
ESCALATE	IH.III.A.S6.006	The claim that accidents correlate more strongly with hours in type than total hours, and the specific 100-hour threshold, are presented as direct Helicopter Flying Handbook statements but cannot be verified in the cited reference; the principle is sound but the source attribution must be confirmed before shipping.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.A.S7.008	Taxiing in compliance with ATC is required, but the After Landing checklist contains items with direct IFR safety implications that must not be forgotten. The correct approach is to comply with the taxi instruction immediately, then complete the remaining checklist items systematically during taxi at a safe pace. Items with direct IFR safety implications include: transponder mode — should be set to ground or standby per local procedures to prevent altitude readout errors on the ground; pitot heat — should be turned off after landing unless conditions warrant continued use (leaving it on can damage the element); avionics — radios should be tuned to ground frequency; and flight plan — confirming the IFR flight plan has been closed or coordinating with ATC/FSS. If the flight plan is not closed, a search and rescue could be initiated 30 minutes after ETA. Per FAA-H-8083-15B, flight plans time out 2 hours after proposed departure if not activated, but after landing, failure to close triggers SAR response. Never rush the close-out of the IFR flight plan regardless of taxi workload.	->	If an IFR flight plan is not closed after landing, ATC will attempt to contact the pilot. If no contact is made within 30 minutes of the ETA (or last known position), ATC notifies the appropriate ARTCC, which initiates search and rescue coordination with the FAA and the Air Force Rescue Coordination Center (AFRCC) or Coast Guard as applicable. The pilot in command is ultimately responsible for ensuring the flight plan is closed — either by coordinating closure with ATC directly upon landing at a towered airport, or by calling FSS at 1-800-WX-BRIEF at non-towered airports under IFR. Failing to close a flight plan can trigger a costly and disruptive SAR response. While ATC may assist in closure at towered airports, pilots should not rely on this and must proactively confirm closure. There is no specific FAR penalty for failing to close an IFR flight plan, but the cost and disruption of an unnecessary SAR response makes closure a critical pilot responsibility.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.K1.003	You are required to report reaching the holding fix when ATC has not issued a time to expect further clearance (EFC) or when you are flying in radar contact but ATC has specifically requested a position report. The report should include: your aircraft identification, the fix where you are holding, your altitude, and the time you arrived at the fix. If you were issued an EFC, you must also report if you are unable to hold at the assigned speed or if fuel becomes a factor.	->	You are required to report reaching a holding fix when NOT in radar contact with ATC, or when in radar contact if ATC has specifically requested the report. The report should include aircraft identification, the fix name, current altitude, and time of arrival at the fix. If you were issued an EFC, you should confirm you can comply with it; if unable to hold at the assigned speed or if fuel endurance becomes a concern, report that as well.
