AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.R3.006	Immediately assess fuel. Under 14 CFR 91.167, you must carry enough fuel to fly to the destination, fly to the alternate, and then fly 45 minutes at normal cruising speed. With destination now LIFR and below any published approach minimums, proceeding there is not viable. You must now evaluate whether the alternate still meets alternate filing criteria (per 14 CFR 91.169 — standard alternate minimums are 600-2 for precision, 800-2 for non-precision, or as published). At 500/1, the alternate may be marginal or below standard alternate minimums. If fuel permits, request a different alternate or divert to a suitable airport now. If fuel is critical, declare minimum fuel or an emergency. Do not continue toward a destination with zero probability of landing.	->	Immediately assess fuel. Under 14 CFR 91.167, you must carry enough fuel to fly to the destination, fly to the alternate, and then fly 45 minutes at normal cruising speed. With destination now LIFR and below any published approach minimums, proceeding there is not viable. You must now evaluate whether the alternate still meets alternate airport minimums per 14 CFR 91.169(c). Standard IFR alternate minimums are: precision approach 1,200 feet ceiling and 3 statute miles visibility, or non-precision approach 1,500 feet ceiling and 3 statute miles visibility. Published minimums for that specific airport may be lower if specified on the approach chart. At 500/1, your alternate is well below both standard and typical published minimums. If fuel permits, request a different alternate or divert to a suitable airport now. If fuel is critical, declare minimum fuel or an emergency. Do not continue toward a destination with zero probability of landing.
AUTO_APPROVE	IH.III.B.R3.008	The answer correctly identifies 14 CFR 91.169(b) (the no-alternate-required criteria), accurately describes the compounded risk factors (night, mountains, no pre-planned alternate, deteriorating weather), and appropriately recommends filing an alternate as a best practice even when not legally required.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.R4.003	For helicopters, the maximum holding airspeed is 90 KIAS unless a higher speed is published on the chart. Exceeding this airspeed increases the turning radius during the pattern, which can cause the helicopter to fly outside the protected airspace boundaries designed for that hold. At higher speeds, drift due to wind also compounds more quickly, making it harder to maintain the inbound course and increasing the risk of airspace incursion or terrain conflict.	->	Holding airspeed limits are set by altitude and published on the chart. Per AIM 5-3-7(j), the regulatory holding speed limits are 200 KIAS below 6,000 feet MSL, 230 KIAS from 6,000–14,000 feet, and 265 KIAS above 14,000 feet. However, helicopters typically operate at much lower speeds (40–60 KIAS or less) due to performance and control limitations, so aircraft capability rather than regulatory limits is usually the constraining factor. Always observe the speed shown on the published holding chart. Exceeding the holding airspeed increases turning radius, causes the helicopter to drift outside the protected airspace boundaries designed for the hold, and complicates wind correction, increasing risk of terrain conflict or airspace incursion.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.R4.004	The primary risks are: (1) Wind displacement on the outbound leg causing the inbound track to miss the fix and potentially exit protected airspace; (2) Extended inbound legs in a headwind that could require more fuel than anticipated; (3) Determining the correct entry procedure while already managing workload in IMC. To manage these risks before reaching the fix: identify the wind's effect on the inbound and outbound legs, plan to triple the wind correction angle on the outbound leg initially as a starting point (doubling is standard, but early adjustments help establish the pattern faster), time the outbound leg to achieve a 1-minute inbound leg, and use all available automation (if equipped) to reduce workload. Calculating entry type early — before reaching the fix — prevents rushed decisions at the fix itself, which is when error chains often begin.	->	The primary risks are: (1) Wind displacement on the outbound leg causing the inbound track to miss the fix and potentially exit protected airspace; (2) Extended inbound legs in a headwind that could require more fuel than anticipated; (3) Determining the correct entry procedure while already managing workload in IMC. To manage these risks before reaching the fix: identify the wind's effect on the inbound and outbound legs, plan to apply a wind correction angle on the outbound heading that is approximately double the wind correction angle used on the inbound leg (per FAA-H-8083-15B standard technique), time the outbound leg to achieve a 1-minute inbound leg, and use all available automation (if equipped) to reduce workload. Calculating entry type early — before reaching the fix — prevents rushed decisions at the fix itself, which is when error chains often begin. Make small adjustments after the first pattern to refine correction based on actual drift observed.
