AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S3.004	The holding course is inbound on the 090° radial (flying toward the station, so inbound heading is 090°). The hold is left turns. With a current heading of 085°, I am nearly aligned with the inbound course. I monitor the CDI and the TO/FROM indicator — fix arrival occurs when the TO flag flips to FROM, indicating station passage. At that moment I note my heading (085°) relative to the inbound holding course (090°). Since I am within 70° of the inbound course on the holding side, I use a direct entry: I immediately turn left (left turns specified) to the outbound heading of 270°, time one minute outbound, then turn left again to intercept and fly the inbound course. Throughout, I maintain my IFR cruise airspeed and begin reducing to holding speed if required — for helicopters, holding speed is not to exceed 90 KIAS below 6,000 ft MSL per AIM unless otherwise specified.	->	The holding course is on the 270° radial with an inbound heading of 090° (flying toward the station). I monitor the CDI and the TO/FROM indicator—fix arrival occurs when the TO flag flips to FROM, indicating station passage. At that moment I note my heading (085°) relative to the inbound holding course (090°). Since I am within 70° of the inbound course on the holding side, I use a direct entry: I immediately turn left (left turns specified) to the outbound heading of 270°, time one minute outbound, then turn left again to intercept and fly the inbound course. Throughout, I maintain my IFR cruise airspeed and verify holding compliance with AIM 5-3-7: Category A aircraft (helicopters) are not to exceed 200 knots indicated airspeed; my 90 KIAS is well within limits.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S3.008	By continuing inbound 12 seconds past the fix before initiating entry, the pilot has flown into the approach segment airspace beyond the IAF without a clearance for the approach, potentially conflicting with the approach course structure and any obstacle clearance designed from that fix outward. At 80 KIAS, 12 seconds equals roughly 480 feet of unauthorized penetration past the fix. The protected holding airspace assumes entry begins at the fix. With GPS, fix arrival recognition differs from traditional navaids: there is no TO/FROM flip and no needle swing. Instead, the pilot must recognize the GPS annunciation — the active leg name changes from the inbound segment to the holding pattern leg (e.g., 'HOLD AT [waypoint]'), and the CDI may briefly go full-scale as the navigator begins computing the outbound leg. The pilot must be briefed in advance to watch for the GPS mode and leg annunciation as the fix arrival cue, just as they would watch for a VOR TO/FROM flip. Failure to understand GPS-specific fix arrival indications is a training gap that becomes dangerous in IMC.	->	By continuing inbound 12 seconds past the fix before initiating entry, the pilot has extended beyond the holding fix, compressing the protected holding airspace and potentially conflicting with the procedure design. At 80 KIAS, 12 seconds equals approximately 1,100 feet of penetration past the fix. The protected holding airspace assumes entry begins at the fix. With GPS, fix arrival recognition differs from traditional navaids: there is no TO/FROM flip and no needle swing. Instead, the pilot must recognize the GPS annunciation — the active leg name changes from the inbound segment to the holding pattern leg (e.g., 'HOLD AT [waypoint]'), and the CDI may briefly go full-scale as the navigator begins computing the outbound leg. The pilot must be briefed in advance to watch for the GPS mode and leg annunciation as the fix arrival cue, just as they would watch for a VOR TO/FROM flip. Failure to understand GPS-specific fix arrival indications is a training gap that becomes dangerous in IMC. The holding clearance authorizes holding at the fix; the violation is failure to enter the pattern at the fix as required by the procedure.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S4.006	In GPS terminal mode (typically within 30 NM of the destination), the CDI full-scale deflection is ±1 NM. In en route mode it is ±2 NM. Three-quarter scale in terminal mode therefore represents approximately 0.75 NM off course. This is far more precise than a VOR (which is ±12° at full scale) but less sensitive than a localizer. The practical implication is that crosswind corrections and tracking must be prompt and accurate — small heading errors compound quickly and can push the needle beyond ¾-scale more easily than with a VOR. Pilots should make small, early wind corrections rather than waiting for significant CDI movement.	->	In GPS terminal mode (typically within 30 NM of the destination), the CDI full-scale deflection is typically ±1 NM, though some RNAV procedures use ±0.3 NM depending on procedure design and equipment certification. Three-quarter scale therefore represents approximately 0.75 NM off course. GPS CDI sensitivity is more precise than a VOR needle (which represents much larger linear deviations at distance from the station), making crosswind corrections and tracking more critical. Pilots must make small, early wind corrections rather than waiting for significant CDI movement, as small heading errors compound quickly in the tighter GPS sensitivity band. Verify the specific CDI sensitivity for your aircraft and procedure in the AFM supplement or approach briefing.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S5.004	Because the inbound leg was 45 seconds longer than the one-minute target, you reduce the outbound leg timing by 45 seconds — fly the outbound leg for only 15 seconds. The general rule is to adjust the outbound leg time by the same amount the inbound deviated from one minute. Additionally, apply wind correction on the outbound: turn further into the wind on the outbound heading to compensate for drift, keeping the inbound leg on the correct radial or course back to the fix.	->	Because the inbound leg was 45 seconds longer than the one-minute target, reduce the next outbound leg by 20–30 seconds on the subsequent circuit, rather than the full 45-second correction. This prevents overcorrection and maintains adequate maneuvering space and turn radius. Monitor subsequent circuits and fine-tune the timing as needed. Additionally, apply wind correction on the outbound: turn further into the wind on the outbound heading to compensate for drift, keeping the inbound leg on the correct radial or course back to the fix. For helicopters at 9,500 feet, ensure the outbound leg remains long enough (typically 30–45 seconds minimum) to establish stable flight and adequate turn geometry.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S6.005	A standard circuit (one minute inbound, one minute outbound, plus turn time of roughly one minute each turn — approximately 4 minutes total per circuit) means you have 14 minutes until EFC. You can complete approximately 3 full circuits (3 × 4 min ≈ 12 minutes) and then arrive over the fix around 1433. To refine the arrival time, during the third outbound leg you can extend or shorten the outbound time to adjust when you cross the fix. You might stretch the third outbound leg slightly so you cross the fix closer to 1435. Always cross the fix on a whole or half-minute boundary to keep your timing predictable for ATC.	->	A standard circuit consists of approximately 1 minute inbound, 1 minute outbound, plus turn time. Turn time depends on your aircraft's airspeed, bank angle, and wind; for a typical helicopter at holding speeds, plan 1–1.5 minutes per turn, so a full circuit is approximately 3.5–4.5 minutes. With 14 minutes until EFC, you can complete approximately 3 full circuits (10.5–13.5 minutes depending on actual turn times). During the third outbound leg, adjust your timing to cross the fix near 1435. Use your actual groundspeed and turn rate to refine the leg times. Always cross the fix on a whole or half-minute boundary for predictability with ATC.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S6.006	If the needle passes abeam sooner than expected each circuit, the wind is pushing you faster along the outbound track — meaning there is a tailwind component on the outbound leg and a headwind on the inbound. As a result, your inbound legs are likely taking longer than one minute. You should reduce outbound time to shorten inbound legs back to one minute. Additionally, since the headwind slows you inbound, you may need to add a small wind correction angle to maintain the inbound track if there is also a crosswind component. Adjust outbound heading accordingly using the triple-the-inbound-correction method.	->	If the needle passes abeam sooner than expected, you are progressing outbound faster than planned, indicating a tailwind on the outbound leg and likely a headwind on the inbound leg. This means your inbound leg will take longer than one minute to cover the same ground distance to the fix. Reduce outbound time to compensate and bring inbound timing back to approximately one minute. Additionally, determine the wind correction angle needed to track inbound accurately toward the fix, then apply approximately three times that correction angle (in the opposite direction) on the outbound heading to account for drift. Re-evaluate after each circuit and adjust as needed.
