AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S7.005	The MFD is showing you that you are arriving at the fix position short of where you should be turning inbound, which suggests your outbound leg timing or distance is insufficient — or a strong headwind on the inbound is causing you to reach the fix sooner than expected. You should lengthen the outbound leg time (standard is 1 minute below 14,000 ft MSL, 1.5 minutes above) or, in a GPS-equipped aircraft, monitor the distance displayed to the hold fix. The MFD's ground track depiction also confirms whether you are lined up on the inbound course prior to fix crossing, allowing real-time adjustments to outbound leg length on subsequent circuits.	->	The MFD is showing you that you are arriving at the fix position sooner than expected, which suggests your outbound leg was too short, or you have a tailwind on the inbound leg compressing the arrival time. You should lengthen the outbound leg time (standard is 1 minute below 14,000 ft MSL, 1.5 minutes above) or, in a GPS-equipped aircraft, monitor the distance-to-fix displayed on the MFD. If the distance closes rapidly, shorten the next outbound leg; if it closes slowly, lengthen it. The MFD's ground track depiction also confirms whether you are lined up on the inbound course prior to fix crossing, allowing real-time adjustments to outbound leg length on subsequent circuits. Note: a headwind on inbound would delay your arrival at the fix, not accelerate it.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S7.007	This is a sensor disagreement that requires immediate cross-checking. First, verify GPS integrity — check for any RAIM alerts or GPS NOTAM flags. Then cross-check the MFD with your heading indicator and raw data CDI to determine which is consistent. In general, in a RAIM-validated GPS environment the GPS ground track on the MFD is highly accurate; however, if holding over a VOR fix on an IFR clearance, the VOR is the primary navigational reference for that fix. A half-dot CDI deflection indicates approximately 6° off course at VOR range. The correct action is to treat this as a potential GPS anomaly, fly the raw data VOR to maintain the inbound course, report the discrepancy to ATC, and request confirmation of your position. Do not blindly trust the MFD when primary navigation reference shows a deviation.	->	This is a sensor disagreement that requires immediate cross-checking. First, verify GPS integrity — check for any RAIM alerts or GPS NOTAM flags. Then cross-check the MFD with your heading indicator and raw data CDI to determine which is consistent. In general, in a RAIM-validated GPS environment the GPS ground track on the MFD is highly accurate; however, if holding over a VOR fix on an IFR clearance, the VOR CDI governs fix crossing and clearance compliance — GPS is supplemental situational awareness. A half-dot CDI deflection indicates approximately 6° off course at VOR range. The correct action is to treat this as a potential GPS anomaly, fly the raw data VOR to maintain the inbound course, report the discrepancy to ATC, and request confirmation of your position. Do not substitute GPS position for VOR fix crossing if the clearance specifies a VOR fix. Follow-up correction: Several factors can cause a false VOR CDI deflection: (1) Course width variation — VOR course sensitivity widens with distance from the station, so at long range a half-dot may represent a larger actual displacement than expected; (2) VOR ground station errors — FAA allows a ±1° VOR accuracy standard, and some stations have known bends in certain radials listed on NOTAMs; (3) Quadrantal error or terrain reflection effects causing signal bends; (4) Receiver error or outdated VOR receiver calibration; (5) An incorrectly set OBS. The pilot should check OBS setting and ATIS/NOTAMs for VOR anomalies, and cross-check with a second VOR if available.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S8.003	Per AIM 5-3-8 and the Instrument Procedures Handbook, helicopters may use published holding airspeed limits. At or below 6,000 ft MSL, the maximum holding airspeed is 200 KIAS. Between 6,001 and 14,000 ft MSL, the limit is 230 KIAS. Above 14,000 ft MSL, the limit is 265 KIAS. However, helicopters conducting IFR operations often fly at much lower speeds. If a chart depicts a specific holding airspeed restriction, that restriction must be complied with and reported to ATC if unable to comply.	->	While AIM 5-3-8 provides standard holding airspeed limits for fixed-wing aircraft at or below 6,000 ft MSL (200 KIAS), between 6,001 and 14,000 ft MSL (230 KIAS), and above 14,000 ft MSL (265 KIAS), helicopter operations may have published restrictions specific to helicopter performance on individual procedures. Per FAA-H-8083-21B, helicopters conducting IFR operations typically hold at much lower airspeeds (often 40–60 knots). Any charted holding airspeed restriction must be complied with. If a chart depicts a specific holding airspeed restriction, you must report to ATC if unable to comply. Always comply with the most restrictive charted limitation for the holding procedure.
AI_EDIT	IH.III.B.S9.006	Holding patterns are one of the highest-workload phases of IFR flight for a helicopter pilot — they involve precise navigation, timing, wind correction, fuel awareness, and ATC communication simultaneously. The human factors risk is highest when a pilot becomes task-saturated or complacent, leading to missed entries, timing errors, or CFIT during turns in IMC. The SRM behavior that most directly addresses this is proactive situational awareness — specifically, briefing the hold before reaching the fix, using all available cockpit resources (GPS, automation, charts), and maintaining the discipline to prioritize aircraft control over communication when workload spikes.	->	Holding patterns are one of the highest-workload phases of IFR flight for a helicopter pilot — they involve precise navigation, timing, wind correction, fuel awareness, and ATC communication simultaneously. The human factors risk is highest when a pilot becomes task-saturated or complacent, leading to missed entries, timing errors, or CFIT during turns in IMC. The SRM behavior that most directly addresses this is proactive situational awareness — specifically, briefing the hold before reaching the fix, using all available cockpit resources (GPS, automation, charts), and maintaining the discipline to prioritize aircraft control over communication when workload spikes.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.A.K2.005	The altimeter should generally be trusted over the VSI in this scenario, because the altimeter is an absolute pressure instrument measuring actual altitude while the VSI is a differential pressure instrument that measures the rate of change. The VSI has a built-in lag of 6–9 seconds, meaning it may reflect a descent that has already stopped. However, if the discrepancy persists, the pilot should consider that the altimeter's static port could be blocked or that the VSI is stuck. Cross-checking with GPS altitude and other instruments is essential. A partially blocked static system could cause both to read incorrectly, requiring use of an alternate static source.	->	In this specific case, the altimeter is most likely correct because the VSI has a built-in lag of 6–9 seconds and may be reflecting a descent that has already ended. The steady altimeter reading combined with a transient VSI descent indication is a classic example of VSI lag. However, if the discrepancy persists beyond 10 seconds, you should suspect a static system malfunction affecting both instruments. Cross-check immediately with GPS altitude and other instruments. A partially blocked static port could cause both to read incorrectly, requiring use of an alternate static source if equipped. Never rely on a single instrument; always use the 6–9 second lag characteristic of the VSI as your initial hypothesis, then verify with independent sources before taking any action.
ESCALATE	IH.IV.A.K3.007	The follow-up answer discusses breaking VSI glass as an emergency procedure for static port blockage; while FAA-H-8083-15B mentions this for fixed-wing aircraft, helicopter-specific applicability, cockpit pressure effects, and modern instrument design variations are unclear and safety-critical, requiring verification against current helicopter POHs and guidance.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.A.R2.001	Spatial disorientation is the inability of a pilot to correctly sense the aircraft's position, motion, or attitude relative to the earth. Helicopter pilots are especially vulnerable because helicopters operate at lower altitudes, slower speeds, and can maneuver in all axes simultaneously — making the vestibular and proprioceptive senses more likely to conflict with instrument indications. Additionally, the vibration environment of a helicopter can mask subtle attitude changes, and pilots transitioning from hover to instrument flight may experience disorientation due to the constant subtle motion of the platform.	->	Spatial disorientation is the inability of a pilot to correctly sense the aircraft's position, motion, or attitude relative to the earth. Helicopter pilots are especially vulnerable because: (1) Helicopters operate at low speeds and in hovering flight, generating vestibular sensations that are difficult for the inner ear to interpret accurately; (2) Transitional flight and frequent attitude changes exceed the thresholds of the semicircular canals and otolith organs, leading to false pitch and roll sensations; (3) In IMC without external visual references, the vestibular system becomes the dominant input and will generate illusions during slow maneuvers common in helicopter operations. The slower motion profiles characteristic of helicopter flight make it particularly difficult for the inner ear to detect attitude changes, increasing reliance on instruments to maintain accurate situational awareness.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.A.R2.002	Three common vestibular illusions are: (1) The Leans — the pilot perceives a wings-level attitude while actually banked, often after a gradual roll that goes undetected by the semicircular canals; (2) Graveyard Spiral — after a prolonged coordinated turn, the pilot senses level flight and pushes forward cyclic while actually in a descending turn, increasing descent rate; (3) Somatogravic Illusion — rapid acceleration causes the pilot to sense a nose-high pitch attitude and apply forward cyclic, potentially placing the aircraft in a dangerous descent. All three can be fatal if the pilot does not override the false sensory input with instrument cross-check.	->	Three common vestibular illusions are: (1) The Leans — the pilot perceives a wings-level attitude while actually banked, often after a gradual roll that goes undetected by the semicircular canals; (2) Graveyard Spiral — after a prolonged coordinated turn, the pilot's vestibular system adapts and stops signaling the turn (semicircular canal adaptation). The pilot now perceives level flight and forward motion, but the aircraft is actually in a continued descending turn. The pilot applies forward cyclic thinking they are recovering from a climb, steepening both bank and descent. The false sensation is one of level flight (both wings and pitch) while actually spiraling down; (3) Somatogravic Illusion — rapid acceleration causes the pilot to sense a nose-high pitch attitude and apply forward cyclic, potentially placing the aircraft in a dangerous descent. All three can be fatal if the pilot does not override the false sensory input with instrument cross-check.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.A.R2.008	Immediate actions: establish attitude first — cross-check attitude indicator, altimeter, VSI, and airspeed indicator simultaneously; make a gentle, controlled correction back to assigned altitude using a pitch change appropriate to the deviation (per IFH guidance, a deviation under 100 feet warrants approximately a 1° pitch correction with VSI correction rate double the altitude deviation); contact ATC to report the deviation if it warrants notification; and re-establish a disciplined radial scan pattern. The relationship between fixation and spatial disorientation is direct: when attention is locked on a single instrument (or non-flight instrument like a moving map), the continuous cross-check breaks down and the pilot loses trend awareness. During this fixation gap, the vestibular system becomes the dominant input, and without external visual references in IMC, it will generate false sensations — particularly if any small attitude change occurred during the fixation period. The pilot may now believe a corrective input is not needed (because the body 'feels' level) even though the altimeter clearly shows otherwise. This scenario illustrates why single-pilot IFR workload management is critical and why automation (autopilot) should be used during high-workload tasks like GPS programming.	->	Before turning back to assigned heading, verify the heading indicator is properly set and has not precessed — cross-check it against the magnetic compass (in straight-and-level, unaccelerated flight) to confirm the indication is accurate. A 15° heading deviation could be instrument error rather than an actual navigation deviation. Also consider: is the deviation toward or away from terrain or other traffic? Check the moving map for proximate terrain and traffic if TCAS/TIS is available. Then contact ATC and advise of the deviation — under 14 CFR 91.123, ATC clearances must be complied with, and if you deviated from your clearance, ATC should be informed promptly. If confirmed actual deviation, execute a controlled turn (approximately 10–15° bank appropriate for the helicopter type) back to assigned heading, maintaining instrument scan throughout the turn. Do not rush the correction with a steep bank — a controlled, gradual turn minimizes the risk of inducing additional vestibular illusions during the correction.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.K1.007	This scenario combines two compounding threats: (1) Startle and fixation — the unexpected cockpit warning triggers a startle response, causing momentary fixation on the alert system rather than aircraft control. In IMC, those 3 seconds of diverted attention allow attitude deviations to develop with no outside visual reference to catch them; (2) Low-inertia rotor RPM decay — the FAA HFH explicitly states that low-inertia rotors decay rapidly after engine failure; every second of delayed collective reduction allows RRPM to bleed toward a stall condition that is unrecoverable. The combination means the pilot faces both a deteriorating attitude (due to physiological fixation) and a deteriorating rotor state (due to rotor inertia characteristics) simultaneously in IMC. Prevention measures include: (a) Thorough type-specific emergency procedure training — the Aeronautical Decision-Making framework emphasizes that automaticity in emergencies comes from realistic, repeated practice; (b) Developing a personal memory aid or flow that prioritizes 'fly first' — lower collective immediately upon any power loss indication before diagnosing alerts; (c) Pre-departure mental rehearsal (chair flying) of IMC engine failure scenarios specific to the aircraft; (d) Using automation (autopilot/stability augmentation) during high-workload phases so that if distraction occurs, the aircraft holds attitude momentarily, buying critical seconds.	->	This scenario combines two compounding threats: (1) Startle and fixation — the unexpected cockpit warning triggers a startle response, causing momentary fixation on the alert system rather than aircraft control. In IMC, those 3 seconds of diverted attention allow attitude deviations to develop with no outside visual reference to catch them; (2) Low-inertia rotor RPM decay — the FAA HFH explicitly states that low-inertia rotors decay rapidly after engine failure; every second of delayed collective reduction allows RRPM to bleed toward a stall condition that becomes impractical to recover from. The combination means the pilot faces both a deteriorating attitude (due to physiological fixation) and a deteriorating rotor state (due to rotor inertia characteristics) simultaneously in IMC. Once rotor blades reach critically low RPM and stall, recovery becomes impractical because the pilot cannot increase collective (which would worsen the stall) and rotor kinetic energy is insufficient to allow rapid RRPM recovery; there is no practical recovery window at dangerously low RPM. Prevention measures include: (a) Thorough type-specific emergency procedure training — the Aeronautical Decision-Making framework emphasizes that automaticity in emergencies comes from realistic, repeated practice; (b) Developing a personal memory aid or flow that prioritizes 'fly first' — lower collective immediately upon any power loss indication before diagnosing alerts; (c) Pre-departure mental rehearsal (chair flying) of IMC engine failure scenarios specific to the aircraft; (d) Using automation (autopilot/stability augmentation) during high-workload phases so that if distraction occurs, the aircraft holds attitude momentarily, buying critical seconds.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.K2.001	For a nose-high unusual attitude recovery: (1) Apply forward cyclic to lower the nose toward the horizon, (2) add power as needed to prevent excessive airspeed loss and rotor RPM decay, and (3) level the wings with cyclic. The priority is to prevent rotor RPM decay first — if RPM is deteriorating, lower collective slightly while simultaneously adding forward cyclic. Once attitude is back near normal, re-establish trim and resume normal instrument cross-check.	->	For a nose-high unusual attitude recovery: (1) Check rotor RPM immediately — if RPM is decaying toward minimum, lower collective slightly to reduce blade pitch and prevent rotor stall; (2) Simultaneously apply forward cyclic to lower the nose toward the horizon; (3) Once attitude is stabilizing and RPM is recovering, add engine power as needed to prevent excessive airspeed loss; (4) Level the wings with cyclic inputs as needed; (5) Re-establish trim and resume normal instrument scan. The priority is rotor RPM preservation — collective reduction prevents stall and must occur concurrently with or even prior to aggressive pitch control, depending on rotor RPM status.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.K3.002	The six basic flight instruments are: attitude indicator, altimeter, vertical speed indicator (VSI), airspeed indicator (ASI), heading indicator, and turn-and-slip indicator. During straight-and-level flight, the altimeter is the primary pitch instrument because it directly shows whether the helicopter is maintaining the desired altitude.	->	The six basic flight instruments are: attitude indicator, altimeter, vertical speed indicator (VSI), airspeed indicator (ASI), heading indicator, and turn-and-slip indicator. During straight-and-level flight, the attitude indicator is the primary pitch instrument because it directly shows the pitch attitude needed to maintain altitude. The altimeter is the supporting/confirming pitch instrument—it verifies whether the pitch attitude established on the attitude indicator is successfully maintaining the desired altitude.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.K3.004	The recommended procedure for IIMC recovery is: (1) Recognize the situation immediately and resist spatial disorientation — trust the instruments. (2) Establish positive aircraft control using the attitude indicator to level the wings and set a safe pitch attitude. (3) Reduce airspeed to a slow, controlled speed to buy time and reduce the risk of CFIT. (4) Climb if terrain or obstacles permit, to gain obstacle clearance while maintaining instrument control. (5) Execute a 180-degree turn using a standard rate turn to reverse course back toward VMC — use the turn-and-slip indicator or attitude indicator to maintain the turn. (6) Declare an emergency on 121.5 MHz or contact ATC on the last assigned frequency for radar assistance. (7) If VMC cannot be regained quickly, consider an instrument approach or ATC-guided descent to a safe landing area. Throughout, maintain a disciplined radial instrument scan to avoid fixation on any single instrument.	->	The recommended procedure for IIMC recovery is: (1) Recognize the situation immediately and resist spatial disorientation — trust the instruments. (2) Establish positive aircraft control using the attitude indicator to level the wings and set a safe pitch attitude. (3) Reduce airspeed to a slow, controlled speed to buy time and reduce the risk of CFIT. (4) Given low-level mountainous terrain, attempt to escape laterally by executing a 180-degree turn to return toward VMC rather than climbing blindly into instrument conditions. If safe terrain clearance cannot be assured, maintain altitude and execute the turn; only climb if ATC radar guidance confirms obstacle clearance. (5) Execute a 180-degree turn using a standard rate turn to reverse course back toward VMC — use the turn-and-slip indicator or attitude indicator to maintain the turn. (6) Declare an emergency on 121.5 MHz or contact ATC on the last assigned frequency for radar assistance. (7) If VMC cannot be regained quickly, request ATC radar vectors to a controlled descent or instrument approach to a safe landing area. Throughout, maintain a disciplined radial instrument scan to avoid fixation on any single instrument.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.K3.008	Regulatory obligations depend on whether an emergency was declared. If the pilot declared an emergency under 14 CFR 91.3, they may operate the helicopter in a manner necessary to meet that emergency. If ATC requests a written report, the pilot may be asked to submit one, but there is no automatic FAA enforcement action for a declared emergency in good faith. Regarding spatial disorientation: if the pilot experienced severe disorientation or incapacitation, they should consider whether a medical condition contributed and whether they are fit to continue operations. FAA guidance encourages voluntary safety reporting through the Aviation Safety Hotline or NASA ASRS (Aviation Safety Reporting System) — an ASRS report filed within 10 days provides immunity from certain certificate action if a violation occurred. From a proficiency standpoint, the pilot should schedule instrument recurrency training with a CFII to address the skill gaps exposed by the event. If spatial disorientation was severe enough to raise concern about an underlying vestibular disorder, the pilot should consult an Aviation Medical Examiner (AME) before further flight.	->	Regulatory obligations depend on whether an emergency was declared. If the pilot declared an emergency under 14 CFR 91.3, they may operate the helicopter in a manner necessary to meet that emergency. If ATC requests a written report, the pilot may be asked to submit one, but there is no automatic FAA enforcement action for a declared emergency in good faith. Regarding spatial disorientation: if the pilot experienced severe disorientation or incapacitation, they should consider whether a medical condition contributed and whether they are fit to continue operations. FAA guidance encourages voluntary safety reporting through the Aviation Safety Hotline or NASA ASRS (Aviation Safety Reporting System) — an ASRS report filed within 10 days provides one-time immunity from civil penalty and certificate action (suspension or revocation) for unintentional violations, provided the pilot has not received a prior violation notice for a similar violation in the prior 5 years. Immunity does not apply to criminal violations, accidents, or intentional violations. From a proficiency standpoint, the pilot should schedule instrument recurrency training with a CFII to address the skill gaps exposed by the event. If spatial disorientation was severe enough to raise concern about an underlying vestibular disorder, the pilot should consult an Aviation Medical Examiner (AME) before further flight.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.R4.001	Mast bumping occurs when the rotor hub contacts the main rotor mast during extreme flapping movements. It is a concern on semirigid (teetering) rotor systems because these rotors are free to flap but have no lead/lag capability and rely on the teetering hinge. In a low-G condition, rotor thrust is reduced and the stabilizing gyroscopic effect is lost, allowing the rotor disk to flap dramatically and strike the mast. Articulated and rigid rotor systems are not subject to mast bumping in the same way.	->	Mast bumping occurs when the rotor hub contacts the main rotor mast during extreme flapping movements. It is a primary concern exclusively on semirigid (teetering) rotor systems because these rotors are free to flap but have no lead/lag capability and rely on the teetering hinge for blade movement. In a low-G condition, rotor thrust is reduced and the stabilizing gyroscopic effect is lost, allowing the rotor disk to flap dramatically and strike the mast. Articulated rotor systems have lead/lag hinges that dissipate blade flapping energy and prevent mast bumping. Rigid rotor systems do not experience mast bumping due to their structural constraints.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.R6.007	FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13 specifically notes that accident rates correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours, and that accident rates decrease significantly after 100 hours in type. At 60 hours in type, this pilot has not yet developed the deeply automatic instrument scan and control responses needed to simultaneously manage a distraction and maintain situational awareness. Her cognitive resources are still consumed by basic instrument interpretation, leaving little reserve for threat management. When she finally recognizes the unusual attitude, her recovery inputs are more likely to be abrupt — precisely the wrong technique for helicopter unusual attitude recovery — because her procedures are not yet automatic. The result is compounded risk: an already serious unusual attitude is handled by a pilot whose attentional and motor resources are most limited.	->	FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13 notes that accident rates correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours, with rates decreasing significantly after 100 hours in type. At 60 hours in type, this pilot has not yet developed the deeply automatic instrument scan and control responses needed to simultaneously manage a distraction and maintain situational awareness. Her cognitive resources are still consumed by basic instrument interpretation, leaving little reserve for threat management. When she finally recognizes the unusual attitude, her recovery inputs are more likely to be abrupt — precisely the wrong technique for helicopter unusual attitude recovery — because her procedures are not yet automatic. The result is compounded risk: an already serious unusual attitude is handled by a pilot whose attentional and motor resources are most limited. Standard recovery technique emphasizes smooth, coordinated control inputs guided by instruments, not aggressive correction.
AI_EDIT	IH.IV.B.S2.001	SRM stands for Single-Pilot Resource Management. Three components directly relevant during unusual attitude recovery are: (1) Situational awareness — knowing where the helicopter is spatially and what instruments are telling you; (2) Workload management — prioritizing the most critical control inputs and instrument scan rather than fixating on secondary tasks; and (3) Risk management — recognizing that delaying recognition or action increases the hazard of spatial disorientation. Automation management is also relevant if autopilot or stability augmentation systems are involved.	->	SRM stands for Single-Pilot Resource Management. The three formal components described in FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13 are: (1) Situational Awareness — accurate knowledge of the aircraft's position, attitude, altitude, and airspeed relative to terrain and obstacles; (2) Risk Management — systematically identifying hazards (spatial disorientation, CFIT) and implementing mitigations (instrument scan discipline, cross-checks, small control inputs); and (3) Personal Attitude — maintaining the discipline and humility to trust instruments rather than physical sensation, and to follow proven procedures even under stress. Alternatively, the FAA describes decision-making using the DECIDE model: Detect the problem, Estimate severity, Choose a course of action, Identify needed actions, Do the action, and Evaluate results. All of these support recovery by keeping the pilot instrument-focused and methodical.
ESCALATE	IH.IV.B.S2.006	The substantive advice on instrument recovery is correct and well-reasoned, but 'Trescott Tip' is not a standard FAA term found in FAA-H-8083-21B, FAA-H-8083-15B, or other approved references. The verifier correctly flags this as non-standard terminology that requires validation before shipping to ensure students are not tested on colloquial or unofficial names.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.K1.004	The 090 radial runs FROM the station to the east, so inbound on the 090 radial means flying westbound (heading approximately 270°). First, identify your current position relative to the 090 radial by checking the CDI with the OBS set to 090. Determine which side of the radial you are on and select an intercept heading — typically 30 to 45 degrees off the inbound course (e.g., 240° if north of the radial, 300° if south). Monitor the CDI for movement toward center. As the needle centers, turn to the inbound course of 270° and apply a wind correction angle to maintain tracking. The CDI will be set to 090 with a FROM indication until you cross the station, then a TO indication on the inbound leg.	->	The 090 radial emanates FROM the station toward the east. Flying inbound ON the 090 radial means flying FROM the east TOWARD the station, requiring a heading of approximately 270° (westbound). First, identify your current position relative to the 090 radial by checking the CDI with the OBS set to 090. You will initially see a FROM flag. Determine which side of the radial you are on and select an intercept heading — typically 30 to 45 degrees off the inbound course (e.g., 240° if north of the radial, 300° if south). Monitor the CDI for needle movement toward center. As the needle centers and you approach the station, the flag will change from FROM to TO. Turn to the inbound heading of 270° and apply a wind correction angle to maintain tracking on the 090 radial inbound.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.K1.006	You are above the MOCA of 2,400 feet, so terrain and obstruction clearance is provided. However, you are below the MEA of 3,000 feet. The MEA guarantees both obstacle clearance AND reliable VOR navigation signal reception. The MOCA only guarantees obstacle clearance, with navigation signal reception assured only within 22 NM of the NAVAID. Flying at 2,500 feet means your VOR reception may be unreliable depending on your distance from the station. You are not legal on this airway IFR unless ATC has specifically cleared you at or above the MOCA and you are within 22 NM of the VOR, or you are operating under a special IFR clearance.	->	Yes, you are legal. You are at 2,500 feet, which is above the MOCA of 2,400 feet, and 14 CFR 91.177(a) permits operation at or above the MOCA (rather than the MEA) when the MOCA is established and you are within 22 NM of the navigational facility. The MEA guarantees both obstacle clearance AND reliable VOR signal reception. The MOCA guarantees obstacle clearance only, with VOR signal reception assured within 22 NM of the station. At 2,500 feet and within 22 NM, you have adequate obstacle clearance and can expect reliable VOR reception. However, verify your distance from the VOR station; if beyond 22 NM, request climb to MEA or request an alternate approach.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.K2.008	Several factors can cause an RNAV approach to be flagged as unavailable despite good signal and RAIM: (1) Expired navigation database — the approach procedure data may have changed in a recent AIRAC cycle, and an expired database triggers this annunciation on some units; (2) The approach was not properly sequenced — the pilot must activate the approach and confirm proper waypoint sequencing rather than just loading it; (3) The aircraft is outside the approach's geographic activation window — some GPS units require the aircraft to be within a certain distance of the airport and on an appropriate vector before the approach will activate; (4) A NOTAM has been issued that affects GPS or WAAS availability for that specific approach at that location — some avionics cross-reference NOTAM data if connected to a data link; (5) Equipment certification limitation — the GPS unit may not be certified (TSO level) for the specific approach type selected (e.g., a TSO-C129 unit cannot fly RNP approaches requiring TSO-C146); (6) Satellite geometry (PDOP) may be marginal even if RAIM is not flagged yet. Troubleshoot by verifying database currency, confirming the correct approach is selected and activated, checking relevant NOTAMs, verifying the aircraft's avionics certification against the approach requirements, and contacting ATC to report the issue and request an alternate approach.	->	Several factors can cause an RNAV approach to be flagged as unavailable despite good signal and RAIM: (1) Expired navigation database — the approach procedure data may have changed in a recent AIRAC cycle, and an expired database triggers this annunciation on some units; (2) The approach was not properly sequenced — the pilot must activate the approach and confirm proper waypoint sequencing rather than just loading it; (3) The aircraft is outside the approach's geographic activation window — some GPS units require the aircraft to be within a certain distance of the airport and on an appropriate vector before the approach will activate; (4) A NOTAM has been issued that affects GPS or WAAS availability for that specific approach at that location — some avionics cross-reference NOTAM data if connected to a data link; (5) Equipment certification limitation — the GPS unit's TSO certification may not match the approach type selected (e.g., a TSO-C129a unit cannot fly LPV approaches, which require WAAS); the aircraft's avionics supplement in the RFM/POH must explicitly approve operation with the specific GPS equipment for the approach type; (6) Satellite geometry (PDOP) may be marginal even if RAIM is not flagged yet. Troubleshoot by verifying database currency, confirming the correct approach is selected and activated, checking relevant NOTAMs, verifying the aircraft's avionics certification against the approach requirements, and contacting ATC to report the issue and request an alternate approach. Follow-up: TSO-C145c covers panel-mount WAAS receivers and TSO-C146c covers portable WAAS receivers; both are concurrent active standards. TSO-C129a covers non-WAAS GPS for LNAV approaches only. Authorization depends on the aircraft's avionics supplement approval in the POH/RFM, not the TSO rating alone.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.R1.006	GPS navigation becomes unreliable when the receiver indicates a loss of required navigation integrity — typically shown by a 'GPS NOT AVAILABLE,' 'RAIM NOT AVAILABLE,' or similar annunciation, or when the number of satellites drops below the minimum required for the operation. On a T-route, RNAV capability is required; if GPS integrity is lost, the pilot is no longer able to legally or safely navigate that route. Risk management steps include: monitoring RAIM or SBAS status throughout the flight; obtaining a RAIM prediction before departure; having a backup navigation plan using conventional NAVAIDs where available; notifying ATC immediately if GPS navigation is lost so an alternative clearance can be issued; and not exceeding the operational limitations of the installed GPS equipment. The MEA on a T-route guarantees obstacle clearance but navigation signal only within 22 NM of a NAVAID, so GPS dependency must be managed carefully.	->	GPS navigation becomes unreliable when the receiver indicates a loss of required navigation integrity — typically shown by a 'GPS NOT AVAILABLE,' 'RAIM NOT AVAILABLE,' or similar annunciation, or when SBAS integrity is lost below the published RNP requirement for the route (typically RNP 0.3 for T-routes). On a published RNAV T-route, GPS integrity is charted and guaranteed by the published minimum altitude; if GPS/SBAS integrity is lost below that minimum, the pilot is no longer authorized to fly that route. Risk management steps include: monitoring RAIM or SBAS status throughout the flight; obtaining a RAIM prediction before departure; having a backup navigation plan using conventional NAVAIDs where available; notifying ATC immediately if GPS navigation is lost so an alternative clearance can be issued; and not exceeding the operational limitations of the installed GPS equipment. MOCA (Minimum Off-route Altitude) guarantees VOR reception within 22 NM of a NAVAID, so GPS dependency must be managed carefully on routes where VOR backup may be limited beyond that range.
ESCALATE	IH.V.A.R2.006	While the principle that hours-in-type is a stronger accident predictor than total hours is well-established in aviation safety literature, the specific comparative claim that a 400-hour pilot with 200 hours in type faces lower disorientation risk than a 1,200-hour pilot with 40 hours in type is presented as fact without citation to a specific accident study or FAA database analysis that directly supports this exact comparison; the assertion requires verification against NTSB accident data.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.S2.008	A centered CDI confirms you are on the selected radial — it tells you the angular direction from the VOR station, but it does not tell you your distance along that radial. With no GPS and no DME indicated, you cannot determine how far along the radial you are. Additionally, you are at the MOCA — which only guarantees VOR reception within 22 NM of the station. At 40 NM from the VOR, the MOCA does not guarantee navigation signal integrity, meaning the centered CDI may be unreliable or a false indication. The weakest link is the combination of being below MEA (at MOCA) beyond the 22 NM guaranteed reception range and having no independent means (DME, GPS, second VOR cross-radial) to confirm position along the route. You should request an immediate climb to the MEA to restore guaranteed signal reception, advise ATC of your navigation equipment status, and not rely solely on the single VOR CDI for position determination until you are within 22 NM of the station or have climbed to MEA.	->	A centered CDI confirms you are on the selected radial — it tells you the angular direction from the VOR station, but it does not tell you your distance along that radial. With no GPS and no DME indicated, you cannot determine how far along the radial you are. Additionally, you are at the MOCA — which only guarantees VOR reception within 22 NM of the station. At 40 NM from the VOR, you are beyond the range where MOCA guarantees signal reception, meaning the centered CDI may be unreliable or a false indication. The weakest link is the combination of being beyond the 22 NM guaranteed reception range and having no independent means (DME, GPS, second VOR cross-radial) to confirm position along the route. You should request an immediate climb to the MEA to restore guaranteed signal integrity along the entire segment, advise ATC of your navigation equipment status, and not rely solely on the single VOR CDI for position determination until you are within 22 NM of the station or have climbed to MEA.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.S3.001	Center the CDI with a TO indication to identify your current position relative to the desired radial, then twist the OBS to set the inbound course (the reciprocal of the radial) you intend to intercept. This orients the instrument so deflection direction tells you which side of the course you are on and which way to turn.	->	After identifying the station, set the OBS to the inbound course (reciprocal of the radial) you intend to intercept. Observe the CDI deflection and TO/FROM indication—this tells you which side of the course you are on and which way to turn to intercept. Then fly the appropriate intercept heading and center the CDI as you approach the course.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.S4.004	The 180° radial inbound means you will be flying TO the station on a course of 360° (reciprocal of the radial). Your current heading is 090°. First, determine the angular relationship: the inbound course is 360° and your current heading is 090°, so the intercept angle is 90°. That is too steep for a smooth intercept, so you would select a 45° intercept angle, giving you a heading of 315° (360° minus 45°). Set the OBS to 360° and fly 315°. The CDI will begin deflecting toward center as you approach the course. Because the VOR CDI has a 10° per dot deflection (roughly 2° per dot), you begin your turn to 360° when the needle is approximately one dot from center — about a 30° lead point — to allow for rollout without overshooting. Once established, track inbound at 360° with corrections for any crosswind.	->	The 180° radial inbound means you will be flying TO the station on a course of 360° (reciprocal of the radial). To select a reasonable intercept angle, choose 45° from the desired course: 360° − 45° = 315° intercept heading. Set the OBS to 360° and fly heading 315°. The CDI will deflect toward center as you close on the course. Standard VOR CDI sensitivity is approximately 2° per full-scale dot. Use a lead point of about one dot deflection from center—turn toward the 360° heading when the needle shows approximately one dot—to allow for rollout and avoid overshoot. Once established, track inbound at 360° with corrections for any crosswind.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.S6.004	At 13.4 NM you are still within the ±1 NM tolerance (limit is 13 to 15 NM on a 14-mile arc — or in this case, 11 to 13 NM on a 12 DME arc, so 13.4 NM means you are already 1.4 NM outside the arc and beyond the tolerance). You must immediately turn toward the station — increase your arc-inbound lead — to reduce the DME distance back toward 12 NM. Lead the turn so you don't overshoot inward. You should use a turn of roughly 20–30° toward the station and monitor the DME closely to roll out as you approach the 12 NM point, anticipating the need to resume the arc heading. If you delay, the deviation will worsen and you risk busting the standard.	->	At 13.4 NM you are outside the ±1 NM tolerance (limits are 11–13 NM on a 12 DME arc), meaning you are 0.4 NM beyond the arc boundary. You must immediately turn toward the station to reduce DME distance back toward 12 NM. Use a turn of roughly 20–30° toward the station and monitor the DME closely. Lead the rollout so you don't overshoot inward, returning to the arc heading as you approach 12 NM. Prompt correction prevents further deviation and maintains the published arc standard.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.S6.008	This is a critical misunderstanding. GPS CDI scaling changes depending on the phase of flight. In en route mode, full-scale CDI deflection represents ±5 NM. In terminal mode (within 30 NM of destination), it transitions to ±1 NM. Once the approach is activated and within 2 NM of the FAF, it transitions to approach mode at ±0.3 NM (LNAV) full scale. So ¾-scale in approach mode represents approximately 0.225 NM — a much tighter physical tolerance than either VOR or en route GPS. The ACS standard of ¾ scale still applies, but the pilot must understand that the CDI is far more sensitive in approach mode, and the same visual deflection represents much less physical distance off course. Overcorrecting aggressively like a VOR will cause the same oscillation problem seen on localizers.	->	This is a critical misunderstanding. GPS CDI scaling changes depending on the phase of flight. Typical values (which vary by manufacturer per TSO-C129 and must be verified in the aircraft POH) are: en route mode ±5 NM full scale; terminal mode (within ~30 NM of destination) ±1 NM full scale; and approach mode (within ~2 NM of the FAF) ±0.3 NM (LNAV) full scale. Therefore, ¾-scale in approach mode represents approximately 0.225 NM — a much tighter physical tolerance than either VOR or en route GPS. The ACS standard of ¾ scale still applies, but the pilot must understand that the CDI is far more sensitive in approach mode, and the same visual deflection represents much less physical distance off course. Overcorrecting aggressively like a VOR will cause oscillation. Always verify your specific GPS/RNAV system's scaling in the aircraft POH or avionics documentation (e.g., G1000, Avidyne).
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.S9.001	You select NAV mode (or HEADING mode to position the aircraft on an intercept angle first, then arm NAV for capture). Before the autopilot can capture the course, you must have the correct OBS course set in the CDI/HSI, the navaid tuned and identified, and the autopilot armed for course capture. The autopilot will fly the selected heading until the CDI begins to center, then transition to track the radial.	->	You select NAV mode (or the equivalent course-capture mode per the aircraft AFM—nomenclature varies; some systems use GPS, LOC, or APR depending on the navaid source). Before the autopilot can capture the course, you must have: (1) the correct OBS course set in the CDI/HSI [or GPS waypoint selected], (2) the navaid tuned and identified [or GPS position verified], (3) the autopilot mode selector positioned correctly per the AFM, and (4) NAV capture armed or auto-engage enabled per the aircraft autopilot supplement. The autopilot will fly the selected heading until the CDI begins to center, then transition to track the radial. Always verify mode annunciation against the AFM before arming for capture.
AI_EDIT	IH.V.A.S9.004	ARM the NAV mode as soon as you are established on the intercept heading and the navaid is tuned, identified, and the OBS is set to 270° (the inbound course). Some systems require NAV to be armed before the CDI begins moving — check the AFM/POH for the specific autopilot's arm-and-capture sequence. You confirm correct transition by observing: (1) the CDI begins to move from full deflection toward center as the aircraft approaches the radial, (2) the autopilot begins a smooth turn toward the course as the CDI centers, and (3) the mode annunciator transitions from HDG/NAV ARM to NAV TRACK or equivalent. The aircraft should roll out wings-level on the 270° course with the CDI centered.	->	Set the OBS to 270° (the reciprocal of the 090 radial, and the direction of the inbound course). ARM NAV mode per the aircraft AFM—the exact timing is critical and AFM-dependent: some systems require NAV to be armed before the CDI begins moving; others allow arming after you are established on the intercept heading. Consult the autopilot supplement for the correct arm sequence. You confirm correct transition by observing: (1) the CDI begins to move from full deflection toward center as the aircraft approaches the radial, (2) the autopilot begins a smooth turn toward the 270° course as the CDI centers, and (3) the mode annunciator transitions from HDG/NAV ARM to NAV TRACK or equivalent. The aircraft should roll out wings-level on the 270° course with the CDI centered.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.K1.008	When ATC issues 'Descend via the STAR,' the pilot is expected to comply with all published altitude constraints and speed restrictions on the STAR — it is a clearance to follow the lateral path AND the vertical profile simultaneously. However, when ATC subsequently issues 'Descend and maintain 6,000,' this cancels the 'descend via' clearance for the altitude dimension. The pilot is now cleared to descend to and maintain 6,000 feet MSL and is no longer required to comply with intermediate published altitude constraints — EXCEPT for any 'AT OR ABOVE' constraints that fall between the aircraft's current position and 6,000 feet, which could still be relevant for obstacle clearance. Speed restrictions on the STAR, however, remain in effect unless ATC explicitly cancels them. This is a common area of confusion: 'Descend and maintain' cancels the vertical profile of the descend via, but does NOT automatically cancel published speed restrictions. The pilot should confirm with ATC if there is any ambiguity.	->	When ATC issues 'Descend via the STAR,' the pilot is expected to comply with all published altitude constraints and speed restrictions on the STAR—it is a clearance to follow the lateral path AND the vertical profile simultaneously. When ATC subsequently issues 'Descend and maintain 6,000,' this cancels the 'descend via' clearance for the vertical profile. However, 'AT OR ABOVE' altitude constraints remain mandatory—if any fix has an 'AT OR ABOVE' constraint higher than 6,000 feet, you cannot descend below that constraint without ATC clearance (e.g., an 'AT OR ABOVE 7,500' constraint is still binding even though you are cleared to 6,000). Only 'AT OR BELOW' constraints are superseded by the lower 'descend and maintain' clearance. Speed restrictions on the STAR remain in effect unless ATC explicitly cancels them. This distinction is critical: obstacle clearance constraints (AT OR ABOVE) cannot be overridden by a lower altitude clearance.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.K2.002	If ATC has not assigned a discrete transponder code, you should squawk 1200 for VFR flight. For IFR flight, ATC will assign a discrete squawk code. Until that code is received and acknowledged, you should squawk the last assigned code or follow ATC instructions. Squawk 7700 is for emergency, 7600 for radio failure, and 7500 for hijack.	->	For IFR flight, ATC will assign a discrete transponder code as part of your initial clearance before you are cleared to depart. You should never operate IFR without an ATC-assigned discrete transponder code. If for some reason you have not received a code assignment before departure, contact ATC for a discrete code before entering controlled airspace. Do not squawk 1200 while operating IFR under ATC control—1200 is only used by VFR aircraft not receiving ATC radar services. If ATC instructs you to squawk a specific code, comply with that instruction. Emergency codes are 7700 (emergency), 7600 (radio failure), and 7500 (hijack).
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.R2.008	This scenario requires applying a layered risk management approach recognizing that each traffic avoidance system has independent failure modes that can produce a falsely clear picture simultaneously: (1) TCAS I only detects transponder-equipped aircraft and provides no RA guidance — non-transponder aircraft and transponder failures are invisible. (2) ADS-B In is limited by TIS-B ground station coverage and only shows ADS-B Out aircraft — at low altitudes common to EMS, TIS-B may be absent. (3) ATC radar has its own coverage gaps and update rates — a 'no traffic' call means no detected radar returns, not confirmation of clear airspace. A proper risk framework uses the PAVE checklist (Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures), recognizes automation complacency as a significant human factor risk (FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13), and treats traffic avoidance equipment as supplementary — not primary — layers of defense. The primary risk control remains: maintaining strict IFR procedure compliance, precise route adherence, and robust ATC communication so that if any layer fails, the others provide protection.	->	This scenario requires applying a layered risk management approach recognizing that each traffic avoidance system has independent failure modes that can produce a falsely clear picture simultaneously: (1) TCAS I provides only Traffic Advisories (TAs)—Resolution Advisories (RAs) are available only on TCAS II. TCAS I detects only transponder-equipped aircraft; non-transponder aircraft and transponder failures are invisible. (2) ADS-B In is limited by TIS-B ground station coverage and only shows ADS-B Out aircraft—at low altitudes common to EMS, TIS-B coverage may be absent or degraded. (3) ATC radar has its own coverage gaps and update rates—a 'no traffic' call reflects only detected radar returns, not confirmation of clear airspace. A proper risk framework uses the PAVE checklist (Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures) and recognizes automation complacency as a significant human factor risk (FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13). Treat traffic avoidance equipment as supplementary—not primary—layers of defense. The primary risk controls remain: maintaining strict IFR procedure compliance, precise route adherence, and robust ATC communication so that if any layer fails, the others provide protection.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.S3.006	You would consult the IFR Low Altitude Enroute Chart and, if applicable, the Area Charts for terminal areas. For an off-airway direct route, you must identify the Minimum IFR Altitude (MIA) or, if flying in mountainous terrain, ensure adequate obstacle clearance per 14 CFR 91.177. You should look up the MEA for any airways near your route as a reference, and check for any restricted airspace, MOAs, or prohibited areas along the route. The chart legend and supplemental information pages also identify navigation requirements for the route. A TEC route or preferred IFR routing publication (available via FAA AIM or Chart Supplement) may also apply.	->	You would consult the IFR Low Altitude Enroute Chart and Area Charts for terminal areas. For an off-airway direct route, you must comply with 14 CFR 91.177, which requires 1,000 feet of obstacle clearance in non-mountainous terrain or 2,000 feet in mountainous terrain. You should review the charts to identify terrain elevation, airways' MEA values (for reference only), restricted airspace, MOAs, and navigation coverage along your proposed route. While MEA applies to published airways, your off-airway altitude must satisfy 91.177 clearance requirements. The chart legend and supplemental information pages will also identify navigation requirements and any preferred IFR routing for your region.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.S4.004	First, verify your radio is on the correct frequency and try calling again. If still no contact, attempt to reach the previous facility (ARTCC) on their frequency or on the emergency frequency 121.5 MHz. You may also try calling other ATC facilities, FSS, or relay through other aircraft. If you cannot establish contact and are operating IFR, 14 CFR 91.185 applies — you must continue the flight using the route/altitude from the assigned clearance, expected altitude, or filed altitude (whichever is highest), and proceed to the destination. Squawk 7600 immediately to alert ATC of the communication failure.	->	First, verify the frequency is correct and make multiple attempts on the assigned frequency. If still no contact, attempt to reach the ARTCC facility you just left, then try other ATC facilities or FSS. You may also try 121.5 MHz as a backup. Squawk 7600 to alert ATC of the communication failure. Per 14 CFR 91.185, if unable to re-establish two-way communication and operating IFR, continue on your assigned altitude; if reassignment was pending, use the altitude ATC advised to expect; if neither is available, use your filed altitude. Proceed to your destination using the last assigned routing and altitude.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.S4.007	The pilot is responsible for knowing the status of Special Use Airspace. 'By NOTAM' means the SUA may be active, and an ATC routing clearance does not automatically authorize penetration of restricted or prohibited airspace — only warning area and alert areas may be entered on an IFR clearance without specific coordination. For restricted or prohibited areas, ATC must confirm the airspace is inactive or that the controlling agency has authorized entry. You should immediately ask ATC: 'N123AB, verify [SUA name] is inactive or that we have authorization to enter.' If ATC confirms, document it; if not confirmed, request an alternate routing. Complying with a clearance into active restricted airspace is a violation regardless of ATC instruction.	->	You retain pilot-in-command responsibility under 14 CFR 91.3. While ATC coordinates with controlling agencies for restricted airspace, you should verify the status when practical. 'By NOTAM' indicates the airspace may be active and you should ask ATC: 'Confirm [SUA name] is inactive or we have authorization to penetrate.' ATC clearances into restricted airspace are based on coordination with the controlling agency, but good-faith reliance on an ATC clearance is a mitigating factor, not a complete defense, in any enforcement action. Warning and alert areas may be entered on IFR clearance without specific authorization; restricted and prohibited areas require confirmation of inactive status or explicit authorization.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.S5.005	On a GPS RNAV system, entering a direct-to clearance causes the unit to compute and display the great-circle course to the selected waypoint immediately. Unlike VOR intercepts where you manually compute a heading, the GPS continuously provides cross-track error and desired track so the CDI deflection represents linear deviation rather than angular deviation. To execute the intercept in a timely manner: (1) immediately enter the direct-to in the GPS, (2) turn to the new course (or use a modest intercept angle if needed), (3) monitor cross-track error to confirm closure on the new track, and (4) confirm the new track and distance to the next required crossing fix, ensuring adequate time to descend or comply with altitude restrictions. Because GPS RNAV CDI sensitivity is typically ±2 NM in en route mode and transitions to ±1 NM in terminal mode, situational awareness of mode changes is critical.	->	On a GPS RNAV system, entering a direct-to clearance causes the unit to compute and display the great-circle course to the selected waypoint immediately. Unlike VOR intercepts where you manually compute a heading, the GPS continuously provides cross-track error and desired track so the CDI deflection represents linear deviation rather than angular deviation. To execute the intercept in a timely manner: (1) immediately enter the direct-to in the GPS, (2) turn to the new course (or use a modest intercept angle if needed), (3) monitor cross-track error to confirm closure on the new track, and (4) confirm the new track and distance to the next required crossing fix, ensuring adequate time to descend or comply with altitude restrictions. GPS RNAV CDI sensitivity transitions automatically based on proximity to waypoint: en route mode typically displays ±5 NM scale (±1 NM full-scale deflection), terminal mode displays ±1 NM scale (±0.3 NM full-scale deflection), and approach mode displays ±0.3 NM scale, so situational awareness of mode changes is critical.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.S6.002	An altitude shown in a box on a procedure chart indicates a mandatory crossing altitude — you must cross that fix at exactly that altitude. An altitude shown in plain text without a box (or with an underline or overline) indicates a minimum or maximum altitude constraint respectively. A plain underlined altitude means 'at or above'; a plain overlined altitude means 'at or below.' Complying with charted procedures means correctly interpreting and meeting each constraint as depicted.	->	A boxed altitude indicates a mandatory crossing altitude—you must cross that fix at exactly that altitude. Altitude restrictions are shown with arrow symbols or specific notations per the FAA Aeronautical Chart User's Guide: an upward-pointing arrow or indicator means minimum altitude (at or above); a downward-pointing arrow or indicator means maximum altitude (at or below). Complying with charted procedures means correctly interpreting and meeting each constraint as depicted on the specific procedure type.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.S9.002	FIS-B NEXRAD imagery has a latency of approximately 5 to 15 minutes, meaning the displayed radar picture is not real-time. A cell that appears to be in one location on the MFD may have moved, intensified, or dissipated by the time the pilot views it. Pilots must treat datalink weather as a strategic planning tool and must not use it for tactical penetration of convective areas or for precise weather avoidance maneuvering.	->	FIS-B NEXRAD imagery has a latency of approximately 5 to 20+ minutes, meaning the displayed radar picture is not real-time. A cell that appears to be in one location on the MFD may have moved, intensified, or dissipated by the time the pilot views it. Pilots must treat datalink weather as a strategic planning tool and must not use it for tactical penetration of convective areas or for precise weather avoidance maneuvering.
AI_EDIT	IH.VI.A.S10.002	Approximately 80% of aviation accidents are caused by pilot error or human factors. This statistic means that the greatest threat to safety on any IFR flight is the pilot's own decision-making, perception, and workload management — not mechanical failure or weather alone. SRM directly addresses this by providing structured techniques to recognize and interrupt the 'error chain' before an accident occurs. During departure, en route, and arrival phases, applying SRM keeps the pilot from becoming fixated, task-saturated, or complacent.	->	Pilot error or human factors are involved as a causal or contributing element in the majority of aviation accidents—estimates range from 70–80% depending on the accident category and data source. This statistic demonstrates that the greatest threat to safety on any IFR flight is the pilot's own decision-making, perception, and workload management — not mechanical failure or weather alone. SRM directly addresses this by providing structured techniques to recognize and interrupt the 'error chain' before an accident occurs. During departure, en route, and arrival phases, applying SRM keeps the pilot from becoming fixated, task-saturated, or complacent.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.A.K1.003	LNAV and LP are both non-precision approaches because they use an MDA and provide lateral guidance only — no approved electronic vertical guidance is provided to the pilot. LNAV/VNAV is considered an approach with vertical guidance (APV) because it provides advisory vertical guidance via WAAS or Baro-VNAV, but it still uses an MDA rather than a DA in some configurations. The key distinction for non-precision is the absence of a certified electronic glideslope and the use of MDA rather than DA.	->	LNAV and LP are both non-precision approaches because they provide lateral guidance only with an MDA and no certified electronic vertical guidance. LNAV/VNAV is classified as an Approach with Vertical Guidance (APV) and uses a DA when vertical guidance is active and available. If LNAV/VNAV vertical guidance becomes unavailable during flight, the approach reverts to LNAV minimums with an MDA. The key distinction for non-precision is the reliance on MDA rather than DA under normal operations.
AUTO_APPROVE	IH.VII.A.K2.007	The answer is detailed, accurate, and correctly distinguishes LNAV, LNAV+V, LP, and LPV modes; it properly classifies them relative to FAA regulations and correctly addresses the regulatory and operational implications at the FAF.
AUTO_APPROVE	IH.VII.A.K2.008	The answer correctly states RAIM failure after FAF requires immediate missed approach per AIM 1-1-18 and 14 CFR 91.175, accurately identifies helicopter-specific risk factors (confined aerodromes, mountainous terrain, no circling alternatives), and the follow-up correctly clarifies WAAS vs. RAIM distinction and RAIM prediction requirements; verifier's concerns are addressed in the follow-up material and regulatory basis is sound.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.A.K5.002	'Proceed VFR' means that after the MAP, the pilot must cancel IFR and proceed to the landing site under VFR conditions. The visual segment between the MAP and the landing site is NOT obstacle-protected by the instrument procedure, so the pilot assumes responsibility for terrain and obstacle clearance. 'Fly Visual' also indicates a visual segment after the MAP, but the procedure has been designed with some level of obstacle evaluation for that segment — however, it still requires visual conditions and pilot judgment. In both cases, the helicopter must have the landing environment in sight before leaving the MAP, and the visual segment must be flown clear of clouds with adequate visibility. 'Proceed VFR' imposes a stricter requirement because no obstacle protection exists for that segment.	->	'Proceed VFR' and 'Fly Visual' both indicate visual flight segments beyond the MAP with minimal or no standard instrument obstacle protection under TERPS. Both require the pilot to maintain visual conditions and assume primary responsibility for terrain and obstacle avoidance. The distinction between the two terms reflects design and procedural labeling rather than substantively different protection levels. In both cases, the helicopter must have the landing environment in sight and must be able to continue VFR to landing.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.A.R5.005	A localizer CDI is approximately 4–5 times more sensitive than a VOR CDI — a full deflection on a localizer represents only about 2.5° off course, versus approximately 12° for a VOR. This means that small lateral deviations produce large needle movements on a localizer. For helicopter pilots, this creates a risk of over-controlling laterally while simultaneously managing the vertical profile on a non-precision approach. If a pilot is chasing the localizer needle with large cyclic inputs, this degrades vertical profile management, potentially leading to an excessive descent rate or altitude bust. The risk is compounded by the fact that the localizer provides no vertical guidance — the pilot must manage descent independently. Smooth, anticipatory control inputs and a pre-briefed, stabilized approach profile are critical to avoiding an unstabilized condition on a LOC approach.	->	A localizer CDI is typically 5 times more sensitive than a VOR CDI. A full-scale deflection on a localizer represents approximately ±2.5° off centerline, compared to approximately ±10–12° for a VOR. This heightened sensitivity on a non-precision LOC approach creates significant over-control risk in helicopters: small lateral deviations produce large needle deflections, tempting pilots to make aggressive cyclic inputs that degrade vertical profile management. Since the localizer provides no vertical guidance, the pilot must independently manage descent rate while maintaining lateral precision. This divided attention during approach can lead to excessive descent rates or altitude deviations. Smooth, anticipatory control inputs and a pre-briefed, stabilized descent profile are essential to avoid approach instability.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.A.R7.005	Drifting even slightly below the MDA — even with approach lights in sight — is potentially illegal and dangerous. Under 14 CFR 91.175(c)(1), seeing only the approach light system (ALS) permits descent to 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation (TDZE), but not lower unless additional runway environment elements are acquired. The risk is loss of obstacle clearance, as the MDA is set to provide a specific buffer above the highest obstacle in the final approach area. In a helicopter, which may be maneuvering at slower airspeeds with greater attitude changes, precise altitude control at MDA is critical. The pilot should proactively use vertical speed and power management to hold the exact MDA altitude and not permit creep-down during maneuvering.	->	Drifting even slightly below the MDA — even with approach lights in sight — is potentially illegal and dangerous. Under 14 CFR 91.175(c)(3)(i), the approach light system is ONE of several acceptable visual references that may be used to continue descent, but only if flight visibility meets or exceeds the prescribed minimum for the approach AND the pilot is in a position from which a normal descent to landing on the runway can be made. Seeing approach lights alone does not authorize descent below MDA unless additional required visual references (such as the runway threshold, touchdown zone, or runway markings) are also acquired. The risk is loss of obstacle clearance, as the MDA is set to provide a specific buffer above the highest obstacle in the final approach area. In a helicopter, which may be maneuvering at slower airspeeds with greater attitude changes, precise altitude control at MDA is critical. The pilot should proactively use vertical speed and power management to hold the exact MDA altitude and not permit creep-down during maneuvering. If required visual references are not clearly identified, a missed approach is mandatory.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.A.S1.007	Under 14 CFR 91.175(c), approach lights alone (without seeing at least the red terminating bars or red side row bars of the ALSF) allow descent to 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation, not all the way to the surface. Seeing only approach lights does not authorize descent to touchdown. For a helicopter, the published HAA (height above airport) or HAT (height above touchdown) applies to the helipad or runway threshold. A key helicopter advantage is that unlike a fixed-wing aircraft requiring forward momentum to land, a helicopter can legally slow to a hover over a helipad at MDA if visual contact is maintained and the approach is conducted under Part 91 in accordance with the published minimums. However, the descent below MDA is still only authorized when the required visual references are clearly identified. If visibility is fluctuating at minimums, the safe and legal choice is a missed approach — the word 'probably' should never drive the decision to continue.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.175(c)(3)(i), the approach light system (ALSF, SSALR, MALSR, ODALS, or REIL) is ONE of several acceptable visual references that may authorize continued descent below MDA. However, approach lights alone do NOT authorize descent to touchdown — you must acquire at least one required visual reference (such as the runway threshold, touchdown zone, runway markings, or specific approach light features like ALSF-2 terminating or side row bars). Descent is only authorized if flight visibility equals or exceeds the published minimum for the approach AND you are in a position from which a normal descent to landing can be made. For a helicopter conducting an approach to a helipad, published minimums are expressed as HAA (height above airport) or HAT (height above touchdown) and apply to that specific landing area. A helicopter's advantage over fixed-wing is operational flexibility — slower speeds, steeper approach angles, and the ability to hover for positioning — but NOT relaxed descent minimums. You cannot legally remain below MDA or continue descent without the required visual references regardless of aircraft type. At minimums visibility with only approach lights visible and no runway in sight, the safe and legally correct action is a missed approach.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.A.S3.005	First, verify the approach is loaded correctly in the FMS/GPS by cross-checking the approach name, airport, runway, and waypoints against the paper or electronic chart. Confirm RAIM or WAAS integrity is available — for WAAS-equipped units, look for the WAAS status annunciation and check that approach mode will activate at the FAF. Confirm there are no active NOTAMs disabling GPS or WAAS in the area. Verify the GPS database is current (updated within the last 28 days). As you approach the FAF, confirm the navigator transitions from terminal to approach sensitivity (CDI narrows to ±0.3 NM for LNAV). If WAAS is providing vertical guidance (LNAV+V or LPV), confirm the appropriate minimums are being used. The unit should annunciate 'APPROACH' or 'LPV/LNAV' active before the FAF.	->	First, verify the approach is loaded correctly in the FMS/GPS by cross-checking the approach name, airport, runway, and waypoints against the paper or electronic chart. Confirm the GPS database is current — the standard requirement is 28 days for IFR approach operations; verify currency per your specific aircraft/FMS manufacturer manual, as some systems may have additional requirements. Check for any active NOTAMs disabling GPS or WAAS in the area. Confirm WAAS or RAIM integrity is available — for WAAS-equipped units, verify the WAAS status annunciation (typically 'WAAS OK' or equivalent) is displayed on the navigator BEFORE beginning the approach, not just at the FAF. Verify the appropriate approach minimums (LNAV, LNAV+V, or LPV) are loaded and match the published approach. As you approach the FAF, confirm the navigator transitions to approach sensitivity (CDI narrows to ±0.3 NM for LNAV). If WAAS is providing vertical guidance (LNAV+V or LPV), the unit should annunciate 'APPROACH' or the specific approach mode active. Do not proceed with the approach if WAAS integrity is unavailable, the database is not current, or the loaded minimums do not match the published procedure.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.A.S13.004	Seeing only the approach lights does not authorize descent below 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation (TDZE) unless you also see the red terminating bars or the red side row bars — per 14 CFR 91.175(c)(1). If you can see those red ALS components in addition to the forward ALS, you may continue to 100 feet above TDZE, but you still cannot continue to landing without additional runway environment references (threshold, markings, touchdown zone, etc.). At night, this assessment is more difficult. If the red ALS bars are visible and estimated flight visibility meets the published minimum, you may descend to 100 feet AGL above TDZE; otherwise, execute the missed approach. Also critical: your position must allow a normal rate of descent to landing — as a helicopter pilot you have more flexibility here, but you cannot deviate from stabilized approach criteria.	->	Seeing the approach lights allows you to descend below MDA if flight visibility meets the published minimum (1 mile in this case). However, to descend BELOW 100 feet AGL, you must visually identify and have distinctly visible at least one of the specific runway environment references listed in 14 CFR 91.175(c)(1): the runway threshold, threshold markings, threshold lights, REIL, VASI, touchdown zone, touchdown zone lights, runway, runway markings, or runway lights. The approach lights themselves—including red terminating bars or red side row bars—are NOT runway environment references per 91.175(c)(1)(ii) and do not authorize descent below 100 feet AGL. At night with low visibility, this assessment is difficult. If only approach lights are visible with no runway threshold or other specific references in sight, you must execute a missed approach. If you do acquire one of the required references and conditions permit a normal rate of descent to landing, you may continue. Critical: maintain stabilized approach criteria and be prepared to go missed if additional runway environment references do not appear as you descend.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.B.K1.007	The main answer and follow-up contain contradictory and imprecise statements about red terminating/side row bars; they are part of the ALS but are not runway environment references per 91.175(c)(3)(ii), yet the answer suggests they have independent descent authority, which is incorrect and unsafe.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.B.K3.008	The answer conflates VOR and ILS localizer interference but does not clearly establish the mechanism; VOR fundamentals operate 108–117.95 MHz and ILS LOC 108–111.95 MHz (overlap only 108–111.95), so direct harmonic interference from a VOR is technically possible but rare and the answer oversimplifies the physics. The follow-up's 'back lobe' explanation is correct but requires clarification that back-course signals are only authorized on published back-course approaches, not standard ILS.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.K4.004	This is an energy-high, off-course situation. The priority is to re-intercept the glideslope from below (since you are below it — one dot low means you are below the glideslope), correct the localizer deviation, and bleed off excess airspeed in a coordinated way. Reduce collective slightly to arrest the descent rate and allow the glideslope needle to center from below, simultaneously apply left cyclic to correct the localizer deviation. The excess airspeed (high kinetic energy) can be used carefully — it provides a buffer, but it must be reduced to target speed by the gate altitude. Avoid the trap of pitching up aggressively to lose altitude, which could cause rotor RPM fluctuation. Use small, deliberate control inputs. If by 500 feet AGL the approach is not stabilized on glideslope, localizer, and at target airspeed, execute a missed approach — do not attempt to salvage a significantly unstabilized approach below minimums.	->	This is an energy-high, above-glideslope situation. The priority is to re-intercept the glideslope from above (since the one-dot-low needle indicates you are above the glideslope), correct the localizer deviation, and bleed off excess airspeed in a coordinated way. Reduce collective slightly to increase descent rate and allow the glideslope needle to center from above, simultaneously apply left cyclic to correct the localizer deviation. The excess airspeed (high kinetic energy) can be used carefully — it provides a buffer, but it must be reduced to target speed by the gate altitude. Avoid the trap of pitching up aggressively, which could cause rotor RPM fluctuation. Use small, deliberate control inputs. If by 500 feet AGL the approach is not stabilized on glideslope, localizer, and at target airspeed, execute a missed approach — do not attempt to salvage a significantly unstabilized approach below minimums.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.R3.006	One dot high on the glideslope means the aircraft is above the published glidepath but still within normal operational tolerance for the ILS glideslope. However, being consistently above glideslope during a precision approach means the aircraft will be higher than normal at DA, requiring more aggressive descent to land, and could result in a long landing or go-around. The risk management process: (1) Verify the glideslope indicator is functioning by cross-checking the raw needle against any FMS vertical guidance. (2) Determine whether the deviation is stable, worsening, or trending toward correction. (3) With sufficient altitude remaining, consider manually overriding or adjusting autopilot pitch mode if permitted by the aircraft's procedures, or disconnecting and hand-flying. (4) If the deviation does not correct before DA, execute the missed approach rather than attempting a salvage landing from an unstabilized position. Continuing an unstabilized approach is a leading cause of CFIT accidents.	->	A one-dot-low glideslope needle indicates the aircraft is above the published glideslope. Being consistently above glideslope during a precision approach means the aircraft will be higher than normal at DA, requiring more aggressive descent to land, and could result in a long landing or go-around. The risk management process: (1) Verify the glideslope indicator is functioning by cross-checking the raw needle against any FMS vertical guidance. (2) Determine whether the deviation is stable, worsening, or trending toward correction. (3) With sufficient altitude remaining, consider manually overriding or adjusting autopilot pitch mode if permitted by the aircraft's procedures, or disconnecting and hand-flying. (4) If the deviation does not correct before DA, execute the missed approach rather than attempting a salvage landing from an unstabilized position. Continuing an unstabilized approach is a leading cause of CFIT accidents.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.B.R3.008	The scenario assumes the FMS will fly a baro-VNAV computed path instead of the ILS glideslope signal on a coupled ILS approach, but in most modern IFR-certified helicopters with approved autopilots, the system captures the ILS signal directly when available. The scenario requires clarification of whether this represents a real operational limitation or is a teaching construct, and the answer conflates mode confusion with baro-VNAV limitations without distinguishing between normal ILS operation and actual failure modes.
AUTO_APPROVE	IH.VII.B.R5.008	The main answer correctly applies hours-in-type risk data, automation bias, and ADM principles; the follow-up answer correctly states BasicMed authorizes turbine aircraft up to 12,500 lbs MTOW with no turbine restriction, which aligns with current 14 CFR Part 68. The verifier's flagged 'concerns' are actually confirmations of correctness, not errors.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.R6.008	An alternator failure during an ILS approach in deteriorating weather creates compounding risks: battery-only power has a finite duration (typically 30 minutes or less depending on aircraft and electrical load), avionics may begin to fail sequentially, and the distraction of managing the electrical failure increases cockpit workload at the most critical phase of flight. Priority order: (1) Fly the aircraft — maintain glideslope and localizer tracking; an unstabilized approach in IMC with a distraction kills pilots. (2) Reduce electrical load immediately — shed all non-essential equipment to extend battery life for the ILS, communications, and transponder. (3) Communicate — advise ATC of the electrical failure so they can provide priority handling and have emergency equipment standing by. (4) Decide — if the approach can be completed before battery depletion and weather permits, continue with commitment to go missed at DA/DH if visual is not acquired. If battery life is uncertain, executing the missed approach now and declaring an emergency may be the lower-risk option. The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook emphasizes that the word 'probably' should be avoided in decision-making — if battery life will 'probably' last, that is not a safe standard. Choose the option known to work.	->	With the loss of the primary EFIS/PFD, the pilot transitions to the backup vacuum AI and raw data instruments. This significantly increases workload because EFIS displays integrate attitude, altitude, airspeed, and navigation on one screen — the pilot must now scan multiple individual instruments. Per 14 CFR 61.57(c), the pilot must have recent ILS experience, but there is no separate or specific 'partial panel currency' event mandated by regulation. However, the pilot should be proficient at flying partial panel through training and recurrent practice. Regarding equipment: 14 CFR 91.205(d) requires that all instruments required for IFR flight be operative at the beginning of IFR flight. Once airborne, 14 CFR 91.3(b) permits the pilot to deviate from any rule to the extent necessary to meet an emergency — so the pilot may legally continue the approach using the backup instruments. Practically, the pilot should declare an emergency and fly the approach carefully using the backup instruments, accepting higher workload and potential risk while prioritizing aircraft control above all else.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S1.008	With a completely blocked static system: (1) The altimeter freezes at the altitude it was reading when the blockage occurred — it will not update as you descend, so you will not know your true altitude; (2) the VSI will drop to zero and remain there regardless of actual climb or descent — it is unreliable; (3) the ASI will become inaccurate — as you descend and static pressure should increase, the trapped static pressure will cause the ASI to over-read (indicating faster than actual). The pitot tube is unaffected, so dynamic pressure input to the ASI is still accurate; the error is on the static side. Management: If the aircraft has an alternate static source, select it immediately. The alternate static source (typically inside the cabin, which is slightly lower pressure than outside) will produce a slightly higher altitude indication, a slightly faster airspeed indication, and a momentary climb indication on the VSI due to the lower cabin pressure — these errors are small and charted in the POH/AFM. If no alternate static source is available, you may break the VSI glass as a last resort to vent the system to cockpit air (check POH authorization first). With the altimeter frozen, you must immediately inform ATC, request vectors or radar altimetry assistance, cross-reference GPS altitude if available, and seriously consider executing a missed approach — continuing an ILS to DA with an unserviceable altimeter is extremely hazardous.	->	With a completely blocked static system: (1) The altimeter freezes at the altitude it was reading when the blockage occurred — it will not update as you descend, so you will not know your true altitude; (2) the VSI will drop to zero and remain there regardless of actual climb or descent — it is unreliable; (3) the ASI will become inaccurate — as you descend, the actual outside static pressure increases, but the trapped static pressure remains constant. This causes the ASI to under-read (indicate slower than actual) because the static pressure input is artificially high relative to the dynamic pressure. Management: If the aircraft has an alternate static source, select it immediately. The alternate static source (typically inside the cabin, which is slightly lower pressure than outside) will produce a slightly higher altitude indication, a slightly faster airspeed indication, and a momentary climb indication on the VSI due to the lower cabin pressure — these errors are small and charted in the POH/AFM. If no alternate static source is available, you may break the VSI glass as a last resort to vent the system to cockpit air (check POH authorization first). With the altimeter frozen, you must immediately inform ATC, request vectors or radar altimetry assistance, cross-reference GPS altitude if available, and seriously consider executing a missed approach — continuing an ILS to DA with an unserviceable altimeter is extremely hazardous.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S2.004	Make one or two additional attempts on the tower frequency, spacing them several seconds apart. If still no response, attempt to raise the previous TRACON frequency to advise them of the communication difficulty. You may continue the approach — an unanswered call does not require you to abandon an IFR approach already in progress. However, you must not land or enter the movement area without tower clearance (at a controlled airport). If you reach the missed approach point with no contact established, execute the published missed approach and continue attempting to reach ATC. Squawk 7600 (radio failure) if all frequencies fail. Document the attempt and time of lost contact.	->	Make one additional attempt on the tower frequency, spacing it several seconds apart to avoid congesting the frequency further. If still no response, contact TRACON on the departure frequency to advise them of the communication difficulty and request relay or further assistance. Per 14 CFR 91.185, you may continue an IFR approach already in progress without two-way radio contact, but you must not enter the runway environment or attempt to land without tower clearance at a controlled airport. Continue to the missed approach point and attempt contact throughout. Execute the published missed approach if tower contact is not established by the MAP. If all ATC frequencies fail, squawk 7600 (radio failure) and proceed to an alternate or suitable landing area per your filed alternate or ATC last clearance. Document the time of lost contact and all attempts made.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.B.S4.004	The distinction between a published ILS side-step (charted procedure with specific minimums) and an ad hoc lateral deviation after FAF is not clearly addressed; the regulatory implications differ significantly and require clarification to avoid pilot misunderstanding.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S5.004	A blocked pitot tube with a blocked drain hole causes the ASI to act like an altimeter — as you descend, static pressure increases relative to the trapped pitot pressure, causing the ASI to show a decreasing reading, or if climbing, an increasing reading. If the ASI is rising while descending on final, this is consistent with both holes being blocked — the trapped pressure makes the ASI respond inversely. Immediate actions: activate pitot heat if available and not already failed, cross-check attitude indicator and VSI to verify actual flight path, do not chase the erroneous ASI, fly attitude and power, advise ATC of the malfunction, and consider executing a missed approach to troubleshoot in a controlled environment rather than continuing to a precision approach minimums environment with an unreliable airspeed indication.	->	A blocked pitot tube with a blocked drain hole traps static pressure inside the pitot line. As you descend, ambient static pressure increases, but the trapped pitot pressure remains constant, making the differential pressure reading *decrease* relative to what the static system sees. This causes the ASI to read *opposite* to your actual altitude change: it rises when you descend and falls when you climb. Immediate actions: (1) do not chase the erroneous ASI; (2) cross-check attitude indicator, VSI, and altimeter to verify your actual flight path; (3) if pitot heat is not already activated, confirm its status (though it may not recover a fully blocked tube); (4) advise ATC of the unreliable airspeed indication; (5) do not continue to precision approach minimums with an unreliable airspeed—execute a missed approach to troubleshoot in controlled airspace. Fly the aircraft by attitude and power reference alone.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S5.008	A consistent 200-foot high altimeter error compared to ATC Mode C readout could result from: (1) an incorrect altimeter setting (Kollsman window) — each 0.10 Hg error equals approximately 100 feet, so a 0.20 Hg error would produce this discrepancy; (2) a faulty encoding altimeter sending incorrect Mode C data to ATC while your primary altimeter is actually correct; (3) an actual altimeter instrument error. First, verify your altimeter setting is the current local altimeter received from ATIS or ATC. Cross-check with the tower-reported field elevation when over the threshold or with GPS altitude as a sanity check. If the altimeter setting is correct and the discrepancy is real, you may be 200 feet lower than indicated when the altimeter reads DH — meaning you could break out of clouds 200 feet lower than expected or descend below DH unknowingly. This is a serious safety-of-flight concern. The correct action is to add 200 feet to your published DH and treat that as your effective DH, or execute a missed approach and resolve the discrepancy before attempting another approach. Notify ATC of the potential altimeter error.	->	A consistent 200-foot high altimeter error compared to ATC Mode C readout could result from: (1) an incorrect altimeter setting (Kollsman window) — each 0.10 Hg error equals approximately 100 feet, so a 0.20 Hg error would produce this discrepancy; (2) a faulty encoding altimeter sending incorrect Mode C data to ATC while your primary altimeter is actually correct; (3) an actual altimeter instrument error. First, verify your altimeter setting is the current local altimeter received from ATIS or ATC. Cross-check with the tower-reported field elevation when over the threshold or with GPS altitude as a sanity check. If the altimeter setting is correct and the discrepancy is real, you may be 200 feet lower than indicated when the altimeter reads DH — meaning you could break out of clouds 200 feet lower than expected or descend below DH unknowingly. This is a serious safety-of-flight concern. The correct action is to add 200 feet to your published DH and treat that as your effective DH, or execute a missed approach and resolve the discrepancy before attempting another approach. Notify ATC of the potential altimeter error. [Follow-up corrected:] The encoding altimeter and primary altimeter are separate systems. The encoder converts pressure altitude to a digital code sent to the transponder; it does not affect the primary altimeter display. The primary altimeter reads from its own aneroid capsule and is independent of the encoder. If the encoder is faulty and sending incorrect Mode C data to ATC, your primary altimeter remains unaffected and accurate—meaning your actual altitude matches your primary altimeter reading. Per 14 CFR 91.217, Mode C altitude information must be within 125 feet of the aircraft's actual pressure altitude. If the encoder is out of tolerance, it must be corrected before the aircraft is operated in Mode C-required airspace. This interpretation changes the risk calculus: if your primary altimeter is correct and ATC's readout is wrong, you are flying safely on your primary reference.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S8.007	Adding airspeed above the published approach speed in turbulence and suspected wind shear increases the energy available for the pilot to counter sudden airspeed losses without the aircraft departing from the approach path or entering a dangerously low-energy state. In wind shear, a sudden headwind loss translates directly to an airspeed loss; if you are already at minimum approach speed, that shear could push the helicopter below ETL (effective translational lift) or into a region where power demand exceeds available power. The practical adjustment is typically half the gust spread or a value specified in the RFM or operator procedures — commonly 5–10 knots. The upper limit is constrained by several factors: (1) The ability to still achieve the required descent rate on the glideslope — too fast and you cannot descend steeply enough without exceeding VNE or losing glideslope. (2) The ability to safely transition to hover or landing at the helipad — arriving too fast over a confined or mountainous helipad reduces the pilot's ability to decelerate safely. (3) Any RFM airspeed limitation for the approach configuration. Exceeding your upper airspeed limit in mountainous terrain creates a different risk — inability to stop — that may be worse than the wind shear risk.	->	Adding airspeed above the published approach speed in turbulence and suspected wind shear increases the energy available for the pilot to counter sudden airspeed losses without the aircraft departing from the approach path or entering a dangerously low-energy state. In wind shear, a sudden headwind loss translates directly to an airspeed loss; if you are already at minimum approach speed, that shear could push the helicopter below ETL (effective translational lift) or into a region where power demand exceeds available power. The practical adjustment is typically half the gust spread or a value specified in the RFM or operator procedures — commonly 5–10 knots. The upper limit is constrained by several factors: (1) The ability to still achieve the required descent rate on the glideslope — too fast and you cannot descend steeply enough without exceeding VNE or losing glideslope. (2) The ability to safely transition to hover or landing at the helipad — arriving too fast over a confined or mountainous helipad reduces the pilot's ability to decelerate safely. (3) Any RFM airspeed limitation for the approach configuration. Exceeding your upper airspeed limit in mountainous terrain creates a different risk — inability to stop — that may be worse than the wind shear risk. [Follow-up corrected:] A significantly increased descent rate on a helicopter approach creates the risk of entering vortex ring state (VRS), also called power settling. VRS occurs when the helicopter descends into its own rotor downwash — conditions favorable for VRS include low forward airspeed (typically below approximately 15–20 knots groundspeed), high power settings, and descent rates exceeding approximately 300 feet per minute. On a steep glideslope with added airspeed, the pilot may find themselves increasing collective to maintain the glideslope, which increases rotor-induced downwash, while simultaneously maintaining a high descent rate. If airspeed decelerates near the threshold (as it must for landing), the combination of high descent rate, high power, and low airspeed is precisely the entry condition for VRS. The pilot must be aware that increasing approach airspeed to counter wind shear is a tradeoff — it must be managed so that the deceleration and transition to landing does not create a VRS entry scenario, particularly on a helipad approach where there is limited space and altitude to recover.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S8.008	A pitot tube blockage with an open drain hole causes the ASI to slowly drop to zero — the drain hole equalizes the pitot chamber to ambient static, causing the indicated airspeed to decay regardless of actual speed. This is a critical failure during an ILS approach in IMC because airspeed is a primary reference for configuration and power management. My response: (1) Immediately recognize the failure — ASI decaying while power and attitude are constant is a clear symptom. (2) Transition to attitude-power reference flying — I know what pitch attitude and power setting produces my target approach airspeed in this helicopter; I must now fly those references and cross-check with VSI and GPS groundspeed if available. (3) Advise ATC of the equipment failure. (4) At 800 feet AGL in IMC, I am well below a safe maneuvering altitude for extended troubleshooting; my primary decision is whether to continue or go missed. If I am stable in attitude/power and have a reliable groundspeed reference (GPS), continuing to an approach-capable airport with a go-around option is reasonable. If I have any doubt about my airspeed reference and am near minimums, the safest action is execute the missed approach, climb to a safe altitude, and troubleshoot or divert to VMC. (5) Do not attempt to use alternate static source for a pitot failure — alternate static affects static instruments (altimeter, VSI), not the pitot side of the ASI.	->	A pitot tube blockage with an open drain hole causes the ASI to slowly drop to zero — the drain hole equalizes the pitot chamber to ambient static, causing the indicated airspeed to decay regardless of actual speed. This is a critical failure during an ILS approach in IMC because airspeed is a primary reference for configuration and power management. My response: (1) Immediately recognize the failure — ASI decaying while power and attitude are constant is a clear symptom. (2) Transition to attitude-power reference flying — I know what pitch attitude and power setting produces my target approach airspeed in this helicopter; I must now fly those references and cross-check with VSI and GPS groundspeed if available. (3) Advise ATC of the equipment failure. (4) At 800 feet AGL in IMC, I am well below a safe maneuvering altitude for extended troubleshooting; my primary decision is whether to continue or go missed. If I am stable in attitude/power and have a reliable groundspeed reference (GPS), continuing to an approach-capable airport with a go-around option is reasonable. If I have any doubt about my airspeed reference and am near minimums, the safest action is execute the missed approach, climb to a safe altitude, and troubleshoot or divert to VMC. (5) Alternate static source does not remedy a pitot blockage and should not be relied upon for the ASI. However, verify that alternate static source is available and functioning for the static instruments (altimeter, VSI), as this affects your overall instrument reliability during the approach.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S10.005	Each inoperative visual aid is listed separately in the inoperative components table found in the front of the Terminal Procedures Publication. The procedure is: (1) Locate the approach in question and identify its base visibility minimum — for example, 1/2 SM. (2) Look up MALSR in the inoperative components table. Depending on the approach category and whether an operative ALSF or other lighting exists, the table will specify an increase, such as adding 1/4 SM. (3) Look up REIL separately; the table may specify an additional increase or note that it does not change minimums if other lighting is adequate. (4) Apply each adjustment additively — do not simply use the higher of the two, but add them together if the table so directs. (5) Compare your final adjusted visibility against the weather report to determine if the approach is legal. As a helicopter pilot, also confirm whether helicopter-specific minimums are listed on the chart, as they may differ from fixed-wing Category I minimums.	->	Each inoperative visual aid is listed separately in the inoperative components table found in the front of the Terminal Procedures Publication. The procedure is: (1) Locate the approach in question and identify its base visibility minimum — for example, 1/2 SM. (2) Look up MALSR in the inoperative components table. The table will specify the adjusted minimum for that specific component based on the approach category and available alternate lighting. (3) Look up REIL separately in the same table; determine the adjustment specified for REIL inoperability on this approach. (4) Apply adjustments exactly as the inoperative components table directs. The table may list cumulative adjustments (additive) for multiple outages, or it may provide a single adjusted minimum that accounts for all listed inoperabilities. Do not assume adjustments stack additively; follow the published table entry precisely for the specific combination of inoperative components. (5) Compare your final adjusted visibility against the weather report to determine if the approach is legal. As a helicopter pilot, also confirm whether helicopter-specific minimums are listed on the chart, as they may differ from fixed-wing minimums.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S10.008	This requires a methodical three-part analysis. First, the inoperative VASI: consult the inoperative components table. VASI inoperability typically requires a visibility increase of 1/4 SM for straight-in approaches where no other vertical visual guidance exists. Apply that adjustment — visibility minimum increases from 1/4 SM to 1/2 SM. Second, the GPS RAIM alert: if the approach requires GPS and the navigator is displaying reduced RAIM or a RAIM alert, you must evaluate whether GPS integrity is sufficient for the approach. Under TSO-C129 or TSO-C146 requirements and AIM 5-1-16, if RAIM is not available or cannot be predicted to be available during the approach, the GPS may not be used as the primary navigation source for that approach. If GPS is listed as required equipment on the approach chart and it cannot provide integrity assurance, the approach cannot be conducted to those minimums — or at all if no alternative navigation source exists. Third, evaluate cumulatively: even if the RAIM issue resolved, you would still need at least 1/2 SM visibility due to the inoperative VASI. If current weather is below 1/2 SM or if RAIM cannot be confirmed adequate, the approach is not legal. The decision must be made before commencing the approach, not at the FAF.	->	This requires a methodical three-part analysis. First, the inoperative VASI: consult the inoperative components table for this specific approach. VASI inoperability adjustment varies depending on other available visual guidance (such as ALSF, MALSR, or REIL) and the approach type. Look up VASI in the table and apply the exact adjustment specified for this approach — do not assume a standard increase. Second, the GPS RAIM alert: if the approach requires GPS and the navigator is displaying a RAIM alert or reduced satellite geometry, you must evaluate whether GPS integrity is sufficient for the approach. Under TSO-C129 or TSO-C146 requirements and AIM 5-1-16, if RAIM is not available or cannot be predicted to be available during the approach, GPS may not be used as the primary navigation source for that approach. If GPS is listed as required equipment on the approach chart and it cannot provide integrity assurance, you must either use an alternate means of navigation to conduct the approach to the same minimums, or the approach cannot be conducted at all. Third, evaluate cumulatively: the VASI adjustment applies regardless of GPS status. If current weather is below the adjusted visibility minimum or if RAIM cannot be confirmed adequate and no alternate navigation exists, the approach is not legal. The decision must be made before commencing the approach, not at the FAF.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S11.004	A rising glideslope needle (needle moving up) means the aircraft is below the glidepath. In this case, if the needle is rising after glideslope intercept, it likely means your actual descent rate of 300 FPM is slightly too aggressive for the actual conditions, or you intercepted the glideslope from slightly below. You should make a small power increase to reduce the descent rate temporarily, allow the needle to center, then re-establish a descent rate that matches the glidepath — which at 60 knots groundspeed should return near 300 FPM. The predetermined rate is a starting reference, not a rigid setting; small, smooth adjustments are expected to keep the needle centered without overcorrecting.	->	A rising (upward) glideslope needle means the aircraft is *above* the glidepath and you must descend back to it. A falling (downward) needle means the aircraft is below the glidepath and you must climb or reduce descent rate to return to it. In this scenario, if the needle is rising (moving up), the aircraft is above the glidepath. You should reduce power or reduce the descent rate to move back down to the glidepath. Once the needle centers, re-establish the predetermined descent rate of approximately 300 FPM (based on your 60 knots groundspeed), making small, smooth power adjustments as needed to keep the needle centered. The predetermined rate is a starting reference; small adjustments are expected throughout the approach to track the glidepath accurately.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S11.008	At DA with no visual contact with the required visual references specified in 14 CFR 91.175, the pilot must immediately execute the published missed approach procedure — no further descent is permitted. Regarding energy state: the predetermined descent rate of 300 FPM means the helicopter arrives at DA with a significant sink rate already established. If the pilot delays the go-around even briefly below DA, that 300 FPM descent will continue to erode terrain clearance at a rate proportional to the delay. In a helicopter, transitioning from 300 FPM descent to a climb requires immediate collective application to arrest the sink rate first, then add power above hover power to establish a positive climb — this is a two-step energy demand that must be anticipated. A pilot who has been passively following the glideslope without mentally preparing for a go-around is at higher risk of delayed reaction at DA. Proper ADM during the approach includes a specific mental commitment to the go-around decision point so that the energy demand of transitioning from descent to climb is managed promptly and correctly.	->	Follow-up answer should read: 'No. BasicMed does not authorize any IFR operations — this is a VFR-only certificate. Therefore, this ILS precision approach cannot be conducted under BasicMed privileges regardless of whether the helicopter is turbine-powered or piston-powered. The aircraft must operate under an FAA medical certificate (First or Second Class) to conduct IFR precision approaches. The removal of the turbine aircraft restriction from BasicMed per 14 CFR Part 68 applies only to VFR operations within the United States below 18,000 MSL.'
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S13.007	Yes — there are very limited, specific exceptions. Under 14 CFR 91.176, Special Authorization CAT II and CAT III approaches permit operations to lower minimums (DH as low as zero for CAT IIIc) with specific aircraft certification, avionics, training, and authorization requirements. Additionally, certain military or special operations under Part 135 or 91 Subpart K may have specific authorizations. However, for standard 14 CFR 91.175 operations, no pilot may descend below DA without the required visual references — period. There is no 'almost there' exception, no weather-improving exception, and no captain's discretion exception under standard IFR rules. Any descent below DA without visual references under 91.175 is a regulatory violation regardless of outcome.	->	Follow-up answer should be revised: 'For a standard CAT I operation under 14 CFR 91.175(c)(1), if only approach lights are visible at DH, you may descend to 100 feet above the TDZE to attempt to acquire runway environment references. However, you cannot land or continue below 100 feet AGL unless you acquire the required runway environment references (runway threshold, markings, lights, VASI, etc.). For CAT II/III operations under 14 CFR 91.176, specific authorizations define different requirements. In all cases, attempting to land with only approach lights visible is a violation of 14 CFR 91.175(c)(1)(ii). The 100-foot descent is a transitional corridor only, not a landing authorization.'
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S15.001	The DA/DH is the altitude (or height above touchdown for DH) at which the pilot must decide to either continue the approach visually or execute a missed approach. On a precision approach such as an ILS, you may transition to visual flight and continue descending only when you have the required visual references in sight at or above the DA/DH. You may not descend below DA/DH unless you have the required visual references and can maintain them. The required visual references are listed in 14 CFR 91.175(c).	->	question text changed
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S15.002	A stabilized visual flight path means maintaining a consistent glidepath angle, airspeed, and rate of descent from DA/DH all the way to the aiming point, with no abrupt control inputs or large deviations. In a helicopter, this is especially critical because helicopters are more susceptible to vortex ring state if airspeed drops too low combined with a high rate of descent, and because the helicopter's agility can tempt pilots into making aggressive last-minute corrections that can destabilize the approach. Maintaining a stable path improves both safety and landing precision.	->	A stabilized visual flight path means maintaining a consistent descent profile, airspeed, and rate of descent from DA/DH all the way to the intended landing point, with no abrupt control inputs or large deviations. In a helicopter, this is especially critical because helicopters are more susceptible to vortex ring state if airspeed drops too low combined with a high rate of descent, and because the helicopter's agility can tempt pilots into making aggressive last-minute corrections that can destabilize the approach. Maintaining a stable path improves both safety and landing precision. (Reference: FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13)
AUTO_APPROVE	IH.VII.B.S15.008	Main answer correctly addresses the regulatory and safety boundaries under 14 CFR 91.175; follow-up question and guidance on helicopter maneuvering, circling minimums, and required visual references are accurate and appropriately cautious.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S16.006	A ground track of 085° versus a final approach course of 092° means the aircraft is being blown to the right (north) of course — there is a right crosswind component pushing the helicopter left relative to the course, or more precisely, the aircraft is drifting left of intended track. To maintain the final approach course, the pilot needs to apply a wind correction angle by heading to the right of 092° — approximately 007° right correction, so a heading of roughly 099° — to achieve a ground track of 092°. The MFD is giving real-time feedback that the current heading is insufficient to track the centerline, and the pilot should increase the crab angle into the wind until the ground track on the MFD matches the final approach course.	->	A ground track of 085° versus a final approach course of 092° means the aircraft is drifting left (south) of the intended course. This indicates a right crosswind component (wind from the right) pushing the helicopter leftward relative to the desired ground track. To maintain the final approach course of 092°, the pilot must apply a wind correction angle by heading to the right of 092° — approximately 7° right correction for a heading of roughly 099° — to achieve a ground track of 092°. The MFD shows in real-time that the current heading is insufficient; increase the crab angle to the right until the MFD ground track matches the final approach course.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.B.S17.002	The five core components of SRM are: (1) Risk Management — identifying and mitigating hazards before and during flight; (2) Situational Awareness — knowing where you are, what your aircraft is doing, and what is happening around you; (3) Workload Management — prioritizing and sequencing tasks to avoid task saturation; (4) CFIT Awareness — actively monitoring terrain and obstacle clearance, especially during instrument approaches; and (5) Automation Management — understanding the mode and status of autopilots, FMS, and GPS systems and using them appropriately without becoming over-reliant.	->	The five core components of SRM are: (1) Risk Management — identifying and mitigating hazards before and during flight; (2) Situational Awareness — knowing where you are, what your aircraft is doing, and what is happening around you; (3) Workload Management — prioritizing and sequencing tasks to avoid task saturation; (4) CFIT Awareness — actively monitoring terrain and obstacle clearance, especially during instrument approaches; and (5) Automation Management — understanding the mode and status of autopilots, FMS, and GPS systems and using them appropriately without becoming over-reliant.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.C.K1.003	Per 14 CFR 91.175(c), to descend below DA/DH or MDA, the pilot must have distinctly visible and identifiable one of the following: (1) the approach light system, except that a pilot may not descend below 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation using approach lights alone unless the red terminating bars or red side row bars are also visible; (2) the threshold, threshold markings, or threshold lights; (3) the runway end identifier lights (REIL); (4) the visual approach slope indicator (VASI); (5) the touchdown zone or touchdown zone markings or lights; (6) the runway or runway markings or runway lights. The pilot must also be in a position to make a normal descent to landing using normal maneuvers.	->	Per 14 CFR 91.175(c), to descend below DA/DH or MDA, the pilot must have distinctly visible and identifiable at least one of the following: (1) the approach light system (provided that if the approach light system is used as the visual reference, the pilot may not descend below 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation using approach lights alone unless the red terminating bars or red side row bars are also visible); (2) the threshold, threshold markings, or threshold lights; (3) the runway end identifier lights (REIL); (4) the visual approach slope indicator (VASI); (5) the touchdown zone or touchdown zone markings or lights; (6) the runway or runway markings or runway lights. Once any one of these references is sighted, the pilot must be in a position to make a normal descent to landing using normal maneuvers.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.C.R5.007	The reference to 14 CFR 91.411 is incorrect (that regulation governs altitude reporting system tests, not database currency); the follow-up answer conflates Part 91 and Part 135 authority in ways that may mislead students about expired database legality; needs authoritative clarification of database currency requirements and exceptions for different operators.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.C.S2.001	The first power-related action is to apply the power setting specified in the POH/RFM for the missed approach procedure — typically maximum allowable torque or a published climb power setting. The vertical speed indicator (VSI) and altimeter are your primary confirmation instruments, but in a helicopter, the torque gauge (or power turbine/engine instrument) tells you immediately whether you have applied the correct power. A positive rate of climb confirmed on the VSI verifies the power setting is achieving the intended result.	->	The first power-related action is to apply the power setting specified in the POH/RFM for the missed approach procedure—typically maximum allowable torque or a published climb power setting. The vertical speed indicator (VSI) and altimeter are your primary instruments to immediately confirm you have achieved and are maintaining a positive rate of climb. The torque gauge confirms you have applied the correct power setting, but the VSI and altimeter directly show whether the aircraft is performing as intended.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.C.S5.008	This position reflects a common and dangerous attitude. FAA guidance consistently emphasizes that checklists exist to catch memory lapses — particularly in high-workload, high-stress environments like a missed approach in IMC. Accident data in FAA-H-8083-21B shows that familiarity breeds complacency, and that accidents often correlate with increased hours in type as pilots over-rely on muscle memory and skip verification steps. The FAA defines proper checklist use as a skill component of IFR operations — not an optional practice. Memory items are only appropriate for immediate-action emergencies where there is no time to reference the checklist. A missed approach is a normal/abnormal procedure, not an immediate emergency, and the physical checklist must be used.	->	This position reflects a common and dangerous attitude. FAA guidance consistently emphasizes that checklists exist to catch memory lapses—particularly in high-workload, high-stress environments like a missed approach in IMC. Accident data in FAA-H-8083-21B shows that familiarity breeds complacency and that accidents often correlate with increased hours in type as pilots over-rely on muscle memory and skip verification steps. Under 14 CFR Part 135, 14 CFR 135.83 explicitly mandates checklist use during all phases of flight. Under 14 CFR Part 91, while no explicit checklist-use regulation exists, 14 CFR 91.9 requires compliance with the RFM, and the RFM's published procedures—including checklists—are part of the FAA-approved operating limitations. Memory items are only appropriate for immediate-action emergencies where there is no time to reference the checklist; a missed approach is a normal/abnormal procedure and the physical checklist must be used.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.C.S7.007	The answer does not clearly address whether continuing a missed approach with degraded GPS is legal under 14 CFR 91.175 if the approach was published with LPV/WAAS minimums; this is a critical safety and regulatory distinction that requires clarification.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.C.S9.004	With 45 minutes of fuel, proceeding to the alternate (35 minutes away) leaves only 10 minutes of reserve — well below the required 30-minute IFR fuel reserve under 14 CFR 91.167. This means diverting to the alternate is no longer a safe option without refueling. I would: (1) declare minimum fuel or consider declaring emergency if appropriate; (2) advise ATC of the fuel situation; (3) request an immediate second approach rather than holding or diverting; (4) if weather is truly below minimums and another approach is unlikely to succeed, look for the closest airport with VMC conditions and adequate fuel. The primary goal is not attempting another approach just because fuel forces it — fuel emergency changes the dynamics and ATC must be informed immediately.	->	With 45 minutes of fuel remaining and a 35-minute flight to the alternate, arriving at the alternate would leave only 10 minutes of fuel — a violation of 14 CFR 91.167, which requires a 30-minute IFR fuel reserve. This is a fuel emergency. I must immediately: (1) Declare an emergency to ATC (both 7700 squawk and verbal declaration of 'fuel emergency'); (2) Advise ATC of exact fuel remaining; (3) Request immediate landing at the nearest suitable airport — either the destination if another approach has a reasonable chance of success, or the closest airport with adequate fuel and weather; (4) Accept any reasonable clearance and vector to expedite landing. 'Declaring minimum fuel' alone is insufficient in this situation; emergency declaration is required. The underlying issue is inadequate preflight planning, but the immediate priority is safely landing the aircraft.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.K1.004	Yes, you may continue below DA if approach lights are in sight, but only to 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation (TDZE). Approach lights alone authorize descent to 100 feet above TDZE but do NOT authorize the full landing. To descend below 100 feet above TDZE and land, you must have at least one of the other required runway environment elements in sight — such as the runway threshold, threshold lights, touchdown zone, or REIL. If you reach 100 feet above TDZE with only approach lights still visible, you must execute a missed approach. This applies under 14 CFR 91.175(c)(3). For helicopter operations, the transition from instruments to visual landing at low altitude with only approach lights is operationally demanding and requires precise power and attitude management.	->	Yes, you may continue below DA if approach lights are in sight, but only to search for additional required runway environment elements down to 100 feet above TDZE. Approach lights alone do not authorize landing; they authorize a limited descent to attempt to locate other required visual references such as the runway threshold, threshold lights, touchdown zone, or REIL. If you reach 100 feet above TDZE with only approach lights still visible and no other required runway environment element is in sight, you must execute a missed approach. This is authorized under 14 CFR 91.175(c)(3). Helicopter operations at these low altitudes with marginal visual references demand precise power and attitude control.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.K2.001	A yellow inscription on a black background indicates a location sign. Location signs identify the runway or taxiway you are currently on — for example, a sign reading '27' in yellow on black tells you that you are on or holding short of Runway 27. They are non-mandatory signs and do not require stopping or clearance to pass.	->	A yellow inscription on a black background indicates a runway position sign. These signs identify your current location on a runway or taxiway by marking specific positions, such as distance markers or taxiway designators. Runway position signs are informational reference signs that help you maintain situational awareness of your ground position during taxi and approach operations. Unlike mandatory instruction signs (red and white), position signs do not direct specific pilot actions, but pilots must read and use them to maintain awareness of their location on the airport surface.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.K2.005	ALSF-2 (Approach Light System with Sequenced Flashing Lights, configuration 2) is a high-intensity approach lighting system 2,400–3,000 feet long with sequenced flashing lights (the 'rabbit') and a red terminating bar. It is typically associated with Category II/III ILS approaches and provides excellent visual guidance in very low visibility conditions. MALSR (Medium-intensity ALS with Runway Alignment Indicator Lights) is shorter (typically 1,400 feet) with sequenced flashers but lower intensity. The ALSF-2 provides better visual acquisition at longer range and in lower visibility due to its greater intensity and length, which is why it supports CAT II/III operations. For helicopters, which have more flexible approach profiles, understanding the ALS type helps anticipate the visual transition point and align properly with the runway.	->	ALSF-2 (Approach Light System with Sequenced Flashing Lights, configuration 2) is a high-intensity approach lighting system 2,400 feet long with sequenced flashing lights (the 'rabbit') and a red terminating bar. It is commonly used to support Category II and III ILS approaches in low-visibility conditions but can also support CAT I approaches. MALSR (Medium-intensity ALS with Runway Alignment Indicator Lights) is 1,400 feet long with sequenced flashers and medium intensity, typically supporting CAT I approaches with good visibility minimums. The ALSF-2 provides superior visual acquisition due to its greater intensity and length, enabling operations in lower visibility conditions. For helicopters, understanding the ALS type helps anticipate the visual transition point and align properly with the runway during night IFR approaches.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.D.K2.007	The treatment of ODALS as a qualifying 'approach lighting system' element under 14 CFR 91.175(c)(3) is not clearly substantiated in current FAA guidance; ambiguity exists regarding whether ODALS alone authorizes descent to 100 feet above TDZE.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.K2.008	Three white and one red on a PAPI indicates the aircraft is slightly above the PAPI glidepath. The ILS electronic glideslope and the PAPI are designed to be co-aligned, but they can diverge for several reasons: the PAPI and ILS glideslope may be set to slightly different angles (e.g., PAPI at 3.0° and ILS at 3.0° but with different TCH), the helicopter's eye-to-wheel height differs from a fixed-wing aircraft the PAPI was calibrated for (shorter landing gear means pilots of helicopters may be higher than indicated on PAPI when on the electronic glideslope), or the PAPI may have a calibration discrepancy. At 200 feet AGL on an ILS, if the ILS CDI and glideslope are centered and the flight instruments are consistent, the pilot should generally cross-check both but recognize that the ILS glideslope is the primary precision guidance for the approach. The PAPI provides a useful secondary cross-check. However, if the ILS shows on-glidepath and the PAPI shows above-path (3W/1R), the helicopter pilot should also consider that the helicopter's lower eye-to-wheel height means being slightly high on the PAPI while on the ILS glideslope may actually result in a normal touchdown — this is a known phenomenon with helicopters on approaches calibrated for larger aircraft.	->	Three white and one red on a PAPI indicates the aircraft is slightly above the PAPI glidepath. The ILS electronic glideslope and the PAPI are designed to be co-aligned, but they can diverge for several reasons: slight differences in glidepath angle setting, different Threshold Crossing Height (TCH) references, or the helicopter's lower eye-to-wheel height compared to the fixed-wing aircraft the PAPI was calibrated for. At 200 feet AGL on an ILS, the ILS glideslope is the primary precision guidance and should remain the pilot's reference. However, a consistent PAPI/ILS divergence warrants cross-check confirmation. Helicopters may appear slightly high on PAPI while on the electronic glideslope due to their lower eye-to-wheel geometry — this is a known phenomenon. The pilot must verify the approach is stabilized and that this divergence is understood before committing below DA. Use ILS glideslope needles as primary guidance and cross-check with visual runway alignment and approach lighting progression.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.K3.002	On a precision approach (e.g., ILS), the pilot reaches a Decision Altitude (DA) and must either continue to land if visual references are established or execute a missed approach immediately. Configuration at DA typically involves approach power set, airspeed stabilized at approach speed per the POH/RFM, and landing light on if appropriate. On a non-precision approach, the aircraft reaches MDA and may level off temporarily while searching for visual references before transitioning to the landing profile. In both cases, once visual contact is made, the pilot transitions to a normal or modified approach profile appropriate for the landing area.	->	On a precision approach (e.g., ILS), the pilot reaches Decision Altitude (DA) at which a definitive go/no-go decision is made: either visual references are established and landing is assured, or a missed approach is executed immediately. Aircraft configuration at DA includes approach power, stabilized airspeed per aircraft performance data, and systems configured as required (gear, lights, etc.). On a non-precision approach, the pilot reaches Minimum Descent Altitude (MDA) and may continue at that altitude or follow a published descent profile while searching for visual references; once the runway environment is identified and a safe landing is assured, the transition to landing descent begins. For helicopters, approach profiles often provide greater flexibility than fixed-wing procedures, and many transitions involve continuous descent from MDA rather than a level-off hold. The critical distinction is that DA is an absolute decision point, while MDA is a minimum altitude floor that permits continued search for visual references within regulatory limits.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.K3.008	A low inertia rotor system decays rapidly when collective is mismanaged, making the window for RPM recovery narrow. With an unexpected autopilot disconnect at 100 feet AGL at night over a hospital helipad, the pilot faces simultaneous demands: manually assuming aircraft control, confirming visual references exist, and making an immediate land-or-missed-approach decision. The aircraft configuration at autopilot disconnect should already be near approach power and airspeed — the pilot must immediately stabilize the aircraft manually, cross-check attitude, airspeed, and most critically rotor RPM. If RPM is in the normal range and the helipad is clearly visible and in a position for a normal landing, continuation is appropriate with a smooth, controlled deceleration to hover/landing. If any doubt exists about rotor RPM, spatial orientation (night transition from instruments), or position relative to the pad, an immediate missed approach must be executed — adding full power while simultaneously maintaining rotor RPM within limits. The danger is that an abrupt collective increase to climb during a missed approach while disoriented can cause a rotor RPM overshoot, while insufficient collective during the manual takeover can allow RPM to decay toward a low rotor RPM/stall condition — a non-recoverable situation in a low-inertia system.	->	A low inertia rotor system loses RPM rapidly if collective is mismanaged, leaving a narrow window for corrective action. With an unexpected autopilot disconnect at 100 feet AGL at night over a hospital helipad, the pilot must simultaneously assume manual control, confirm visual references, and make an immediate land-or-missed-approach decision. At autopilot disconnect, the aircraft should already be configured at approach power and airspeed; the pilot must immediately stabilize manually, cross-check attitude, airspeed, and critically, rotor RPM. If RPM is within normal operating limits and the helipad is clearly visible in a position suitable for landing, continuation with a smooth, controlled deceleration to hover is appropriate. If any doubt exists regarding rotor RPM, spatial orientation, or landing pad position, an immediate missed approach must be executed while maintaining collective management that preserves rotor RPM within limits. The danger of low-altitude manual takeover is that abrupt collective inputs to climb during a missed approach can cause rotor RPM overshoot or, more critically, insufficient unloading of the rotor during the transition allows RPM to decay below the minimum operating range — a situation that becomes non-recoverable at 100 feet AGL due to insufficient altitude for autorotation recovery. At this altitude, prevention of RPM decay through smooth, controlled collective management is paramount.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.R1.008	A full-scale localizer CDI deflection represents a deviation of approximately 2.5 degrees or more from the centerline. Given that the ILS localizer is approximately 4-5 times more sensitive than a VOR CDI at full deflection, a full-scale deviation at the FAF means the aircraft may already be near or at the edge of the protected obstacle clearance area for the procedure. Unlike VOR, where full scale is 12 degrees, localizer full scale is a much smaller angular deviation — meaning the aircraft is still relatively close geographically but outside the OCS (obstacle clearance surface) protection. Continuing this approach risks obstacle contact, terrain, or antenna farm incursion on the approach corridor. The belief that 'the needle will come in' is a textbook example of the hazardous attitude of rationalization. The correct action at the FAF with any significant deviation — and certainly full-scale — is immediate missed approach execution.	->	A full-scale localizer CDI deflection represents an angular deviation of approximately ±2.5° from the localizer centerline at the runway threshold. The localizer beam is significantly more sensitive than a standard VOR signal (VOR is ±10°), meaning a full-scale deviation indicates the aircraft is outside the protected obstacle clearance surface (OCS) designed for the approach procedure. Continuing an approach with a full-scale localizer deviation at the FAF means the aircraft may be in unprotected airspace where obstacles are not guaranteed to clear. This compounds the risk of an unstable approach: not only is the aircraft misaligned, but the clearance margins that make the procedure safe are no longer valid. The belief that the needle will center up shortly is a textbook hazardous attitude — rationalization. The correct action with any significant deviation at the FAF, and especially full-scale deviation, is immediate missed approach execution per 14 CFR 91.175.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.R2.002	A full-scale downward deflection of the glideslope needle means the aircraft is above the glidepath — the needle deflects toward the glidepath, so a needle pegged low means the glidepath is below the aircraft. Conversely, if the needle is pegged high (full-scale up deflection), the aircraft is well below the glidepath. Full-scale glideslope deflection typically represents approximately 0.7° off the glidepath centerline, which at typical ILS glidepath intercept distances (e.g., 5–7 NM) can translate to hundreds of feet of vertical deviation — placing the aircraft dangerously close to obstacles or terrain.	->	A full-scale downward deflection of the glideslope needle means the aircraft is ABOVE the glidepath. Conversely, a full-scale upward deflection means the aircraft is BELOW the glidepath. The CDI needle always deflects toward the desired signal. Full-scale glideslope deflection represents approximately 0.7° angular deviation from the glidepath centerline. At typical glideslope capture and approach geometry, this angular deviation can translate to several hundred feet of vertical separation from the glidepath. Any full-scale deflection requires immediate corrective action to return to glidepath centerline.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.D.R3.008	The specific claim that accident rates decrease 'significantly after 100 hours in type' is presented as established FAA fact but cannot be directly verified in cited handbooks; while hours-in-type correlation is sound FAA philosophy, the precise 100-hour threshold requires citation to accident data or official FAA risk analysis documents rather than flying handbooks.
AI_EDIT	IH.VII.D.S2.008	This is a convergence of multiple risk factors requiring structured ADM. Full analysis: (1) Braking action 'nil' (Mu < 0.05) means essentially no deceleration capability — any aircraft that rolls out on that surface risks a runway excursion. For a helicopter, if landing on a runway surface, nil braking creates directional control and rollout hazard. However, a helicopter can land vertically or at very low forward speed, which may mitigate the braking issue if a clear area of sufficient size is available. Assess: Can you execute a vertical or near-zero-groundspeed landing within the available surface? (2) Fuel: 45 minutes remaining, alternate 90 miles. At typical IFR cruise speed (100+ KTAS), 90 miles is approximately 50-55 minutes — this is already marginal and may not meet the 45-minute fuel reserve requirement under IFR (14 CFR 91.167 requires fuel to the alternate plus 45 minutes at normal cruise speed). You may already be in an emergency fuel scenario if you divert now. (3) Options: (a) Continue approach and attempt a hover/vertical landing in a clear, confirmed area — confirm via radio or visual if possible; (b) Declare minimum fuel or emergency to ATC and request priority handling; (c) Divert immediately and accept that fuel will be critically low on arrival. (4) Key principle: 14 CFR 91.167 fuel requirements must be met at the time of departure; if you are now fuel-critical, PIC must declare accordingly. The 'nil braking' PIREP combined with marginal fuel is a genuine emergency scenario — do not allow schedule pressure or sunk-cost thinking to push you into a bad decision. Declare, communicate, and execute the safest available option.	->	This is a convergence of multiple risk factors requiring structured ADM. Full analysis: (1) Braking action 'nil' (Mu < 0.05) means essentially no deceleration capability. For a helicopter, this is mitigated by the ability to land vertically or at very low forward speed, but you must confirm a suitable landing area is available and clear. (2) Fuel: 45 minutes remaining, alternate 90 miles away. At approach/descent speeds (typically 70–90 KTAS for a helicopter), 90 miles will require approximately 60–70 minutes of flight time, already exceeding your 45-minute reserve. Perform a real-time fuel calculation: can you reach the alternate and maintain the required 45-minute IFR fuel reserve per 14 CFR 91.167? If the calculation does not work, you are already in a fuel emergency scenario. (3) Options: (a) If fuel math works, continue approach and execute a vertical landing in a confirmed clear area; (b) If fuel will be critically tight, declare minimum fuel or emergency to ATC immediately and request priority vectors to the alternate; (c) Do not allow schedule pressure or sunk-cost thinking to override the fuel calculation. (4) Key principle: Declare fuel status early if marginal; 14 CFR 91.3 (PIC authority and responsibility) allows you to deviate from clearances in an emergency. Nil braking + marginal fuel = decision point before FAF.
ESCALATE	IH.VII.D.S4.007	The follow-up answer conflates powered-flight RPM recovery with autorotation entry procedures; lowering collective in powered flight with an operating engine differs fundamentally from autorotation collective management, and the answer risks instructing unsafe technique by not clearly distinguishing the two scenarios.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.A.K1.007	They begin the approach at the EFC time — 20 minutes from now — because an EFC time takes priority over the filed ETA when planning the approach under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3). The EFC time was specifically issued so ATC knows when to expect the aircraft to proceed. Regarding the missed approach: yes, they must execute the missed approach if they do not break out at minimums, just as they would in normal IFR operations. After the missed approach, they should hold as published at the missed approach holding fix, or as filed if none is published, and wait for the filed ETA to pass before attempting again if needed. ATC will have protected airspace based on the EFC time, so executing a standard missed approach and hold is the correct and expected action.	->	They begin the approach at the EFC time — 20 minutes from now — because the EFC time takes priority over the filed ETA when planning the approach under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3). Regarding the missed approach: yes, they must execute the missed approach if they do not break out at minimums. After the missed approach, they should hold at the published missed approach point or fix. If conditions improve or they regain communications, they may attempt another approach; however, the regulation does not explicitly authorize multiple approach attempts during lost comm. Any decision to re-attempt should be consistent with pilot-in-command authority under 14 CFR 91.3 and should prioritize safety. ATC will have protected airspace based on the EFC time, making prompt contact restoration critical.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.A.K1.008	A steady green light gun signal directed at an aircraft in flight means 'cleared to land.' However, in IMC the pilot cannot see the light gun signal from the tower — light gun signals are only practical in VMC or at close range. If somehow the pilot does receive a light gun signal and can interpret it, they should follow the signal as a form of ATC communication. Regarding deviations from 91.185: the regulation itself at 91.3(b) allows a pilot in command to deviate from any regulation to the extent necessary to meet an emergency. If, for example, a nearby airport has much better weather and the pilot determines it is the safest course of action to divert there rather than continue to the destination in severe weather, they may deviate under 91.3(b) — but they must submit a written report to the ATC facility whose airspace was affected if requested. Safety of flight always overrides procedural compliance; however, 91.185 procedures are specifically designed to keep you predictable to ATC and protect your airspace.	->	Light gun signals are impractical in IMC because the pilot cannot visually acquire the tower or the light beam from altitude. In IMC during lost communications, do not expect or rely on light gun signals; instead, comply with 14 CFR 91.185 procedures and set transponder to code 7600. Regarding deviations from 91.185: 14 CFR 91.3(b) permits the pilot in command to deviate from any regulation to the extent necessary to meet an emergency—for example, if deteriorating weather makes the destination unsafe and an alternate offers better conditions. If such a deviation is made, the pilot must file a written report with the FAA upon request per 14 CFR 91.3(c) and should notify ATC as soon as communications are restored. However, 91.185 procedures are designed to keep your flight path predictable to ATC and protect your airspace, so deviations should be reserved for genuine emergencies.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.A.S1.005	This is a transmitter failure rather than a total radio failure — you have receive capability but not transmit. Your recognition clue is one-way communication: you hear ATC but get no response to your calls. This is distinct from total radio failure. A unique action you can take is to advise ATC of the situation by keying the mic in a specific pattern if requested — for example, ATC may ask you to 'ident' or key the mic a certain number of times to confirm you are receiving their instructions. You should squawk 7600 and also squawk 7700 briefly if in distress. ATC has light gun signal protocols for aircraft with no radio, and you should be prepared to use your transponder ident (IDENT button) as a communication tool while proceeding under 14 CFR 91.185 lost communications procedures.	->	This is a transmitter failure rather than a total radio failure — you have receive capability but not transmit. Your recognition clue is one-way communication: you hear ATC but get no response to your calls. This is distinct from total radio failure. For a transmitter-only failure, squawk and maintain 7600. Only squawk 7700 if the situation becomes a true emergency (e.g., fuel emergency, structural damage). A unique action you can take is to use your transponder IDENT button or key the microphone in a pattern if ATC requests it to confirm you are receiving their instructions. You should proceed under 14 CFR 91.185 lost communications procedures. Light gun signals are available through AIM 4-2-13 but are less commonly used with modern radar systems.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.A.S3.006	Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(1)(ii), when being radar vectored at the time of comm failure, you should fly the direct route from the point where the vector started to the fix, route, or airway specified in the radar vector clearance. If no specific fix was named in the vector instruction, reference your last full route clearance to identify the next fix on your approach clearance or flight plan route and proceed direct to that point. This keeps you predictable to ATC. Since you are already on the approach phase, continuing to your destination is appropriate — you are positioned for the approach, and ATC will anticipate your arrival. Continuing is the correct decision unless a safety-of-flight emergency exists.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(1)(i), when being radar vectored at the time of comm failure, you should fly the direct route from the point where the vector started to the fix, route, or airway specified in the radar vector clearance. If no specific fix was named in the vector instruction, reference your last full route clearance to identify the next fix on your approach clearance or flight plan route and proceed direct to that point. This keeps you predictable to ATC. Since you are already on the approach phase, continuing to your destination is appropriate — you are positioned for the approach, and ATC will anticipate your arrival. Continuing is the correct decision unless a safety-of-flight emergency exists.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.A.S4.001	14 CFR 91.185 governs lost communications procedures. For IFR flight, you should begin the approach at the destination at the expected approach time (EAT) if you received one, or if no EAT was received, at the estimated time of arrival (ETA) calculated from your filed or amended flight plan. The rule is to arrive over the destination at the latest of: (1) the last assigned altitude, the MEA, or the altitude ATC told you to expect, and (2) the EAT if one was received, or your filed ETA if not.	->	14 CFR 91.185(c)(3) governs when you may begin an approach at your destination after lost communications. If you received an Expected Approach Time (EAT) from ATC, begin your approach at that time. If no EAT was issued, begin your approach at the Estimated Time of Arrival (ETA) from your filed flight plan. (Note: The altitude rule—maintaining the highest of last assigned altitude, MEA, or expect altitude—is governed by 91.185(c)(2) and applies throughout the flight, but is separate from the timing requirement in 91.185(c)(3).)
ESCALATE	IH.VIII.A.S4.005	The answer incorrectly interprets 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3)(i) by conflating an informal recalculation of ETA with a formally amended flight plan filed before loss of comms; the regulation requires use of the filed ETA or an officially amended ETA, not a mental recalculation made after comms are lost.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.A.S4.008	This scenario involves both an EAT (1730Z) and an EFC (Expect Further Clearance at 1730Z). Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3)(ii), since an EAT was received, you hold and begin the approach at 1730Z — not your filed ETA of 1710Z. For holding altitude, per 14 CFR 91.185(c)(2), you fly the highest of: (1) the last assigned altitude, (2) the MEA for the route segment you are on, or (3) the altitude ATC told you to expect. You hold at that altitude until it is time to descend for the approach at 1730Z. Squawk 7600. At 1730Z, leave the holding fix, fly the RNAV (GPS) RWY 18 approach, and if the approach is missed, execute the published missed approach procedure. Note: in a helicopter, confirm the approach is authorized for helicopters or has helicopter-specific minima on the chart.	->	This scenario involves an EFC (Expect Further Clearance at 1730Z) that effectively establishes the time to commence your approach. Under 14 CFR 91.185(c)(3)(ii), in a lost-comm situation where you have received an expected approach time or further clearance instruction, you hold and begin the approach at the specified time — in this case, 1730Z — rather than your filed ETA of 1710Z. For holding altitude, per 14 CFR 91.185(c)(2), you fly the highest of: (1) the last assigned altitude, (2) the MEA for the route segment you are on, or (3) the altitude ATC told you to expect. You hold at that altitude until it is time to descend for the approach at 1730Z. Squawk 7600. At 1730Z, leave the holding fix, fly the RNAV (GPS) RWY 18 approach, and if the approach is missed, execute the published missed approach procedure. Note: confirm the approach is authorized for helicopters or has helicopter-specific minima on the chart. Also verify RAIM availability before the approach time; if RAIM is unavailable or fails before your approach window, you should consider a conventional approach or diversion to an alternate.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.A.S5.006	Having only 50 hours in type means your automation familiarity, system knowledge, and pattern recognition in that specific aircraft are still developing — placing you in a higher-risk category regardless of total hours. SRM demands honest self-assessment (the 'I' in the IMSAFE checklist extends to experience). With limited type experience, you should increase reliance on checklists for comm failure procedures, be conservative about workload acceptance, slow down decision-making to avoid errors, and be more inclined toward declaring an emergency if the situation compounds. The SRM principle is to choose options you know will work rather than 'probably' work — meaning you should default to established 91.185 procedures rather than improvising unfamiliar system workarounds.	->	Having only 50 hours in type means your automation familiarity, system knowledge, and pattern recognition in that specific aircraft are still developing — placing you in a higher-risk category regardless of total hours. SRM demands honest self-assessment of experience level as a personal risk factor. With limited type experience, you should increase reliance on checklists for comm failure procedures, be conservative about workload acceptance, slow down decision-making to avoid errors, and be more inclined toward declaring an emergency if the situation compounds. The SRM principle is to choose options you know will work rather than 'probably' work — meaning you should default to established 91.185 procedures rather than improvising unfamiliar system workarounds. This aligns with FAA guidance on ADM and risk management that emphasizes personal limitations and type-specific experience as key elements of the risk equation.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.R3.008	FAA data from the Helicopter Flying Handbook indicates that accidents correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours. A pilot with thousands of total hours but fewer than 100 hours in the specific helicopter type may have automation habits, rotor RPM decay rates, torque response characteristics, and instrument layout familiarity that differ significantly from the current aircraft. During a high-workload event like a single-engine instrument approach, the pilot may default to muscle memory or mental models from a previous aircraft type — leading to incorrect control inputs, delayed recognition of instrument deviations, or misjudgment of single-engine performance margins. This type-specific inexperience is especially dangerous in IMC because visual cues that might otherwise allow the pilot to correct are absent. The risk mitigation is to acknowledge this currency gap during pre-flight risk assessment, ensure thorough type-specific training and simulator time for single-engine IMC scenarios, and potentially increase personal minimums for single-engine approaches until type-specific proficiency is established.	->	FAA guidance and accident data emphasize that accidents correlate more strongly with hours in a specific aircraft model than with total flight hours. A pilot with thousands of total hours but fewer than 100 hours in the specific helicopter type may have automation habits, rotor RPM decay rates, torque response characteristics, and instrument layout familiarity that differ significantly from the current aircraft. During a high-workload event like a single-engine instrument approach, the pilot may default to muscle memory or mental models from a previous aircraft type — leading to incorrect control inputs, delayed recognition of instrument deviations, or misjudgment of single-engine performance margins. This type-specific inexperience is especially dangerous in IMC because visual cues that might otherwise allow the pilot to correct are absent. Risk mitigation includes acknowledging this currency gap during pre-flight risk assessment, ensuring thorough type-specific training and simulator time for single-engine IMC scenarios, and increasing personal minimums for single-engine approaches until type-specific proficiency is established. The decision-making principle known as the Trescott Tip reinforces this: if an action will only 'probably' work, choose instead a course of action known to work—avoiding uncertain outcomes in critical phases.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.R4.002	The primary onboard collision hazard detection systems include TCAS (Traffic Collision Avoidance System), which provides traffic advisories (TAs) and resolution advisories (RAs) based on transponder returns, and ADS-B In, which displays nearby ADS-B Out equipped traffic on a moving map or traffic display. TAWS (Terrain Awareness and Warning System) addresses terrain and obstacle collision hazards. The pilot should also monitor ATC for traffic calls.	->	Onboard collision-hazard detection systems include: (1) TCAS (Traffic Collision Avoidance System), if equipped—required on aircraft with MCTOW greater than 5,000 pounds operating in certain controlled airspace above 10,000 feet MSL—which provides traffic advisories (TAs) and resolution advisories (RAs) based on transponder returns; (2) ADS-B Out, required since January 1, 2020 on most aircraft in controlled airspace (14 CFR 91.225), which broadcasts position to other equipped aircraft; (3) ADS-B In, an optional enhancement that displays nearby ADS-B Out equipped traffic on a compatible display; and (4) TAWS (Terrain Awareness and Warning System), if equipped, which addresses terrain and obstacle collision hazards. The pilot should also maintain communication with ATC for traffic advisories. Not all multiengine helicopters have all systems installed; equipping and operational status depend on aircraft certification, weight class, and operational environment.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.R4.003	TAWS (Terrain Awareness and Warning System) provides alerts for controlled flight into terrain (CFIT) by comparing the aircraft's current position, altitude, and flight path against a terrain and obstacle database. It warns of potential ground or obstacle collisions. TCAS, by contrast, detects other transponder-equipped aircraft and advises on traffic conflicts. TAWS addresses terrain/obstacle collision hazards; TCAS addresses mid-air collision hazards. Both are important during an instrument approach, especially with degraded crew capacity due to engine failure.	->	TAWS (Terrain Awareness and Warning System) provides alerts for controlled flight into terrain (CFIT) by comparing the aircraft's current position, altitude, and flight path against a terrain and obstacle database. It warns of potential ground or obstacle collisions. TCAS, by contrast, detects other transponder-equipped aircraft and advises on traffic conflicts. TAWS addresses terrain/obstacle collision hazards; TCAS addresses mid-air collision hazards. Both are important during an instrument approach, especially with degraded crew capacity due to engine failure. Note: 14 CFR 91.223 requires TAWS on most turbine-powered aircraft with MCTOW greater than 10,000 pounds. Lighter or piston-powered helicopters typically do not have TAWS installed, so availability depends on aircraft type and certification.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.R5.005	An inoperative engine is a significant aircraft system change that can affect approach capability. Under 14 CFR Part 97 and TPP Inoperative Components Tables, minimums are raised when certain equipment is inoperative; however, an inoperative engine is an aircraft airworthiness issue governed by the RFM, not directly addressed in the TPP inoperative components table. The pilot must: (1) Confirm the aircraft is airworthy and within OEI performance limits for the approach per the RFM; (2) Complete all OEI checklists so the aircraft is fully configured before commencing or continuing the approach; (3) Verify the aircraft can perform the missed approach procedure on one engine at the applicable weight and density altitude — if it cannot, a lower or different approach minimum may effectively apply operationally even if not charted. Descending to minimums before confirming OEI configuration and performance capability is unsafe because the missed approach becomes the last safety net, and an unconfigured aircraft may not be able to execute it.	->	Inoperative equipment and an inoperative engine affect approach minimums through two separate frameworks: (1) If any airborne or ground-based navigation or approach aid (e.g., ILS localizer, glideslope) is inoperative, consult the TPP Inoperative Components Table to determine raised minimums per 14 CFR 97.3(d). (2) An inoperative engine is an aircraft airworthiness and performance issue governed by the RFM OEI section. The pilot must confirm the aircraft is within OEI performance limits and can safely execute the missed approach on the remaining engine at current weight, altitude, and density altitude. The pilot must: (1) Complete all OEI checklists and confirm the aircraft is fully configured per the RFM before commencing the approach; (2) Verify missed approach performance on one engine — if it cannot be accomplished, a lower minimums approach or diversion is required; (3) Descend to minimums only after both OEI configuration and performance capability are confirmed. The missed approach is the last safety net; an unconfigured or performance-limited aircraft cannot execute it reliably.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.S2.008	At the MAP, initiate the missed approach immediately regardless of engine time limits — safety of flight is the priority. Apply OEI maximum continuous power (MCP) rather than 2.5-minute power if the time limit has been reached, accepting a reduced climb performance. Use cyclic to establish the published missed approach climb airspeed (typically Vx or Vy OEI), set collective at MCP, and trim coordinated flight with pedals. Reduce drag by retracting gear (if applicable) at a safe altitude. Advise ATC of the engine situation and declare an emergency. Exceeding the OEI 2.5-minute time limit risks catastrophic engine failure, hot section damage, turbine blade shedding, and compressor stall — the engine may fail entirely. The aircraft will require mandatory inspection and likely hot section replacement. However, the RFM may authorize exceeding limits in an actual emergency to save the aircraft — the pilot must exercise judgment and document the exceedance. The key takeaway is that during a real emergency, preserving the aircraft and occupants outweighs engine preservation, but the pilot must immediately manage power back to MCP as soon as climb performance allows.	->	At the MAP, initiate the missed approach immediately — safety of flight is the priority. Once the immediate climb is established, operate at OEI maximum continuous power (MCP), which is the highest authorized sustained power per the RFM. Exceeding published engine time limits is not authorized in normal operations; however, if the aircraft is in genuine peril and the RFM permits emergency power exceedance, the pilot may exceed limits only to save the aircraft. Use cyclic to establish the published missed approach climb airspeed (typically Vx or Vy OEI), set collective at MCP, trim coordinated flight with pedals, and retract gear if applicable at a safe altitude. Advise ATC and consider declaring an emergency. Exceeding OEI time limits risks catastrophic engine failure, turbine blade shedding, compressor stall, and hot section damage. After landing safely, you must make an entry in the aircraft maintenance logbook documenting the exceedance per 14 CFR 91.417. The helicopter is grounded and cannot return to service until an FAA-certificated mechanic inspects or replaces affected engine components per the manufacturer's maintenance manual and 14 CFR 43.9.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.S6.008	Under 14 CFR 91.185, in IMC you continue the approach using the last received routing and altitude clearance. Squawk 7600 immediately. If you were already cleared for the approach, continue to fly the full published procedure to the MAP/DA. If you reach the MAP or DA and have the required visual references (one of the ten specified references per 14 CFR 91.175(c)), you may descend and land. You should land as soon as practicable. At a towered airport, watch for light gun signals if below the clouds — a steady green from the tower while on final means cleared to land. Document the communications failure and advise ATC after landing.	->	Under 14 CFR 91.185, squawk 7600 immediately. If you were already cleared for the approach, continue to fly the full published procedure to the MAP/DA using the last received routing and altitude clearance. At the MAP or DA, if you have the required visual references (one of the ten specified references per 14 CFR 91.175(c)), you may descend and land. With one engine inoperative, you must confirm before accepting the approach that single-engine missed approach performance clears terrain and obstacles — if it does not, execute the missed approach at DA rather than risk an unsafe continuation. Light gun signals from the tower are a backup tool but are not a primary means of authorization during lost communications in IMC; they apply primarily when transitioning to visual conditions. Land as soon as practicable and advise ATC after landing. Document the communications failure in the logbook.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.S9.001	The glide slope indicator has a full-scale deflection of approximately 0.7° above or below center (full scale is typically marked by the needle reaching the outer dot). ¾-scale deflection means the needle must stay within 75% of that full travel — roughly within the inner ¾ of the indicator scale — on both the high and low side. In practical terms, the needle must not reach the outer dot or peg against the case; a small but visible deflection is acceptable, but the pilot must correct before the needle moves beyond that ¾ threshold.	->	Full-scale CDI/glide slope deflection means the needle has moved one full dot-width from center on either side. ¾-scale deflection means the needle must remain within approximately ¾ of one dot-width from center. In terms of angular deviation, glide slope full-scale is approximately 0.7°, so ¾-scale represents roughly 0.525° deviation. In practical terms, the needle should not reach the outer dot on either side of center; it must stay visibly closer to the center line than the threshold of full-scale deflection.
AI_EDIT	IH.VIII.B.S9.004	A half-dot low on the glide slope exceeds the ¾-scale standard (since ¾ of full scale is approximately ¾ of a dot's travel, a half-dot deflection is still within tolerance — but if the full-scale deflection equals one dot, then half-dot is within ¾-scale). With a quarter-dot lateral deflection, the pilot is well within the ¾-scale lateral standard. Corrective technique: apply a small increase in collective/power to arrest the below-glide-slope trend while simultaneously applying a gentle lateral cyclic correction to the left to recenter the localizer. With an inoperative left engine, the pilot must be alert to asymmetric torque effects — the operating right engine may introduce a left yaw tendency depending on rotor system design, requiring right pedal input. Corrections should be small and coordinated to avoid over-controlling in the OEI regime.	->	A half-dot low on glide slope is within the ¾-scale standard (since ¾-scale = 0.75 dots, and half-dot = 0.5 dots). The pilot IS within limits. With a quarter-dot lateral deflection, the pilot is also well within the ¾-scale lateral standard. Corrective technique: Apply a small increase in collective/power to arrest the below-glide-slope trend while simultaneously applying a gentle lateral cyclic correction to the left to recenter the localizer. With an inoperative left engine, be alert to asymmetric torque effects—the operating right engine may introduce a left yaw tendency depending on rotor system design, requiring right pedal input. Corrections should be small and coordinated to avoid over-controlling in the OEI regime.
