AI_EDIT	IH.IX.A.K1.008	Cyclic trim: The FAA-H-8083-21B specifically identifies cyclic trim as an item that is NOT automatically deferrable — if it is not listed in an approved MEL as deferrable, it requires repair before the next flight. Under 91.213(d), the pilot must verify the item is not required by 91.205, the RFM, or the type certificate data sheet. If any of those require it, it cannot be deferred without an MEL that explicitly permits it. Log the discrepancy and ground the aircraft for repair or MEL evaluation. Fuel quantity gauges: This is counterintuitive — 14 CFR 27.1337(b)(1) requires fuel quantity gauges to be accurate only when reading empty. This means an erratically-reading fuel quantity gauge that is not reading empty may not constitute an airworthiness defect under the certification standard. However, the pilot must still log the discrepancy, and a mechanic must evaluate whether the gauge's behavior affects the ability to determine usable fuel. The pilot's preflight fuel check must always be accomplished by visual inspection or measuring — never by relying on gauges alone — and this becomes even more critical with a known gauge discrepancy. The discrepancy should still be logged and evaluated; the point is that the regulatory accuracy standard is more limited than most pilots assume.	->	Cyclic trim: The FAA-H-8083-21B specifically identifies cyclic trim as an item that is NOT automatically deferrable — if it is not listed in an approved MEL as deferrable, it requires repair before the next flight. Under 91.213(d), the pilot must verify the item is not required by 91.205, the RFM, or the type certificate data sheet. If any of those require it, it cannot be deferred without an MEL that explicitly permits it. Log the discrepancy and ground the aircraft for repair or MEL evaluation. Fuel quantity gauges: An erratically-reading fuel quantity gauge creates an operational airworthiness issue under 14 CFR 91.7(b) because the pilot cannot reliably determine usable fuel state. While 14 CFR 27.1337(b)(1) specifies certification accuracy standards for empty indication, operational reliability during normal flight is required for safe operation. The discrepancy must be logged and evaluated by maintenance. Unless an approved MEL specifically lists and permits deferral of fuel quantity gauge failures under defined conditions, the aircraft should not be dispatched without repair or verification that the gauge is functioning normally. Pilots must always confirm fuel quantity by visual inspection, never by gauge alone, especially when a gauge malfunction is known.
AI_EDIT	IH.IX.A.R1.008	Postflight inspections are frequently skipped or rushed due to complacency ('we just flew it and it was fine'), fatigue after a long flight, time pressure, and normalization of deviance — the mistaken belief that because nothing has gone wrong before, the risk must be low. In helicopter operations, postflight inspections are especially critical because helicopters have significantly more rotating components, dynamic systems, and wear-sensitive hardware than fixed-wing aircraft. Items like main rotor blade delamination, tail rotor pitch links, mast bumping evidence, hydraulic line chafing, and transmission chip detector indicators can only be caught on the ground. Missing these during postflight means the next PIC unknowingly departs with an undetected hazard — breaking the feedback loop that makes the maintenance system effective. The FAA's documented position, per FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13, is that approximately 80% of aviation accidents are caused by human factors/pilot error, and that accidents typically result from a chain of multiple poor decisions rather than a single catastrophic error. A skipped postflight is often one link in that error chain — it rarely kills by itself, but it removes a critical safety check. Thoroughly documenting discrepancies and performing consistent postflight inspections is one of the highest-value risk mitigation behaviors available to a pilot.	->	Postflight inspections are frequently skipped or rushed due to complacency ('we just flew it and it was fine'), fatigue after a long flight, time pressure, and normalization of deviance — the mistaken belief that because nothing has gone wrong before, the risk must be low. In helicopter operations, postflight inspections are especially critical because helicopters have significantly more rotating components, dynamic systems, and wear-sensitive hardware than fixed-wing aircraft. Items like main rotor blade delamination, tail rotor pitch links, mast bumping evidence, hydraulic line chafing, and transmission chip detector indicators can only be caught on the ground. Missing these during postflight means the next PIC unknowingly departs with an undetected hazard — breaking the feedback loop that makes the maintenance system effective. The FAA's documented position, per FAA and NTSB accident data, is that a significant percentage of aviation accidents involve human factors or pilot error as contributing causes, and that accidents typically result from a chain of multiple poor decisions rather than a single catastrophic error. A skipped postflight is often one link in that error chain — it rarely kills by itself, but it removes a critical safety check. Thoroughly documenting discrepancies and performing consistent postflight inspections is one of the highest-value risk mitigation behaviors available to a pilot.
