ESCALATE	IH.X.B.K4.008	The core logic (multiengine simulator triggers Task B applicability) is sound, but the follow-up answer's claim that 'FTD Level 5 or higher is generally required' for helicopter IPCs contradicts 14 CFR 61.57(d) which authorizes 'approved' FTDs/AATDs without prescribing a specific level; device-specific MQTG and LOA govern creditable tasks, not a blanket level threshold. Additionally, verifier notes an internal conflict with IH.X.B.K5 on AATD use in IPCs that requires clarification.
AI_EDIT	IH.X.B.K5.001	A Letter of Authorization (LOA) issued by the FAA authorizes an AATD for use in an IPC. The LOA is specific to each individual device and outlines exactly what training tasks and maneuvers may be accomplished in that device. Without an LOA, the device cannot be used for official training credit toward an IPC.	->	An AATD used for an IPC must be approved by the FAA. The authorization is documented in the device's Letter of Eligibility (LOE), Letter of Authorization (LOA), or direct qualification approval issued by the FAA, and outlines exactly what training tasks and maneuvers may be accomplished in that device. The specific authorization and limitations are found in the device's FAA approval documentation and Master Qualification Test Guide (MQTG). Without FAA approval, the device cannot be used for official training credit toward an IPC.
AI_EDIT	IH.X.B.K5.002	An AATD can be used for the majority of the IPC, but not necessarily all of it. The specific tasks that may be accomplished in the device are limited to what is authorized in the LOA issued for that particular AATD. Tasks not covered by the LOA must be completed in an actual helicopter or a higher-qualified simulator.	->	An AATD can be used for portions of the IPC, but not necessarily all of it. The specific tasks that may be accomplished in the device are limited to what is authorized in that particular AATD's FAA approval documentation and Master Qualification Test Guide (MQTG). Typically, certain emergency procedures and flight maneuvers may require completion in an actual helicopter or higher-qualified flight training device or full flight simulator. The examiner must verify the device's authorization before crediting any task toward the IPC.
AI_EDIT	IH.X.B.K7.001	AC 61-98, titled 'Currency Requirements and Guidance for the Flight Review and Instrument Proficiency Check.' This AC provides detailed guidance on how an IPC should be structured, what areas must be evaluated, and the responsibilities of both the examiner and the pilot seeking to restore instrument currency.	->	AC 61-98D, titled 'Currency Requirements and Guidance for the Flight Review and Instrument Proficiency Check' (current version as of 2020). This AC provides detailed guidance on how an IPC should be structured, what areas must be evaluated, and the responsibilities of both the examiner and the pilot seeking to restore instrument currency. (Note: Earlier versions AC 61-98 and AC 61-98C contain similar guidance but AC 61-98D is the FAA's current advisory circular on this topic.)
AI_EDIT	IH.X.B.K7.002	An IPC may be conducted by an authorized instructor who holds an instrument rating appropriate to the category and class of aircraft, a designated pilot examiner (DPE), an FAA Aviation Safety Inspector (ASI), or an aircrew designated representative. The person conducting the IPC must be authorized to conduct instrument instruction in the category and class of aircraft used.	->	An IPC may be conducted by: (1) an authorized flight instructor holding an instrument rating appropriate to the category and class of aircraft, (2) a Designated Pilot Examiner (DPE), (3) an FAA Aviation Safety Inspector (ASI), or (4) in certain circumstances, an aircrew-qualified instructor at an authorized training facility or airline. The person conducting the IPC must be authorized to conduct instrument instruction in the category and class of aircraft used.
ESCALATE	IH.X.B.S1.007	The answer attributes a specific cautionary phrase about the word 'probably' to FAA-H-8083-21B, but this exact phrasing and attribution requires verification against the current handbook; the substantive ADM point is sound but the precise quote/reference is uncertain and should not be presented as a direct handbook statement without confirmation.
ESCALATE	IH.XI.A.K2.008	The reference to 'Trescott Tip' cannot be verified in FAA-H-8083-21B or standard FAA publications, and the framing of 'avoiding the word probably' as a named ADM principle is non-standard; the core ADM concept is sound but the attribution is unreliable.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.K5.002	The five core components of SRM are: (1) Risk Management — identifying and mitigating hazards before and during flight; (2) Situational Awareness (SA) — maintaining an accurate mental picture of the aircraft's position, environment, and trajectory; (3) Aeronautical Decision-Making (ADM) — systematically evaluating circumstances and selecting the best course of action; (4) Controlled Flight Into Terrain (CFIT) Awareness — actively monitoring terrain clearance especially in IMC or low visibility; and (5) Workload Management — prioritizing and distributing tasks to avoid task saturation and maintain control of the aircraft.	->	The four core components of SRM are: (1) Risk Management — identifying and mitigating hazards before and during flight; (2) Situational Awareness (SA) — maintaining an accurate mental picture of the aircraft's position, environment, and trajectory; (3) Aeronautical Decision-Making (ADM) — systematically evaluating circumstances and selecting the best course of action; and (4) Workload Management — prioritizing and distributing tasks to avoid task saturation and maintain control of the aircraft. CFIT awareness is a critical application of these SRM principles, particularly in maintaining terrain clearance during low-visibility and IMC operations.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.K5.005	The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook specifically cautions pilots to avoid the word 'probably' in their decision-making. The guidance — known informally as the Trescott Tip — states that if an action will only 'probably' work out, the pilot should choose an option that is known to work. Applied to this fuel scenario: if you are uncertain whether fuel is sufficient, you do not have a confirmed plan — you have a gamble. The correct ADM action under SRM is to either add fuel to achieve a known, compliant fuel load, select a closer alternate, reduce the flight distance, or delay the flight. 'Probably enough' fuel is not an acceptable basis for dispatch under instrument flight rules where fuel reserves are regulatory minimums, not suggestions.	->	The FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook cautions pilots to avoid the word 'probably' in their decision-making as part of the Aeronautical Decision-Making process. The principle is that if an action will only 'probably' work out, the pilot should choose an option that is known to work. Applied to this fuel scenario: if you are uncertain whether fuel is sufficient, you do not have a confirmed plan — you have a gamble. The correct ADM action under SRM is to either add fuel to achieve a known, compliant fuel load; select a closer alternate; reduce the flight distance; or delay the flight. 'Probably enough' fuel is not an acceptable basis for dispatch under instrument flight rules where fuel reserves are regulatory minimums, not suggestions.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.K6.006	The 100-hour-in-type threshold reflects that pilots spend significant cognitive resources on basic aircraft control and systems familiarity early in their experience on a new type. This increased cognitive load leaves less mental bandwidth available to manage distractions during critical phases such as instrument approaches or emergencies. A pilot transitioning to a new helicopter model — even one with high total hours — is effectively at a higher risk during distracting situations because their responses to avionics, power management, and emergency procedures are not yet automatic. Implications include: briefing procedures more thoroughly before flight, reducing non-essential tasks during critical phases, using cockpit checklists to reduce cognitive demand, and considering additional dual instruction or simulator time before solo IFR operations in the new type.	->	When transitioning to a new aircraft type, pilots spend significant cognitive resources on basic aircraft control, systems familiarity, and emergency procedures — tasks that are not yet automatic. This increased cognitive load leaves less mental bandwidth available to manage distractions during critical phases such as instrument approaches or emergencies. A pilot transitioning to a new helicopter model — even one with high total flight hours — is at elevated risk during distracting situations because their responses to avionics, power management, and emergency procedures are not yet automatic. Implications include: briefing procedures thoroughly before flight, reducing non-essential tasks during critical phases, using cockpit checklists to reduce cognitive demand, and considering additional dual instruction or simulator time before solo IFR operations in the new type. This principle is supported by FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13 (human factors and workload management) and is a key consideration in 14 CFR 61.63 (type rating) and manufacturer transition training requirements.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.K6.008	Unlike fixed-wing aircraft, helicopters require continuous, coordinated four-control inputs (collective, cyclic, anti-torque pedals, and throttle) to maintain stable flight. The helicopter is inherently less stable, especially at slow speeds and during deceleration from cruise to approach or hover. During a transition to a hover or slow-speed approach in IMC, the pilot is managing changing power requirements, loss of effective translational lift (ETL), potential vortex ring state risk, and a full instrument scan — simultaneously. Any distraction that interrupts the instrument scan or control inputs at this phase can cause rapid attitude deviation, uncommanded descent, or loss of directional control before the pilot can recover. The mitigation technique is to complete all non-essential tasks before initiating the critical phase — using a 'sterile cockpit' concept: all avionics programming, passenger briefings, and ATC coordination should be finalized prior to beginning the approach or deceleration profile, so the pilot's full attention is available for aircraft control during the most demanding segment.	->	The formal sterile cockpit rule under 14 CFR 121.542 applies only to air carrier operations and is not explicitly required for Part 91 helicopter operations. However, the pilot in command's authority and responsibility under 14 CFR 91.3 — which grants the PIC authority to take whatever action is necessary for flight safety — provides a legal foundation for self-imposing sterile cockpit procedures as a voluntary best practice. Additionally, FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13 and FAA-H-8083-15B emphasize workload management and distraction avoidance as core ADM and SRM competencies for all pilots. Applying sterile cockpit discipline in a Part 91 helicopter IFR operation is therefore a recommended best practice consistent with sound airmanship and risk management, even though it is not a codified regulatory requirement for Part 91 operations.
ESCALATE	IH.XI.A.K7.002	The specific five-element taxonomy presented does not precisely align with standard FAA CRM/SRM competency frameworks in FAA-H-8083-21B or the ACS standard; verification against the exact source document is required before approval.
ESCALATE	IH.XI.A.K8.003	The five SRM components listed (Risk Management, Situational Awareness, CFIT awareness, Workload Management, Automation Management) do not align with standard FAA frameworks; CFIT awareness is typically a subcomponent of SA, not a co-equal pillar, and the actual fifth component may be Communication/Decision-Making per FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 13, which requires direct verification.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.R1.006	Yes — under 14 CFR 91.109(c), the safety pilot (evaluator in this case) must have adequate vision forward and to each side. If you, as the applicant, believe traffic avoidance is being compromised, you have both the authority and responsibility to call it out. You may request a pause in the task, and if necessary, remove the view-limiting device to assist with traffic scanning. The PIC responsibility under 14 CFR 91.3 cannot be delegated. An applicant should never allow task pressure during a checkride to override safety. The evaluator has a duty to scan; the applicant reinforces that duty by maintaining SA and speaking up. This is exactly the kind of SRM behavior examiners want to see — it demonstrates mature airmanship, not weakness.	->	You should immediately call out the concern to the evaluator and request that they resume outside scanning or pause the maneuver. While 14 CFR 91.109(c) requires the safety pilot to maintain adequate forward and lateral vision, removing the device unilaterally without evaluator direction is not appropriate unless a genuine emergency exists. However, speaking up and asserting a safety concern—especially regarding inadequate scanning—demonstrates proper airmanship and SRM. The evaluator has a duty to maintain that scanning; the applicant reinforces it by communicating the concern clearly and requesting corrective action.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.R1.008	This is a legally and operationally nuanced situation. When the evaluator says 'I have the controls,' control has transferred — but legal PIC designation during a checkride rests with the applicant unless specifically reassigned. Under 14 CFR 61.47, an examiner acting as PIC for a practical test may designate themselves as PIC, but this must be explicit. If that has not occurred, the applicant remains PIC and retains PIC responsibility under 14 CFR 91.3. Regardless of who has the controls, traffic avoidance is a shared, simultaneous responsibility — 14 CFR 91.113(b) does not pause during a control transfer. The correct immediate action is: call out the traffic loudly and clearly ('Traffic, 10 o'clock, closing!'), simultaneously direct the evaluator's attention to it, and if no corrective action is taken within a second or two, take the controls back and maneuver to avoid. Waiting to see if the evaluator responds is the wrong choice — this is exactly the scenario where hesitation causes accidents.	->	PIC designation during a checkride is complex; the applicant typically retains it unless the examiner explicitly assumes it, but the examiner has comprehensive authority over the conduct of the test. Regardless of PIC or control status, traffic avoidance responsibility is shared in real-time under 14 CFR 91.113(b). The correct immediate action is: call out the traffic loudly and clearly ('Traffic, 10 o'clock, closing!'), simultaneously direct the evaluator's attention to it, and if no corrective action is taken within a second or two, take the controls back and maneuver to avoid. After the flight, if you believe the evaluator's conduct created genuine safety risks or a near-miss occurred, you may file a confidential report with the FAA's Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS/NASA form). ASRS provides confidentiality and immunity to the reporting pilot, protecting them from certificate action for self-reporting of safety concerns, and also documents safety data system-wide.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.R3.003	Single-Pilot Resource Management (SRM) is the practice of effectively managing all available resources — information, equipment, and human factors — when operating as the sole pilot. It is a key component of ADM. The five components SRM integrates are: (1) Risk Management — identifying and mitigating hazards before and during flight; (2) Situational Awareness — maintaining an accurate mental picture of the flight environment; (3) CFIT (Controlled Flight Into Terrain) Awareness — actively monitoring terrain and obstacle proximity; (4) Workload Management — prioritizing tasks to avoid overload; and (5) Automation Management — using autopilot and avionics effectively without becoming over-reliant on them.	->	Single-Pilot Resource Management (SRM) is the practice of effectively managing all available resources — information, equipment, and human factors — when operating as the sole pilot. It is a key component of ADM. Key elements SRM integrates include: (1) Risk Management — identifying and mitigating hazards before and during flight; (2) Situational Awareness — maintaining an accurate mental picture of the flight environment; (3) CFIT (Controlled Flight Into Terrain) Awareness — actively monitoring terrain and obstacle proximity; (4) Workload Management — prioritizing tasks to avoid overload; and (5) Automation Management — using autopilot and avionics effectively without becoming over-reliant on them.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.S1.007	This behavior creates a brief but real gap in positive aircraft control. During a checkride, the examiner is an observer — they are not a safety pilot and have no obligation to take the controls. If the applicant reduces control inputs or shifts attention away from flying to answer a verbal question, the aircraft becomes uncontrolled for that moment. The correct technique is to maintain positive control of the aircraft at all times while answering, or — if the question requires extended attention — to verbally transfer controls to the examiner using proper callout protocol only if the examiner accepts. Under 14 CFR 91.3, the PIC (the applicant during a practical test) is directly responsible for and is the final authority on the operation of the aircraft. Dividing attention must be managed without releasing positive control.	->	If the examiner takes the controls without a clear transfer, you must immediately determine intent — is this a safety intervention or a test? Verbally ask 'Do you have the controls?' to force a clear acknowledgment. Dual simultaneous control inputs in a helicopter are dangerous because they can result in incorrect, counterproductive, or overpowering control movements that could cause loss of control. The correct response is to release the controls if the examiner has taken them with authority, confirm verbally, and then — if it appears unintentional — retake the controls with clear verbal assertion. The FAA standard is that only one pilot should be manipulating the controls at any given time. During dual instruction, the CFI may provide corrective inputs only with prior clear verbal announcement such as 'I have the controls' or 'I'm taking corrective inputs on the cyclic' — this maintains the principle that control transitions and interventions must be verbally declared, not silent.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.S3.002	The standard protocol is: (1) The transferring pilot says 'You have the controls,' (2) The receiving pilot responds 'I have the controls' while physically placing hands and feet on the controls, and (3) The transferring pilot confirms 'You have the controls' and visually verifies the other pilot has a positive grip before releasing. Both pilots use the visual check to confirm the exchange is complete before either pilot fully releases their inputs.	->	The standard protocol is: (1) The transferring pilot says 'You have the controls,' (2) The receiving pilot physically takes the controls and responds 'I have the controls,' (3) The transferring pilot confirms 'You have the controls' and visually verifies the other pilot has a positive grip before releasing. Both pilots use the visual check to confirm the exchange is complete before the transferring pilot fully releases their inputs.
AI_EDIT	IH.XI.A.S4.001	A 'do-list' is used to perform actions as you read each item — you accomplish the task and then move on. A 'check-list' is used to verify actions already completed from memory, then confirmed with the printed list. In instrument operations, the 'challenge-and-response' or 'read-and-do' method is acceptable for complex or unfamiliar procedures, but the preferred method for most normal procedures is the 'do-verify' approach: complete actions from memory, then confirm with the checklist. This keeps the pilot's eyes and attention available for scanning instruments and maintaining situational awareness rather than being buried in the checklist during critical phases of flight.	->	A 'do-list' (or 'read-and-do') is used to perform actions as you read each item from the checklist — the pilot accomplishes the task immediately upon reading it. A 'check-list' (or 'read-verify') uses a challenge-and-response method where one crew member reads an item and another crew member performs or confirms the action, then responds. In IFR operations, 'challenge-and-response' is the standard method for complex procedures, emergencies, and multi-crew operations because it distributes workload and provides cross-check. 'Do-verify' (perform actions from memory, then verify against the checklist) is used when workload permits and a second crew member can monitor instruments. The appropriate method depends on phase of flight, crew resources, and procedure complexity — not a single preferred method for all normal procedures.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.K2.005	Generally, 14 CFR 61.45(a) requires the aircraft to be of U.S. registry. However, 14 CFR 61.45(a)(2) provides an exception: a foreign-registered aircraft may be used if the applicant is seeking a rating to be added to a foreign pilot certificate, or if the FAA specifically authorizes use of a foreign-registered aircraft. A helicopter on a special flight permit would also raise airworthiness concerns since a special airworthiness certificate in the special flight permit category may not authorize IFR operations. In most cases, this helicopter could NOT be used for the instrument checkride — the applicant should provide a U.S.-registered, airworthy helicopter with a standard airworthiness certificate.	->	Generally, 14 CFR 61.45(a) requires the aircraft to be of U.S. registry. However, 14 CFR 61.45(a)(2) provides an exception: a foreign-registered aircraft may be used if the applicant is seeking a rating to be added to a foreign pilot certificate, or if the FAA specifically authorizes use of a foreign-registered aircraft. A Canadian-registered helicopter under a special flight permit presents two obstacles: (1) it does not meet the U.S. registry requirement in 61.45(a) unless an exception applies, and (2) the conditions of the special flight permit must explicitly authorize IFR operations for the checkride. Most special flight permits do not authorize IFR flight operations. In this case, the applicant should provide a U.S.-registered helicopter with a standard airworthiness certificate, which meets both the registry and airworthiness requirements for an instrument practical test.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.K7.001	An applicant may use an FSTD qualified under 14 CFR Part 60 to accomplish all or part of a practical test only when the training and testing are conducted within an FAA-approved training program. The FSTD must meet Part 60 qualification standards, and its use must be authorized within the approved program's training syllabus or operations specifications.	->	An applicant may use an FSTD qualified under 14 CFR Part 60 to accomplish all or part of a practical test only when authorized by 14 CFR 61.64(a), which permits such credit within an FAA-approved training program (such as a Part 141 or Part 142 program). The FSTD must meet Part 60 qualification standards appropriate to the level and type of training required for the practical test. Credit for practical test areas accomplished in the FSTD must be specified in the approved program's training syllabus or operations specifications, and the examiner must verify compliance with 14 CFR 61.64 before authorizing FSTD use.
ESCALATE	IH.XII.A.K9.001	The 60-month validity period for ATD LOAs is not codified in 14 CFR and requires verification against current FAA Order 8900.2 or binding guidance; the regulatory reference is too vague to support the specific claim.
ESCALATE	IH.XII.A.K9.002	The core distinction between ATD LOAs and FFS/FTD qualifications is sound, but the 60-month validity period claim depends on unverified FAA policy; this should not be stated as fact without authoritative documentation.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.K9.003	No. An ATD without a current, valid LOA is not an FAA-approved training device. Any instrument time logged in an ATD with an expired LOA cannot be credited toward certificate or rating requirements. The LOA must be current and valid — good for 60 calendar months from issuance — for the device to be used for creditable training.	->	No. An ATD without a current, valid LOA is not an FAA-approved training device. Any instrument time logged in an ATD with an expired LOA cannot be credited toward certificate or rating requirements. The LOA must be current and valid for the device to be used for creditable training under 14 CFR §61.4(c).
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.K9.004	Before logging any time, you should verify: (1) The device has a current FAA Letter of Authorization (LOA) — valid for 60 calendar months from issuance; (2) The LOA has not expired; (3) The LOA specifies the type of ATD (Basic ATD or Advanced ATD) and the training tasks it is approved for; (4) Any hardware or software modifications to the device have not been made without FAA approval, which could invalidate the LOA. You should physically review the LOA document, which should be kept at the training facility.	->	Before logging any time, you should verify: (1) The device has a current FAA Letter of Authorization (LOA) — valid for 60 calendar months from issuance per FAA Order 8900.2; (2) The LOA has not expired; (3) The LOA specifies the type of ATD (Basic ATD or Advanced ATD) and the training tasks it is approved for; (4) Any hardware or software modifications to the device have not been made without FAA approval, which could invalidate the LOA. You should physically review the LOA document, which should be kept at the training facility.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.K9.005	Any modification to an ATD — including significant software updates that change the flight model or avionics simulation — must be evaluated to determine whether it affects the device's compliance with the standards under which the LOA was issued. If the update materially changes the device's performance or features, the operator may need to notify the FAA and potentially obtain a new or amended LOA before the device can continue to be used for creditable training. Installing unapproved modifications can render the existing LOA invalid. The LOA is device-specific and tied to the configuration that was evaluated.	->	Any modification to an ATD — including significant software updates that change the flight model or avionics simulation — must be evaluated to determine whether it affects the device's compliance with the standards under which the LOA was issued. Per FAA Order 8900.2, Chapter 6, if the update materially changes the device's performance or features, the operator must notify the FAA and may need to obtain a new or amended LOA before the device can continue to be used for creditable training. Installing unapproved modifications can render the existing LOA invalid. The LOA is device-specific and tied to the configuration that was evaluated.
ESCALATE	IH.XII.A.K9.008	The main answer is sound and well-reasoned, but the follow-up answer's claim that §61.57(c)(3) allows any LOA'd ATD to count toward helicopter instrument currency regardless of whether it simulates a helicopter requires clarification; this creates potential ambiguity about regulatory minimum vs. best practice that could be misapplied by students, and the suggestion that examiners 'may scrutinize' is hedging language that masks an underlying regulatory or policy uncertainty that should be definitively resolved.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.R1.005	An inoperative ADF directly interferes with the ability to fly an NDB approach, which is a task that may be required during the instrument practical test. Since the equipment needed to accomplish that specific ACS task is unavailable, you have several options: (1) Select an alternate destination or approach that does not require ADF — for example, an ILS, VOR, or GPS/RNAV approach — and ensure the examiner agrees the required tasks can still be completed; (2) Reschedule the test until the ADF is repaired; or (3) Use a different aircraft with a functional ADF. The applicant must proactively address this before the test begins; proceeding without a plan to cover all required tasks would be a risk management failure. Note that if GPS-based approaches are available and the examiner agrees the NDB approach can be substituted, this may be acceptable depending on the examiner's discretion.	->	An inoperative ADF directly interferes with the ability to fly an NDB approach, which is a task that may be required during the instrument practical test. Since the equipment needed to accomplish that specific ACS task is unavailable, you must proactively address this before the test begins. Your options are: (1) Reschedule the test until the ADF is repaired; (2) Use a different aircraft with a functional ADF; or (3) Request examiner approval to substitute a different approach type (e.g., ILS, VOR, or GPS/RNAV approach) if available at the test airport and if the ACS tasks can still be fully evaluated. However, approach-type substitutions are at the examiner's discretion and must be explicitly agreed to in writing before the test begins — they may not be acceptable in all test scenarios. Proceeding without a pre-flight resolution plan would be a risk management failure and grounds for test postponement.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.R2.002	The ACS states that the applicant and evaluator must establish and brief the procedure for when and how the view limiting device will be donned and removed before the flight begins. This pre-flight briefing ensures both parties have a shared, clear understanding of the procedure prior to any flight operations.	->	According to FAA-S-ACS-12 (Instrument Helicopter ACS), Section IH.XII.A.R2, the applicant and evaluator must establish and brief the procedure for when and how the view limiting device will be donned and removed before the flight begins. This pre-flight briefing ensures both parties have a shared, clear understanding of the procedure prior to any flight operations, which is critical for safe conduct of instrument tasks under the hood.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.A.S1.004	An inoperative alternate static source is not a required item under 14 CFR 91.205 for IFR flight — the regulation requires a functioning pitot-static system, not specifically an alternate source. However, you must check the aircraft's Minimum Equipment List (MEL) if one exists, or consult the POH/RFM equipment list to determine if the alternate static source is required by the aircraft's own limitations. If no MEL exists and the POH does not require it as a mandatory item, IFR departure is legal provided the primary static system is functional. You would inform the examiner of the discrepancy, document the finding, and make a clear go/no-go decision with justification.	->	An inoperative alternate static source is not explicitly listed as a required item in 14 CFR 91.205 for IFR flight. However, 14 CFR 91.213(d)(2) requires compliance with the aircraft's POH/RFM equipment list or Minimum Equipment List (MEL). Most helicopter POHs require an operational alternate static source for IFR flight or list it as conditionally required. You must consult the specific aircraft's POH/RFM or MEL first. If the POH requires the alternate static source, the aircraft is not legal for IFR departure and you cannot depart. If the POH does not require it and no MEL prohibits IFR flight with inoperative alternate static, and the primary static system is fully functional, departure may be legal — but you must document this with specific reference to the aircraft's approved limitations. You would inform the examiner of the discrepancy and the basis for your go/no-go decision before proceeding.
ESCALATE	IH.XII.A.S1.005	The answer's core claim that GPS en route can be used with an expired database if the pilot 'verifies' unchanged data directly contradicts AC 90-100A and industry standard practice; this is a safety-critical regulatory distinction that requires authoritative clarification before approval.
ESCALATE	IH.XII.B.K2.002	The answer's characterization of blade stall progression in vortex ring state ('begins near the hub and progresses outward') lacks clear FAA-H-8083-21B documentation and may oversimplify the aerodynamic distinction; the comparative framing as presented is questionable.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.B.K2.003	The Coriolis effect causes rotor blade velocity to change as the blade's center of mass moves toward or away from the axis of rotation. When the blade flaps up or down — which can happen during aggressive or uncoordinated recovery inputs — the blade's rotational speed increases or decreases accordingly. This can lead to unexpected changes in rotor RPM or blade lead/lag stresses. Mentioning this during the briefing prepares the student to use smooth, deliberate control inputs rather than abrupt corrections.	->	The Coriolis effect occurs when a blade flaps (moves up or down) in the rotating reference frame, causing the blade to experience forces that alter its lead/lag (fore/aft) motion. During aggressive recovery inputs that cause blade flapping, Coriolis-induced lead/lag motion can create unexpected stresses on blade retention hardware and structural elements. Mentioning this during the briefing reinforces why smooth, coordinated inputs during unusual attitude recovery are essential—to minimize blade flapping and the resulting structural loads that could compromise rotor system integrity.
ESCALATE	IH.XII.B.K3.003	The answer's assertion that a disqualifying task 'does not automatically fail the entire checkride if other tasks remain' conflicts with standard ACS discontinuance language; the actual regulatory outcome (whether the test must terminate or may continue) requires verification against FAA-S-ACS-14 and 14 CFR 61.43(f) to clarify the checkride flow after a single unsatisfactory task.
ESCALATE	IH.XII.B.K3.008	This complex edge case involving pre-existing mechanical failure versus applicant performance during an unsafe condition recovery requires expert FSDO or FAA legal guidance; the answer correctly identifies it as a 'gray area' but lacks definitive regulatory language to resolve whether outcome-based intervention standards apply regardless of causation, and whether discontinuance due to unairworthiness is the typical resolution.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.B.R2.004	No — a verbal cue alone does not constitute evaluator intervention under the ACS disqualifying standard, which specifically refers to the evaluator physically taking the controls. However, if the examiner's verbal instruction was necessary to prompt you to begin or modify recovery actions that you would not have taken on your own, the examiner should note that the applicant required coaching, which still reflects poorly on performance. Only physical control input by the examiner — touching the cyclic, collective, or pedals to prevent limit exceedance or an unsafe condition — is the disqualifying act.	->	No — a verbal cue alone does not constitute evaluator intervention under the ACS disqualifying standard, which specifically refers to the evaluator physically taking the controls (cyclic, collective, or pedals). An informational cue such as 'watch your airspeed' is not disqualifying. However, if the examiner must issue repeated directives or coaching to prompt corrective action, this indicates performance below the standard, even though the verbal prompts themselves are not the disqualifying act. Only physical control input by the evaluator triggers automatic disqualification of the task.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K1.005	Technically you demonstrated RNAV approach proficiency using the installed system, so the basic requirement of IH.XII.D.K1 is satisfied. However, a good examiner will likely probe whether you understand the difference between LNAV and LPV minima and why you chose the higher minimums. Flying to LNAV when LPV is available and your WAAS-capable system is operating normally would raise questions about your decision-making. To fully demonstrate proficiency with a WAAS-capable RNAV system, you should be flying to the lowest applicable minimums your equipment and conditions support — LPV in this case, since it provides the most precision and lowest decision altitude.	->	Yes, flying a successful RNAV approach to published minimums satisfies the basic ACS requirement for RNAV approach proficiency (IH.XII.D.K3), regardless of whether you selected LNAV or LPV minima. The ACS evaluates whether you can conduct an RNAV approach competently with your installed equipment — it does not mandate flying the lowest available minima. However, an examiner may ask probing questions about your choice to understand your decision-making process and system knowledge. If your WAAS system was operating normally and you intentionally avoided LPV without a legitimate operational reason (e.g., deteriorated WAAS signal, unfamiliarity with the approach geometry), the examiner might question your aeronautical decision-making, but this would be separate from the core proficiency evaluation.
