AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K3.007	Yes, you could still receive an unsatisfactory grade for the approach even if you landed safely. The ACS standard states that exceeding ¾ scale deflection during the final approach segment is a disqualifying deviation regardless of whether a missed approach was executed. As an examiner, I would weigh: (1) how far past ¾ scale the needle went and for how long, (2) whether corrective action was prompt and appropriate, (3) whether the deviation represented a systemic aircraft control problem or an isolated gust event, and (4) whether the overall approach demonstrated acceptable proficiency. A brief momentary exceedance with immediate correction is treated differently than a sustained or repeated deflection, but technically any exceedance beyond ¾ scale is a standard bust. The decision to fail or retest a specific task element depends on totality of performance and examiner judgment.	->	Yes, you have demonstrated an unsatisfactory performance for the specific task element of maintaining approach course. The ACS standard is objective: exceeding ¾ scale deflection on final approach is a disqualifying deviation. However, whether this results in a failed checkride or a retest of that specific task element depends on overall proficiency and examiner judgment. As an examiner, I would weigh: (1) the magnitude and duration of the overshoot, (2) the promptness and appropriateness of your corrective action, (3) whether the deviation represents a systemic aircraft control problem or an isolated event, and (4) overall demonstrated proficiency across the entire flight. A brief momentary exceedance with immediate correction may allow a retest of just that task; sustained or repeated deflections would more likely result in an overall checkride failure. But understand: any exceedance beyond ¾ scale on final approach violates the ACS standard itself, regardless of outcome.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K4.008	On an LPV approach, the lateral and vertical guidance is provided by WAAS GPS and displayed on a CDI or PFD in the same physical format as an ILS. The ¾ scale deflection standard applies equally — ¾ of the total needle travel from the on-path centered indication constitutes a ¾ scale deflection regardless of whether the source is an ILS or WAAS. However, LPV angular sensitivity can vary by procedure and is not always identical to a traditional ILS glideslope. ILS glideslope full-scale deflection is typically around 0.7° (full scale above or below glidepath), while LPV angular sensitivity is set by the approach procedure design and may differ slightly. In both cases, ¾ of the full travel from center is the threshold. A pilot must treat ¾ scale deflection on an LPV with the same urgency as on an ILS — if vertical deviation reaches that point prior to decision altitude, a missed approach is the appropriate action.	->	On an LPV approach, the lateral and vertical guidance is provided by WAAS GPS and displayed on a CDI or PFD in the same physical format as an ILS. LPV is designed to provide approach guidance operationally equivalent to ILS Category I with a standard 3° glidepath. The ¾ scale deflection standard applies equally — ¾ of the total needle travel from the on-path centered indication constitutes a ¾ scale deflection regardless of whether the source is an ILS or WAAS. ILS glideslope full-scale deflection corresponds to approximately ±0.7° from the on-path indication, and LPV is designed to match this angular sensitivity. In both cases, ¾ of the full travel from center is the threshold. A pilot must treat ¾ scale deflection on an LPV with the same urgency as on an ILS — if vertical or lateral deviation reaches that point prior to decision altitude, a missed approach is the appropriate action.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K5.006	The 'dive and drive' technique — descending rapidly to MDA at or after the FAF and then flying level — is a legally acceptable way to fly a non-precision approach. However, in helicopter IFR operations it carries several hazards: (1) A rapid descent rate increases workload and the risk of descending below MDA accidentally; (2) Flying level at MDA for an extended time in IMC increases CFIT exposure — you are at the lowest legal altitude for an extended period; (3) Power changes required are large and abrupt. A constant-angle descent (Continuous Descent Final Approach / CDFA) is preferred because it approximates a stabilized approach, reduces CFIT risk, decreases the time at MDA, and is more consistent with helicopter precision flying technique. The FAA recommends CDFA for non-precision approaches. Note: under CDFA, if the runway environment is not acquired at the point corresponding to the MDA on the descent profile, the missed approach must be initiated — you do NOT level off.	->	The 'dive and drive' technique — descending rapidly to MDA at or after the FAF and then flying level — is a legally acceptable way to fly a non-precision approach. However, in helicopter IFR operations it carries several hazards: (1) A rapid descent rate increases workload and the risk of descending below MDA accidentally; (2) Flying level at MDA for an extended time in IMC increases CFIT exposure — you are at the lowest legal altitude for an extended period; (3) Power changes required are large and abrupt. A constant-angle descent (Continuous Descent Final Approach / CDFA) is the FAA-recommended technique because it approximates a stabilized approach, reduces CFIT risk, decreases the time at MDA, and is more consistent with helicopter precision flying technique. Under 14 CFR 91.175(c), you must establish required visual references before descending below the published MDA. If flying CDFA, best practice is to initiate a go-missed if visual references are not visible at the point on your descent profile corresponding to MDA. If flying a dive-and-drive profile, you may continue level at MDA until the MAP; if visual references are not established by the MAP, a go-missed is mandatory.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K5.008	To verify currency and legality: (1) Check NOTAMs via 1800wxbrief.com or ATIS/D-ATIS for the status of the NDB — FAA NOTAM system will indicate if the NDB is out of service; (2) Confirm the approach chart is current — approach procedure charts are revised on a 56-day cycle, and the pilot must use the currently effective chart; (3) Verify the ADF equipment aboard the helicopter is operational and on the required equipment list for IFR flight. Under 14 CFR 91.205 and the aircraft's MEL or POH equipment list, ADF must be functional to shoot an NDB approach. If the NDB is notam'd out of service, the approach is not authorized — shooting it anyway violates 14 CFR 91.175 and potentially 14 CFR 91.187 (failure to report a deviation). Additionally, since the NDB provides the only lateral guidance for this approach, flying it without a functioning station means the pilot has no way to verify course guidance, which creates a serious CFIT hazard. A GPS with an approved alternate means of navigation may not substitute unless an RNAV (GPS) approach is published for that runway.	->	To verify currency and legality: (1) Check NOTAMs via 1800wxbrief.com or ATIS/D-ATIS for the status of the NDB — FAA NOTAM system will indicate if the NDB is out of service; (2) Confirm the approach chart is current — approach procedure charts are revised on a 56-day cycle, and the pilot must use the currently effective chart; (3) Verify the ADF equipment aboard the helicopter is operational and on the required equipment list for IFR flight. Under 14 CFR 91.205 and the aircraft's MEL or POH equipment list, ADF must be functional to shoot an NDB approach. If the NDB is notam'd out of service, the approach is not authorized — shooting it anyway violates 14 CFR 91.175 (conducting an approach not authorized by the procedure). GPS cannot substitute for the NDB unless specifically authorized by ATC or an alternate approach procedure is published and available. Since the NDB provides the only lateral guidance for this approach, flying it without a functioning station means the pilot has no way to verify course guidance, which creates a serious CFIT hazard. If already airborne and the NDB fails, declare to ATC immediately and request an alternate approach or divert.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K6.002	LNAV/VNAV (Lateral Navigation / Vertical Navigation) and LPV (Localizer Performance with Vertical Guidance) are two lines of minima that provide advisory or approved vertical guidance on RNAV (GPS) approach charts. LNAV/VNAV uses barometric VNAV or WAAS to provide a computed glide path and uses a DA. LPV uses WAAS satellite-based guidance and also uses a DA. Both are displayed as a vertical deviation indicator on a compatible GPS/FMS receiver. Note that LPV is technically an approach with vertical guidance (APV), while LNAV/VNAV with baro-VNAV is commonly used as advisory guidance on non-precision procedures.	->	LPV (Localizer Performance with Vertical Guidance) and LNAV/VNAV (Lateral Navigation / Vertical Navigation) are two lines of minima that provide vertical guidance on RNAV (GPS) approach charts. LPV uses WAAS satellite-based guidance and provides an APV (Approach with Vertical Guidance) with published DA and high-integrity vertical deviation display. LNAV/VNAV also uses published DA and may be supported by WAAS vertical guidance or baro-VNAV (advisory) vertical guidance depending on aircraft avionics and conditions. Both are displayed as vertical deviation indicator on a compatible GPS/FMS receiver. Note that LPV integrity is higher than baro-VNAV and meets APV standards, while baro-VNAV is advisory only and subject to temperature and environmental limitations.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K6.005	If WAAS is unreliable or not available, you cannot use LPV or WAAS-based LNAV/VNAV minima. You must revert to LNAV minima (non-precision), and you lose the satellite-based vertical guidance. If your avionics support baro-VNAV and conditions are met (temperature limitations apply), you may still display advisory baro-VNAV guidance and use LNAV/VNAV minima with DA if authorized. Otherwise, you fly the LNAV approach using the MDA with step-down fix compliance, and any vertical guidance shown is advisory only. For helicopters, you may also use helicopter-specific HAL (Helicopter Approach Lighting) procedures if published. Pilots must always verify the RAIM or WAAS status prior to commencing an RNAV approach.	->	If WAAS is unreliable or not available, you cannot use LPV or WAAS-based LNAV/VNAV minima per 14 CFR 91.175. You must revert to LNAV minima (non-precision). If your avionics support baro-VNAV and conditions are met (published surface temperature minimum, typically –10°C or warmer, and valid temperature correction applied), you may use LNAV/VNAV minima with DA and display advisory baro-VNAV guidance. Otherwise, fly the LNAV approach using the MDA with required step-down fix compliance. Per 14 CFR 91.205 and AIM 1-1-18, you must check RAIM or WAAS status *before* commencing the RNAV approach to confirm integrity for your intended operation. If the status is invalid for your approach type, request an alternate approach compatible with your remaining authorized equipment.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K6.008	Legally, the pilot may use supplemental advisory vertical guidance from an EFB or third-party app to fly a CDFA technique on a non-precision approach, provided the underlying approach procedure is followed — specifically, all step-down fix altitudes must be respected and the MDA must not be violated. The CDFA technique itself is endorsed by the FAA (AIM 5-4-19) as a method to improve safety and stabilization on non-precision approaches by flying a constant-angle descent to MDA. However, there are significant risks with third-party EFB advisory guidance: (1) the database may not be current — electronic databases update every 28 days and the pilot must verify currency; (2) the app may not meet TSO standards for IFR navigation, meaning it cannot be used as a primary navigation source; (3) the advisory glide path may not account for terrain-specific step-down fix altitudes at that specific procedure; (4) in helicopters specifically, low-inertia rotor systems demand precise altitude management — an incorrect advisory path could lead to a controlled flight into terrain (CFIT) event. The EFB is supplemental only. The published approach plate and its altitudes remain legally binding.	->	Legally, the pilot may use supplemental advisory vertical guidance from an EFB or third-party app to fly a CDFA technique on a non-precision approach, provided the underlying approach procedure is followed — specifically, all published step-down fix altitudes must be respected and the MDA must not be violated. The CDFA technique itself is endorsed by the FAA (AIM 5-4-19) as a method to improve safety and stabilization on non-precision approaches by flying a constant-angle descent to MDA. However, there are significant risks with third-party EFB advisory guidance: (1) the EFB database currency must be verified per the publisher's update cycle — FAA approach plates update on a 56-day cycle; (2) the EFB must be authorized under 14 CFR 91.21(b) as a portable electronic device and must not interfere with aircraft systems; (3) the app may not meet TSO standards for IFR navigation and cannot be used as a primary navigation source; (4) the advisory glide path is a tool to execute CDFA technique but does not authorize deviation from published altitudes or step-down constraints; (5) in helicopters specifically, low-inertia rotor systems demand precise altitude management — an incorrect advisory path could lead to a controlled flight into terrain (CFIT) event. For baro-VNAV approaches, verify any temperature limitations noted on the approach plate. The published approach plate and its altitudes remain legally binding.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K7.003	Non-precision approaches provide lateral guidance only — they do not provide electronic vertical (glideslope) guidance. Examples include VOR, NDB, LOC (without glideslope), RNAV/GPS LNAV approaches, and LDA approaches without glideslope. Precision approaches, by contrast, provide both lateral and vertical electronic guidance — such as ILS, PAR, and LPV approaches (LPV technically is an APV/approach with vertical guidance, not classic precision, but offers DA minimums). The key distinction is the absence of an electronic glideslope on non-precision approaches, which results in MDA-based (rather than DA-based) minima.	->	Non-precision approaches provide lateral guidance only — they do not provide electronic vertical (glideslope) guidance. Examples include VOR, NDB, LOC (without glideslope), and RNAV/GPS LNAV approaches. Precision approaches provide both lateral and vertical electronic guidance — such as ILS, PAR, and LPV approaches. The key distinction is the absence of an electronic glideslope on non-precision approaches, which results in MDA-based (rather than DA-based) minima. Note: LPV approaches are technically classified as APV (Approach with Vertical guidance) and use DA-based minimums similar to precision approaches; however, for ACS testing purposes, examiners typically select traditional non-precision approaches (VOR, NDB, LNAV) to properly evaluate MDA-based flying skills.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K7.007	The applicant is incorrect. An RNAV approach with LNAV+V (advisory vertical guidance) is still a non-precision approach. The 'V' in LNAV+V indicates vertically-guided advisory information only — it is not a certified electronic glidepath and does not change the approach type or its minimums. The minimums used are still LNAV MDA-based minima, not a decision altitude. Only LPV approaches (which use WAAS SBAS vertical guidance to a DA) are sometimes called 'APV' (Approach with Vertical guidance) and approach precision-like minima, but even LPV is not officially classified as a precision approach by ICAO or the FAA. LNAV+V is therefore a non-precision approach and satisfies the ACS requirement for a non-precision approach type.	->	The applicant is incorrect. An RNAV approach with LNAV+V (advisory vertical guidance) is a non-precision approach. The 'V' in LNAV+V indicates vertically-guided advisory information only — it is not a certified electronic glidepath and does not change the approach type or its minimums. The minimums used are LNAV MDA-based minima, not a decision altitude. LNAV+V therefore satisfies the ACS non-precision approach requirement. Regarding LPV approaches: LPV is classified by the FAA as APV (Approach with Vertical guidance), not as traditional non-precision or precision. While LPV uses a Decision Altitude (DA) similar to precision approaches, it is technically distinct from both categories. For ACS testing purposes, examiners should select traditional non-precision approaches (LNAV, VOR, NDB) to properly evaluate the applicant's ability to fly MDA-based descents and execute missed approach procedures at MDA. Substituting an LPV for the non-precision requirement does not meet the intent of the ACS, which is to demonstrate proficiency with MDA-based flying.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K8.008	You may descend to the procedure turn altitude only after passing the IAF and turning outbound — not before. The procedure turn altitude is published on the chart and must not be descended below until you are established inbound on the final approach course and have passed the appropriate fix. Regarding outbound distance: you must remain within 10 NM of the facility (or the distance specified on the chart) unless a different limit is depicted. This boundary defines the protected airspace for the procedure turn. Exceeding the published distance limit puts you outside obstacle clearance protection. In a helicopter, because of slower speeds, this is rarely a problem operationally, but the regulation applies equally regardless of aircraft type or speed.	->	You may descend to the procedure turn altitude after passing the IAF and establishing on the outbound course, following the descent profile depicted on the chart. The procedure turn altitude is published on the chart and must not be descended below until you are established inbound on the final approach course. Regarding outbound distance: you must remain within 10 NM of the facility (or the distance specified on the chart) unless a different limit is depicted. This boundary defines the protected airspace for the procedure turn. Exceeding the published distance limit puts you outside obstacle clearance protection. In a helicopter, because of slower speeds, this is rarely a problem operationally, but the regulation applies equally regardless of aircraft type or speed. [Ref: 14 CFR 91.175; AIM 5-4-9(a)(1); FAA-H-8083-15B Chapter 10]
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K11.002	A precision approach must meet three criteria: it must be a standard instrument approach procedure (published by the FAA), it must have a published Decision Altitude (DA), and it must provide approved vertical guidance. The vertical guidance must come from an approved source — historically ILS glideslope, MLS, or PAR. An approach that provides advisory vertical guidance only (such as a LNAV/VNAV approach using barometric VNAV) is technically an approach with vertical guidance (APV), not a true precision approach, because the vertical guidance has not been approved to precision approach standards.	->	A precision approach is defined by its use of an approved precision-guidance signal source that meets FAA certification standards for vertical guidance integrity and accuracy. True precision approaches use ILS glideslope, PAR, or GLS (GBAS). LNAV/VNAV approaches use GPS/WAAS or barometric vertical navigation, which do not meet the precision approach signal source and integrity standards and are therefore classified as Approaches with Vertical Guidance (APV). The key distinction is the signal source, its certified accuracy and integrity monitoring, and international standardization around ILS-like performance, rather than merely whether guidance is characterized as 'approved' in a general sense. [Ref: 14 CFR 91.175; FAA-H-8083-15B Chapter 9; AIM 1-1-18]
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K11.007	The pilot is correct that LPV approaches with DA as low as 200 feet AGL are operationally equivalent to ILS Category I approaches in terms of minimums. However, the FAA and ICAO maintain a formal distinction: LPV approaches are classified as Approaches with Vertical Guidance (APV), not precision approaches, because the WAAS/GPS vertical guidance does not meet the historical precision approach certification standard under ICAO Annex 10. The classification matters in several ways: (1) Some foreign airports and regulatory authorities only authorize pilots with precision approach currency or authorization — LPV may not satisfy that requirement abroad; (2) Certain operator authorizations (Part 135, Part 121) may specifically require ILS or PAR for certain operations; (3) Aircraft certification and autopilot coupling for Category II/III operations require ILS, not GPS. So while day-to-day operationally the difference in minimums may be negligible, the regulatory and authorization distinctions remain real.	->	The FAA maintains LPV as APV rather than precision primarily for regulatory consistency and international harmonization with ICAO Annex 10, which historically defined precision approaches around ILS-type signal standards. While WAAS integrity monitoring is robust and comparable to ILS, reclassification would require multilateral international standards coordination and would diverge from established global interoperability conventions that use ILS as the reference standard. The practical result is that ILS remains the global standard for precision approach operations, and the FAA has chosen to avoid creating dual classification regimes. The difference in operational performance between LPV and ILS Cat I is minimal, but the classification reflects historical standards and international harmonization rather than current performance gaps. [Ref: FAA-H-8083-15B Chapter 9; AIM 1-1-18; ICAO Annex 10]
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K12.002	During a precision approach, the aircraft navigational equipment must provide both centerline (lateral) guidance and vertical (glidepath) guidance simultaneously. Approach types that qualify as precision approaches for helicopters include: the Instrument Landing System (ILS), the Localizer Performance with Vertical Guidance (LPV) using WAAS GPS, and the Precision Approach Radar (PAR). LPV approaches have decision altitudes that can be as low as 200 feet HAT and are flown to a DA rather than a MDA, qualifying them as precision-equivalent approaches under current standards.	->	During a precision approach, the aircraft navigational equipment must provide both centerline (lateral) guidance and vertical (glidepath) guidance simultaneously. Approach types that qualify as precision approaches for helicopters include: the Instrument Landing System (ILS), the Localizer Performance with Vertical Guidance (LPV) using WAAS GPS, and the Precision Approach Radar (PAR). LPV approaches provide Decision Altitudes that can be as low as 200 feet HAT and are flown to a DA rather than an MDA, making them true precision approaches under current FAA standards. Note that PAR, while qualifying as a precision approach, is rarely available for civil helicopter operations in the NAS.
AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.K12.005	At DA, the regulations require that you have the necessary visual references to continue the approach — if you do, you may continue below DA. However, being one dot high on glidepath and right of centerline at DA means you are not in a normal position to make a safe landing. In a helicopter, you have more flexibility than a fixed-wing aircraft due to your ability to maneuver precisely, but you must still be able to make a normal landing from your current position. If the deviation is manageable and you can correct to a safe landing attitude and alignment, you may continue. If the runway environment is not clearly in sight, or you judge the deviation too great to correct safely, you must execute the missed approach immediately. The key decision point is at DA — you cannot descend below DA in the hopes of getting into a position to land.	->	At DA, 14 CFR 91.175(c) requires that if the required visual references are not in sight, you must execute a missed approach immediately — you cannot descend below DA without them. In this scenario, if the runway environment is clearly visible and you have the necessary visual references, you may continue below DA. However, being one dot high on glidepath and right of centerline at DA means you are not in a normal position for landing. Your decision depends on whether you can safely correct to a proper landing configuration and alignment from your current position. While helicopters offer more maneuverability than fixed-wing aircraft, the regulatory requirement at DA remains the same: the presence of required visual references determines whether you may continue. If you judge the deviation too great to correct safely, or if visual references become obscured, you must execute the missed approach immediately. You cannot descend below DA hoping to gain a better position.
