AI_EDIT	IH.XII.D.S2.008	A rotor RPM decay at the missed approach transition point reveals that the applicant was not adequately prepared for the rapid power application required or failed to properly manage collective and throttle (in piston/turbine aircraft with manual throttle correlation) during the critical transition. In a helicopter, low rotor RPM is a serious hazard — the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that rotor stall is not recoverable, making early recognition imperative. The correct response is to simultaneously lower collective slightly to arrest the decay, increase throttle if applicable, then smoothly re-apply collective as RPM recovers to the normal operating range, all while maintaining a positive climb attitude. The evaluator will note this as a deficiency in power management and rotor RPM control, which are core helicopter instrument skills.	->	A rotor RPM decay at the missed approach transition point reveals that the applicant was not adequately prepared for the rapid power application required or failed to properly manage collective and throttle (in piston/turbine aircraft with manual throttle correlation) during the critical transition. In a helicopter, low rotor RPM is a serious hazard — the FAA Helicopter Flying Handbook notes that rotor stall is not recoverable, making early recognition imperative. The correct response is to simultaneously lower collective slightly to arrest the decay, increase throttle if applicable, then smoothly re-apply collective as RPM recovers to the normal operating range, all while maintaining a positive climb attitude. The evaluator will note this as a deficiency in power management and rotor RPM control, which are core helicopter instrument skills. A brief rotor RPM decay during the missed approach transition is evaluated on how quickly the pilot detects and recovers it. If RPM drops momentarily but recovers promptly to within limits through correct collective and throttle management, this is typically noted as a minor technique deficiency. However, if RPM sustains below the published minimum power-on limit for more than a few seconds, or if the pilot fails to recognize or correct the decay, this constitutes an operating limit excursion and unsatisfactory performance. The evaluator will intervene if rotor stall risk is imminent (very low RPM unrecovered). A test discontinuation occurs only if safety limits are exceeded or corrective action is not demonstrated.
