Question ID:   IH.III.B.S1.006
ACS Code:      IH.III.B.S1
Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
You are flying a helicopter at 110 knots and arrive at a holding fix from a heading of 215°. The published hold is on the 160 radial (inbound course 340°), right turns. Describe how you determine which entry sector your arrival heading falls in and which entry you will fly.

Answer (at time of rejection):
The inbound course is 340°. To determine entry sectors for a right-hand pattern: the direct entry sector spans from roughly 160° clockwise to 200° relative to the inbound (i.e., headings from 340° rotating right through 160° to 200°); teardrop sector from approximately 200° to 250°; parallel sector from approximately 250° to 340° (the inbound side). My arrival heading of 215° — measured relative to the inbound of 340° — falls in the teardrop sector (between 200° and 250° offset from the inbound). I will use a teardrop entry: cross the fix, turn right to a heading approximately 30° to the right of the outbound (170° + 30° = approximately 010° outbound heading — wait, outbound is 160°, so 30° offset non-holding side for right turns means turn right to approximately 190°). Actually, for right-hand turns, teardrop offset is to the left of the outbound (non-holding side): outbound is 160°, offset 30° left = 130°. Fly 130° outbound for 1 minute, then turn right to intercept the inbound 340° course back to the fix.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
Answer contains significant confusion and self-correction in the teardrop entry calculation. The reasoning is convoluted and the final answer (130° outbound) is presented without clear derivation. While the answer may ultimately be correct, the work shown is unclear and would not instill confidence in a student.
The explanation of how entry sectors are defined and measured is imprecise. The answer conflates absolute headings with sector definitions relative to the inbound course, making it difficult to follow the logic.
A clearer pedagogical approach would separate: (1) identifying the inbound course (340°), (2) identifying outbound course (160°), (3) applying the 70/110 rule to find which sector the 215° arrival heading falls into, and (4) then prescribing the specific outbound heading for teardrop entry without internal recalculation.

