Question ID:   IH.IV.B.S2.006
ACS Code:      IH.IV.B.S2
Difficulty:    intermediate

Question:
The FAA's Trescott Tip states that if a course of action will only 'probably' work, choose one known to work. How do you apply this SRM principle when you are in an unusual attitude in IMC and considering whether to attempt a visual recovery versus maintaining full instrument reference?

Answer (at time of rejection):
In IMC, attempting a visual recovery — looking outside for a horizon that either doesn't exist or may be misleading (false horizon, terrain, lights) — is an action that will only 'probably' work. Maintaining full instrument reference during an unusual attitude recovery is the action known to work. Applying the Trescott Tip, you commit to instruments: trust the attitude indicator, cross-check altimeter and ASI, execute the standard recovery sequence. Looking outside in IMC introduces spatial disorientation risk and graveyard spiral risk. Unless you have a confirmed visual break from clouds with clear horizon and sufficient altitude, the instrument recovery is the known-to-work option. This is also consistent with partial-panel guidance: use small precise inputs and avoid abrupt corrections driven by physical sensation.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The 'Trescott Tip' is referenced but this is a colloquial or internal name for a principle that is not formally identified in FAA-H-8083-21B with that specific attribution or title. The principle itself — choosing known-to-work solutions over probable ones — is consistent with FAA guidance on decision-making and risk management, but the specific terminology 'Trescott Tip' is not standard FAA vocabulary and may not be recognized in the formal handbooks. Low confidence in whether this term appears in approved FAA training materials.

