Answer (at time of rejection):
The pilot must treat this NOTAM seriously. ILS localizer signals operate in the 108–111.95 MHz frequency range, and VOR stations operate in the 108–117.95 MHz range — the lower end of both bands overlap. A VOR transmitter experiencing harmonic distortion can generate spurious signals that interfere with localizer receivers tuned to an adjacent or harmonically related frequency. This can produce false CDI deflections, erratic flag behavior, or a false course indication that mimics a valid signal. To assess integrity, the pilot should: (1) verify the localizer identifier is being received correctly (Morse code ID), since a false signal may not carry the correct identifier or may produce a continuous tone; (2) cross-check lateral position using GPS, if available, against the ILS centerline; (3) check whether the CDI behavior is consistent with expected aircraft position during approach; (4) brief the approach assuming the ILS may be unreliable and identify an alternate approach or diversion airport. At a hospital helipad, there may be no alternate instrument approach, making this risk management decision critical. The pilot should notify ATC of the NOTAM concern and request a PIREPs from preceding traffic if any.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The answer conflates VOR/LOC interference but does not clearly distinguish that VOR and ILS localizer operate in different frequency bands. VOR operates 108–117.95 MHz; ILS LOC operates 108–111.95 MHz. The overlap is only 108–111.95 MHz. While harmonic distortion is possible, the answer's mechanism needs clarification: a VOR transmitter harmonic (not fundamental) would cause interference, which is a rarer scenario. The explanation could be more precise about which harmonics and at what power levels this becomes problematic.
