The specific risk is mode confusion — the pilot may believe the autopilot is tracking the ILS glideslope (a precision approach with protected obstacle clearance) when it is actually flying a baro-VNAV computed path, which has different and generally less precise obstacle clearance guarantees. Baro-VNAV is affected by non-standard temperature (particularly significant in mountainous/cold weather environments) and does not provide the same integrity as an ILS glideslope signal. In mountainous terrain, even small deviations below the published glidepath can result in CFIT. To mitigate: (1) Before the FAF, verify exactly which mode the autoflight system is using — confirm the autopilot is capturing and tracking the ILS glideslope signal, not a computed VNAV path, by checking the mode annunciator. (2) Cross-check the raw ILS glideslope needle against the FMS vertical path throughout the approach. (3) If the FMS VNAV and ILS glideslope diverge, the ILS raw data is authoritative for a precision approach — the pilot should fly the ILS glideslope signal. (4) If the aircraft is only capable of flying baro-VNAV on this procedure, the approach should be treated as a non-precision approach and flown to LNAV/VNAV minimums, not ILS DA, with appropriate obstacle clearance expectations. Mode awareness is a critical component of autoflight system management.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The answer conflates baro-VNAV with FMS vertical guidance in a way that may not be universally applicable. Many modern glass cockpit helicopters (e.g., Garmin G1000 NXi, etc.) with approved approaches will track the ILS glideslope directly via the autopilot, not via a baro-VNAV computed path. The scenario assumes the FMS is computing a baro-VNAV path instead of using the ILS signal, which is unusual if the aircraft has an approved ILS coupler.
The follow-up answer regarding cold temperature effects on baro-VNAV is accurate, but the original scenario may oversimplify how modern autopilots manage mode selection on precision approaches.

