The risk is subtle but significant: if the FMS waypoint coordinates are outdated, the autopilot will track toward the wrong point — potentially into terrain or obstruction-laden airspace. The risk management framework requires: (1) Currency verification — confirm the navigation database is current before flight; databases update every 28 days and an expired database should not be used for IFR navigation without specific authorization or cross-checking. (2) Cross-check — compare the FMS-depicted route against the published chart in real time; if the lateral track deviates from the published missed approach course, distrust the FMS and revert to raw data or manual navigation. (3) Distrust threshold — any time the automation behavior is inconsistent with the published procedure, the pilot should immediately revert to manual flight and raw navigation sources. Per FAA guidance, automation reduces workload only when functioning correctly and within known parameters; when in doubt, fly the aircraft manually and navigate by raw data.

