Question:
During the landing maneuver following a coupled ILS approach, the autopilot disconnects unexpectedly at 150 feet AGL just as you are decelerating over the threshold. The helicopter has a low-inertia rotor system. Describe exactly how you maintain positive control in the first three seconds after the disconnect, and what makes low-inertia rotor systems uniquely hazardous at this moment.

Answer (at time of rejection):
Immediately upon autopilot disconnect, the pilot must take over all three axes simultaneously — the hands go directly to the controls with positive, purposeful inputs rather than abrupt or reactive ones. In the first three seconds: (1) Cyclic — establish the correct deceleration attitude and stop any drift or bank that may have developed; (2) Collective — confirm power setting is appropriate for the approach profile and arrest any developing sink rate; (3) Pedals — trim out any yaw. Low-inertia rotor systems are uniquely hazardous because rotor RPM decays much faster than high-inertia systems when collective is mismanaged or a power interruption occurs. The Helicopter Flying Handbook states that low-inertia rotors decay rapidly at autorotation entry. Even without engine failure, aggressive collective inputs combined with a momentary distraction during the disconnect can cause transient RPM excursions that compromise lift authority at the exact moment it is most needed — low altitude, decelerating, close to the ground. The pilot must keep rotor RPM in the normal operating range at all times, which requires smooth collective management from the instant of disconnect.

Reason for rejection (verifier notes):
The primary answer discusses low-inertia rotor decay and is generally sound, but the follow-up answer contains a potentially problematic statement: 'Immediately lower collective to reduce blade pitch angle and allow the rotor system to accelerate back into the normal RPM range — this is the same initial action as autorotation entry.' This conflates two different scenarios. In an autorotation after engine failure, collective is lowered to minimize drag and allow translational lift/inflow to support RPM. However, in a coupled ILS disconnect scenario at 150 feet in powered flight, the engine is still running. If RPM decays during powered flight, the initial corrective action should be to reduce pitch (lower collective) only if power/torque margins allow, but the context suggests this is a transient RPM loss during a deceleration maneuver, not an engine failure. The follow-up answer risks conflating engine-out procedures with in-flight RPM management.

